window.CHLOEDB_DOCS_INDEX={"v":1,"sections":[
["index.html","ChloeDB documentation","","ChloeDB is an embedded search database with SQL: full text, structured columns, vectors and graph edges over the same rows, in one file, combined in one query by one planner. It runs inside your program like SQLite, or as a server and a cluster with the same SQL. Quickstart Install it and run your first search in five minutes. Concepts The file, tables, the three indexes, scores and the planner. Search guide Words, typos, filters, facets, vectors and hybrid ranking, on one small catalogue. SQL reference Every statement, function and option."],
["index.html#where-to-start","ChloeDB documentation","Where to start","you are making start with then a Python app, script or notebook, with the database in your process the quickstart's Python section ( pip install chloedb , no server) Python , the search guide , and Build a search page for a web app a JavaScript or TypeScript app (Node, Bun, Deno, a framework's server code) JavaScript : npm install chloedb and a database in your process the search guide , whose queries run as await db.query(sql, params) a server that several programs, languages or machines use the quickstart : install chloedb-server , a table, a search, the command center chloedb-server , and Build an app on chloedb-server for an app over its HTTP API a cluster: shards, replicas, failover Operate a cluster , on one machine first Runbooks and cluster limits retrieval for a language model Retrieval for RAG LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex long-term memory for Claude Code Install ChloeCtx : it plugs into Claude Code, and needs neither chloedb-server nor Python ChloeCtx Something failed? Troubleshooting has the usual messages, each with what to do, and chloedb-server doctor checks this machine for their causes."],
["index.html#cookbook","ChloeDB documentation","Cookbook","Complete programs for common jobs. Each one runs, as written, every time the code changes. Build a search page A search box for a small web app: typos, highlights, did you mean, more like this and a filter. Retrieval for RAG Chunk documents, search them by words and meaning, and hand a model the passages with their sources. Memory for an agent Remember turns and facts, recall what matters to a new message, and keep facts current. Crawl a site into search Point the crawler at a site, get a table with its fields, and search it. Search your logs Send syslog and OpenTelemetry logs to a table, and find the last 15 minutes' errors, a flapping interface, an IP."],
["index.html#use-it-from","ChloeDB documentation","Use it from","Python import chloedb : a database in your process, or a client of a server, with the same methods; DataFrames too JavaScript Node, Bun and Deno in-process, or a fetch client for any runtime Java in-process over JNI, and a JDBC driver HTTP and gRPC chloedb-server, its command center, and a cluster of them"],
["index.html#apps","ChloeDB documentation","Apps","Apps on a cluster run beside chloedb-server and show in its command center: the crawler , Sheets and ChloeCtx , memory for Claude Code."],
["index.html#how-these-pages-are-tested","ChloeDB documentation","How these pages are tested","The guide and cookbook pages are run as scripts: docs/guide/run.py runs every code block on a page, in order, in an empty directory, against the programs built from the same commit, and checks the output shown under a block against what it printed. A page that says so at its top has passed. The reference pages (SQL, server, clients, apps) are rendered from the Markdown in docs/chloedb/ and docs/chloectx/ ."],
["quickstart.html","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","","This page takes you from nothing to a full-text search with SQL in about five minutes, with nothing to compile. You install ChloeDB's server, make a table, put a few rows in it, search them, and open the command center in a browser. The last section does the same from Python, with the database inside your process and no server at all. You need macOS 11 or later (Apple silicon or Intel), or Linux on x86-64 or aarch64 with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12 and newer), and curl . On Windows, use WSL 2 or Docker."],
["quickstart.html#1-install-the-server","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","1. Install the server","Pick one. Each gives you a chloedb-server program, prebuilt (a download of about 50 MB), in under a minute. The install script puts it in ~/.local/bin (it says what to add to your PATH if that folder is not on it yet). It checks the download against the release's SHA-256 and changes nothing else. Read it first if you like. It asks GitHub's API ( api.github.com ) which release is the newest, and when a proxy or firewall blocks that address, or your network's address has used up GitHub's 60 requests an hour for clients without an account (an office, a school, a CI runner), it asks github.com and then this website instead, and says which one answered. If it stops with these lines, none answered: A copy of the script from before 18 November 2026 asked GitHub's API alone, and stopped with could not find a chloedb-server release on https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb/releases (set CHLOEDB_VERSION to pick one) . Either way, name the version, and the script downloads it without asking. The variable goes after the pipe, where sh reads it (in front of curl , the script never sees it): pipx, uv or pip since 0.3.7 : the same server, from PyPI , which asks GitHub nothing, on the same Macs and on Linux with glibc 2.34 or later. pipx install and uv tool install put chloedb-server on your PATH ; uvx chloedb-server runs it without installing it. In a virtualenv, pip puts it in the virtualenv's bin , on your PATH while the virtualenv is active; \"chloedb[server]\" installs the Python package beside it, for the last section of this page: Homebrew is not available yet: there is no tap to install from. Use the install script above, which works on the same Macs and Linux machines. Docker : skip to ChloeDB in Docker below, which runs the same steps against the image. By hand : each chloedb-server-v<version> release has an archive per platform ( linux-x86_64 , linux-aarch64 , macos-arm64 , macos-x86_64 ) with a .sha256 beside it: Check that it runs: If it does not, or a later step stops with an error, Troubleshooting has the usual messages and what to do; chloedb-server doctor checks this machine for their causes."],
["quickstart.html#2-start-it","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","2. Start it","A database is a file, with its write-ahead log and the server's own logs beside it ( quickstart.chloedb-wal , quickstart.chloedb.logs ). Give the server a path that does not exist yet and it creates an empty database there; it listens on 127.0.0.1:7700 , which only this machine can reach. Everything below talks to it over HTTP with JSON. To keep the commands short, sql sends the statement it reads from its input to POST /v1/sql , which runs any statement:"],
["quickstart.html#3-make-a-table-and-fill-it","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","3. Make a table and fill it","Every column is indexed for what its type is for: TEXT for full-text search (words stemmed, so \"jackets\" finds \"jacket\"), KEYWORD for exact values and facets, numbers for ranges and sorting. Rows go in with SQL ( INSERT INTO products VALUES (...) ), from files ( chloedb-server import ), or as JSON documents to POST /v1/write : Each answer says how many rows it wrote ( \"written\" ), and the rows are durable once it returns."],
["quickstart.html#4-search","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","4. Search","The rows that mention \"waterproof\", best match first. MATCH finds them, bm25 ranks them: The answer's rows are the matches, in the order of its columns ( \"objects\": true beside \"sql\" gives each row as an object instead); stats says how the planner ran the statement, step by step. Full text and structured filters in one statement, which the planner runs together: waterproof things under 100 that are not shoes. Typos are forgiven when you ask: fuzzy => 1 lets each word be one edit away, so \"jaket\" finds the jackets. Facets are a GROUP BY over the matches:"],
["quickstart.html#5-open-the-command-center","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","5. Open the command center","Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 in a browser. The command center opens on Start here , which walks through the first steps and ticks each one off once it is done; once they are, the same address opens Home , the server's health. If the crawler, Sheets or ChloeCtx were installed beside chloedb-server ( install.sh --with ), the server started them too, as apps (unless it was given --start-apps off ), and their tables ( crawl_* , sheets_* , ctx_* ) are listed apart from yours, under Tables of apps. Tables lists products : its page searches it from one box (by words, by meaning, or both), shows its rows, how full each field is, and what uses it, and Save as a saved search keeps the query as a stored function that SQL, HTTP, Python, JavaScript and agents (over MCP) all call. The SQL workbench runs any SQL you type and shows the plan the planner chose; Ask (Ctrl+J) answers a question in words by querying the tables, with an Anthropic API key (the server's, or one you give the tab). The older query console is still at /classic . With --demo 20000 , a new database starts with a catalogue of 20,000 products to try it on; here a second server, on port 7710, so that both run at once: When you are done, stop the servers (the databases stay in their files, and the next start opens them as they were):"],
["quickstart.html#chloedb-from-python-with-no-server","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","ChloeDB from Python, with no server","The Python package runs a database inside your process, the way sqlite3 runs SQLite. It is a prebuilt wheel for CPython 3.10 and later on the same platforms as the server: macOS 11 or later, and Linux with glibc 2.28 or later (release 0.2.0's wheels need 2.35). Install it from PyPI: chloedb.open creates the database the first time and opens it after; one process at a time may open it to write. The same methods talk to a server: chloedb.connect(\"http://127.0.0.1:7700\") in place of chloedb.open(...) . Python goes on from here, and Build a search page makes a search box with typos, highlights and filters from these pieces."],
["quickstart.html#chloedb-in-docker","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","ChloeDB in Docker","The image ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server (Linux, x86-64 and arm64) keeps its database in /data : mount a volume there to keep it. Inside a container the server listens on all of the container's interfaces, which it allows only with a token, so pick one. Publishing the port on 127.0.0.1 keeps it to this machine. Then every request sends the token, and the console asks for it once: and steps 3 to 5 run as written (the /v1/write call needs the same authorization header). Arguments after the image replace its default ones ( --db /data/chloedb.chloedb --listen 0.0.0.0:7700 --grpc 0.0.0.0:7701 ): add --demo 20000 by passing --db /data/demo.chloedb --listen 0.0.0.0:7700 --demo 20000 . docker rm -f chloedb stops it; the volume keeps the database."],
["quickstart.html#next","Quickstart: a first search in five minutes","Next","The query language : every function, with vectors ( similarity ), hybrid ranking, graph traversal, joins and aggregates. The server : loading files ( chloedb-server import ), schemas, embedded vector fields, API keys, replication and shards. Python , JavaScript and Java : the database in your process. SQLite , Parquet and S3 and Postgres : bring your data in."],
["concepts.html","Concepts","","ChloeDB is a search engine and a database in one library. This page names the parts you meet when you use it: the file, tables and their fields, the three kinds of index every row gets, scores, the planner, and the server that serves the same database to many clients or many machines. How ChloeDB works goes deeper into each."],
["concepts.html#one-database-one-file","Concepts","One database, one file","A database is one file plus a write-ahead log beside it ( books.chloedb and books.chloedb-wal ), the way SQLite keeps one in WAL mode, and a small directory ( -readers ) where processes that read it keep their place. Your program opens it as a library, from Rust, Python , JavaScript or Java , with no server and no configuration. The same file can be served by chloedb-server when many clients, or many machines, need it. Writes are transactions with snapshot isolation: a query sees the database as it was when it started, readers never wait for the writer, and a crash leaves the last committed state. One process writes a database at a time; other processes can open it read-only and follow its commits. Back up a database that is in use with checkpoint , not by copying its files ( backups )."],
["concepts.html#tables-fields-and-keys","Concepts","Tables, fields and keys","A database holds tables, and a table has typed fields and, usually, a primary key. A table is created from a schema, a JSON object as above or SQL: type holds searched with TEXT words, analysed (lowercased, stemmed, stopwords dropped) MATCH , bm25 KEYWORD , KEYWORD[] exact values: ids, tags, names = , IN , LIKE 'p%' , GROUP BY INT , FLOAT , BOOL , TIMESTAMP numbers, flags and times ranges, ORDER BY , aggregates JSON documents whose keys no schema names attrs.color = 'red' VECTOR embeddings, given or computed by a model similarity , distance SPARSE learned term weights (SPLADE and the like) similarity EDGES typed, weighted links to other rows REACHABLE , PATH , ppr MEDIA images and sounds, by URI the vectors and text made from them A vector field with embed is filled by an embedding model from other fields when a row is written, so you insert text and search by meaning. The model runs inside ChloeDB or behind an API (OpenAI, Cohere, Voyage, Gemini, Ollama). Inside it, every build runs potion-retrieval-32M , a fast static model; BERT models such as bge-small-en-v1.5 run only in a build from source with the bert feature, which the prebuilt server, wheels, npm package and Docker image leave out ( a BERT model does not run ). The SQL reference lists every type and option."],
["concepts.html#every-row-indexed-three-ways","Concepts","Every row, indexed three ways","Each row is indexed by every kind of index its fields call for, in the same place, so one query can use them all: Full text : posting lists of each word's rows, compressed, and scored with BM25. Columns : each field's values stored by column, for filters, sorting, facets and aggregates. Vectors : an HNSW graph (or a flat or IVF index) over each vector field, for nearest neighbours. Rows can also hold edges to other rows, which turn a table into a graph that the same queries walk ( graphs ). Rows are written into immutable segments . A new row goes to the write-ahead log and a memory table first; full memory tables are written as segments, and segments are merged in the background, as Lucene and SQLite's FTS5 do. You do not manage segments, but EXPLAIN shows a plan for each."],
["concepts.html#queries-scores-and-the-planner","Concepts","Queries, scores and the planner","Everything is SQL. Search is a set of functions inside it: MATCH selects rows with words, bm25 , similarity and others score them, rrf and weighted sums blend scores, and score() is the score a row was ranked by. A ranking can multiply in a column ( * (1 + 0.1 * rating) ), decay with age, run a learned model, or rerank its top rows with a cross-encoder. The search guide shows each in use. A cost-based planner decides, for each segment and from exact statistics, how to run each part of a statement: which condition to read from an index, which to test row by row, whether to search vectors before a filter or within it. You write what you want, and EXPLAIN says what it chose."],
["concepts.html#from-a-file-to-a-cluster","Concepts","From a file to a cluster","The same database, and the same SQL, run three ways: how when start with In your process chloedb.open(\"books.chloedb\") one program owns the data: an app, a notebook, an agent Python or JavaScript One server chloedb-server --db books.chloedb many clients, other languages, a browser UI, a web app with several worker processes the quickstart A cluster a coordinator over shards, each with replicas more data or traffic than one machine holds, or a copy to fail over to Operate a cluster One process at a time writes a database file, so a program whose processes all write it, such as a web app run by several workers or a reloader, serves it with a server and uses the clients ( Build a search page does). chloedb-server serves a database over HTTP JSON and gRPC, with a command center in the browser for queries, health and performance, and an agent that answers questions by writing SQL. Several servers run as shards behind a coordinator that answers like one database, and a primary ships its writes to replicas. The Python, JavaScript and Java clients have the same methods for a file and a server, so code moves from one to the other unchanged."],
["concepts.html#apps-on-a-cluster","Concepts","Apps on a cluster","Some programs built on ChloeDB run beside a server and appear in its command center: the crawler , which turns a site into a table, Sheets , spreadsheets whose cells are rows, and ChloeCtx , long-term memory for Claude Code. Apps on a cluster says how they attach. ChloeCtx also runs on its own, beside Claude Code on your machine, with no server: Install ChloeCtx ."],
["build-an-app.html","Build an app on chloedb-server","","A shop in one page: search, stock that never oversells, holds that expire, and click events counted exactly once. Each part is a few lines of SQL or JSON over HTTP, with links to the reference for the rest."],
["build-an-app.html#run-a-server","Build an app on chloedb-server","Run a server","Download the server for your machine (no Rust or build needed), and start it on a new database: The server listens on http://127.0.0.1:7700 (HTTP JSON, and the command center in a browser) and on 7701 (gRPC). A second server on the same machine, for your tests say, needs both ports of its own: --listen moves only the HTTP one, and a second server left on 7701 stops with \"the address is in use\", so give it --grpc another port too, or --grpc off . A new --db starts with no tables. --seq-no numbers every write, which the stock below needs. Run it has the options, the source build ( cd chloedb-server && cargo build --release ) and the image."],
["build-an-app.html#make-the-tables","Build an app on chloedb-server","Make the tables","POST /v1/sql takes one statement: TEXT is searched by words, KEYWORD is matched whole (filters, facets), and every column is indexed. emb is computed by the server from the title and body when a row is written, by a small model (potion-base-8M, 30 MB) that it downloads from Hugging Face when the table is created. Rows written before the model is ready get their vectors once it is; GET /v1/schema shows it \"loaded\": true . expire_by makes a hold vanish at its until . shard_by keeps a buyer's events on one shard once there are several . The query language has every type and option."],
["build-an-app.html#write-rows","Build an app on chloedb-server","Write rows","POST /v1/write applies a list of operations in one transaction: insert fails when the key exists, upsert replaces the row, set changes one column, add adds to a number (and creates the row with doc when it is missing), and delete removes a row by key . A 200 answer means the rows are durable. Files load through /v1/import (JSONL, CSV, Parquet, SQLite)."],
["build-an-app.html#search","Build an app on chloedb-server","Search","Rows come as lists in the order of columns , or as objects with \"objects\": true . A prefix filters but scores nothing in bm25 , so a query ranked by bm25(title, 'te*') alone returns no rows: rank by the words typed whole, or by a column while the first word is being typed ( full text ). POST /v1/correct gives a \"did you mean\" ( did you mean ). A page in a browser cannot send these requests itself from another origin: chloedb-server refuses every POST from a page of another site, a query too, and sends no CORS headers, so your app's server code queries it and answers the page ( a search box in a web app ). Typo tolerance, synonyms, phrase and prefix search, geographic and time decay, and reranking by a model are in the query language ."],
["build-an-app.html#read-one-row-by-its-key","Build an app on chloedb-server","Read one row by its key","No SQL is parsed: a read by key costs about what a key-value store's does ( rows by key )."],
["build-an-app.html#sell-stock-without-overselling","Build an app on chloedb-server","Sell stock without overselling","Read the row with its seq_no , then write it back only if nobody wrote it since ( if_seq_no ). Of several buyers racing for the last item, exactly one write applies; the others get 409 and read again. The Python package's client has the same calls ( chloedb.connect(url).get(\"l1\", table=\"listings\") , .write(ops) ); it needs only the standard library ( Python ). More in conditional writes ."],
["build-an-app.html#hold-an-item-for-ten-minutes","Build an app on chloedb-server","Hold an item for ten minutes","From until on, the hold is gone from every query, count and read by key, with nothing to clean up. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM holds WHERE listing = 'l1' is the number of live holds."],
["build-an-app.html#count-events-exactly-once","Build an app on chloedb-server","Count events exactly once","Clicks and views come from a log (Kafka, a queue, a file). Send them to POST /v1/stream as NDJSON, each with its place in the log ( source ): the server keeps each source's last offset in the same transaction as the rows, so an event sent twice, after a crash or a retry, is skipped rather than counted again. Send the same lines again and the answer says \"written\":0,\"skipped\":4 . Streams has Kafka consumers and the change feed."],
["build-an-app.html#when-a-write-fails","Build an app on chloedb-server","When a write fails","An error means nothing of the write was applied, with one exception on a cluster (the last below): 429 : the server refused the request for its load before doing anything (it is behind on flushing writes to disk, or its write queue is full). The Python and JavaScript clients send it again after the answer's Retry-After , for up to 10 seconds, and so does a coordinator for its shards; from curl , wait a second and send it again. A lost answer (a timeout, a dropped connection): send the request again with the same \"idempotency_key\" . A server that applied it answers its first answer instead of applying it twice. Keys are kept for an hour. 409 with if_seq_no : the row changed; read it again. \"partial\": true , on a cluster: some shards took their part of the write and keep it, and the others did not. The answer names each shard's part and an idempotency_key (yours, or one the coordinator made): send the same request again with that key, and only the shards that did not take their part apply it. When a write fails has the details."],
["build-an-app.html#grow-to-a-cluster","Build an app on chloedb-server","Grow to a cluster","Several servers as shards, and one more as a coordinator, answer like one database: the same tables, SQL and HTTP API. Stop the server above, then: Rows go to shards by their key, or by the shard_by column, so a buyer's events sit on one shard. A shard added later starts as the first ones did: here on a new database with no --schema , since this cluster began with no tables. The coordinator gives it the cluster's tables, and a rebalance moves slots onto it while rows keep being written: Replicas of each shard take over when its primary fails. Through a coordinator, a few things work differently: Graph queries ( REACHABLE , PATH , ppr ) run on one database only ( graphs on a cluster ). INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING and DO UPDATE , and UPDATE or DELETE with a LIMIT , are refused. INSERT OR REPLACE works. A write across shards can fail on one shard after others took their part: send it again with the key its error gives ( above )."],
["troubleshooting.html","Troubleshooting","","The errors people meet first with ChloeDB, each with its message as the program prints it, what it means and what to do. Search this page for a few words of your message. These are the messages of release 0.3.7. chloedb-server doctor checks this machine for the usual causes at once: its version against the latest release, the Python package, the models folder, port 7700 and glibc. It changes nothing, prints a fix: line under each check that is not ok , and exits 1 when one fails: Without --offline it also asks GitHub which release is the latest, and says so when it cannot. --python names the interpreter whose package it checks ( python3 by default), --models the folder and --port the port."],
["troubleshooting.html#installing","Troubleshooting","Installing",""],
["troubleshooting.html#the-install-script-stops-could-not-tell-which-chloedb-server-release-is-the-newest","Troubleshooting","The install script stops: could not tell which chloedb-server release is the newest","Without CHLOEDB_VERSION , the script asks which release is the newest, in turn: GitHub's API ( api.github.com ), then the address github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb/releases/latest leads to, then this website's versions.json , and it says which one answered. It stops as above when none did, with each address and what it said: a proxy or firewall that blocks those addresses, or no network. A proxy that blocks only api.github.com , or an address that has used up GitHub's 60 requests an hour for clients without an account (a shared office or school network, a CI runner), no longer stops it. Copies of the script from before 18 November 2026, when it learned the other two, stop at the API instead, with these lines: In either case, name the version, and the script downloads it without asking. The archives come from github.com 's downloads, which often still work where the other addresses are refused. The variable goes after the pipe, where sh reads it; in front of curl it reaches only curl , and the script fails the same way. The last line the script prints is the command to run, with your --with and CHLOEDB_INSTALL_DIR kept: Or install the same server from PyPI, which asks GitHub nothing: pipx install chloedb-server , uv tool install chloedb-server , pip install chloedb-server in a virtualenv, or uvx chloedb-server to run it without installing it (macOS 11+, and Linux with glibc 2.34 or later). The quickstart has the other ways, by hand included."],
["troubleshooting.html#the-install-scripts-other-messages","Troubleshooting","The install script's other messages","message what to do the prebuilt server needs glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12), and this machine has 2.31: ... the prebuilt archives need a newer Linux; the PyPI wheels need glibc 2.34; use the Docker image ( ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server ), or build from source this Linux has no glibc (Alpine and other musl systems): ... use the Docker image, or build from source Windows has no prebuilt server yet: run this inside WSL 2, or use Docker ... run the script inside WSL 2 could not download https://github.com/.../chloedb-server-v0.3.9/... (is 0.3.9 a released version, and does it have chloedb-server? ...) CHLOEDB_VERSION names a version that has no release, or --with a program that release does not have: see the releases /home/me/.local/bin is not on your PATH. To add it, put this line in your shell's profile (~/.zshrc, ~/.bashrc): ... not an error: the server is installed; add the line it prints, then open a new terminal"],
["troubleshooting.html#chloedb-server-command-not-found","Troubleshooting","chloedb-server: command not found","The folder the server was installed into is not on your PATH : ~/.local/bin for the install script (it printed the line to add to your shell's profile), the virtualenv's bin for pip (activate it: . .venv/bin/activate ), or pipx ensurepath for pipx. Until then, run it by its path, ~/.local/bin/chloedb-server ."],
["troubleshooting.html#modulenotfounderror-no-module-named-chloedb","Troubleshooting","ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'chloedb'","The Python that runs your script is not the one chloedb was installed into: activate the virtualenv ( . .venv/bin/activate ), or install it with that interpreter, python3 -m pip install chloedb . chloedb-server doctor --python PATH checks a given interpreter."],
["troubleshooting.html#importerror-this-is-chloedb-003-a-placeholder-","Troubleshooting","ImportError: This is chloedb 0.0.3, a placeholder ...","pip installed the placeholder that held the name on PyPI, which it falls back to when ChloeDB has no wheel for this platform or this Python. The wheels are for CPython 3.10 or later on macOS 11 or later and on Linux x86-64 and aarch64 with glibc 2.28 or later. Check python3 --version ; elsewhere (Windows outside WSL 2, Alpine, an older Python) build from source . On a machine the wheels cover, pip install --upgrade chloedb replaces an old placeholder."],
["troubleshooting.html#opening-a-database","Troubleshooting","Opening a database",""],
["troubleshooting.html#busy-the-database-is-open-elsewhere","Troubleshooting","busy: the database is open elsewhere","One process at a time can open a database to write it, and the file is open to write already: in another process (a script still running, a chloedb-server serving it, the second process of Flask's debug reloader, another web worker), or in this process, by an earlier open of the same path that was not closed (a module imported twice, a notebook cell run again, a hot reload in Next.js). since 0.3.8 (unreleased) The message goes on to say which, from the process id the writer keeps beside the file ( this process has it open for writing already , process N, the parent of this process for a reloader, or process N has it open , with its command on Linux), and what to do; Python and JavaScript add the fix in their own words. Before 0.3.8 it stopped at the database is open elsewhere . In one process, open the database once and share it; close it before opening it again. In JavaScript, keep it on globalThis ( globalThis.db ??= await open(path) ) so that a hot reload finds it. Under a reloader (Flask's debug mode, Django's runserver, uvicorn --reload ), open it when a request first needs it rather than at import, or run without the reloader. To read it from other processes while one writes, open it read-only there: chloedb.open(path, read_only=True) in Python, Database.open(path, { readOnly: true }) in JavaScript. To write from several processes, serve the file with chloedb-server --db path and use the server's clients ( chloedb.connect(url) ), as Build a search page does. chloedb-server --db path stops with the second line above while a script has the file open: stop the script first, or let it use the server too. Python's common errors has more on each case."],
["troubleshooting.html#corrupt-database-no-valid-meta-slot","Troubleshooting","corrupt database: no valid meta slot","Most often the file is not a ChloeDB database at all: a JSON Lines file, a CSV or a SQLite database given where a database was expected. ChloeDB leaves that file as it was, but makes an empty <file>-readers folder beside it, which you can delete. To load such a file's rows, give it to a database: db.import_file(path) (JSON Lines, JSON, CSV, TSV), db.import_sqlite(path) or db.import_parquet(path) in Python, POST /v1/import or the command center's import on a server ( loading rows ). A ChloeDB database that is really damaged says which file and what is damaged, and how to recover ( damaged files )."],
["troubleshooting.html#does-not-exist-pass-a-schema-to-create-it","Troubleshooting","does not exist: pass a schema to create it","The first is chloedb.open(path) without a schema where no database is: a misspelt path, or a script started from another folder (a relative path is read from the current directory). Give the right path, or the schema to create it. The second is chloedb-server import --db new.chloedb rows.jsonl : a JSON Lines, JSON or CSV file does not say its types, so a new database needs --schema ; a SQLite or Parquet file creates its tables itself. Or create the table first ( CREATE TABLE , from Python or on a server) and import into it: chloedb-server import adds the rows to the database's first table, or --table . A running server takes files through POST /v1/import and the command center's Import a file , which proposes the types from the file's values."],
["troubleshooting.html#models-and-vectors","Troubleshooting","Models and vectors",""],
["troubleshooting.html#model-potion-retrieval-32m-no-modelsafetensors-fetch-it-first","Troubleshooting","model potion-retrieval-32M: no .../model.safetensors (fetch it first)","A vector field names an embedding model that is not in the models folder. Python's db.embed_errors says so (above), a server's GET /v1/schema says it as each embedded field's error , with \"loaded\": false , and GET /v1/models lists what would open. Meanwhile the writes and queries that need the model fail: Fetch it once: chloedb-server fetch-model potion-retrieval-32M (about 130 MB). The models folder is $CHLOEDB_MODELS when that is set, else ~/.local/share/chloedb/models ( ~/.local/share/strata/models on a machine that has that folder from before the rename); a server takes --models DIR , Python chloedb.open(..., models=DIR) and JavaScript the models option. Open the database again after fetching it; a running server opens a model fetched through it (its Models page, or POST /v1/admin/models/fetch ). A server fetches a model it knows by itself when a table names it (unless it runs with --no-fetch-models , for a machine that cannot reach huggingface.co), and fills in the vectors of the rows written meanwhile; the error shows until the download ends, and GET /v1/schema then says \"loaded\": true . since 0.3.8 (unreleased) Meanwhile each embedded field in GET /v1/schema and GET /v1/tables says how its model's download goes, as fetch ( queued , running with bytes of size , done , or failed with its error ), and the command center's Add meaning shows it ( Downloading the model potion-retrieval-32M: 40.9 MB of 125 MB… ) and offers the Meaning search once it ends."],
["troubleshooting.html#a-bert-model-such-as-bge-small-does-not-run","Troubleshooting","A BERT model such as bge-small does not run","The prebuilt programs (the install script's archives, the PyPI wheels, the npm package and the Docker image) are built without the bert feature, so the BERT models ChloeDB knows do not run in them: bge-small-en-v1.5 , snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v1.5 , the ColBERT model answerai-colbert-small-v1 , and the cross-encoders ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 and bge-reranker-base . chloedb-server fetch-model bge-small-en-v1.5 downloads the model (about 133 MB) and then stops with the message above, and a table that names one lists it in Python's db.embed_errors , which refuses the writes that need it, while a server stores those rows without their vectors. Models of images, sound and ONNX exports need the vision , audio and onnx features, also left out of the prebuilt programs. since 0.3.8 (unreleased) chloedb-server doctor warns about each model in the models folder that its build cannot run, and names the feature. What to do instead: A model that runs in every build : potion-retrieval-32M (Model2Vec, 512 dimensions, the one the guides use) or the smaller potion-base-8M , and their int8 copies ( embedded vector fields ). A hosted model : openai/text-embedding-3-small , cohere/embed-v4.0 , voyage/voyage-3.5 , or ollama/nomic-embed-text from an Ollama on your own machine, with no key ( hosted models ). The prebuilt programs call them. Your own vectors : make them with any library (fastembed, sentence-transformers) and write them into a VECTOR field with its dim and no embed , then pass the query's vector the same way ( ORDER BY similarity(emb, :v) , with v a list of floats or a numpy array). A build from source with the feature : cargo build --release --features bert in chloedb-server/ ( --features accelerate on a Mac), or MATURIN_PEP517_ARGS=\"--features bert\" pip install ./chloedb-py for Python ( from source )."],
["troubleshooting.html#running-a-server","Troubleshooting","Running a server",""],
["troubleshooting.html#the-address-is-in-use","Troubleshooting","the address is in use","Another program has the port: often a chloedb-server started earlier and still running. lsof -i :7700 names it. A server listens on two ports, HTTP on 7700 and gRPC on 7701, so a second server on the same machine needs both moved: --listen 127.0.0.1:7710 --grpc 127.0.0.1:7711 , or --grpc off when nothing uses gRPC."],
["troubleshooting.html#is-reachable-beyond-this-machine-set---token","Troubleshooting","is reachable beyond this machine: set --token","A server that listens on an address other machines can reach ( 0.0.0.0 , a LAN address, inside a container) must have a token or an API key, so that nobody else can change it. Give it --token (or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN ) and send the token from every client, or listen on 127.0.0.1 ( security )."],
["troubleshooting.html#401-missing-or-wrong-token","Troubleshooting","401 : missing or wrong token","The server has a token or API keys, and the request sent none, or one it does not know. Send it as Authorization: Bearer <key> : curl -H 'authorization: Bearer ...' , chloedb.connect(url, token=...) in Python and connect(url, { token }) in JavaScript, or set $CHLOEDB_TOKEN , which both clients read. Python raises it as UnauthorizedError: missing or wrong token (HTTP 401) . A key that is known but may not do what was asked gets 403 instead, naming the scope it needs: a write statement needs a key with the write scope; \"page\" has read ."],
["troubleshooting.html#a-page-from-httplocalhost3000-cannot-change-this-server","Troubleshooting","a page from http://localhost:3000 cannot change this server","A browser page of another origin called the server directly, and chloedb-server refuses those (403), reads included: call it from your app's own server code instead, or serve the page from the server's own origin ( JavaScript in a browser )."],
["troubleshooting.html#urlerror-urlopen-error-errno-111-connection-refused","Troubleshooting","URLError: <urlopen error [Errno 111] Connection refused>","A Python client found no server at its URL: start the server, or check the address and port its log printed ( HTTP and UI on http://127.0.0.1:7700/ ). It is an OSError , not a ChloeDBError ( Python's common errors )."],
["troubleshooting.html#chloectx","Troubleshooting","ChloeCtx","chloectx doctor prints a line per check, ok , warn or FAIL , and each FAIL line says how to fix it: Install ChloeCtx goes through them."],
["search.html","Search guide","","This page builds a small catalogue of books and searches it every way ChloeDB can: words, phrases and typos, filters and facets, meaning, a blend of the two, and results spread across authors. Each query is SQL, and every block runs, in order, against the code it documents. The SQL reference has every function and option; this page shows which to reach for. You need Python with the chloedb package and chloedb-server on your PATH ( quickstart ). The server program fetches the embedding model once; everything else runs in your Python process, with no server. Running this page's SQL yourself. Each SQL block below is one statement, with the rows it returns under it. Pass it to the database as a string: in Python, db.query(sql) , with a value for each :name placeholder as a keyword argument ( db.query(sql, q=\"dragon\") ); in JavaScript, await db.query(sql, {q: \"dragon\"}) ( JavaScript ); over HTTP, POST /v1/sql with {\"sql\": \"...\", \"params\": {\"q\": \"dragon\"}} to a chloedb-server that serves the file ( chloedb-server --db books.chloedb , HTTP API ). The command center's SQL workbench runs them as they are. The first search below shows the Python form."],
["search.html#the-catalogue","Search guide","The catalogue","Twelve books, one JSON object a line: A table with a primary key, text to search, keywords and numbers to filter on, and a vector that ChloeDB computes from the title and description with a small embedding model. Fetch the model once (it is about 130 MB, kept in ~/.local/share/chloedb/models , or wherever CHLOEDB_MODELS says; a machine with ~/.local/share/strata/models from before the rename keeps using that): The same table in SQL, as db.sql(...) and a server's POST /v1/sql take it, is CREATE TABLE books (id KEYWORD PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT, description TEXT, author KEYWORD, genres KEYWORD[], year INT, rating FLOAT, emb VECTOR OPTIONS(embed = 'title, description', model = 'potion-retrieval-32M')) ."],
["search.html#words-match-and-bm25","Search guide","Words: MATCH and bm25","MATCH says whether a row has the words; bm25 scores how well, and score() is the score a row was ranked by. Both take the field to search: The same statement from Python, with the search's words as a parameter: Words are stemmed, so dragon finds dragons . MATCH reads a small query language: words side by side must all be there, OR and - (not) combine them, quotes ask for a phrase, * for a prefix:"],
["search.html#as-you-type-prefixes","Search guide","As you type: prefixes","bm25 reads a prefix as MATCH does: drag* ranks by the words of the field that begin drag ( weights, required and excluded words ). So a search box can send what has been typed so far, its last word with a * while it is being typed, to both: Changed: bm25 read drag* as the word drag before, which no book has, and a ranking by bm25 returns only the rows it scores, so the first query found nothing until the last word was whole. Against a server or package of 0.3.7 or earlier, rank by the words already typed in full, or by a column while there are none: db.search(text) ranks with bm25 too, so it finds nothing for a word half typed unless the word ends in * . A prefix is lowercased but not stemmed, and matches the stemmed words that start with it. To complete whole values instead (the searches people ran before, names kept as KEYWORD ), PREFIX(field, :typed) reads a keyword field's sorted values, with typos if asked ( prefixes and autocomplete )."],
["search.html#typos","Search guide","Typos","fuzzy => 'auto' lets a word be an edit or two away, more for longer words; ranked, it corrects a typo rather than widening every word: db.search(text) searches the words as written, and so does the search box on a table's page in the command center: a search that should forgive typos asks for it in its SQL, or writes ~ after a word ( dragn~ ), which bm25 reads as fuzzy => 'auto' for that word. With a MATCH , a row must have every word, each as typed or within its edits, and a bm25 of the same text on the same field takes the MATCH 's fuzzy : Build a search page puts this behind a search box, with the matched words highlighted and \"did you mean\" for a misspelt word."],
["search.html#filters-and-facets","Search guide","Filters and facets","Text, keyword, number and list conditions mix freely in WHERE , and the planner picks the order. A multi-valued keyword such as genres matches a row when any of its values does: Facets, the counts beside a search box, are a GROUP BY over the rows that match:"],
["search.html#meaning-vectors","Search guide","Meaning: vectors","similarity(emb, :v) scores rows by how close their vector is to a query's. In Python a parameter {\"embed\": text} is the text's vector by the field's own model, so a question finds books that share none of its words:"],
["search.html#similar-rows-more-like-this","Search guide","Similar rows: more like this","{\"vector_of\": key} is the vector a row already has, so ranking by similarity to it lists the rows nearest it in meaning: \"more like this\" under a product, \"readers also liked\" under a book. id <> :id leaves the row itself out, and any other condition goes in the same WHERE : The vector is read from the table the statement names ( {\"vector_of\": \"b01\", \"table\": \"books\"} names one). The parameter is the same from JavaScript ( {vector_of: \"b01\"} ), in the params of a server's POST /v1/sql , and through a client of a server ( chloedb.connect )."],
["search.html#both-hybrid-ranking","Search guide","Both: hybrid ranking","Words find exact names and rare terms; vectors find paraphrases. rrf fuses the two rankings by reciprocal rank, and needs no tuning: When one side should count more, normalise each score to 0..1 and weigh them: ORDER BY 0.3 * minmax(bm25(description, :q)) + 0.7 * minmax(similarity(emb, :v)) DESC . The same blend can boost by a column, * (1 + 0.1 * rating) ; ranking expressions has the rest. For the common case, db.search(text) writes this query itself: BM25 over the text fields fused with the vector field's similarity."],
["search.html#spread-results-out-max-k-per","Search guide","Spread results out: MAX k PER","A ranking where one author fills the page reads badly. MAX 1 PER author keeps each author's best row and the order of the rest:"],
["search.html#what-the-planner-did","Search guide","What the planner did","Each answer says how it was found. In Python, stats[\"plan\"] holds a line for each segment the query read: which conditions came from an index, which were tested row by row, and how the rows were ranked. Reading a plan explains the lines, and a server's POST /v1/explain takes the same SELECT and answers its plan as a tree too."],
["search.html#next","Search guide","Next","Build a search page : this page's queries behind a search box in a Python web app, with highlights, did you mean and more like this. Retrieval for RAG : the hybrid query above, feeding passages to a model. The SQL reference : joins, window functions, graphs, rank profiles, rerankers. Python : queries built in Python rather than SQL text, Arrow results, writes."],
["search-page.html","Build a search page","","This page builds the search page of a small shop in Python: a search box that forgives typos, the words it matched highlighted in the results, \"did you mean\" under a misspelt search, a filter by category with the count of each, and \"more like this\" under every product. The web page runs on Python's own http.server , so it needs nothing but the standard library and chloedb ; the Flask version at the end uses the same functions. You need Python 3.10 or later with the chloedb package and chloedb-server on your PATH . The package's server extra installs both, prebuilt (from 0.3.7):"],
["search-page.html#why-the-page-talks-to-a-server","Build a search page","Why the page talks to a server","Two parts of a search page are routes of chloedb-server , not methods of a database opened in your process: highlighting ( POST /v1/highlight ) and did you mean ( POST /v1/correct ). A web app also tends to run as more than one process: Flask's debug reloader runs your module twice, and gunicorn starts several workers. Only one process at a time can open a database file to write it, and a second one fails with BusyError: busy: the database is open elsewhere . So the catalogue lives in chloedb-server, and the page is its client through chloedb.connect , which has the same methods as a database in your process ( query , insert , search , get ). Every process of the app can connect, and none of them holds the file. A page that needs neither highlights nor did you mean can skip the server: each process opens the file with chloedb.open(path, read_only=True) , and one other process writes it ( Python )."],
["search-page.html#the-catalogue","Build a search page","The catalogue","Seventeen products, one JSON object a line: \"More like this\" compares products by meaning, with a vector that ChloeDB computes from each product's name and description by a small embedding model. Fetch the model once (about 130 MB, into ~/.local/share/chloedb/models , or wherever CHLOEDB_MODELS says), then start the server on a new database file. It listens on 127.0.0.1:7700 , which only this machine can reach: On your own machine, run chloedb-server --db shop.chloedb in a terminal of its own instead, and leave it running. The table has the fields to show ( name , description , price ), a keyword to filter on ( category ), body , a text field generated from the name and the description, which is what the search box searches, and emb , the vector:"],
["search-page.html#the-queries-as-functions","Build a search page","The queries, as functions","Everything the page asks the database is in one module. Values always go in as parameters ( :q , :category ), never into the SQL text, so nothing a visitor types can change a statement: The sections below try each function from Python before the page puts them together."],
["search-page.html#typos","Build a search page","Typos","MATCH(body, :q, fuzzy => 'auto') keeps the products that have every word of the search, each spelt as typed or an edit away (two edits for words of six letters or more; none for words of one or two letters). bm25(body, :q) ranks them, and since it ranks the same text on the same field, it takes the MATCH 's fuzzy too ( typos ). So \"jaket\" finds every jacket: Typos are forgiven only where a query asks for it: db.search(text) and the table page of the command center search the words as written, so a search box should send its text through a query with fuzzy => 'auto' , as shop.search does. Every word of the search must be there; for a box where any word will do, rank with bm25(body, :q, fuzzy => 'auto') and leave the MATCH out (a ranked bm25 returns the rows that have any of its words)."],
["search-page.html#a-filter-with-its-counts","Build a search page","A filter, with its counts","The filter is one more condition in the WHERE , and its counts are a GROUP BY over the same conditions, so each count is the number of products its link shows:"],
["search-page.html#highlights","Build a search page","Highlights","POST /v1/highlight marks the words a search matches in any texts you send it, as the field's analyzer reads them, so \"waterprof\" marks Waterproof and \"jaket\" marks jacket . The answer is HTML-escaped, with <mark> tags around each match, ready to put in a page ( size cuts each text to the stretch of about that many characters with the most matches; 0 keeps it whole): Highlighting reads typos a little more strictly than the search does: a word of four letters or fewer is marked only where it is spelt as typed."],
["search-page.html#did-you-mean","Build a search page","Did you mean","POST /v1/correct replaces the words of a search that no product has with the word of the field they most likely meant. It suggests a word only when it is fairly sure, which on a small catalogue means a word that many products use: one edit from what was typed, and in five products or more ( did you mean ). Six products say \"jacket\", and two say \"leather\", so the first typo gets a suggestion and the second does not, although the search finds both:"],
["search-page.html#more-like-this","Build a search page","More like this","{\"vector_of\": key} is the stored vector of the row with that key, so ordering by similarity(emb, :v) lists the products nearest it in meaning. id <> :key leaves out the product itself, and any other condition (the same category, in stock) goes in the same WHERE :"],
["search-page.html#the-page","Build a search page","The page","app.py serves the page at http://127.0.0.1:8000 : a form with the search box and a highest price, did you mean, the category links with their counts, and the results with their words marked and a \"More like this\" link each. It escapes everything it did not get from /v1/highlight , which escapes for it, and shows an error page when chloedb-server does not answer: Start it, and ask it for a search with a typo, as a browser would: Open http://127.0.0.1:8000 in a browser and try \"sleping bag\", \"lether\", \"warm\" or \"hiking boots\" with a highest price. The server's own command center, at http://127.0.0.1:7700 , shows the same table and the queries the page sent ( Queries ). The browser talks only to the app, and the app to chloedb-server. That keeps the server's address and keys out of the page, and it is also the only way that works: chloedb-server refuses requests from pages of other sites, reads included ( security ). When you are done, stop both (the catalogue stays in shop.chloedb ):"],
["search-page.html#the-same-page-in-flask","Build a search page","The same page in Flask","The functions of shop.py drop into any web framework. This is the page in Flask ( pip install flask , then flask --app flask_app run --debug ), shortened to the search and \"more like this\". It is shown, not run with this page, which installs nothing but chloedb : | safe is for the highlighted names alone, which /v1/highlight has escaped already; Jinja escapes the rest. With --debug , Flask runs this module in two processes, one that watches the files and one that serves. Both are clients of the one chloedb-server, so the second starts as the first did. Had the module opened the file itself with chloedb.open(\"shop.chloedb\") , the second would have failed with BusyError: busy: the database is open elsewhere , since the first holds it, and every request with it ( troubleshooting ). For an asyncio framework such as FastAPI, chloedb.AsyncClient has the same methods as coroutines ( from asyncio )."],
["search-page.html#before-other-people-use-it","Build a search page","Before other people use it","Give chloedb-server a token before it listens beyond this machine, and the app a key: chloedb.connect(SERVER, token=...) , or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN ( security ). A key that can only read the products table is enough for this page. Change a product with UPDATE or upsert : upsert replaces the whole row, so it needs every field, while UPDATE products SET price = 79 WHERE id = 'p03' changes one. Writes are searchable on the next query. Troubleshooting has the errors people meet first, each with its message and what to do."],
["search-page.html#next","Build a search page","Next","The search guide : phrases, prefixes as you type, vectors, hybrid ranking and results spread across a field. Python : every method of chloedb.connect and chloedb.open , and their errors. The SQL reference : boosts by a column ( bm25(body, :q) * (1 + 0.1 * rating) ), synonyms and rank profiles."],
["sql.html","ChloeDB's query language","","ChloeDB speaks standard SQL over the tables of a database: SELECT with functions for full text, vectors, rank fusion and graphs, aggregates, typo-tolerant and synonym searches, and subqueries, WITH and joins of a table with itself; INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE ; CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE and DROP TABLE ; and functions stored in the database ( CREATE FUNCTION ), which queries call. Text, structured and vector conditions mix freely in one statement, and the planner decides how to evaluate them together. It reads like PostgreSQL's SQL and gives the same answers for most queries, but it is not PostgreSQL: a few expressions give other answers, some statements are refused, and no Postgres client connects to it, since ChloeDB serves no Postgres wire protocol. Differences from PostgreSQL lists them, and Running SQL the ways a statement gets in."],
["sql.html#a-first-look","ChloeDB's query language","A first look",""],
["sql.html#statement-shape","ChloeDB's query language","Statement shape","Tables by name. A database holds one or more tables , and a query reads the tables it names (in any case): one, or several joined, read by subqueries or named in WITH ( below ). Quoted names. A table or column name in double quotes is the name inside them, so any name can be written: \"order\" (a word SQL reserves), \"doc no\" , \"say \"\"hi\"\"\" (a quote doubled), and FROM \"papers\" reads papers. Python's table() builds queries with no quoting at all. Strings are single-quoted, 'sale' . Where a value goes, a double-quoted \"sale\" is a string too, as SQLite and MySQL read it, and a bare name is an error rather than a string: WHERE tag = sale says no such column: sale . Clauses ChloeDB does not run are errors , never ignored: FETCH , FOR UPDATE , TOP , SELECT INTO , GROUP BY ... WITH ROLLUP and the like. An error says where and why: the parser's give the line, the column and the token ( SQL syntax error at line 2, column 1 (near \"FORM products\"): ... found FORM (did you mean FROM?) ), and a misspelt column, table or function names the ones near it and what there is. Conditions the indexes cannot answer test each row the others find: a function or arithmetic of a column ( lower(brand) = 'acme' , price * 2 > 100 , CAST(price AS INT) = 5 ), two columns compared ( price > rating ), LIKE with % or _ inside the pattern, ILIKE , IS NULL of a field whose index does not keep it, a CASE , and a comparison of a TEXT field, by its stored text ( title LIKE '%tent%' , title = 'Tent 2' ). A field with a substring index answers LIKE and ILIKE itself. The other conditions of the WHERE still narrow the scan by their indexes, so year = 2020 AND title LIKE '%tent%' reads the rows of 2020 only, and a WHERE of such conditions alone reads every row of the table. A field that is not stored (a generated TEXT field, an embedded vector) has no value to test. SELECT of no table , SELECT 1 + 1 or SELECT now() , gives one row of its values; its plan says it reads one row of no table . SELECT * of no table is an error, since there are no columns to give. CAST(x AS type) (or x::type ) makes an INT (rounded to the nearest whole number: a FLOAT 's value half to even, as PostgreSQL rounds a double precision , so CAST(price AS INT) of 2.5 is 2; a number written in the statement, or a value made of such numbers alone ( CASE WHEN qty > 0 THEN 2.5 END ), half away from zero, as PostgreSQL rounds a numeric , so CAST(2.5 AS INT) is 3), a FLOAT , TEXT , and of a value a BOOL ( 'true' , 'yes' , 1 ) or a TIMESTAMP ( CAST('2026-11-02' AS TIMESTAMP) ); text that is not a number is an error. SELECT * returns every stored, non-vector column. score() is the value of the ORDER BY score for each row (over a WITH result read without an ORDER BY , the score the WITH ranked its rows by); hops() is a row's distance in a graph traversal ( below ); seq_no() is the sequence number of the write that last changed the row, in a database that numbers its writes, for conditional writes . It is a column of a query on one table, and cannot be aggregated or ordered by. A ranking expression that scores each row by itself is a column too ( bm25(title, :q) AS t ), and features() stands for the inputs of the query's ranking. ORDER BY takes one key, or several. The scan orders by the first and breaks its ties by a second that is the primary key (or, in a facet query, the group column); other keys sort the rows the WHERE keeps in memory, as over joined rows ( ORDER BY rating DESC, price ). Ranking by a score is always best first; a KEYWORD or stored TEXT column first sorts the rows by its text, NULL last either way, which the row evaluator does after reading every row the WHERE keeps; so does a function of a row's values without a score ( ORDER BY lower(brand) , ORDER BY date_trunc('day', at) , ORDER BY hash(id, 7) ); ORDER BY distance(…) ASC ranks by Euclidean distance, nearest first ( below ). MAX k PER field at its end keeps at most k rows of each value ( below ). A key may name a selected column by its alias, as in PostgreSQL: SELECT id, bm25(name, :q) AS score FROM products ORDER BY score DESC ranks by the bm25 (an alias is read before a column of the same name). Changed: a scored alias was refused before ( no such column: score ). Parameters are :name placeholders, bound from the API or from the shell's --param name=value . A vector parameter is a JSON array. Limits. An expression nests at most 1,000 levels, counting parentheses and the operators of a chain such as a OR b OR … (SQLite has the same default); a long list of values belongs in IN (…) , which has no such limit. A LIMIT larger than the table returns every row that qualifies. A query ranked by a score without a LIMIT returns 100 rows, counted after its OFFSET , on one database and through a coordinator alike, and says so: its stats hold implicit_limit: 100 , and truncated: true with a plan line ( no LIMIT: ... ) when more rows matched ( result.truncated and result.implicit_limit in Python, result.truncated and result.implicitLimit in JavaScript). A DELETE is never cut by that default: DELETE ... ORDER BY <score> without a LIMIT is refused, since its order would only choose which rows a LIMIT keeps; write the match in WHERE ( DELETE FROM t WHERE MATCH(body, 'x') ) to delete every row that matches. (Before, it deleted the top 100.) Memory and time. What a query builds up (its groups, COUNT(DISTINCT) and percentile values, the rows it orders before its LIMIT and the rows it returns, a graph walk's nodes, a compound query's parts and join tables) is counted against a memory budget as it grows, and a query that would go past it fails with a memory: error naming the budget, giving back what it held, instead of the process running out of memory. chloedb-server's queries share --query-memory-mb ( limits ). A database opened in a program (Rust's Db , Python's Database , JavaScript's and Java's) counts its queries against one budget its whole process shares: a quarter of the machine's memory (the cgroup's limit, else RAM), or CHLOEDB_QUERY_MEMORY_MB MiB ( 0 : none), read when the first database opens; a database or a query may be given a budget of its own ( memory in Python and JavaScript , ExecOptions::memory in Rust). Such a database sets no deadline, no row window and no group cap of its own, unlike a server ( --timeout-ms , --max-result-window , --max-groups ), but for JavaScript's, which answers at most the server's 10,000 rows a query and reads past them by cursors , so that code tested on one runs on the other: a query given a deadline ( timeout_ms ), or cancelled from another thread ( interrupt() , an AbortSignal , ExecOptions::cancel ), stops within a few thousand rows of it, checking between segments and every 1,024 rows it groups, orders, walks or joins."],
["sql.html#columns-and-types","ChloeDB's query language","Columns and types","type indexed as filters INT , FLOAT (finite: no NaN or infinity) frame-of-reference column with zone maps, range histogram = , != , < , <= , > , >= , BETWEEN , IS [NOT] NULL KEYWORD dictionary codes and one docID list per value; with substring = true , a substring index too = , != , IN , NOT IN , IS [NOT] NULL , prefixes ( LIKE 'p%' , PREFIX ); GROUP BY ; with a substring index, any LIKE and ILIKE BOOL an INT column of 1 and 0 the field alone ( WHERE done , NOT done ), = , != , IS [NOT] TRUE , IS [NOT] FALSE , IS [NOT] NULL ; GROUP BY TIMESTAMP an INT column of microseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC as INT , with times written as text: at >= '2026-10-01' TEXT inverted index (optionally with positions); may be generated from other text fields; with substring = true , a substring index too MATCH ; with a substring index, LIKE and ILIKE VECTOR(d) float32 rows plus 8-bit codes (or 1-bit codes, \"codes\": \"binary\" , or RaBitQ codes of 1, 2 or 4 bits, \"rabitq1\" , \"rabitq2\" , \"rabitq4\" ), with HNSW, IVF or flat search; may be declared as a model's embedding of other fields ( embedded vector fields ) ordering by similarity / distance ; a similarity graph VECTOR(d)[] several vectors per row (a page's patches, a song's frames): 1-bit codes searched, 8-bit codes to score (float32 too with keep_f32 ), and maps from rows to their vectors and back ordering by maxsim SPARSE inverted index of learned term weights (SPLADE and similar) ordering by similarity MEDIA a file by reference: URI, SHA-256, type, size; a KEYWORD index of the hashes = , IN on the hash; GROUP BY EDGES out-edge CSR and reverse index per segment REACHABLE , PATH , ppr JSON an object's keys no schema names: one sorted dictionary of path, type, value terms with a docID list each ( JSON fields ) on paths ( attrs.color ): = , != , < , <= , > , >= , BETWEEN , IN , NOT IN , LIKE 'p%' , PREFIX , IS [NOT] NULL , IS TRUE , the path alone Predicates combine with AND , OR , NOT , AT_LEAST and parentheses, across all of these, and max_rows lets an AT_LEAST choose its count. A KEYWORD compares with text: k = 1 is an error that points to k = '1' , as n = '2' is on an INT (a write still stores the number 1 in a KEYWORD as '1' ). INT values are 64-bit integers and compare exactly: id = 9007199254740993 finds that row and not 9007199254740992, which a double cannot tell apart. Arithmetic of INT s ( + , - , * , % ) is exact while its result fits in 64 bits; a result that does not is a FLOAT , not an error ( n * 9223372036854775807 of an n of 3 is 2.7670116110564327e19 ), / always gives a FLOAT , and dividing by 0 (with / or % ) gives NULL . A client that reads such a column as integers gets a float where a result overflowed. A fraction bounds integers by rounding inward ( n > 2.5 is n >= 3 , n = 2.5 matches nothing). A write gives an INT a float with no fraction as its whole number ( 2.0 is 2, as pandas writes whole numbers in a column with gaps), from SQL, JSON and CSV alike, and refuses 2.5 . NULL follows SQL's three-valued logic. A comparison with a missing value is neither true nor false, and so is its negation: rating != 2 and NOT rating > 2 leave out the rows without a rating. Ask for them with rating IS NULL ( IS NOT NULL for the others). So does a comparison no value can meet, such as passengers != 1.5 on an INT field. MATCH and graph predicates are always true or false, so NOT MATCH(body, 'x') includes rows without a body. A filter on a multi-valued field holds when some value matches, and its negation when none does, so tags != 'red' includes rows without tags ( tags IS NULL finds those). rating = NULL is an error that points to IS NULL . rating IN (1, NULL) is TRUE where the rating is 1 and UNKNOWN elsewhere, so rating NOT IN (1, NULL) , like NOT IN a subquery that returns a NULL , keeps no row; a multi-valued field's IN is TRUE or FALSE (the NULL matches none of its values). LIKE '%' and PREFIX(f, '') hold for every row with a value and are UNKNOWN for the others, so their NOT keeps no row of a single-valued field. A scalar subquery that finds no row is NULL , and a comparison with it keeps no row either way. A BOOL is TRUE or FALSE ; a write also takes 1 and 0 and the text 'true' , 'false' , 't' , 'f' , '1' and '0' , and refuses anything else. A field named alone is a condition ( WHERE done AND NOT archived ), with NULL neither true nor false: NOT done and done != TRUE leave out the rows without a value, done IS NOT TRUE takes them in. SUM(done) counts the trues, and answers come back as booleans. A TIMESTAMP is a moment, kept to the microsecond in UTC and written as ISO 8601 text: a date ( '2026-10-01' , midnight UTC) or a date and time ( '2026-10-01T12:30:00Z' , '2026-10-01 14:30:05.25+02:00' ), with an offset or none (UTC). A number is refused, so seconds are not mistaken for microseconds. Comparisons, BETWEEN , IN , ORDER BY , MIN , MAX and COUNT work on it ( SUM and AVG do not), and answers are ISO 8601 text in UTC, such as 2026-10-01T12:30:05.250Z . In decay(at, origin, scale) on a TIMESTAMP , the origin is a time or now() , and the scale and offset are seconds: decay(published, now(), 2592000) halves a row's weight for each 30 days of age. date_trunc , EXTRACT and strftime take parts of times , for GROUP BY a day, a week or a month, and intervals move a time ( now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' ). A DATE column is a TIMESTAMP : a date written alone is its midnight UTC, and a time given is kept. A database whose tables use BOOL or TIMESTAMP is written in file format 3 , which versions of ChloeDB before these types refuse. KEYWORD and INT fields may be multi-valued ( \"multi\": true in the schema): a row holds a list, such as ARRAY['red', 'blue'] , and a filter matches a row if any of its values matches. A facet counts each distinct value once per row. A multi-valued field also compares with a list of values as a set, by PostgreSQL's array operators. The list is ARRAY[...] of values or a list parameter ( :groups ), and its values are written as the field's are: condition holds where read from the index as tags @> ARRAY['red', 'sale'] the row has every value of the list (contains all) an AND of equalities; every row for an empty list tags && ARRAY['red', 'sale'] the row has a value of the list (overlaps) IN the values; no row for an empty list groups <@ :allowed each value of the row is in the list (contained by), on an INT[] field NOT of the ranges between the list's values The list may come first: ARRAY['red'] <@ tags is tags @> ARRAY['red'] . A row without values is the empty set, so @> an empty list and <@ any list hold for it, and NOT of a set operator holds where the set operator does not, as for other conditions on a multi-valued field. <@ is how a row lists what it needs and a query what it has: with required INT[] holding the groups a reader must belong to, WHERE required <@ :user_groups keeps the documents whose every required group is one of the user's (and those that require none), and it filters a vector or text search as any condition does. A KEYWORD[] field does not take <@ yet, since its index cannot list the values outside a list; an INT[] of ids does. Over rows in memory (a WITH result), && compares any two lists, and @> a list column with a list written out. A KEYWORD[] field may hold the semantic ID of a vector field: every prefix of a code such as 12.7.201.0 , filled in by the writer, so sid = '12.7' finds the rows under a prefix. sid_at(sid, n) is the first n numbers of a code ( sid_at(sid, 2) is '12.7' ), in SELECT , WHERE and GROUP BY . A SPARSE value is a JSON object of terms and non-negative weights, {\"tok\": 1.25, \"##en\": 0.4} , as a learned sparse model (SPLADE, BGE-M3's sparse output, OpenSearch's neural sparse models) writes it. Terms are taken as given, not analysed, and weights are stored in steps of 1/1024. A MEDIA value names a file, {\"uri\": \"https://cdn.example.com/a.png\", \"sha256\": \"…\", \"mime\": \"image/png\", \"bytes\": 48213, \"width\": 640, \"height\": 480} , and a write may give only its URI, 'https://cdn.example.com/a.png' , or its bytes, '{\"data\": \"<base64>\"}' , for the server to fill in ( media fields ). Its index holds the hash: image = '9f86d0…' finds the rows of that file. OPTIONS(kind = 'image') takes only images ( 'audio' , 'video' , 'pdf' ), whose vectors a VECTOR field can hold: emb VECTOR OPTIONS(embed = 'image', model = 'siglip-base-patch16-224') ( vectors of images ), sound_emb VECTOR OPTIONS(embed = 'recording', model = 'clap-htsat-unfused') ( vectors of sound ), and whose captions or transcripts a TEXT field can: caption TEXT OPTIONS(derive = 'image', model = 'blip-image-captioning-base') , transcript TEXT OPTIONS(derive = 'recording', model = 'whisper-small') ( text from media ). A language model can make a TEXT field of another's text, told what by a prompt: product TEXT OPTIONS(derive = 'body', model = 'anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5', prompt = 'Name the product this review is about.') ( text from text )."],
["sql.html#parts-of-times-date_trunc-extract-and-strftime","ChloeDB's query language","Parts of times: date_trunc , EXTRACT and strftime","date_trunc(unit, t) is the time cut to the start of its second , minute , hour , day , week (from Monday, as ISO 8601 and PostgreSQL count weeks), month , quarter or year , a TIMESTAMP . The unit is text in any case, and a plural ( 'days' ) is taken too. date_bin(width, t [, origin]) is the start of the bucket of a fixed width that the time is in, a TIMESTAMP , as PostgreSQL's date_bin : buckets are counted from origin (a TIMESTAMP or ISO 8601 text; 1970-01-01 without one), so date_bin('15 minutes', at, '2026-01-01T00:05:00Z') starts buckets at :05, :20, :35 and :50. The width is a whole number and a unit: microseconds ( us ), milliseconds ( ms ), seconds ( s ), minutes ( m ), hours ( h ), days ( d ) or weeks ( w ), singular or plural, as '5 minutes' , '30s' or '1 day' ; months and years are not a fixed width, and are date_trunc 's. date_bin('1 day', t) is date_trunc('day', t) . OpenSearch's date_histogram with a fixed_interval runs as one. date_part(part, t) , and EXTRACT(part FROM t) which is the same function, is a number: year , isoyear , quarter (1 to 4), month (1 to 12), week (the ISO week, 1 to 53), day (of the month), doy (of the year, 1 to 366), dow (0 for Sunday to 6 for Saturday, as PostgreSQL counts), isodow (1 for Monday to 7 for Sunday), hour , minute , and as a FLOAT , second (with its fraction) and epoch (seconds since 1970). time_bucket(width, t) is another name for date_bin(width, t) , as TimescaleDB spells it: time_bucket('5 minutes', ts) gives 03:00, 03:05, 03:10. The width can also be an INTERVAL '5 minutes' . strftime(format, t) is text, written as SQLite's strftime writes it: %Y year, %m month 01-12, %d day 01-31, %H hour 00-23, %M minute, %S second, %f seconds with milliseconds ( SS.SSS ), %j day of the year 001-366, %w day of the week 0-6 from Sunday, %u 1-7 from Monday, %W week of the year 00-53 from the first Monday, %V the ISO week 01-53 and %G its year, %s seconds since 1970, %F for %Y-%m-%d , %T for %H:%M:%S , and %% for a % . A day. CAST(t AS DATE) , DATE(t) and date_trunc('day', t) are the same, the start of the time's day; ChloeDB keeps it a TIMESTAMP (there is no separate DATE type). CURRENT_DATE is the start of today, date_trunc('day', now()) , so WHERE at >= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '7 days' keeps the last seven days and today, and GROUP BY CAST(at AS DATE) counts by day. They all read times in UTC. A NULL time gives NULL , text is read as a time ( date_trunc('month', '2026-11-05') ), and any other value is an error, as are a unit, part or directive they do not have (the message lists the ones they do). A GROUP BY of one of them over a TIMESTAMP field of the table (indexed, one value per row) runs in the executor, as a GROUP BY of a column does: it reads the field's column, has no limit on the number of rows, and over shards each shard groups its own rows and the coordinator merges the groups. The rows without a time form the group NULL . Anywhere else, in SELECT , WHERE and over joined or derived rows, they are computed row by row; in WHERE they are a condition the indexes cannot answer , so a range of times is faster written on the field ( at >= '2026-11-01' AND at < '2026-12-01' ) than as EXTRACT(month FROM at) = 11 . A plan that uses them needs the plan feature dates , which shards of a version without these functions refuse, and one with date_bin also needs the plan feature date_bin ."],
["sql.html#samples-splits-and-shuffles-hash","ChloeDB's query language","Samples, splits and shuffles: hash","hash(x [, seed]) is an INT from 0 to 2 63 - 1 that only x and seed (an INT , 0 without one) decide: the same on every machine, in every version of ChloeDB, and on a file and over shards, so a sample or a split made with it is the same rows wherever and whenever it runs. Another seed gives another sample. A NULL value or seed gives NULL , so a row whose x is NULL is in no part; hash a column every row holds, such as the key. x is one value: text, a number, a BOOL or a TIMESTAMP (a list is an error). To hash several columns, chain them through the seed: hash(item, hash(user, 7)) . It is the 64-bit FNV-1a of the seed's 8 bytes (little-endian) and then the value's, through SplitMix64's finalizer, shifted right by one. A text's bytes are its UTF-8; an INT , a BOOL (1 or 0) and a TIMESTAMP (microseconds since 1970) are their integer's 8 bytes, little-endian; a FLOAT is its IEEE 754 bits, little-endian (-0 as 0). So it is the same value elsewhere, in Python for instance: In WHERE , hash is a condition the indexes cannot answer : the other conditions narrow the rows by their indexes, and those rows are hashed one by one. ORDER BY hash(id, 7) sorts the rows by it, as ORDER BY lower(name) or a part of a time sorts them: the rows the table's query finds, in memory. A plan that uses it needs the plan feature hash , which shards of a version without the function refuse. A frame samples and splits with it: frame.sample(fraction=0.1, seed=7) , train, test = frame.split(0.8, seed=7) ."],
["sql.html#json-fields-keys-no-schema-names","ChloeDB's query language","JSON fields: keys no schema names","A JSON field holds an object whose keys are not declared, for attributes that only a few rows have and that nobody knew when the table was made: a product's voltage , certs or dims.w . A row adds keys by holding them, and filters on any key run from the field's index: Paths. attrs.dims.w is the key w of the object at dims ; attrs['a.b'] names a key that holds a dot, and json_extract(attrs, 'dims.w') is the same path as a function, '$.dims.w' too, as SQLite and DuckDB write it, in SELECT , ORDER BY and GROUP BY as in WHERE ( '$' is the whole value). Changed: 0.3.8 and earlier read a '$.' path as NULL outside WHERE . In a join, qualify the field: p.attrs.color . An array holds its elements at its own path, as a multi-valued field does: attrs.certs = 'UL' matches [\"CE\", \"UL\"] , and {\"items\": [{\"sku\": \"x\"}, {\"sku\": \"y\"}]} holds x and y at items.sku . JSON null and an empty array are no value. Types. A comparison sees the values of its literal's type only, as if the path were a column of that type: attrs.voltage > 5 reads the numbers at voltage (integers and doubles alike), attrs.voltage = 'n/a' its strings, attrs.ok and attrs.ok = TRUE its booleans. A row with no value of that type there is NULL to the comparison, so NOT (attrs.voltage > 5) and attrs.voltage != 5 leave out rows whose voltage is missing or a string, and IS NULL finds rows without the path at all. An IN list of several types reads each type for its own values. No row is refused for holding a type other rows do not. Reads. SELECT attrs returns the object as compact JSON text with sorted keys. A path in SELECT , ORDER BY , GROUP BY or an aggregate returns its value, in a column named as the query writes the path ( attrs.dims.w ): a string, an integer, a double or a boolean; a list for an array of values or for several values through an array; an object as its JSON text; NULL where the row has none (an empty array included). A list makes a group per value, as a multi-valued column does, and a boolean is a group apart from the number 1. Groups, aggregates and sorts read the index. GROUP BY attrs.color , AVG(attrs.price) and ORDER BY attrs.price read each row's value from the field's terms, as a declared column's are read, so they take no row's object: a path's counts come from its terms' docID lists, as a facet's do. A row holds one value term per path; where that term is not the whole of the value (an object, an array, a string longer than 256 bytes, a number such as 5.0 or 2^53 + 1 that its term would give as another), the index marks the row, and those rows alone are read from their objects. On a million rows with 1,000 sparse keys (a 4-core cloud VM, chloedb/results/json ), GROUP BY the most held key takes 2.7 ms, against 2.4 ms for a declared column and 1.9 s when each row's object was read, and AVG of a number key held by 82,000 rows 22 ms, against 14 ms and 552 ms. Selecting a path reads it from the objects of the rows returned. A query that mixes paths with what the executor does not run itself (a join, a function of a path, GROUP BY lower(attrs.color) ) reads them from the rows' objects instead, in memory (up to a million rows), as over joined rows ; the answer is the same either way. Writes. A value is a JSON object, given as text ( '{\"k\": 1}' ), as an object in JSON Lines or the HTTP API, or as a dict from Python; NULL is no object. A write is refused, naming the limit, for anything else, for an empty key, and for an object nested more than 16 levels, with a path longer than 256 bytes or with more than 1,000 values and objects. A string longer than 256 bytes is kept and returned, and IS NOT NULL sees it, but comparisons do not: its value is not indexed (its words are, with text ). Text. attrs JSON OPTIONS(text = true) also indexes the strings of each row's object as text, one a line in path order, long ones included, so MATCH(attrs, 'charger NOT lamp') finds rows by the words of any of their strings and bm25(attrs, 'usb charger') ranks them as it ranks a TEXT field holding the same lines (with a cluster's statistics through a coordinator). Keys, numbers and booleans are not text: a path reads them. The option takes an analyzer ( 'english' , the default, or another a TEXT field takes), and the text has positions, so a phrase may span two strings that follow each other. A database with such a field needs the feature json_text besides json . The index is one sorted dictionary per segment of terms path \\0 type value (strings as UTF-8, numbers in an order-preserving encoding of doubles), so = and IN read one term's docID list, a range or LIKE 'p%' one run of terms, and IS NOT NULL the path's own term. A row pays only for the paths it holds, which is what keeps thousands of sparse keys cheap where as many declared columns would each take a slot in every row. A JSON field is stored; it cannot be the primary key, multi-valued, have a default, or be generated or the source of a generated or embedded field. A database with a JSON field is written in file format 3 with the feature json , and plans that read paths carry the plan feature json , and json_columns when a Search groups, aggregates, orders or selects by them, which a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses by name. A segment written before marks (its section json.<field>.marks missing) reads every row holding the path from its object, until a merge rewrites it. Which paths a field holds: json_paths Nobody declared the keys, so the table says what they are. json_paths lists every path a JSON field's objects hold, with how many rows hold each, the types of its values and what they are, read from the field's index (its terms and their docID lists) without reading a row: For the two rows above, the first gives: path rows share strings numbers bools min max distinct values a\\.b 1 0.5 0 0 1 1 [[true,1]] certs 1 0.5 1 0 0 2 [[\"CE\",1],[\"UL\",1]] color 1 0.5 1 0 0 1 [[\"black\",1]] dims 1 0.5 0 0 0 0 [] dims.w 1 0.5 0 1 0 40.5 40.5 1 [[40.5,1]] voltage 1 0.5 0 1 0 5.0 5.0 1 [[5,1]] A row per path , in path order, a key's dot escaped ( a\\.b ). rows counts the rows that hold the path at all (an object there counts), share is their share of the rows read, and strings , numbers and bools count the rows holding a value of each type, so a path whose rows disagree on its type says so. min and max are its numbers' range; distinct counts its values, and values lists them as JSON with their rows, most first, when there are at most 100 (past that both are NULL ). The third argument narrows the listing to a path and the paths below it ( 'dims' , '$.dims' , 'a\\.b' ). filter => counts only the rows a condition keeps, any condition a WHERE could hold: json_paths('products', 'attrs', filter => 'attrs.color IS NOT NULL') tells which other keys the rows with a color have. The listing is read whole : SELECT * and nothing else, at most 10,000 paths (past that, the first 10,000 in order). A string longer than 256 bytes is not indexed, so it counts at its path in rows but in no type. Rows deleted or updated count as they are now. Over HTTP , GET /v1/tables/T/paths?field=attrs&path=dims&where=... gives the same as JSON, each path with its keys , and a coordinator sums its shards' listings, so the counts are the cluster's. The command center draws the listing as a tree in Schema , completes attrs. in the workbench with the keys at each level, and lists the paths in the query builder as fields to filter, group, measure and sort by."],
["sql.html#some-of-the-conditions-at_least","ChloeDB's query language","Some of the conditions: AT_LEAST","AT_LEAST(n, c1, c2, …) holds where at least n of the conditions do. A condition is anything WHERE takes: a comparison, IN , IS NULL , MATCH , a graph predicate, a subquery condition, a group in parentheses, another AT_LEAST . n is a count, a percentage of the conditions ( '75%' , rounded down) or a parameter ( :n ), 1 or more ( 0 and '0%' are errors: none of the conditions is no condition). Either is clamped to between 1 and the number of conditions, as MATCH 's groups are: AT_LEAST(1, …) is the OR of the conditions, and a count past the end is their AND . NULL . A condition that is UNKNOWN (a comparison with a NULL ) is not met. Under NOT , AT_LEAST follows SQL's three-valued logic: NOT AT_LEAST(n, …) holds where fewer than n conditions would be TRUE even if every UNKNOWN one were, so it never returns a row whose answer depends on a NULL . With a = 1 and c NULL , AT_LEAST(2, a > 0, c = 'x') leaves the row out, and so does NOT AT_LEAST(2, a > 0, c = 'x') . count_true(c1, c2, …) is how many of the conditions hold, a number for ranking expressions and SELECT , exactly the sum of CASE WHEN c THEN 1 ELSE 0 END over them: a condition on a NULL counts 0. In a SELECT over rows in memory (a join, a WITH result) its conditions cannot use MATCH or graph predicates, which only a table's indexes answer. How it runs. Each condition becomes the set of rows it holds for, as WHERE evaluates it, and a counter adds the sets up 64 rows to a machine word, in ⌈log2(y + 1)⌉ words for each 64 rows; sets with few rows are merged instead. A ranking that checks its candidates one at a time tests their conditions in turn, until the count is reached or out of reach. The cost grows with the number of conditions, not with n : over 1,000,000 rows where each condition holds for an independent 20% of them, counting the rows took 3.0 ms for 2 of 4 conditions, 4.1 ms for 4 of 8 and 9.3 ms for 8 of 16, against 0.37 ms for one condition. The same conditions written as an OR of every AND of n took 8.2 ms for 2 of 4 (6 AND s) and 114 ms for 4 of 8 (70), and 8 of 16 would need 12,870 ( chloedb-at-least-bench on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, chloedb/results/strata/at_least.json ). A plan with AT_LEAST or count_true needs the plan feature at_least . From Python, at_least(2, match(\"title\", \"covid\"), col(\"year\") >= 2020) and count_true(…) build them."],
["sql.html#as-many-as-fit-min_rows-and-max_rows","ChloeDB's query language","As many as fit: min_rows and max_rows","An \"x of y\" condition can choose its own x. With max_rows or min_rows , an AT_LEAST , a MATCH(…, min => n) or a bm25(…, min => n) asks for all y of its items first, and relaxes towards n while the rows are too few, stopping before a level that would match more than max_rows : The bounds are counts of rows, 1 or more, and min_rows is at most max_rows (else no level could meet both, an error). The level. From the strictest level, all y items, a guard relaxes one item at a time while the level matches fewer than min_rows rows and the next looser one no more than max_rows , down to n . With max_rows alone that is the loosest level that fits, and with min_rows alone the strictest with enough rows. If even all y match more than max_rows , the query runs at y. The rows counted are those the rest of WHERE keeps; LIMIT does not cut them. Where. A guard is an AT_LEAST or a MATCH joined to the rest of WHERE by AND (not one under OR , NOT or another condition), or a bm25 of the first ranking of ORDER BY . MATCH and bm25 need a min , the loosest level; AT_LEAST 's is its n . A query has one guard at most, and a rerank phase none. A SELECT over a WITH result that is not a rerank phase counts that result's rows. The plan line gives each level's rows and the level chosen: guard: AT_LEAST 3 of 4 (4: 12, 3: 340, 2: 5120, 1: 23000; max_rows 2000) . Its cost is counting the rows of every level, over all the rows the rest of WHERE keeps, before running at one: a guarded query costs about what counting its matches does, whatever its LIMIT . Over 1,000,000 rows where each condition holds for an independent 20% of them, AT_LEAST(1, …, max_rows => 10000) LIMIT 100 took 2.5 ms over 4 conditions, 6.1 ms over 8 and 11.6 ms over 16, against 0.25, 0.51 and 0.98 ms for the same query written at the level chosen. For four words of different frequencies, over rows of 12 words drawn by Zipf's law from 5,000, bm25(body, …, min => 1, max_rows => 1000) took 2.4 ms at the median of 50 queries ( LIMIT 10 ), against 1.4 ms at the level chosen and 0.75 ms without min , and a MATCH guarded alike 2.3 ms against 1.3 ms. A text's words are counted once each, as AT_LEAST 's conditions are, unless it has required or excluded words, when each level is counted in turn ( chloedb-guard-bench on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, chloedb/results/strata/guard.json ). What it keeps. BEIR's queries are sentences, and few rows hold all of a sentence's words. On five BEIR collections the top 100 rows by bm25(body, :q, min => '100%') held 0.03 to 0.40 of the relevant rows (recall@100), against 0.13 to 0.97 for any of the words; min => 1, min_rows => 100 , all the words while 100 rows have them, else as many as 100 rows have, held 0.10 to 0.96, within 0.4 to 7.7 points of any word, with nDCG@10 within 0.013 of it, at a median of 0.15 to 2.05 ms a query, 0.8 to 1.6 times the any-word query's ( chloedb-bm25-eval , the _r100 variants, 3 runs each on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, chloedb/results/text/bm25_guard.json ). Over shards , a coordinator first asks every shard for its counts (protocol 8, so a shard of an older server is refused by name), adds them up, chooses the level as one database would, and sends the shards the query at that level: one round more. A shard answering a coordinator runs the level it is sent, never one of its own. With rows like those above split over 4 shards on one machine, the guarded AT_LEAST of 4, 8 and 16 conditions took 4.3, 7.4 and 12.6 ms through the coordinator (1.6, 2.6 and 1.8 ms at the level chosen) against 2.3, 3.9 and 7.7 ms on one database, and the guarded bm25 4.3 ms against 1.7 ms, medians of 50 queries ( chloedb-guard-shards-bench , the same VM, chloedb-server/results/guard/shards-4.json ). A plan with a guard needs the plan feature guard . From Python, at_least(2, …, max_rows=2000) , match(field, query, min=2, max_rows=500) and bm25(field, text, min=2, min_rows=50) ."],
["sql.html#full-text-match","ChloeDB's query language","Full text: MATCH","MATCH(field, query) is a boolean predicate. The query grammar follows SQLite FTS5: query matches vaccine mrna both words (juxtaposition is AND ) vaccine OR vaccination either vaccine -animal , vaccine NOT animal the first without the second \"messenger rna\" the phrase (needs positions on the field) vacc* every indexed term with that prefix vacine~ , vacine~1 the word and the indexed terms an edit or two from it ( typos ) vaccine AND (mrna OR \"messenger rna\") -animal any combination, with parentheses (mrna vaccine trial phase)@2 , (…)@50% at least 2 of the group's items, or half of them Operators are uppercase; lowercase and , or and not are ordinary words (and stopwords). Parentheses and NOT s nest at most 100 deep. Words and phrases are analysed like the field's documents, by its analyzer : a word that analyses to several terms ( e-mail ) becomes a phrase, and one that analyses to none (a stopword) is dropped. A prefix is lowercased and folded but not stemmed, and matches the stemmed terms that start with it. A weight ( vaccine^2 , \"messenger rna\"^0.5 ) is read and ignored, since MATCH only says whether a row matches: vaccine^2 mrna matches what vaccine mrna does, so one text can go to both MATCH and bm25 . A weight of 0 is read and ignored too, while bm25 scores nothing for that word. A ^ before something that starts like a number but is not a weight ( vaccine^1e308 , vaccine^-1 , vaccine^2x ), and a ~ with more than 2 typos ( apple~9 ), are errors in MATCH , where they would otherwise be searched as words; bm25 reads its text leniently, and there they are words. A prefix filters; it does not score. bm25 scores a text's whole words and passes over its prefixes ( bm25(name, 'leather sof*') scores leather alone), so a query ranked by bm25(name, 'sof*') , which has no whole word, returns no row, even where MATCH(name, 'sof*') keeps many, and neither does a ranking expression with it in ( bm25(name, 'sof*') + rating ). For search as you type, filter by what was typed with its last word as a prefix and rank by the words typed whole, or, while the first word is still being typed, by a column: A bm25 takes its MATCH 's fuzzy only when its text is the MATCH 's ( typos ), so here it names its own. A KEYWORD field of whole values (a product's name, a past query) completes with PREFIX instead, ordered by a column. A group with @n matches a row with at least n of its items: its operands, whether juxtaposed, joined by AND or by OR , each a word, a phrase, a prefix, a typo-tolerant word or a parenthesised query ( vaccine AND (mrna \"messenger rna\" trial~ (phase OR stage))@2 ). A negated item ( -animal ) is not counted: the row must not have it. An item written twice is one item, as in bm25 's min ( (apple apple cherry)@2 needs both words), and so are words that analyse to the same term. A stopword is not an item, and a word's synonyms and typo neighbours count as it does. @p% asks for p percent of the items, rounded down; a count or percentage of 0 is an error, and either is clamped to between 1 and the number of items, as Lucene's minimum_should_match is, so @1 is any of them and a count past the end is all of them. MATCH(body, 'a b c d', min => 2) (or min => '75%' ) treats the whole query as one group. A group nests anywhere a parenthesised query does. A group reads each item's matches once, so it costs about what an OR of its items does."],
["sql.html#prefixes-and-autocomplete","ChloeDB's query language","Prefixes and autocomplete","kw LIKE 'p%' and PREFIX(kw, 'p') select the rows of a KEYWORD field whose value starts with p , byte for byte (no case folding). A field's values are sorted in its dictionary, so the values with a prefix are one run of it, found by two binary searches per segment: a short run unions its values' lists, and one of more than 1,024 values of a single-valued field scans the code column, as a range of numbers does. A multi-valued field matches a row when one of its values starts with the prefix, and NOT when none does. LIKE on a table's own KEYWORD field reads its index for a value (no wildcard: = ) or text followed by % . Another pattern ( _ anywhere, or % before the end) and LIKE on a TEXT field test each row the other conditions keep, as over joined or derived rows. A field with a substring index answers every pattern from it. ESCAPE 'c' makes the character after c literal: LIKE 'snake!_%' ESCAPE '!' is the prefix snake_ . PREFIX needs no escaping. ILIKE tests each row the other conditions keep. To find values in any case among many rows by an index, give the field a substring index , or store the value lowercased (and a display form beside it) and use LIKE . PREFIX(x, p) also works over joined or derived rows, as a test of each value; PREFIX(kw, '') and LIKE '%' are IS NOT NULL . PREFIX(kw, p, fuzzy => 1 | 2 | 'auto') also selects the values that start with a prefix within that many edits of p whose first letter is p 's own, with the edits MATCH counts ( typos ); 'auto' goes by p 's length, as it does for a word: none up to 2 letters, 1 up to 5, else 2. The dictionary is walked as a trie, one row of the edit-distance table per letter, jumping past every value whose start is already too far from p and past the whole run of a start close enough, so the cost follows the values near what was typed rather than the dictionary's size. The values found are a few runs of the dictionary, read as an exact prefix's one is. A value's edits are the fewest of any of its prefixes, so a completion of p itself has none even when a shorter prefix is also close enough. Typo matches rank by the ORDER BY like exact ones; to rank completions by their edits, exact ones first and each tier by its count, put the tiers in the ranking expression: ORDER BY CASE WHEN PREFIX(id, :typed) THEN 2 WHEN PREFIX(id, :typed, fuzzy => 1) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END * 1000000 + n DESC (the factor larger than any count). LIKE takes no typos. Counts kept with UPDATE ... SET n = n + 1 or a write's add change in place, so a query log's popularity stays current without rewriting rows. A plan with a prefix needs the plan feature prefix , and one with typos also prefix_edits , so a shard of an older version refuses it rather than misreading it. The same run of the dictionary answers semantic-ID next codes . Cluster a completion table by its key. ORDER BY n DESC LIMIT 10 reads the count column's blocks best first ( ordering by a column ), and with OPTIONS(cluster_by = 'id') a prefix's rows sit together in each segment, so the ten most asked queries under a prefix read about ten blocks whatever the prefix's length: Over ORCAS's 10.4 million queries, loaded in no particular order and merged by the default policy (11 segments), 2,000 typed queries drawn by clicks, one thread on a 4-core cloud VM (Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz), p99 in ms: characters typed 1 2 3 4 6 8 12 rows matching (median) 560,354 64,887 9,461 2,418 415 68 6 every match read 46.7 25.4 18.2 17.1 9.9 9.1 2.6 blocks best first 9.5 13.0 8.8 9.7 6.3 6.5 2.6 blocks, clustered by id 0.27 0.53 0.48 0.38 0.35 0.33 0.28 The full runs, with medians and the 105 segments straight after the load, are results/text/complete-orcas.json and complete-orcas-clustered.json ( chloedb-complete-bench ). Typos cost the walk of each segment's dictionary, which finds each value's fewest edits. On the same clustered table, the median top 10 with fuzzy => 'auto' takes 1.9 ms at 3 letters and 2.7 ms at 4, where it allows one edit, and 8.2 to 11.9 ms from 6 letters, where it allows two (p99 up to 24 ms); with fuzzy => 1 it takes 3.3 to 4.1 ms from 6 letters (p99 up to 8.5 ms). Up to 2 letters 'auto' allows none, so it costs what an exact prefix does. Straight after a load, with 105 segments, the same queries take 2.7 to 3.9 times as long. Ranked exact completions first (the CASE above), the median is 5.1 to 15 ms from 2 letters and 28 ms at 1 (p99 up to 62 ms), since a ranking expression is not a column whose blocks can be read best first: every completion is ranked. Results: complete-orcas-clustered-fuzzy.json , complete-orcas-clustered-fuzzy1.json and complete-orcas-clustered-fuzzy-exact-first.json (one thread of the 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz)."],
["sql.html#substrings-like-and-ilike-anywhere-in-a-value","ChloeDB's query language","Substrings: LIKE and ILIKE anywhere in a value","A TEXT or KEYWORD column declared with OPTIONS(substring = true) gets a substring index, and LIKE and ILIKE on it are answered from that index, whatever the pattern. It finds what MATCH cannot, because it has no tokenizer: part of a word, an IP address, a path, an identifier, punctuation, text in a script the analyzer does not split. The answer is the one a test of every row gives. % is any run of characters and _ one character; LIKE compares byte for byte, and ILIKE compares both sides lowercased a character at a time, as PostgreSQL does and as it does over joined or derived rows : a capital Σ matches σ and the final ς wherever it stands, and a capital İ is one i since 0.3.4 . A row without a value matches neither a pattern nor its NOT , and a NULL pattern ( LIKE :p with :p NULL) matches no row, nor does its NOT since 0.3.4 . The index only changes how long the answer takes. How it answers. Each segment holds an FM-index of the field's values: the Burrows-Wheeler transform of their bytes in a wavelet tree, with a sample of the suffix array that says which row a match is in. Counting the places a string occurs takes time by the string's length, not by the table's size, and each row found then costs one to a few microseconds. A pattern with one string ( '%lit%' , 'lit%' , '%lit' , 'lit' ) is answered from the index alone, under LIKE and ILIKE since 0.3.8 (unreleased) : ILIKE searches the spellings that exist ( error , Error , ERROR ), not every combination of cases. A pattern with several strings ( '%a%b%' ) or with _ takes its candidates from the string that occurs least and tests their stored values, read in the order the table keeps them. A LIMIT without an ORDER BY on such a column stops at the first rows it needs since 0.3.8 (unreleased) . A pattern in too many rows reads the values instead. Where a string occurs in more than about one row for every 2 KiB of a segment's stored rows (4 KiB where each candidate's value is tested), listing its rows from the index would cost more than testing each value, so the query tests the values, as a table without the index does. The rows count whole, since a test reads them whole: a title that is a sixth of its row is listed up to six times as often before the values are read. Beside conditions that leave few rows ( pid = 24200 AND body LIKE '%e%' ), the pattern is tested on those rows' values alone, whichever of the two the index's counts say is cheaper. What the column must be : one value per row and stored (the stored values answer for rows not yet in a segment's index, and for the candidates above). It need not be searched by MATCH : body TEXT OPTIONS(substring = true, indexed = false) keeps only the substring index. ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN takes the option. In JSON it is \"substring\": true . A KEYWORD column's value or prefix ( LIKE 'abc' , LIKE 'abc%' ) still reads its dictionary, which is faster, and takes ESCAPE . A pattern the substring index answers does not take ESCAPE . Rows just written are tested by their values until a flush writes their segment; a segment gets its index when it is flushed or merged. What it costs. On three logs of 0.7 to 4.7 million lines the index took 0.94 to 1.02 of the bytes of the text it indexes, which made the file 1.8 to 2.3 times as large; loading took 1.3 to 2.6 times as long and a merge 2.2 to 3.6 times, since each segment's index is built when it is flushed and again when it is merged. A pattern in few rows took 0.02 to 2 ms where a test of every row took 0.6 to 4 s, and a pattern in very many rows a sixth to an eighth of that test ( results/substring , on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz). It makes the database file format 3 with the feature substring:1 , which a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses by name. The format is specified in chloedb/notes/substring.md ."],
["sql.html#scores","ChloeDB's query language","Scores","Each function below takes the options its section lists, as name => value . An option it does not take, or a value it cannot ( k1 => 'x' , b => 2 , ef => 'x' ), is an error that lists the options it takes: unknown option bogus for bm25: k1, b, algo, ... . Changed: such an option was ignored before, so a misspelt one ( fuzy => 1 ) gave an answer as if it had not been written."],
["sql.html#bm25field-text","ChloeDB's query language","bm25(field, text)","BM25 of the analysed terms of text , disjunctively: a row scores if it contains any of them, unless the text requires or excludes words . Ranked queries drop English function words (NLTK's 153-word list), except that its single letters ( d , m , o , s , t , y ) are kept unless they end a contraction: \"vitamin d\" and \"T cell\" keep their letter, \"don't\" and \"it's\" drop it. A term written twice weighs twice. Used in ORDER BY without a MATCH , it is a ranked search; with one, it ranks the matching rows. option default effect k1 , b 1.2, 0.75 BM25 parameters ( k1 >= 0 , 0 <= b <= 1 ) algo planner's choice (MaxScore) 'maxscore' , 'bmw' (Block-Max WAND) or 'exhaustive' stopwords 1 (0 on a simple field) 0 keeps English function words in the query, 1 drops them fuzzy 0 1 , 2 or 'auto' : also score the indexed terms that many edits from each word; 'auto' corrects only the words that look like typos ( typos ) synonyms 0 1 : also score each word's synonyms syntax 'query' 'plain' reads + , - , quotes and ^ as punctuation: the text is words alone; 'terms' reads analysed terms, each weighted by ^w , as they are written ( query expansion ) min none n or 'p%' : rank only the rows with at least that many of the text's optional words and phrases expand 0 n : also rank by the terms the query's top n rows hold most ( query expansion ), with expand_terms (20) and expand_weight (0.85) Keeping function words is better for short questions: on Quora it gains 0.055 nDCG@10, while on TREC-COVID, whose queries are full questions, dropping them gains the same amount. Weights, required and excluded words The text may weigh a word, require it or exclude it, as a search box does: text ranks vaccine mrna the rows with either word vaccine^2 mrna the same rows, vaccine weighing twice: as vaccine vaccine mrna +mrna vaccine the rows with mrna , by both words vaccine -animal the rows with vaccine and without animal +\"messenger rna\" vaccine^0.5 the rows with the phrase (with both words, on a field without positions), by its words and half of vaccine +covid^0 treatment the rows with covid , by treatment alone vacc* trial the rows with a word that begins vacc , or with trial vacine~ trial , vacine~1 the rows with a word an edit or two from vacine , or with trial ^w after a word or a closing quote multiplies its terms' BM25 by w , a number of at least 0 ( 2 , 0.5 , .5 ). Weights add up, as a word written twice does. A word weighing 0 scores nothing, and a text whose words all weigh 0 ranks no row. + before a word or a quoted phrase requires it, - excludes it: a row must contain every required one and none of the excluded ones, and ranks by the others too. An excluded word does not score, and a word the query drops as a stopword is neither required nor excluded. A sign counts only at the start of a word and before a letter, digit or quote: covid-19 , a - alone, +-x and x^ are words. Quotes alone change nothing: \"messenger rna\" scores what messenger rna does. word* ranks by the words of the field that begin with word , as MATCH 's prefix matches them (lowercased and folded, not stemmed), scored as one word whose document frequency is the largest of theirs, as a typo's neighbours are. When more than 128 words begin so, the 128 in the most rows score, and the plan says so: bm25 vacc*: the 128 terms in the most rows, of the 412 it begins . word~ and word~1 , word~2 rank by the word and the words within that many edits, as fuzzy does for one word ( ~ alone goes by its length, as 'auto' does). Both take signs and weights ( +vacc*^2 ), and with syntax => 'plain' both are words as written. So ORDER BY bm25(title, :typed) DESC with :typed = 'sof*' ranks the rows MATCH(title, 'sof*') keeps, for search as you type. Changed: bm25 read vacc* and vacine~ as the words vacc and vacine before, which ranked no row where MATCH found some. With fuzzy or synonyms , a word's typo neighbours and synonyms take the highest weight the word is written with (writing it twice weighs the word twice, not them), and a required or excluded word counts as present when one of them is, as in MATCH . A required or excluded word is a filter on the ranked rows: the top-k loop (all three algo s), a rank-safe boost , a rerank phase (where a row the filter drops scores 0, as one without the words does) and shards all apply it, so the rows and scores are those of the rows the text allows. Weights and signs cost little. With each query's first word weighed 2 ( word^2 ) or required ( +word ), or its last word excluded ( -word ), the median query took 0.27, 0.28 and 0.23 ms on Quora's 10,000 queries against 0.25 ms for the plain text, and 0.24, 0.18 and 0.22 ms on FiQA against 0.22 ms; a required word can make a query faster, as the filter drops rows before they are scored. Text written without operators ranks and times as the plain text does on all five collections measured ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_term_operators.json , one core of a 4-core cloud VM while another job held the other three). The operators were put on words mechanically to time them, so these queries rank worse than the plain text: an operator helps when it says what the person searching means. min => n (or 'p%' ) ranks only the rows with at least n of the text's optional words and phrases: those without a sign, each counted once however often it is written, whatever its weight ( ^0 counts too). Required and excluded words are not counted; a text without optional words ignores min . Counts are clamped to between 1 and the number of optional words, and a percentage rounds down, as in MATCH 's groups: bm25(body, 'vaccine mrna trial phase', min => 2) ranks the rows with two of the four words, by all of them. With syntax => 'plain' , each word is an optional word. min filters the ranked rows, as a required word does, so its cost depends on how many rows hold one of the words. With min => 2 the median query took 0.24 ms on Quora's 10,000 queries (0.25 ms plain) and 0.41 ms on FiQA (0.23 ms plain). On TREC-COVID, whose query words appear in most of its documents, it took 1.6 ms (0.5 ms plain), because the filter counts every row that holds one of the words ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_min_match.json , one core of a 4-core cloud VM while another job held the other three). min is for leaving rows out, not for ranking better. Asking every BEIR query for two of its words, or half of them, left nDCG@10 unchanged on TREC-COVID and lowered it elsewhere, by as much as 0.077 (SciFact, half the words). syntax => 'plain' reads the text as words alone, as bm25 did before: for text not written for a search box, such as a user's message passed through, or a benchmark's recorded queries. BEIR's hold +1 , -word or x^2 here and there, so the evaluations and benchmarks in this repository pass syntax => 'plain' , and so does ChloeCtx. A plan that ranks with weights, signs, prefixes or typos ( word* , word~ ; a shard from before them, which takes text_operators , reads them as words, so finish an upgrade before relying on them), or a MATCH with weights, needs the plan feature text_operators , and one with min or a MATCH group (…)@n needs min_match . From Python, bm25(\"body\", \"+mrna vaccine^2\") reads them, bm25(\"body\", text, syntax=\"plain\") does not, and bm25(\"body\", text, min=2) and match(\"body\", text, min=\"75%\") ask for some of the words. Changed: bm25 read + , - , \" and ^ as punctuation before; text with them now ranks as above, and syntax => 'plain' keeps the old reading. In MATCH , ^ separated words: vaccine^2 matched vaccine and 2 , and now matches vaccine ; and )@2 closes a group a row needs 2 of, where @2 was a word before."],
["sql.html#similarityfield-vector-and-distancefield-vector","ChloeDB's query language","similarity(field, vector) and distance(field, vector)","The dot product (cosine for unit vectors) and the Euclidean (L2) distance between a row's vector and the query vector, which is a bound parameter or a JSON array of the field's dimension. Text instead of a vector. On a field a model embeds ( OPTIONS(embed = 'body', model = '...') ), the second argument may be text, a literal or a parameter that holds text: ORDER BY similarity(emb, 'how do I reset my password') DESC , or similarity(emb, :q) with :q bound to a string. The text is embedded as a query by the field's own model when the query is planned, once however often the query repeats it, and the query runs as if its vector had been given ( distance and maxsim take text the same way; on a multi-vector field the text is its tokens' vectors). similarity(emb, embed(:q)) says the same. In a rank profile too, on chloedb-server: one text parameter feeds both bm25(title, :q) and similarity(emb, :q) (the profile written out, its texts embedded, the plan still naming the profile). A string that is a JSON array of numbers is still a vector, and a SPARSE field's terms are still text. embed('model', text) is the query vector of a text by the model named, wherever a vector goes: against a field of no model, similarity(raw, embed('potion-base-8M', :q)) , or in a projection, SELECT id, embed('potion-base-8M', 'tents') AS v FROM docs . The model is one an embedded field uses, hash:N , or a directory directly under the models directory, named as llm() names one (letters, digits, _ , - and . , never a path; chloedb-server fetch-model fetches the known ones). A model behind an API ( openai/... , an endpoint.json ) is taken only when a field uses it, so that a query spends no key a table was not given. The text is a value or a parameter, not a column: the model runs once a query, not once a row, and a column is embedded when rows are written, by declaring its vector field OPTIONS(embed = 'column', model = '...') . The text is embedded where the query is planned: by the database from Rust, Python and JavaScript, and by the server (a coordinator for its shards) on the cores it embeds writes with, as a parameter's {\"embed\": \"text\"} is. With a static model (Model2Vec, such as potion-retrieval-32M ) that takes tens of microseconds; the median query on SciFact, its text embedded and every row measured, took 0.45 ms ( results ). similarity is the raw dot product of the vectors as written: ChloeDB does not normalise the vectors a write supplies (the models it runs itself write unit vectors), so on vectors of other lengths long vectors rank first. For cosine similarity, write unit vectors. ORDER BY distance(emb, :v) ASC ranks by $\\lVert v - q \\rVert$, nearest first, and score() is minus the distance (ranking is always best first). On a field a model embeds the vectors are unit length, where distance orders rows as the dot product does, so the index searches them ( strategy => 'flat' measures every row); on any other field every row WHERE keeps is measured exactly, a scan of the field's float32 vectors, since its index ranks by the dot product. Changed: distance() ranked like similarity(...) DESC before, which is the nearest row only for unit vectors, and its score() was the similarity; a shard from before this change still ranks it so. In an expression, distance() stays refused: use similarity() . option effect strategy 'auto' (default: the planner decides, by its cost model, which sizes the work by the vectors' dimensions: an unfiltered search scans every vector exactly where that costs no more than the index, as in a segment of 20,000 vectors of 64 dimensions), 'prefilter' (evaluate the filter, scan its members exactly), 'index' (search HNSW or IVF with the filter inside), 'postfilter' (search with a larger k and drop non-matching rows), 'flat' (exact scan), 'partition' (walk the graphs of the partitions the filter can match) ef HNSW search breadth (default 200; 64 before). On random unit vectors of 64 dimensions, the hard case for a graph (real embeddings cluster, and need less), the index found 0.73 of the true top 10 at ef 64 and 0.96 at 200 over 20,000 rows (0.13 and 0.24 ms at the median), and 0.40 and 0.71 over 200,000 (0.24 and 0.35 ms); the default search of the 20,000, now an exact scan, finds all of them in 0.15 ms (200 queries, cargo test --test vector_defaults -- --ignored , one run on a 4-core Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz shared with other builds) nprobe IVF clusters probed rerank how many candidates are re-scored in float32 Vector partitions A filter that keeps a fifth of the rows makes an HNSW search either walk its graph testing every row it reaches, or fetch five times the candidates and drop the rest. A field partitioned by the column the filter names does neither: each segment keeps, besides the field's graph of every row, a graph per partition of the rows, and a search whose WHERE limits that column walks only the graphs of the partitions that can match. partition_bounds splits an INT or FLOAT column into ranges: here below 1, 1 to 2, 2 to 3, 3 to 4, and 4 and up (1 to 63 increasing numbers). A KEYWORD column is partitioned by value and takes no bounds ( partition_by = 'category' ): each segment builds a graph for each of its 64 most frequent values. The column must be stored, indexed and single-valued, and the field an HNSW field of one vector a row. Rows without a value in the column are only in the field's graph. The planner reads the first condition on the column among the filter's top-level AND s: a range ( >= , < , BETWEEN ), = or IN . A partition whose rows all pass it is walked with no test at all, so rating >= 4 costs what an unfiltered search of a fifth of the rows costs; a partition that only partly matches, or a filter with other conditions, tests the rest on each row the walk reaches. The planner uses partitions when its cost model predicts them faster than its other plans, and strategy => 'partition' uses them wherever they apply. A search without a condition on the column uses the field's graph, as before. On 1M MS MARCO passages (768-dimension vectors, one segment, the 4-core cloud container), the engine comparison's rating >= 4 (20% of rows) took 176–184 µs with partitions against 364–438 µs, at recall@10 0.963 against 0.961; rating >= 4.5 (10%) 383 µs against 674–777 µs. At 2% ( rating >= 4.9 ) the partitions took 1.1 ms where the planner's exact scan of the matching rows takes 1.6–2.2 ms, finding 0.995 of the exact answers instead of 0.999 ( chloedb/results/vector/partitions-msmarco-1000000x768.json ). The partitions' graphs take as much space as the field's graph again (128 bytes a row at m = 16 , 3% of that table's file) but share its vectors and codes. Building that table took 320 s instead of 179 s, and merges build the partitions again from scratch. A database with partitions is written in file format 3 with the feature vector_partition , which versions of ChloeDB from before them refuse by name. A VECTOR field keeps, besides its float32 rows, a compact code per row that the search scans: 8 bits per dimension by default, or 1 bit per dimension with \"codes\": \"binary\" in the field's schema (or the build option vector.codes = 'binary' ). Binary codes are 32 times smaller than the vectors and 8 times smaller than 8-bit codes; to make up for the coarser scores, the search re-scores 8 times as many candidates in float32. On synthetic 768-dimensional data that kept recall@10 at 0.985–0.994 with flat, IVF and HNSW search, at an eighth of the memory ( results ); check recall on your own embeddings before choosing them, since some models lose more to one-bit codes than others. RaBitQ codes ( \"codes\": \"rabitq1\" , \"rabitq2\" or \"rabitq4\" , or codes = 'rabitq4' in OPTIONS ) keep 1, 2 or 4 bits per dimension of the vector after a fixed random rotation, and two numbers per row that give each estimated score an error bound. The search keeps 8, 4 or 2 times as many candidates, and skips the float32 row of any candidate whose bound shows it cannot reach the top results found so far. On FiQA's Cohere embeddings, at recall@10 1.000 with a flat scan, 1-bit RaBitQ codes read 91 float32 rows a query where binary codes read 320, 2-bit codes 28 and 4-bit codes 13, against SQ8's 40 ( results ). Their scan is slower per row than binary or SQ8 codes of the same field: 4-bit codes take half the memory of SQ8 and scan about 1.5 times slower. They pay off when reading the float32 rows is the cost, as with a table larger than memory. A database with RaBitQ codes is written in file format 3 with the feature rabitq , which versions of ChloeDB from before them refuse by name."],
["sql.html#similarityfield-weights-on-a-sparse-field","ChloeDB's query language","similarity(field, weights) on a SPARSE field","The dot product of a row's term weights with the query's, Σ q_t · w_t over the terms both have. The query is a JSON object of term weights, bound as a parameter or written as a string: It runs on the same machinery as bm25 (MaxScore, Block-Max WAND, the filter strategies), so algo => 'maxscore' | 'bmw' | 'exhaustive' applies, and it fuses with other scores in rrf , weighted sums and products. distance is not defined on a SPARSE field. A SPARSE field embedded by a learned sparse model ( sp SPARSE OPTIONS(embed = 'body', model = '...') ) gets its rows' weights from the model; the query's are the parameter {\"embed\": \"some words\", \"field\": \"sp\"} (from Rust, Db::embed_query_in ), weighed by the same model."],
["sql.html#maxsimfield-vectors-several-query-vectors","ChloeDB's query language","maxsim(field, vectors) : several query vectors","The best similarity of a row's vector with any of several query vectors, $\\max_i q_i \\cdot v$: a row near any one of a user's recent items, or of a query's several phrasings. The vectors are a bound list of vectors, a tuple of vector parameters, a JSON array of arrays, or a subquery that returns one column of vectors, which runs first: It runs one search per query vector, with the filter and the options of similarity ( strategy , ef , nprobe , rerank ), and scores the union of their top k exactly against every query vector. The union holds the exact top k : a row among them is among the top k of the query vector it is closest to. Each added query vector costs one more search ( what that costs ). A row without a vector scores NULL , a subquery with no rows ranks nothing, and more than 64 vectors is an error unless max_vectors => n raises the limit. It is a score wherever similarity is one: in rrf , minmax() sums and ranking expressions, on one database or over shards. On a multi-vector field ( VECTOR(d)[] ), maxsim is late interaction as ColBERT and ColPali score it: the sum over the query vectors of each one's best match among the row's vectors, $\\sum_i \\max_j q_i \\cdot v_j$. Each query vector's search returns its best depth vectors (64), which name candidate rows; the candidates rows (200, at least the LIMIT ) with the largest sum of the matches found are scored over all their vectors, with their 8-bit codes, or float32 when the field keeps them. A row without vectors ranks nowhere. depth => n and candidates => n trade time for recall; with both at least the number of vectors, the answer is exact. similarity refuses a multi-vector field, and so do graph functions. A multi-vector field can also be the vectors of a text's tokens, which a model of late interaction (ColBERT) computes when rows are written: tok VECTOR(96)[] OPTIONS(embed = 'body', model = 'answerai-colbert-small-v1') ( a vector a token ). A query gives the vectors of its own tokens as the parameter ( {\"embed\": \"the text\", \"field\": \"tok\"} on the server, Db::embed_query_in from Rust). Its usual place is a rerank phase over BM25's best rows, which scores each of them over all its vectors: On BEIR's SciFact and NFCorpus, answerai-colbert-small-v1 over BM25's top 20 scores nDCG@10 0.738 and 0.354 (BM25 alone 0.691 and 0.329) in 80–90 ms a query on four cores, most of it encoding the query; that ranks better than the cross-encoder below, in a fifth of its time. The cost is at write: 0.1–0.16 s a document to embed, and a database about 29 times BM25's (half that with pool_factor = 2 , which scores the same). The results have the details. dot(a, b) is the dot product of two vectors as a value, for rows in memory: SELECT id, dot(emb, :q) FROM h over a WITH result, a join, or a table's own rows. It equals similarity to the bit, and in a ranking expression over a table ( ORDER BY bm25(title, :q) + 2 * coalesce(dot(emb, :q), 0) DESC ) it is similarity(emb, :q) : NULL for a row without a vector."],
["sql.html#rrfs1-s2--reciprocal-rank-fusion","ChloeDB's query language","rrf(s1, s2, …) : reciprocal rank fusion","Each input ranks the rows that pass WHERE , to a depth of 100 by default, and a row's fused score is $$ \\mathrm{rrf}(d) = \\sum_{i} \\frac{1}{k + \\mathrm{rank}_i(d)}, \\qquad k = 60 $$ Options: k => 60 , depth => 100 , and weights => [w1, w2, …] , one number of at least 0 per input, which multiplies that input's terms (weighted RRF): $$ \\mathrm{rrf}(d) = \\sum_{i} \\frac{w_i}{k + \\mathrm{rank}_i(d)} $$ rrf(bm25(body, :q), similarity(emb, :v), weights => [0.3, 0.7]) leans on the vectors; the weights are a list ( [0.3, 0.7] , ARRAY[0.3, 0.7] ) or a parameter holding one, and all of them 1 is plain rrf . A list of another length, or of weights all 0, is an error. The depth is raised to the page's LIMIT when it asks for more rows, as in weighted sums and products, but not by its OFFSET : pages of one size ( LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0, 20, 40 ... ) rank the same candidates, so they are the pages of one ranking, with no row on two pages and none skipped. A page past the candidates comes back short, with truncated: true and a plan line that says so; to page deeper, give the ranking a larger depth => , the same on every page. Changed: the depth was raised to LIMIT + OFFSET before, so each page ranked other candidates, and paging repeated and skipped rows."],
["sql.html#rankedkeys-a-ranking-from-elsewhere","ChloeDB's query language","ranked(keys) : a ranking from elsewhere","ranked(:keys) is a ranking computed outside ChloeDB, given as primary keys, best first: a recommender's candidates, a reranker's order, or the rows a semantic-ID decoder generated. It is a score like any other, so it fuses with ChloeDB's own rankings: A listed row scores -(its position) , from -1 for the first key; a key listed twice ranks where it is first. A row the list leaves out has NULL , so in an expression it ranks last unless coalesce gives it a value, and in a weighted sum it takes the input's worst value, as a missing column does. Times another score ( ranked(:keys) * rating ) it multiplies as arithmetic, not as a product of scores, which would count its negative values as 0. A key no live row has is skipped. On its own, ORDER BY ranked(:keys) DESC answers the listed rows that WHERE keeps, in list order. The keys are a parameter holding a list (an array of keys in the HTTP API), a tuple ( ranked(('a', 'b')) ) or an array ( ranked(ARRAY['a', 'b']) ). Over shards, each shard scores its own rows from the list and the coordinator merges them, so no extra round is needed. A plan that uses it needs the plan feature ranked ."],
["sql.html#weighted-sums-tmm-minmax-and-zscore","ChloeDB's query language","Weighted sums: tmm() , minmax() and zscore()","A weighted sum of normalised scores is weighted (convex) fusion: 0.2 * tmm(bm25(body, :t)) + 0.8 * tmm(similarity(emb, :q)) . Weights are numeric constants; + and - combine any number of terms. Candidates are the union of each input's top 100, re-scored exactly by every input and normalised over that union ($C$). Three normalisers put a score on one scale: normaliser a row's value a row without a value tmm(x) , theoretical min-max $\\dfrac{x - lo}{\\max_C x - lo}$, lo the least value x can take 0 minmax(x) $\\dfrac{x - \\min_C x}{\\max_C x - \\min_C x}$, 1 where all are equal 0 zscore(x) $\\dfrac{x - \\mathrm{mean}_C x}{\\mathrm{sd}_C x}$, 0 where all are equal the least of the candidates' Use tmm unless your own judged queries say otherwise. Its lower end is fixed by what the score is, not by the candidates: a row's value depends only on the best candidate, which is the same at any depth, while minmax moves every row whenever the weakest candidate changes, which it does with depth and with what each input happens to return. Bruch, Gai and Ingber ( TOIS 2023 ) find this convex combination beats RRF in and out of domain once its one weight is tuned, and that a few dozen judged queries tune it; the relevance lab tunes it on yours ( what it gives on BEIR ). lo is the score's own: 0 for bm25 , a SPARSE similarity (weights at least 0, as learned models give them), rrf , ppr and decay ; -1 for similarity and a one-vector maxsim , the least cosine of unit vectors (the vectors of an embedded field, and of most models; for vectors of other lengths, give lo ); -n for maxsim of n vectors on a multi-vector field. Anything else, a column or an expression, needs it written: tmm(rating, lo => 0) . With hi too, tmm(x, lo => a, hi => b) is $(x - a) / (b - a)$, each row's own value (0 without one), fixed bounds that read nothing of the other candidates. Options, given on any input: depth => 100 (candidates per input), exact => 1 (score every row that passes WHERE instead of the top-100 union). The top-100 shortcut recovers 89–95% of the exact fused top 10 ( results ). A row without a value of a column input ( 0.7 * minmax(bm25(body, :t)) + 0.3 * minmax(rating) for a row whose rating is NULL ) takes that input's worst value among the candidates: its lowest when the weight is positive and its highest when the weight is negative (0 and 1 after min-max), so a missing value never lifts a row. Under tmm and zscore it takes the value in the table above. A plan with tmm or zscore needs the plan feature score_norms , which a version of ChloeDB from before them refuses by name. Changed: a sum of bare scores, 0.2 * bm25(body, :t) + 0.8 * similarity(emb, :q) , was this fusion before ranking expressions; + now adds raw values, as in any expression below. Write minmax() around each term for the fusion. For one release, EXPLAIN and a query's plan lines add a note when an expression adds raw relevance scores. norm => 'none' , which kept raw scores in a sum, is accepted and changes nothing."],
["sql.html#ranking-expressions","ChloeDB's query language","Ranking expressions","ORDER BY takes any expression of scores, numeric columns and numbers, ranked best first ( DESC ; negate it to rank the smallest first): part meaning + - * / % , unary - arithmetic on raw values bm25 , similarity , ppr , decay , rrf , ranked , a numeric column, hops() , seq_no() the leaves: a row's value of each ln(x) , log(x) (base 10), log(b, x) , exp(x) , sqrt(x) , pow(x, y) , abs(x) , round(x[, digits]) , sigmoid(x) , clamp(x, lo, hi) numeric functions least(…) , greatest(…) , coalesce(…) over their arguments that are not NULL tmm(x[, lo => a][, hi => b]) x from its least possible value to the best row's, put on [0, 1]: 0 where x is NULL ( above ) minmax(x) x over the rows ranked, put on [0, 1]: 1 where all are equal, 0 where x is NULL zscore(x) x less the rows' mean, over their deviation; NULL takes the least of the rows' rank(x) 1 plus the number of rows ranked with a greater x ; NULL places after every other CASE WHEN c THEN x … ELSE y END the first branch whose condition holds (any WHERE condition), else y , else NULL count_true(c, …) how many of the conditions hold ( AT_LEAST ) NULL is a missing value (a NULL column, a row without a vector, one a traversal does not reach) or a result that is not a finite number ( ln(0) , a division by 0, an overflow). Arithmetic and functions of NULL are NULL , except coalesce , least and greatest , which skip it. A row whose score is NULL ranks after every other, and its score() is NULL . Candidates are the top depth (100) rows of each relevance leaf ( bm25 , similarity , a graph's, ranked ), and of every input of rrf ; an expression with no such leaf ( rating * 2 + year / 1000 ), or exact => 1 on any leaf, ranks every row that passes WHERE . A rank-safe boost ranks every row its text score matches. Each leaf scores every candidate exactly, and tmm() , minmax() , zscore() and rank() are taken over the candidates. Over shards, each shard returns its candidates' leaf values and the coordinator runs the expression, so a split collection ranks like the whole one. Rank-safe boosts An expression with one text score ( bm25 , or similarity on a SPARSE field) whose other parts are numeric columns, decay , CASE conditions, numbers and seq_no() finds its exact top rows: the rows that scoring every row the text matches would rank first, not only the best of the text's top 100. Set functions ( tmm() , minmax() , zscore() , rank() ), rrf and exact => 1 take the candidates above instead. The text score's top-k loop (MaxScore, Block-Max WAND or exhaustive, as algo says) ranks by the whole expression. From the k-th best value so far and each column's smallest and largest value, it works out the lowest text score a row needs, and skips the rows below it. A row the text does not match is not a candidate. When the expression does not rise with the text score ( rating - bm25(body, :t) ), no text score can be ruled out: the loop scores every matching row, and the plan line says every match scored . Over shards, each shard returns its own exact top rows: its top LIMIT + OFFSET over all its segments, and the rows tied with the last of them. The cluster's top rows are each in their own shard's, so a split collection ranks like the whole one. A shard also returns the text score's top depth rows, which a coordinator from before rank-safe boosts fused from, only to a coordinator older than shard protocol 10 ( gRPC ). A plan that ranks by an expression needs the plan feature rank_expressions ; a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the plan by that name. Weighted sums of minmax() , products of scores and rrf are written as before, so older shards still run them. Rerank phases A ranking can go on in phases, as a search engine's rank profile does: a cheap first phase over every candidate, then a costlier one over its top rows. SQL writes a phase as a WITH that ranks a table and cuts it, ranked again by the next WITH or the query itself: The planner makes one search of them, with phases. Each phase ranks the rows of the one before by an expression, best first, and may read any of the table's columns, since the rows are the table's. Every row a phase reads goes on: one the expression gives no value ranks last with a NULL score (and a row a bm25 phase's terms miss scores 0), and rows tied keep the order the phase before gave them. LIMIT and OFFSET on the query cut the last phase, and score() is the last phase's score. The first WITH may filter ( WHERE ) and rank as any query does; each later step reads the step before alone, with no WHERE or grouping, ranks by one expression DESC (which may hold correlated subqueries ), and each WITH keeps the table's columns ( * ) and has a LIMIT . WITH results before the first phase, a user's history say, are computed first, for the phases' subqueries to read. Other WITH queries plan as before: the table restricted to the rows of the WITH . A phase whose expression is row-local is a second phase: over shards, each shard scores it on its own top rows by the first phase. A phase with a set function ( tmm() , minmax() , zscore() , rank() ), rrf or a weighted sum reads every row it reranks, so it is a global phase: it runs once over the merged rows (on the coordinator over shards), and so does every phase after it. The coordinator keeps the top rows of the whole collection by the first phase before it ranks them by the next, so a split collection answers exactly as the whole one does. EXPLAIN shows each phase and which kind it is, and a query's plan lines say how many rows each reranked. The first phase's LIMIT counts against the rows a query may reach ( max_rows ), and the phases against its deadline. From Python, rerank(expr, count) adds a phase: t.select(\"id\", score()).order_by(bm25(\"body\", q)).rerank(col(\"rating\"), 100).limit(10) . A plan with phases needs the plan feature rank_phases . Correlated subqueries: a ranking that reads other rows A scalar subquery in a ranking may name a column of the row it ranks ( first.cat ). It is then correlated, and gives a number for each row: how often the user met the row's category, how long ago, how close the row is to what they bought. Where. The rows ranked must be a bounded set: a rerank phase , whose rows are the phase before's top rows; an ORDER BY over a table, or over a WITH result, of at most 10,000 rows (more is an error that suggests a phase); or the columns of a ranked query, alone or in an expression ( coalesce(price, 0) + (SELECT ...) ). A subquery naming the outer row in WHERE , IN or EXISTS is an error, but for an EXISTS that names it by one equality , a semi join. What it names. name.column , where name is the outer query's table or WITH result (or its alias) and the column one of its columns. The subquery reads a WITH result in memory, compared any way an expression over rows in memory can be, or a table through its indexes, whose conditions compare one of its columns with the outer row's ( e.cat = items.cat , e.price < items.price ) as they would a value. Its value is a number, a FLOAT ( COUNT , SUM , MIN , MAX , AVG of numbers, or a boolean as 0 or 1), so a correlated COUNT reads 10.0 where PostgreSQL gives the integer 10 . One of no rows is NULL ( COUNT gives 0), which ranks last unless coalesce d. A NULL of the outer row is compared as SQL compares it: e.price < first.price holds for no event when the row has no price. How it runs. First the rows ranked (the phases before, or the rows a bounded ORDER BY reads), with the columns the subqueries name. The parts of a subquery that do not read the outer row run once ( h above, and its rows where h.action = 'buy' ). A subquery whose outer columns meet only equalities ANDed into its WHERE ( h.cat = first.cat ) then runs once, grouped by them over the values the rows hold ( h.cat IN (...) ), and a value no group has takes the subquery's value over no rows; any other runs once for each distinct value of the columns it names. Then the ranking sorts the rows, with each row's values, and the plan lines say how much ran: correlated: 3 subqueries over 1000 rows, 597 values, 3 runs . Over shards a coordinator runs the steps before, and the subqueries over the shards' rows, and sends each row's values to the shards inside the plan, by key, so that every shard ranks with the same ones. A plan with them needs the plan feature correlated . Cost at 1,000 candidates, measured with chloedb-correlated-bench on a cloud VM (4-core Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, 16.9 GB, Linux 6.18): 100,000 items in 200 categories, 400,000 events of 2,000 users, a history of 50 events, one thread, 300 requests of each shape. BM25's top 1,000 reranked to 20 took 1.29 ms (mean) without subqueries, and reading the history added 1.67 ms. A subquery added, over the rerank alone (with the history's read): subquery values runs added, mean by category, over h 199 2 4.90 ms three by category, over h 596 6 8.14 ms by category, over the events table 199 2 4.09 ms by item, over the events table 992 2 21.6 ms by embedding ( dot(h.emb, first.emb) ), over h 992 992 10.5 ms A grouped run costs about what reading the candidates' values does. A subquery that runs once per value costs about 10 µs a run over rows in memory, and one grouped by a key reads an IN list as long as the candidates ( e.item IN (...) ), which is most of its cost. A second run gave the same numbers within 0.4 ms ( chloedb/results/correlated/ ). Features: the values a ranking read A ranking expression that scores each row by itself is a column, with the value the ranking computes for the row, and features() lists the inputs of the query's ranking, to log beside what users chose and train a model on (Vespa's match-features): features() stands for one column per input, each once, named by its SQL text: every leaf of the ranking (a score such as bm25(body, :t) or similarity(emb, :q) , a column, decay(..) , hops() ), the first phase's first, then each phase's expression that scores a row by itself and is not a leaf. With rerank phases it lists every phase's. An expression with a set function ( tmm() , minmax() , zscore() , rank() ), rrf or a weighted sum ranks rows against each other, so it is not a column: its inputs are, and score() is its value. A ranking by distance(..) lists the similarity(..) it ranks by. features() takes no alias and needs an ORDER BY . A selected expression may use anything a ranking expression does but the set functions: bm25 , similarity , decay , columns, numbers, arithmetic, ln and the other functions, CASE . Its value is the ranking's, bit for bit: a row a bm25 's terms miss scores 0, and one without a value is NULL . ORDER BY 2 ranks by the second column when it is one. Over shards a feature is one database's value: a coordinator sums the shards' BM25 statistics of the terms any column scores, and its fetch round computes the columns for the page alone, with those statistics. A plan with such a column needs the plan feature rank_features . From Python, features() goes in select , and so does any expression of scores ( select(\"id\", bm25(\"title\", q).alias(\"t\")) ); Python names a feature as it writes the expression. Rank profiles: a ranking kept with its table A ranking used by many queries can be kept with its table under a name, as a search engine keeps a rank profile, and queries rank by the name: The ranking is a query of the table that ranks every row: SELECT * FROM table ORDER BY expression (any ranking expression , or a numeric column with ASC or DESC ; CREATE checks that every column it names is the table's), or rerank phases over it. It has no WHERE , and no LIMIT after its last ranking: the query that ranks by it filters and cuts. WITH (name => value, ...) after it gives its parameters defaults: numbers, strings, TRUE and FALSE . Names are letters, digits and _ , in any case; CREATE refuses a name the table has already, and CREATE OR REPLACE replaces it. ORDER BY rank_profile('name', parameter => value, ...) is the whole ORDER BY of a query of the profile's table, without ASC , a WITH , a join or GROUP BY . The query ranks as if it were written out: its WHERE filters the rows the first ranking reads, and its columns, LIMIT and OFFSET are the last ranking's, so score() and features() work as with the ranking written out. Each parameter of the ranking is the call's argument (a value, or a parameter of the query: q => :q ), else the profile's default, else the query's parameter of the same name. Kept and replicated as writes are : a profile is logged in its own transaction (numbered where writes are, shipped to replicas), and dropped with its table. A database with a profile needs the format 3 feature rank_profiles , which a version of ChloeDB from before profiles refuses by name; once the last profile is dropped and flushed, the database no longer needs it. A plan names its profile and a hash of its definition ( EXPLAIN shows rank profile: recall (hash ...) ) and needs the plan feature rank_profiles . A database whose profile of that name is another (replaced since, or a shard that missed the change) refuses the plan rather than rank otherwise. Through a coordinator, CREATE and DROP RANK PROFILE run on every shard, and the coordinator serves the profiles every replica holds alike. Ranking models: gbdt('model', features…) A gradient-boosted tree model, trained by LightGBM or XGBoost on the features a query logged, ranks rows as a ranking expression of them: The model is the file <models>/<model>/gbdt.json in the models directory, the one embedded fields' models come from ( --models on a server, models= in Python, else ~/.local/share/chloedb/models (or ~/.local/share/strata/models , on a machine that has that folder from before the rename; before 0.3.2, always)): LightGBM's booster.dump_model() written with json.dump (numerical and categorical splits, any missing-value handling, a random forest too), or XGBoost's booster.save_model(\"gbdt.json\") (a gbtree ; categorical splits, multiclass and dart are refused). A name is letters, digits, _ , - and . . A model is read when a query first uses it and again when its file changes. Its arguments are its features , in the order it was trained on, each any ranking expression of the row (a score, a column, arithmetic); as many as the model reads. A NULL feature is a missing value, which goes where the model sends missing values (LightGBM: NaN ; or 0 under zero_as_missing ). The score is the model's raw one , the sum of its trees, bit for bit what LightGBM's predict(raw_score=True) and XGBoost's predict(output_margin=True) give (XGBoost's in f32 , from its base score): sigmoid(gbdt(…)) is a binary model's probability. A random forest's score is the sum of its trees, as LightGBM's raw score is. Rank it over the top rows of a cheaper ranking. In a rerank phase a model is a second phase, which each shard scores on its own rows, one tree at a time over them: a LambdaMART model of 300 trees over BM25's top 100 takes 0.7 to 1.0 ms a query at the median on SciFact, NFCorpus and FiQA, against 0.02 to 0.2 ms for BM25 alone ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_gbdt.json , a 4-core cloud VM). ORDER BY gbdt(bm25(…), columns…) alone is exact, a rank-safe boost bounded by the leaves each tree can reach from the text score's bound and each column's range; but summed over hundreds of trees such a bound sits well above what rows score, so the top-k loop skips few matches and scores the rest with every tree: 5.5 to 70 ms on the same collections. A plan names the model and the hash of its file ( EXPLAIN shows ranking model: docs_ltr (hash …) ) and needs the plan feature rank_models . A database or shard whose file of that name is another refuses the plan rather than rank with another model; a plan without a hash runs with the file there. A coordinator scores the shards' rows with the model too, so it needs the same file in its own models directory. features() lists a model's inputs' leaves and then the model's score, and from Python gbdt(\"docs_ltr\", bm25(\"title\", t), col(\"rating\")) builds the same ranking. Cross-encoders: cross_encoder('model', fields…, query) A cross-encoder reads the query and a row's text together and scores how well the text answers it. It costs thousands of times what BM25 does per row, so it reranks the top rows of a cheaper ranking, in a rerank phase of its own: A server reranks at most 20 rows a query by default: start it with --max-rerank 100 to rerank the top 100, as the measurements below do. The model is a Hugging Face directory <models>/<model> holding config.json , tokenizer.json and its weights. A model.onnx , any sequence classifier with one label exported to ONNX (int8 ones too, as Hugging Face repositories ship them under onnx/ , such as model_qint8_avx512_vnni.onnx ), runs through ONNX Runtime in a server or Python package built with --features onnx , which downloads ONNX Runtime's library when it builds and links it in; its rows are sorted by length into batches of 16, so that little of a batch is padding. A model.safetensors , a BertForSequenceClassification (such as cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 ) or an XLMRobertaForSequenceClassification (such as BAAI/bge-reranker-base ) with one label, runs through candle in f32 in one built with --features bert ( accelerate on a Mac), as BERT embedding models do. A directory with both runs its model.onnx ; without the feature its weights need, a query by it is refused. chloedb-server fetch-model ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2-int8 (or gte-reranker-modernbert-base-int8 , a ModernBERT model) downloads an int8 export ready to run. A model is read when a query first uses it and again when one of its files changes. builtin:overlap needs no files: the share of the query's distinct words the text holds, for tests and demos. A model served elsewhere is called over HTTP, a batch of rows a request (feature hosted , on by default), for models too large or slow for the server's CPU: a provider's by name, cohere/rerank-v3.5 , voyage/rerank-2.5 or jina/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual , with the key in COHERE_API_KEY (or CO_API_KEY ), VOYAGE_API_KEY or JINA_API_KEY ; or a server of one's own that a directory <models>/<model> names in an endpoint.json , such as Hugging Face TEI on a GPU ( {\"api\": \"tei\", \"url\": \"http://gpu:8080/rerank\"} ), or vLLM, Infinity or LiteLLM ( \"api\": \"vllm\" , \"infinity\" or \"litellm\" , with a base_url ; they answer at /rerank in Cohere's shape). endpoint.json takes the keys an embedding model's does that apply ( url or base_url , model , key_env , key_header , headers , batch , max_chars , concurrency , retries , timeout_s ), and COHERE_BASE_URL , VOYAGE_BASE_URL or JINA_BASE_URL sends a provider's requests to a server of its API. A request carries 1,000 rows at most (TEI's default batch, 32, for a server of one's own), four are sent at once, a rate limit or a server's error is tried twice more (pauses of at most 5 s; a request times out after 10 s), and a refused key, a server that keeps failing or an answer that misses a row fails the query. A provider's score is its relevance_score , between 0 and 1; TEI's is the logit ( raw_scores ), as a model run here gives it. A row with no text is not sent, and gets no score. The plan's hash is of the endpoint (its API, URL and model, not its key), so a plan made with one server is refused by another. The text is the values of the fields named, stored TEXT or KEYWORD fields, joined by a space ( NULL s left out, each value of a list), and the query is the last argument, a string or a parameter. Query and text together are cut to 256 tokens (or the model's length, if shorter), the longer of the two first, as Hugging Face's tokenizers cut a pair; a cross_encoder.json in the model's directory, {\"max_tokens\": 512} , sets another length. A language model grades each row instead: anthropic/<model> (a Claude model, such as anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5 , its key in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ), openai/<chat model> , or a chat server of one's own that an endpoint.json names with \"api\": \"chat\" (OpenAI's /chat/completions , as vLLM, Ollama, LM Studio and LiteLLM serve it; base_url is its /v1 ) or \"api\": \"anthropic\" . Each row is its own request, eight at once ( concurrency ), with UMBRELA's prompt (the one /v1/judge asks): the model reads the query and the row's text and answers a grade from 0 (nothing to do with the query) to 3 (the exact answer), which is the row's score. A model costs a request a row and about a second each, so it reranks a few dozen rows at most, and gives many rows the same grade: add the first phase's score to break ties ( cross_encoder('anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5', body, :q) + 0.01 * bm25(body, :q) ). An answer without a grade leaves the row without a score, and it ranks last; a request times out after 30 s. The score is the label's logit , what sentence-transformers' CrossEncoder.predict gives with an identity activation ( sigmoid() of it is the probability for a model trained with one), equal to PyTorch's within 1e-5. A phase may mix it with other scores of the row ( cross_encoder(…) + 0.1 * rating ), and phases after it rerank the rows it kept. It runs once per query, in a global phase : on a database over the rows the phase before it kept, and through a coordinator once on the coordinator, which reads the text of the rows its shards ranked best in the fetch round of query then fetch and scores them; shards need no model. A cross-encoder is refused as the first ranking (rerank a cheaper ranking's top rows), as a column, and among features() . Its cost is per row and per token : on four cores ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 takes about 50 ms a row of 256 tokens in f32 through candle, and its int8 export through ONNX Runtime about 20 ms. BM25's top 20 takes 0.9 to 1.0 s at the median on SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna in f32 and 0.37 to 0.43 s in int8, and the top 100 4.4 to 4.8 s and 1.8 to 2.0 s, where BM25 alone takes 0.1 to 1 ms; cut at 512 tokens, as before, the top 20 took 1.3 to 1.6 s ( results , a 4-core cloud VM). --max-rerank on a server (default 20, a quarter of the default deadline at p99 in f32 ) refuses a phase that would score more rows, and the query's deadline ( --timeout-ms , 5 s) is checked between batches of 16 rows. Its layers run on the threads the queries share (one per core), so reranks running at once take turns on the cores rather than each starting a thread per core, and queries that ask for a model not yet open wait for one of them to open it. Whether it helps depends on the collection : reranking BM25's top 100 by ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 , a model trained on web questions, raises NFCorpus's nDCG@10 from 0.329 to 0.352, leaves SciFact's where it was (0.687 against 0.691) and lowers ArguAna's from 0.362 to 0.312; see the results . Measure it on your own queries before paying for it. A plan names the model and the hash of its files (FNV-1a of the three, or of a served model's endpoint; EXPLAIN shows ranking model: … (hash …) ), with the plan feature rank_models , as for gbdt . From Python, .order_by(bm25(\"body\", q)).rerank(cross_encoder(\"ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2\", [\"title\", \"body\"], q), 100) builds the same query. The rank log: what each query served A ranking is trained on what it served. A query run with a log writes one row per row it answered to a log table, with the values its ranking read as it computed them, so a model trained later sees what serving saw, however the tables change in between: new rows that move BM25's statistics, counters that grow, rows deleted. A logged query is one SELECT ranked by an ORDER BY ( rerank phases and rank profiles included) of a table with a primary key: \"log\" in the body of POST /v1/query or /v1/sql ( true , or an object of options), db.query_logged(sql, params, context=...) in Python , Db::query_logged in Rust. It runs with score() and features() added to its columns, answers with the columns it asked for and the request's id ( request ), and writes its rows to the log in one transaction once it has answered: a logged query costs a write. Options : context , whom the request served (a user, a session, a query class), which the export reads events by; request , the request's id (a random one when left out; logging a request's id again overwrites its rows at the same positions); variant , the client's name for the ranking that served (an A/B arm); candidates , a number of rows from the query's OFFSET to log, more than its LIMIT returns, those past the LIMIT logged as not shown (unshown negatives); ts , when it was served (ISO 8601 text or microseconds; now when left out), for replaying past traffic; table , another log table; interleave , a second ranking to show in the same list ( interleaving ). A row holds its request, time and context, the key of the row served ( row_key , as text), its position from 0 (the OFFSET included), whether it was shown , its score() , and features : a JSON object of each features() column, named by its SQL text, whose numbers read back to the bits the ranking computed. The request's first row also keeps its query : the SQL as the client sent it and its parameters as they were bound (a vector as {\"$vector\": [...]} , a time as {\"$ts\": microseconds} ), which the relevance autopilot replays. A table made by hand serves as a log if it has these columns, of these types (with retain = '90 days' , say); it may leave out query , and then keeps no query (a log made before the column was added: ALTER TABLE rank_log ADD COLUMN query KEYWORD OPTIONS(indexed = false) ). Through a coordinator the query is the coordinator's, so the values are one database's ( features over shards ), and the rows go to the shards their keys place them on; the first logged query makes the table on every shard. A replica refuses a logged query, the log being a write; a primary ships the table it makes to its copies, as it ships the rows. A shard's query ( \"shard\": true ) is not logged. Cost. Computing the values is nearly free, since the ranking computes them anyway; the write is the cost. On a 4-core cloud VM over HTTP, with 100,000 documents and one client, a query took 1.87 ms at the median plain, 1.85 ms with its values added and 4.33 ms logged (7.81 ms logging 100 candidates); writing the same ten rows alone through /v1/write took 1.97 ms. Without the disk's flush ( --no-sync ) the logged query took 3.50 ms: the sync is a third of the log's cost here, and the rest is the write itself. Four clients got 1,620 plain and 670 logged queries a second. A row took 152 bytes in the segments (an Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, 16.9 GB, Linux 6.18; chloedb-server/results/rank_log/vm.json and vm-no-sync.json , from chloedb-rank-log-bench ). The export read 70,000 records a second, each with a history of 20 events and a label. Exporting the log for training The export reads a log table back from one snapshot, one record per logged row, with what happened around it as of its time, from a table of events (a user's views, clicks, purchases): the context's last events before the row (its history, for a sequence model), and a label from the events on the row's key after it, within a window. Records come as NDJSON from POST /v1/rank_log/export , as a list of dicts from db.export_rank_log(...) in Python, and from Db::export_rank_log in Rust, context by context, each context's rows in the order they were served. Times are microseconds since the epoch. since and until keep the rows served in [since, until) , and table names another log. events names a table, its column of contexts ( user , KEYWORD or INT ), of the rows the events were about ( item ), of when they happened ( time , a TIMESTAMP or an INT of microseconds), and optionally of what they were ( action ). OPTIONS(shard_by = 'usr', cluster_by = 'usr') keeps each user's events together, on one shard and in few pages. History : with history set to n, the context's last n events before the row's time, oldest first. Label : with window , a period ( '30 minutes' ) or seconds, the best grade among the context's events on the row's key in the window after the row's time, grades giving each action's (an event whose action has none is not a label; without grades every event grades 1), and 0 when there is none. A label written on the row wins. A row without a context has no history and no label but its own. As of the row's time , by the events' own times: an event timed before a row is in its history even when it arrived after the row was served, which a query at the time could not have seen. The features are the values served, never recomputed. Through a coordinator the export and the interleaving credit run their queries over every shard, each read whole by a scroll, rather than from one snapshot ( cluster limits ). Logs placed by context (a hand-made log with context KEYWORD NOT NULL and shard_by = 'context' ) and events placed by user then give each shard every row and event of its contexts. Interleaving two rankings An A/B test shows each user one ranking and compares the users; interleaving shows each user both, in one list, and asks which ranking's rows the user acted on. Each request sets the two side by side for the same user and the same query, so it tells two rankings apart with ten to a hundred times fewer requests than an A/B test of the same two (Chapelle et al., \"Large-scale validation and analysis of interleaved search evaluation\", TOIS 2012). The list is team draft (Radlinski, Kurup and Joachims, CIKM 2008): both queries run, on one snapshot, and in each round the ranking with fewer rows in the list, or a coin's choice when they have as many, adds its best row not yet in it, up to the logged query's LIMIT . A row both rankings hold goes to the one that took it. The coin is seeded by the request's id, so the same request draws the same list. When one ranking runs out, the other's rows fill the list for neither team. teams in the answer names each row's ranking; show the rows, not the teams. The second ranking is a SELECT of the same table answering the same columns, with the same parameters. Both need a LIMIT and no OFFSET (a draft is of a first page), and candidates and variant are left out: each row shown is logged with its own ranking's score and features and its team's name as its variant ( variants , [\"a\", \"b\"] unless named). The credit : POST /v1/rank_log/interleaving takes the export's body and reads the log as the export does, labels included. A request is interleaved when its shown rows carry two variants, and it goes to the team whose rows its user acted on more (a label above 0), or is a tie; a request nobody acted on counts for neither. delta is Chapelle et al.'s Δ, (wins of the first + ties / 2) / requests acted on − 0.5, above 0 when the first ranking (by name) is preferred; p is the two-sided sign test of the wins, ties left out. Db::interleaving_credit gives the same in Rust, and the command center's Recsys eval draws it. The relevance autopilot The autopilot reads the queries the rank log kept, proposes new numbers for the rank profile they rank by, and shows the measured before and after on the database's own data before anything changes. (The numbers above show the shape of an answer; they were not measured.) The workload is each distinct query (its SQL and parameters, named by a qid ) that a logged request asked with ORDER BY rank_profile(...) , the most recent first ( max_queries , 500), logged since since . The profile is the one most of them rank by unless table and profile name one. The autopilot tunes a profile because a profile is what a proposal can change; a query that ranks by an expression of its own is left out. Grades are the rank log's labels (a row's mean over the query's requests) and the judgments table's, relevance_judgments unless judgments names another, which win for the same row: {id: '<qid>#<row_key>', qid, query, row_key, grade, source, ts} , grades 0 to 3 (UMBRELA's scale and the relevance lab's). A query needs a row graded above 0 to count, and the autopilot says nothing with fewer than min_queries (20) such queries. The search is coordinate ascent (Metzler and Croft, 2007) over the profile's numbers: the defaults of its parameters ( WITH (w => 0.2) ) and the numeric literals of its rankings' ORDER BY expressions, 0 and 1 left out as structure. Each number is tried at 2, 0.5, 1.25, 0.8, 4 and 0.25 times its value, and a step is taken when the training queries' mean nDCG@k ( k , 10) rises, until no step helps or budget (60) candidates have been replayed. A proposal is the profile with some numbers moved, nothing else. The proof : every query reads one snapshot ( snapshot is its sequence number), so both rankings see the same rows and the same statistics. The share held_out (0.3) of the graded queries, picked by a seeded hash of their qid , is kept out of the search, and the verdict is drawn from those alone: propose when their mean nDCG@k rose by more than min_gain (0.005), more of them were made better than worse and the sign test of the two says so at alpha (0.05) (a few queries up by much and more down by a little is not_significant , though its mean rose), and the 95th percentile of the queries' time did not grow by more than max_slowdown (1.5 times, beyond 2 ms). Otherwise no_change , no_gain , not_significant , slower , too_few_judged or no_workload , each with a reason . The report has the training, held-out and overall measures, a bootstrap interval of the held-out change, each query's nDCG both ways, and the share of the top k that was graded. With the same data and seed a report is made again the same. Rows no one graded gain nothing, which favours the ranking now (its rows are the ones people saw and graded). The report lists them ( holes , max_holes , 200); with \"judge\": true the server has Claude grade them first (UMBRELA's prompt, as POST /v1/judge does; the server's ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or api_key ), writes the grades to the judgments table with source claude , and measures again. Proposals are kept in relevance_proposals with the report ( keep , true): GET /v1/relevance/proposals lists them, GET /v1/relevance/proposals/:id gives one with its proof, POST .../:id/apply runs its CREATE OR REPLACE RANK PROFILE and POST .../:id/dismiss closes it. Applying is refused once the profile has changed since the measure (the proof is of another profile: run the autopilot again), and for a verdict other than propose unless {\"force\": true} . A proposal's row is not taken at its word: its statement is applied only when it is the profile now with some of its numbers moved (never another ranking, even with force ), and its proof is made again first, on the log and the grades as they are, with the options it was measured with (kept in the proof as options ); a proof made again that does not propose it is refused unless {\"force\": true} . Running, applying and dismissing need an admin key, and so does any write to relevance_proposals and relevance_judgments (a write key's answers 403 scope_refused ). The command center's Labs have it as Relevance autopilot. In Rust : Db::autopilot(&Options) gives the report and reads only; Db::keep_proposal , Db::apply_proposal and Db::add_judgments write. A coordinator does not run it, since its shards hold a snapshot each: run it on a single server or on each shard's server. Sequence models: encode('model', history) A sequence model reads what a user did, newest first, and gives a vector in the space of an item field, so that the items it ranks first are the ones the user is likely to want next: The history is a subquery of the user's events, newest first: its first column is an item's primary key in the model's table, then optionally what the event was (a text column: view , buy , a rating) and when (a TIMESTAMP , or a number of microseconds since the epoch, as the rank log's export writes them). With two columns the second is the time when it is a TIMESTAMP and the action when it is text; a number there is refused, since it could be either, so give microseconds as the third column ( SELECT item, NULL, micros ). Order it by time, descending: a row newer than one above it is an error rather than a history read backwards. Of its rows whose item has a vector, the model reads the newest, up to the length it was trained on, as training reads a history. now => is the time of the request (a TIMESTAMP , microseconds or ISO 8601 text; the clock's by default): the time since the newest event is part of what the model reads. Where it goes : similarity(field, encode(…)) ranks the field's nearest items (the dot product with the model's vector: a search of the field's index, its best scored exactly, as maxsim runs one), maxsim(field, encode(…)) the same as one query vector, and dot(field, encode(…)) is a number in any ranking expression. A user with no events has no vector: similarity and maxsim then return no rows, and dot is NULL . The history runs once a query, however often encode appears in it. The model is a small causal transformer (SASRec's): each event is its item's vector, projected, plus a learned vector for its action, for the time until the next event (in powers of two of seconds) and for its position; two layers of attention over the events, each seeing only the ones before it, then the last event's output, projected into the item field's space and normalized. It is the directory <models>/<model>/ ( config.json , model.safetensors and items.safetensors ), in the models directory gbdt 's files come from. The config names the table and the vector field the model was trained on and their dimension: a database without that field, or with one of another dimension, refuses the model, and so do similarity and maxsim over another field. items.safetensors holds the item vectors it was trained with, and is part of the model's version (its hash): a history whose items have other vectors in the field is refused, naming one, since the model's vector would be in a space it never saw. An item the file does not list (one added since) is read with the field's vector. Training is chloedb.train.sequence in Python, with PyTorch ( pip install torch ), from a table of events, the rank log's export, or lists of (item, action, time) : It trains next-item prediction over each user's events, with every item in the field ( negatives=\"all\" ) or the other targets of a batch ( \"in_batch\" , for many items; with logq=True and uniform_negatives=n it no longer learns to rank popular items low) as the negatives, and writes the model where models= says (the models directory by default), with the item vectors it was trained with. With learn_items=True the item vectors are trained too, starting from the field's: chloedb.train.write_vectors writes them into the field, changing nothing else of each row, and until then encode refuses the model. Otherwise the field's vectors are kept as they are and only the model learns. - Equal to PyTorch's : ChloeDB's vector is the one the PyTorch module gives for the same history, within 2·10⁻⁵ ( chloedb/tests/sequence.rs ), and the items a query ranks are the ones PyTorch's vector ranks. - How well it ranks : on MovieLens-1M and Amazon Beauty, leave-one-out over every item as SASRec is measured, a model of two layers that reads items only has Recall@10 0.278 and 0.066 (NDCG@10 0.159 and 0.041). That is above the original SASRec's published 0.250 and 0.061, but below SASRec trained the same way as this model, with cross-entropy over every item (SASRec+, 0.315 on MovieLens), and on Beauty below counted transitions from the same events ( chloedb.next_items , 0.069): good, not the best published. Reading times too, it has 0.319 and 0.077. Both MovieLens runs stopped at a time limit while still improving. ChloeDB's answers equal PyTorch's on every user ( results/sequence , with the protocol and the published numbers), and CI checks ranking quality on MovieLens-1M against popularity and item-kNN baselines and ChloeDB's other sources ( chloedb/results/sequence/gate.py ): there a model trained for an hour has Recall@10 0.306, ppr over what came after what 0.242, counted transitions 0.211 and item-kNN 0.069. - What it costs : the model alone, on one thread of a cloud VM's Xeon at 2.80 GHz, takes 0.09 ms for 10 events, 0.28 ms for 50 and 1.5 ms for 200 (p50); the whole query, reading the history and searching the field, 0.6 to 0.7 ms on Beauty's 50 events and 2 to 3 ms on MovieLens's 200. Keep max_len as short as the ranking allows. - A plan names the model and the hash of its files and needs the plan feature encode ; a database or shard whose files of that name are others refuses it. A coordinator runs encode itself, from the history its shards return, so it needs the model; the shards don't."],
["sql.html#decayfield-origin-scale--closeness-to-a-value","ChloeDB's query language","decay(field, origin, scale, …) : closeness to a value","A numeric column's closeness to origin , as in Elasticsearch's decay functions. With d = max(0, |x − origin| − offset) , the score is 1 at d = 0 and decay at d = scale : shape score 'exp' (default) decay^(d / scale) 'gauss' decay^((d / scale)²) 'linear' max(0, 1 − (1 − decay) · d / scale) , reaching 0 at scale / (1 − decay) Options: offset => 0 , decay => 0.5 , shape => 'exp' . Rows without a value score 0. now() as the origin is the current Unix time in seconds, so decay(published, now(), 86400) halves a row's score for each day of age. decay ranks on its own, inside rrf and weighted sums, and in products."],
["sql.html#products-boosting-relevance-by-a-rows-values","ChloeDB's query language","Products: boosting relevance by a row's values","Multiplying scores boosts relevance by what a row holds: A product's factors are scores ( bm25 , similarity , ppr , decay ) and numeric columns, one of them a relevance score ( bm25 , similarity , a graph's); a product of a row's own values alone ( rating * clicks , decay(..) * rating ) is arithmetic, and ranks as the numbers multiply. Candidates are the top depth (100) rows of each relevance factor, scored exactly by every factor; a product of one text score and row values ( bm25(..) * decay(..) , bm25(..) * rating ) is a rank-safe boost and ranks every row the text matches. A factor below 0 (a negative similarity) or missing (a NULL column) counts as 0, so the product of two negatives cannot rank high. Over shards, each shard returns its factors' raw scores and the coordinator multiplies them, so a split collection ranks like the whole one."],
["sql.html#a-column","ChloeDB's query language","A column","ORDER BY year DESC orders by a numeric column. Rows without a value come last in either direction (as in Elasticsearch); ORDER BY year DESC NULLS FIRST puts them first. Their score() is NULL . With no ORDER BY , rows come in storage order. With a LIMIT , the rows can come from the column's blocks of 128 rows, best block first by its zone map (its largest value for DESC , smallest for ASC ), stopping at the first block whose best value is worse than the k-th row found. A row's filter is checked only when its value can still make the cut, and blocks the filter's own zone maps rule out are never read. So the ten most clicked of the 560,000 ORCAS queries starting with a read about 150 of 81,000 blocks, and about 10 when the table is clustered by query ( OPTIONS(cluster_by = 'id') ), where each prefix's rows sit together. The planner keeps the plain scan of the matches when they are a few thousand rows, or under 1 in 64 of the rows the zone maps admit (a rare prefix of an unclustered table, whose k-th best value is low enough that most blocks could hold a better one), and switches to it when the blocks have cost half of what it would (a filter that rejects the best values, WHERE n < 3 ORDER BY n DESC ); NULLS FIRST always scans. Values written in place are read first, so counters kept with UPDATE ... SET n = n + 1 rank by their current value."],
["sql.html#typos-and-synonyms","ChloeDB's query language","Typos and synonyms","MATCH and bm25 take two options that widen a search beyond the words written: fuzzy => 1 or 2 also matches the indexed terms within that many edits of each word, counting an insertion, deletion, substitution or swap of two neighbouring letters as one edit (optimal string alignment). fuzzy => 'auto' allows no edits for words of 1 or 2 letters, 1 for 3 to 5 letters and 2 for longer ones. In the MATCH grammar, word~ is 'auto' for one word and word~1 , word~2 choose. A ranked query adds the 50 nearest terms of each word, each weighted 1 − edits / length (at least 0.1), as Lucene's fuzzy queries do. A word and its typo neighbours share one document frequency, the largest among them, so a rare misspelling scores below the word rather than above it; the expansion is found once per query over every segment's dictionary, so all segments, and the plan line bm25 expanded with N terms , agree. Each dictionary is walked as a trie, skipping every run of terms whose start is already too many edits away, rather than tested term by term. A skip inside a block of the front-coded dictionary passes terms by their lengths alone, and each letter's row of edits is worked out for up to 63 letters of the word at once in a machine word (Wu and Manber's bit-parallel matching, with Hyyrö's swap of two letters). For a word of a typed query looked up in the 797,472 analysed terms of ORCAS's 10.4 million queries, the median lookup takes 1.4 ms with one edit and 7.8 ms with two (2.1 and 16.5 ms before the in-block skip and the bit-parallel rows, run just before on the same machine), against 77 and 106 ms testing every term (one thread of a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz; results/text/fuzzy-orcas.json , chloedb-fuzzy-bench ). A query walks a copy of each segment's terms laid out flat in memory rather than the dictionary itself: each term whole, where it ends and how many bytes it shares with the one before, so a step decodes nothing and a jump past a run of terms reads only those shared counts. The copy is made at the segment's first typo query (12 ms for 530,000 terms) and kept while the segment lives, at the terms' bytes plus 5 bytes a term (6.5 MB for 2 million MS MARCO passages, against the dictionary's 4.7 MB). On a shard of 5 million MS MARCO passages in three segments, with the engine comparison's typed queries, a word's walks over the three segments take 9.4 ms against 22.6 ms over the dictionaries, the median typed query 17.0 ms against 29.1 ms, and four clients, one thread each, get 215 typed queries a second against 124 (a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz). A ranked query with fuzzy => 'auto' corrects typos rather than widening every word, by how many documents hold the word and its neighbours, as a spelling corrector does. A word in at least 1 in 10,000 of the documents, and in 2 or more, is taken as spelled right and gets no neighbours: typos are rare words. Another word gets only its neighbours in more documents than it, each weighted, besides 1 − edits / length , by the chance it was meant: x / (x + df) with x = df′ × 0.01^edits , where df counts the word's documents and df′ the neighbour's (a noisy channel that takes each term as likely as its documents and each edit as a 1 in 100 slip). A word in no document is a typo of each of its neighbours (chance 1), and a word nearly as common as its neighbour almost surely means itself. A shard chooses the neighbours by its own counts, the same in a coordinator's statistics round as in its query, and takes the chances from the collection's counts the coordinator sends. MATCH , and bm25 with fuzzy => 1 or 2 , still widen every word. A bm25 of the text a MATCH filters by takes the MATCH 's fuzzy when it names none: WHERE MATCH(title, 'wireles hedphones', fuzzy => 'auto') ORDER BY bm25(title, 'wireles hedphones') DESC ranks the rows the filter found, rather than scoring the words as written and dropping them all. The text must be the same (case and outer spaces aside), on the same field. A typo is looked up as typed too : under a stemming analyzer such as english , a word the index does not hold looks for neighbours of the word as written as well as of its stem, so wireles (stem wirel , three edits from wireless ) finds wireless one edit away. On 500,477 passages, half of MS MARCO's first million, with the first 300 dev queries, each given a typo as the engine comparison does (two letters of its longest word swapped; 271 queries take one), the typed query's top 10 holds 0.685 of the top 10 that plain bm25 gives the query as written, against 0.346 widening every word and 0.383 without typo tolerance. On the queries as written, 'auto' keeps 0.946 of plain bm25 's top 10, against 0.340 widening every word. The median typed query takes 10.0 ms, against 48.5 ms before this change and 20.6 ms widening every word on its faster walk and scoring, and the median query as written 0.35 ms (19 ms widening every word), since it walks no dictionary. Through a coordinator over two shards of the million passages (both halves, on the same machine), the median typed query takes 15.8 ms against 112 ms before (p99 30 against 287 ms), and four clients get 137 typed queries a second against 17.7: a shard also walks once per query rather than twice, its statistics round and its query sharing the walk. On BEIR's SciFact and NFCorpus, whose queries are spelled right, nDCG@10 with 'auto' went from 0.673 and 0.294 widening every word to 0.693 and 0.331, level with plain bm25 (0.691 and 0.329). The thresholds were chosen on MS MARCO's dev queries, not BEIR's test queries. One segment on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz; cargo run --release --example typo_queries . - synonyms => 1 also matches what the server's synonym list says a word or phrase means. The list is in Solr's format: laptop, notebook, portable computer makes each find the others, and tv => television makes tv find television but not the reverse. Both sides are analysed like documents, so laptops finds notebook . A one-word synonym of a word shares that word's document frequency, as Lucene's synonym queries do; the words of a longer synonym score on their own. chloedb-server loads it from --synonyms FILE and edits it at /v1/synonyms ; from Rust, set ExecOptions::synonyms . Without a list, synonyms => 1 is an error that says so. - A table's own list (experimental). CREATE SYNONYMS ON products AS 'sofa, couch\\ntv => television' gives a table rules of its own, which synonyms => 1 on that table's searches uses after the server's list. It is kept as the stored function synonyms_products() , so it has versions ( CREATE OR REPLACE SYNONYMS makes the next, CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION synonyms_products FROM VERSION 1 goes back) and a coordinator writes it on every shard; DROP SYNONYMS [IF EXISTS] ON products removes it. The rules are checked when the list is made. GET /v1/synonyms lists each table's. - Mined synonyms (experimental) are proposals for that list, never applied by themselves: Db::mine_synonyms or POST /v1/synonyms/mine reads the words of a stored text field and embeds the 3,000 most frequent with the model of one of the table's vector fields, proposing pairs whose vectors are close (cosine 0.6 or more) and whose spellings are not alike (a model of word pieces puts adel by adela ; numbers such as b10 and b11 still pass); or reads a table of searches ( context , ts in seconds, text ) and proposes a => b where people of two or more contexts rewrote one word of a search into another within a minute. On Wayfair's WANDS ( chloedb-synonyms-eval , chloedb/results/text/synonyms_wands.json , an Intel Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 4 cores), the words of 42,994 product names embedded by potion-retrieval-32M gave pairs such as kids, children , bike, bicycle and picture, photo beside related words that are not synonyms ( animal, dog , hawaii, honolulu ), and none of the lists made a measurable difference to nDCG@10 (0.4327 without; 0.4343 with the 33 rules at cosine 0.6, p = 0.31), while the 462 rules at 0.4 lowered it to 0.4231 (p = 0.007). Read the proposals before keeping them. A mining keeps to bounds whoever asks: 200,000 rows read, 5,000 words compared, a cosine of 0.3 or more and 10 proposals a word at most; on a server it takes an admin key."],
["sql.html#query-expansion-expand","ChloeDB's query language","Query expansion: expand","Experimental. This is new in 0.3.0, and its options and answers may change in a later release. bm25(body, :q, expand => 10) ranks by the query's words and by the words its first ranking's top 10 rows hold most: pseudo-relevance feedback (RM3). It finds rows that use other words for what the query asks, such as antibodies for vaccines . How. The query runs in two steps. The first ranks the rows as the call would without expand , through the query's WHERE , and reads the field's text of the top n ( SELECT body, score() FROM papers WHERE year >= 2023 ORDER BY bm25(body, :q) DESC LIMIT 10 ). Each of those rows weighs its terms by its share of the scores and each term's share of the row; the expand_terms best terms over all of them share 1 − expand_weight of the weight, and the query's own terms share expand_weight . The second step is the query with the call rewritten to those analysed, weighted terms ( bm25(body, 'vaccin^0.85 antibodi^0.03 immun^0.03 ...', syntax => 'terms') ), and the answer's plan lines start with what was added: bm25(body) expanded from 10 rows of papers with 20 terms: antibodi 0.031, immun 0.029, ... . Options. expand is the rows read, from 0 (no expansion, the call as written) to 100; expand_terms the terms added, up to 100; expand_weight the query's own share, from 0 to 1. Each may be a parameter ( expand => :n ). A call that expands takes no min , min_rows or max_rows . Where. In a SELECT of one table, in its columns, WHERE or ORDER BY , and in a WITH or a subquery of one table. A join is refused, and so is expand in a rank profile or a stored function. On a coordinator over shards the first step is an ordinary query of the shards, merged, and the coordinator rewrites the query, so every shard ranks by the same terms. Cost. A second query and the analysis of n rows' text: on BEIR's SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna the median query took 1.8, 1.7 and 2.9 ms against 0.09, 0.03 and 0.77 ms for plain bm25 (one core of a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz). Quality. The defaults (10 rows, 20 terms, 0.85) were chosen on SciFact's train and NFCorpus's dev judgements ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_rm3_tuning_*.json ). On the test judgements, nDCG@10 went from 0.691 to 0.693 on SciFact (p = 0.55), 0.329 to 0.346 on NFCorpus (p = 0.0001) and 0.362 to 0.369 on ArguAna (p = 0.0006) ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_rm3.json , chloedb-bm25-eval --variants plain,rm3 ). Expansion helps most where queries are short and documents use a field's own vocabulary; a setting that keeps less of the query (0.5) lowered SciFact to 0.646, so measure before lowering expand_weight ."],
["sql.html#understanding-a-querys-words-understand","ChloeDB's query language","Understanding a query's words: understand","Experimental. This is new in 0.3.0, and its options and answers may change in a later release. bm25(field, :q, understand => 'boost') reads the words of a search for the values of the table's KEYWORD fields they name ( white , a colour; coffee table , a class) and for ranges of numbers ( under $300 ), and ranks by the text and by them: What names a value. A field's values are read with a query ( SELECT color, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY color , once per version of the table but at most once a second for a table that takes writes, one query at a time reading it while the others use the one before; on a coordinator once a minute) and analysed as the text field is. Words name a value when they are a run of its words, in its order, that ends with its last word, its head: coffee table names Coffee Tables and Coffee & Cocktail Tables , and chair every value that ends in chairs, but table does not name Table Lamps . A run inside a longer one of another field is that one's ( coffee of coffee table is not the colour coffee). Values of several fields named by the same words are one condition, any of them. A check against the rows. A run counts only when, of the rows whose text holds its words, at least understand_share (0.05) hold one of its values: up is not the brand Up when almost no row that says up is of it. That is one query a run, counting both ( SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(CASE WHEN .. THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) ... WHERE MATCH(..) ), and runs of the same words and values share one. A text is understood in its first 32 words, and a call uses its first 16 runs, so that a pasted page costs what a query does. Ranges. under , below , less than , up to , over , above , more than , at least , between .. and .. , since , after and before followed by a number ( $1,500 , 2.5 , 10k ) are a range of a numeric field named in understand_fields : the one named next to them ( over 10 citations ), a field named for years for a year ( since 2020 ), or the only one. Their words leave the ranked text. Modes. 'boost' multiplies the text score by 1 + understand_weight (0.25) for each condition a row meets, a rank-safe boost . 'filter' also puts each run that names a value in full, and each range, in the WHERE (a row without a value of the field is kept). 'off' is the call as written. Each option may be a parameter ( understand => :mode ). Fields. Without understand_fields , every KEYWORD field of the table but its key, and a field of more than 20,000 values is left out as a name rather than a facet (its read stops at 20,001); a listed field reads up to 200,000. A call that understands ranks the rows of one table, without a join, and goes with expand (it understands first). Db::understand(table, field, text, options) (chloedb-server's POST /v1/understand ) returns what a text says without ranking: its runs, the values each names, their shares, the ranges and the conditions. Measured. On Wayfair's WANDS (42,994 products, 480 queries; name and description as the text, class, category, colour, style, material, shape and product type as keyword fields) 'boost' moved nDCG@10 from 0.707 to 0.731 on the odd-numbered queries (p = 0.002), and the classes its words named held the class the query asks for in 262 of 343 queries; 'filter' lowered it to 0.631, since a product judged relevant often lacks the value or holds another. On Amazon's ESCI (its 8,956 US test queries of the small version and the 164,900 products judged for them; brand and colour listed) 'boost' moved it from 0.394 to 0.396 (p = 0.007): its titles already hold their brands. Without the check against the rows, ESCI's ranking fell to 0.391. The weight and share were chosen on the even-numbered queries of both. A query took 0.9 to 1.0 ms at the median, against 0.07 to 0.12 ms for bm25 alone ( chloedb-understand-eval , chloedb/results/text/understand_wands.json and understand_esci.json , Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, 4 cores)."],
["sql.html#did-you-mean-correcting-a-querys-words","ChloeDB's query language","Did you mean: correcting a query's words","Experimental. This is new in 0.3.0, and its options and answers may change in a later release. Db::correct(table, field, text) (chloedb-server's POST /v1/correct ) returns the text with the words that look like typos of a TEXT field's terms replaced, for a search box to offer as \"did you mean\": A word is corrected only when no row holds it: a word some document uses is a word of the collection. Words with digits or capitals inside ( p150n , dLNs ) are names and codes, and stay. Its neighbours are the field's terms one edit away (up to 5 letters) or two (longer), as fuzzy => 'auto' finds them, and it takes the one most likely meant if that chance is at least 0.5: the noisy channel of typos , with an unseen word counted as in 0.05 documents, so that a neighbour one edit away must be in 5 or more documents. The word shown keeps the typed word's ending on the corrected term ( ifnlammation gives inflammation ). On BEIR's queries with a typo typed in (two letters of the longest word swapped), it corrected 0.73, 0.66 and 0.85 of them back to the words as written on SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna; of the queries it changed, 0.91, 0.90 and 0.98 were corrected back; and it changed 4.0%, 2.8% and 0% of the queries as written ( chloedb/results/text/bm25_did_you_mean.json , variant typed_corrected ). For ranking, fuzzy => 'auto' stays better than ranking the corrected text: nDCG@10 0.692 against 0.675 on SciFact, as it widens each word with its likely neighbours rather than choosing one. A coordinator corrects as one server does, by its shards' document counts summed: the words' own, then, for a word in none, those of its neighbours (each shard's nearest 50 within the edits allowed)."],
["sql.html#times-later-and-earlier-interval","ChloeDB's query language","Times later and earlier: INTERVAL","INTERVAL 'text' is a length of time: numbers each with a unit, '15 minutes' , '1 day 12 hours' , '-2 weeks' , '90s' , or INTERVAL '15' MINUTE . The units are microseconds , milliseconds , seconds , minutes , hours , days , weeks , months and years , singular or plural, and us , ms , s , sec , min , m (a minute), h , hr , d , w , mon and y . It is added to a time or subtracted from one; alone it is an error. A time written as a value ( now() , CURRENT_TIMESTAMP , TIMESTAMP '2026-10-01' , CAST('...' AS TIMESTAMP) , date_trunc('day', now()) ) moved by an interval is one value, computed once for the statement: ts > now() - INTERVAL '2 hours' is a range of ts , which the index answers, in WHERE , ON and a join's side alike. Months and years move it on the calendar, a day past the new month's end becoming its last day (January 31 plus a month is February 28 or 29), as in PostgreSQL. A column moved by an interval ( due + INTERVAL '2 days' ) is computed row by row, in microseconds; a month or a year has no fixed number of them, so an interval with months moves only values. A NULL time gives NULL . A number added to a time written as a value is an error that points to INTERVAL ( now() - 3600 could mean seconds or microseconds). Over the rows, a number added to a TIMESTAMP column counts microseconds: at + 3600000000 is an hour later."],
["sql.html#aggregates","ChloeDB's query language","Aggregates","GROUP BY takes one or more indexed KEYWORD , INT or FLOAT columns, or parts of times of a TIMESTAMP column ( date_trunc('day', at) ), by name, position or alias, which the index groups. A stored TEXT column, or a column kept without an index, groups too ( GROUP BY billing_country , SELECT DISTINCT billing_country ): its stored values are read with the rows and grouped in memory, as over derived rows, each distinct text its own group (case and spaces count). A multi-valued column puts a row in one group per value, over one table, joined rows or derived rows alike, and so does SELECT DISTINCT , which is GROUP BY its columns. The rows without a value form the group NULL , as in SQL, and SELECT DISTINCT lists NULL once. Aggregates are COUNT(*) , COUNT(f) , COUNT(DISTINCT f) , SUM(f) and AVG(f) over numeric columns, MIN(f) and MAX(f) over numeric or keyword columns, the percentiles of numeric columns, and the statistical aggregates below. COUNT(f) , COUNT(DISTINCT f) , MIN(f) and MAX(f) of a stored TEXT column (or a column kept without an index) count and compare its stored values, read with the rows. Every selected column is a group column or inside an aggregate. Without GROUP BY they summarize every row that passes WHERE , in one row ( COUNT(*) 0 and the others NULL when none does). FILTER (WHERE condition) after an aggregate aggregates only the rows the condition holds for: COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE severity = 'error') counts the errors of each group, beside COUNT(*) of all its rows. It is the aggregate of a CASE ( COUNT(CASE WHEN severity = 'error' THEN 1 END) ), and any aggregate takes it, in SELECT , HAVING and ORDER BY ; a column without an alias is named as written. FILTER on a function that is not an aggregate, or on a window aggregate, is an error. Statistical aggregates : STDDEV(x) and STDDEV_SAMP(x) , the sample standard deviation; STDDEV_POP(x) ; VARIANCE(x) and VAR_SAMP(x) , the sample variance; VAR_POP(x) ; COVAR_SAMP(y, x) and COVAR_POP(y, x) ; and CORR(y, x) , the Pearson correlation. As in PostgreSQL, NULL s are left out (the two-variable ones take the rows with both values), the sample forms are NULL for fewer than two values and the population forms for none, and CORR is NULL when either variable does not vary. They are computed from sums and counts in one pass (the sum of squares less the square of the sum over n), so they merge over shards as SUM and COUNT do, and with values far larger than their spread (times in microseconds) lose digits to rounding. HAVING filters groups by their aggregates, written out or by alias, with comparisons, BETWEEN , AND , OR and NOT , in three-valued logic: an aggregate of no values ( AVG(rating) of a group without ratings) is NULL , so neither AVG(rating) < 3 nor NOT (AVG(rating) < 3) keeps that group. ORDER BY a group column, an aggregate or a position, each by name or by its alias in the SELECT list ( SELECT zone AS z, COUNT(*) AS n ... GROUP BY zone ORDER BY n DESC, z ), with a tie-break; without one, the largest groups come first. The group of rows without a value comes last in either direction, and first with NULLS FIRST ( ORDER BY passenger_count DESC NULLS FIRST ). LIMIT and OFFSET apply to groups. Changed: 0.3.8 and earlier refused ORDER BY a group column's alias, and NULLS FIRST after GROUP BY or DISTINCT . A SUM or AVG of FLOAT values is exact: the values are added without rounding and the total is rounded once, so it does not depend on the order rows are read in, nor on the engine that runs it: ChloeDB's operators and DataFusion add the same way, so a total, and a RANK or an order over it, stay the same when a table grows past the rows at which DataFusion takes a plan over (two totals of 43.62 tie on both, where adding in order gives one of them as 43.620000000000005). Changed: 0.3.8 and earlier added in order on DataFusion, so such a tie broke once a query read more than 4,096 rows. A database groups its segments on several threads and merges them, and the answer is the same bits as on one thread. A coordinator over shards merges partial aggregates (sums and counts for AVG , the distinct values for COUNT(DISTINCT) ), so the answers are the same as on one server, except that each shard sends its FLOAT sum rounded once, so a sum over shards can differ from one server's in its last bit."],
["sql.html#percentiles","ChloeDB's query language","Percentiles","PERCENTILE_CONT(p) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY f) is the value a fraction p (0 to 1) of the way from the group's least value of f to its greatest, interpolated between the two values around that position, a FLOAT ; MEDIAN(f) is PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) . PERCENTILE_DISC(p) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY f) is the least value that at least a fraction p of the values are at most: one of the values, of f 's type (a TIMESTAMP 's times too). The answers are PostgreSQL's: of n values in order, PERCENTILE_DISC(p) is the one at position ⌈p·n⌉ (counting from 1, the first for p = 0), and PERCENTILE_CONT(p) the one at position p·(n − 1) counting from 0, interpolated in doubles. NULL s are left out, and a group without values gives NULL . A column without an alias is named as the call reads, PERCENTILE_CONT(price, 0.9) . Of columns and expressions. Over one table they take indexed INT and FLOAT columns, read from the columns as the other aggregates are; over joined or derived rows, any numeric expression ( MEDIAN(s.price * 1.2) ). p is a constant or a parameter ( :p ); the order is ascending ( ORDER BY f DESC is an error: write 1 - p ). They are aggregates of groups, not window functions. Exact, so they hold the values. A group keeps each of its values (8 bytes each, counted against the query's memory budget ) and orders them once when it is read: a median over 5 million rows holds 40 MB. A shard sends a group's values to the coordinator as runs of equal values, so prices that repeat travel once with their count, and the coordinator's answer is one server's. Plans with a percentile carry the plan feature percentiles , which a version of ChloeDB from before them refuses by name."],
["sql.html#approximate-percentiles","ChloeDB's query language","Approximate percentiles","approx_percentile(f, p) is PERCENTILE_DISC(p) of the group's values (the one at position ⌈p·n⌉ in order) within 1% of it, a FLOAT , from a DDSketch of the group's values rather than the values themselves. p = 0 and 1 give the least and greatest values exactly. It takes what the exact percentiles take, INT and FLOAT columns over one table and numeric expressions over joined or derived rows, except TIMESTAMP s. A few KB a group, however many rows. A sketch counts each value in a bucket of values within 1% of each other, and keeps at most 1,024 buckets of each sign (4 KB): the bound holds for values up to about 10⁸ times apart, as latencies are, and past that the least ones are folded together, so the low percentiles of such a spread lose it first. A p99 over 100 million rows holds a few KB rather than 800 MB. Exact merges. Sketches of segments, threads and shards add up to the sketch of all the values, so the answer does not depend on how rows were split: a coordinator's answer is one server's. A shard sends a group's sketch as versioned bytes (base64 text), which a coordinator reads only in the version it knows. Plans with one carry the plan feature approx_percentiles , which a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses by name."],
["sql.html#rollups-create-rollup-a-materialized-view-of-counts-over-time","ChloeDB's query language","Rollups: CREATE ROLLUP , a materialized view of counts over time","Experimental. Rollups are new in 0.3.1, and their options, columns and refresh may change in a later release. A rollup is a table of counts, sums, least and greatest values per bucket of time and per group, kept up to date from another table as rows arrive: ChloeDB's materialized view (a continuous aggregate) of a GROUP BY of time. Precomputed daily or hourly counts, kept up to date without a job of your own, are one: a time-bucket query over weeks of logs reads a few thousand rows of the rollup instead of millions of the table. It needs an admin key that is not limited to tables, and check_sql checks one without making it. The bucket is date_bin('<width>', field) , any whole number of seconds, minutes, hours, days or weeks counted from 1970-01-01, or date_trunc('second' | 'minute' | 'hour' | 'day', field) , of a TIMESTAMP column. The groups are KEYWORD , INT , BOOL or TIMESTAMP columns, and the aggregates COUNT(*) , COUNT(f) (the rows whose f is not NULL), and SUM(f) , MIN(f) , MAX(f) and AVG(f) of numeric columns ( MIN and MAX of a TIMESTAMP too). A WHERE keeps some rows only; FROM , when written, names the table after ON . The rollup is an ordinary table , named by the statement, with a row per bucket and group: rollup_key (its primary key, a number made of the bucket and the group's place in it), the bucket ( bucket , or the name AS gives it), the group columns, typed as in the table, and a column per aggregate, count , count_<field> , sum_<field> , min_<field> and max_<field> unless AS names it. AVG(f) keeps sum_f and count_f , so the mean is SUM(sum_f) / SUM(count_f) , which skips NULLs as AVG(f) does (a rollup made before 0.3.4 keeps sum_f and count only, whose quotient counts the rows without a value too; make it again for the other). A count column ( COUNT(*) ) is always kept. A rollup that counts a field's values is marked needs_chloedb_0_3_4 in its definition, which 0.3.3 refuses by name rather than refresh it wrong. Its columns are kept in the columns only, not again as whole rows ( stored = false ), which a rollup's queries never need: about 40 bytes a row. Query it by name, and add its rows up into larger buckets or fewer groups ( SUM(count) , MIN(min_latency) ); a query of the table is not rewritten onto it. OPTIONS(retain = ...) is the rollup's own retention , kept by its bucket: a rollup outlives the rows it counts. How it is kept. CREATE ROLLUP folds the table's rows at once, a day of buckets to a query: 20 million lines in about 8 seconds. On a server it answers what it made: the rollup, whether it is filled , the buckets folded and the rows written; it leaves the first fold of a table of over a million rows to its refresher, a few seconds on, and until then the rollup has fewer rows ( REFRESH ROLLUP fills it at once). From then on a refresh compares, bucket by bucket, how many rows of the table the bucket holds with the rollup's count for it (one count by bucket for a day of buckets at a time: every bucket of 20 million lines over 30 days in a quarter of a second), and folds again the buckets that differ, each in one transaction. A plain refresh compares the buckets from the clock less the lateness (10 minutes by default; from the newest row, when that is older) to the newest row, and any the rollup has not folded up to now: a row dated in the future is folded, but does not take the window away from the present. FULL compares them all, and REBUILD folds them all again whatever their counts. Only buckets that hold rows, in the table or in the rollup, are compared, so a row dated in 1970 or 2099 costs a query, not a walk over every bucket between. One refresh of a rollup runs at a time; another waits for it. A server refreshes every rollup every few seconds, and all their buckets every few minutes, each rollup on its own: one whose refresh fails is logged by name, and the others go on. GET /v1/tables gives a rollup's table a rollup entry with the table it reads, its last good refresh and its last error. An embedding program calls Db::spawn_rollups (or runs REFRESH ROLLUP ; Db::rollup_health says how each went). Buckets older than the table's retain are not compared again, so they keep what they counted. A bucket of a time so far from 1970 that its key would not fit in 64 bits (a time in nanoseconds stored as microseconds, say) is not folded, and the refresh says so in the log. For tables rows are added to , such as logs: a change that leaves a bucket's count as it was (an UPDATE , or a delete and an insert into one bucket between two refreshes) is never seen by a plain or a FULL refresh, only by REFRESH ROLLUP name REBUILD . Its table and the one it reads stay as the rollup needs them. DROP TABLE of a table a rollup reads is refused while the rollup is there ( DROP ROLLUP first, or drop both in one DROP TABLE logs, logs_5m ); DROP TABLE of a rollup's own table drops its definition too. ALTER TABLE that drops or renames a column a rollup reads, or that changes a rollup's own table, is refused. The definition is kept as a stored function , rollup__<name>() , whose body is the statement, so it needs nothing new on disk and travels with the database. On a server with shards, each shard keeps a rollup of its own rows; rollups through a coordinator come later."],
["sql.html#subqueries-with-and-joins","ChloeDB's query language","Subqueries, WITH and joins","Each plans into operators over the single-table queries above ( EXPLAIN shows them), all run on one snapshot, so every single-table query uses the indexes. Each is bound as it is built, never printed as SQL and parsed again: IN (SELECT ...) and NOT IN run the subquery first and filter with its values (a multi-valued column contributes each value). EXISTS is true or false, and a scalar subquery must return one column and at most one row. An EXISTS or NOT EXISTS in WHERE whose subquery names the outer row by one equality, WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer = c.id AND o.total > 100) , is a semi join (an anti join for NOT EXISTS ): it runs as c.id IN (SELECT o.customer FROM orders o WHERE o.total > 100) , the subquery once, with NULL s as EXISTS has them ( NOT EXISTS keeps an outer row whose c.id is NULL ). The subquery may have a LIMIT , not groups, aggregates or an OFFSET ; one that names the outer row anywhere else is an error. A subquery's values reach the outer query's filter as values, so its indexes serve it: year > (SELECT AVG(year) ...) is a range of year . A scalar subquery that names a column of the outer row is correlated : it ranks, and is not a condition. WITH results and FROM (SELECT ...) are read like a table. A WITH whose rows are rows of the table (no aggregates) keeps their keys, so an outer query over it reads the table restricted to those rows, in the WITH 's order unless it orders them itself, and (SELECT id FROM top) can seed a graph. The outer query may name any of the table's columns ( SELECT id, category FROM top ), while * is the WITH 's own columns, as in any SQL. A score() or hops() the WITH selects ( score() AS s ) is read from its rows, by name or by * . A column a WITH or a FROM (SELECT ...) selects as s.item , without an alias, is its column item , as SQL names it, so the query reading it says ls.item ; two columns of one name keep their tables' ( s.id , p.id ). WITH RECURSIVE is not supported: REACHABLE and PATH walk links. Joins are JOIN (inner), LEFT JOIN , FULL [OUTER] JOIN , and RIGHT JOIN as the first join of a FROM ( a RIGHT JOIN b ON ... runs as b LEFT JOIN a ON ... , and * keeps a 's columns first), of a table with another, with itself or with a WITH result; name each side ( papers a JOIN papers b ). ON needs an equality between the sides, of two columns of one kind: KEYWORD , or INT and FLOAT compared as numbers ( p.venue = a.id , an INT with a KEYWORD , is an error). A multi-valued column joins each of its values. Comparing an EDGES field with the key follows its links ( a.cites = b.id : b is a row a links to; b.cites = a.id : b links to a ); links name keys of their own table, so they join a table with itself. Each WHERE or ON condition on one side filters that side's scan, and the second side reads only the rows whose join values the first side holds ( b.id IN (...) , or for links the rows linking to them), before a hash join in memory. Groups of the second side's rows, a GROUP BY of its key (and of other columns of its rows) that aggregates only fields of the first side and is ordered by that key among others, are made in the first side's scan, by its join key, before the join ( SELECT p.id, p.title, COUNT(*) AS n FROM reviews r JOIN products p ON r.product_id = p.id GROUP BY p.id, p.title ORDER BY n DESC, p.id ): the join reads a row per group rather than per row. Text, vector and graph functions name one side ( MATCH(a.body, ...) ); ORDER BY a score ranks by that side's scan, and when the order is the first side's, scans of a growing number of its rows stop once LIMIT rows join. Joined columns of the answer are named by their expression ( b.title ) unless aliased, where PostgreSQL names them by the column alone ( title ): pandas and chloedb.dbapi 's cursor.description see b.title , so alias a column whose name a program reads ( b.title AS title ). FULL JOIN gives the rows of a LEFT JOIN , then each row of the right side that matched no row of the left, with NULL s for the left's columns, in the right's order ( ORDER BY orders them as any rows): SELECT coalesce(o.sku, s.sku) AS sku, o.qty, s.qty FROM orders o FULL JOIN stock s ON o.sku = s.sku . A NULL key matches nothing, so its row comes once, from its own side. Since both sides keep their unmatched rows, no condition filters either side's scan: ON 's other conditions decide which pairs join (a row they part from every match keeps its NULL s), and WHERE filters the joined rows, so WHERE o.sku IS NULL keeps the stock no order names. Both sides are read whole, without the restriction to the first side's join values or the growing scan of a LIMIT . Joins after it join its rows as they join any. It runs on the operators (DataFusion leaves it to them), and through a coordinator, which joins the rows its shards answer; a plan with one needs the plan feature full_join . Over joined or derived rows, WHERE , GROUP BY , aggregates, HAVING , ORDER BY , DISTINCT and LIMIT also take arithmetic, || , LIKE , ILIKE , CASE , COALESCE , LOWER , UPPER , LENGTH , ABS , ROUND , DOT , HASH and the numeric functions of ranking expressions ( ln , pow , least , ...). So does the SELECT list over one table ( SELECT id, rating * 2, lower(category), dot(emb, :q) FROM papers WHERE ... ): the table's query finds the rows and the columns are computed in memory, typed as over derived rows. A SELECT that also selects a ranking's value ( bm25(..) * 2 , hops() ) computes its columns as ranking expressions do, as FLOAT s. Their conditions follow the same three-valued logic as a single table's, multi-valued columns included ( NOT (b.tags = 'red') keeps rows without tags). Integer arithmetic is exact, and a result too big for an integer is a FLOAT ; / gives a FLOAT , and dividing by zero gives NULL . || with a NULL gives NULL , as in PostgreSQL ( coalesce(note, '') keeps the rest), and LIKE with a NULL side is NULL . ROUND(x) gives an INT : of a FLOAT it rounds half to even, as PostgreSQL rounds a double precision ( ROUND(price) of 2.5 is 2 and of 3.5 is 4), and of a number written in the statement, or of a value made of such numbers alone ( ROUND(CASE WHEN qty > 0 THEN 2.5 END) ), half away from zero, as PostgreSQL rounds a numeric ( ROUND(2.5) is 3); ROUND(x, digits) , ROUND(x, 0) included, rounds half away from zero, as PostgreSQL's round(numeric, int) does. A ranking expression's round rounds half away from zero, as it always has. ORDER BY puts rows without a value last in either direction unless NULLS FIRST , as over a single table. Engines. ChloeDB's own operators run these in memory, a row at a time. With the feature datafusion , which chloedb-server and the Python package turn on and ChloeCtx leaves off, DataFusion runs them on column batches once their single-table queries read more than 4,096 rows; below that the operators answer, faster on so few, reusing the rows DataFusion's attempt already read. Both give the same rows in the same order, and the same sums of floats, which both add exactly ( aggregates ). On two tables of 100,000 rows, a join with a group took 285 ms on the operators and 60 ms on DataFusion. On two tables of 100 rows it took 0.6 ms on the operators and 2.9 ms on DataFusion; the default engine ( auto ) answered in 1.2 ms. Measured with chloedb/examples/df_join.rs on a 4-core cloud Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 2026-11-01. CHLOEDB_ENGINE sets the engine: auto : the default; operators ; datafusion : DataFusion, whatever the size; datafusion-only : a query DataFusion does not run fails, to find what it lacks. The operators run what DataFusion does not run yet, and the plan's first line says why. Today that is joins along links, multi-valued columns as sort keys, correlated subqueries, and maxsim or encode over a subquery. chloedb-server runs them on its engine over its own tables and as a coordinator , which runs them above the rows its shards answer for each single-table query: on DataFusion, as Arrow columns from shards of protocol 10 . - On the operators, each single-table query, WITH result and side of a join holds at most 1,000,000 rows. On DataFusion they hold as many rows as the query's memory budget allows, and past it sorts and groups spill to disk. CROSS joins, and a RIGHT JOIN after another join, are not supported. The executor's plan lines ( --plan , /v1/explain 's plan ) list each single-table query that ran, with its rows and its own plan."],
["sql.html#window-functions","ChloeDB's query language","Window functions","A window function gives each row a value computed over other rows, its partition , without folding them into one as GROUP BY does: a row's rank among its category's, the value of the row before, a running total, a moving average. Functions. ROW_NUMBER() ; RANK() and DENSE_RANK() ; PERCENT_RANK() and CUME_DIST() , FLOAT s; NTILE(n) , buckets 1 to n as even as they can be, the first ones a row larger (as SQL says, and PostgreSQL and SQLite do); LAG(x [, offset [, default]]) and LEAD(..) , x of the row offset rows before or after (1 by default), or default ( NULL without one) past the partition's ends; FIRST_VALUE(x) , LAST_VALUE(x) and NTH_VALUE(x, n) of the row's frame; and the aggregates COUNT(*) , COUNT(x) , SUM , AVG , MIN and MAX of the frame. NTILE 's, NTH_VALUE 's and LAG 's numbers are constants. COUNT(DISTINCT ..) , FILTER (WHERE ..) and IGNORE NULLS are errors. OVER (PARTITION BY .. ORDER BY ..) . The partition is the rows with equal values of its expressions ( NULL equal to NULL ); without PARTITION BY it is every row. The window's ORDER BY orders the partition, its NULL s last unless NULLS FIRST as elsewhere; rows whose order keys are all equal are peers , and rows that tie keep the order they came in. Both take expressions of the rows ( PARTITION BY year % 10 , ORDER BY lower(title) ), or over groups of the groups' values and aggregates. Frames. FIRST_VALUE , LAST_VALUE , NTH_VALUE and the aggregates read the rows of the row's frame: ROWS BETWEEN start AND end counts rows from the row, and RANGE BETWEEN start AND end takes the rows whose value of the window's one ORDER BY key is within the offsets of the row's (a row without a value has its peers, the other rows without one). The ends are UNBOUNDED PRECEDING , n PRECEDING , CURRENT ROW (in RANGE , the row's first or last peer), n FOLLOWING and UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING , in that order; ROWS 2 PRECEDING is ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW . Without a frame, a window with an ORDER BY reads from the partition's first row to the row's last peer, so SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY day) is a running total that counts a day's rows together, and a window without one reads the whole partition. The other functions read the partition whatever the frame says. GROUPS frames are errors. Where they go. A window function is a column of SELECT , a key of ORDER BY , or part of a condition of QUALIFY , which filters the rows after the windows are computed and may name a selected window by its alias ( QUALIFY r <= 3 ). It is not part of WHERE , GROUP BY or HAVING , which come before, and a window function's argument, partition or order holds no other: compute that one in a subquery in FROM . In a grouped query the windows are computed over the groups HAVING keeps; SELECT DISTINCT , ORDER BY and LIMIT come after them, so COUNT(*) OVER () ... LIMIT 5 counts every row. WINDOW w AS (..) names a window that OVER w uses as it is, and that OVER (w ORDER BY ..) or OVER (w ROWS ..) extends with an order or a frame it lacks; a window may name one before it ( WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY a), v AS (w ORDER BY b) ). Engines. The operators sort each partition in memory; DataFusion runs windows on column batches, sorting and spilling within the query's memory budget, and both answer alike. The operators run a window DataFusion does not lower yet: a LAG or LEAD whose default is not a constant of the value's type, FIRST_VALUE and LAST_VALUE over a RANGE frame with an offset, NTH_VALUE over a RANGE frame that starts after the partition's first row, a RANGE offset of another type than its key ( 0.5 over an INT ), and windows over multi-valued columns. The plan's node is Window ( Window: RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY cites DESC) in EXPLAIN , the functions computed after the rows' own columns), and its JSON needs the plan feature window ; a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the plan by that name."],
["sql.html#union-intersect-and-except","ChloeDB's query language","UNION, INTERSECT and EXCEPT","A set operation takes the rows of two queries together: UNION the rows of either, INTERSECT those of both, EXCEPT those of the first that the second lacks. Columns. Both sides select as many columns, matched by place, and the answer's columns are named as the first side's. Rows are equal when each of their values is, NULL equal to NULL and an INT equal to the FLOAT of its value. ALL . Without it each row comes once. With it, UNION ALL keeps every row of both sides; INTERSECT ALL keeps a row as many times as both sides have it, the fewer of the two counts; EXCEPT ALL as many times as the first side has it more than the second. Order. INTERSECT comes before UNION and EXCEPT , which go left to right, and parentheses group sides: a UNION b INTERSECT c is a UNION (b INTERSECT c) . ORDER BY , LIMIT and OFFSET at the end apply to the whole and name its columns; a side in parentheses takes its own. Without an ORDER BY the rows come in the sides' order: UNION ALL the first side's rows, then the second's; UNION those rows each where it first comes; INTERSECT and EXCEPT rows of the first side in its order, the first ones of rows equal to one another. Anywhere a query goes : a WITH result, a derived table FROM (SELECT .. UNION ..) d , or a subquery x IN (SELECT .. UNION ..) , and each side may group, join, rank, or compute windows. UNION BY NAME is an error. Engines. Both answer alike. The operators run a set operation DataFusion does not lower: one whose sides give a column different types (an INT and a FLOAT ), and INTERSECT , EXCEPT or UNION without ALL of a multi-valued column. The plan's node is SetOp ( Union , Intersect all and so on in EXPLAIN , the sides below it), and its JSON needs the plan feature set_ops ; a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the plan by that name."],
["sql.html#the-first-row-of-each-group-distinct-on","ChloeDB's query language","The first row of each group: DISTINCT ON","SELECT DISTINCT ON (keys) ... keeps, of the rows with equal keys, the first in the query's order, as PostgreSQL does: a row per group, the groups in the order of their first rows, and LIMIT and OFFSET count groups. With rows of a long video or document in a table of segments, it answers each video once, by its best segment: Keys are expressions of the rows ( video , lower(lang) , year % 10 ), or columns of the answer by name or number ( DISTINCT ON (2) ), and need not be selected. Keys are equal when their values are: NULL with NULL , and a multi-valued column's list with the same list. The order. PostgreSQL requires ORDER BY to start with the keys; ChloeDB does not, so ORDER BY a score gives the best row of each group, the best groups first. When ORDER BY does start with the keys, the answer is PostgreSQL's. Without ORDER BY , which row of a group is kept is not defined. Reads. When the query's order is the scan's (a score, or one numeric column), a LIMIT reads the scan in growing slices: four times the rows asked for (at least 32), then four times as many, until that many groups have a row or the scan runs out. A group with many rows near the top costs more reads, never a wrong answer. DISTINCT ON one keyword or INT field of a table ranked by bm25 reads less: each segment keeps each group's first row as it scores ( MAX k PER does the same with k rows), so the scan returns the rows asked for. Ordered by several columns, the rows are sorted in memory, and the scan holds at most 1,000,000 rows. Shards. A coordinator keeps the first rows over its shards' merged rows, so three shards give what one database does, whether or not the table is placed by the keys ( shard_by ). The plan's node is DistinctOn ( Distinct on: video in EXPLAIN ), and its JSON needs the plan features operators and distinct_on ; a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the plan by that name."],
["sql.html#at-most-k-rows-of-a-value-max-k-per","ChloeDB's query language","At most k rows of a value: MAX k PER","ORDER BY ... MAX k PER field keeps, of the rows with equal values of field (a KEYWORD or a number of one value: a TEXT field is an error, as rows rarely share a whole text), the first k in the query's order, and the rows kept stay in that order: a ranking with at most two clips of a video, three products of a brand or two arguments of a debate. LIMIT and OFFSET count the rows kept. It ends ORDER BY , and needs one: the rows a value keeps are its first in that order. k is a whole number, 1 or more, and MAX 1 PER x is DISTINCT ON (x) , with the same plan. A field of one value. field is a field of the rows ( video , t.brand in a join), and NULL is one value of its own. A multi-valued field is refused, since a row with several values would be in several groups. Not with DISTINCT or DISTINCT ON , which already keep one row of each. Reads. A table's own rows ranked by bm25 and capped by a keyword or INT field keep the cap as they are scored: each segment keeps each value's best k rows (and the rows tied with its k-th) and skips the rows that can no longer reach the LIMIT , so the query reads its rows once. Other rankings, and caps over joined or derived rows or by other fields, read growing slices of the ranking until LIMIT rows are kept or it runs out, as DISTINCT ON does. A field whose values cannot fill the LIMIT , such as one row of each of 15 topics for a LIMIT of 20, reads every row that matches either way, and returns 15. Shards. A coordinator keeps each value's first rows over its shards' merged rows, so three shards give what one database does. The plan's node is DistinctOn with per ( At most 2 per: video in EXPLAIN ). With k above 1 its JSON needs the plan feature per_cap , so a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the plan rather than keeping one row of each value. Python's builder writes it .max_per(2, \"video\") . On ArguAna (BEIR's 8,674 arguments from 592 debates on 15 topics; a document's debate and topic are in its id), every one of the 1,406 queries ranked by bm25(body, :q) , its own argument left out as BEIR scores it, top 20. Times are the median over queries of each query's median of 3 runs, one thread on a 4-core cloud VM (Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz): top 20 nDCG@10 debates topics p50 ms p99 ms uncapped 0.489 7.5 3.8 1.6 4.1 MAX 1 PER debate 0.307 20.0 7.2 2.4 5.4 MAX 2 PER debate 0.379 14.5 5.9 2.3 5.0 MAX 3 PER debate 0.419 12.6 5.4 2.3 5.5 MAX 1 PER topic 0.301 14.9 15.0 2.8 5.7 MAX 2 PER topic 0.376 16.3 11.4 2.6 5.4 MAX 3 PER topic 0.416 14.2 9.1 2.4 5.4 Debates and topics are the mean number of each in a query's answer. Each segment keeps the cap as it scores, so a cap costs about 1.5 times the uncapped query whatever the field: BM25 can skip fewer rows, since the 20th row kept scores lower than the 20th of all. One row of each of the 15 topics cannot fill 20 rows, so that query scores every argument that matches, once, and returns 15. Before the scan kept the cap, the same queries read growing slices of the ranking: 6.0 to 6.1 ms by debate, 7.1 and 18.8 ms for three and two per topic, and 549 ms for one per topic, which read every row again at each slice (with the uncapped query at 3.3 ms in that run). Caps trade relevance for spread. ArguAna's answer to a query is the argument against it, in the query's own debate, so a cap on debates also caps how many of the right debate's arguments the ranking can show: nDCG@10 falls from 0.489 to 0.307 with one per debate, and to 0.419 with three. The run is chloedb-per-cap-bench ( results/text/per_cap.json )."],
["sql.html#as-much-as-fits-pack-n-tokens","ChloeDB's query language","As much as fits: PACK n TOKENS","Experimental: what PACK reads and how it chooses may change between releases. Against the top rows of a ranking cut in the client ( measured on SciFact and NFCorpus), it reaches more of the relevant documents when the budget holds one to three (SciFact 0.74 against 0.65 at 500 tokens) and spends less of the budget on them, and the two are the same once it holds five or more. A query's answer is often the context a language model is given, and the model has a budget of tokens, not of rows. PACK n TOKENS at the end of a SELECT fills that budget: it reads the query's rows in its order and decides each once, so that a long row, a row that repeats one already taken and a row linked to one taken are weighed inside the query rather than by the application after a top k. Each row, in the query's order, is: taken whole when its text fits what is left of the budget; excerpted when it is longer than one row may be ( EXCERPT n ; a quarter of the budget by default) or than what is left: the stretch of its longest text column with the most terms of the query's bm25 and MATCH texts (or its start, when it has none), marked … where it was cut. Terms are found as the column's analyzer makes them (stemmed for english , dictionary words for Chinese and Japanese under standard ), and the text is cut between words, or inside a run without spaces (Chinese, Japanese, Thai, a URL, base64), so an excerpt never spends more than its room; skipped as a near-duplicate of a row taken before it, with DEDUPE x : at least the share x of the shorter one's terms (as its analyzer makes them) are in the other, or, with DEDUPE x ON emb , the cosine of their vectors is at least x; skipped as over budget when not even an excerpt of 32 tokens fits; skipped as no text when none of its counted columns holds text. With FOLLOW edges n HOP (1 to 3 hops, 1 by default), each row taken brings the rows its EDGES field reaches, up to 16, right after it in the order of its edges, if they pass the query's WHERE , decided the same way ( linked from k ). The rows are read by key, a hop at a time, so FOLLOW works through a coordinator, where graph walks do not: a decision a file note links to is taken with the note, though it shares no word with the task. The answer is the rows taken, in that order, with two more columns: pack_tokens , the tokens the row spends, and pack_decision ( take , excerpt , linked from k ). WITH RECEIPT adds the rows skipped ( skip: near-duplicate of k , skip: over budget , skip: no text ), with their text columns NULL , so that the text of the rows answered is still what fits the budget, and the columns pack_score , the query's score of the row, and pack_used , the tokens spent up to and with it. In Rust, Db::pack answers the rows and a receipt with every decision and the snapshot read ( txn and seq_no ). The budget is a whole number from 1 to 10,000,000, or a parameter that holds one: PACK :budget TOKENS . The text counted is every column a row read holds text in, but the key's, or the columns OF a, b names. Tokens are an estimate ( chloedb::query::pack::count_tokens ), as the real count depends on the model's tokenizer: a run of Latin letters is a token per six letters, a run of another alphabet's (Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic, Hebrew, the scripts of India) a token per three, a run of digits a token per three, a CJK or Thai character or a punctuation mark one each. Against OpenAI's cl100k_base and o200k_base it counts English and code 13 to 27% high, the safe side; other scripts are counted high against o200k_base but low against cl100k_base, by up to 2.4 times for Hindi ( measured ). Reads. The rows are read in growing reads, the first 64, then the first 320, 1,344 and so on, each from OFFSET , and the rows seen before are passed over by their keys; reading stops once what is left of the budget is too small for any row, or once 16 rows in a row were skipped over budget (what is left is too small for the rows the query finds, such as a title and a description, which need room for both and an excerpt; the rows already read are still decided), so a query whose first rows fill the budget reads once. A deeper read rather than the next page, because a fused ranking ( rrf , a weighted sum) ranks its candidates to a depth that grows with the LIMIT : a deeper read is a better ranking of the same rows, and one that comes back short is the end of the ranking. (In a table without a primary key the reads are pages instead.) LIMIT is the most candidates read (1,000 without one), and OFFSET where they start. Every read, and every row followed, is a query of its own on the same snapshot: on a server, a lease held for the pack (kept for the client with hold , or the client's own snapshot ), and the statement is admitted once, by its text, and held to one deadline. Through a coordinator each shard reads its latest version at each read. Choosing is greedy in the query's order. A row that does not fit whole is excerpted rather than skipped, so that one long log cannot spend the budget, and a smaller row after one over the budget is still taken. Where. On a file, a server and a coordinator alike: a coordinator merges each read from its shards, and packs. A key limited to tables runs it as it runs the query without the clause. PACK inside a subquery is not the clause ( pack stays a name there, and anywhere it is not followed by a number and TOKENS )."],
["sql.html#graphs","ChloeDB's query language","Graphs","Rows with an EDGES field are nodes of a graph. Graph predicates and scores take seeds : a key ( 'p1' ), a parameter, a tuple of keys ( ('p1', 'p2') ), or a subquery, whose filters, ranking and LIMIT are those of any query. A subquery over the same table seeds with its rows; one over another table returns one column, whose values are the keys to start from, such as the items a user's recent events name: ppr(cooc, (SELECT item FROM events WHERE usr = :u ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 20)) . With weighted => 1 , a seed's restart weight is its row's score, summed over the rows that name it: ppr(cooc, (SELECT item FROM events WHERE usr = :u ORDER BY w DESC), weighted => 1) starts from each item in proportion to the sum of its events' w , and ordering the events by decay(ts, ...) instead weighs recent ones more. function what it is options (defaults) REACHABLE(f, seeds, …) rows v with min_hops ≤ d (seeds, v ) ≤ hops hops 1 , min_hops 1 , dir 'out' (or 'in' , 'both' ), label 'a,b' , through <predicate> , max_nodes 100000 PATH(f, from, to, …) the rows of one shortest (unweighted) path max_hops 6 , dir , label , through , max_nodes hops() a row's distance from the seeds, or its position on the path; ORDER BY hops() is nearest first ppr(f, seeds, …) personalized PageRank, usable wherever a score is, including rrf and weighted sums alpha 0.15 , eps 1e-4 (a row is pushed while its residual is at least eps times its number of edges, so scaling every weight alike changes nothing), dir 'both' , label , max_pushes (a walk cut there says so: max_pushes_reached in the stats), weighted 0 ( 1 : the seed subquery's scores are the restart weights) a VECTOR field as f REACHABLE and PATH follow similarity edges: a row's neighbours are its k most similar rows k 10 , min_sim Graph queries run on one database: a coordinator refuses them, since a row's edges point at rows other shards hold ( graphs on a cluster ). The query console 's Graph tab builds these queries (seeds from keys, a search or a condition; links or similarity; neighbourhood, path or PageRank) and draws the answer. through restricts the rows a traversal may pass through (seeds and end points are exempt); the rest of WHERE filters the rows it ends at. max_nodes bounds the rows a traversal meets besides its seeds (and a path's ends): a REACHABLE that would meet more, or a PATH that meets more before it finds a path, is an error saying so, never a part of the answer. max_hops => 0 is a path of no hops (a seed that is also a target); max_nodes and k are 1 or more. REACHABLE(f, seeds, hops => 0, min_hops => 0) is the seed set itself, so NOT of it removes the seeds from a result. hops() refers to the first REACHABLE or PATH in WHERE . The Graphs page explains how edges are stored and what these queries cost. Edges are written as part of their row: a JSON array of target keys, each optionally with a label and a positive weight."],
["sql.html#edge-tables","ChloeDB's query language","Edge tables","An EDGES field belongs to its source row, so adding one edge rewrites the row, its text and its vectors. A graph kept up as events arrive, such as items seen together, goes in an edge table instead: a table whose rows are edges, declared with OPTIONS(edges = 'src, dst, w') . A walk over the table whose keys it holds names the edge table where it would name a field: Each row is an edge from the row whose key is in src to the row whose key is in dst , weighted by w , or by 1 without a weight column ( edges = 'src, dst' ). src and dst are stored, single-valued KEYWORD or INT columns of the type of the other table's key, and the weight is a stored FLOAT or INT . If src references a table, walks are over that table. Writing an edge writes its row and nothing else: insert it, delete it, or add to its weight with an add operation, whose doc creates the row on the edge's first event ( writes ). Edges are directed, from src to dst . For a click on item i after the user's last items h ₁ … h ₅ ( h ₁ the most recent), adding 1/ g to the edge h _g → i records what comes after what: ppr(cooc, :x, dir => 'out') and REACHABLE(cooc, :x, dir => 'out') follow the items that came after x , and dir => 'both' , ppr 's default, reads them as seen together. That is five add operations per event. For the next item alone, counts of what came after the last one or two items are cheaper and exact ( Next items ). An edge is followed while its weight, as it is now, is above 0. UPDATE cooc SET w = w * 0.9 decays every weight in place, and an edge whose weight is 0 or NULL stays in the table without being followed. src and dst are never changed in place: an UPDATE of a KEYWORD one rewrites the row, and set , add or an UPDATE of an INT one is refused, as for shard_by (delete the row and insert it again). REACHABLE , PATH , hops() and ppr take an edge table with every option but label , since its edges have none. A walk over cooc and a walk over an EDGES field holding the same edges and weights find the same rows at the same hops and the same PageRank, up to rounding. Each segment of the edge table indexes its rows by the keys they link, which flushes and merges build, so a walk finds a row's edges with one lookup of its key in each of the edge table's segments ( how, and what it costs )."],
["sql.html#stored-functions","ChloeDB's query language","Stored functions","A function is kept in the database under a name, and any query calls it as it calls a built-in one. Its body is SQL: an expression of its parameters for a function that returns a value, a SELECT for one that returns rows ( RETURNS TABLE ). A function that returns rows stands where another database has a view ( CREATE VIEW is refused here): CREATE FUNCTION spend_by_country() RETURNS TABLE AS SELECT country, SUM(total) AS spend FROM invoices GROUP BY country , read with SELECT * FROM spend_by_country() , joined or filtered like a table, and it may take parameters and keeps its versions. The definition : CREATE [OR REPLACE] FUNCTION name(parameter TYPE [DEFAULT value], ...) RETURNS TYPE | RETURNS TABLE [LANGUAGE sql] [OPTIONS (description = '...')] AS body , the body also between $$ and $$ . Types are INT , FLOAT , TEXT , KEYWORD , BOOL , TIMESTAMP , JSON , VECTOR or VECTOR(n) , and KEYWORD[] and INT[] ; a default is a number, a quoted string, TRUE , FALSE or NULL , and the parameters after one with a default have one too. In the body a parameter is :name , as in a rank profile , so the body runs as written as a query with the same parameters. A body that returns a value reads only its parameters: a column is passed in as an argument. Names are letters, digits and _ , in any case; the name of a built-in function ( lower , bm25 , coalesce , ...) is refused, and there is one function of a name, whatever its parameters. CREATE checks that a RETURNS TABLE body plans against the tables, its parameters given their defaults or a value of their type. A call is written out : binding replaces each call with its body, the arguments in place of the parameters, so the query plans and ranks as if it were written out (indexes, score() and a shard's pushdown included), and EXPLAIN begins with calls stored function boosted@2 . Arguments are by position, then by name ( w => 0.5 ); a missing one takes its default. A literal argument of another type than its parameter's is refused; any other expression is computed as the body would with it, and the value is the body's ( RETURNS FLOAT of :a + :b with two INT s is an INT ). A function that returns rows is called in FROM , alone, joined, or in WITH , and its arguments are values or parameters of the query, not columns. A function may call others, up to 16 deep. Versions never change : CREATE writes version 1; CREATE OR REPLACE the next version, or nothing when the definition is the current one's; a new version returns what the old one did (a value, or rows). DROP FUNCTION writes a tombstone version, so version numbers keep rising and \"f@2\" always means one body. A query calls the current version, the highest that is not a tombstone, or pins one with its name and version in double quotes, which still runs after a drop. CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f FROM VERSION n makes a new version with version n 's definition; DROP FUNCTION f VERSION n deletes an old version (not the current one). Kept as rows : each version is a row of the system table _functions ( id name@version , name , version , language , kind , signature , body , hash , description , created , created_by , dropped ), written by its own transaction, so it is logged, numbered and shipped to replicas as writes are, and checkpoints and copies carry it. SELECT * FROM _functions reads it; it is not one of the database's tables, and only CREATE and DROP FUNCTION write it. The first function adds it, and the database then needs the format 3 feature functions , which a version of ChloeDB from before stored functions refuses by name. A database from before field ids keeps none (upgrade it first). From Rust , Db::sql runs both statements, Db::define_function runs a parsed one and records who wrote it, and Db::functions lists every version. On chloedb-server , CREATE and DROP FUNCTION need an admin key, whose name is the version's created_by ; any key that may read calls them. GET /v1/functions lists each function with its current version and every version's definition. Through a coordinator , a version has one number and one body on every shard: CREATE and DROP FUNCTION ask every replica which versions it holds, number the new one past all of them, and write it on each shard, so they wait for every replica to answer. A coordinator calls the versions every replica holds alike (a new version once every shard has it) and stops calling a dropped function as soon as any replica holds the drop. A shard added to the cluster gets every version under its number. Each shard records the coordinator's credential as created_by ."],
["sql.html#language-models-llm","ChloeDB's query language","Language models: llm()","llm(model, instructions, input, ...) is a language model's answer to the instructions about the inputs, for each row a query returns. A stored function keeps the prompt and the model under a name, versioned as any function is: a new prompt or model is a new version, \"summarize@1\"(body) still asks the old one, and FROM VERSION n rolls back. Where it runs. A query that calls llm() runs in two steps: the query without the calls finds its rows (its WHERE , ORDER BY and LIMIT as written), computing what the calls read of each row, and then the model is asked about those rows, eight at once, and the columns are computed of the answers. A call may be part of a column, in arithmetic, || , comparisons, CASE and functions of values; a CASE asks only for the branch it takes, and coalesce only where the values before the call are NULL . llm() is a column of SELECT or part of ORDER BY ; anywhere else ( WHERE , GROUP BY , HAVING , a join, a WITH , a subquery, UNION ) it is refused, as it would run for rows the query reads rather than returns. In ORDER BY the calls rank the rows the query finds without that ORDER BY and its LIMIT : the top rows of a first ranking, as a rerank phase ranks them. Rows that tie keep the first ranking's order, a NULL ranks last, LIMIT and OFFSET cut the ranked rows, and score() is the first key's value. Arguments. model is a string: anthropic/<model> (a Claude model, its key in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ), openai/<chat model> , or the name of a model directory whose endpoint.json names a chat server ( \"api\": \"chat\" for OpenAI's /chat/completions , as vLLM, Ollama and LiteLLM serve it, or \"api\": \"anthropic\" ), as for text made of text . A directory's name is letters, digits, _ , - and . (two such names joined by / at most): an absolute path, .. or a backslash is refused, so a query reads no endpoint.json outside the models directory, and an endpoint.json 's key_env names a provider's key variable or a CHLOEDB_MODEL_KEY_* one. A query or a row cannot choose the model. The instructions are the system prompt, a string or a parameter; each input is a part of the user's message, apart by a blank line, and a NULL input is left out. A call with no input is NULL , and asks nothing. Answers are text, trimmed. choices => 'a,b,c' asks for one of them and gives it as written there (the answer in any case, or the one choice an answer that says more names), else NULL . returns => 'number' gives the first number of the answer as a FLOAT , and returns => 'bool' its yes or no as a BOOL , else NULL . max_tokens => n bounds the answer (1024 for text, 64 otherwise). The model answers with its own sampling defaults: no temperature is sent (current Claude models refuse one). Cost. Each distinct question (model, prompt and input) is asked once, and its answer kept in memory (10,000 of them), so the same query again asks nothing. A query asks about 100 rows at most, and a process makes 600 requests a minute at most for llm() : past either, the query fails before asking (chloedb-server's --llm-rows and --llm-per-minute , chloedb::query::llm::set_limits from Rust). A failed request fails the query (503), after the endpoint's retries. The answer's plan lines end with one that says how many rows were asked about, how many requests were made, and how many answers came from memory; EXPLAIN shows the plan of the rows. Inputs are data. A row's text is the user's message and the instructions the system prompt, and the model has no tools, so a row's text can change that row's answer and nothing else. A coordinator asks the model about the merged rows, on the coordinator, so no shard asks about a row the merge cuts, and its shards need no key. A body that calls llm() is a function that returns a value: CREATE refuses one in a RETURNS TABLE body, and checks each call's model and options. chloedb-server counts the requests in chloedb_llm_requests_total , chloedb_llm_cached_total and chloedb_llm_failures_total ."],
["sql.html#classifiers-create-classifier-and-classify","ChloeDB's query language","Classifiers: CREATE CLASSIFIER and classify()","Experimental. This is new in 0.3.0, and its options and answers may change in a later release. A classifier learns a table's labels from its rows' embeddings, and is kept as a stored function of a vector: versioned, rolled back and dropped as any function is, and held by every shard of a cluster. It classifies rows by their stored vectors, and a query's text by the same model's vector of it. Training. CREATE CLASSIFIER name ON table(vector_field) LABELS column [OPTIONS (...)] [WHERE condition] reads SELECT vector_field, column FROM table WHERE column IS NOT NULL [AND condition] , at most max_rows rows (200,000), keeps the labels with at least min_rows rows (2), and holds out a share of them ( holdout , 0.2, chosen by a hash of each vector, so the same rows every time) to measure the model it keeps. method = 'logistic' (the default) fits softmax regression (Adam, epochs 30, learning_rate 0.01, l2 0.0001); method = 'centroid' takes each label's mean vector, in one pass. The labels are the values of a KEYWORD , TEXT , INT or BOOL column, and the field a VECTOR field of one vector a row; a classifier has 10,000 labels at most. The statement answers one row: labels , rows , train_accuracy , holdout_rows , holdout_accuracy , took_ms . A coordinator reads the rows from its shards, trains, and writes the function on every shard. The function is name(v VECTOR, returns TEXT DEFAULT 'label') RETURNS KEYWORD , its body a call of classify(:v, model, returns => :returns) , model the classifier as text: its labels, a row of weights a label quantized to 8 bits with a scale of its own, and a bias (about a kilobyte a label for a 512-number vector), and the table, field and embedding model it was trained on. name(v) is the label with the highest probability (the softmax of the scores), NULL for a NULL vector or one below the classifier's min_score option (0); name(v, 'score') that probability; name(v, 'labels') the best label and those whose probability is at least min_score and a tenth, best first. Where it runs. A call of a value of no row, a parameter's vector, is computed before the query is planned, so it stands anywhere: WHERE , ORDER BY , a join (its 'labels' , a list, is a column of the rows only). A call of a row's vector is a column of the rows a query returns, or ranks them in ORDER BY , as llm() is: computed after the query finds them, for 100,000 rows at most. To filter or group rows by their class, keep it in a column: a KEYWORD field OPTIONS (derive = 'title', model = 'classifier:topic') holds the label of each row written ( keywords made of text ). Text. Db::classify(name, text, k) (Rust) and POST /v1/classify {\"name\", \"text\", \"k\"} embed a text with the model of the field the classifier was trained on and give its k best labels with their probabilities. Measured on Wayfair's WANDS ( chloedb-classify-eval , chloedb/results/text/classify_wands.json , an Intel Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 4 cores): 42,994 products, emb potion-retrieval-32M's vector of a product's name, 501 product classes. The logistic classifier names the class of 81.9% of held-out products (centroid 81.3%) and the class of 62.9% of the 480 queries (centroid 51.0%; among its best three, 80.4%), at 0.07 ms a query's text, embedding included; it trains in 31 s (centroid 2 s), and a row's class costs 40 µs. As a boost, bm25(body, :q) * (1 + 0.1 * CASE WHEN class = topic(:v) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) raised nDCG@10 from 0.433 to 0.439 (p = 0.002, the same on the odd-numbered queries not used to choose the weight), and a weight of 0.5 lowered it: a wrong class costs the rows that are right."],
["sql.html#recommenders-create-recommender-and-recommend","ChloeDB's query language","Recommenders: CREATE RECOMMENDER and recommend()","A recommender keeps counts of a table of events (what came right after what, what was seen together, how often each item was seen, each user's last items) as the events are written, and recommend(name, user) ranks items for a user by them, fused with the similarity of the user's recent items and with popularity, leaving out the items the user had. The definition. CREATE [OR REPLACE] RECOMMENDER name ON events(user, item, time) [ITEMS items[(vector_field)]] [OPTIONS (window = 3, history = 20)] . The user and the item are stored, single-valued KEYWORD or INT columns of the events table, and the time a TIMESTAMP , INT or FLOAT column (a NULL time places the event last). ITEMS names the table whose keys the items are (of the item column's type), and its VECTOR field, if any, for the similar items. window (1 to 50) is how many events apart two items still count as seen together, and history (1 to 1,000) how many of a user's last events are kept, recommended from and left out of the answer. OR REPLACE counts the events again. What it keeps. The statement counts the events already in the table, in one transaction, and answers one row: recommender , counts , events , users , pairs , took_ms . The counts are rows of an ordinary table, <name>_counts ( id , kind , src , dst , next , co , n , recent ), which any query reads: a pair row's next is how often dst came right after src in one user's events, and its co how often the two came within window events of each other (both ways); an item row's n its events; a user row's recent the user's last history events as JSON. The definition is a stored function name() , whose value is the definition (a function of that name that is not a recommender is refused), so it is versioned, kept by checkpoints and copies, and shipped to replicas as functions are. Fed from writes. Every transaction that inserts rows into the events table, by SQL, /v1/write , an import or the Rust API, also adds to the counts at its commit, in the same transaction, so an event and its counts are written, logged and shipped together, and the next recommend sees them. An event placed between two older ones by its time takes back the pair they were and counts the two it makes. A row that replaces a row of its key (an upsert) is not a new event; deleting or updating events changes no count ( CREATE OR REPLACE RECOMMENDER counts again). An event costs about 2 × window + 3 writes to the counts, each a read and a write by key, so writes of events take tens of times longer than without a recommender, and CREATE holds the writer while it counts the table (seconds per hundred thousand events). Counting at write rather than when asked keeps a call to a few reads by key whatever the size of the events table: counting a million events at each call would read them all. recommend(name, user [, k => 100, next => 1, co => 1, similar => 1, popular => 0.2]) reads the user's row and builds four lists of up to 200 items: what came next after the user's last item (and, at 0.4, after the one before), the items seen together with each of the user's recent items (the more recent weighing more, each item's counts as shares of its own), the items nearest the user's last eight by maxsim over the items' vectors, and the most popular items. An item scores the sum over the lists of the list's weight over 60 plus its rank there (reciprocal rank fusion); a weight of 0 leaves its list out. The user's recent items are left out, and the best k (at most 10,000) are the ranking: the call is then ranked(...) of their keys, so a row it does not list scores NULL , and ORDER BY recommend(...) on its own answers only the rows it lists (in a sum or rrf with other scores, the others rank by those). A user with no events gets the popular items. A call reads about two rows by key and one ranked page per recent item, some milliseconds. The answer's plan lines begin with one for each call: recommend shop for 'u42': 100 items, from 12 next, 85 seen together, 200 similar, 120 popular; 9 of the user's items left out . FROM recommend(...) is a table of the items, best first, with item , score , and each list's rank of the item ( next , co , similar , popular , NULL where it is not in a list); a LIMIT lowers k . It takes no join, WHERE , ORDER BY or GROUP BY : rank a table by ORDER BY recommend(...) for those. Where it runs. On one database, and on chloedb-server over its own tables ( CREATE and DROP RECOMMENDER need an admin key). A coordinator refuses both the statement and the call, since a user's events are spread over shards ( cluster limits ); counts of your own in an edge table or a next-items table work there. A version of ChloeDB from before recommenders opens the database, but does not feed the counts."],
["sql.html#chunks-of-a-tables-text-chunks_of","ChloeDB's query language","Chunks of a table's text: chunks_of()","Experimental. This is new in 0.3.0, and its options and answers may change in a later release. chunks_of(table, text_field) is a table of the pieces of each row's text, for a table of chunks that a vector field embeds, or a budgeted context packs from. Splits. A text is cut where it says most: before a Markdown heading, then at blank lines, then after a sentence's end, then between words, and between characters only for a word longer than a chunk. The pieces are joined in order while a chunk holds at most max_tokens (256, from 16 to 8192), and a heading always starts a chunk. With overlap => m (0), each chunk after the first starts with about m tokens of the end of the one before, from a word's start. Tokens are estimated as a quarter of the characters, rounded up: close for English and most models' tokenizers, and the same on every machine. Columns. id (the row's key), n (0, 1, ...), chunk_id ( '<id>#<n>' , a key for a table of chunks), chunk , tokens (with the overlap), and start_char and end_char , where the chunk's own text is in the row's, in characters. The query names some of them, or * . The query is read as written: its WHERE is a condition of the source table's rows, and its LIMIT cuts the chunks. It takes no join, ORDER BY , GROUP BY or OFFSET : write the chunks to a table to do those. INSERT INTO t (columns) SELECT ... FROM chunks_of(...) writes them, as INSERT s of 500 rows each. A coordinator reads the rows from its shards and splits them itself. The field is a stored TEXT field. A call reads as many of the table's rows as its LIMIT needs (each row with text gives a chunk at least; when rows without text leave the LIMIT unfilled it reads twice as many again), 1,000,000 at most without one, and what it reads may hold 1 GiB at most (or the query memory budget, if smaller): a read the database makes for itself is not held to a server's --max-rows , so this bounds it."],
["sql.html#running-sql","ChloeDB's query language","Running SQL","A statement reaches ChloeDB from a program, over HTTP or in the command center. ChloeDB serves no Postgres wire protocol, so psql and the tools built on Postgres's drivers (DBeaver, Metabase, dbt's Postgres adapter, SQLAlchemy's Postgres dialect) do not connect to it. The ways in, quickest first: Python, on a file. pip install chloedb , then chloedb.open(path).sql(statement) runs any statement on a database file in the process, and chloedb.dbapi is a DB-API 2.0 driver that pandas' read_sql , and tools written for sqlite3 , take ( Python ): A server, and its SQL workbench. uvx chloedb-server --demo 20000 --db products.chloedb (or pip install chloedb-server , then chloedb-server ... ) serves a database on http://127.0.0.1:7700 , whose command center has a SQL workbench (completion with the columns' types, parameters, the plan as a tree, and a check before a write). POST /v1/sql takes one statement, and chloedb-server cc is the command center in a terminal, with a SQL screen: Other languages. db.sql in JavaScript , the JDBC driver ( jdbc:chloedb: ) in Java, and Db::sql in Rust, which takes any statement, on any table, writes and CREATE TABLE included. The chloedb shell , built from source, runs one statement or reads statements from standard input. It opens a database that exists and does not create one; these lines read the demo catalogue the server above makes: flag effect --param name=value binds :name : a number, a string, or a JSON array for a vector --plan prints the plan lines the executor recorded for each statement --repeat n runs each statement n times and reports the median time --read-only opens the database read-only, to read it while another process writes it ( readers in other processes ); without it, the shell opens a database another process has open to write read-only, and says so From Rust, Db::query takes a SELECT and a Params map. Over the network, chloedb-server serves the same SQL over every table of a database."],
["sql.html#tables","ChloeDB's query language","Tables","A database starts with the table it was created with, and CREATE TABLE adds more. SHOW TABLES lists their names, a row each (through db.sql or POST /v1/sql ; a key limited to tables sees its own), and GET /v1/tables their columns too. An example, a table of orders: KEYWORD holds exact values (ids, statuses), TEXT words searched with MATCH , INT and FLOAT numbers, TIMESTAMP times, and VECTOR embeddings; a type followed by [] holds a list. It needs an admin key. A larger example: Each table has its own columns, primary key, indexes and merges; the tables share the file, its write-ahead log and transactions. Types are TEXT , KEYWORD , INT (or INTEGER , BIGINT ), FLOAT (or DOUBLE , REAL ), BOOL (or BOOLEAN ), TIMESTAMP (or TIMESTAMPTZ , TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE , DATETIME , and DATE , which is a TIMESTAMP : a date written alone is its midnight UTC), VECTOR(dimensions) (1 to 65,536 dimensions), SPARSE , MEDIA and EDGES ; KEYWORD[] and INT[] hold lists, and VECTOR(dimensions)[] several vectors per row ( multi-vector fields ). A BOOL or TIMESTAMP column holds one value and is neither the primary key nor a reference. OPTIONS(...) takes the options of the JSON schema : substring (true: LIKE and ILIKE on the column read a substring index ), positions , stored and indexed (true or false; an indexed TEXT or SPARSE field is stored unless generated, since merges rebuild its index from the stored values), from (the fields a generated TEXT field joins), analyzer ( 'english' , the default: stopwords dropped and words stemmed, so running finds run ; 'simple' : every word kept as written, for names and codes; 'standard' : text in any language and script , Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Thai included; 'cjk' : standard with pairs of characters as the terms of Chinese and Japanese; or a language, such as 'french' , 'german' , 'arabic' , 'hindi' or 'chinese' , which is standard with what the language needs, a stemmer for most), index ( 'hnsw' with m and ef_construction , 'flat' , or 'ivf' with nlist ), partition_by with partition_bounds (a graph per range or value of a column, for filtered searches ), codes ( 'sq8' , 'binary' , 'rabitq1' , 'rabitq2' or 'rabitq4' ; a multi-vector field searches 'binary' by default), keep_f32 (a multi-vector field keeps its float32 vectors to score with, four times the bytes of its 8-bit codes), pool (a multi-vector field merges a row's vectors whose cosine similarity is at least this, such as 0.9 , into their mean when it is written), pool_factor (a multi-vector field clusters a row's vectors into one group for every this many, such as 2 , by Ward's linkage, and keeps each group's mean when it is written; exclusive with pool ), kind of a MEDIA field, derive (a MEDIA field) with model for text made from media , embed with model for an embedded vector field , which may leave out its dimensions when ChloeDB knows the model's ( VECTOR alone), or an embedded SPARSE field , and on a KEYWORD[] semantic_id (the vector field) with codebook for a semantic-ID field : sid KEYWORD[] OPTIONS(semantic_id = 'emb', codebook = 'items-rq') . Field lists are a string, 'name, bio' , or ARRAY['name', 'bio'] . The primary key is one column: PRIMARY KEY on it, or a PRIMARY KEY (column) constraint. A table without one only takes plain INSERT . REFERENCES table , or FOREIGN KEY (column) REFERENCES table , records that a KEYWORD or INT column holds primary keys of that table, which must exist (or be the table being created) and have a primary key of the column's type. References are not enforced: a row may name a key that is not there, and an inner join along the reference leaves that row out ( LEFT JOIN keeps it). Joins along them are ordinary joins ( FROM wrote w JOIN authors a ON w.author = a.id ). NOT NULL refuses a row without a value in that column, from any write: SQL, JSON, a file or the Rust API. DEFAULT value is the value a write that leaves the column out gets: a constant of the column's type on a single-valued TEXT , KEYWORD , INT , FLOAT , BOOL or TIMESTAMP column that is not the key, or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (or now() ) on a TIMESTAMP , the time of the statement, the same in all its rows. An INSERT that leaves a column out, a JSON row without its key and a CSV file without its column give it the default; a NULL that a write gives stays NULL , and a column with NOT NULL refuses it. In SQL the keyword DEFAULT stands for a column's default, in VALUES and in SET , and CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (or now() , LOCALTIMESTAMP ) is the time of the statement there and in conditions ( SET seen = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP , also in ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE ). The Rust API writes a row as it is given; Schema::defaults returns a row of the defaults to start from. The JSON schema's options are \"not_null\": true and \"default\" ( \"now\" for the time of the write). OPTIONS(shard_by = 'column') after the columns places the table's rows on shards by that column instead of by the primary key, so that a user's events sit on one shard together. The column is a stored, single-valued KEYWORD or INT with NOT NULL , other than the key, and a row keeps it: UPDATE does not change it (delete the row and insert it again). In JSON it is \"shard_by\": \"usr\" . One database places nothing, so the option only matters to a coordinator. OPTIONS(cluster_by = 'column') stores each segment's rows sorted by that column, rows of one value in the order they were written: a flush sorts the rows it writes, and a merge sorts the rows of the segments it joins. A user's history then sits in one run of each segment instead of scattered across it, so reading it touches a few pages rather than one per row. The column is an indexed, single-valued KEYWORD , INT or TIMESTAMP , and a row keeps it ( UPDATE does not change it). Queries return the same rows either way; only ORDER BY fixes their order. In JSON it is \"cluster_by\": \"usr\" . OPTIONS(retain = '400 days') drops rows once their time is older than the period: a flush does not write them, and a merge leaves them out of the segment it writes, or drops a segment whose rows are all past it without rewriting or reading anything; the merger looks for such segments on its own. Such a table's segments merge only within windows of time, a thirtieth of the period each (from a second to a day): a segment holds rows of one window, as of its newest row, so a table kept 30 days holds segments of a day at most, and each leaves whole once its newest row is past the period rather than being rewritten as its rows expire. Until then a query still finds them, so filter on the time where that matters ( WHERE ts > '2025-10-01' ). The time is the table's only TIMESTAMP column, or the one retain_by = 'column' names (indexed, single-valued, and not changed by UPDATE ); a row without one is kept. A period is a whole number and a unit: seconds, minutes, hours, days or weeks ( '90 s' , '12h' , '2 weeks' ). In JSON it is \"retain\": \"400 days\" with \"retain_by\" . OPTIONS(expire_by = 'column') expires each row at the time its column holds, as a key-value store's TTL does: from that moment on queries, counts, joins and reads by key leave the row out, and a write by its key finds no row there (an INSERT of the key succeeds, an UPDATE changes nothing). Rows without a time never expire, and UPDATE ... SET until = ... (or a set in place) moves a row's time. A server that takes writes deletes expired rows every few seconds, and Db::expire (Python: expire() ) does it at once; those deletes are logged as any delete is, so replicas hold the same rows without reading a clock. Unlike retain , which drops old rows when segments are written and leaves them visible until then, an expired row is gone for reads at once. The column is an indexed, single-valued TIMESTAMP , the table has a primary key, and DROP COLUMN keeps the column ( RENAME COLUMN renames the option too). It makes the database format 3, feature expire_by . In JSON it is \"expire_by\": \"until\" . - OPTIONS(compression = 'zstd') compresses the table's stored rows (its doc store) with zstd in 32 KiB blocks instead of LZ4 in 16 KiB ones. On four logs from Loghub the doc store took 32% to 39% less, and the database 9% to 16% less, while a read of one row by key took 12% to 40% longer (48 µs instead of 34 on 11M lines) and a scan of every row about as long ( chloedb/results/log-bytes/ , on a 4-core cloud container). It suits tables that are written far more than read row by row, such as logs; queries that count, filter, group or search without returning rows read no stored rows either way. Segments written after the option use it, and older ones keep LZ4 until a merge rewrites them. It makes the database format 3, feature zstd . In JSON it is \"compression\": \"zstd\" ; 'lz4' is the default. - OPTIONS(edges = 'src, dst, w') makes each row an edge of a graph over another table's rows, which walks over that table follow ( edge tables ). In JSON it is \"edges\": \"src, dst, w\" . - DROP TABLE drops tables other than the first. A table that another table references goes with it ( DROP TABLE wrote, authors ) or with CASCADE , which removes those references. - Every other clause ( UNIQUE , CHECK , ON DELETE , constraint names, a DEFAULT that is an expression, CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT ) is an error. CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE and DROP TABLE run through Db::sql , Python's in-process chloedb.Database , and chloedb-server 's POST /v1/sql , where a coordinator runs them on every shard."],
["sql.html#changing-a-tables-columns","ChloeDB's query language","Changing a table's columns","ALTER TABLE changes the columns of any table, the first included, while it holds rows; the changes of one statement apply in order, as one change. ADD COLUMN (or ADD ) takes a column as CREATE TABLE does, with its type, NOT NULL , DEFAULT , REFERENCES and OPTIONS , and puts it last. Rows written before it read it as its default, or NULL without one (a TIMESTAMP with DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP too: the default is the time of each later write): in SELECT , WHERE , ORDER BY , aggregates and searches alike. Writes from then on take it. A NOT NULL column without a default can only be added to a table without rows. A column added with embed gets its model and embeds the rows written from then on; the rows before get theirs from the backfill , which a server runs once the model opens (from Rust, Db::embed_backfill ), and until then have none. A generated TEXT column, a semantic-ID column, EDGES and a primary key cannot be added. DROP COLUMN drops a column and its values; a column added again under the same name is a new column, NULL in the older rows. The primary key, a table's only column, a column another field names (as the source of a generated text, an embedding, a caption or semantic IDs) and one the table's options name ( shard_by , cluster_by , retain_by , expire_by , edges ) stay, and the statement says what names it; so do columns a rank profile of the table ranks by. RENAME COLUMN a TO b keeps the column's values, and the fields and options that name it follow it; a rank profile does not, so a column a profile ranks by keeps its name until the profile is dropped or replaced. IF EXISTS after ALTER TABLE skips a table that is not there; ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS and DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS skip a column that is, or is not. Positions ( FIRST , AFTER ), ALTER COLUMN , RENAME TO (of the table) and CASCADE are errors. A change writes nothing but the table's schema: it waits for writes in flight, writes the memtable as segments (a write-ahead log names columns by position), and commits the new schema. A segment written before a column was added does not hold it; when it is opened, the column's index is built in memory over its rows' default, and the next merge that rewrites the segment writes the column for real. A dropped column's values stay in the older segments, unread, until merges rewrite them. The Rust API is Db::alter_table(table, &[Alter::AddField(field), Alter::DropField(name), Alter::RenameField { from, to }]) ; a database with a column added later needs the feature added_fields , so a version of ChloeDB from before it refuses the database. On chloedb-server, a coordinator runs ADD COLUMN and RENAME COLUMN on every shard and then serves the table as altered; it refuses DROP COLUMN , since its plans name a table's columns by position and a query planned while some shards had dropped the column would read the next one. A primary ships the change to its replicas in its place among the writes, as it ships CREATE TABLE and DROP TABLE , and its replicas give the table the same columns under the same field ids ( replication )."],
["sql.html#writes","ChloeDB's query language","Writes","A write names its table, any table of the database. INSERT fails on a primary key that already exists. INSERT OR REPLACE (or REPLACE INTO ) replaces that row, an upsert. ON CONFLICT (key) DO NOTHING (or INSERT OR IGNORE ) writes only the rows whose key is new, and the first of a key the statement repeats. ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET ... inserts the new keys and sets fields of the rows already there, where excluded.column is the value the statement gave; a key that comes twice in one such statement is an error. On a cluster, a table placed by shard_by keeps its keys unique across shards for INSERT , INSERT OR REPLACE replaces a row only on its shard_by value's shard, ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE takes rows of one shard, and UPDATE / DELETE ... LIMIT pick their rows first ( cluster limits ). Lists are ARRAY[…] ; a vector is a parameter, ARRAY[…] or a JSON array string; a multi-vector value is a list of vectors ( ARRAY[ARRAY[…], ARRAY[…]] , a parameter holding a list of vectors, or '[[…], […]]' ), and one vector given to it is a list of one; a SPARSE value is a JSON object string, and a MEDIA value a URI or a JSON object string. UPDATE takes any WHERE a SELECT of its table takes, and a LIMIT . A WHERE the table's indexes answer runs as it is; another (a TEXT column compared with = , LIKE of a TEXT column, lower(...) , IN (SELECT ...) , [NOT] EXISTS ) picks the rows' keys with that SELECT , under the writer lock as the write runs, and writes those rows, so the rows a SELECT with that WHERE counts are the rows written ( EXPLAIN says picks its rows by key ). Through a coordinator, one whose WHERE has a subquery picks the keys over every shard first ( cluster limits ). Setting only single-valued INT and FLOAT fields ( SET clicks = clicks + 1 ) changes them in place, without rewriting the row, which suits counters and prices that change often. Setting any other field rewrites the row, which needs every text field stored or generated. SET takes a value or any expression of the row: arithmetic, || , CASE , NOT , comparisons, lower , upper , length , abs , round and coalesce over the row's columns, and in ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE over excluded.column too. Every expression reads the row as it was, so SET a = b, b = a swaps two fields. The result is stored as the field's type: a whole number fits an INT ( clicks = price * 2 ), but 7 / 2 is 3.5 and an error there ( round(clicks / 2) fits); one value set to a multi-valued field becomes a list of it. NULL in gives NULL out, as in SQL, except field + number and field - number , which count a NULL as zero, as a counter does; coalesce(field, 0) + other does the same elsewhere. A multi-valued field without values is NULL to coalesce and IS NULL . Searches, scores and aggregates ( MATCH , bm25 , score() , COUNT ) are errors in SET . A statement that cannot set one of its rows sets none. DELETE takes any WHERE a SELECT of its table takes, as UPDATE does, and ORDER BY with LIMIT to delete the first rows in that order. DELETE FROM t without one deletes every row (there is no TRUNCATE ). RETURNING , UPDATE ... FROM , DELETE ... USING , INSERT ... SELECT (but from chunks_of ), ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE and DO UPDATE ... WHERE are errors."],
["sql.html#loading-a-file-csv-tsv-json-lines","ChloeDB's query language","Loading a file: CSV, TSV, JSON lines","Many rows at once come from a file rather than INSERT s: POST /v1/import?table=orders with the file as the body and its content type ( text/csv , text/tab-separated-values , application/x-ndjson or application/json ), or the MCP tool import with the file's text in data (or the rows as objects in rows ). A CSV's first line names the columns; columns the table does not have are skipped and named in the answer, and an empty cell is the column's DEFAULT ( ?empty=null makes it NULL ). The table must exist first ( CREATE TABLE , above), or, with an admin key, the MCP tool's create: true makes it from the rows: whole numbers become INT , numbers FLOAT , true / false BOOL , ISO dates TIMESTAMP , text of two and a half words or more on average TEXT and other text KEYWORD , with a column named id (or the one key names) as the primary key, and the answer shows the CREATE TABLE it ran. Parquet and SQLite files load the same way ( server ), and the command center's Import page takes a file dropped on it. Each statement is one transaction, and returns the number of rows it wrote. From Rust, the transaction API ( Database::begin , WriteTxn::insert , upsert , set , delete , commit , and WriteTxn::table for the other tables) groups several writes into one, across tables too; WriteTxn::upsert_if_newer keeps whichever version of a row has the highest version number, for replaying a feed out of order."],
["sql.html#explain","ChloeDB's query language","EXPLAIN","EXPLAIN <statement> shows the plan of a SELECT , INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE without running it: a row per line, in a column named plan , the root first and each operator's input indented under it. It runs in-process ( db.sql in Python, Db::sql in Rust); over HTTP, send the statement without EXPLAIN to POST /v1/explain , which answers the same lines as tree . A Search is the rows of one table that its filter holds for, ranked by its order ; the operators above it group, keep, sort, cut and name them, and a write's operator ( Insert , Update , Delete ) sits on top. The plan is the statement as bound: values have their field's type, and a NOT over a field that can be null also says x IS NOT NULL , since a row without a value matches neither a comparison nor its negation. EXPLAIN FORMAT JSON (or EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) ) gives the plan as one row of JSON: fields by their position in the table, typed values ( {\"int\": 5} , {\"text\": \"5\"} ), a version and the features the plan needs. ChloeDB refuses a plan from a newer version by name. EXPLAIN ANALYZE and other formats are refused. A compound query's plan has operators over rows in memory above its Search es, and its JSON needs the feature operators : Join (or Left join ) matches its two inputs on the keys after on , targets(f) being the rows an EDGES field links to, with any other ON condition after AND ; With names its results, which Ref reads; Key order of top puts rows in the order of the WITH result top ; Distinct keeps each row once, and Distinct on: video the first row of each group . A subquery sits in its condition: IN values of (...) (its values), IN keys of (...) (its rows' keys), EXISTS (...) and a scalar (...) , each shown on one line with ; between its operators. SELECT DISTINCT of the table alone is a GROUP BY , a single-table query. Every statement runs as its plan, and a plan in JSON runs on its own: Db::run in Rust, Database.run in Python and POST /v1/plan over HTTP take one, and check it against the table's schema as the SQL binder checks a statement."],
["sql.html#reading-a-plan","ChloeDB's query language","Reading a plan","--plan shows what the planner decided, segment by segment. The same statement can get different plans as the data changes. In the evaluation, an OR filter matching about 15% of documents was scanned exactly on SciFact's 5,183 documents, but on the larger collections the planner measured 11% near the query and post-filtered the HNSW result. An RRF query's topic filter matched 11% of Quora globally but only 3.4% near the query, so the planner switched to an exact scan of its 58,767 members, at 3.1 ms. Planning and execution describes the choices."],
["sql.html#differences-from-postgresql","ChloeDB's query language","Differences from PostgreSQL","A query written for PostgreSQL mostly runs here and gives the same rows, but not always. These are the differences people porting SQL from PostgreSQL meet, as this release has them: first the answers that differ without an error, then what is refused (most refusals' messages say what to do instead). Connecting. There is no Postgres wire protocol: psql , DBeaver, Metabase, dbt's Postgres adapter and SQLAlchemy's Postgres dialect do not connect, and there is no dbt adapter or SQLAlchemy dialect of ChloeDB's own. Running SQL lists the ways in: Python's chloedb.dbapi (DB-API 2.0, which pandas' read_sql reads), JDBC, HTTP and the SQL workbench. Following Postgres copies a Postgres database into ChloeDB; it does not let Postgres's clients in. Answers that differ, with no error: expression ChloeDB PostgreSQL to get PostgreSQL's 7 / 2 3.5 : / always gives a FLOAT 3 for two integers (7 - 7 % 2) / 2 (3.0) ORDER BY x DESC with NULL s NULL s last NULL s first ORDER BY x DESC NULLS FIRST greatest(1, 2) , least(1, 2) 2.0 , 1.0 ( FLOAT ) 2 , 1 CAST(greatest(a, b) AS INT) SELECT a.id, b.title FROM a JOIN b ... columns a.id , b.title id , title a.id AS id, b.title AS title a correlated (SELECT COUNT(*) ... WHERE t.a = o.a) 10.0 ( FLOAT ) 10 alias it and read it as a number Ascending order puts NULL s last in both. Following Postgres makes a numeric column a FLOAT , so CAST and round take its halves to the even neighbour (2.5 to 2), where PostgreSQL rounds a numeric 's away from zero (to 3). Changed: 0.3.7 and earlier rounded every half away from zero, kept the rows without a value in n != 1.5 on an INT column, and gave 'a' for 'a' || NULL ; each now answers as PostgreSQL does. Changed: 0.3.8 and earlier refused ORDER BY the alias of a group column after GROUP BY or DISTINCT (\"no such column\") and NULLS FIRST there, read json_extract(doc, '$.a.b') as NULL outside WHERE , and on DataFusion (a query over more than 4,096 rows) added FLOAT sums in order, so totals that tie on fewer rows could differ in their last digit and a RANK over them change; each now answers as PostgreSQL does, and as on fewer rows. TEXT and KEYWORD . A TEXT column is text searched by its words ( MATCH , bm25 ), and a KEYWORD a value compared whole; PostgreSQL's text is both. A stored TEXT column also groups ( GROUP BY country , SELECT DISTINCT country ) and compares with = and LIKE , by its stored text read row by row, where a KEYWORD 's index answers both. Imports make a column of prose a TEXT one, by its values (more than two and a half words a value on average: SQLite , Parquet ), and following Postgres makes every text and varchar one: create the table first with KEYWORD for the columns you group and filter by often. Writes. An UPDATE or a DELETE takes any WHERE a SELECT of its table takes ( writes ); one the indexes cannot answer (a subquery, a TEXT column compared, a function of a column) picks the rows' keys first, under the writer lock. Changed: 0.3.7 and earlier refused such a WHERE in a write. Refused: UPDATE ... FROM , DELETE ... USING , RETURNING , TRUNCATE ( DELETE FROM t deletes every row), and INSERT ... SELECT except from chunks_of . Transactions. BEGIN , COMMIT and ROLLBACK are refused: each statement is a transaction of its own. Several writes commit together as the operations of one POST /v1/write (or write(ops) in Python and JavaScript), and through Rust's transaction API ( Loading a file , at its end). Tables, views and indexes. Refused: CREATE VIEW and CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW (a stored function RETURNS TABLE is a view with parameters, and a rollup keeps counts up to date), CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT and temporary tables (the messages say what to write instead), CREATE INDEX (every column is indexed by its type when the table is made), ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE , ALTER TABLE ... RENAME TO , UNIQUE , CHECK , and a primary key of more than one column. REFERENCES are recorded, not enforced. The first table of a database cannot be dropped. Queries. Refused: WITH RECURSIVE (walk a graph with REACHABLE and PATH ), GROUP BY ROLLUP , CUBE and GROUPING SETS (a UNION ALL of GROUP BY s), CROSS JOIN , tables listed with commas, LATERAL , JOIN ... USING , NATURAL JOIN , a join whose ON has no equality of the two tables' columns, and a window frame RANGE with an INTERVAL offset; the messages of the ROLLUP and USING refusals show the query to write instead. INTERVAL '1 month' moves a time written as a value, not a column's, and two times do not subtract: EXTRACT(epoch FROM b) - EXTRACT(epoch FROM a) is the seconds between them. Functions. Over rows ChloeDB computes lower , upper , length , abs , round , coalesce , ln , log , exp , sqrt , pow , least , greatest , sigmoid , clamp , dot , date_trunc , date_part ( EXTRACT ), strftime , date_bin , time_bucket , hash , CASE and CAST , besides its search, vector and JSON functions; over groups, the aggregates . Among those PostgreSQL has and ChloeDB does not: substr , replace , trim , concat , position , split_part , regexp_replace , nullif , floor , ceil , trunc , random , md5 , to_char , age and string_agg . An unknown function's error lists those that exist. The catalog. There is no information_schema or pg_catalog , and DESCRIBE runs in the SQL workbench only. SHOW TABLES lists the tables, and GET /v1/tables gives each table's columns and the CREATE TABLE that makes it."],
["graphs.html","Graphs in ChloeDB","","Rows can link to other rows. A traversal, a shortest path or personalized PageRank runs in the same SQL statement as BM25, filters and vector search, and can start from any of them. On arXiv, the papers cited by a new paper's two nearest neighbours find twice as many of its references as vector search alone. A graph built from the vectors themselves does not help."],
["graphs.html#rows-are-nodes","Graphs in ChloeDB","Rows are nodes","Graph databases keep nodes and edges in stores of their own. ChloeDB keeps them in a table: a row is a node, and an EDGES field holds the row's outgoing edges as a JSON array of targets, each a primary key of the same table, optionally with a label and a positive weight. Formally, a snapshot S holds rows V with keys κ : V → K , and edges $$ E \\subseteq V \\times K \\times L \\times \\mathbb{R}_{>0}, \\qquad (u, k, \\ell, w):\\ \\text{row } u \\text{ points at key } k \\text{ with label } \\ell \\text{ and weight } w $$ An edge is resolved when a query follows it: $$ \\rho_S(k) = \\text{the live row } v \\in V \\text{ with } \\kappa(v) = k, \\text{ if there is one} $$ A key has at most one live row in a snapshot, so ρ S is a function. When it has none (the target was deleted, or never existed), the edge dangles and traversals skip it, as a foreign key without a constraint would. Edges belong to their source row and point at keys, not storage positions. They are written, logged, updated, deleted, merged and snapshotted with the row, and nothing else in the engine had to change. A merge rewrites every docID and an update moves a row to a newer segment, but neither changes a key, so no edge is ever rewritten. The price is a lookup per hop, and most of the engineering below goes into making it cheap."],
["graphs.html#storing-edges","Graphs in ChloeDB","Storing edges","Every segment, on disk or built from the memtable, gets one section per indexed EDGES field, read in place through mmap : Out-edges. A CSR over local docIDs: offsets, then per edge its source, its target key (an i64 , or offsets and bytes for string keys), a label code and a weight. Labels and weights are stored only when some edge has one. Reverse index. Edges grouped by target key, the groups ordered by the key's 64-bit hash under the radix directory of the primary-key index, with each edge's source stored in group order. \"Which rows point at key k \" is one directory read and one key comparison per group, then a sequential read. Integer hashes are a bijection and need no comparison; colliding string keys form separate groups. Same-segment hints. Per edge, the docID its target had in this segment when the segment was built. Since ρ S has one answer, a hinted row that is still live is that answer, and the hop needs no lookup. A hop from u along its edges therefore costs, per edge, $$ c_{\\text{out}} = p_{\\text{hint}}\\,c_{\\text{live}} + (1 - p_{\\text{hint}})\\Bigl(p_{\\text{memo}}\\,c_{\\text{memo}} + (1 - p_{\\text{memo}}) \\sum_{i \\le j^*} c_{\\text{probe},i}\\Bigr) $$ where p hint is the share of targets built into the same segment and still live, p memo the share already resolved earlier in the query, and j * the segment where the key is found. A hop against the edges costs one reverse-index probe per segment for u 's key, then a liveness check per source. The section takes about $$ b \\approx \\underbrace{8} {\\text{target}} + \\underbrace{4} {\\text{source}} + \\underbrace{4 + 4} {\\text{reverse}} + \\underbrace{4} {\\text{hint}} + \\frac{8\\,\\lvert V \\rvert + 16\\,\\lvert K_E \\rvert}{\\lvert E \\rvert}\\ \\text{bytes per edge} $$ with K E the distinct targets. That comes to 27 bytes per edge on arXiv, where the graph is under 5% of the file:"],
["graphs.html#the-sql","Graphs in ChloeDB","The SQL","Two predicates, a column and a score join the existing functions. Each takes seeds: a key, a parameter, a tuple of keys, or any subquery over the same table, so a vector search, a BM25 query or a facet filter can be the way into the graph. The functions and their options are listed in the query language reference ."],
["graphs.html#planning-and-traversal","Graphs in ChloeDB","Planning and traversal","A traversal does not respect segment boundaries, so graph computations run first, over the whole snapshot. Seeds are resolved (a seed subquery runs as a query of its own on the same snapshot), and the traversal runs. Its result becomes, per segment, a materialised document set with exact cardinality, plus each row's distance and PageRank score. The planner then sees a graph predicate as a filter whose size it knows exactly: a vector search over a neighbourhood of a few hundred rows becomes an exact scan of those rows. The traversals: Reachability: breadth-first search, frontier by frontier; a search that would meet more than max_nodes rows besides its seeds is an error rather than an answer that depends on the order of the edges. Shortest paths: bidirectional breadth-first search, always expanding the smaller frontier, forward along the edges or backward against them. Personalized PageRank: forward push (Andersen, Chung and Lang, 2006). The vector π solves $$ \\pi = \\alpha\\, e_S + (1 - \\alpha)\\, \\pi W, \\qquad W_{uv} = \\frac{w_{uv}}{\\sum_{x} w_{ux}} $$ where e S is the restart distribution over the seeds. Every row has an estimate p and a residual r , with r = e S at the start, and pushing a row u does $$ p(u) \\mathrel{+}= \\alpha\\, r(u), \\qquad r(v) \\mathrel{+}= (1 - \\alpha)\\, r(u)\\, W_{uv}\\ \\ \\forall v, \\qquad r(u) \\leftarrow 0 $$ while some r ( u ) ≥ ε·deg( u ). Each estimate is then within ε·deg( u ) of π( u ), and the work is bounded by 1/(αε) pushes whatever the graph's size. A row is queued only once its residual reaches ε, the least any threshold can be. Rows that merely received a little mass are never resolved, and resolving them had been most of the original cost."],
["graphs.html#speed","Graphs in ChloeDB","Speed","Two graphs, each queried from 200 random seeds on a warm cache, on one thread of an Apple M2 MacBook Air, through SQL (parsing and planning included; COUNT(*) , so that no rows are fetched): arXiv: 168,343 papers (ogbn-arxiv, less 1,000 held out) with 1,148,979 citations, in two segments. Synthetic: a power-law graph of 1,000,000 rows and 9,998,469 edges, in four segments. Row i links to 10 earlier rows, each the target of a random earlier edge with probability ½ and a uniform earlier row otherwise. The baselines answer the same queries, and the benchmark checks that every count and path length agrees: SQLite: an edge table (src, dst) as a WITHOUT ROWID primary key, an index on (dst, src) , and recursive CTEs. CSR: the same traversal code on two in-memory arrays. This is the lower bound: no keys, no segments, no SQL. arXiv, 200 seeds per query. Source: chloedb/results/graph/latency_arxiv.json . On the million-row graph ( chloedb/results/graph/synthetic_1000000.json ; loaded in 2.7 s into a 307 MB file, 27 bytes per edge): query (200 seeds) result ChloeDB p50 / p95 ms SQLite p50 / p95 ms CSR p50 / p95 ms REACHABLE , 1 hop out 10 rows 0.017 / 0.022 0.012 / 0.013 0.001 / 0.001 REACHABLE , 2 hops out 108 rows 0.032 / 0.045 0.061 / 0.066 0.004 / 0.005 REACHABLE , 3 hops out 890 rows 0.098 / 0.153 0.597 / 0.680 0.030 / 0.037 REACHABLE , 1 hop in 8 rows 0.014 / 0.019 0.010 / 0.017 0.001 / 0.001 REACHABLE , 2 hops both ways 1,586 rows 0.104 / 0.468 0.471 / 3.26 0.019 / 0.110 PATH either way 200 of 200 found 0.079 / 0.145 — 0.025 / 0.071 ppr , ε = 10 −4 133 pushes 3.49 / 4.13 — 0.66 / 0.80 ppr , ε = 10 −5 1,020 pushes 25.6 / 30.1 — 5.47 / 6.32 Against SQLite , one-hop queries cost about the same, roughly 10–15 µs, mostly parsing and planning on both sides. From two hops on, the gap opens, and the tail is where it shows most: two hops both ways on arXiv touch 3,790 rows on average and up to tens of thousands, and their p95 is 1.6 ms in ChloeDB against 14.8 ms in SQLite. The recursive CTE re-expands rows reached at several depths and joins through a B-tree per row, where ChloeDB reads a CSR slice. Shortest paths and PageRank have no practical CTE form. Against the CSR , ChloeDB pays two costs. About 10 µs of SQL per query dominates small traversals. Key resolution dominates large ones: PageRank takes 2.4 times the CSR's time on arXiv and 5.3 times on the million-row graph, because a target outside its own segment is a hash probe into each segment's primary-key index until found, a cache miss each. Three changes found by profiling on the Xeon VM that first ran these benchmarks cut PageRank on the million-row graph from 21.4 to 5.8 ms there (3.5 ms on the M2): reverse groups by key, with the sources stored inline; same-segment hints and a per-query memo; queueing only rows that can pass the push threshold."],
["graphs.html#does-the-graph-help-search","Graphs in ChloeDB","Does the graph help search?","The rows here are nodes with text, vectors and edges at once. The question the engine cannot answer by itself is whether the edges add anything to a search that already has the text and the vectors, and whether edges made from the vectors (each row linked to its nearest rows) add anything. The task: for a new paper, find the papers it cites from its title and abstract. This is citation recommendation, and the same shape as finding the records a new question depends on. 1,000 papers of OGB's test split (809 from 2019, 191 from 2020, each citing at least five papers) are held out of the table. Each held-out paper's title and abstract is the query. Its references in the table (15.3 on average) are the relevant rows. Vectors come from a static embedder (Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M, 512 dimensions), the same one ChloeCtx uses. Each method returns 20 papers. Methods that start from vector seeds have one parameter, the number of seeds S , swept from 1 to 50. Papers with even ids choose S by nDCG@20 (516 queries) and papers with odd ids report it (484 queries), so no reported number was tuned on its own queries. Comparisons are paired over the same queries, with a 95% bootstrap interval and a two-sided sign-flip test. $$ \\mathrm{recall@}k = \\frac{\\lvert R_k \\cap T \\rvert}{\\lvert T \\rvert}, \\qquad \\mathrm{nDCG@}k = \\frac{\\sum_{i \\le k} \\mathbb{1}[r_i \\in T] / \\log_2(i + 1)}{\\sum_{i \\le \\min(k, \\lvert T \\rvert)} 1 / \\log_2(i + 1)} $$ Source: chloedb/results/graph/quality_stats.json . Times are from the quality runs on the M2, after one untimed pass per method. The graph doubles what vector search finds. The best method is also the simplest: take the two papers nearest the query, follow their citations, and rank what they cite by similarity to the query. It reaches nDCG@20 0.253 against 0.123 for vector search (+0.130, 95% interval 0.111–0.149, p < 0.0001), 0.161 for BM25 and 0.160 for hybrid search, in 0.2 ms. Papers on the same topic cite the same work, and the nearest papers' reference lists are a better candidate set than the nearest papers themselves. PageRank from the same seeds reaches 0.211 at its best (from one seed), below the plain one-hop expansion: on this graph the papers two or three hops away dilute the direct citations. Fusing PageRank with the vector ranking lowers it to 0.179, because the vector ranking's own top results are mostly not references. Fusing BM25, vectors and PageRank from hybrid seeds also reaches 0.211, at the cost of BM25 over long queries (48 ms). Fewer seeds are better. With more seeds, the citations of papers less similar to the query crowd out those of the nearest ones. In a first version of the experiment the seeds themselves stayed in the candidate set; ranked by similarity they took the top places, and from 20 seeds on the \"graph\" method returned exactly the vector search's results. Every graph method above removes the seeds with NOT REACHABLE(cites, seeds, hops => 0, min_hops => 0) . A graph made of vectors adds nothing. A VECTOR field can be traversed as a graph, each row's 10 most similar rows being its neighbours. On this task it returns what vector search already found when the seeds are kept (recall@20 0.112 against 0.111 at 10 seeds, on all queries), and is worse when they are removed (nDCG@20 0.079). A similarity graph only re-expresses the vector space, while citations carry information the text does not. Rows with vectors are useful as the way into a graph of real relations, not as a replacement for one. The implicit similarity graph stays in ChloeDB for exploratory queries, at 0.12 ms per hop of 10 neighbours."],
["graphs.html#edge-tables","Graphs in ChloeDB","Edge tables","An edge table keeps a graph whose edges change one at a time, such as items seen together, apart from the rows it links: each of its rows is an edge, and adding one, changing its weight or deleting it writes that row alone. Each of its segments indexes its rows by the keys they link, in a section of its own ( edge_table ): the distinct keys its rows name, numbered in the order of their hashes, with the same radix directory as the primary-key index; each row's source and target as key numbers, and its weight when the segment was built; for each key, the rows that leave it and the rows that reach it, in row order. A hop from row u looks up u 's key in each segment of the edge table, then reads the rows that leave it (or reach it, or both, by direction). For each row it checks that the row is live and reads its weight as it is now, in-place writes included, so a decayed or zeroed weight counts at once. The neighbour is the row's other end, a key number, which the walk resolves to a row of the walked table once per segment. Two shortcuts keep a long walk cheap without costing a short one anything. A segment's key numbers get a dense memo once the walk has resolved a thirty-second of them. A segment whose weights were written in place gets a flat copy of its weights once the walk has read a sixteenth as many as its in-place layers hold. The section is smaller than an EDGES field's graph for the same edges, because a key is stored once per segment rather than once per edge. A hop costs more, because an EDGES field's out-edges sit with their row and most targets resolve through a same-segment hint. chloedb-edges-bench (in chloedb-server ) measures both: Writes. It streams 300,000 click events of 10,000 users on 50,000 items (Zipf 1.0) through /v1/stream . Each event adds 1 to the weight of the edges between its item and the user's last five items, both ways, with add operations whose doc creates an edge's row on its first event. That is 9.0 edge operations per event and 1,896,100 edges in the end. Walks. ppr is seeded with a user's last 20 items, for 1,000 users, over the edge table and over an EDGES field of items holding the same edges and weights. Each query runs once to warm up, then once timed. edge table EDGES field bytes per edge (index) 20.6 33.2 ppr p50 ms 21.9 15.3 ppr p90 ms 30.1 26.6 ppr p99 ms 38.7 37.4 Walks cost about 1.4 times as much at the median, and about the same at p99. Both rank the same 200 rows in the same order in all 1,000 walks. The edge table had 10 segments here. In a first run whose merges had left it 15, its p50 was 28.5 ms against 17.6, since a hop looks up its key in every segment. Writes. The events alone take 14.1 µs each (71,155 per second). With the edges, they take 190.2 µs (5,257 per second): 19.6 µs per edge operation, most of which create a row. Weights as counts. 54 of the 1,000 walks found nothing over either graph, when ppr pushed a row only once its residual reached eps times its weighted degree: at the default eps of 10⁻⁴, a seed whose edges' weights added up to more than 500 was never pushed. It now compares the residual with eps times the row's number of edges, so that scaling every weight alike (counts against frequencies) changes nothing; the weights still divide what a push spreads. Those numbers were measured before the change. A walk that stops at max_pushes says so in its plan line ( stopped at max_pushes ) and in the stats ( max_pushes_reached: true ). Cloud VM: 4 cores of an Intel Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 16.9 GB, Linux 6.18. Source: chloedb-server/results/edges/vm.json ."],
["graphs.html#on-a-cluster","Graphs in ChloeDB","On a cluster","Graph queries run on one database, not through a coordinator . A walk reads every row it meets in one snapshot, and on a sharded cluster a row's edges point at rows that other shards hold, so a shard cannot walk its part alone and the coordinator has no edges to walk. A coordinator therefore refuses REACHABLE , PATH , hops() and ppr with an error saying so, before it asks any shard, as does a shard asked for its part of one. The other queries of the table, and writes to its EDGES field or edge table, work through the coordinator as usual. Until a coordinator walks the graph itself: Keep the graph where it is whole. A server of its own ( chloedb-server --db ), or an embedded database in the application ( Python , JavaScript , Java ), runs every graph query of this page. A primary and its replicas , without a coordinator, keep it available. Walk from the application. Each hop is one read of the frontier's rows through the coordinator: SELECT id, cites FROM papers WHERE id IN (:frontier) gives each row's out-edges (the field's JSON text), and over an edge table SELECT dst, w FROM cooc WHERE src IN (:frontier) gives them too, while WHERE dst IN (...) reaches back along them. A breadth-first walk of h hops is h such reads. The tests walk two hops this way on three shards and find the rows REACHABLE(..., hops => 2) finds on one database. Personalized PageRank is harder this way, since its pushes go round by round over a growing set of rows; run it on a whole database."],
["graphs.html#limits-and-next-steps","Graphs in ChloeDB","Limits and next steps","One quality task, one embedder. With a stronger embedder, vector search would score above 0.123 and the relative gain over it would likely shrink. The gains over BM25 (+57%) and hybrid search (+58%) are the more robust numbers. Cross-segment hops cost lookups , two to five times the in-memory CSR on these graphs. A snapshot-wide resolved CSR, rebuilt when the segment set changes, would close the gap for read-mostly tables; merging into fewer segments narrows it today. No patterns. There is no multi-hop pattern matching with a predicate per step; through and labels cover one constraint per traversal. SQL/PGQ's MATCH would be the standard syntax for more. Unweighted paths. Weights are used by PageRank only. Walks across shards. A coordinator refuses graph queries ( above ). It could run them itself, asking the shards for the edges of each frontier and sending them the rows it reached with their distances and PageRank scores, at a round trip per hop. That needs a call to read edges by key and a plan that carries the rows' values, so it is a change of the shard protocol. ChloeCtx. Its records have natural edges the store does not keep yet: a distilled note to the turn it came from, a superseded decision to its replacement, an error to the edit that fixed it. The arXiv result suggests the pattern to try (recall's best hits as seeds, then the records linked to them), but ChloeCtx's earlier study found that widening a hit to its neighbouring records gained little, so this needs its own measurement first. The full report, with every table, is ChloeDB graphs . Reproducing the results has the commands."],
["next-items.html","Next items","","What a user will open, watch or buy next, from what came after the same items before: counts kept in an ordinary table as events arrive, read with backoff from the last two items to the last one. They update with every event, explain themselves, and are the baseline that a learned sequence model has to beat."],
["next-items.html#counts-in-a-table","Next items","Counts in a table","Each row counts how often one item came right after one context: the last item, or the last two. The key is the context's items and then the next item, so with cluster_by = 'id' a context's rows sit together. In Python, chloedb.ngrams writes the statement ( ngrams.create_table() ) or the schema ( ngrams.schema() , for chloedb.open or chloedb-server --schema ), the writes of an event and the read: ngrams.ops(history, item) returns one add per order, each of which creates its row on the context's first event, so an event costs two writes; order=3 also counts the last three items. They are the operations chloedb-server's /v1/write takes ( writes ), so Client.write sends them and Database.write applies them in-process. Items are strings without the two separators the keys use ( \\x1f between a context's items, \\x1e before the next item). Counts can decay in place, so that recent behaviour weighs more: UPDATE next SET n = n * 0.9 rewrites no row, and a row whose weight reaches 0 is no longer read."],
["next-items.html#reading-with-backoff","Next items","Reading with backoff","next_items(db, history, k=10, order=2, backoff=0.4) scores each item by stupid backoff ( Brants et al., 2007 ): an item seen after the last two items scores its share of that context's weight; one seen only after the last item scores its share there times 0.4; a pair never seen backs off the same way. It returns (item, score) pairs, best first, ties by item. The answer is the same as scoring every item of both contexts, but it reads less. Per context it runs a sum and then reads rows best first, a page at a time, and stops at the first row that cannot reach the top k . Then it checks that the items it takes from the last item's context have no row in the pair's context, by their keys. Over a server it is the same SQL, which any language can run:"],
["next-items.html#measured","Next items","Measured","On Amazon Beauty (22,363 users' reviews of 12,101 products in time order, the 5-core set sequential recommenders report on), each user's last item predicted from the items before it, ranked among all items, with the counts in-process on one thread of a 4-core cloud VM (Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz): counts from order Recall@10 NDCG@10 p50 ms p99 ms every transition before each user's last item 1 0.0688 0.0459 0.57 1.46 2 0.0694 0.0450 0.64 1.69 only those before the last two, as SASRec trains 1 0.0607 0.0404 0.49 1.23 2 0.0613 0.0398 0.57 1.38 SASRec's published numbers on the same split are 0.0605 and 0.0318 (as TIGER reports them, from P5), so counted the way SASRec trains, counts match its recall and beat its NDCG: a strong baseline on this data. Two caveats: 43.5% of users wrote their last two reviews on the same day, which the usual preprocessing leaves in product order and which favours counted transitions; and with under nine reviews per user, the pair's context adds little over the last item's (order 3 adds nothing). Answers were the same as scoring every item for the first 2,000 users. Fewer than 10 scored items were filled with the most frequent ones. Script and results: results/sequence/ngrams_beauty.py and ngrams-beauty.json . A sequence model ( encode() ) trained on the same split, reading items only, has 0.0662 and 0.0406 ( results ), at 0.6 ms a query. On Beauty, Sports and Toys, the walk of the next section ranks above both that model and a generative decoder over semantic IDs , and rrf of the model's scores and the walk's ranks above the walk: on Beauty, Recall@10 0.0834 and NDCG@10 0.0511, and Recall@10 0.0855 with the decoder's answer added ( results )."],
["next-items.html#several-steps-ahead","Next items","Several steps ahead","Counts predict the next item. For items further ahead, or for \"seen together\", the same events can feed an edge table , whose edges are directed: adding 1/ g to the edge from the item g steps back to the new one records what comes after what, and ppr(cooc, :x, dir => 'out') walks forward from the last items. dir => 'both' , ppr 's default, reads the same edges as seen together. A walk runs on one database: a coordinator refuses ppr and the other graph queries ( graphs on a cluster ), while the counts above run anywhere. Personalized PageRank pushes a row only while its share of the walk is at least eps (1e-4 by default) times the sum of its edges' weights, so a single seed whose edges add up to more than 10,000 is never pushed and the walk returns nothing. Pass a smaller eps , as above, or keep the weights decayed or normalised."],
["next-items.html#kept-for-you-create-recommender","Next items","Kept for you: CREATE RECOMMENDER","CREATE RECOMMENDER shop ON events(usr, item, ts) ITEMS items(emb) keeps the counts of order 1 and items seen together from the writes of an events table, and ORDER BY recommend('shop', :u) fuses them with the similarity of the user's recent items and with popularity, leaving out what the user had ( recommenders ). It runs on one database; a coordinator refuses it, and the tables of this page are what a cluster uses."],
["next-items.html#which-to-use","Next items","Which to use","Counts are exact and live: an event changes the next answer, and each suggestion is explained by a count. They know only the last one or two items and nothing about a context never seen, where they fall back to the last item alone. A walk over directed edges reaches several steps ahead and related items, at five writes per event. Learned sequence models, which read long histories and combine what they have not seen together, are the next step; these counts are the baseline they have to beat."],
["slices.html","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","","A slice is a copy of the rows of a table near a device's user, which the device searches itself: near in space (a geo slice, for \"near me\" search) or near in meaning ( a vector slice , for recommendations and \"more like this\"). chloedb-server builds a geo slice from a table with a text field and a latitude and longitude: a manifest (the schema, a grid of square tiles, the text field's statistics) and one file of rows per tile. An app fetches the manifest and the tiles around its user, and searches them with the server's statistics, so every place it holds scores exactly as on the server. When it can prove that its answer is the server's, nothing leaves the device; otherwise it sends the same query to the server. Each answer says where it was answered and why."],
["slices.html#when-an-answer-comes-from-the-device","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","When an answer comes from the device","The server ranks by bm25(text, q) / (1 + d² / d0²) , where d is the distance to the user and d0 the distance at which a place counts half. A place the device does not hold is at least e away, the distance to the nearest tile it lacks, so it scores at most the sum of the query words' best scores (in the manifest) divided by 1 + e² / d0² . When the k-th score on the device reaches that, the device's top k is the server's. Each answer's why is one of: why where what it means proved device the k-th local score beats the most a place not held could score no_match device no place in the table has a word of the search short server the device holds fewer than k matching places bound server a place beyond the tiles held could have scored higher stale server the table changed since the slice was built So a device answers short searches for common things near its user (pizza, a pharmacy) itself, and asks the server for rare things, for large k, and for wide d0 ."],
["slices.html#turn-it-on","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","Turn it on","Slices exist only on a server started with --slices DIR . Build one from the command center ( Data → Slices → Build a slice ) or over HTTP: lat0 , lon0 are the grid's origin (the middle of the area, usually), tile_m the side of a tile in metres. POST /v1/admin/slices with {\"table\": \"places\"} alone rebuilds a slice as it was built, and --slices-rebuild-s N rebuilds every stale slice each N seconds. Without a slice the helpers below fail to open, and an app sends its searches to the server as it would without them."],
["slices.html#fresh-or-stale","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","Fresh or stale","Before a device trusts its slice it asks GET /v1/slices/<table>/status (every 30 seconds by default), which answers the slice's version and whether the table changed since the build. A device fetches a slice of another version, and while the table has changed it sends every search to the server ( max_lag lets it keep answering for a while). A slice is a snapshot: a row written after the build is not on any device until the next build."],
["slices.html#from-python","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","From Python","chloedb.nearby(url, table, dir, token=None, reach=2, status_every=30, max_lag=0, report_every=20) : reach is how many tiles each way it holds (2 is a 5 by 5 square), dir a cache it may delete. The result is a Result with where , why , bound , edge_m , device_ms , server_ms , and ask , the request that asks the server."],
["slices.html#from-javascript","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","From JavaScript","It runs where the native addon does (Node, Bun, Deno), with the same options as Python's in camel case ( statusEvery and maxLag in milliseconds)."],
["slices.html#vector-slices-more-like-this-on-the-device","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","Vector slices: \"more like this\" on the device","A slice can also cut a table by meaning: its VECTOR field into k-means clusters, each a file of rows. A device holds the clusters nearest its user's taste (a vector, such as the user's history summed) and answers similarity searches itself (recommendations for the user, tracks like the one playing) when it can prove that no row it lacks could beat its k-th. clusters sets their number (about the square root of the rows by default, at most 4,096 then). The server's search is exact ( similarity(emb, :v, strategy => 'flat', rerank => <rows>) re-scores every row), so a device's proved answer is the server's to the last bit. The manifest holds each cluster's centre, radius, angular radius and longest and shortest vector, from which a device bounds the most a row of a cluster it lacks could score. In many dimensions that bound is loose (a cluster's rows point every way from its centre, so none comes near the worst case), and on its own it proves few answers. So the slice also holds codes.bin.gz : every row's vector as one byte a dimension (64 bytes a row for 64 dimensions, a sixteenth of the rows' JSON), which bounds each row within half a step a dimension. A device that fetched the codes proves most searches near its user's taste, and when it cannot, the answer names the few clusters ( need ) whose rows could still beat its k-th. Once it holds them its answer is proved, so the helpers fetch them when they come to at most refine bytes (2 MB by default) and answer on the device. focus keeps the clusters that fit in rows rows and drops those that fall beyond half as many again, so a taste that drifts does not fetch and drop the same clusters over and over. codes=False ( codes: false ) skips the codes; {\"codes\": false} builds a slice without them. On Yambda's 629,298 tracks (64-dimension vectors, 4,599 users, measured in a cloud container; the replay has every number), with 4,096 clusters and 20,000 tracks on the device: search proved by the clusters' centres proved with the codes proved after fetching what the codes name recommend for me, top 10 4.5% 94.9% 100%, a median 64 KB when it fetches recommend for me, top 100 2.1% 61.3% 100%, a median 157 KB more like the last track, top 10 4.6% 54.6% 100%, a median 340 KB The codes are 30 bytes a track gzipped (19 MB for the catalogue). No proved answer was wrong. More clusters (smaller ones) make each fetch smaller and the manifest larger: 4,096 clusters of this catalogue make a 3.6 MB manifest, the default 793 a 0.7 MB one with fetches about seven times as large."],
["slices.html#what-the-server-sees","Slices: search on the device, the server as fallback","What the server sees","Each helper reports its counts every 20 searches ( POST /v1/slices/<table>/usage : searches, why, time on the device and on the server), and the server counts the manifests and tiles it serves. The command center's Slices view shows them since the server started: the share answered on the device, the devices, a map of the tiles by rows, size or downloads, the reasons searches went to the server, the last hour by minute, the busiest tiles, and the slice's version with Rebuild and Delete. For a vector slice the map is a grid of its clusters, busiest first: route what POST /v1/admin/slices builds a geo slice ( text , lat , lon , lat0 , lon0 ) or a vector slice ( vector , clusters , codes ), or with {table} alone rebuilds one as it was built GET /v1/admin/slices every slice, with its counts, and the tables that could have one GET , DELETE /v1/admin/slices/<table> a slice's tiles and last hour; deletes it GET /v1/slices/<table>/manifest.json , /tiles/<x>_<y>.jsonl.gz , /codes.bin.gz the files a device fetches (read scope); a vector slice's tile x is its cluster GET /v1/slices/<table>/status version, built, fresh, changed POST /v1/slices/<table>/usage a device's counts chloedb-slice (in the chloedb crate) writes the same files from a database file without a server, for any web server or CDN to serve. chloedb-server/demo/nearby is a demo over New York's places."],
["design.html","ChloeDB: design","","ChloeDB is to search what SQLite is to relational data: a library that keeps a whole database in one file, needs no server or configuration, gives ACID transactions, and is queried with SQL. Every table is indexed for full text (posting lists), structured columns (a columnar forward index) and vectors, rows can hold graph edges to other rows, and a cost-based planner combines all of it in one statement. This page is the design: the file format, transactions, every index and the planner. To use ChloeDB, start with the README or Build an app ."],
["design.html#goals","ChloeDB: design","Goals","Embedded and single-file. One .chloedb file plus a -wal sidecar, like SQLite in WAL mode. Opening a database maps the file and loads nothing eagerly, so databases larger than RAM work and a cold open costs the same whatever the size. ACID with snapshot isolation. One writer, any number of readers. Readers never block and always see a consistent snapshot; a crash at any point leaves the last committed state. Threads that write at once take turns, and those that wait share the next commit. SQL with search extensions. SELECT … WHERE … ORDER BY … LIMIT , GROUP BY for facets, plus MATCH (boolean full text), bm25() , similarity() and distance() (vectors and learned sparse weights), rrf() , weighted sums and products (fusion and boosts), decay() (closeness to a value or a time) and graph traversal, and INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE . The query language page is the reference. Index families, freely combined. Full text (BM25, boolean and phrase queries), structured fields (equality, IN , ranges, facets) and vectors (approximate and exact nearest neighbours), in any combination: filtered vector search, filtered BM25, hybrid ranking, facets over any result. Graphs over the same rows. A row can hold typed, weighted edges to other rows. Traversals, shortest paths and personalized PageRank run in the same statement as everything else ( Graphs ). Disk-aware. Every structure is read in place through mmap , with no deserialisation, and laid out so that a query's I/O is proportional to its work. One process writes a database at a time; other processes can read it while it is written . Joins across tables, and sharding and replication in chloedb-server , came later; current limits has where they stand."],
["design.html#architecture","ChloeDB: design","Architecture","A statement goes through four stages. The binder turns SQL into a predicate tree and a scorer; the planner picks, per segment and from exact statistics, how to evaluate each part; operators run against zero-copy views of the segment's sections; per-segment results are merged."],
["design.html#storage-engine","ChloeDB: design","Storage engine",""],
["design.html#immutable-segments-not-a-b-tree","ChloeDB: design","Immutable segments, not a B-tree","SQLite updates B-trees in place. Compressed posting lists, bit-packed columns and vector graphs do not survive that: inserting one docID into a compressed partition means re-encoding it, and the best encodings are chosen per whole list. SQLite's own FTS5 already stores its index as immutable segments merged in the background, as do Lucene, Tantivy and PISA. ChloeDB uses that log-structured design for the whole table: every index lives in immutable segments, writes go to a WAL and an in-memory memtable, and segments are merged in the background."],
["design.html#one-file","ChloeDB: design","One file","Pages are 4 KiB. Pages 0 and 1 are two meta slots (magic, format version, transaction id, manifest and freelist locations, page count, CRC32); the rest are extents, runs of pages holding segment data, manifests, delete sets and freelists. Segments are written once, never modified, and freed only when no snapshot references them. Backups. While a database is open, its rows since the last flush are in the -wal sidecar only, so a copy of the file alone misses them, and copying both while it is written is not consistent either. The safe ways: Database::checkpoint(dest) (Python db.checkpoint(dest) , chloedb-server POST /v1/admin/checkpoint ) writes a consistent copy while the database is in use, checking every segment against its checksum first, so that damage is never copied into a backup under a fresh checksum, and checking the copy before naming it; and a clean close ( Db::close , close() in the bindings) flushes the rows in memory into the file and leaves the log empty, so the file alone is then a complete copy, as Database::flush makes it at any moment until the next write. The file is read through one mmap . Sections read at random positions (the HNSW graph and its codes, float32 vectors for re-ranking, dictionaries, the doc store, the key index) are advised MADV_RANDOM , so a cold page fault reads one page instead of the kernel's read-around of up to 32. Sections read a run at a time (postings, document lengths, columns, IVF clusters) keep read-ahead. Readers open lazily per field, so a query touches only the headers of the fields it uses."],
["design.html#commits-by-shadow-paging","ChloeDB: design","Commits by shadow paging","A torn meta write fails its checksum and the other slot still names the previous, fully written state. Nothing reachable from a valid meta slot is ever overwritten (the LMDB discipline), so there is no rollback journal. When the newest version itself is damaged (its meta slot, manifest or free list fails its checksum, a segment it names does not open, or the file is shorter than it says), the version in the other slot is still whole, but falling back to it is right only if the write-ahead log still holds what the newer version had flushed: otherwise the log, emptied after that flush, would be replayed onto a version it does not follow, and acknowledged rows would be gone without a word. Opening falls back only when the log's transactions show it covers the gap (the newer version flushed nothing, the log still holds a transaction it flushed, or it holds the first transaction after the older version's), says so in Database::damaged , and the next commit overwrites the damaged slot. Otherwise it fails, naming the file, what is damaged and the way out, and changes nothing (it no longer cuts the newer version's pages off the file first): restore a backup, or open with DbOptions::recover (Python recover=True , chloedb-server --recover ), which copies the file to <path>.damaged-<time> , opens the version before, and says in Database::damaged which transactions were lost (chloedb-server then reports degraded in /v1/health , and /v1/admin/check fails). A log whose header is damaged, or names another database, while it holds committed transactions is refused the same way, where it used to be replaced by an empty one. A meta slot of zeros counts as damaged where a version was written there: slot 0 always (a database is created with it), slot 1 once slot 0 holds a later version than the one the database was created with (commits take the slots in turn), or once the log starts past the transactions after that version, or when pages past it sit beside no log at all. Slot 1 of zeros beside the version of creation and an empty log is what a crash during the first commit leaves, so it opens as that. The main file is not written between versions and each commit of one empties the log, so neither can say that a log which is gone held transactions: the writer says it in a second record in <path>-readers/WRITER ( WRITER in the directory layout), the log record, which names the database and the version on disk, written and synced before the first append after that version, after a version made while the log holds transactions it does not (a background flush's, a merge's), and at an open that replays a log. When the record names the database and the version being opened, the log file is missing, and no sealed log is beside it, opening (read-only too) fails, naming the log, the transactions committed after that version and recover , and changes nothing; recover opens the version on disk, says in Database::damaged what was lost, and clears the record. A crash never removes a log (it is replaced only by a rename over it), sealing one leaves the sealed log, and a checkpoint or a copy of the file has a database id or a path of its own, so only a deleted log is refused: after a clean close, which flushes, deleting the log loses nothing and opens. Not caught: a log deleted together with -readers (or WRITER ), a copy of the file opened elsewhere without its log, and a log deleted while a background flush holds a sealed one. Whatever sync is, a version's pages are synced before its meta slot names them, and so is the WAL before it, so that the version to fall back to is always whole and no transaction outlives one it came after. The meta slot is synced last, since the next commit overwrites the other one. Without sync , then, a power loss loses the transactions since the last flush, newest first, and nothing else. A new database is written under a hidden name beside its path ( .<name>.creating-… ), then linked (a file) or renamed (a directory) to its name whole, so a crash while it is created leaves a database there or nothing, and the path can be created again. Every manifest, free list, delete set and segment has a CRC32, and so does each section of a segment (a field's postings, positions or column, the doc store, the keys). Opening checks the meta slots, the manifest, the free list and every delete set and update file, but not whole segments, which would read the entire database at each start. Instead a query checks each section of a segment against its CRC32 the first time it reads that section, which reads the whole section once, and fails rather than answer from damaged bytes, while queries that read other sections go on ( DbOptions::check_sections , on by default; the keys are checked as the segment opens, and segments written before sections had a CRC32 are not checked this way). The doc store, which holds every stored value and is most of a segment's bytes, is checked a block at a time instead: each of its compressed blocks (16 KiB of rows) has a CRC32 of its own (the section store.crc , 4 bytes a block), checked when a query first decompresses the block, so the first row fetched reads one block rather than the whole doc store (a segment written before store.crc checks its doc store whole, and older versions of ChloeDB pass the section over). While it checks a section the kernel may read ahead in it, even in one advised to be read at random. On a database of 1,000,000 documents (687 MB: text with positions, 64-dimensional HNSW vectors), with the file in the page cache, opening took 4 ms more, the first text query 13 to 17 ms more and the first vector query 41 to 49 ms more; with it evicted, opening took 14 ms more and the first text query 45 to 275 ms more, while the first vector query was no slower, since reading its sections in long runs saves the search's page-at-a-time reads; later queries cost the same ( results , three rounds on the cloud container). A reader also checks the structure of the segment data it uses: offsets and lengths against the section that holds them, a posting list's partitions against the list's length and docID range, and the whole of a long text posting list (512 postings or more: its docIDs in order, its encodings consistent) the first time a query reads it, so a damaged segment cannot make ChloeDB read outside the file. A query that reaches damage these checks let through can still fail with a panic (chloedb-server answers 500 and goes on). A merge checks every segment it reads against its checksum first, so that damage is never copied into a new segment under a fresh checksum, and DbOptions::verify_checksums (chloedb-server's --verify-checksums ) checks them all at open, where a damaged segment in the newest version means falling back to the version before. Replay also checks each WAL operation against its table's schema and treats one that could not have been committed as damage, like a failed checksum. The fuzz targets in chloedb/fuzz (cargo fuzz) feed the engine damaged posting lists, segments, WALs, manifests and free lists, and arbitrary SQL; chloedb/tests/corrupt_input.rs runs the same targets on a few thousand inputs in CI. chloedb/tests/sql_oracles.rs checks SQL's answers with SQLancer's oracles (Rigger and Su), which need no second engine: a random condition splits a table into the rows where it is true, false and unknown, and rows, counts, sums, minimums, maximums, groups, DISTINCT values and HAVING over the whole table must be those of the three parts combined (ternary logic partitioning); the same condition counted, returned, and run over a derived table, which filters and groups in memory, must agree (NoREC); pages must be slices of the whole order; and two or three shards must answer as one database, over rows in segments and the memtable, deleted and updated rows and multi-valued columns ( CHLOEDB_SQL_ORACLE_RUNS scales the number of queries). Miri runs in CI too, over the zero-copy byte views that every index reads its data through and the posting lists, columns and keys read through them, checking each access for undefined behaviour (it found views of Vec<u8> buffers that relied on the allocator for their 8-byte alignment; such bytes are now copied to an aligned start). A test layer ( storage::io , feature fault-injection ) records every write, truncation and sync, and builds what a power loss at any call could leave of each file, sector by sector, or makes a call fail as a full or failing disk would (a failed sync loses the unsynced writes, as Linux does). tests/storage_faults.rs runs a workload of transactions, flushes, merges, vacuums and bulk loads with each of its several hundred calls failing in turn and checks the reopened database; CI runs it on Linux and macOS. tests/storage_model.rs checks the storage API against a model of every row: random transactions on two tables (inserts, upserts, conditional upserts, deletes, values set and added in place, rollbacks), SQL writes, flushes, merges, vacuums, bulk loads and reopens, in both layouts, with the rows, key lookups and eight queries (ranges, keywords, text, floats, NULLs, a NOT, a count and a top-k) compared after each step, and every pinned snapshot still returning the rows of its moment ( CHLOEDB_MODEL_SEEDS=100 runs more seeds than CI's eight)."],
["design.html#wal-memtable-and-snapshots","ChloeDB: design","WAL, memtable and snapshots","Small transactions would otherwise write a segment each. Instead, inserts and deletes append checksummed records to the -wal sidecar and fsync at commit; recovery ignores uncommitted records. An append writes over zeros the writer wrote past the last record beforehand, 256 KiB at a time ( wal::AHEAD ), so that a commit does not change the file's length: an fdatasync after a write that grew the file has to make the new length durable too, and on a disk slow to sync that took twice as long as one after a write over blocks already written (1.20 ms against 0.54 ms on the node of the 16 November benchmarks ). Replay stops at the zeros, a record of length 0, as at a torn tail; opening, closing, a flush and a seal cut them off, and versions from before them cut them off as a torn tail. FileStats::wal_bytes counts the records, not the zeros. A write transaction holds the writer lock from Database::begin to its commit, and a second begin waits for it, up to DbOptions::write_wait (5 s; chloedb-server sets 0 and retries until each request's deadline), before it fails with Busy . Db 's writes ( Db::write_ops , SQL INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE , and the Python, JavaScript and Java packages' writes) share commits (group commit): while one commits, the writes that come wait, and the next transaction takes them all, one fsync for the group, each write still answered alone (when the group fails, each runs again by itself, so only one at fault fails). SQL statements share a transaction only when no two can touch the same row ( which ones ), since a statement finds its rows in the last committed version, which does not show the transaction's own changes. Replay stops at the first damaged record and cuts the log there, after copying it to -wal.damaged if commits follow the damage. With sync , more than one commit after the damage means the log was damaged after it was durable, not torn by a crash during the last append, and opening fails unless DbOptions::wal_drop_damaged (chloedb-server's --wal-drop-damaged ) says to drop them. The memtable holds the WAL's documents. Its chunks of 1,024 documents are append-only: a full chunk is built once as a small in-memory segment, and the last one, which writes still fill, is built only when a query needs its indexes, at most once per change of it and shared by every snapshot of that change. Queries therefore see committed writes at once, and the memtable needs no index code of its own, while a commit indexes nothing: single-row writes no longer rebuild a chunk each. A read by key ( Snapshot::find , rows by key ) reads the last chunk's documents as written, newest first, and probes the segments' key indexes, so it builds nothing either. With publish_snapshots on (ChloeCtx turns it on), the writer publishes the snapshot with the commit and readers only take it. When it grows past a threshold it is flushed into a segment by a shadow-paging commit and the WAL is truncated. With a flusher thread ( Database::spawn_flusher ; chloedb-server starts one) the commit that fills the memtable does not wait for that: the flusher seals the memtable and the WAL (renamed to -wal.<txn> , with commits going on in a new one), builds the segments while writes go on, and installs them in a commit that carries over what those writes deleted or set in place in the sealed documents, keeps the rest in the memtable, and deletes the sealed log. Opening replays a sealed log no flush installed before the open one. The flusher holds the writer lock only to seal and to install, and writes that start meanwhile wait for it instead of failing with Busy . Once the memtable holds twice its threshold, new writes wait for the flush under way, so they go at the pace of flushes. A commit that returns Ok is durable and one that returns an error wrote nothing, so a failed commit can be retried: a flush that fails after the WAL append (a full disk, say) does not fail the commit, shows in Database::flush_error , and is tried again by the next commit, and writes are refused before they reach the WAL once the memtable holds twice its threshold while flushes still fail. Bulk loads bypass the WAL. A database can also number its writes, the order replication ships them in ( Database::start_seq_no , which cannot be undone): each write transaction gets the next sequence number under the database's term ( Database::begin_term raises it when a replica becomes primary), logged in its commit record and kept across flushes, crashes, checkpoints and reopening. Its WAL then starts STRWAL02 , with a header naming the features its records use, and the database needs feature seq_no , so older versions refuse it rather than lose the numbers; other databases log as before. Each row of a numbered database keeps the number of the write that last changed it ( Database::seq_of ), in its segment and beside values set in place; rows written before numbering, bulk loaded or attached have number 0. A bulk load and attached segments are not shipped, so they only seed such a database: once it has numbered a write, or its commits are followed ( Database::on_commit , as a primary's and a replica's are), it refuses them, and the importers ( ingest::import , Arrow, SQLite and Parquet) upsert in transactions instead. With DbOptions::ship_log_bytes it also keeps its latest transactions as it logged them, which Database::log_from reads for a replica and Database::apply_shipped applies on one, under the primary's numbers, after checking that they follow the replica's last transaction and come from its term or a newer one. A replica that the log no longer reaches installs a checkpoint of the primary instead, in one commit ( Database::install_copy ). chloedb-server ships them from a primary to its replicas, and sends a replica too far behind a copy ( replication ). A snapshot says the last transaction it holds ( Snapshot::seq_no ), so a read can wait on a replica for a version that holds a given write ( reads that see a write ), and lists each live row's key and number ( Snapshot::row_versions ), which a primary and its replicas compare by digests ( comparing copies ). A replica is made the primary of a new term by hand, which fences the old primary ( promotion ). Every document has a primary key. Each segment has a primary-key index and a delete set, stored as its own small extent and replaced copy-on-write. The index is a radix directory over the top bits of the keys' 64-bit hashes, about four entries a slot, and an entry per document of its key's fingerprint (the hash's low 32 bits) and its docID, 8 bytes; a lookup reads the slot, then the key of each entry whose fingerprint matches, to compare it. A KEYWORD key that is indexed, as one is unless its options say indexed = false , is read from the field's keyword column, which holds each key once already, so the index adds about 9 bytes a row; an INT key or one that is not indexed is kept in the index. A database written before field ids keeps the layout of the versions before (16-byte entries of the whole hash and the docID, and the keys in the index). A segment of more than 4,096 keys also gets a split-block Bloom filter of its key hashes, in memory only, built from the index on its first lookup: a read by key probes the segments newest first until one holds the key, so most probes are of segments that lack it, and those mostly end at the filter's one cache line. An update is a delete plus an insert. A snapshot is an immutable manifest version (segments, their delete sets, collection statistics) held by reference count: readers pin one, the writer publishes the next. Taking one costs at most 0.13 µs. A database can hold several tables, each with its own schema, segments, memtable and merges, under one WAL, one writer and one manifest: a transaction can write several tables at once, and a snapshot sees all of them at the same version. SQL creates and drops tables ( CREATE TABLE , DROP TABLE ) and adds, drops and renames their columns while they hold rows ( ALTER TABLE ), and each statement reads or writes the table it names. Format 3 is the last format number: its manifest lists the features the database uses (field ids, columns added to a table that held rows, BOOL and TIMESTAMP columns, NOT NULL and DEFAULT , a text analyzer other than english , MEDIA fields, JSON fields and those whose strings are text, numbered writes, rank profiles, rows that expire at a time they hold, expire_by , a doc store compressed with zstd, zstd , the key index of a table with a primary key, compact_keys , and positions in the packed layout, packed_positions ), and a later change adds a feature instead of a number. A version of ChloeDB refuses a database that needs a feature it does not have, and says which. Every field has an id, which names it on disk: in its segments' section names, their stored rows and the values set in place, and in a list of ids each segment keeps, which check compares with the manifest. The manifest keeps each table's ids and the next one to give (feature field_ids ), so a field is never read by its position or its name. It is the ground for changing a table's fields without rewriting its segments: ALTER TABLE adds a field under a new id, which a segment written before reads as the field's default (feature added_fields ), drops one by leaving its id out, and renames one under the same id. Every database this version creates has field ids. One written before them named its fields by position and name, in format 1 (one table), 2 (several) or 3; this version refuses to open it and says how to copy it: chloedb::upgrade::upgrade(path) ( chloedb-server upgrade PATH , chloedb.upgrade(path) in Python) writes a new database beside it with the same tables, rows, vectors and rank profiles, in the same layout, puts it in its place and keeps the old one as <path>.before-field-ids . A crash during the copy leaves the old database where it was, and one between the two renames is finished by the next call. ChloeCtx copies each of its stores the first time it opens it. A database that numbers its writes (replication) or keeps stream offsets is not copied, since its copy could not go on from their numbers: create a new one and fill it with Db::load_from . Databases that earlier versions wrote, in both layouts, are kept in the repository ( chloedb/tests/fixtures ): every later version's tests check that it refuses them, opens them to be copied, copies them, and writes the sets of this version byte for byte."],
["design.html#merges","ChloeDB: design","Merges","A tiered policy merges segments of similar size. A merge reads the live documents of its inputs back (stored fields from the doc store, vectors and unstored fields from their indexes, generated columns recomputed) and rebuilds every index, which drops deleted documents and re-optimises encodings for the longer lists. An HNSW graph starts from its inputs' graphs, as Lucene's merges do. The graph of the largest input that lost no documents is kept, its nodes with their levels and links (Lucene 9.9), and the graphs of the other whole inputs are joined to it (Lucene 10.2): in each, a set of nodes that covers the rest (every other node has a quarter of its neighbours in it) is inserted as usual, and every other node searches layer 0 from its old neighbours already in the merged graph and their neighbours there, with half the beam. Graphs are built and merged on their SQ8 codes, as they are searched, reading a byte per dimension instead of four (build option vector.hnsw.build ; f32 scores with float32 rows). Merging four segments of 25,000 TREC-COVID vectors (1,024 dimensions) so takes 12.2 to 12.3 s, against 29.6 to 30.0 s on float32 rows from the largest input's graph and 37.9 s from scratch, at recall@10 0.987 to 0.989 at ef 64 against 0.992 to 0.993 (at ef 100, 0.992 to 0.993 against 0.995). At 64 dimensions, where a row is 256 bytes, the codes save nothing and joining takes 4.7 to 4.9 s against 5.6 to 5.7 s, at the same recall (two runs each on the cloud container's 4-core Xeon, four threads, examples/graph_start.rs ; the queries are the collection's 50 and 500 documents not indexed). A whole TREC-COVID graph (171,332 vectors) builds in 36.6 s on its codes and 82.0 s on float32 rows, with recall@10 within 0.014 at every ef from 10 to 256 and within 0.006 from 16 on (one run each, 50 queries, chloedb-vector-bench ). vacuum moves data down and shrinks the file. A merge does not hold all its inputs' documents at once. It decodes them in batches of about 32 MB of input image (16,384 to 262,144 documents, a batch spanning inputs), and the segment builder takes each batch as it comes: what has to see every document before it can be written (a TEXT field's posting runs, the primary keys) is kept from earlier batches in compact form (runs packed as varints, keys in one buffer), while another thread decodes the next batch. The merged image is then streamed into its reserved space a megabyte at a time, checksummed on the way, rather than assembled in memory first. It is the same bytes as a build of all the documents at once. Merging four segments of 400,000 short texts (225 MB of image) so peaks at 2.6 times the input on one thread and 3.5 to 3.8 on four, instead of 4.3 and 5.2 to 5.4; four of 40,000 long texts (376 MB) at 1.9 and 2.5 instead of 3.9 and 5.0, in 17.5 to 17.6 s instead of 22.6 to 22.9 s on one thread and 7.7 to 7.8 s instead of 10.9 to 11.3 s on four; and four of 150,000 texts with 64-dim vectors (443 MB) at 1.7 and 2.3 instead of 2.7 and 3.3 to 3.6. A merge that fits in one or two batches takes about what it did (two runs each on the cloud container's 4-core Xeon, examples/merge_memory.rs ). A merge of more than merge_spill_bytes of live input image (default 64 MB) also writes its output as it goes, to unnamed temporary files beside the database: the sections that grow with every document (a vector field's float32 rows, the doc store's compressed blocks) a megabyte at a time as the batches come in, and every other section of 64 KB or more once it is finished. A file loses its name as soon as it is created, so a crash leaves nothing behind (opening removes one a process died too early to remove), and its writes keep to write_bytes_per_sec . Writing the image then copies each section from its file and gives the file's space back as it goes (on Linux), so the disk holds at most one more copy of the merged segment meanwhile. The merge so holds its builders' working state and not its output, and the image is the same bytes. Merging eight segments of 125,000 rows shaped like the scale probe's (256-dim vectors in an HNSW graph and a 63-word text, 1,963 MB of image) on four threads peaks at 1.72 to 1.76 GB instead of 3.15 GB, in 259 to 261 s instead of 286 s; eight of 25,000 (395 MB), at 443 to 488 MB instead of 774 to 799 MB, in 44.4 to 44.9 s instead of 43.1 to 44.0 s ( examples/merge_memory.rs , one run of the 1M merge without spilling, two of the others). The 2M merge of the scale probe's MS MARCO table (eight segments of 250,000 passages, 4.5 GB of image, 64% of it float32 rows and doc store) peaks at 4.0 to 4.1 GB of heap instead of 7.6 GB, in 240 s against 231 and 277 s (two runs each; the other spilling run took 328 s with a compile beside it; chloedb-scale , results ); before merges decoded their inputs in batches it was estimated at 16 to 18 GB. Each figure is from the cloud container's 4-core Xeon. A merge that spills then returns the heap it freed to the system (glibc's malloc_trim ): after the 200,000-row merge the process keeps 195 MB resident instead of 315 MB. Merges stay out of the way of writes. A merge builds its segment with no lock held, then reserves a segment id and space for the image (free pages of the file, or a file of its own in the directory layout) and writes it there, also with no lock held. It takes the writer lock only to install the image, in a commit that marks the input documents deleted since it started as deleted in the merged segment; writes that start meanwhile wait for it instead of failing with Busy . Until the install, commits leave the reserved space alone and the free list on disk counts it as free, so a crash leaves nothing behind. Several merges can run at once, each on segments no other merge has claimed. With merger threads ( Database::spawn_merger ; chloedb-server starts them) the database merges by itself whenever a version is published and every 5 s: up to threads merges at once (default: half the cores, 1 to 4); within a memory budget, by default half of the process's cgroup memory limit or of the machine's RAM. A merge is estimated from the image bytes of its inputs' live documents: 5.5 + 0.5 × its build threads times the bytes of its first four batches (decoded documents, the builder's structures and the merged sections; measured up to 4.0 times on one thread, 6.6 on four and 8.2 on eight, in merges of short texts small enough to fit one batch), plus, for each byte after them, 2.75 + 0.25 × its build threads times the share of the input in sections the merge holds, which leaves out the float32 rows and the doc store since it spills them (measured 1.9 to 2.7 on one thread and 2.4 to 3.4 on four in merges of 280 to 1,963 MB, 10-word texts taking the most, then texts with 64-dim vectors, long texts, and texts with 256- and 384-dim vectors; merge_spill_bytes set high enough that a merge past four batches does not spill makes it 2 + 0.25 × its build threads times every byte). The scale probe's 2M merge on four threads is so estimated at 7.0 GB, against 4.0 to 4.1 GB measured, where it was estimated at 14.2 GB before merges spilled (and measured 7.6 GB): it fits the default budget of a machine with 16 GB. One that does not fit is cut to its smallest inputs, or waits for running merges to finish; writing no faster than write_bytes_per_sec , if set. The merges running are recorded in a file beside the database ( <file>-merging , or MERGING in the directory). If the process dies during one (killed for running out of memory, typically), opening finds it in the record, and the segments it was merging are then merged only in groups estimated at under half its memory, halving again at each interruption, and are left alone once no group of them fits, rather than restarting into the same merge. Database::merge_status reports the merges running, their memory, the budget, the merges cut to fit and the interrupted ones."],
["design.html#cold-segments-on-object-storage","ChloeDB: design","Cold segments on object storage","In the directory layout, a database can keep segments on object storage (S3, or a store that speaks its API, or a directory such as a mounted file system) and read them through a local cache ( DbOptions::cold ; --cold-store on the server ). Making a segment cold changes nothing in it: its image and a table of the crc32 of each of its 256 KiB parts go to the store under keys only this database uses ( <db id>/seg-<id>.strata and .crc ), its header and keys (the pk and fields sections) are copied into a cache file, and a commit marks the segment cold in the manifest (feature cold_segments , so older versions refuse the database by name) while its local file goes as a merged-away segment's does. A cold segment opens over its cache file, a sparse file of the image's length mapped as a local image is. The header and keys are always there, so opening, writes (which look up keys) and deletes by key never wait on the store. Any other part is fetched the first time a query reads a section that holds it (a whole section; the doc store a 16 KiB block at a time), in runs of up to 8 MB, eight requests at once, checked against its crc32 and written into the cache file, which the mapping then shows. A section is handed out only once all of it has arrived, so a query reads fetched bytes or fails with the store's error; it never reads a hole, and a failure is not kept, so the next query tries again. The cache file outlives the process: reopened, the database finds the parts already there by their checksums. The cache keeps up to cache_bytes (default 8 GiB) of fetched parts besides the headers and keys. Past it, the least recently read segments lose their fetched parts: each is opened again over a new cache file holding its header and keys, swapped in, and the old file is unlinked, so queries already running keep reading it and its space returns when they end. Eviction is by segment, not by part. keep_local_bytes keeps the newest segments local up to that many bytes and makes the older ones (of at least min_segment_bytes , default 16 MB) cold, oldest first, from a background thread ( Database::spawn_cold ), which also evicts. Cold segments are not merged, since their rows would have to be fetched: they are the large ones a tiered policy has stopped merging anyway. One whose rows are all deleted, or past the table's retention, is dropped as any other is. Once no version names a cold segment and no snapshot reads it, its objects are deleted. Uploads and deletes are recorded first in PENDING in the cache directory, so that objects a crash left behind are deleted when the database next opens. A checkpoint fetches cold segments whole and holds them as local files: it opens without the store and shares nothing with the source. A copy made with cp names the source's objects: either may delete objects the other still reads, so it is not a backup, and must not be opened while the source is in use. The cache files are the writer's to fetch into and evict, so a read-only open ( below ) refuses a database once it has cold segments, saying so."],
["design.html#readers-in-other-processes","ChloeDB: design","Readers in other processes","One process writes a database, and others can open it read-only and query it while it is written: the workers of a web server, a script, a command-line shell ( DbOptions::read_only ; read_only=True in Python , readOnly in JavaScript and Java , chloedb --read-only , which the shell also falls back to when another process has the database open to write it). A reader holds what the writer's own process would after opening the database: the version on disk, with the transactions logged since replayed into memtables of its own. Before a snapshot (at most once each DbOptions::refresh_interval ; by default before every one) it looks whether the files changed, with a few small reads and a stat . When only the log grew, it replays what was appended; when there is a new version (a flush, a merge, a vacuum, a table created or dropped) or a new log, it reads the database again and keeps the segments it already has open. A read that a change cut through is read again, so a reader sees each commit whole, in the order they were made, and never goes back. Database::refresh looks at once. Every write to a reader fails, and it starts no flusher and no merger. A reader's memtables take as much memory as the writer's; segments are shared through the page cache. The locks are flock s, so the processes must be on one machine. The writer holds the database file ( LOCK in the directory layout) shared and <file>-readers/WRITER ( WRITER in the directory) exclusively, and, on Linux, a write lock on one byte of the file far past its pages (an open file description's fcntl lock), which readers do not take; a reader holds the database file shared. A second writer finds the byte or WRITER taken and fails with Busy . The byte lock is the file's own, so on Linux a second writer that reaches the file by another name (a symlink, a hard link) or after <file>-readers was deleted is refused too; it used to get a WRITER of its own and write the file beside the first. macOS has no byte lock: its flock and fcntl locks are one kind, so the shared locks on the file would refuse it, and there only WRITER , found by the path, keeps a second writer out. Versions of ChloeDB from before readers take the database file exclusively, so a writer of such a version and a reader or writer of this one keep each other out, and a reader never reads files whose writer does not know about it. The writer writes a new session id into WRITER each time it opens the database (opening may cut a torn log tail), and a reader that sees it change reads the database again from the start. After it, at byte 24, the writer keeps the log record that says the log holds transactions after the version on disk ( commits by shadow paging ); -readers is part of the database for that, and is deleted only with the log. The log is never cut or emptied in place: to start one again the writer writes a new file and renames it over the old one, so a reader reading the old one reads what it held. In the single file, the writer reuses the pages that older versions freed, which a reader could still be reading. Each reader keeps a pin in <file>-readers/ , a 24-byte file it holds locked, with the oldest version it may read: that of its oldest snapshot, or the one it holds. The writer reuses a page only once every pin is past the version that freed it, as it does for its own snapshots, and removes the pins of readers that ended. A reader's own thread looks for changes once a second when no query did, so a reader left idle holds back the pages of a second of writes, not more. A reader of the single file must be able to write in that directory (a read-only file system is fine: nothing writes the file there). A reader that hangs without ending keeps its pin, and with it the pages freed after its version, until it ends. The directory layout needs no pins: its files are written once and only removed, and a reader that finds one gone reads CURRENT again. A reader cannot write a checkpoint yet; back a database up from its writer ( Database::checkpoint )."],
["design.html#segments","ChloeDB: design","Segments","A segment is n documents with local docIDs 0..n , stored as a header and a directory of named sections ( text.body.terms , text.body.post , col.year , vec.emb.ivf , …), each with its offset, length and CRC32. Sections are 64-byte aligned, and those over a page are page-aligned, so any &[u64] view of a section is aligned and an index can be used straight from the mapped file. Each index component has a builder (documents in docID order to section bytes) and a reader (sections to a zero-copy view). A build of 1,024 documents or more runs on several threads (build option build.threads , by default the cores, at most 8): the fields build at once, longest first; a TEXT field's documents are analysed in runs, one per thread, and its posting lists are then encoded in ranges of terms, one per thread. The image is the same bytes as a build on one thread, and as a build that takes the documents in batches ( SegmentBuild , which merges use). On TREC-COVID (171,332 documents, 192 MB of text), a text segment builds in 2.9 to 3.2 s on four threads and 9.0 to 9.7 s on one (two runs each on the cloud container's 4-core Xeon). An HNSW graph uses threads of its own ( vector.threads , default 1; chloedb-server sets the cores), whose inserts race, so its links vary slightly from build to build. Operators run per segment and their results are merged; collection-wide statistics keep BM25 scores comparable across segments. A ranked text query ( bm25 , and the rank-safe boosts of it) searches the largest segments first, and each segment it searches after others starts at the k -th best score they found, skipping from the start what cannot reach it, as Lucene shares its minimum competitive score between segments; the rows and scores are those of one segment holding them all. On the first 5,000,000 MS MARCO passages in three segments, one query per thread as a loaded server runs them, that took bm25 from 496 to 727 queries a second at one client and from 1,904 to 2,789 at four, a filtered bm25 from 230 to 294 and a boost by year from 101 to 134 (medians of two rounds of three passes, the benchmark harness's queries in shard mode, on the cloud container's 4-core Xeon)."],
["design.html#index-families","ChloeDB: design","Index families",""],
["design.html#full-text","ChloeDB: design","Full text","Analyzer. Unicode word split, lowercase, ASCII folding, an English stopword list and a light English stemmer (words longer than 64 bytes are kept whole: the stemmer takes time in the square of a word's length): english , every TEXT field's unless its schema names another: simple keeps every word as written (for names and codes). A field's queries use its analyzer, and the schema records it by name and version, so a version of ChloeDB whose analysis differs refuses the field instead of misreading it. Text in other languages. english and simple read a word as a run of letters and digits, which leaves a Chinese or Japanese sentence one term and cuts Hindi and Thai words at their marks. The standard analyzer reads any script: it normalizes the text (NFKC, so fullwidth letters and ligatures are the plain ones, then lowercase), keeps the words of scripts written with spaces whole with their marks (and 3.14 one number), takes Chinese and Japanese words ( 東京都庁 is 東京 and 都庁 ) and Thai, Lao, Khmer and Burmese words from ICU's dictionaries, makes the overlapping pairs of syllables of Korean its terms ( 서울특별시 is 서울 , 울특 , 특별 , 별시 : a search for a word finds it with whatever particle follows it), and folds the accents of Latin and Greek letters ( café is cafe ), which people leave out of searches. It removes no stopwords, so a phrase matches exactly the words it names, and stems nothing. cjk is standard with pairs of characters for Chinese and Japanese too ( 東京 , 京都 , 都庁 ), for a field where any run of two characters or more must be found whatever a dictionary takes for a word ( 京都庁 finds 東京都庁 , which its words do not), at the price of more terms and a lower rank quality. A language's analyzer is standard with the language's normalization and stemmer before the accents fold: arabic , danish , dutch , finnish , french , german , greek , hungarian , italian , norwegian , portuguese , romanian , russian , spanish , swedish , tamil and turkish stem with Snowball; hindi , bengali and telugu unify their spellings and stem lightly, by the rules of Lucene's analyzers for them; persian unifies the Arabic and Persian forms of its letters; and chinese reads traditional characters as the simplified ones, so a search in either writing finds both. A table names one for a field: body TEXT OPTIONS(analyzer = 'standard') . ICU's dictionaries are pinned to one version, since they decide what a term is. On MIRACL's judged queries in 11 languages ( results ) standard scores nDCG@10 0.457 on Thai, 0.374 on Korean, 0.295 on Japanese and 0.174 on Chinese, where english scores 0.289, 0.262, 0.048 and 0.000, and a language's analyzer adds 0.03 to 0.06 over simple on Arabic, Bengali, Finnish and Hindi. For Hindi, Bengali and Telugu, name the language: standard alone ranks a little below simple there. Term dictionary. Sorted terms in front-coded blocks of 32, with a sparse in-memory index over block first-terms. Each entry gives the document frequency and the posting list's location. Postings. DocIDs, term frequencies and per-block maximum scores, so that top-k can skip blocks. DocIDs of terms in at least 512 documents are Loom lists; rarer terms use BP128 blocks. Frequencies are bit-packed in 128-posting blocks addressed by posting rank. Against BP128 for every list, Loom is 2–4% smaller with 7.6–11× faster conjunctions. Top-k. MaxScore by default; on the BEIR queries it beats Block-Max WAND by 1.9–2.6×. A filter either drives scoring (its members are scored directly) or is probed per candidate, by a fitted crossover. Positions (optional per field) live in a separate stream, read only by phrase queries. They are stored as deltas per posting, and a table created by this version writes them packed ( text.<field>.ppos , feature packed_positions ): each block of 128 postings is one byte of bit width, the block's least delta, and every delta less that least in that many bits (frame of reference). Log lines repeat their words at the same places, so a common word's block often needs 0 to 3 bits a position where a varint needs 8. On four of Loghub's logs, merged with a zstd doc store, positions went from 6.9–13.6 bytes a line to 1.3–5.0 and the database from 54.4–77.9 to 48.1–72.9, while phrase queries took as long, within the runs' noise ( results , on the cloud container's 4-core Xeon). A table created before keeps varints ( text.<field>.pos ) in every segment it writes, so its database does not need the feature. Learned sparse fields. A SPARSE field holds a weight per term, as SPLADE or BGE-M3's sparse head produce, in the same kind of inverted index with the weights quantised into the frequency slot. similarity(field, :q) ranks by the dot product with the query's weights through the same MaxScore and Block-Max WAND, block bounds and filter strategies as BM25. Scores are BM25 with k 1 = 1.2, b = 0.75 and Lucene's idf, over statistics summed across the snapshot's segments, so a document scores the same in any segment: $$ \\mathrm{bm25}(d, q) = \\sum_{t \\in q} \\mathrm{idf}(t)\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{tf} {t,d}\\,(k_1 + 1)}{\\mathrm{tf} {t,d} + k_1\\bigl(1 - b + b\\,\\lvert d \\rvert / \\mathrm{avgdl}\\bigr)}, \\qquad \\mathrm{idf}(t) = \\ln\\Bigl(1 + \\frac{N - \\mathrm{df}_t + 0.5}{\\mathrm{df}_t + 0.5}\\Bigr) $$ N and avgdl count the documents with at least one term in the field, as Lucene's docCount does, so rows without a value do not shrink the average length. Document lengths are quantised to one byte. Ranked queries drop English function words, and a term written twice weighs twice. A relevance gate in CI holds the ranking still: BM25 through SQL on the test splits of SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna (plain, and with fuzzy => 'auto' on the first two) must keep nDCG@10, recall@100 and recall@1000 within 0.002 of the numbers in chloedb/tests/fixtures/relevance_gate.json . A change that moves them fails until the pull request that makes it commits the new numbers, which the test writes ( CHLOEDB_RELEVANCE_GATE=update ); the failure gives a paired randomization p-value over the queries. The analyzers of other languages are held the same way on MIRACL's Yoruba and Swahili passages ( simple and standard , nDCG@10 and recall@100 on the dev queries, relevance_gate_miracl.json ). Settings are tuned on the dev or train splits ( chloedb-bm25-eval --split dev ), and the hybrid study picks its fusion weight on half of the queries and reports the other half."],
["design.html#structured-fields","ChloeDB: design","Structured fields","Columns. Integers and floats in 128-document blocks, frame-of-reference bit-packed, with min/max zone maps per block. Keywords are dictionary encoded with bit-packed codes. A column answers \"value of document d \" in O(1) and scans at memory bandwidth. Keyword postings. Every keyword value also gets a docID list, so equality and IN become set operations. The encoding is Pyramid (plain sorted arrays below 64 documents), which has the fastest unions and count-only intersections for facet counts. Per-candidate probes use the code column and a bitset of accepted codes (4–5 ns), which beats probing the lists (5–33 ns). Range postings. Numeric fields get an equi-depth histogram of 64 buckets whose buckets are docID lists. A range is the union of the buckets it covers, plus the boundary buckets filtered through the column. The histogram doubles as the selectivity estimator. Doc store. Rows in LZ4-compressed 16 KiB blocks with a block index (zstd and 32 KiB blocks for a table with compression = 'zstd' ); it serves projections and is the source for merges. Substring index. A stored TEXT or KEYWORD field with substring = true gets an FM-index of its values in each segment: the Burrows-Wheeler transform of their bytes, cut into parts of at most 64 MiB, each in a Huffman-shaped 4-ary wavelet tree, with a sample of the suffix array that gives the row of a match. LIKE and ILIKE with any pattern are answered from it ( substrings ); the bytes are indexed as they are, so nothing about case or Unicode is frozen in the file. On logs it is about as large as the text it indexes (0.94 to 1.02 of its bytes), and a flush or a merge of the table takes 1.3 to 3.6 times as long ( results , on a 4-core cloud VM, Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz). The format is in chloedb/notes/substring.md . Generated columns. A TEXT field may be generated from other stored text fields ( body = title + text in the evaluation table), indexed but not stored, and recomputed by merges. Any other indexed TEXT or SPARSE field must be stored, since a merge rebuilds its index from the stored values: a schema refuses one that is not, and a merge of a database created before that check fails rather than empty the index. Loading files. chloedb::ingest reads rows from JSON Lines, JSON and CSV or TSV files against the schema, and ingest::import bulk-loads them into an empty table in one commit (a bad row writes nothing) or upserts them in batches; chloedb-server import , the server's POST /v1/import and Python's import_file use it ( loading rows ). Schema::from_json reads a schema in a short form, types by name and only the options that differ from the defaults ( schemas ). Embedded vector fields. A VECTOR field may be declared as a model's embedding of other fields ( \"embed\": {\"from\": [\"title\"], \"model\": \"potion-retrieval-32M\"} ). The schema records it; given the model, writes that leave the vector out get it computed and rewrites of a source recompute it (a SQL write computes them before it takes the writer lock, so other writes do not wait for the model), while merges keep the stored vectors ( embedded vector fields )."],
["design.html#vectors","ChloeDB: design","Vectors","Forward vectors. Every vector field stores float32 vectors for exact re-ranking and 8-bit scalar-quantised codes (SQ8, per-dimension min/max) for scanning: 4× less I/O, and with the query quantised too, integer dot products at about 14 ns per 1,024-dimension vector with NEON usdot on the M2 (AVX-512 VNNI on x86-64). A field can keep 1-bit codes instead ( \"codes\": \"binary\" ), an eighth of the memory, with 8 times as many candidates re-ranked, or RaBitQ codes of 1, 2 or 4 bits ( \"rabitq1\" , \"rabitq2\" , \"rabitq4\" ), whose per-row error bounds let the re-ranking skip candidates that cannot make the top k ( results ). Flat. An exact scan of the codes plus float32 re-ranking: the right answer for small segments and very selective filters. IVF. k-means centroids; each cluster's members are stored contiguously as a docID list followed by their codes. Probing a cluster is one sequential read, and a filter is applied by intersecting the cluster's list with the filter's, the same operation as a text conjunction. HNSW. A navigable small-world graph with fixed-degree adjacency lists in the segment. Fastest in memory; random I/O on disk."],
["design.html#graph-edges","ChloeDB: design","Graph edges","An EDGES field holds a row's outgoing edges as targets' primary keys, optionally with a label and a weight. Per segment, ChloeDB stores the out-edges as a CSR over local docIDs and a reverse index grouped by target key; a hop resolves keys against the snapshot's primary-key indexes. Because edges point at keys and not at storage positions, they survive updates, deletes and merges untouched. The Graphs page covers the layout, the traversals and their cost."],
["design.html#planning-and-execution","ChloeDB: design","Planning and execution","The binder turns WHERE into a predicate tree over text leaves ( MATCH ), structured leaves (comparisons, IN , ranges) and graph leaves ( REACHABLE , PATH ), and turns ORDER BY into a scorer: BM25, vector, fusion, PageRank, a column, or none. Statistics. Exact list lengths (document frequencies, keyword list sizes), histogram bucket counts, per-segment document counts and ANN parameters. Conjunctions assume independence; disjunctions use inclusion–exclusion. Filters: materialise or probe. A predicate is either materialised (a docID set from postings) or probed (a per-candidate check against the forward column). Materialising costs work proportional to the lists; probing costs work proportional to the candidates. The planner materialises when the candidate stream is long relative to the lists and probes otherwise. Range filters also choose between a zone-map column scan and histogram bucket postings. Ranked retrieval. ORDER BY strategies none or a column iterate the filter; top-k heap or column sort bm25 MaxScore or Block-Max WAND over postings, with the filter as a skip constraint similarity pre-filtered exact scan, IVF with in-filter intersection, HNSW with the filter inside, or post-filtering with oversampling (too few matches widen an HNSW walk from where it stopped) fusion top- k ′ from each input, union, exact re-scoring of every input on the union; or an exact scan"],
["design.html#filtered-vector-search","ChloeDB: design","Filtered vector search","For a filter of estimated selectivity s over n rows and a top- k query, the strategies cost, in distance computations or their equivalent: $$ C_{\\text{pre}} \\approx s\\,n, \\qquad C_{\\text{IVF}} \\propto \\frac{\\mathrm{nprobe}}{\\sqrt{s}}\\ \\text{clusters}, \\qquad C_{\\text{HNSW}} \\propto \\frac{1}{s}\\ \\text{times the unfiltered visits}, \\qquad C_{\\text{post}} \\approx \\frac{k}{s}\\ \\text{candidates} $$ What matters to the index strategies is the selectivity near the query , which differs from the global one whenever the filter correlates with the query's topic. When the global estimate picks an index strategy, the planner checks it with a 32-result unfiltered search: the smoothed fraction of those results that pass the filter replaces s for in-index search and bounds it from above for post-filtering. If that sample finds at most one match, a second, 160-neighbour sample follows. The results show what each refinement bought."],
["design.html#graphs-facets-and-segments","ChloeDB: design","Graphs, facets and segments","Graphs run before planning, across segments, because a traversal does not respect segment boundaries. Seeds are resolved (a seed subquery runs as its own query on the same snapshot), the traversal runs, and its result becomes per segment a materialised document set with exact cardinality, plus hop distances and PageRank scores. The planner then treats REACHABLE like any other filter, only with an exact estimate: a vector search over a reachable set of a few hundred rows becomes a pre-filtered exact scan. Facets. GROUP BY a keyword either reads the dictionary codes of the result's documents (small results) or intersects the result with each value's Pyramid list, counting only (large results, few values). Segments. Each segment runs the same physical plan with its own statistics, and per-segment top-k lists are merged. The plan the planner chose is visible: the chloedb shell's --plan flag prints the executor's plan lines for each statement."],
["design.html#where-it-fits","ChloeDB: design","Where it fits","ChloeDB is built for embedded retrieval: local search over documents with metadata and embeddings, such as retrieval for a model on a laptop, a desktop app's search box or an edge device, with SQL and one file to back up (with a checkpoint , which copies it consistently while it is in use) or ship. It suits read-mostly corpora with steady writes: commits are visible at once, and batching amortises their sync, but merges rebuild every index of the segments they combine, and HNSW builds dominate load time (IVF builds 2.1–2.5× faster for write-heavy vector workloads). It is not meant for relational workloads with joins, high write concurrency, or corpora larger than one machine. Current limits. A database holds several tables, which SQL creates, drops, queries and writes by name ( CREATE TABLE , DROP TABLE , INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE through Db::sql , see the query language ), and a query may read several: joins of tables with each other or with themselves, subqueries and WITH work across tables, and graph walks across tables are the next step. The Rust transaction API ( Database::begin , WriteTxn::insert , set , add , delete , WriteTxn::table , commit ) groups several writes, across tables too; add increments a numeric field from the value the transaction sees, its own writes included. One process writes a database and others may read it at the same time ( readers in other processes ); a second writer fails with Busy . A query searches a snapshot's segments on ExecOptions::threads threads (one by default): the calling thread and threads borrowed from a pool the process shares, as many as the cores. chloedb-server serves a database, or a set of shards, over the network, and the Python package runs one in-process or talks to a server."],
["design.html#code-layout","ChloeDB: design","Code layout","path what chloedb/src/storage/ file format, meta slots and shadow-paging commits, WAL, memtable, snapshots, primary keys, merges, vacuum chloedb/src/segment/ immutable segments of named, aligned sections, read zero-copy through mmap chloedb/src/text/ analyser, Loom and BP128 postings, positions, BM25 with MaxScore and Block-Max WAND chloedb/src/forward/ columns with zone maps, keyword dictionaries and Pyramid lists, range histograms, facets, doc store chloedb/src/vector/ float32 vectors with SQ8, binary or RaBitQ codes, flat, IVF and HNSW, filtered search chloedb/src/substring/ the substring index: an FM-index of a field's values per segment, and LIKE and ILIKE from it chloedb/src/graph/ EDGES fields: out-edge CSR and reverse index per segment; BFS, shortest paths, personalized PageRank chloedb/src/query/ SQL binder, plan IR and EXPLAIN , logical plan, cost-based planner, executor, fusion, graph predicates and scores chloedb/src/baselines/ SQLite + FTS5 and Tantivy baselines (feature baselines ) chloedb/src/bin/ chloedb shell, chloedb-eval , component benchmarks, chloedb-baselines , chloedb-graph-bench The full design is in chloedb/DESIGN.md , and the long-form report, with every chart and table, is here ."],
["rag.html","Retrieval for RAG","","A model answers questions about your documents well when it is handed the right passages, with their sources. This recipe splits documents into passages, stores them in ChloeDB with a vector each, and retrieves for a question by words and meaning at once, at most two passages from any one document, so that the model sees several sources. Then it builds the prompt and, if you have an Anthropic API key, asks Claude. You need Python with the chloedb package and chloedb-server on your PATH ( quickstart )."],
["rag.html#the-documents","Retrieval for RAG","The documents","Any directory of text or Markdown files will do. Here are three short ones:"],
["rag.html#split-and-store","Retrieval for RAG","Split and store","A passage is a section of a file: the text under one heading, cut further if it is long. Each passage keeps its file and heading, so an answer can cite them. text is generated from the heading and the body, so that BM25 reads all of a passage's words as one field (it is indexed, not stored), and the emb field is computed from the same two by the embedding model when a row is written: upsert replaces a passage with the same key, so running it again after editing a file updates its passages. (A section that disappears from a file stays until you delete it: db.delete(keys) , or DELETE FROM passages WHERE doc = 'returns.md' before writing the file's passages again.)"],
["rag.html#retrieve","Retrieval for RAG","Retrieve","One query does the retrieval. rrf fuses a BM25 ranking over the text with a vector ranking, which finds passages that use the question's words and passages that mean the same thing in others; MAX 2 PER doc keeps at most two passages of a file: The question says parcel and broken , which no passage does: the vector side finds the damaged-items passage by meaning, and BM25 still ranks exact terms such as an order number or a product name first when a question has them. BM25 ranks text , one field with all of a passage's words, rather than each field on its own. rrf gives every ranking it fuses the same weight, so rrf(bm25(heading, :q), bm25(body, :q), similarity(emb, :v)) counts the words twice against the meaning once, and a heading of two words as much as the body. On BEIR's SciFact with potion-retrieval-32M's vectors, nDCG@10 was 0.7006 for rrf(bm25(body, :q), similarity(emb, :v)) over one field of title and text, against 0.6513 for rrf(bm25(text, :q), bm25(title, :q), similarity(emb, :v)) over the two fields (measured on 17 November 2026 on a shared 4-core cloud container). Give a table one generated field of the text you search ( \"from\": [...] ), and keep the separate fields for showing and filtering."],
["rag.html#ask","Retrieval for RAG","Ask","The passages go into the prompt with their sources, and the model is told to answer from them and cite them. Without ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set, the script prints the prompt it would send:"],
["rag.html#make-it-better","Retrieval for RAG","Make it better","Rank by more than text. Multiply in what you know about a passage: ORDER BY rrf(...) * (1 + 0.2 * is_current) DESC , or decay old ones by date with decay . Filter before ranking. A WHERE clause (a product line, a language, what a user may see) is planned with the search, not applied after it: WHERE audience IN ('public', :team) . Rerank the top. A cross-encoder reads the question and each of the top passages together and scores them more precisely, in the same query: cross-encoders . The released builds run the hosted ones ( cohere/rerank-v3.5 , voyage/rerank-2.5 or jina/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual , with the provider's key), not the local ones (below). Serve it. The same query runs on chloedb-server over HTTP ( chloedb.connect(url) has the same query ), so several workers can retrieve from one index, and on a cluster when the documents outgrow a machine."],
["rag.html#another-embedding-model","Retrieval for RAG","Another embedding model","The released builds, pip install chloedb , npm install chloedb , the prebuilt chloedb-server (from PyPI, the install script or the image), run three kinds of embedding model: the static models this page uses, potion-retrieval-32M and potion-base-8M (and their -int8 copies), which chloedb-server fetch-model downloads; hosted models, which ChloeDB calls with your key, such as openai/text-embedding-3-small or voyage/voyage-3.5 , and models served by Ollama on your machine, such as ollama/bge-m3 , with no key ( hosted models ); vectors you compute yourself. They do not run BERT models such as bge-small-en-v1.5 and snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v1.5 , ColBERT models, or local cross-encoders such as ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 : those need a build with the bert feature (or onnx for the int8 cross-encoders), which only a build from source has ( cargo build --release --features bert in chloedb-server , maturin develop --release --features bert in chloedb-py ). On a released build, chloedb-server fetch-model bge-small-en-v1.5 downloads the model and then stops with an error that it is a BERT model, which needs a build with the bert feature. The numbers the server reference gives for bge-small were measured with such a build. To use one of those models, or any other, compute the vectors where it runs and store them: a vector field without embed , with the model's dimensions, takes the vectors each row brings, and similarity(emb, :v) takes the question's vector as a list. Embed the question with the same model: The block is not run with this page, as it needs a library besides chloedb . A row written without a vector has none: a vector ranking leaves it out, and rrf finds it by its words alone."],
["agent-memory.html","Memory for an agent","","An agent that works over many sessions needs to remember: what was said, what was decided, and facts that change. This recipe keeps an agent's memory in one ChloeDB file: every turn as a row, facts under keys that new values replace, and a recall query that finds what matters to a new message by words and meaning, favours what is recent, and spreads its picks across sessions. If the agent is Claude Code, you do not need to build this: ChloeCtx is this idea built out for it, with hooks that record every session and a daemon that decides when to bring history back. Install ChloeCtx as a Claude Code plugin, or from a checkout, and every new session has it. Use this recipe for an agent of your own. You need Python with the chloedb package and chloedb-server on your PATH ( quickstart ), and the embedding model the tables below name. Fetch it once (about 130 MB, into ~/.local/share/chloedb/models , or $CHLOEDB_MODELS ; a machine that already has it, from the RAG recipe say, checks it and downloads nothing):"],
["agent-memory.html#the-store","Memory for an agent","The store","Two tables in one file. turns holds what was said, keyed by session and position; facts holds what the agent knows now, one row per key: recall ranks turns by a fusion of BM25 and vector similarity, multiplies it by decay , which halves a turn's weight for every 30 days of age (2,592,000 seconds), and keeps at most two turns of a session with MAX 2 PER session , so one long session does not crowd out the others. Facts need no history: upsert replaces a key's row, so a fact is always its latest value."],
["agent-memory.html#remember-then-recall","Memory for an agent","Remember, then recall","Two past sessions, a few weeks apart, and a fact that changed between them: A new message arrives. Before answering, the agent recalls the turns and facts that bear on it and puts them in front of the model: The printer's paper width comes back though the new message never says 58 mm , and the deploy target is its current value, not the one from September. What goes into the model's context is a few lines, not two sessions of transcript."],
["agent-memory.html#make-it-better","Memory for an agent","Make it better","Leave out what the model can still see. Give each turn the conversation it came from and filter it out of recall while that conversation is open: WHERE session != :current . Forget on a schedule. OPTIONS(retain = '400 days') on a table, or an expire_by column per row, drops old turns in the background ( tables ). Let the model search too. Give the agent a tool that runs a read-only SELECT over its memory; ChloeCtx's memory_sql does exactly that. Distil. After each session, ask a small model for the decisions and rules it contained and learn them as facts; ChloeCtx's distiller does this with claude -p . Share it. On chloedb-server the same class works through chloedb.connect(url) , so several agents read and write one memory."],
["crawl-to-search.html","Crawl a site into search","","chloedb-crawl reads a site, finds the fields its pages carry (JSON-LD, meta tags, spec tables, the site's own template), makes a table from them and fills it. This recipe crawls a small shop, served on your machine so that it runs anywhere, and searches the table it made with SQL. You need chloedb-server and chloedb-crawl on your PATH . since 0.3.2 From release 0.3.2, the installer puts both there prebuilt, with nothing to compile: Release 0.3.0 and earlier have no prebuilt crawler. With those, or to build from source, use a checkout of the repository (Rust and a C compiler, several minutes): (cd chloedb-server && cargo build --release) && (cd chloedb-crawl && cargo build --release) , then add both target/release directories to PATH ."],
["crawl-to-search.html#a-site-to-crawl","Crawl a site into search","A site to crawl","A shop with a home page that links to six products. Each product page has its fields in JSON-LD, as most shops do. This script writes the site's pages into shop/ : Serve it on port 8099:"],
["crawl-to-search.html#look-before-you-crawl","Crawl a site into search","Look before you crawl","preview reads a few pages and prints, as JSON, the table it would make and why, writing nothing. Its sql is the table. The crawler fetches the public internet only, so a site on your own machine or network has to be named with --allow-host ( reach ):"],
["crawl-to-search.html#crawl","Crawl a site into search","Crawl","crawl --local DIR starts a cluster of its own in DIR (a coordinator on 127.0.0.1:7700 and a shard), crawls into it, prints its progress and then the table it made, and stops the cluster. --table names the table, which is otherwise named after the site's host. On a site you don't run, keep the defaults, which space the requests out and obey robots.txt; this one is ours, so it can go faster:"],
["crawl-to-search.html#search-it","Crawl a site into search","Search it","The same directory serves the same cluster again. serve starts it with the crawler's API beside it, which the command center at http://127.0.0.1:7700 shows under Apps → Crawler : The table has a column for each field most pages had: the page's own ( url , title , body , ...), those of the JSON-LD, such as offers_price , and price , which it learned from the site's own template (the <p class=\"price\"> every product page has). Ask the server which: Then search it like any table: full text over the pages' text, filtered and ranked by the fields the crawler found."],
["crawl-to-search.html#next","Crawl a site into search","Next","The crawler : every option, the fields it finds, how it grows a cluster, and its API. The page for site owners : what the crawler sends and how to keep it off a site. --embed potion-base-8M embeds each page's title and body into a vector field, emb , for the hybrid search the search guide shows."],
["logs.html","Search your logs","","ChloeDB keeps log lines in an ordinary table and finds them the way an on-call engineer looks at 3 a.m.: any part of a line (an interface name, an IP, an error code) from a substring index, the last 15 minutes with now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' , counts per host and minute with time_bucket . This page starts a server, makes a table for syslog, a key that may only write and read those tables, sends lines to it, and runs the first five queries of an incident. The command center's Log explorer does the same with a search box. You need chloedb-server on your PATH ( install ), and curl and python3 ."],
["logs.html#a-server","Search your logs","A server","A server with no token and no key is open to anything on this machine. Its first key, an admin key, closes it; keep that one for yourself:"],
["logs.html#a-table-for-log-lines","Search your logs","A table for log lines","A log table is any table with a TIMESTAMP and a text column. This one names its columns as OpenTelemetry does ( ts , severity , host , service , body , attributes ), so that the OpenTelemetry Collector and a JSON lines import both fill it. substring = true gives host and body a substring index , which answers LIKE and ILIKE with % anywhere ( '%GigabitEthernet0/1%' , '%10.0.12.2%' ) from the index rather than by reading every line. retain drops lines older than 30 days. A log line has no key, so the table has none. Making a table takes an admin key:"],
["logs.html#a-key-for-the-on-call-engineer-and-the-log-shipper","Search your logs","A key for the on-call engineer and the log shipper","The oncall preset makes a key limited to tables : it writes and reads the tables its prefix names (rows, imports and OpenTelemetry logs alike), writes notes into its ChloeCtx spaces ctx_logs_* , and reads the fleet's health, events, alerts and metrics. It cannot read other tables or change the server."],
["logs.html#send-the-lines","Search your logs","Send the lines",""],
["logs.html#from-an-opentelemetry-collector","Search your logs","From an OpenTelemetry Collector","POST /v1/logs takes OTLP over HTTP (protobuf or JSON, gzip or not), the protocol every OpenTelemetry SDK and the Collector export; ?table= names the table, and a table that does not exist is made with OTLP's columns . This Collector listens for syslog on UDP port 5514 and for OTLP from your applications, and sends both to logs_syslog with the key above in its environment ( otelcol-contrib --config collector.yaml ): A Collector retries an export that failed, and a row has no key to tell a retry from a new line, so a retried export can leave a line twice. What the Collector sends is OTLP JSON, which curl can send too: An empty answer is OTLP's success: no record was refused (one refused is counted in partialSuccess )."],
["logs.html#from-a-file-json-lines","Search your logs","From a file: JSON lines","POST /v1/import?format=jsonl takes one JSON object a line, with the table's columns. This script writes an hour of lines from five routers and switches, with an incident in its last ten minutes: GigabitEthernet0/1 on core-rtr-02 going down and up, its BGP session to 10.0.12.2 dropping each time, and OSPF on core-rtr-03 losing the same neighbour. The same call imports lines from a script or a cron job: printf '%s\\n' '{\"ts\": \"...\", \"host\": \"...\", \"body\": \"...\"}' | curl ... --data-binary @- ."],
["logs.html#the-first-five-queries-of-an-incident","Search your logs","The first five queries of an incident","Every query here runs with the on-call key, through POST /v1/sql . This shell function sends one and prints its rows: 1. What failed in the last 15 minutes, and where. now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' is one time for the whole statement, so the index on ts answers the range: 2. Per host per minute. time_bucket('1 minute', ts) is the start of each line's minute ( '5 minutes' , '1 hour' for wider buckets), grouped by the executor from the ts column: 3. An interface flapping. LIKE with % on both sides is answered from the substring index of body , whatever the pattern. A link that changed state more than 3 times in 5 minutes is flapping: 4. Everything about one IP , on every host. An IP is not a word the text analyzer keeps whole, so it is found as a substring: 5. The top kinds of line. Syslog names the kind of each line ( %LINK-3-UPDOWN ); kept as a path of the JSON column, it is a group like a column: A line without such a name is grouped by its text with the numbers, addresses, ids and times taken out, which the Log explorer's Patterns does in the page (SQL has no regular expressions to do it). The key may not read a table outside logs_* :"],
["logs.html#the-log-explorer","Search your logs","The Log explorer","The command center (the server's address in a browser, here http://127.0.0.1:7700/#/explorer) has a Log explorer under Operate. Sign in with the on-call key and it lists the tables the key reads that hold log lines (any table with a TIMESTAMP ), and shows one of them: a search box : words and \"a phrase\" find the line's text ( ILIKE '%...%' on a column with a substring index, MATCH on one without, or pick Substring or Words), host:core-rtr-02 a column's value ( * for any text), attributes.iface:Gi0/1 a JSON path, code>=4 a comparison, level>=warn the levels at or above warn as the table spells them, -debug leaves lines out, and WHERE ... takes a SQL condition as written; what a line is , under the search box: its text (a log's message or body; in another table, its title), its time, and the row's other fields, each changeable. The other fields show beside the text when the table is not a log (it has no level or host column), on a line whose text is empty, or when other fields is on; when most of the newest lines leave the text empty and another column is full, the lines show that one and say so. A table with no text column (BGP updates, events) shows each row's fields, and a word in the search box finds a field's exact value; the time range : the last 15 minutes, hour, day or week ( ts > now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' ), or a window from and to; lines over time : a count per minute (per 5 or 15 minutes, or 2 hours, over longer ranges) with errors and warnings apart, time_bucket grouped by the server; a click on a bar zooms into it; the lines , newest first, followed live every 3 seconds (Pause stops it), and for a line clicked, the lines before and after it on the same host; patterns : the newest 2,000 matching lines grouped by their text with numbers, addresses, ids and times as placeholders, most frequent first; a click shows a pattern's lines; the SQL it ran , each statement to copy or open in the SQL workbench. Without a log table, it shows how to send logs here: the table above, the Collector's settings with this server's address, the key, and the JSON lines import, with buttons that make the table and the key for an admin."],
["python.html","ChloeDB from Python","","The chloedb package runs a ChloeDB database inside a Python process, the way sqlite3 runs SQLite, or talks to a running chloedb-server . Both have the same methods, so a script written against a local file works against a server. LangChain, LangGraph and LlamaIndex take ChloeDB as a vector store, a checkpointer and a memory store, and chloedb.dbapi is a DB-API 2.0 driver: see frameworks . For dataframe work, db[\"table\"] is a lazy frame written as Polars writes one, run where the data is, with the answer as Arrow: see DataFrames ."],
["python.html#a-first-search","ChloeDB from Python","A first search","chloedb.open(path, schema) creates the database the first time and opens it every time after, so the script runs again as it is. upsert replaces the rows whose keys it is given, so nothing is written twice; it replaces whole rows, and a field the new row leaves out is then empty. To change one field, use UPDATE : db.sql(\"UPDATE books SET year = 2000 WHERE id = 'b1'\") . Values go in as parameters ( :q , :y ), never into the SQL text. The same methods work against a server: chloedb.serve() starts a chloedb-server of its own and returns a client of it (with pip install \"chloedb[server]\" ), and chloedb.connect(\"http://127.0.0.1:7700\") is a client of one already running. A server's tables are made with SQL: Rows like a given row (\"more like this\") rank by similarity to its stored vector, {\"vector_of\": key} : similar rows . When something fails, common errors has the messages people meet first and what each means. The search guide goes through every kind of search on a small catalogue, and Build a search page puts them in a Python web page."],
["python.html#install","ChloeDB from Python","Install","pip picks the wheel for your machine, so nothing is compiled. since 0.3.7 The server extra also installs chloedb-server , the prebuilt server of the same release, which chloedb.serve() starts. If import chloedb raises an ImportError that calls it a placeholder, pip installed chloedb 0.0.x, which only held the name before 0.3.0 was published: pip install --upgrade chloedb replaces it. pip also falls back to it when no release has a wheel for your machine (an older glibc, say): chloedb-server doctor says which you have. The old name, import strata , still works for a release, with a DeprecationWarning ; release 0.1.0 was published as strata-db and imports as strata only. Each platform's wheel, the client alone with no build, and a build from source (which the BERT embedding models need) are at the end of this page ."],
["python.html#a-database-in-your-process","ChloeDB from Python","A database in your process","chloedb.open(path, schema) opens the database at path , or creates it with schema if it does not exist. The schema is a dict, JSON text or the path of a JSON file, in the form the server takes . Database.create(path, schema) and Database.open(path) do one or the other. models= says where embedding and ranking models, and the captioners of text made from media , are (default $CHLOEDB_MODELS , else ~/.local/share/chloedb/models (or ~/.local/share/strata/models , on a machine that has that folder from before the rename; before 0.3.2, always); chloedb-server fetch-model NAME downloads one there), and directory=True creates the database as a directory of segment files. A database written by a version of ChloeDB from before field ids does not open: chloedb.upgrade(path) copies it into this version's format in its place, keeps the old one as <path>.before-field-ids , and returns (old_path, tables, rows) , or None when the database needs no upgrade. Nothing may have it open meanwhile. One process at a time can have a database open to write it. Others can open it with chloedb.open(path, read_only=True) (or Database.open(path, read_only=True) ) to read it while it is written, the other workers of a web server under gunicorn or uwsgi, say, or the second process of Flask's debug reloader: each query sees the writer's commits so far, every write raises ChloeDBError , and refresh() looks for new commits at once ( how readers follow the writer ). Within a process, threads can query at once: every call releases the GIL while it runs. Threads can write at once too: a write waits while another holds the writer, and the writes that wait share the next commit (one disk flush for all of them), so none fails as busy. For writes from several processes, serve the database with chloedb-server and use the client below. A second open to write a file that is open to write already, in another process or in this one, raises BusyError ( common errors ). Segments are merged in the background as writes add them, one merge at a time, as a server merges them, so a table written to for weeks stays in a few segments without the rows its updates replaced. A with block, or close() , writes the rows still in memory into the file (so that the file alone holds the database, backups ), merges the small segments that writes left, gives the file system back the space merges freed when it is an eighth of the file or more, and closes the file. optimize() goes further, for a table that is done changing: one segment, up to 2,000,000 rows. A commit waits for the disk ( fsync ), so a write that returned is still there after a power failure. On a disk that is slow to sync, that caps one-row commits from one thread at a few hundred to a few thousand a second (writes from several threads share a sync). sync=False (to chloedb.open , Database.create , Database.open and connect ) commits without waiting for the disk: a commit still reaches the operating system before it returns, so a crash of the program loses nothing, but a power failure or a crash of the machine may lose the transactions committed since the last flush, newest first, and the database stays consistent, as SQLite's synchronous=NORMAL in WAL mode does. Bulk loads, flush() , close() and merges sync either way. The file does not keep the choice: each open makes it."],
["python.html#backing-up-and-damaged-files","ChloeDB from Python","Backing up, and damaged files","A database is its file plus <path>-wal , the write-ahead log, which holds the rows written since the last flush; copying the file alone while the database is open loses them. To back up a database: while it is open and in use: db.checkpoint(\"backup.chloedb\") writes a consistent copy (the destination must not exist), checks every segment against its checksum first, so that a damaged database fails the checkpoint instead of being copied, checks the copy, and only then gives it its name. Restore by opening the copy, or renaming it into place while nothing has the database open; after close() (or a with block): close() writes the rows still in memory into the file and leaves the log empty, so the file alone is then a complete copy; db.flush() does the same at any moment, until the next write. Copying a database's files while it is written is not a backup, even with its -wal . When the newest version of a database or its log is damaged so that opening it would lose acknowledged writes (bit rot, a copy cut short, a log deleted while it held transactions), chloedb.open raises ChloeDBError naming the file, what is damaged and how to recover, and changes nothing: restore a backup, or open with recover=True to accept the loss. That keeps copies of the damaged files first ( <path>.damaged-<time> , <path>-wal.damaged ), opens what is left, issues a RuntimeWarning , and db.damaged says what was lost (it is None for a database that opened whole)."],
["python.html#writing","ChloeDB from Python","Writing","Rows are dicts of field name to value: str , int , float , None , bool for a bool field, a datetime or date (or ISO 8601 text) for a timestamp field, a list for a multi field, a list of floats or a numpy array for a vector, a dict of term weights for a sparse field, a URI or a dict for a media field (in-process, the whole value or {\"uri\": …, \"data\": <base64>} : nothing is fetched, see media fields ). A naive datetime is taken as UTC. A field the schema does not have is an error. Results give a bool field's values as bool and a timestamp field's as ISO 8601 text in UTC ( \"2026-10-01T12:30:00Z\" ), in-process and from the client alike; datetime.fromisoformat reads them. method what it does insert(rows) adds a row (a dict) or rows (any iterable of dicts) whose keys must be new; all or none are written. Fewer than 10,000 rows go through the write-ahead log into memory, which gathers the rows of many inserts into one segment once it holds 10,000; from 10,000 rows on, they are bulk-loaded straight into segments. A vector may be a numpy array of float32 or float64, which is read from its bytes rather than a Python float at a time. upsert(rows) adds rows, replacing the rows with their keys, in transactions of 10,000 delete(keys) deletes the rows with a key or keys; returns how many there were get(key) , get_many(keys) the row with a primary key (a dict of the columns SELECT * gives), or None , read without SQL ( rows by key ); get_many answers a list in the keys' order. Each takes table= expire() deletes the rows whose expire_by time has passed, which reads leave out already; returns how many sql(\"UPDATE ...\") INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE statements; returns the rows written. It also runs CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE and DROP TABLE ( tables ) and CREATE and DROP RANK PROFILE ( rank profiles ), which return 0, and writes any table add_column(name, type, **options) , drop_column(name) , rename_column(old, new) change a table's columns while it holds rows, as ALTER TABLE does: options are the field's, in the short form of a schema ( db.add_column(\"price\", \"float\", default=0.0) , db.add_column(\"emb\", \"vector\", embed=[\"title\"], model=\"potion-retrieval-32M\") ); rows written before a column was added read its default, or None . Each takes table= . In-process only: through the client, send ALTER TABLE with sql import_file(path, format=None) loads a JSON Lines, JSON, CSV or TSV file ( how files are read ); returns {rows, bulk, skipped_fields} write(ops) applies write operations in one transaction, as the server's /v1/write takes them: insert , upsert and delete , set of a field ( {\"op\": \"set\", \"key\": k, \"field\": \"status\", \"value\": \"SOLD\"} : a number in place, another by writing the row again with the value), and add to a number in place ( {\"op\": \"add\", \"key\": k, \"field\": \"n\", \"value\": 1} ; with a doc instead of a key , an add inserts the row when no row has its key); each names its table or writes the first; returns the rows written import_sqlite(path, tables=None, schemas=None, append=False) imports a SQLite database's tables, each into the table of its name, created when missing ( SQLite in and out ); a table without a key that has rows takes more only with append=True ; returns what it did and what did not carry over export_sqlite(path, tables=None, fts=True, indexes=True) writes the tables to a new SQLite file, with indexes, an FTS5 index over the text and the ChloeDB schemas import_parquet(path, table=None, schema=None, key=None) imports a Parquet file or a directory of them (Spark's part files, Hive partitions) into one table, created from the columns when missing ( Parquet and S3 ); returns what it did export_parquet(path, tables=None) writes the tables to Parquet: a .parquet file for one table, else a directory of them, each keeping its ChloeDB schema import_arrow(data, table=None) writes the rows of a pyarrow Table or RecordBatchReader, a polars DataFrame, a DuckDB relation or anything with __arrow_c_stream__ , columns into the fields of their names; bulk-loaded into an empty table, else upserted chloedb.from_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\", \"shop.chloedb\") creates a database from a SQLite file's tables, and chloedb.plan_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\") says what it would make of them without writing; edit the plan and pass it to either as schemas . chloedb.from_parquet(\"events.parquet\", \"lake.chloedb\") and chloedb.plan_parquet(...) do the same for Parquet. Any of these paths, and import_file 's, may be an s3:// URL, read with the AWS_* variables ( S3 ). insert , upsert , delete and import_file write the database's first table, the one schema describes, or the one table= names ( db.insert(rows, table=\"events\") ); sql names the table it writes, and tables() lists every table's schema. Through a coordinator, delete on a table placed by a column takes that column's value as routing= ( tables on shards ). A key a row leaves out gets its field's default , and a field with not_null refuses a row without a value ( schema options ); a timestamp defaulting to \"now\" takes one time for all the rows of a call. Vectors of embedded fields that a row leaves out are computed by the field's model when it is open, here or behind an API: a field naming openai/text-embedding-3-small , cohere/embed-v4.0 , voyage/voyage-3.5 , gemini/gemini-embedding-001 , ollama/nomic-embed-text or another hosted model gets its vectors from the provider, with the key in the process's environment ( OPENAI_API_KEY , ...), so inserting plain text is enough. A write whose vectors the provider will not give (after its retries) raises ChloeDBError and writes nothing. db.embed_errors lists the embedded fields whose model did not open (a hosted model without its key says which variable to set), whose vectors rows must then bring: a write that leaves one to a model ChloeDB could run but has not open fails, and says so, unless db.set_embed_later() lets it store the row without its vector, to be filled in once the model opens (a server does it itself; from Rust, Db::embed_backfill ) (before, such a row was stored without a vector, and no error said so; a server still does that, and fills them in itself). A table records the fingerprint of its model's weights (the SHA-256 of its weights file) when it is created with the model in its models directory, and a model of the same name with other weights does not open for it: it is listed in db.embed_errors , with what to do. It also lists the semantic-ID fields whose codebook did not open."],
["python.html#querying","ChloeDB from Python","Querying","query(sql, params) runs a SELECT in ChloeDB's SQL . Parameters bind :name placeholders, from a dict, keyword arguments, or both: A parameter is a number, a string, None , a list of numbers or a numpy array (a vector), a list, a dict of term weights, {\"embed\": text} (the text's vector by an embedded field's model; add \"field\" to pick one) or {\"vector_of\": key} (a stored row's vector, for more like this ). Either reads the table the statement's FROM names since 0.3.2 , unless it names another with \"table\" (before 0.3.2, the first table). The answer is a Result : for row in result , result[i] rows as dicts of column to value result.rows , result.columns rows as lists, in column order result.keys , result.scores each row's primary key and the score it was ranked by result.column(\"title\") , result.scalar() one column; the first value (a count, a sum) result.stats what the engine did result.to_pandas() a DataFrame (with pandas installed) query_arrow(sql, params) runs a SELECT as query does and answers with its columns as Arrow arrays, an ArrowResult , without making a Python object of each value; a query of one table that neither groups nor counts reads its columns from the database's segments straight into the arrays. pyarrow, polars, pandas and DuckDB read the arrays where the database wrote them, without copying them, through the Arrow PyCapsule interface : ChloeDB Arrow int int64 float float64 keyword , text , json string (a JSON value as its text) bool bool timestamp timestamp[us, tz=UTC] vector list<float32> a multi field list<int64> or list<string> A computed column has the type of its values: a column of only NULLs (or of no rows) is null , and one of integers and floats float64 . Values of other types mixed in one column (a CASE that answers a number in some rows and text in others) are not one Arrow array: query_arrow raises ChloeDBError naming the column, and query answers it. r.columns , len(r) and r.stats are as a Result 's. query_arrow comes with the package's default feature datafusion . A Client 's query_arrow asks the server for an Arrow answer ( \"format\": \"arrow\" ) and returns a pyarrow Table with the same column types; it needs pyarrow, and a server too old for Arrow answers JSON, which it types from the values. search(text, table=None, limit=10, where=None, columns=\"*\", mode=\"auto\") writes the query for the common case, on the first table or on table since 0.3.2 (which reads that table alone: a client with a key limited to other tables than the first can search its own). It ranks by BM25 over the text fields (the generated ones if the table has any, such as a combined body , else every indexed text field) fused by reciprocal rank with the similarity of the first embedded vector field whose model is open; mode=\"text\" or \"vector\" uses one side only. where adds a condition, built in Python or in SQL with params for its placeholders: embed(text, field=None) returns the query vector of a text ( embed(image=data, field=\"emb\") of an image file's bytes, by a model of images , with the package built with --features vision , and embed(audio=data) of a sound file's, by a model of sound , built with --features audio ), embed_tokens(text, field) the vectors of a query's tokens by the ColBERT model of a multi-vector field , as maxsim takes them ( {\"embed\": text, \"field\": \"tok\"} as a parameter gives the same), and len(db) the number of rows. plan(sql, params) returns a statement's plan as a dict without running it, and run(plan) runs a plan: a read's Result , or the number of rows a write wrote."],
["python.html#similar-rows-more-like-this-vector_of","ChloeDB from Python","Similar rows (more like this): vector_of","{\"vector_of\": key} as a parameter is the vector a stored row already has, so ranking by similarity to it lists the rows nearest that row in meaning: \"more like this\" under a product, \"readers also liked\" under a book. Leave the row itself out by its key, and put any other condition in the same WHERE : The vector is the row's first VECTOR field's, embedded or given in its rows, from the table the statement's FROM names; {\"vector_of\": key, \"field\": \"emb\", \"table\": \"books\"} names another field or table. A key no row has is an error: query error: no row \"b99\" . The search guide runs it on a catalogue."],
["python.html#limits-memory-deadlines-interrupting","ChloeDB from Python","Limits: memory, deadlines, interrupting","A query runs in your process, so what it builds up (its groups, the rows it orders and returns, a percentile's values, a join's tables) is held by your program. The queries of every database a process opens share a memory budget, by default a quarter of the machine's memory (the cgroup's limit in a container, else RAM), or CHLOEDB_QUERY_MEMORY_MB MiB when that is set when the first database opens ( 0 : no budget). A query that would hold more stops and raises ChloeDBError with code memory , naming the budget and how to raise it, and gives back what it held; the program and its other queries go on. SELECT * FROM big on a table larger than the budget, a GROUP BY over a column with a value per row or a percentile over every row fail this way rather than taking the machine's memory. The counts are estimates of what a query grows, not of every allocation, and the index a query reads is mapped from the file and not counted. A query has no deadline unless it is given one. timeout_ms (to query , query_arrow , scroll , sql , search and run , as a Client 's query takes it) stops it once it has run that long: it raises DeadlineError , naming the timeout_ms . memory= gives a query a budget of its own, in bytes ( memory=0 : none). A database's own limits, for every query that does not give its own, are set when it opens or later: db.interrupt() , from any thread, stops the queries of the database running now, as sqlite3.Connection.interrupt does: each raises DeadlineError within a few thousand rows, and queries that start after it run. Calls release the GIL while they run, so another thread (a watchdog, a web server's request that was cancelled) can call it: A query checks its deadline and interrupt between segments and every 1,024 rows it groups, orders, walks or joins. A write is not cut short: sql 's limits apply to a SELECT . Ctrl-C reaches Python only when a call returns, so a notebook stops a runaway query with timeout_ms or interrupt() from another thread."],
["python.html#queries-built-in-python","ChloeDB from Python","Queries built in Python","table() starts a query of the table built from Python values instead of SQL text, so nothing is quoted or escaped: fields are named as the schema names them ( col(\"order\") , col(\"doc no\") ), and values keep their types (a str is text whatever quotes it holds, a date or datetime a timestamp, UTC unless it says otherwise). Each method returns a new query; iterating one, or run() , runs it and gives its Result : col(name) , lit(value) a field; a value where an expression is wanted == , != , < , <= , > , >= comparisons with a value or another expression & , \\| , ~ and, or, not (Python's and , or , not and chained comparisons such as 1 < col(\"x\") < 5 raise TypeError ) .isin(values) , .between(lo, hi) , .is_null() , .is_not_null() as SQL's IN , BETWEEN (a bound of None is open), IS NULL match(field, query, min=None, min_rows=None, max_rows=None) the full-text condition, in MATCH's syntax ; min=2 or min=\"75%\" asks for that many of its items, and with max_rows (or min_rows ) for as many as fit at_least(n, *conds, min_rows=None, max_rows=None) , count_true(*conds) at least n of the conditions hold ( n a count, or a percentage such as \"75%\" ), as SQL's AT_LEAST , or with max_rows (or min_rows ) as many as fit ; how many hold, a number to rank by bm25(field, text, syntax=None, min=None, min_rows=None, max_rows=None) , similarity(field, vector) , rrf(*scores, k=60, depth=100) , ranked(keys) scores; bm25 reads weights, required and excluded words ( \"+mrna vaccine^2 -animal\" ) unless syntax=\"plain\" , and in order_by with max_rows ranks the rows with as many of its words as fit ; similarity takes a vector ( embed(text) gives a text's), or {term: weight} for a SPARSE field; ranked is a ranking from elsewhere , primary keys best first + , - , * , / , % , ** , abs() , round() , ln , log , exp , sqrt , sigmoid , clamp , least , greatest , coalesce , tmm(x, lo=None, hi=None) , minmax , zscore , rank , case((cond, value), ..., otherwise=None) a ranking expression of scores, fields and numbers: bm25(\"body\", q) * (1 + 0.2 * coalesce(col(\"rating\"), 0)) . + adds raw values; 0.3 * tmm(bm25(...)) + 0.7 * tmm(similarity(...)) is weighted fusion gbdt(model, *features) a ranking model 's score of a row from its features: gbdt(\"docs_ltr\", bm25(\"body\", q), col(\"rating\")) , the LightGBM or XGBoost model <models>/docs_ltr/gbdt.json reachable(field, seeds, hops=1, ...) , path(field, seeds, to, ...) , ppr(field, seeds, ...) graph conditions and PageRank as a score, with the SQL functions' options as keyword arguments score() , hops() the score a row was ranked by; its hops from a traversal's seeds features() in select , a column for each input of the ranking ( features ), named as Python writes it; any expression of scores that scores a row by itself is a column too count() , col(f).count(distinct=True) , .sum() , .avg() , .min() , .max() aggregates, for select , having and order_by over groups .alias(name) , .asc() , .desc(nulls_first=False) a column's name; an order_by direction select(...) names the columns (without it, the stored fields but vectors, as SELECT * ), where(...) keeps rows, order_by(...) ranks by a score best first or orders by fields ascending, rerank(expr, count) ranks its top count rows again ( rerank phases ), group_by(...) and having(...) group, distinct_on(...) keeps the first row of each group in the query's order, max_per(k, field) keeps at most k rows of each value , and limit(n) and offset(n) page (groups, after distinct_on ). A query is a plan , the same JSON that plan(sql) gives: q.plan() shows it, and a Client runs it on a server the same way."],
["python.html#a-server-from-python","ChloeDB from Python","A server from Python","chloedb.connect(url, token=None, timeout=30, retry_for=10) returns a Client of a running chloedb-server, using only the standard library. A method given table= , and table(name) , read that table alone, never the first one, so a key limited to tables works with them since 0.3.2 . It has schema , tables , query , sql , search , insert , upsert , delete , import_file (an upload to /v1/import , up to the server's --max-body-mb , 256 MiB unless set), import_sqlite(path, tables=None, append=False) and plan_sqlite(path) (a SQLite file's tables, uploaded the same way; SQLite in and out ), export_sqlite(path, tables=None) (the server's tables downloaded as a SQLite file), import_parquet(path, table=None, key=None) , plan_parquet(path) and export_parquet(path, table=None) ( Parquet over HTTP ) and len() , as above, plus get(key) , get_many(keys) and put(key, row, if_seq_no=None) for rows by key , write(ops) for raw write operations , explain , run(plan) (a plan from explain(...)[\"ir\"] or a table() query, run by /v1/plan ), table() ( queries built in Python ), embed(text, field=None) (a text's vector by the server's model, from /v1/embed , or embed(image=data) an image's and embed(audio=data) a sound's), embed_tokens(text, field) (a query's vectors a token, for a multi-vector field), query_logged and export_rank_log ( the rank log ), health , stats and ask(question) for the agent . A server answers at most --max-result-window rows a query (10,000 by default). scroll(sql, params, page_size=10_000, arrow=False, keep_alive_ms=None) reads past it: it yields the answer a page at a time, Result s or with arrow=True pyarrow Tables, each page at most page_size rows and at most the window, every page from the version of the database the first read ( scrolls ). Leaving the loop early ends the scroll on the server. A Database has scroll too, whose pages are slices of one answer, since a database in your process has no window. The server embeds {\"embed\": text} parameters and the rows it is sent with its own models. Errors the server reports raise chloedb.ChloeDBError (or a subclass of it, below ), with the HTTP status as .status , the JSON answer as .answer and a stable .code ; a server that cannot be reached raises urllib.error.URLError . Each thread keeps one connection to the server open between its requests (a new one after 4 seconds idle, or when the server closed it), so a checkpointer's steps over https do not pay a TLS handshake each; through a proxy ( $HTTPS_PROXY , $HTTP_PROXY ) each request goes by urllib as before. A float that is not a number (NaN, infinity) is sent as null , as a database in your process stores it. A request the server refuses for its load (429: behind on flushing its writes, its write queue full, too many queries running) did nothing, so the client sends it again after the Retry-After the server gives, for up to retry_for seconds (10; 0 sends each request once), and raises the 429 after that. A write that failed partway on a cluster (an error with \"partial\": true ) is not sent again, since the shards that took it keep it, unless it has an idempotency key. write(ops, idempotency_key=k) and sql(statement, idempotency_key=k) send a write with an idempotency key , so that sending it again does not apply it twice. A write that failed partway through a coordinator, after some shards took their part, says so in its error's answer and names its key:"],
["python.html#a-server-started-from-python","ChloeDB from Python","A server started from Python","since 0.3.7 chloedb.serve(db=None, listen=None, token=None, args=(), binary=None, wait=60) starts a chloedb-server on this machine and returns a Client of it, which stops the server when it is closed or its with block ends (and this Python process stops any it started when it ends). It is for a script, a notebook or a test that wants the server's API (the command center at the client's url , several processes on one database, a cluster's API before there is a cluster) without starting the program by hand: Without db it serves a new database in a temporary folder, removed when the server stops. It listens on 127.0.0.1 at a free port ( listen=\"host:port\" picks the address; beyond loopback the server needs a token , which it is given in its environment and the client sends), with the gRPC API off and no apps started; args are more of the server's flags , such as [\"--demo\", \"20000\"] , and win over those. wait is how many seconds it may take to answer /livez ; one that stops first raises ChloeDBError with what it printed. The client's server is the Server : its url , db , process (the subprocess.Popen ), running , output() (what it printed), and stop(timeout=10) , which sends SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after timeout seconds. A copy of the client (pickled, or session() ) talks to the same server and leaves it running when closed. The program is binary when given, else $CHLOEDB_SERVER_BIN , else the one the chloedb-server package installed ( pip install chloedb-server , or the server extra above), else chloedb-server on the PATH ; chloedb.find_server() says which, and raises FileNotFoundError saying what to install when there is none. Like the client, it needs only the standard library."],
["python.html#from-asyncio","ChloeDB from Python","From asyncio","since 0.3.2 chloedb.AsyncClient(url, token=None, timeout=30, retry_for=10) is the client for asyncio (FastAPI, aiohttp): the same methods, each a coroutine, whose request runs in a thread of the event loop's default executor, so the package still needs nothing beyond the standard library and the loop is never blocked. What reads the server and is a property or len() on Client is a coroutine here: await db.schema() , await db.count() . scroll is an async iterator, session() gives an AsyncSession , and client is the Client underneath."],
["python.html#reading-your-own-writes","ChloeDB from Python","Reading your own writes","A read from a replica , or through a coordinator whose shards have replicas, can be answered by a copy that has not applied a write yet. The servers fix that with sequence numbers ( reads that see a write ), when they number their writes ( --seq-no ): A write's count is a chloedb.Written : an int (it compares, adds, prints and serialises as one, so code that used the number goes on working) with the write's seq_no from a server, or seq_nos , one per shard, from a coordinator; both are None from a server without --seq-no . write , insert , upsert , delete , put , a write by sql and a write plan by run answer one; insert and the others that send several requests answer the latest numbers of all of them. query , query_arrow , search , get , get_many , scroll , a read by sql , a read plan by run and sid_next take min_seq_no= and min_seq_nos= : the copy that answers holds those writes. A replica that has not applied them waits up to a second, then answers 503, and a coordinator passes the read to the shard's primary; a server refuses a number past its last write (409). client.session() (or chloedb.Session(url, ...) ) is a client that does this itself: it keeps the latest numbers its own writes answered and sends them with each of its reads, so reading your own writes through a coordinator or from a replica is one line. A session is safe to share between threads; session.observe(written) adds a write made by another client. Numbers given to a read explicitly are sent with the session's, the later of each. The numbers go with reads only: a server refuses them on a write (a write sees every write before it), so a session leaves them off an INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE by sql , judging a statement by its first word. A Database takes min_seq_no and min_seq_nos and ignores them: a database in your process reads every write it committed."],
["python.html#errors","ChloeDB from Python","Errors","Every error ChloeDB raises is a chloedb.ChloeDBError ( except chloedb.ChloeDBError catches them all) with a stable .code , the same in your process and from a server: the server's JSON code when it gives one, else the kind the engine's message starts with ( busy: ... ), else the HTTP status. The common codes are raised as subclasses: code class when bad_request BadRequestError a bad schema, query, value or request (HTTP 400, 413, 422); sending it again fails again unauthorized UnauthorizedError no token, or one without the rights the request needs (401, 403) not_found NotFoundError no such route, table or cursor (404); get of a missing key answers None instead conflict ConflictError refused by the state it met (409): an if_seq_no that no longer holds, an idempotency key still running, a min_seq_no past the server's last write conflict DuplicateKeyError since 0.3.2 an INSERT of a key the table already holds; a ConflictError and a BadRequestError both (it was a BadRequestError alone before), with .table , .field (the key field), .key and .row (the row's place in the write, from 1) where the message names them partial_write PartialWriteError a coordinator wrote some shards and not others ( answer[\"partial\"] ): send it again with the same idempotency_key busy BusyError the database is open to write already, in another process or in this one (the message says which, and what to do instead), or another writer holds it ( common errors ) overloaded OverloadedError refused for the server's load before any work (429), after the client's retries timeout DeadlineError a deadline passed: a query's timeout_ms , a coordinator's wait for a shard (504), the engine's own unavailable UnavailableError no copy can serve it now (503): a replica behind, a primary replaced, a hosted model down A write through a coordinator that may or may not be applied (its shard's primary stopped answering, or was replaced while the write was being acknowledged) keeps its status's class, UnavailableError (503) or ConflictError (409), and says so: .in_doubt is true and .idempotency_key is the key to send it again with, which applies it once (the coordinator's own when the write had none; the server's Failover ). Do not send it again as it was: without the key it could be applied twice. chloedb.__version__ is the package's version since 0.3.2 , and the package ships py.typed , so type checkers read its annotations. Other codes have no subclass: memory (a query past its memory budget, limits ), corrupt , io , internal (another 5xx) and error . chloedb.error_code(message, status, answer) gives the code of any message. A server that cannot be reached still raises urllib.error.URLError , and a client's own timeout TimeoutError , as before."],
["python.html#database-and-client-what-differs","ChloeDB from Python","Database and Client: what differs","A script moves between chloedb.open and chloedb.connect unchanged as long as it keeps to what both have. timeout_ms means the same on both: a query's deadline ( limits ). Options that only a server can honour raise TypeError on a Database rather than being taken as query parameters: idempotency_key (to sql , write ), keep_alive_ms (to scroll ), and timeout_ms to a method that runs no query ( plan ); a query parameter of one of those names goes in the params dict. The rest of the differences: Database (in your process) Client (a server) a write's count int Written , an int with seq_no / seq_nos min_seq_no , min_seq_nos taken and ignored sent: the answering copy holds those writes timeout_ms the query's deadline, none by default sent: the query's deadline, the server's --timeout-ms (5 s) by default memory , interrupt() a query's budget; stops the running queries the server's --query-memory-mb idempotency_key , keep_alive_ms TypeError sent scroll slices of one answer pages of a server-side scroll, past --max-result-window export_sqlite returns a dict returns the tables' names export_parquet tables= , returns a dict table= , returns the table's name import_sqlite , import_parquet take schemas= / schema= the server's schemas a plan plan(sql) explain(sql)[\"ir\"] only here optimize , vacuum , expire , refresh , add_column , drop_column , rename_column , import_arrow , embed_errors put , stream , source , sources , plan_sqlite , plan_parquet , health , stats , ask , session errors ChloeDBError subclasses, status None the same classes and codes, with status and answer unreachable urllib.error.URLError"],
["python.html#streams-and-kafka","ChloeDB from Python","Streams and Kafka","client.stream(events) writes an iterable of stream lines through /v1/stream as it is read, each an operation or one event's operations as {\"ops\": [...], \"source\": {\"name\", \"offset\", \"ts\"}} , and returns the stream's last answer ( lines , written , skipped ). client.source(name) is the offset of the last event a source wrote (None for none), and client.sources() lists them all. A server skips the operations of an offset it has reached, so a stream resumed from source(name) + 1 , or from anywhere before it, writes each event once. chloedb.ingest.kafka is a Kafka (or Redpanda) consumer built on it (for one that needs no process of yours, the server reads topics itself with a Kafka connector ), which needs confluent-kafka ( pip install confluent-kafka , or pip install \"./chloedb-py[kafka]\" ). Each partition is a source named <topic>/<partition> (after prefix= ), and a partition given to the consumer is read from after the offset the server has, not from what the consumer group committed to Kafka, so each event is written once across consumer restarts, server restarts and rebalances. Within a partition the events are written in order. The function gives an event's operations, which commit together with its offset; None writes the offset alone (for events to leave out). Without one, each value is JSON: an operation, or {\"ops\": [...]} . The consumer runs until interrupted, or until max_events= events were read or none came for idle= seconds, and returns counts ( events , written , skipped , streams , retries ). It keeps a stream open for seconds_per_stream= (1 by default), then commits the stream's offsets to Kafka for Kafka's own lag tools and opens the next. When the server cannot be reached, or answers 429 or 5xx, it waits and reads every partition again from the server's offsets, for up to retry_for= seconds (60). An event the function fails on stops it after the events before it are written, so a restart begins with that event. config= adds librdkafka settings, and consumers of one group share the partitions. ingest.consume(client, log, to_ops) runs the same loop over another log, through the small ingest.Log interface."],
["python.html#common-errors","ChloeDB from Python","Common errors","The errors people meet first, each as the last line of its traceback, with what it means and what to do. The troubleshooting page has the server's and the installer's too."],
["python.html#busyerror-busy-the-database-is-open-elsewhere","ChloeDB from Python","BusyError: busy: the database is open elsewhere","chloedb.open of a file that is open to write already: in another process (a second script, a chloedb-server serving it, the process that Flask's debug reloader starts beside yours, a second gunicorn worker), or in this one, by an earlier chloedb.open of the same path that is still open (a module imported under two names, a notebook cell run again before the old db was closed, a test that never closed its database). since 0.3.8 (unreleased) The message goes on to say which, from the process id the writer keeps beside the file: this process has it open for writing already , process N (command), the parent of this process (a reloader, as above), or process N (command) has it open for writing for any other process (the command on Linux), with what to do in each case, in Python's words at the end. Before 0.3.8 it stopped at the database is open elsewhere . In one process, open a database once and share the object: a db at module level that every request and thread uses (threads may query and write it at once). db.close() , or a with block, closes it, and the next open then succeeds. Under a reloader (Flask's debug=True , Django's runserver, uvicorn --reload ), open the database in a function the routes call, with functools.cache on it so that one is kept, rather than at import: the reloader's first process imports the module and never serves, so only the process that serves opens it. Or run without the reloader: app.run(debug=True, use_reloader=False) . To read it from other processes while one writes it, open it there with chloedb.open(path, read_only=True) : each query sees the writer's commits, and writes raise ChloeDBError . To write from several processes (a web app's workers, a reloader), serve the file with chloedb-server --db path and connect each process with chloedb.connect(url) , as Build a search page does. chloedb-server --db path on a file a script has open stops with ERROR chloedb_server: open path: busy: the database is open elsewhere : stop the script, or let it use the server."],
["python.html#urlerror-urlopen-error-errno-111-connection-refused","ChloeDB from Python","URLError: <urlopen error [Errno 111] Connection refused>","A client ( chloedb.connect , chloedb.AsyncClient ) found nothing listening at its URL: the server is not running, or it listens on another address or port (its log's HTTP and UI on ... line says which). curl -s http://127.0.0.1:7700/livez answers when one is up. The client tries again only when a server refused a request for its load (429), so it fails at once when none is there. URLError is not a ChloeDBError , so except chloedb.ChloeDBError lets it through; it is an OSError , as the client's own TimeoutError is:"],
["python.html#badrequesterror--bad-value-for-minutes-five","ChloeDB from Python","BadRequestError: ... bad value for minutes: \"five\"","A value of another type than its field's: here text for an INT field, in the first row of the write (rows count from 1). An insert writes all of its rows or none. Form fields and query strings arrive as text, so convert them before writing ( int(form[\"minutes\"]) ); db.tables() lists each field's type. The same mistake in SQL says query error: minutes is an INT field: give it a number, not text 'five' , and a field the table does not have says schema error: row 1: schema error: no field minuts ."],
["python.html#chloedberror-bookschloedb-does-not-exist-pass-a-schema-to-create-it","ChloeDB from Python","ChloeDBError: books.chloedb does not exist: pass a schema to create it","chloedb.open(path) without a schema, where no database is: a misspelt path, or a script started from another folder (a relative path is read from the current directory). Give the right path, or the schema to create the database. Its code is error ."],
["python.html#chloedberror-corrupt-database-no-valid-meta-slot","ChloeDB from Python","ChloeDBError: corrupt database: no valid meta slot","Most often the file is not a ChloeDB database at all: a JSON Lines file, a CSV or a SQLite database opened by mistake. ChloeDB does not change that file, but leaves an empty <path>-readers folder beside it, which you can delete. Rows from such a file go into a database with db.import_file(path) (JSON Lines, JSON, CSV, TSV), db.import_sqlite(path) or db.import_parquet(path) . A database that is really damaged names what is damaged and how to recover ( damaged files ). Its code is corrupt ."],
["python.html#a-vector-field-whose-model-is-not-open","ChloeDB from Python","A vector field whose model is not open","A write that leaves a vector to a model the database could not open. db.embed_errors says why, for each such field: Fetch the model once with chloedb-server fetch-model potion-retrieval-32M (into ~/.local/share/chloedb/models , or $CHLOEDB_MODELS ), or open the database with models= the folder that has it, then open the database again. Until then, a query with {\"embed\": text} fails with query error: no embedded field has its model open . The BERT models ( bge-small-en-v1.5 and others) do not run in the prebuilt wheels: embed_errors then says model bge-small-en-v1.5 is a BERT model: build with the bert feature , and troubleshooting says what to use instead."],
["python.html#the-rank-log","ChloeDB from Python","The rank log","query_logged(sql, params, context=..., variant=..., candidates=...) runs a ranked query and writes what it served to a rank log : a row per row answered, with its position, its score and the values its ranking read, as they were computed. candidates logs that many rows from the query's OFFSET , those past its LIMIT as not shown, and ts (a datetime or microseconds) says when it was served, now by default. The Result is the query's, with result.request , the id its log rows carry, for labelling them later. export_rank_log(...) reads the log back as a list of dicts for training, each with its context's history and its label as of the row's time, from a table of events:"],
["python.html#semantic-id-codebooks","ChloeDB from Python","Semantic-ID codebooks","A semantic-ID field gives each row a code from its vector by a codebook, which chloedb.train.codebook trains and saves in the models directory before a table names it: It returns the SHA-256 of the file, which the table records. The options are levels (1 to 4, default 3), k centroids a level (up to 256), iterations of k-means a level (25), sample , the vectors it trains on (100 a centroid by default, drawn at random), seed (the same vectors and options give the same codebook), balanced , table (a database's first by default) and models (the default models directory, as for Database.create ). Pass the same models= to Database.create and Database.open : a codebook that does not open is listed in db.embed_errors , and writes that need a code then fail. chloedb.train.codebook_report(db, \"items-rq\", field=\"emb\") (or of vectors, as above) says what a codebook makes of a set of vectors, before a field names it for good: shared_rows are the rows whose code another row has too: only the tie number tells them apart, so a decoder that writes codes cannot. A level's perplexity is how many of its numbers the rows are spread over when weighed by use ( k when every number has as many rows as any other), and residual the share of the vectors' squared length the code so far leaves unsaid. Plain k-means uses the later levels' numbers unevenly, as above: what a level leaves of the vectors points every way alike, and there most centroids end on one far vector each while a few near the middle take the rest. balanced=True trains each level with every centroid given the same number of the training vectors. On the 12,101 items of Amazon Beauty (text vectors of 384 dimensions, 3 levels of 256) the third level's perplexity goes from 57 to 251 and the rows sharing a code from 3,288 to 2,601; training takes 17 s, not 6 ( results ). It holds a distance for every training vector and centroid (8 bytes each: 52 MB at the default sample ). Rows are coded by their nearest centroid either way."],
["python.html#next-items","ChloeDB from Python","Next items","chloedb.ngrams keeps counts of what came after the last one or two items in a table, and chloedb.next_items(db, history, k=10) reads them with backoff ( Next items ):"],
["python.html#sequence-models","ChloeDB from Python","Sequence models","encode('model', history) turns a user's events into a vector of an item field, by a model that chloedb.train.sequence trains with PyTorch ( pip install torch ) and saves in the models directory: The histories come from one of events (a table of the database: its columns of users, items, and optionally actions, times, and order for events with equal times), sequences (a dict from user to a list of (item, action, time in seconds) , oldest first) or records (the rank log's export with history : each record's history is a training example, and its row the target when its label is positive or missing). The item vectors are the field's, or vectors (a dict from key to vector). What it learns : every event is the target of the events before it. Each item is scored as similarity scores it, the dot product of the model's unit vector with the item's vector, over temperature (0.05), and the loss is cross-entropy against the other targets of the batch ( negatives=\"in_batch\" , the default) or every item ( \"all\" , for catalogs that fit). In a batch, an item that is often a target is a negative as often, which teaches the model to rank what is popular low: logq=True takes from each negative's score the log of the chance that the batch holds it, worked out from how often each item is a target (the target of its own row keeps its score), and uniform_negatives=n adds n items drawn evenly from all of them to every batch, so items that are seldom or never targets are negatives too. Use both for a catalog too large for \"all\" : on Yambda-50M's 629,298 tracks they took Recall@10 on held-out events from 0.0229 to 0.0334 after four epochs ( results ). With learn_items=True the item vectors are trained too, starting from the field's, and returned normalized in out[\"vectors\"] ; without it the field keeps its vectors and the model learns to map histories into their space, so a new item is found as soon as it has a vector. The item vectors go with the model : it is saved with the vectors it was trained with ( items.safetensors , part of its hash), and encode refuses a history whose items have other vectors in the field. chloedb.train.write_vectors(db, name, models=None) writes them into the field the model's config names, by an UPDATE of that field alone for each item (a Database or a Client ); returns the rows written. chloedb._sequence.read_items(path) reads them back. Options : width (64), layers (2), heads (1), ffn (4 × width), max_len (200 events), gap_buckets (32), actions (the actions seen by default; others count as one more), epochs (50), batch (128), lr (1e-3), dropout (0.2), seed . With holdout=True each user's last event is kept out of training, and training stops when NDCG@10 on those has not risen for patience epochs (5), keeping the best epoch. max_seconds stops it after the first epoch that ends past that many seconds. log=print prints each epoch's loss and metrics, which out[\"history\"] also holds. It returns {\"path\", \"history\", \"vectors\"} . Pass the same models= to Database.open , or start the server with --models : a coordinator needs the model, its shards don't."],
["python.html#search-on-the-device","ChloeDB from Python","Search on the device","chloedb.nearby(url, table, dir) holds a slice of a chloedb-server's table near a user: the rows of the tiles around them in a database in dir , searched with the server's statistics. move_to(lat, lon) fetches the tiles it lacks, and search(q, k=10, d0=1000) answers on the device when it can prove the answer is the server's, and asks the server otherwise; the result's where is \"device\" or \"server\" and why the reason. For a vector slice , focus(taste, rows=20_000) holds the clusters nearest a taste vector and similar(vector, k=10) answers a similarity search the same way, fetching the few clusters its codes name when that proves the answer."],
["python.html#wheels-platforms-and-building-from-source","ChloeDB from Python","Wheels, platforms and building from source","The package is in chloedb-py/ : a Rust extension built with maturin , for CPython 3.10 and later. pip install chloedb ( install ) is all most machines need; this is what it installs, and the other ways."],
["python.html#prebuilt","ChloeDB from Python","Prebuilt","Each chloedb-py-v<version> release on GitHub has a wheel per platform, for Python 3.10 and later, that installs with no Rust and no compiler. The same wheels are on PyPI , so pip picks the one for your machine: platform wheel macOS 11+ on Apple silicon chloedb-<version>-cp310-abi3-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl macOS 11+ on Intel chloedb-<version>-cp310-abi3-macosx_11_0_x86_64.whl Linux x86-64, glibc 2.28+ (RHEL 8, Amazon Linux 2023, Debian 10, Ubuntu 20.04 and later) chloedb-<version>-cp310-abi3-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl Linux aarch64, glibc 2.28+ chloedb-<version>-cp310-abi3-manylinux_2_28_aarch64.whl Linux x86-64 and aarch64, glibc 2.35+ (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12 and later), release 0.2.0 chloedb-0.2.0-cp310-abi3-manylinux_2_35_x86_64.whl , ..._aarch64.whl The workflow py-release.yml builds them on a tag chloedb-py-v<version> (or strata-py-v<version> ; the version in chloedb-py/pyproject.toml ), installs each one into an empty virtualenv and runs the embedded tests against it, then attaches them to the release. The Linux wheels are built in the manylinux_2_28 container (AlmaLinux 8). The same tag publishes them to PyPI, from 0.3.0 on; pip install chloedb -f https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb/releases/expanded_assets/chloedb-py-v<version> installs a release from GitHub instead. Windows and Alpine (musl) have no wheel: build from source there (Windows through WSL 2)."],
["python.html#only-the-client-with-no-build","ChloeDB from Python","Only the client, with no build","chloedb.connect , the client of a running chloedb-server, needs only Python's standard library. Without the extension, from a checkout of the repository, put chloedb-py/python on the path and import it: The package imports without the extension, and chloedb.Client , chloedb.connect , chloedb.serve , chloedb.ChloeDBError , Result and the query builder work. A database in the process ( chloedb.open , chloedb.Database , from_sqlite , the codebook training) raises ImportError saying what to install."],
["python.html#from-source","ChloeDB from Python","From source","It needs Rust (stable) and a C compiler; the build takes about 12 minutes on 4 cores. or, while working on it: The package includes DataFusion , which runs joins, groups and sorts over many joined rows ( --no-default-features leaves it out). Model2Vec embedding models need nothing more. For the BERT models, build with --features bert ( --features accelerate on a Mac): maturin develop --release --features bert , or MATURIN_PEP517_ARGS=\"--features bert\" pip install ./chloedb-py . The extension runs on any CPU of its architecture: ChloeDB picks its SIMD kernels (AVX2, AVX-512, the aarch64 dot product instructions) at run time. RUSTFLAGS=\"-C target-cpu=native\" builds one for the building machine only."],
["frames.html","DataFrames in Python","","store[\"table\"] is a chloedb.Frame : a lazy dataframe written the way Polars writes one. Nothing is read until you ask for rows. Then the whole chain compiles to one SQL statement, which runs where the data is (a file in your process, or a chloedb-server), and the answer comes back as an Arrow table, which pandas, Polars, NumPy, DuckDB and PyTorch read without a copy. The same code runs on a file and on a server."],
["frames.html#install","DataFrames in Python","Install","Frames are part of the chloedb package ( Python ) and need pyarrow 14 or later; pandas and Polars are optional, for to_pandas() and to_polars() : Frames came in release 0.2.0: release 0.1.0's wheel ( strata-db , from before the rename) has no frames and no frame extra."],
["frames.html#a-first-frame","DataFrames in Python","A first frame","cdb.connect(path) opens a database file, or makes it with its first create_table ; cdb.connect(url) is a Client of a server or a coordinator of shards, with token= when the server asks for one. Both give frames and create_table the same way."],
["frames.html#making-tables","DataFrames in Python","Making tables","create_table(name, data, ...) makes a table from rows and returns its frame. data is anything that streams Arrow (a pyarrow Table, a Polars DataFrame, a DuckDB relation, another frame), a pandas DataFrame, or a list of dicts. The schema comes from the Arrow types as the Parquet import makes one: strings are KEYWORD (or TEXT when they read as prose), lists of numbers of one length are vectors, timestamps are TIMESTAMP. argument key the primary key column (default a column named id ; \"\" for none) text , keyword columns to make TEXT (indexed for search ) or KEYWORD embed vector fields a model fills from other fields, where the rows are written schema the whole schema instead if_exists \"fail\" (the default), \"append\" (upsert by key) or \"replace\" frame.write_to(store, \"table\") runs a frame and writes its answer to a table of any store: a server's rows to a local file for a notebook, or features computed on a file to a server. The key is the source table's when the answer has its column."],
["frames.html#building-a-frame","DataFrames in Python","Building a frame","Each method returns a new frame, and none of them runs anything. method filter(cond, ..., name=value) the rows where every condition holds; conditions on indexed fields are answered by the indexes select(*cols, name=expr) , with_columns(...) the answer's columns; every column with these added or replaced. Aggregates alone give one row: select(n=cdb.len(), total=col(\"x\").sum()) drop(*cols) , rename({old: new}) sort(by, ..., descending=False, nulls_last=True) rows without a value come last head(n=5) , slice(offset, length) the first rows; an unsorted, unranked frame's are any rows, as in SQL group_by(*keys, name=expr).agg(...) , .len() a row per group; a key may be an expression ( month=col(\"ts\").dt.truncate(\"month\") ). A filter after it is HAVING join(other, on=, left_on=, right_on=, how=\"inner\", suffix=\"_right\") how is \"inner\" or \"left\" , and both frames are of one store unique() , unique(subset, keep=\"first\") each row once; the first row of each value in the frame's order ( DISTINCT ON ) union(*frames) , cdb.concat(frames) the rows of each ( UNION ALL ; distinct=True for UNION ) sample(fraction=, seed=) , sample(n, seed=) , split(fraction, seed=) , shuffle(seed=) a seeded sample, train/test split or order ( below )"],
["frames.html#expressions","DataFrames in Python","Expressions","col(name) , cdb.lit(value) , + - * / % ** , == != < <= > >= , & \\| ~ columns, values, arithmetic, comparisons, and, or, not .is_in(values) , .is_between(lo, hi) , .is_null() , .is_not_null() , .fill_null(value) , .cast(\"int\") cdb.when(cond).then(a).when(...).then(b).otherwise(c) CASE WHEN .str.lower() , .upper() , .len_chars() , .contains(s) , .starts_with(s) , .ends_with(s) , .like(p) , .ilike(p) strings .dt.truncate(\"month\") , .year() , .quarter() , .month() , .week() , .day() , .weekday() , .hour() , .epoch() , .strftime(\"%Y-%m\") timestamps .sum() , .mean() , .median() , .quantile(q, interpolation=\"linear\") , .min() , .max() , .count() , .n_unique() , cdb.len() aggregates, for agg or a select of totals .over(*partition_by, order_by=, descending=) with .rank(method=\"min\") , .cum_sum() , .shift(n) or an aggregate windows: col(\"amount\").rank(descending=True).over(\"user\") , col(\"x\").shift(1).over(\"user\", order_by=\"ts\") .hash(seed=0) an int from 0 to 2 63 - 1 of the value and the seed, the same everywhere ( SQL's hash ); col(\"item\").hash(col(\"user\").hash(7)) hashes two columns Every function of queries built in Python is an expression of a frame too: cdb.bm25 , cdb.similarity , cdb.rrf , cdb.minmax , cdb.zscore , cdb.gbdt , cdb.coalesce , and the rest."],
["frames.html#search-and-ranking","DataFrames in Python","Search and ranking","Search is a verb of the frame. Each of these keeps the k best rows (100 by default), best first, and adds a score column: search reads weights, required and excluded words ( \"+mrna vaccine^2 -animal\" ) unless syntax=\"plain\" . rerank is a rerank phase , for a costlier score over the rows of the search before it."],
["frames.html#samples-and-splits","DataFrames in Python","Samples and splits","A row is in a sample by the hash of its key ( hash ), or of the columns on , so a seed gives the same rows from a file and from a server, today and after more rows are written: the rows already sampled stay sampled. A smaller fraction's rows are among a larger's, and split 's two frames are the rows under the fraction and the rest. A frame without its table's key (a group-by's) names the columns with on= , and a row whose on is NULL is in no sample. sample(n) and shuffle sort the frame's rows by their hash, where the data is."],
["frames.html#getting-the-rows","DataFrames in Python","Getting the rows","to_arrow() , collect() a pyarrow Table to_polars() , pl.DataFrame(frame) a Polars DataFrame, sharing the Arrow memory to_pandas(arrow_dtypes=True) a pandas DataFrame backed by the Arrow columns ( False copies into NumPy) to_numpy() a 2-D array of the columns, or of the one vector column, (rows, dimensions) float32: embeddings for a model to_dicts() , iter_batches(batch_size) a dict a row; RecordBatches count() , item() the number of rows ( COUNT(*) , run where the data is); the one value of a one-row, one-column answer A frame streams Arrow ( __arrow_c_stream__ ), so DuckDB reads it by name ( duckdb.sql(\"SELECT ... FROM frame\") ) and pa.table(frame) runs it. In a notebook a frame shows its first ten rows."],
["frames.html#writing","DataFrames in Python","Writing","Writes are explicit; a frame never changes a table as a side effect."],
["frames.html#what-runs-where","DataFrames in Python","What runs where","A frame is one SQL statement, so it runs where the data is and only the answer moves: a group-by over a server's ten million rows sends back a row per group. frame.sql() shows the statement and frame.explain() the plan the store runs. A filter on an indexed field is answered by its index. A group-by on indexed KEYWORD, number, BOOL or TIMESTAMP columns (or parts of a timestamp) folds into the table's own query; any other key first computes its rows in a WITH step, with only the columns it reads. Joins are inner or left, between tables of one store. To join a server's table with a local one, copy one side with write_to first. A server answers at most --max-result-window rows a query (10,000 by default, its limits ), so a frame reads a server's answer by a scroll , a page of up to the window at a time, every page from one version: to_arrow() joins the pages, and iter_batches() yields them as they come. A sorted frame, or one that groups or joins, is held by the server between pages, at most its --max-scroll-rows (1,000,000) rows; a plain filter and select reads any number. Through a coordinator, a filter and select, or a frame sorted by a column, is merged from its shards' pages as they come, two pages held at most. A server from before scrolls reads a frame in one query, and needs a larger window for one of more rows. From a server, the answer comes back as an Arrow stream, its columns typed as a file's are, so the same frame gives the same table from either. A server too old for Arrow answers JSON, which the client types from the table's schema. Not there yet: right, full and cross joins, and pivot and melt ."],
["javascript.html","ChloeDB from JavaScript","","The chloedb package opens a ChloeDB file inside a Node, Bun or Deno process, the way better-sqlite3 opens a SQLite file: no server, no configuration. It can also talk to a running chloedb-server from any JavaScript runtime with fetch (a browser, a worker, Node, Bun, Deno). Both have the same methods, so code written against a local file works against a server."],
["javascript.html#install","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Install","The package is in chloedb-js/ : a Node-API addon written in Rust ( napi-rs ) and the JavaScript around it, an ES module, for Node 18 or later, Bun and Deno."],
["javascript.html#prebuilt","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Prebuilt","bun add chloedb , pnpm add chloedb and yarn add chloedb work the same way. npm installs the JavaScript and, as an optional dependency, the addon built for your machine ( @chloedb/darwin-arm64 , @chloedb/linux-x64-gnu , ...), so nothing is compiled: no Rust, no C compiler. It imports as \"chloedb\" . Windows and Alpine (musl) have no prebuilt addon: build from source there (Windows through WSL 2). Release 0.1.0, from before the rename, was published as strata-db and imports as \"strata-db\" . platform addon package release tarball macOS 11+ on Apple silicon @chloedb/darwin-arm64 chloedb-<version>-darwin-arm64.tgz macOS 11+ on Intel @chloedb/darwin-x64 chloedb-<version>-darwin-x64.tgz Linux x86-64, glibc 2.35+ (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12 and later) @chloedb/linux-x64-gnu chloedb-<version>-linux-x64-gnu.tgz Linux arm64, glibc 2.35+ @chloedb/linux-arm64-gnu chloedb-<version>-linux-arm64-gnu.tgz If importing chloedb throws an error that calls it a placeholder, npm installed chloedb 0.0.x, which only held the name until ChloeDB's releases were published there (the first one, 0.0.1, still says Strata, ChloeDB's name before the rename). npm install chloedb@latest in that app replaces it and rewrites the range in package.json (a plain npm install chloedb keeps the ^0.0.x range already there, and the placeholder with it). Each chloedb-js-v<version> release on GitHub also has a tarball per platform with the addon built in, for a machine that cannot reach npm's registry. Install the tarball for your platform, or its URL: The workflow js-release.yml builds both on a chloedb-js-v<version> tag, the way napi-rs packages are laid out ( chloedb names each platform's package as an optional dependency, of which npm installs the one of your machine), and installs each into an empty app and runs it there before attaching the tarballs to the release and publishing the packages to npm (the workflow's header says what publishing needs). node scripts/pack.mjs <platform> packs a tarball by hand, and --npm <platform> and --npm-main the packages npm gets."],
["javascript.html#from-source","ChloeDB from JavaScript","From source","It needs Rust (stable) and a C compiler. npm install of the directory builds the addon itself when chloedb.node is missing. npm run build -- --features bert builds more of ChloeDB's features in ( bert , onnx , vision , audio ; datafusion , parquet and s3 are on by default). $CHLOEDB_NODE_ADDON (or $STRATA_NODE_ADDON ) loads an addon from another place. The client alone ( chloedb/client ) is plain JavaScript with no addon: a bundler takes it into a page, and the package's browser and workerd conditions give it for chloedb too."],
["javascript.html#a-database-in-the-process","ChloeDB from JavaScript","A database in the process","open(path, {schema}) opens the file, or creates it with the schema when it does not exist; Database.create(path, schema) and Database.open(path) do one or the other. A schema is an object, JSON text or the path of a JSON file, in the short form or the long one . directory: true keeps a database as a directory of segment files, models is where embedded fields' models are ( $CHLOEDB_MODELS , else ~/.local/share/chloedb/models (or ~/.local/share/strata/models , on a machine that has that folder from before the rename and no ~/.local/share/chloedb ; before 0.3.2, always); chloedb-server fetch-model fetches them), and threads how many cores index builds and each query use (every core by default). Every call answers a promise and runs on a worker thread of Node's pool, so queries and writes leave the event loop free and many run at once. Writes made at once wait for each other and share commits (one disk flush for all those waiting), so none fails as busy. One process at a time may have a database open to write it, and only once: a second open of the file, by another process or by the same one, gets \"busy: the database is open elsewhere\", so a program opens it once and shares the Database (in a dev server that runs modules again, on globalThis ). Others can open it with Database.open(path, { readOnly: true }) to read it while it is written, the workers of a cluster, say: each query sees the writer's commits so far, every write fails, and refresh() looks for new commits at once ( how readers follow the writer ). For writes from several processes, serve it with chloedb-server."],
["javascript.html#querying","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Querying","call what db.query<T>(sql, params, {timeoutMs, memory, signal, pageSize, keepAliveMs}) a SELECT: a Result ( limits ); with pageSize , its first page and a cursor ( past the window ) db.sql<T>(sql, params, {timeoutMs, memory, signal}) any statement: a SELECT's Result , or the rows an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE wrote; CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE and DROP TABLE too db.execute(sql, params) an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE (or a CREATE , ALTER or DROP ): the rows it wrote, a number; a SELECT is refused ( TypeError ) db.next<T>(cursor, {keepAliveMs}) , db.closeCursor(cursor) , db.scroll<T>(sql, params, {pageSize}) a cursor's next page (its cursor null after the last), a cursor ended (true if it was open), and every page of a SELECT as an async iterator db.highlight(query, texts, {field, size, pre, post, html}) texts (one, or a list) with the words query matches marked, as TEXT field field reads them (stems, synonyms, typos, so* ), cut to about size characters (160; 0: whole), HTML-escaped around <mark> tags db.correct(text, {field}) \"did you mean\": {text, changed, words} , the words no row holds and that look like typos replaced by those meant, each word {word, term, to, toWord, edits, chance} ( did you mean ) db.search(text, {limit, where, columns, mode, textFields, vectorField, params, table}) the rows that best match a text: BM25 over the text fields ( mode: \"text\" ), the similarity of an embedded vector field ( \"vector\" ), or both fused by reciprocal rank ( \"hybrid\" , what \"auto\" picks when a model is open) db.plan(sql, params) , db.run(plan) a statement's plan as JSON, without running it; running a plan db.embed(text, {field, image, audio}) , db.embedTokens(text, field) a query vector by an embedded field's model (of an image's or a sound's bytes too), a multi-vector field's token vectors db.queryLogged(sql, params, {context, variant, ...}) , db.exportRankLog({...}) the rank log : a ranked query that logs what it served, and the log as records for training db.sidNext(prefixes, {field, where}) , db.trainCodebook(field, name, {balanced}) semantic IDs: the codes that can follow prefixes, and training a codebook ( balanced: true uses every level's numbers evenly) Parameters are :name in the SQL and an object of values: numbers, strings, booleans, null, Date s, arrays (an array of numbers or a Float32Array is a vector), an object of numbers (sparse term weights), {embed: \"some text\"} (the text's vector by an embedded field's model) or {vector_of: key} (a stored row's vector). A Result has columns , rows (arrays in column order), keys (each row's primary key), scores (what it was ranked by), stats (what the engine did) and, a page of a cursor's, cursor . Iterating it gives each row as an object; r.at(i) , r.objects() , r.column(name) and r.scalar() (a count, a sum) read it other ways. field may be left out of highlight and correct when the table has one indexed TEXT field. Both are chloedb-server's routes ( POST /v1/highlight and POST /v1/correct ) on a Client , and the same answers from the database in the process, so a search page's code runs on either."],
["javascript.html#reading-past-the-window-cursors","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Reading past the window: cursors","A query answers at most 10,000 rows, counted to the end of its OFFSET , as chloedb-server's does ( --max-result-window ): LIMIT 10 OFFSET 10000 is refused with a BadRequestError that says how to read on, and so is a query without LIMIT that matches more. ( changed: a Database had no window before, and the same query worked in the process and failed on a server.) { maxResultWindow: 0 } when it opens, or db.setLimits({ maxResultWindow: 0 }) , lifts it for that database, as --max-result-window 0 does for a server; another number moves it. Every row of a query, past the window, comes a page at a time from a cursor. pageSize on query answers the first page and its cursor ; next(cursor) answers the next one, until a page whose cursor is null. Every page reads the version of the database the first one read, so rows written or deleted meanwhile neither appear nor go missing, and a page holds at most the window's rows: A cursor not read for keepAliveMs (60 seconds, at most an hour) is gone, and next rejects with NotFoundError ; closeCursor(cursor) ends one sooner. A plain scan of one table (no ORDER BY , groups or counts) reads its pages as they are asked for; any other query runs whole when the cursor starts and holds what it answered (an ordered one, its rows' places and scores; a join or groups, the rows), up to a million rows, the most the cursors of one database hold at once, as a server's --max-scroll-rows . On a Client the cursor is the server's scroll , with the same options."],
["javascript.html#limits-memory-deadlines-aborting","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Limits: memory, deadlines, aborting","A query runs in your process, so what it builds up (its groups, the rows it orders and returns, a join's tables) is held by your program. The queries of every database a process opens share a memory budget, by default a quarter of the machine's memory (the cgroup's limit in a container, else RAM), or CHLOEDB_QUERY_MEMORY_MB MiB when that is set when the first database opens ( 0 : no budget). A query that would hold more rejects with a ChloeDBError of code memory , naming the budget and how to raise it, and gives back what it held, instead of the process running out of memory. A query has no deadline unless it is given one. db.query , db.sql , db.run and db.search take a last argument of options: timeoutMs (its deadline, as a Client 's query takes it: it rejects with DeadlineError naming it), memory (a budget of its own in bytes; 0 : none) and signal , an AbortSignal that stops it when it aborts (it rejects with the signal's reason, as an aborted fetch does). A database's own limits, for every query that does not give its own, are set when it opens or later; db.interrupt() stops every query of the database running now, and returns how many there were: A query checks its deadline and its signal between segments and every 1,024 rows it groups, orders, walks or joins, so it stops within milliseconds; it holds a thread of libuv's pool ( UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE , 4 by default, which fs and dns share) until then. A write is not cut short: sql 's limits apply to a SELECT."],
["javascript.html#writing","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Writing","call what db.insert(rows, {table}) rows whose keys are new; all or none are written. 10,000 rows or more are bulk-loaded into new segments in one commit db.upsert(rows, {table}) rows, replacing the rows with their keys, in transactions of 10,000 db.delete(keys, {table}) the rows with these keys db.write(ops) write operations in one transaction: insert, upsert, delete, set of a field (a number in place, another by writing the row again) and add to a number in place db.get(key) , db.getMany(keys) rows by key without SQL, or null db.addColumn(name, type, options) , dropColumn , renameColumn ALTER TABLE db.expire() , db.optimize() , db.vacuum() , db.close() rows past their expire_by ; a table that is done changing readied for queries (its segments merged into as few as can be, deleted rows dropped, the file shrunk); the space merges freed returned to the file system; the database closed, after merging small segments and returning the space merges freed when it is an eighth of the file or more ( await using db = ... closes it too). Segments merge in the background as writes add them, one merge at a time A row is an object of field name to value. A vector may be a Float32Array (read from its bytes, without a number per element in between), a Float64Array or an array; a TIMESTAMP a Date or ISO 8601 text; a list field an array or a Set . Values come back as chloedb-server answers them: TIMESTAMPs as ISO 8601 text in UTC, vectors as arrays of numbers. An INT past 2^53 (a 64-bit key, a hash) comes back as a BigInt , and a BigInt may be written or passed as a parameter anywhere a number may; every other integer is a number. (Reading them whole takes a runtime whose JSON.parse gives a reviver the number's text: Node 21 and later, Deno, recent browsers.)"],
["javascript.html#files","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Files","call what db.importFile(path, {format, table}) a JSON Lines, JSON, CSV or TSV file: bulk-loaded into an empty table, else upserted; returns {rows, bulk, skippedFields} fromSqlite(sqlitePath, path) , planSqlite , db.importSqlite , db.exportSqlite SQLite in and out fromParquet(parquetPath, path) , planParquet , db.importParquet , db.exportParquet Parquet files and directories in and out, and s3:// URLs upgrade(path) a database from before field ids, copied into this version's format Errors reject with a ChloeDBError , whose message is the database's ( StrataError , its name before the rename, is the same class), and whose code is stable, the same here, from a server and in Python: the server's JSON code when it gives one, else the kind the message starts with ( busy: ... ), else the HTTP status. The common codes reject as subclasses of ChloeDBError , so instanceof ChloeDBError still holds for every one: code class when bad_request BadRequestError a bad schema, query, value or request (400, 413, 422) unauthorized UnauthorizedError no token, or one without the rights needed (401, 403) not_found NotFoundError no such route, table or cursor (404) conflict ConflictError refused by the state it met (409): an ifSeqNo that no longer holds, an idempotency key still running, a minSeqNo past the server's last write partial_write PartialWriteError a coordinator wrote some shards and not others: send it again with the same idempotency key busy BusyError the database is open to write elsewhere, in another process or by another open in this one (the message says which, and what to do instead), or another writer holds it overloaded OverloadedError refused for the server's load (429), after the client's retries timeout DeadlineError a deadline passed: timeoutMs , a coordinator's wait for a shard (504) unavailable UnavailableError no copy can serve it now (503) A write through a coordinator that may or may not be applied (its shard's primary stopped answering, or was replaced while the write was being acknowledged) keeps its status's class, UnavailableError (503) or ConflictError (409), and says so: err.inDoubt is true and err.idempotencyKey is the key to send it again with ( { idempotencyKey } ), which applies it once; without the key it could be applied twice ( Failover ). Other codes are memory (a query past its memory budget), corrupt , io , internal and error (which is also what open of a file that does not exist, without a schema, rejects with: \"x.chloedb does not exist: pass a schema to create it\"); errorCode(message, status, answer) gives the code of any message."],
["javascript.html#a-server-from-javascript","ChloeDB from JavaScript","A server from JavaScript","Client has the methods of Database that a server offers, over its HTTP API , and more of the server's own: health() , stats() , explain(sql) , put(key, row, {ifSeqNo}) , stream(events) (an iterable or async iterable of stream events , applied once), source(name) and sources() , ask(question) (the server's agent), and the file calls, which upload a path's bytes (Node, Bun, Deno) or bytes given (a Uint8Array , a Blob ). exportSqlite(null) and exportParquet(null) give the file's bytes instead of writing it. Rows are written 1,000 a request, keys read 10,000 a request. An error the server answers rejects with a ChloeDBError whose status is the HTTP status and answer its JSON; one where the server cannot be reached rejects with fetch 's error. count() reads the server's /v1/health , whose docs counts its first table: on a server started without a schema, whose tables were made since by CREATE TABLE , that is an empty placeholder table, and count() answers 0; (await client.query(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products\")).scalar() counts a table on any server. highlight , correct and cursors ( pageSize , next , closeCursor , scroll ) are the server's POST /v1/highlight , POST /v1/correct and scrolls . timeout (milliseconds, 30 seconds by default) bounds each request, and fetch takes another fetch. A request the server refuses for its load (429: behind on flushing its writes, its write queue full, too many queries running) did nothing, so the client sends it again after the Retry-After the server gives, for up to retryFor milliseconds (10 seconds; 0 sends each request once), and rejects with the 429 after that. A write that failed partway on a cluster (an error with \"partial\": true ) is not sent again, since the shards that took it keep it, unless it has an idempotency key. write(ops, {idempotencyKey}) and sql(sql, params, {idempotencyKey}) send a write with an idempotency key , so that sending it again does not apply it twice. A write that failed partway through a coordinator rejects with a ChloeDBError whose answer says so ( partial: true ) and names its key: await client.write(ops, { idempotencyKey: e.answer.idempotency_key }) writes the rest."],
["javascript.html#reading-your-own-writes","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Reading your own writes","A read from a replica , or through a coordinator whose shards have replicas, can be answered by a copy that has not applied a write yet. Servers that number their writes ( --seq-no ) fix that with sequence numbers ( reads that see a write ): writeResult(ops, {idempotencyKey}) is write answering {written, seqNo, seqNos, skipped} : seqNo from a server, seqNos (one per shard) from a coordinator, neither from a server without --seq-no . write , insert , upsert , delete , put and sql still answer the number of rows. query(sql, params, {minSeqNo, minSeqNos}) , search(text, {minSeqNo, minSeqNos}) , get(key, {minSeqNo, minSeqNos}) , getMany , sql (a SELECT), run (a read plan) and sidNext take them: the copy that answers holds those writes (a replica waits up to a second; a server refuses a number past its last write, 409). client.session() (or new Session(url, options) ) is a client that does this itself: it keeps the latest numbers its own writes answered ( session.seqNo , session.seqNos ) and sends them with each of its reads, so reading your own writes is one line. session.observe(answer) adds another client's write. The numbers go with reads only: a server refuses them on a write, so a session leaves them off an INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE by sql , judging a statement by its first word. A Database in the process ignores them: it reads every write it committed."],
["javascript.html#a-search-box-in-a-web-app","ChloeDB from JavaScript","A search box in a web app","A search box sends what was typed to your app's own server code, which queries ChloeDB and answers the page. A page cannot query chloedb-server itself unless chloedb-server serves the page's origin."],
["javascript.html#why-a-page-cannot-call-chloedb-server","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Why a page cannot call chloedb-server","chloedb-server refuses every request but GET and HEAD that carries an Origin other than the server's own, reads included: a POST /v1/query with a SELECT from a page on http://localhost:3000 to a server on port 7700 gets 403, \"a page from http://localhost:3000 cannot change this server\". Its error_code , scope_refused , and its hint to ask for a key with another scope do not apply: no key lets such a request through. The server sends no CORS headers and answers a preflight OPTIONS with 405, so a browser does not even send the client's POST (it sends JSON, which needs the preflight), and the call rejects with fetch 's network error, which the browser's console shows as a CORS error. There is no search-only key to put in a page, as Algolia has one. So the page calls a route of your app, and the route queries ChloeDB: over HTTP from the server side, where fetch sends no Origin , or in the process, with the database open there. A page calls chloedb-server directly only from the server's own origin: a page it serves, or a page behind a reverse proxy that serves your app and ChloeDB under one host name, passes the Host header on, and starts chloedb-server with --allow-host and that name ( security )."],
["javascript.html#search-as-you-type","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Search as you type","search() ranks by bm25 of its text as written, so a word half typed ( so ) finds nothing; so* and lether~ are a prefix and a word with its typos there, as in a query, where ORDER BY bm25(name, 'sof*') ranks the rows MATCH(name, 'sof*') keeps ( weights, required and excluded words ; changed: bm25 read sof* as the word sof before, and returned no rows). A search box writes its own query: it filters with MATCH by what was typed, its last word a prefix while it is being typed, and ranks by bm25 of the words typed whole, or by a column while the first word is still being typed. fuzzy => 'auto' takes typos in the words ( lether ). These are the queries for a table products ( id , a TEXT name , a KEYWORD brand , price , rating and in_stock ), shared by the two routes below: Typed lether so , the hits are filtered by MATCH(name, 'lether so*', fuzzy => 'auto') and ranked by bm25(name, 'lether', fuzzy => 'auto') ; typed so , they are filtered by MATCH(name, 'so*', fuzzy => 'auto') and ranked by rating."],
["javascript.html#a-route-over-http","ChloeDB from JavaScript","A route over HTTP","A Next.js route handler (the App Router's app/api/search/route.ts ) answers the hits, a facet of their brands, each hit's name with the words that matched marked ( highlight , the server's POST /v1/highlight ) and a \"did you mean\" ( correct , POST /v1/correct ). The client needs nothing but fetch , so it runs on Node and on the Edge runtime alike: GET /api/search?q=lether%20so answers: marked is HTML-escaped around its <mark> tags, so the page can set it as HTML (React's dangerouslySetInnerHTML ); every other value is plain text, for the page to escape as usual. correct changes a word only when no row holds it and a neighbour one edit away is in 5 rows or more, so a small table gets few corrections. A deep page of hits comes from a cursor ( pageSize , then next(cursor) ), as OFFSET past 10,000 rows is refused."],
["javascript.html#the-same-in-the-process","ChloeDB from JavaScript","The same in the process","With the database in the app's process, there is no server to run, and the route is the same: a Database has highlight and correct too, and answers them as the server does. Open the file once and share the Database : Next.js bundles server code, and its bundler cannot take the addon ( next build stops with \"chloedb.node (node addon) is not placeable in ESM chunks\"), so leave the package out of the bundle: The route runs on Node, Next.js's default for route handlers: on the Edge runtime ( export const runtime = \"edge\" ) \"chloedb\" is the client alone, which has no open . One process may open the file to write it. When another process writes it (chloedb-server, an import job), or the app runs as several processes, open it with { readOnly: true } in the app: each query sees the writer's commits so far."],
["javascript.html#what-a-route-sends","ChloeDB from JavaScript","What a route sends","Response.json(result) sends the whole Result : columns , the rows as arrays, keys , scores and stats , whose plan lists what the engine did ( \"seg 0: bm25 MaxScore, filter Probe\" ). Send result.objects() , the rows as objects, or what you make of them, as the routes above do. An INT past 2^53 comes back as a BigInt , which JSON.stringify and Response.json refuse (\"Do not know how to serialize a BigInt\"); write it as text first, JSON.stringify(rows, (k, v) => (typeof v === \"bigint\" ? String(v) : v)) ."],
["javascript.html#typescript","ChloeDB from JavaScript","TypeScript","query() answers a Result whose rows are Record<string, Value> ; query<Hit>() (and sql<Hit>() , next<Hit>() , scroll<Hit>() , get<Hit>() , getMany<Hit>() ) types its rows as Hit , a type or an interface . Nothing checks the type against the query's columns: it says what the query answers. sql() answers Result | number , a SELECT's rows or the rows a write wrote; execute() answers a number. A schema's field types are checked ( type: \"keywrd\" is an error). The declarations type-check as they are, with skipLibCheck off, any target from ES2020 and no Node types: they name TypeScript's esnext.disposable library themselves, for Symbol.asyncDispose ( await using db = await open(...) ), so code for a page or a worker needs neither \"types\": [\"node\"] nor \"lib\": [..., \"ESNext.Disposable\"] . ( changed: before, with TypeScript 7, which includes no @types package that types does not name, tsc stopped with \"Property 'asyncDispose' does not exist on type 'SymbolConstructor'\", a row type was a cast, as Result<Hit> , and an interface was refused for one.)"],
["javascript.html#search-on-the-device","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Search on the device","openNearby(url, table, dir) holds a slice of a chloedb-server's table near a user: the rows of the tiles around them in a database in dir , searched with the server's statistics. moveTo(lat, lon) fetches the tiles it lacks, and search(q, { k, d0 }) answers on the device when it can prove the answer is the server's, and asks the server otherwise; the result's where is \"device\" or \"server\" and why the reason. For a vector slice , focus(taste, { rows }) holds the clusters nearest a taste vector and similar(vector, { k }) answers a similarity search the same way, fetching the few clusters its codes name when that proves the answer."],
["javascript.html#bun-and-deno","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Bun and Deno","Bun loads the addon as Node does: bun run app.js . Deno loads it through its Node compatibility with --allow-ffi (and --allow-read , --allow-write , --allow-env for the files and $CHLOEDB_MODELS ); the client needs only --allow-net ."],
["javascript.html#tests","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Tests",""],
["javascript.html#your-apps-tests","ChloeDB from JavaScript","Your app's tests","A test opens a database of its own. open(\":memory:\", { schema }) (or Database.create(\":memory:\", schema) ) is a new, empty one each time, in a directory of its own under the system's temporary directory that close() removes (and the process's exit, for one left open); open(\":memory:\") without a schema, or Database.open(\":memory:\") , is refused, as there is nothing to open. ( changed: open(\":memory:\") made a file named :memory: in the working directory before, which the next run opened again.) A test of a file opens it in a directory of its own ( mkdtemp ) and removes the directory after: a database in a file is the file and two entries beside it, <name>-wal and the directory <name>-readers . A test against chloedb-server starts a server of its own, on other ports than the app's server: --listen moves only the HTTP port, and a second server left on gRPC's 7701 stops with \"the address is in use\", so give it --grpc another port too, or --grpc off :"],
["javascript.html#the-packages-tests","ChloeDB from JavaScript","The package's tests","The client's tests start chloedb-server/target/release/chloedb-server (or $CHLOEDB_SERVER ) and are skipped when it is not built. test/types.test.mjs type-checks test/types/example.ts , a typed app, with TypeScript 7.0.2 (by npx , or $CHLOEDB_TSC ) and no Node types, and is skipped only when npx cannot fetch it."],
["rust.html","ChloeDB from Rust","","The chloedb crate is ChloeDB itself: the Python, JavaScript and Java packages and chloedb-server are built on it. A Rust program that depends on it opens a database file in its own process, with no server, and reads and writes it through SQL or through typed write operations."],
["rust.html#add-it","ChloeDB from Rust","Add it","The crate is not on crates.io yet. The chloedb there is a placeholder that only holds the name (it still says Strata, ChloeDB's name before November 2026). Depend on the repository instead, at a release's tag: Cargo finds the crate in the repository's chloedb/ folder. Building it needs Rust (stable) and a C compiler ( xcode-select --install on macOS, build-essential on Debian and Ubuntu), because the tokenizer's regex library is C. The first build compiles ChloeDB and its dependencies, which takes several minutes. The default features are embed (embedding models run in the process) and hosted (embedding models behind an HTTP API); sqlite , parquet , s3 , bert and onnx add SQLite and Parquet import and export, s3:// URLs, and more model kinds ( chloedb/Cargo.toml describes each one). cargo doc -p chloedb --open shows the API of the version you depend on. The examples below use these imports:"],
["rust.html#open-or-create-a-database","ChloeDB from Rust","Open or create a database","Db::open opens a database that exists. On a path where there is none, it fails with I/O error: No such file or directory (os error 2) , which does not name the path, so create the database first. Db::create_empty makes a database with no tables; SQL then creates them: CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS leaves a table that is there as it is, so the program can run again on the same file. A database can hold many tables, and every statement names the tables it reads or writes ( the query language has the column types). Db::create(path, schema, options) makes a database whose first table is given as a Schema instead, for example from JSON: Call db.close() before the program lets go of the database. It writes the rows still in memory into the file, so that the file alone holds the database (a copy of it is then a backup), and gives back to the file system the space that merges freed. The database still answers after it. A program that keeps writing for a long time also merges its segments in the background, as the Python and JavaScript packages do when they open a database: db.spawn_merger(MergerOptions { threads: 1, ..MergerOptions::default() }) , with chloedb::storage::MergerOptions . Without it, a table written to for a long time collects segments, and each query visits every one."],
["rust.html#write","ChloeDB from Rust","Write","SQL writes take parameters, named :name in the statement and set with Params::set : db.sql answers SqlResult::Written(n) for a write, with the number of rows it wrote. Each SQL statement is a transaction of its own; BEGIN and COMMIT are refused. To write several rows in one transaction, all or none, give db.write_ops a list of operations. A document is the row's values in the order of the table's columns: Insert fails when a row with the key is there, and Upsert replaces it. Set , Add and AddOrInsert change one numeric field of a row in place. Threads that call write_ops at the same time share commits. The storage layer has its own transaction, db.storage().begin() , for a program that reads its own writes before it commits. Name the table there with tx.table(\"notes\") : WriteTxn::insert and WriteTxn::upsert write the database's first table, which in a database made by Db::create_empty is a hidden placeholder, so a row for notes fails with schema error: document has 4 values, table has 1 fields ."],
["rust.html#search-and-query","ChloeDB from Rust","Search and query","db.sql answers a SELECT with SqlResult::Rows , and db.query with the rows alone. MATCH filters by words, bm25 ranks by them, and similarity ranks by a vector field: A result has its columns and its rows , and each value is a Value ( Null , Int , Float , Str , Vector , List , Bool , Timestamp ), read with as_str , as_i64 and as_f64 . The query language has every statement and function: full text , scores and their fusion with rrf , filters, grouping and joins. The examples give vectors as values. A field that ChloeDB embeds itself ( VECTOR(...) OPTIONS(embed = 'body', model = ...) ) needs its model loaded first, with db.open_embedders(&models_dir) , as the Python package does when it opens a database."],
["rust.html#errors","ChloeDB from Rust","Errors","Every call returns chloedb::Result<T> , whose error is chloedb::Error (it implements std::error::Error , so ? also works in a function that returns Result<T, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> ). Its kind says what to do: variant when Error::Io the file system failed, or the path does not exist ( Db::open ) Error::Schema a document or schema does not fit the table; a key the table holds already starts with duplicate primary key Error::Query a statement failed to parse, bind or plan Error::Busy another process, or another open in this one, has the database open for writing ( the database is open elsewhere: ... , which goes on to say which since 0.3.8 (unreleased) ), or another write held it for longer than DbOptions::write_wait (5 seconds) Error::Timeout , Error::Memory a query ran past its deadline or its memory budget Error::Corrupt the file failed a check Error::Unavailable a hosted embedding model did not answer One process writes a database at a time. Other processes on the same machine can read it while it does, opened with DbOptions { read_only: true, ..DbOptions::default() } ; their writes fail. For many processes, or other machines, run chloedb-server over the file instead."],
["rust.html#where-the-examples-come-from","ChloeDB from Rust","Where the examples come from","The calls on this page are the ones ChloeDB's own tests make, which CI compiles and runs: chloedb/tests/empty_database.rs (a database with no tables, then CREATE TABLE ), tests/sql_tables.rs (SQL over several tables), tests/many_writers.rs ( write_ops and Error::Busy ), tests/analyzer_languages.rs ( MATCH and bm25 with parameters), tests/alter_table.rs (vectors as parameters and similarity ) and tests/cold_tier.rs ( storage().begin() and tx.table ). They are the next place to look for how a call is used."],
["java.html","ChloeDB from Java","","The ai.chloetech.chloedb package runs a ChloeDB database inside a Java process, the way sqlite-jdbc runs SQLite: the database is a file the JVM opens, read and written by ChloeDB's native library over JNI, with no server and nothing to configure. It has a JDBC driver too ( jdbc:chloedb:books.chloedb ), for code and tools that speak JDBC. It works on Java 11 and later. Before ChloeDB was renamed, the package was ai.chloetech.strata and its jar ai.chloetech:strata : change the imports and the dependency when you rebuild ( StrataException is ChloeDBException , and the driver's Strata* classes are ChloeDB* ). URLs that begin jdbc:strata: still open a database."],
["java.html#build-it","ChloeDB from Java","Build it","The package is in chloedb-java/ : Java classes ( src/main/java ) and the Rust crate of their native library ( src/*.rs ). Building it needs Rust (stable), a C compiler, a JDK (11 or later) and Maven. The jar ( target/java/chloedb-0.3.7.jar ) carries the native library for the platform it was built on (Linux or macOS, x86-64 or ARM), and needs nothing else at run time: no dependency, no server. A project uses it from the local repository: or, with Gradle, implementation(\"ai.chloetech:chloedb:0.3.7\") with mavenLocal() among the repositories. -Dcargo.profile=ci builds the library without link-time optimisation, much faster, for a build that only runs the tests. -Dcargo.features=bert (comma-separated) turns on Cargo features as the Python package has them: bert for BERT embedding models ( accelerate on a Mac), onnx , vision , audio . The library already includes DataFusion, Parquet and s3:// URLs. -Dcargo.skip=true packages the library cargo built before."],
["java.html#a-database-in-your-process","ChloeDB from Java","A database in your process","Database.create takes the schema as a map (the short form or the long one), JSON text, or the path of a JSON file. Database.open opens a database the file already holds. Both take new Database.Options() : .directory(true) keeps a new database as a directory of segment files, .models(dir) says where embedding models are, .threads(n) how many cores queries and index builds use (the machine's by default). Only one process may have a database open to write it at a time; another gets a ChloeDBException saying the database is busy. Other processes can open it with Database.open(path, new Database.Options().readOnly(true)) to read it while it is written: each query sees the writer's commits so far, every write fails, and refresh() looks for new commits at once ( how readers follow the writer ). For writes from several processes, serve it with chloedb-server . Within the process, one Database is safe to use from many threads at once: queries run in parallel, writes made at once wait for each other and share commits (one disk flush for all those waiting, none failing as busy), and a call into the library does not hold the JVM. Segments merge in the background as writes add them, one merge at a time. Close the database when done, with close() or try-with-resources, which also merges small segments and gives the file system back the space merges freed when it is an eighth of the file or more; one left open is closed when the garbage collector finds it."],
["java.html#rows-and-values","ChloeDB from Java","Rows and values","A row is a map of field name to value, and a query's answer gives values back as Java types: ChloeDB written as read as KEYWORD, TEXT String String INT Integer , Long (any integer type, BigInteger within 64 bits) Long FLOAT Double , Float , BigDecimal Double BOOL Boolean Boolean TIMESTAMP Instant , OffsetDateTime , ZonedDateTime , Date ; LocalDateTime and LocalDate as UTC; ISO 8601 text Instant VECTOR float[] (kept as float32), double[] , a List of numbers float[] a multi-valued field a List a List SPARSE a map of term to weight a String of JSON JSON a Map , or JSON text its JSON text null is NULL. Maps keep their order where it matters ( LinkedHashMap ); Map.of takes no null, so a row with a NULL field is a HashMap or a LinkedHashMap . A value of another type throws IllegalArgumentException before anything is written. insert writes rows whose keys must be new, all or none: 10,000 rows or more are bulk-loaded into new segments in one commit, fewer go through the write-ahead log. upsert inserts or replaces rows by key, delete takes a key or a collection of them, and get and getMany read rows by key without SQL ( null for a key no row has). write takes the operations chloedb-server's /v1/write takes ( insert , upsert , delete , set of a field, a number in place and another by writing the row again, and add to a number in place) and applies them in one transaction."],
["java.html#queries","ChloeDB from Java","Queries","query runs a SELECT (or an EXPLAIN) and gives a Result ; execute runs any other statement (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, CREATE TABLE, ALTER TABLE) and gives the rows it wrote. Parameters ( :name in the SQL) are a map: A Result has columns() , rows() (lists in column order), keys() (each row's primary key), scores() (what each row was ranked by) and stats() (what the engine did, as JSON text). Iterating it gives each row as a map of column to value; row(i) , column(name) and scalar() (the first value of the first row: a count) read parts of it. plan(sql) gives a statement's plan as a map without running it. search(text) gives the rows that best match a text, as the Python package's search does: BM25 over the text fields, the similarity of an embedded vector field to the text's embedding, or both fused by reciprocal rank, which it picks when the table has an embedded field whose model is open. queryLogged and exportRankLog keep and export a rank log for training a ranking, with the options chloedb-server takes for them."],
["java.html#limits-memory-deadlines-interrupting","ChloeDB from Java","Limits: memory, deadlines, interrupting","A query runs in your process, so what it builds up (its groups, the rows it orders and returns, a join's tables) is held by the JVM's process, outside its heap. The queries of every database a process opens share a memory budget, by default a quarter of the machine's memory (the cgroup's limit in a container, else RAM), or CHLOEDB_QUERY_MEMORY_MB MiB when that is set when the first database opens ( 0 : no budget). A query that would hold more throws a ChloeDBException whose message starts memory: , naming the budget and how to raise it, instead of the process running out of memory. A query has no deadline unless it is given one. query(sql, params, options) , execute(sql, params, options) and SearchOptions.limits(options) take a QueryOptions : timeoutMs (past it the query stops and throws a ChloeDBException whose message starts timeout: , naming it) and memory (a budget of its own in bytes; 0 : none). A database's own limits, for every query that does not give its own, come with Database.Options or setLimits ; interrupt() , from any thread, stops the queries of the database running now and returns how many there were: A query checks its deadline and interrupt between segments and every 1,024 rows it groups, orders, walks or joins, so it stops within milliseconds. A write is not cut short. Through JDBC, Statement.setQueryTimeout(seconds) is each query's deadline (past it, a SQLTimeoutException , state HYT00 ) and Statement.cancel() , from another thread, stops the statement's query (a SQLException , state HY008 )."],
["java.html#tables","ChloeDB from Java","Tables","The methods that act on a table ( insert , upsert , delete , get , getMany , search , schema , importFile , addColumn , dropColumn , renameColumn , embed ) act on the database's first table. db.table(\"events\") gives another, with the same methods: tables() gives every table's schema. addColumn(\"price\", \"float\", Map.of(\"default\", 1.5)) , dropColumn and renameColumn change a table's columns, as ALTER TABLE does."],
["java.html#files","ChloeDB from Java","Files","Their options are maps, with the names the SQLite and Parquet pages give ( tables , schemas , blobsBase64 , fts , indexes , table , schema , key ). An option a method does not take throws IllegalArgumentException naming those it takes, before anything is read or written. Database.planSqlite and Database.planParquet say what an import would do without doing it, and lastImport() what the import that made a database did."],
["java.html#jdbc","ChloeDB from Java","JDBC","The jar is also a JDBC driver: jdbc:chloedb:PATH (or jdbc:strata:PATH , by the old name) opens the database at PATH in the process, as jdbc:sqlite:PATH does a SQLite file. DriverManager finds it by itself (the jar lists it under META-INF/services ). Options follow a ? in the URL or come as connection properties: schema (the path of a schema's JSON file, with which a database that does not exist yet is created: jdbc:chloedb:books.chloedb?schema=books.json ), models , threads , and readonly=true , which refuses statements that write. A URL may also name the path as jdbc:chloedb:file:books.chloedb . Statements are ChloeDB's SQL ( the query language ), with ? parameters numbered from 1. setObject takes the values the rest of the package takes: a float[] is a vector ( ORDER BY similarity(v, ?) ), a List a list. Batches ( addBatch , executeBatch ) run one statement after another; one that fails stops the batch with a BatchUpdateException that counts the ones before it, which stay written. Results come back whole, so a result set can scroll if the statement is made with ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_INSENSITIVE ; result sets are read-only. getObject gives an INT as Long , a FLOAT as Double , a TIMESTAMP as java.sql.Timestamp , a vector as float[] and a list as List ; the getters convert ( getInt , getString , getObject(i, Instant.class) , getArray for vectors). Times are in UTC. Transactions : each statement commits as it runs, as it does through the rest of the package. Auto-commit is always on: setAutoCommit(false) throws SQLFeatureNotSupportedException rather than pretending a rollback could undo anything, and commit() and rollback() throw too. A tool or framework that needs transactions across statements cannot use ChloeDB through JDBC yet. Connections share the database. A process may have a database open once, so the connections to one file (a pool's, say) share one Database , which the last of them to close closes. connection.unwrap(Database.class) gives it, for what SQL does not do: search , importParquet , embed . Metadata ( getMetaData() ) lists tables, columns with their types (KEYWORD and TEXT as VARCHAR , INT as BIGINT , FLOAT as DOUBLE , BOOL, TIMESTAMP, vectors and lists as ARRAY ), keys, references and the SQL types: what database browsers ask a driver. Tables have no catalog or schema, and table and column patterns match regardless of case, as ChloeDB's SQL names do. Errors are SQLException s: a statement ChloeDB cannot parse or plan a SQLSyntaxErrorException , a query past setQueryTimeout a SQLTimeoutException and one stopped by cancel() state HY008 ( limits ), what the driver does not do a SQLFeatureNotSupportedException ."],
["java.html#errors","ChloeDB from Java","Errors","The database's errors (a bad schema, query, value or file, a database open elsewhere) are ChloeDBException s, with the message the HTTP API and the Python package give. An argument of the wrong kind is an IllegalArgumentException , and a call on a closed database an IllegalStateException ."],
["java.html#how-it-works","ChloeDB from Java","How it works","The JNI boundary is two functions: call(handle, method, args) runs one of the library's methods with its arguments as one value and gives its answer as another, both in a small tagged binary form ( codec.rs , and Codec.java on the Java side), and free(handle) drops a database once Java no longer holds it. So no JNI call is made per value, and what a method does lives in one place ( calls.rs ), as the Python package does it: rows, schemas and options are read by the code that reads files and the HTTP API. JNI rather than the Foreign Function and Memory API (Panama) because Panama is final only from Java 22, and JNI runs on every Java from 11. The library is loaded from the path in the system property chloedb.library (or CHLOEDB_JNI_LIBRARY ; the old names strata.library and STRATA_JNI_LIBRARY work too) if set, else from the jar, unpacked to a temporary file that is removed once loaded, else from java.library.path . A jar and a library built from different checkouts refuse each other. Java 22 and later warn when a library is loaded without --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED (the jar's manifest says so for an executable jar); pass it to the JVM. The Python package's query builder ( chloedb.Query ), Arrow answers, client of chloedb-server and training helpers have no Java counterpart yet: queries are SQL."],
["frameworks.html","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","","The Python package has adapters for the frameworks agents and retrieval pipelines are built with: a LangChain vector store, a LangGraph checkpointer and long-term memory store, a LlamaIndex vector store, and a DB-API driver for pandas and anything else that takes a Python database connection. Each one takes a database's path or a chloedb-server's URL, so the code below runs unchanged on a file and on a cluster, and one file holds an agent's documents, checkpoints and memories side by side, each in a table of its own. This page builds a small support assistant's storage with all four, in one file, support.chloedb . Nothing on it needs an API key: ChloeDB embeds the texts itself with a model it runs in the process. The full list of arguments, methods and filter operators is in the reference ."],
["frameworks.html#install","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","Install","Each extra pulls in its framework's core package only ( langchain-core , langgraph , llama-index-core ). The examples embed with potion-retrieval-32M , a 129 MB static model that runs on a CPU in a few microseconds a text; fetch it once:"],
["frameworks.html#langchain-a-vector-store-and-a-retriever","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","LangChain: a vector store and a retriever","ChloeDBVectorStore keeps each document as a row with its text (indexed for BM25), its metadata (a JSON field, so a filter on any key runs from an index) and its vector. Given model= instead of a LangChain Embeddings , ChloeDB embeds the documents as they are written and each query's text as it runs. A question in other words than the article's finds it by its vector: An exact code is what vectors miss and words find. mode=\"hybrid\" ranks by both, fused by reciprocal rank, and filter keeps only the documents whose metadata matches, written as MongoDB's filters (the operators LangChain's other stores take): A filter's operators are checked, its keys and values are not: a key no document has ( {\"topik\": \"uploads\"} ), or a value of another type than the key holds ( {\"updated\": \"2026\"} , where updated is a number), keeps no document and raises nothing, so an empty answer can be a filter's typo. An unknown operator is refused with the list of those it takes ( unknown filter operator '$gtee': use $eq, $ne, $gt, ... ). as_retriever() is the same store as LangChain's Retriever , to put in a chain:"],
["frameworks.html#langgraph-a-checkpointer-and-long-term-memory","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","LangGraph: a checkpointer and long-term memory","ChloeDBSaver keeps a graph's checkpoints, so a conversation goes on after a restart or from another process, and ChloeDBStore is LangGraph's long-term memory: items by namespace and key, searched by meaning or filtered by value. This graph has no LLM in it, so it runs anywhere: one node remembers what the user says about themselves, the next recalls the memories closest to the message. Adapters given the same path in one process share one open database. A database file is open in one process at a time, though, and another process that opens it while this one has it is refused as busy: processes that work on one database at once go through a server . Each run below is a new process. The memories outlive the conversation they were said in, and the conversation's state outlives the process: The checkpointer kept every step of thread t1 , so the graph can show its state as it is, or as it was at any step (LangGraph's time travel):"],
["frameworks.html#llamaindex-an-index-over-the-same-articles","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","LlamaIndex: an index over the same articles","chloedb.llama_index.ChloeDBVectorStore keeps LlamaIndex's nodes. With model= , store.embed_model is ChloeDB's model as LlamaIndex's, so the index needs no other embedding model and no API key: LlamaIndex keeps a node's document under the metadata keys doc_id , document_id and ref_doc_id , so a document's own doc_id in its metadata is replaced by LlamaIndex's id: it does not come back, and no filter finds it. Give yours another name, such as kb_id . as_query_engine(vector_store_query_mode=\"hybrid\") ranks by words and vectors together, as the LangChain store's mode=\"hybrid\" does; a query engine needs an LLM, which LlamaIndex takes from Settings.llm ."],
["frameworks.html#pandas-and-db-api-sql-over-all-of-it","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","pandas and DB-API: SQL over all of it","Everything above went into tables of one file, and chloedb.dbapi opens it as a PEP 249 connection, so pandas and any tool written for sqlite3 reads it with SQL, ChloeDB's search functions included: pandas warns about every DB-API connection but sqlite3 's, as it has not tested them; read_sql reads this one as it reads SQLite's, and the filter above only quiets the warning. The tables the adapters made are ordinary ChloeDB tables: each has a key id , the text, the metadata as JSON and the vector. Search with SQL shows what else a query can do with them."],
["frameworks.html#on-a-server-or-a-cluster","LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and pandas","On a server or a cluster","Every adapter takes a chloedb-server's or a coordinator's URL where a path goes, with the server's token as token= or in $CHLOEDB_TOKEN : Each adapter reads through a session of its own, so its reads see its own writes from any copy: start the servers with --seq-no , and a LangGraph thread resumed a moment after its last step reads the checkpoint that step wrote, also through a coordinator, where the checkpoints of a thread sit on one shard. A DB-API read through a server is fetched a page at a time, past the server's result window. Run a server says how to start one, and the server's vector fields embed with the same models."],
["frameworks-reference.html","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","","The Python package plugs ChloeDB into the frameworks agents and retrieval pipelines are built with, and into anything that takes a Python DB-API connection: module what it is install chloedb.langchain ChloeDBVectorStore , a LangChain vector store (and, through as_retriever() , a retriever) pip install \"chloedb[langchain]\" chloedb.langgraph ChloeDBSaver , a LangGraph checkpointer, and ChloeDBStore , a LangGraph store for long-term memory pip install \"chloedb[langgraph]\" chloedb.llama_index ChloeDBVectorStore , a LlamaIndex vector store, and ChloeDBEmbedding pip install \"chloedb[llama-index]\" chloedb.dbapi a PEP 249 driver: connect() , cursors, pandas.read_sql nothing more Each of them takes where the data is in the same way: a database's path (opened in the process, and made at the first write when it does not exist), the http:// or https:// URL of a chloedb-server or a coordinator (with token= , or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN ), or a chloedb.Database or chloedb.Client already open. Adapters given the same path in one process share one open database (a process opens a database once to write it), so a checkpointer and a store can both name \"agent.chloedb\" ; a database opened by chloedb.open is passed as itself. A DB-API connection to that path shares it too, and closing the connection closes the database only when no adapter or other connection holds it. Through a server, each adapter reads with a Session of its own, so that its reads see its own writes whichever copy answers: a LangGraph thread resumed a moment after its last step reads the checkpoint that step wrote, also through a coordinator and from a replica. Start the servers with --seq-no for that ( reads that see a write ); without it, a write answers no number and the reads are a plain client's. A chloedb.Client given to an adapter is used through its session too. The same code runs on a file on a laptop and on a cluster, and one database can hold an agent's vectors, checkpoints, memories and its other tables side by side, each in a table of its own."],
["frameworks-reference.html#langchain","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","LangChain","ChloeDBVectorStore keeps each document as a row: its id, its text (a TEXT field, indexed for BM25), its metadata (a JSON field , so a filter on any key runs from the field's index) and its vector. A search ranks by vector, by words, or by both fused by reciprocal rank , so one store answers a question asked in other words and a query of exact names, codes and error messages, which vectors miss. Without an embedding model, ChloeDB embeds the texts itself, when they are written, and each query's text, with a model of a vector field : potion-retrieval-32M by default, any model a field takes otherwise (a hosted one too, such as openai/text-embedding-3-small ). Nothing then embeds in Python: argument default what it does db \"langchain.chloedb\" where: a path, a server's URL, a Database or a Client table \"langchain\" the table, made when missing embedding None a LangChain Embeddings ; None for ChloeDB to embed with model model \"potion-retrieval-32M\" when embedding is None the model ChloeDB embeds with dim the first vector's length the vectors' dimensions, to make the table before the first write distance \"cosine\" \"cosine\" makes vectors unit length, so the score is the cosine similarity; \"dot\" keeps them as the model writes them mode \"vector\" how a search ranks unless it says: \"vector\" , \"text\" (BM25) or \"hybrid\" The store has LangChain's methods: add_texts , add_documents (a document's id is kept), get_by_ids , delete(ids) or delete(filter=...) , similarity_search , similarity_search_with_score (higher is closer: the cosine similarity, the BM25 score or the fused score), similarity_search_with_relevance_scores (by vector the cosine similarity, negative ones as 0, which is what Chroma, FAISS and PGVector report for cosine; hybrid, the fused score over the best it can be, a document first in both rankings, so 1; by words it raises NotImplementedError , since BM25 has no scale from 0 to 1; a search's own mode= decides, else the store's), similarity_search_by_vector , max_marginal_relevance_search , from_texts , as_retriever and the async forms of each. A text written again under its id replaces the one before."],
["frameworks-reference.html#filters","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","Filters","Filters are written as MongoDB's, as LangChain's other stores take them, and each runs as a condition on the metadata's JSON field, from its index: filter keeps the documents whose metadata {\"topic\": \"db\"} has topic equal to \"db\" (a list holds the value when one of its values is it) {\"author\": {\"name\": \"Ann\"}} has name equal to \"Ann\" in the object at author {\"year\": {\"$gte\": 2020, \"$lt\": 2026}} compares: $eq , $ne , $gt , $gte , $lt , $lte , $between ( [lo, hi] ) {\"topic\": {\"$in\": [\"db\", \"ml\"]}} is one of the values; $nin none of them {\"score\": {\"$exists\": True}} has a value there ( False : has none) {\"name\": {\"$like\": \"chl%\"}} starts with the text before % {\"tags\": {\"$contains\": \"x\"}} holds the value in a list {\"$or\": [{...}, {...}]} , {\"$and\": [...]} , {\"$not\": {...}} combines filters $ne and $nin keep documents without the key, as MongoDB's do. A key is one level whatever it holds ( {\"a.b\": 1} is the key a.b , not b inside a ). Metadata is kept as JSON: a value that JSON cannot hold is kept as its text, and an object deeper than 16 levels, with more than 1,000 values or with a path longer than 256 bytes is refused, as a JSON field refuses it."],
["frameworks-reference.html#langgraph","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","LangGraph",""],
["frameworks-reference.html#the-checkpointer","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","The checkpointer","ChloeDBSaver keeps a graph's checkpoints, and the writes pending on each, as rows of two tables ( langgraph_checkpoints and langgraph_writes , made when missing, placed and clustered by thread_id , so that on a cluster a thread's rows sit on one shard, a thread is read from it alone, and a task's writes go in one transaction; tables made by 0.3.0 or earlier keep the placement by key they were made with), so that a thread goes on after a restart, from another process, or, through a server, from another machine. It has the methods LangGraph calls, synchronous and async alike (the async ones run the same calls on a worker thread): get_tuple , list (with filter on the metadata, before and limit ), put , put_writes , delete_thread and copy_thread . Interrupts, resumes ( Command(resume=...) ), forks from an old checkpoint and subgraphs work as with LangGraph's own savers. Checkpoints and writes are written by the graph's serializer (LangGraph's JsonPlusSerializer unless serde= says otherwise), as base64 text in fields that are stored and not indexed; the metadata is also kept in a JSON field, so that list(filter={\"source\": \"input\"}) runs from its index. Metadata past what a JSON field takes (16 levels, 1,000 values, paths of 256 bytes) is indexed in part: its top-level values first, then each object or list that fits whole; a filter on a part left out does not find the checkpoint."],
["frameworks-reference.html#the-store","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","The store","ChloeDBStore is LangGraph's BaseStore , the long-term memory a graph's nodes read and write through store= : items by namespace and key, in one table ( langgraph_store ). Filters on items' values run from a JSON field's index, with the operators above (LangGraph's own are $eq , $ne , $gt , $gte , $lt and $lte ). A value past what the field takes is kept whole and indexed in part, as a checkpoint's metadata is: an item holding a long transcript is still found by its kind . Search by meaning : index={\"dims\": 1536, \"embed\": OpenAIEmbeddings(), \"fields\": [\"text\"]} (or \"embed\": \"openai:text-embedding-3-small\" ), as LangGraph's stores take it, embeds in Python; index={\"model\": \"potion-retrieval-32M\", \"fields\": [...]} has ChloeDB embed each item's text as it is written, and each query's. fields are the paths of the text in a value ( [\"$\"] , the whole value, by default), and put(..., index=False) or index=[paths] overrides them for one item. An item's texts are embedded together, as one text. Time to live : ttl={\"default_ttl\": 60, \"refresh_on_read\": True} (minutes), or put(..., ttl=...) . The table expires each row at its time ( expire_by ), so an expired item is gone from every read at once, and a read moves an item's time on when refresh_on_read is on. Without a query , a search returns the newest items first. Namespaces : list_namespaces groups the items by namespace in the server; past the most groups a query may make ( --max-groups , 65,536), it reads the namespaces a page at a time in their order instead."],
["frameworks-reference.html#llamaindex","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","LlamaIndex","ChloeDBVectorStore keeps nodes as the LangChain store keeps documents, with each node's document ( ref_doc_id ) and the rest of the node besides, so that delete(ref_doc_id) deletes a document's nodes and a query returns the nodes as they were written. Queries rank in the modes default (by vector), text_search and sparse (by BM25) and hybrid (both, fused by reciprocal rank), among the nodes that MetadataFilters keep ( == , != , > , >= , < , <= , in , nin , any , all , contains , is_empty , with and , or and not ), doc_ids and node_ids . The store also has get_nodes , delete_nodes and clear . An empty list of ids names no node in get_nodes and delete_nodes , with filters or without, as in LlamaIndex's own stores, and delete_nodes with filters that hold no condition deletes nothing ( clear() deletes every node); in a query an empty list limits nothing, as LlamaIndex's retrievers pass it. With model=\"potion-retrieval-32M\" the table embeds the nodes that come without a vector, and every query's text; store.embed_model is that model as LlamaIndex's, so the index needs no other:"],
["frameworks-reference.html#db-api","ChloeDB in LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex and DB-API tools","DB-API","chloedb.dbapi is a PEP 249 driver, for code and tools written for any Python database: scripts written for sqlite3 , pandas.read_sql , notebooks and report tools. paramstyle is \"named\" : :name in the SQL, bound from a mapping, with ChloeDB's own parameters too (a vector, {\"embed\": text} ). A datetime or date binds as ISO 8601 text (a naive one as UTC), and a NaN as NULL, in a file and through a server alike. ChloeDB has no binary type: Binary() and a bytes parameter raise NotSupportedError (keep bytes as base64 text). A read ( SELECT , WITH , EXPLAIN ) keeps its rows for fetchone , fetchmany and fetchall . Through a server, a SELECT or WITH is read a page of 10,000 rows at a time as the rows are fetched (a scroll ), so it reads past the server's result window as a file does; rowcount is how many rows it answered once that is known (they came in one page, or were all fetched), -1 until then. A write sets rowcount to the rows it wrote. A ranked query without LIMIT answers its best 100 rows: the cursor then warns (a UserWarning ) and holds a Warning in cursor.messages . A TIMESTAMP column comes back as a datetime in UTC. ChloeDB commits each statement as it runs: commit() has nothing to do, and rollback() raises NotSupportedError . with conn: commits at its end and leaves the connection open, as sqlite3 and psycopg do; contextlib.closing(conn) closes it. description 's type codes are the columns' ChloeDB types ( \"text\" , \"keyword\" , \"int\" , \"float\" , \"bool\" , \"timestamp\" , ...), which compare equal to the module's STRING , NUMBER and DATETIME . A connection reads the tables' types once, and again after a CREATE , ALTER or DROP it ran. Errors are the module's PEP 249 classes ( ProgrammingError for a bad statement, OperationalError for a busy, overloaded or unreachable database, IntegrityError for a conflict), each with the ChloeDB error's code and the original error as its __cause__ . connect(path, read_only=True) opens a database another process writes, to read it."],
["server.html","chloedb-server","","chloedb-server serves a ChloeDB database over HTTP JSON and gRPC. Every server has a command center in the browser, with a query console and an agent that answers questions by writing SQL. Run several servers as shards behind one more as a coordinator, and the coordinator answers like one database. New to it? Build an app goes from an empty server to search, stock, holds, events counted once and a cluster in one page. This page is the reference: part sections Start it Run it (every flag), Schemas , Loading rows Models and media Embedded vector fields , Media fields , Semantic IDs The API HTTP API , Security , gRPC A cluster Sharding , Replication , On Kubernetes Running it Deadlines, admission and metrics : limits, health, logs, alerts The command center The UI , The agent , The on-call agent , Agents over MCP Old names. ChloeDB was called Strata until November 2026. The names on this page are the new ones, and the old ones ( strata-server , STRATA_* settings, x-strata-* headers) still work for a release: Compatibility lists them."],
["server.html#run-it","chloedb-server","Run it","A prebuilt server needs no Rust, no C compiler and no first build of several minutes. Each server release (tagged chloedb-server-v<version> ) has one archive a platform: linux-x86_64 and linux-aarch64 (glibc 2.35 or later: Debian 12, Ubuntu 22.04 and newer), macos-arm64 and macos-x86_64 (macOS 11 or later), each with its .sha256 beside it. The archive holds chloedb-server , and strata-server , its old name, beside it; release 0.1.0, from before the rename, was published as strata-server-v0.1.0 . macOS keeps an archive a browser downloaded from running until xattr -d com.apple.quarantine chloedb-server-$p/chloedb-server ; curl sets no such mark. since 0.3.7 The same binary is on PyPI as chloedb-server , a wheel per platform, so pip, uv and pipx install it (or run it without installing it), with no archive to unpack: The wheels are tagged for what the binary needs: manylinux_2_34 (glibc 2.34 or later: RHEL 9, Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12 and newer) on Linux x86_64 and aarch64, macosx_11_0 on macOS, so pip takes one only where it runs; there is none for Windows or Alpine, and no source distribution. The program lands in the environment's bin ; python -m chloedb_server ARGS runs it where that is not on the PATH , and chloedb.serve() starts one from Python and returns a client of it ( pip install \"chloedb[server]\" installs the client and the server of the same release). The server-release workflow makes the wheels from the archives above, installs and runs each on its platform, and publishes them on the tag. From source (Rust stable and a C compiler; the first build takes several minutes): cargo build --release --no-default-features builds a server without DataFusion, Parquet and s3:// paths, from 321 crates rather than 412: joins, groups and sorts then run on ChloeDB's own operators ( CHLOEDB_ENGINE , below), and import and export take no Parquet files or s3:// paths. Or from the image every merge to main publishes ( linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 ). Beyond the container's own loopback the server needs a token, which the command center then asks for: The image has a user 10001 ( chloedb ) that owns its /data : run it as that user, not root, with --user 10001:10001 as above, and a new volume works. A volume or directory a root image wrote before needs to be given to that user once: docker run --rm -v chloedb:/data --entrypoint chown ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server:latest -R 10001:10001 /data . The image's own user stays root for now, so that an operator installed before 0.2.1, which runs new images on volumes a root image wrote and gives its pods no user, keeps working; the operator from 0.2.1 runs the servers, the coordinators and the pre-flight as user 10001 itself, and gives the shards' volumes to its group ( fsGroup ). The benchmark tools ( chloedb-server-bench , engine-compare ) are not in the image; docker build --target bench builds an image with them. A --db that does not exist is created: with --schema 's table, with --demo 's, or with neither as a database with no tables yet, which CREATE TABLE and the loads fill. Until its first table exists it lists none, and a write that names no table is refused; then that table is the one requests naming none go to, as a database's first table is. The tables of the apps beside a server do not count: their own state (Sheets' sheets_* and ChloeCtx's ctx_* , which they make before anyone else, and the app engine's sa_* ) never takes a request that names no table, and the data they fill (a crawl's crawl_* , network operations' netops_* ) takes them only while the database has no other table. (Such a database keeps an empty first table named _placeholder , which listings, exports and writes leave alone; an older version of ChloeDB opens the file and lists it.) --demo N creates a product catalogue of N rows (titles, descriptions, brands, categories, tags, prices, ratings, sales and a 64-dimension vector) to try things on. Its emb field is declared as the hash:64 model's embedding of the title and description ( below ): hashed bags of words, not a learned model, so \"similar\" means \"shares words\". flag default effect --db PATH the database to serve; created if missing, with --schema or --demo , else with no tables --schema FILE the schema of a new database: a JSON file, or the JSON itself (below) --directory off create the new database as a directory of segment files instead of one file --wal-drop-damaged off open a database whose write-ahead log was damaged after it was durable, dropping the transactions from the damage on (the log is copied to <log>.damaged first); without it the server refuses to start on one --seq-no off number the database's writes, for conditional writes and seq_no() ; it cannot be undone, and versions of ChloeDB from before it refuse the database --replicas URL,URL,… be the primary of this database: ship its writes to these replicas' gRPC addresses; needs --seq-no . With --replica : the shard's other copies (its primary and the other replicas), which it ships to once promoted, and the only ones a promotion asked for with a key may name --replica off be a replica : apply the writes a primary ships here, and refuse writes from clients; needs --seq-no and --grpc --promote off start as the primary of a new term, above every term the database knows, shipping to --replicas if any: needed to start a database that was a replica, or whose primary another replaced, as a primary ( promotion ) --write-acks A all with --replicas : the copies that hold a write before it is answered: all , majority or primary --failover-after-ms N 0 in a cluster: a replica that hears nothing from its primary for this long asks its shard's copies to elect it primary, and a primary that does not hear from a majority of its copies for most of it serves no reads (0: failover by hand only) --ship-log-mb N 64 with --replicas or --replica : the latest transactions kept in memory to ship; a replica further behind is sent a copy of the primary --scrub-every-s N 86400 on a database server: how often it reads every segment against its checksum while it serves, at --scrub-mib-s (64 MiB/s) at most, to find data damaged on disk (0: only when asked, POST /v1/admin/scrub ); the first scrub starts within ten minutes --merge-nice N 10 on Linux, the nice value (0 to 19) merges run at, so that a merge gets the cores reads and writes leave idle; 0 runs them at the priority of reads and writes --verify-every-s N 600 with --replicas : how often each replica's rows are compared with the primary's; a replica whose rows differ is sent a copy of the primary (0: only when asked, POST /v1/admin/verify ) --verify-checksums off check every segment against its checksum before serving (reads the whole database once); a damaged newest version is skipped for the one before it --shards URL,URL,… run as a coordinator over these shard servers' gRPC addresses --coordinator-listen ADDR with --db , --grpc and --shards : also serve a coordinator over --shards at this HTTP address, in this server's process, which calls this server's shard in-process ( a coordinator beside a shard ) --local-shard URL with --coordinator-listen : the URL of --shards that is this server's own gRPC address --peer-url URL $CHLOEDB_PEER_URL , else $CHLOEDB_POD_IP and the --listen port, else the --listen address on a coordinator: the address other coordinators reach its HTTP API at; it names itself so to its shards --contain FILE contain.json beside --alerts ; in memory without either on a coordinator: where its Contain rules and their removals are kept across a restart --slot-map FILE equal runs, epoch 1 with --shards : the slot map to route writes by, which the shards are given --shard-timeout-ms N 0 with --shards : give up on a shard after this many milliseconds (0: at the query's deadline) --partial off with --shards : answer without the shards that have no copy answering, flagged \"partial\" ; a request's own \"partial\" overrides it --listen ADDR 127.0.0.1:7700 HTTP: the API, /metrics and the UI --grpc ADDR 127.0.0.1:7701 gRPC ( off to disable) --opensearch-listen ADDR off also answer OpenSearch's REST API here, for applications written for OpenSearch or Elasticsearch; --opensearch-version V is the version it reports ( 2.11.0 ; 7.10.2 for clients of Elasticsearch 7) --token T $CHLOEDB_TOKEN require Authorization: Bearer T on the API and on gRPC; needed to listen beyond loopback ( security ) --no-token off listen beyond loopback without a token anyway --allow-host NAME also answer HTTP requests for this host name (repeatable) --tls-cert FILE , --tls-key FILE serve HTTP and gRPC over TLS with this PEM certificate chain and key --header-timeout-secs N 30 the longest a client may take to send a request's headers (or a TLS handshake, 10 s at most), and the longest an idle HTTP/1 connection stays open; then the server closes it --idle-timeout-secs N 300 the longest any connection, HTTP/2 and gRPC too, stays open with no request under way (an HTTP/2 client that answers the server's pings would otherwise hold its connection for ever); 0: no limit --body-timeout-secs N 30 the longest a request body may pause; one that has taken longer than this must also have averaged 1 KiB a second, so that a client that trickles a byte at a time is cut off; not on gRPC, whose streams of writes pause between batches; 0: no limit --write-timeout-secs N 60 the longest a write may wait for the client to read its answer before the server closes the connection; 0: no limit --max-connections N 0 connections each listener (HTTP, OpenSearch, gRPC) holds open at once; past it new ones wait to be accepted, but for 8 kept for /livez , /readyz and /v1/health (two at once per client address), which answer any other request 503 (0: three quarters of the process's open-file limit, over every listener together, which the server raises to its hard limit, up to 2^20, at start; connections ) --max-connections-per-client N 0 connections one client address may hold of a listener's; a connection past it is closed at once, before any request (the client sees a reset; the server logs it at info, a line a second at most, and counts it in chloedb_connections_closed_past_share_total ) (0: half of --max-connections ). Behind a proxy that keeps a few connections to the server the default does; one that opens a connection per client may need more --stop-grace-ms N 20000 once the server stops taking connections, how long the requests under way have to finish before it stops anyway (a follower's feed ends at once) --cluster-token T $CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN , else --token the token the servers of a cluster send each other (a coordinator its shards, a primary its replicas); give every server of the cluster the same one. With it, only it makes the calls between servers, and --token is an admin's ( security ) --shard-ca FILE a CA certificate (PEM) a coordinator trusts for https:// shards, and a primary for https:// replicas, besides the public roots --timeout-ms N 5000 default query deadline --max-timeout-ms N 60000 the longest deadline a request's timeout_ms may ask for --max-concurrent N 64 queries running at once; more wait for a slot until their deadline --admission-wait-ms N 0 how long a client's query waits for a slot before it is refused with 429 and Retry-After ( admission ); 0: until its deadline --max-result-window N 10000 the most rows a query may reach, OFFSET included ( limits ), and a scroll 's page; 0: no limit --max-scroll-rows N 1000000 the rows the open scrolls of ordered queries, groups and joins hold between them (a plain scan holds none); 0: only plain scans scroll --answer-cache-mb N 64 memory for answers kept between queries ( answer cache ); 0: none --max-groups N 65536 the most groups a GROUP BY may make ( limits ); 0: no limit --max-rerank N 20 the most rows a cross-encoder 's rerank phase may score, on a database or a coordinator, whether the model runs here or is served elsewhere; 0: no limit --query-memory-mb N a third of the cgroup memory limit, or of RAM memory the running queries may hold together ( limits ); 0: no limit --filter-cache-mb N 64 memory for filters kept between queries, per segment ( limits ); 0: none kept --max-queued-writes N 1024 write requests held at once; more are refused with 429 ( limits ); 0: no limit --model-calls-per-key-day N 10000 model calls an API key may make a day (UTC) with this server's own credentials: /v1/agent and the on-call agent a call a step, /v1/judge a call a row, llm() a call a question it asks (answers kept in memory are free); past it they answer 429 until the next day. The --token , the cluster's token and a caller that brings its own api_key are not counted; GET /v1/admin/model-budget shows today's counts by key id; 0: no limit --max-body-mb N 256 the largest body of a write or an import, and of a gRPC message; other requests take up to 16 MiB --max-statement-bytes N 1048576 the longest SQL statement a request may send ( /v1/query , /v1/sql , /v1/explain , gRPC and the MCP tools that run them), in bytes; a longer one is a 400 query_invalid before it is parsed. A list of values passed as a parameter ( id IN (:ids) ) does not count; 0: no limit since 0.3.4 --max-statement-elements N 250000 the most elements (words, values and symbols) such a statement may hold, as ClickHouse's max_ast_elements bounds; id IN ('a', 'b', ...) is two an id; 0: no limit since 0.3.4 --statement-budget SPEC none what one statement may cost, checked once its stored functions and rank profiles are written out and as it runs: name=n pairs apart by commas, of nodes , depth , list , plan_ms and rows_read ( nodes=50000,rows_read=10000000 ); a key's budget and a request's budget lower it, never raise it ( statement budgets ) since 0.3.4 --otlp-retain PERIOD none (kept for ever) how long a table OpenTelemetry logs make keeps its rows ( '30 days' , '12h' ); tables already there keep theirs --model ID claude-haiku-5-5 the agent's default model --models DIR $CHLOEDB_MODELS , else ~/.local/share/chloedb/models (or ~/.local/share/strata/models , on a machine that has that folder from before the rename; before 0.3.2, always) where the models of embedded vector fields , ranking models ( <name>/gbdt.json ) and cross-encoders ( <name>/config.json , tokenizer.json , model.safetensors ) are --no-backfill off leave the rows stored while their embedded field had no model open without vectors; by default they get them once the model opens --no-fetch-models off do not download the models ChloeDB knows that an embedded field names and --models lacks --copy-from PATH fill the new database ( --db with --schema ) with this database's rows, re-embedding the vector fields whose embedding changed --synonyms FILE the synonym list for synonyms => 1 searches ( format ): read at start if it exists, written by PUT /v1/synonyms --backup-dir DIR where POST /v1/admin/checkpoint writes checkpoints ( backups ); created if missing --cold-store URL keep segments cold on object storage: an s3://bucket/prefix/ URL, or a directory; the database must be a directory ( --directory ) --cold-cache-gb N 8 the cold tier's cache of fetched parts, segments' headers and keys apart --cold-keep-local-gb N keep the newest segments local up to this many GiB and make the older ones cold; without it, none goes cold by itself --cold-min-segment-mb N 16 segments smaller than this stay local --cold-cache-dir DIR cold in the database's directory the cold tier's cache --media-fetch LIST nothing what writes to media fields may fetch, comma-separated: file:/srv/images (a directory), cdn.example.com (HTTPS), *.example.com , http://host:port --media-store DIR where files written as bytes are kept, by hash ( media:<sha256> ); created if missing --media-max-bytes N 52428800 (50 MiB) the largest file a media value may name --media-timeout-s N 30 how long one fetch may take --log FILTER $CHLOEDB_LOG , else info what to log : a level ( error , warn , info , debug , trace ), or levels per module ( info,chloedb=debug ); debug logs every request --log-format F $CHLOEDB_LOG_FORMAT , else text log lines as text or json (one object per line) --slow-ms N 1000 log queries and writes that take this many milliseconds or more, with their SQL, and keep such queries' profiles (0: none); PUT /v1/profile/settings changes it while the server runs --log-store PATH DB.logs beside the database; none on a coordinator the log store : a ChloeDB database where the server keeps its log lines and queries, searched with SQL; off for none --log-retain PERIOD 7 days how long the log store keeps a line or a query ( '12h' , '2 weeks' ) --log-store-mb N 1024 the most the log store may take on disk; past it the oldest go --log-queries N 200 the queries a second the log store keeps of those that ended well and took less than --slow-ms ; every slow or failed one is kept --no-read-stats off do not count the documents queries return and the keys reads ask for (the index inspector 's reads); its statistics of each table still work --node-name NAME $CHLOEDB_NODE_NAME , else HOST:PORT (the host name and the HTTP port) what the log store calls this server, in its node column --app NAME=URL $CHLOEDB_APPS an app the command center shows as a view (repeatable, or comma-separated): the server forwards /v1/apps/NAME/... to it --app-token T $CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN the token the server sends its apps ( Authorization: Bearer T ) --start-apps auto\\|off\\|NAMES auto ( $CHLOEDB_START_APPS ) the apps the server starts itself , as child processes: auto starts the crawler, Sheets and ChloeCtx when chloedb-crawl , chloedb-sheets and chloectx are installed beside chloedb-server ; names ( sheets,chloectx ) start those, looked for on the PATH too chloedb-server import , chloedb-server fetch-model , chloedb-server quantize-model , chloedb-server promote and chloedb-server restore are subcommands: loading rows , embedded vector fields , promotion and restoring a checkpoint . CHLOEDB_ENGINE chooses the engine of joins, groups and sorts over joined or derived rows , over a database's own tables and on a coordinator above its shards' rows. The default, auto , uses DataFusion when a query's single-table parts read more than 4,096 rows and ChloeDB's operators below that. The other values are operators , datafusion and datafusion-only . Build with --no-default-features to leave DataFusion out of the server. since 0.3.2 chloedb-server doctor checks a machine for the guides and changes nothing: this server's version against the latest release on GitHub ( --offline skips asking), whether the Python package chloedb is ChloeDB or PyPI's placeholder ( --python picks the interpreter), the models folder and what is in it (a model this build cannot run, such as a BERT model in a release build, is a warn that says why), whether port 7700 is free ( --port ), and on Linux glibc against what the prebuilt programs and wheels need. Each line is ok , warn or FAIL with the command that fixes it; it exits 1 when a line is FAIL . chloedb-server upgrade PATH copies a database written by a version of ChloeDB from before field ids, which this one refuses to serve, into this version's format in its place, and keeps the old one as PATH.before-field-ids ( field ids ). Stop the server that has it open first. A database started with --seq-no is not copied: create a new one and fill it with --copy-from ."],
["server.html#schemas","chloedb-server","Schemas","A database starts with the table its schema names, or with none, and the schema is set when the database is created ( --schema FILE , chloedb-server import --schema FILE , the query console's schema designer, or chloedb.Database.create in Python ). CREATE TABLE and DROP TABLE over POST /v1/sql add and drop more, ALTER TABLE adds, drops and renames the columns of any table while it serves, and the server serves every table: SQL names its tables, write operations and imports name theirs ( \"table\" , ?table= ; the first table when they name none), GET /v1/tables lists the schemas and GET /v1/schema?table= gives one. CREATE RANK PROFILE and DROP RANK PROFILE keep rankings with a table the same way. A primary takes all five and ships them to its replicas as it ships writes, and answers once the copies its acks name hold them. A schema is JSON: the table's name, its key, and its fields by type, with options only where they differ from the defaults. type holds used by text words, searched by relevance MATCH , bm25() keyword exact values: ids, categories, tags = , IN , GROUP BY (facets) int , float numbers ranges, sorting, sums, boosts bool true or false conditions ( WHERE done ), GROUP BY timestamp a moment in UTC, to the microsecond, as ISO 8601 text ( \"2026-10-01T12:30:00Z\" ) ranges, sorting, MIN / MAX , boosts by age vector a fixed number of floats; with multi , a list of them, [[...], [...]] similarity() , distance() ; maxsim() sparse learned term weights (SPLADE, BGE-M3), {\"term\": weight} similarity() media an image, audio, video or PDF file by URI, with its hash, type and size ( media fields ) = and IN on the hash, GROUP BY edges links to other rows by key, a JSON array graph queries option types default what it does stored all true; false for vectors and generated text the value is kept and returned in results indexed all true the value can be searched and filtered on multi keyword , int , vector false a list of values per row (stored, not the key); a list of vectors, ranked by maxsim keep_f32 multi vector false keep the float32 vectors to score with instead of 8-bit codes pool multi vector merge a row's vectors at least this similar (cosine, such as 0.9 ) into their mean at write pool_factor multi vector cluster a row's vectors into one group for every this many (such as 2 , half as many) by Ward's linkage, and keep each group's mean at write; not with pool positions text true keep word positions, for phrase searches substring text , keyword (one value, stored) false a substring index of the values, from which LIKE and ILIKE with any pattern are answered ( substrings ) analyzer text \"english\" how text becomes words to search: \"english\" drops stopwords and stems ( running finds run ), \"simple\" keeps every word as written, \"standard\" reads text in any language and script , \"cjk\" is standard with pairs of characters for Chinese and Japanese, and a language ( \"french\" , \"arabic\" , \"hindi\" , \"chinese\" , ...) is standard with its stemmer; another than \"english\" makes the database format 3 from text generated: the named fields' text joined, such as a body of title and description to search at once dim vector the model's, for a known embed model the number of dimensions index vector \"hnsw\" (m 16, ef_construction 200) \"flat\" (an exact scan), {\"hnsw\": {\"m\": 32, \"ef_construction\": 400}} or {\"ivf\": {\"nlist\": 1024}} codes vector \"sq8\" (a byte a dimension) \"binary\" : a bit a dimension, 1/32 of the vectors, rescored; \"rabitq1\" , \"rabitq2\" or \"rabitq4\" : RaBitQ codes of that many bits a dimension, whose error bounds skip rescoring rows that cannot rank ( results ); RaBitQ makes the database format 3 partition one-vector vector with \"hnsw\" {\"by\": \"rating\", \"bounds\": [1, 2, 3, 4]} : one more graph per range of a stored, indexed int or float column (below 1, 1 to 2, ..., 4 and up), or {\"by\": \"category\"} : per value of a keyword column (a segment's 64 most frequent), walked instead of the field's graph by a search whose WHERE limits that column ( partitions ); makes the database format 3 kind media any \"image\" , \"audio\" , \"video\" or \"pdf\" : the files the field takes embed vector {\"from\": [fields], \"model\": name} : the vectors are that model's embedding of those fields ( below ) not_null all but the key false a write must give the field a value ( NOT NULL ) default single-valued text , keyword , int , float , bool , timestamp , not the key or generated the value a write that leaves the field out gets; \"now\" on a timestamp is the time of the write, one time for all the rows of a request, statement or file key names the primary key, a keyword or int field; rows are upserted and deleted by it, and a table without one only takes inserts. \"shard_by\": \"usr\" places the table's rows on shards by that field instead of by the key, \"cluster_by\": \"usr\" keeps each segment's rows sorted by that field, so that a user's history is read in one run, and \"retain\": \"400 days\" drops rows older than that (by the table's TIMESTAMP field, or the one \"retain_by\" names) when segments are flushed and merged ( table options ). \"references\": \"authors\" on a keyword or int field records that it holds keys of that table ( references ); a new database's first table can reference only itself. An option a type does not know is an error, so a typo does not pass silently. GET /v1/schema returns the first table's schema ( ?table= another's) in this form as short (and in the long form ChloeDB stores, as schema ); POST /v1/schema/check validates one and returns both, with the CREATE TABLE statement that makes the table as sql . The long form is accepted wherever a schema is read. The query language page describes the types further."],
["server.html#loading-rows","chloedb-server","Loading rows","Rows come as JSON Lines (an object per line), a JSON array of objects, or CSV or TSV with a header row. Each column goes to the field of its name; columns the schema has no field for are skipped and reported. A row's values are what a write takes: true or false for a bool (or 1, 0, \"true\" , \"false\" ), ISO 8601 text for a timestamp , a list for a multi field, an array of numbers for a vector, an object of weights for a sparse field. In CSV and TSV an empty cell takes the column's default when it has one and is null otherwise, a list is a|b|c or a JSON array, and vectors, sparse weights and edges are JSON. A field a row leaves out (a key the object lacks, a column the file lacks) gets its default , while an explicit null stays null; a field with not_null refuses a row that ends up without a value, naming the row. Vectors of embedded fields that a row leaves out are computed from its other fields. From the command line, into a database no server has open (an open database is locked): Into an empty table the rows are bulk-loaded straight into segments, in one commit: a bad row is reported with its line or row number, and nothing is written. Into a table that has rows they are upserted, 10,000 rows a transaction, and so they are into any table of a database that numbers its writes ( --seq-no ): a bulk load writes no log record, so replicas would never get its rows. The format comes from each file's extension ( .jsonl , .ndjson , .json , .csv , .tsv ) or --format . A SQLite database's tables import the same way, each into the table of its name, created from SQLite's declarations when missing, and chloedb-server export writes a database to a new SQLite file ( SQLite in and out ): Parquet files and directories of them (what Spark, DuckDB, polars and pandas write) import into one table each, created from the columns' types when missing, and export writes Parquet when the name ends in .parquet or / ; any file may be an s3:// URL ( Parquet and S3 ): To a running server, POST /v1/import with the rows as the body, the format as ?format= (or the content type) and the table as ?table= (the first table without it): The content type must not be text/plain , a form or missing (a web page on another site can send those without asking; see security ): text/csv , text/tab-separated-values , application/x-ndjson , application/json or application/octet-stream all work. A CSV or TSV header that names a field twice, or names no field of the table, is refused (a file split by ; reads as one column). A row with more or fewer values than its header is counted in the answer's ragged_rows (its missing cells empty, its extra ones left out), and refused with its line by ?strict=true . The server reads every row once before it writes any, so a bad row writes nothing, then reads them again 1,000 at a time and upserts each thousand through its writer while it reads the next (a coordinator routes them to the shards), so an import holds its body and two thousand rows, not every row. A request may be up to --max-body-mb (256 MiB). The UI's Schema and import tab uploads a file this way, and Python has import_file both in-process and as a client. A SQLite file goes the same way, as ?format=sqlite (or the content type application/vnd.sqlite3 , or no format at all: the server knows the file by its first bytes), and GET /v1/export sends the database back as a SQLite file ( over HTTP ): The server flushes the memtable into segments on a thread of its own: a write that fills it (10,000 rows and deletes not yet flushed) returns without waiting for the segments to build, and writes go on meanwhile. Once the memtable holds twice that, writes wait for the flush under way. Segments build their vector indexes on every core."],
["server.html#embedded-vector-fields","chloedb-server","Embedded vector fields","A VECTOR field can say what its vectors are: a model's embedding of other fields. The sources are stored (or generated) TEXT or KEYWORD fields, and the text embedded is their non-empty values joined by newlines, as for a generated field; or one media field of kind image or audio , whose files a model of images and text, or of sound and text, embeds ( vectors of images , of sound ). The declaration is kept in the database, returned by /v1/schema and shown by the UI (\"emb_title ← title\") and the agent. When the server starts it opens each field's model from --models and logs what each field is. With the model open: writes that leave the vector out get it computed: /v1/write documents and SQL INSERT , and an UPDATE that changes a source recomputes it, all before the write queues for the writer (on as many writes at once as there are cores), so the writer never waits for a model (with bge-small on 4 cores, an in-place UPDATE beside two clients inserting rows went from 4.7 to 500 a second, and four inserting clients from 10 to 23 rows a second: results ); a vector a write supplies is kept as given; an UPDATE that changes a source recomputes the vector; merges do not (they keep the stored vectors); a parameter {\"embed\": \"some text\", \"field\": \"emb_title\"} is the text's vector by that field's model, so queries and rows are always embedded alike ( field defaults to the first embedded field with a model; with no table , it is the field of the table the statement queries, refused when it reads two tables with such a field, and only for a statement that queries none the first table that has one, of those the key may read; {\"vector_of\": key} resolves alike); the query console's \"Similar to: a description\" uses the chosen vector field's model. A vector a token A multi-vector field ( \"multi\": true ; VECTOR(d)[] in SQL) embedded from text holds a vector for each token of its text, by a model of late interaction, ColBERT, and maxsim ranks rows by the sum over the query's tokens of each one's best match in the row: ChloeDB reads a checkpoint of the colbert-ai library (its artifact.metadata beside the BERT's config.json , tokenizer.json and model.safetensors , whose linear.weight projects each token) as the library does: a document is [CLS] [D] text [SEP] , cut to the model's doc_maxlen tokens, and its punctuation gets no vectors; a query is [CLS] [Q] text [SEP] , padded with [MASK] to query_maxlen tokens, each of them a vector. {\"embed\": \"text\", \"field\": \"tok\"} is the query's list of vectors, as maxsim takes it. Such a field embeds text, not media; a model of one vector a text is refused for it, and ColBERT for a field of one vector. pool_factor and pool pool embedded vectors as they pool written ones, when a row's text is embedded. hash:N gives a vector a word, for tests. Term weights of a learned sparse model A SPARSE field can be embedded too, by a learned sparse model: the model weighs each document's text once, at write, and the field holds the weights. A query's text is weighed without a model, a lookup of its words' weights in the model's IDF table, so a query costs what a bm25 costs: In SQL, sp SPARSE OPTIONS(embed = 'title, body', model = 'opensearch-sparse-doc-v3') . Writes that leave the field out get its terms, an UPDATE that changes a source weighs it again, a row stored while the model was not open gets them when it opens, and {\"embed\": \"some text\", \"field\": \"sp\"} is the query's term weights, as similarity(sp, :q) takes them. Fused with BM25 in rrf(bm25(body, :t), similarity(sp, :q)) , it is the fast way to rank by meaning: on SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna it came within 1.2 to 1.8 points of nDCG@10 of granite-embedding-small-english-r2's dense vectors, with queries 50 to 80 times faster (0.2 to 2.6 ms at p50 against 6 to 50 ms, on a 4-core Xeon cloud machine; the study ). The model is a directory of the models directory with model.onnx (the document encoder with its pooling, [batch, vocabulary] weights out), tokenizer.json , config.json and idf.json (the query side's weights), run by ONNX Runtime: build with --features onnx . research/cpu-encoders/sparse_onnx_export.py writes one, int8, from OpenSearch's opensearch-neural-sparse-encoding-doc-v3-distill (DistilBERT, 67M parameters, English); it is not fetched by name. A model of vectors is refused for a SPARSE field, and a sparse model for a VECTOR field. A database with such a field is written in file format 3 with the feature sparse_embed , which versions of ChloeDB from before it refuse by name. Several fields can embed different sources side by side, and a query picks one: ORDER BY similarity(emb_title, :q) , or both fused, rrf(similarity(emb_title, :q), similarity(emb_both, :p)) . Each 512-dimension field costs about 0.5 KB a row as 8-bit codes, plus its HNSW graph. The model can be computed here (the table below) or sit behind an API, such as openai/text-embedding-3-small ( hosted models ). A field may also name a model the server cannot open (its API key not set, a model directory not there yet): rows written meanwhile are stored without the vector, and text cannot be searched against the field (only rows, with vector_of ). /v1/schema says, for each embedded field, whether its model is open here and why not; GET /v1/models lists the models a schema can name and whether each would open on this server. Changing a field's model. A field's vectors are its model's (the field keeps a fingerprint of the weights), so a field does not change model in place. The command center's Models page (Data) lists every vector field with its model, how the model makes vectors and how the field is searched (HNSW, flat or IVF; SQ8, binary or RaBitQ codes), and every model here with its size and the fields that use it (fetch one, or make an int8 copy of a static one). Change on a field changes it beside the old one, so searches go on: ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN emb_next VECTOR OPTIONS(embed = 'title, body', model = 'potion-retrieval-32M-int8', ...) , with the model and technique chosen; the server fetches a model it knows and fills in the vectors of the rows already there (the backfill); the same words searched on both fields, side by side, with how many of the top 10 rows they share; ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN emb TO emb_prev, RENAME COLUMN emb_next TO emb : queries that name emb use the new vectors, and text given for it is embedded by the new model (a query that passes its own vectors must pass the new model's); the same two renames the other way undo it; ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN emb_prev frees the old vectors (a coordinator refuses DROP COLUMN , and keeps the column). Each step is SQL the page shows before running, and could be run from the workbench. Rows stored without vectors get them later. When a field's model opens (the server started again with the key set, a table created or altered, a model fetched), the server finds the rows of the field that have no vector and fills them in through the writer, 256 rows a write, as any write goes: replicas get them from the primary, and in a cluster each shard fills in its own. A row given a vector meanwhile keeps it, and a row whose text changed meanwhile gets the vector of its new text. A row whose sources are all empty stays without one. /v1/schema shows the progress under each embedded field's backfill ( {\"state\": \"done\", \"missing\": 600, \"embedded\": 599} ; running ; failed , with the error, tried again five minutes later when the model failed), and the log says when it starts and ends. --no-backfill turns it off. From Rust: Db::missing_vectors and Db::embed_backfill . Models are fetched when a field names them. A field naming one of the models below that the models directory lacks gets it from Hugging Face when the server starts (or when a table naming it is created), then its rows are filled in as above. --no-fetch-models turns that off, for a server without access to huggingface.co; chloedb-server fetch-model fetches a model by hand. model what it is potion-retrieval-32M Model2Vec (a static token table, averaged), 512 dimensions; the model ChloeCtx uses tens of microseconds a row on one core potion-base-8M Model2Vec, 256 dimensions smallest potion-retrieval-32M-int8 , potion-base-8M-int8 the same models with their tables in int8: 31 MB and 7 MB instead of 123 MB and 29 MB, ranking BEIR's SciFact, NFCorpus and ArguAna within 0.004 nDCG@10 of the originals ( results ) fetched as the original, then written in int8 on this machine bge-small-en-v1.5 BERT encoder, 384 dimensions build with --features bert ; milliseconds a row snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v1.5 BERT encoder, 768 dimensions build with --features bert ; slower, better siglip-base-patch16-224 SigLIP base: images and text in one space, 768 dimensions build with --features vision ; about 0.5 s an image and 80 ms a text on a 4-vCPU cloud VM clap-htsat-unfused CLAP (HTSAT and RoBERTa): sound and text in one space, 512 dimensions build with --features audio ; about 0.27 s for each 10 seconds of sound and 20 ms a text on a 4-vCPU cloud VM answerai-colbert-small-v1 ColBERT (BERT, 33M parameters), a 96-dimension vector a token, for a multi-vector field build with --features bert hash:N hashed bag of words, N dimensions, 1 to 65,536 (on a multi-vector field, a vector a word) no files; tests and the demo ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 a cross-encoder (BERT, 22M parameters, English), not an embedding model build with --features bert bge-reranker-base a cross-encoder (XLM-RoBERTa, 278M parameters, English and Chinese) build with --features bert ; larger and slower ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2-int8 ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 quantized to int8, its repository's ONNX export for this CPU (ARM64's on ARM, AVX-512 VNNI's on an x86-64 with VNNI, AVX2's on one without) build with --features onnx gte-reranker-modernbert-base-int8 a cross-encoder (ModernBERT, 149M parameters, English, 8,192 tokens), its repository's int8 ONNX export build with --features onnx cohere/rerank-v3.5 , voyage/rerank-2.5 , jina/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual cross-encoders served by Cohere, Voyage AI and Jina AI (any of their models by name), or a server of one's own named by <models>/<name>/endpoint.json (TEI, vLLM) nothing to fetch; the key in COHERE_API_KEY , VOYAGE_API_KEY or JINA_API_KEY ( served elsewhere ) On a Mac, build with --features accelerate instead of bert : the BERT models' matrix products then run in Apple's Accelerate framework. A model of this list (and the captioners and cross-encoders ChloeDB knows) is fetched from the Hugging Face commit it was pinned at, not from main , and each file is checked against the SHA-256 pinned for it ( chloedb/src/model_pins.rs ): a file that differs is refused and not kept, so a model changed upstream under the same name cannot slip in. A table created with its model in the models directory records the SHA-256 of the model's weights file in its schema ( embed.fingerprint ), and refuses to embed with a model of the same name whose weights differ; tables from before, and those whose model was not there when they were created, open their model by name, as before. Changed: files came from resolve/main , unchecked, and a field recorded the model's name only. A static model in int8. potion-retrieval-32M-int8 and potion-base-8M-int8 are fetched as their originals are (the pinned files, checked), then written with their tables in int8, Model2Vec's own format of one scale for the whole table, which the vector's unit length cancels. The conversion gives the same file on every machine, so the fingerprint a table records is the same everywhere. A copy is a model of its own name, whose vectors differ from the original's in the third decimal (mean cosine 0.9994 on SciFact's documents): a table embedded by potion-retrieval-32M keeps it, and one that should use the int8 copy names it, emb VECTOR OPTIONS(embed = 'body', model = 'potion-retrieval-32M-int8') . chloedb-server quantize-model DIR writes any Model2Vec model in int8 ( --to-type F16 for half precision), into DIR-int8 beside it unless --to says where; from Rust, chloedb::embed::quantize_static . A model with its table in F16, BF16 or I8, and with Model2Vec's weights and mapping tensors, is read as it is. chloedb-server fetch-model NAME downloads one of these (or any Hugging Face repository owner/name with config.json , tokenizer.json and model.safetensors ; CLAP's pytorch_model.bin ; and artifact.metadata too for a name with colbert in it, such as colbert-ir/colbertv2.0 ) into the models directory; it also downloads the captioner and the speech model of text from media ."],
["server.html#hosted-models","chloedb-server","Hosted models","A field can name a model behind an API as <provider>/<model> , and ChloeDB calls the provider itself: on every write that leaves the vector out (in batches, before the write queues for the writer, as for a model computed here) and for every {\"embed\": \"text\"} parameter. Rows go in as plain text and come back by meaning, with no embedding pipeline of your own: provider names, for example the key, in the server's environment base URL (to point at another server of the API) OpenAI openai/text-embedding-3-small (1536), openai/text-embedding-3-large (3072) OPENAI_API_KEY OPENAI_BASE_URL Cohere cohere/embed-v4.0 (1536), cohere/embed-english-v3.0 (1024), cohere/embed-multilingual-v3.0 COHERE_API_KEY or CO_API_KEY COHERE_BASE_URL Voyage AI voyage/voyage-3.5 (1024), voyage/voyage-3-large , voyage/voyage-code-3 VOYAGE_API_KEY VOYAGE_BASE_URL Google Gemini gemini/gemini-embedding-001 (3072), gemini/text-embedding-004 (768) GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY GEMINI_BASE_URL Mistral mistral/mistral-embed (1024), mistral/codestral-embed MISTRAL_API_KEY MISTRAL_BASE_URL Jina AI jina/jina-embeddings-v3 (1024), jina/jina-embeddings-v4 JINA_API_KEY JINA_BASE_URL Ollama (local) ollama/nomic-embed-text (768), ollama/mxbai-embed-large (1024), ollama/bge-m3 none OLLAMA_HOST (default localhost:11434 ) Any model name after the prefix is sent as it is, so a provider's newer models need no new ChloeDB; a field naming one ChloeDB does not know gives dim . Keys are read from the environment when the server opens the model, and never stored in the database or shown in errors: a schema names its model, not its key. Without its key a model does not open, /v1/schema says which variable to set, and writes must bring their vectors until it is set. In a cluster, each shard embeds the rows it takes and the coordinator embeds queries, so each needs the key. What ChloeDB does for each request: Batches of texts (OpenAI 256 a request, Cohere 96, Gemini 100, Voyage 128, Ollama 32, and by characters too), with up to 4 requests of a model in flight at once across every write and query (2 for Ollama), so a large import runs at the provider's speed and a burst of writes does not trip its rate limit. Retries on a rate limit (429, waiting as long as Retry-After says), a server error (5xx), a timeout or a dropped connection: six times, waiting half a second and doubling. A refused key (401, 403), a wrong answer (vectors of another size, too few) or a request the API calls bad fails at once. A write whose vectors cannot be had fails whole with 503 ( unavailable: model openai/...: ... answered 429 ... ), and nothing of it is written; a query fails alike. Long texts are cut to what the model takes (about 24,000 characters for OpenAI, 8,000 for Gemini; Cohere, Voyage, Jina and Ollama cut them themselves), and a text the API still calls too long is cut by half and sent again. Documents and queries are embedded as the API asks: Cohere's search_document and search_query , Voyage's document and query , Gemini's RETRIEVAL_DOCUMENT and RETRIEVAL_QUERY , Jina's retrieval.passage and retrieval.query , and the prefixes nomic-embed-text, mxbai-embed-large and snowflake-arctic-embed were trained with. Fewer dimensions. A field of fewer dimensions than the model makes asks for them, from a model that can shorten its vectors (OpenAI's text-embedding-3, Cohere's embed-v4.0, Voyage 3 and 3.5, Gemini, Jina v3 and v4): {\"type\": \"vector\", \"dim\": 512, \"embed\": {\"model\": \"openai/text-embedding-3-large\", ...}} keeps 512 numbers a row. A model that cannot is refused for a field of another size. Unit vectors and a query cache. Every vector is normalized, as ChloeDB's dot product needs; the last 4,096 query texts' vectors are kept, so a repeated search sends nothing. Usage. /metrics counts each field's requests, retries, failures and the tokens the provider says it counted ( chloedb_embed_requests_total , chloedb_embed_retries_total , chloedb_embed_failures_total , chloedb_embed_tokens_total ), and /v1/schema shows them under each embedded field's usage . Other servers of these APIs. vLLM, Hugging Face Text Embeddings Inference, LM Studio, LiteLLM, Azure OpenAI, Together and Fireworks speak OpenAI's API. Point openai/<model> at one with OPENAI_BASE_URL (a server named by URL may need no key), or give it a name of its own: a directory under --models holding an endpoint.json , which the schema then names like any model. api is openai , cohere , voyage , gemini , jina or ollama . base_url gets the API's path added ( /embeddings ); url is used as given instead (Azure's .../deployments/NAME/embeddings?api-version=2024-02-01 , with \"key_header\": \"api-key\" ). The rest are optional: model (the API's name; default the directory's), dim , key_env , key_header , headers (an object), document_prefix , query_prefix , dimensions (whether the model takes a number of dimensions), batch , batch_chars , max_chars , concurrency , retries and timeout_s (60). key_env names the variable the key is read from, and it is a provider's key variable ( OPENAI_API_KEY , ANTHROPIC_API_KEY , ...) or one whose name starts with CHLOEDB_MODEL_KEY_ : an endpoint sends its key to the URL it names, so no other variable of the server's (its tokens, a cloud's credentials) can be one; any other name is refused when the model is opened. An endpoint.json in models/openai/text-embedding-3-small/ changes these for that name only. ChloeDB's builds have hosted models on (feature hosted , a default). Changing your mind. Vectors are stored, so changing a field's sources or model means re-embedding. Create a database with the new schema and fill it from the old one: Rows are copied field by field by name; a vector field is copied when the old one has the same dimensions, sources and model, and embedded anew otherwise; fields the new schema drops are left out. The old database may be one from before field ids , which opens only to be copied. Db::load_from does the same from Rust. Which fields to embed. Embed the text that carries what people search for, usually title and description; keep brand, price, category and dates out of the vector and filter on them instead. Measured on four BEIR collections with titles (nDCG@10; Apple M2, 2026-10-27; all tables, recall and times ): collection model title text title + text title + text with BM25 SciFact Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M 0.487 0.613 0.633 0.697 SciFact BGE-small v1.5 0.598 0.697 0.713 0.724 SciFact Arctic Embed M v1.5 0.576 0.704 0.716 0.729 NFCorpus Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M 0.283 0.295 0.308 0.340 NFCorpus BGE-small v1.5 0.318 0.349 0.344 0.365 NFCorpus Arctic Embed M v1.5 0.321 0.361 0.363 0.370 SciDocs Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M 0.128 0.120 0.137 0.159 SciDocs BGE-small v1.5 0.195 0.198 0.205 0.195 TREC-COVID Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M 0.504 0.482 0.446 0.654 Title + text is the best single vector, or within 0.005 of it, everywhere but TREC-COVID with Model2Vec, where short COVID questions match its on-topic titles better than a static model's average over long abstracts. Fusing the vector with BM25 ( rrf(bm25(body, :t), similarity(emb, :q)) ) beats the best single vector in 8 of the 9 runs; the exception is SciDocs with BGE-small, where BM25 (0.157) is far behind the vector and fusion pulls it from 0.205 to 0.195. So start with one vector of title + description searched together with BM25, check the fusion against the vector alone on your own queries, and when titles are informative and descriptions long, above all with a static model, add a title-only field and compare the two. On the M2's CPU, Model2Vec loaded and embedded 12,000 to 22,000 texts a second and the BERT models 8 to 27 (build with --features accelerate on a Mac). chloedb-embed-eval (in chloedb/ ) runs this comparison: it loads a BEIR collection into fields embedding its titles, its texts and both, by one model, and reports nDCG@10 and recall@100 for each, with BM25, hybrid and the collection's Cohere vectors as references."],
["server.html#media-fields","chloedb-server","Media fields","A media field holds a file by reference: its URI, the SHA-256 of its bytes, its type and its size, and for the types ChloeDB reads the headers of, an image's width and height, a recording's duration or a PDF's pages. The bytes are not in the database. A write gives one of the whole value, as above: taken as given, nothing is fetched; a URI, \"https://cdn.example.com/a.png\" or {\"uri\": \"file:/srv/images/a.png\"} : the server fetches the file if --media-fetch lists it, and fills in the rest; the bytes, base64, with a URI or without: {\"uri\": \"a.png\", \"data\": \"iVBORw0K…\"} keeps that URI, and {\"data\": \"iVBORw0K…\"} keeps the file in --media-store under its hash, as media:<sha256> . This happens before the write queues for the writer, for /v1/write , SQL INSERT and UPDATE , and imports alike, up to 16 files fetched at once; a value that cannot be resolved fails the whole request (400, or 504 for a fetch that timed out) and nothing of it is written. A sha256 given with a URI or bytes is checked. What the log, the segments and replicas get is the resolved value, so a replica never fetches, and a replayed log does not either. What may be fetched. Nothing, until --media-fetch names it: a file: directory takes the files under it (after symbolic links and .. are resolved), a host name takes https:// URLs of it on port 443, *.example.com its subdomains, and http://host:port plain HTTP there. A fetch follows at most 5 redirects, each to a URL the list has, reads at most --media-max-bytes and gives up after --media-timeout-s . The host checked is the one fetched: the list is compared with the URL as the HTTP client parses it, before it connects and at each redirect, and a URL that URL parsers could read differently is refused, whatever the list says: one with a backslash or a control character (a tab, a newline) in it, a user name or password, a host with characters other than letters, digits, - , _ and . (an internationalised name is written in its xn-- form, a percent-encoded one is refused), or a host ending in a number that is not an IPv4 address written as four decimal numbers. The list is the server's guard against writes that would make it read its own files or reach hosts on its network: keep it to the places the files are. Queries return the value as JSON text. The field's index is of hashes, so WHERE image = :sha256 , IN and GROUP BY image find and count the rows of a file. kind refuses files of another type, by their bytes' headers. A coordinator does not fetch: start each shard with the --media-* options. A database with a media field is written in file format 3 ."],
["server.html#vectors-of-images","chloedb-server","Vectors of images","An embedded vector field can be the embedding of a media field of kind image , by a model of images and text ( siglip-base-patch16-224 , built with --features vision ): A write's images are decoded (JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF, BMP) and embedded with the value's other work, before the write queues for the writer, from the bytes the write gave or fetched; a value written whole is read from where its URI says (the media store, or a place --media-fetch lists), and must still hash to its sha256 . An image that cannot be read or decoded fails the request, as a value that cannot be resolved does. The vectors are logged with the row, so replicas get them and run no model. An embedded field embeds one media field or text fields, not both; a second field can embed the text beside it. A query embeds an image file with the model's image tower, {\"embed\": {\"image\": \"<base64>\"}, \"field\": \"emb\"} , and a text with its text tower, {\"embed\": \"a dog on a beach\", \"field\": \"emb\"} ; the image's vector and the caption's are in one space, so either finds images: Through ChloeDB, siglip-base-patch16-224 retrieves as well as open_clip's published results for it say (Karpathy's test splits, five captions an image, exact search; chloedb-media-eval , cloud container with 4 vCPUs, results ): split text to image R@1 / R@5 / R@10 image to text R@1 / R@5 / R@10 Flickr30K, 1,000 images 74.7 / 92.3 / 95.7 89.2 / 97.9 / 99.3 published 74.7 / 92.3 / 95.6 89.1 / 98.0 / 99.3 COCO, 5,000 images 47.8 / 72.4 / 80.9 65.7 / 85.6 / 91.4 published 47.8 / 72.4 / 81.0 65.7 / 85.4 / 91.3"],
["server.html#vectors-of-sound","chloedb-server","Vectors of sound","An embedded vector field can also be the embedding of a media field of kind audio , by a model of sound and text ( clap-htsat-unfused , LAION's CLAP, built with --features audio ): Sound is decoded as for transcripts , resampled to CLAP's 48 kHz and cut into 10-second windows; a window shorter than 10 seconds is repeated to fill them. A recording's vector is the normalized mean of its windows' vectors, so all of it counts: the transformers library's default takes one 10 seconds at random from a longer recording. Writes, logs, replicas and the errors of a file that cannot be read are as for vectors of images . A query embeds a sound file with the model's audio tower, {\"embed\": {\"audio\": \"<base64>\"}, \"field\": \"emb\"} , and a text with its text tower, {\"embed\": \"rain on a tin roof\", \"field\": \"emb\"} ; either finds recordings. ChloeDB's vectors are the transformers library's: on the first 20 recordings of Clotho's evaluation split and their 100 captions, every vector ChloeDB made had a cosine of 1.000000 with the one transformers 4.46.3 made from the same samples. On the whole split, 1,045 recordings of 15 to 30 seconds with five captions each, exact search ( chloedb-clap-eval , cloud container with 4 vCPUs, results ): vectors of the recordings text to audio R@1 / R@5 / R@10 audio to text R@1 / R@5 / R@10 ChloeDB, the mean of 10-second windows 15.9 / 40.1 / 53.6 23.2 / 48.6 / 61.7 transformers ' default, one 10 seconds at random 15.8 / 37.8 / 51.2 21.7 / 46.4 / 59.8 A recording of 22 seconds takes about 0.7 s to read and embed, and a caption 20 ms, on a 4-vCPU cloud VM."],
["server.html#text-from-media","chloedb-server","Text from media","A text field can be a model's text of a media field's file: a caption of each row's image, made by BLIP's captioner ( blip-image-captioning-base , built with --features vision ), or a transcript of each row's recording, made by Whisper ( whisper-small , built with --features audio ). caption TEXT OPTIONS(derive = 'image', model = 'blip-image-captioning-base') declares it in CREATE TABLE . The text is made as vectors of images are: before the write queues for the writer, from the bytes the write gave or fetched, for /v1/write , SQL INSERT and imports; an UPDATE that changes the file makes it again, and a file that cannot be read or decoded fails the request. A text a write gives is kept as given, so captions made elsewhere (by a larger model, or by people) go in the same field. The field is stored and logged with the row: merges and replicas never run the model, and a database opened without it still reads and searches the text. From then on it is text like any other: BM25, phrases, synonyms, MATCH , a generated field over it, and an embedded vector of it ( \"embed\": {\"from\": [\"caption\"], ...} ) all read it. /v1/schema lists each derived field under derived , with whether this server has its model open. model what it makes blip-image-captioning-base a caption of an image, greedily, at most 20 tokens, as the transformers library makes it by default build with --features vision ; chloedb-server fetch-model blip-image-captioning-base ; about 2 s an image on a 4-vCPU cloud VM whisper-small a transcript of speech in any of Whisper's 99 languages: each 30 seconds decoded as the transformers library does by default (greedily, without timestamps), in the language detected in the file's first 30 seconds, and the windows' texts joined by spaces build with --features audio ; chloedb-server fetch-model whisper-small ; about 5.4 s for each 30 seconds of sound, or part of it, on a 4-vCPU cloud VM ( transformers takes 2.9 s) colors the names of an image's main colours no files; tests and demos ChloeDB's captions are the transformers library's: on 100 images of Flickr30K's test split (the first by file name), BLIP base through ChloeDB and through transformers 4.46.3 wrote the same caption for 97 of them; the other three part at a word that two choices nearly tie for ( a man in a red shirt against a man wearing a red shirt ), most likely from small differences in resizing the image and in float arithmetic. Changing the model of a field means a new database filled from the old one ( --copy-from ): text of the same model is copied, and text of another is made again. Sound is decoded from WAV, FLAC, MP3, Ogg Vorbis and AAC (in MP4) files, mixed down to one channel and resampled to Whisper's 16 kHz; a recording longer than an hour is refused. A recording is cut every 30 seconds, so a word across a cut can be lost or split: Whisper's long-form mode, which moves its window by the timestamps it predicts, is not implemented. ChloeDB's transcripts are the transformers library's: on 200 utterances of LibriSpeech's test-clean (every 13th, all 40 speakers), whisper-small through ChloeDB and through transformers 4.46.3 wrote the same 200 transcripts, with a word error rate of 3.8% ( chloedb-asr-eval , results ; words compared without punctuation, a stricter count than Whisper's own normalizer). Searching the utterances by what was said, ORDER BY bm25(transcript, :t) , ranked the right one first for 198 of 200. For finding images by text, the captions add little to an image model's vectors. With SigLIP's vectors and BM25 over BLIP's captions, fused in SQL ( chloedb-media-eval , cloud container with 4 vCPUs, results ), R@1: ranking Flickr30K 1K, text to image image to text COCO (first 1K of 5K), text to image image to text similarity(emb, :q) 74.7 89.2 67.6 82.8 bm25(caption, :t) 14.6 21.1 22.7 34.5 rrf(similarity(emb, :q), bm25(caption, :t)) 34.0 52.8 39.9 58.4 0.95 * minmax(similarity(emb, :q)) + 0.05 * minmax(bm25(caption, :t)) 74.7 89.0 68.0 84.3 rrf weighs a weak list as much as a strong one; a small weight on the captions is level with the vectors on Flickr30K and 0.3 to 1.5 better on COCO, whose training captions BLIP was fine-tuned on. What a caption is for is text the rest of the database reads: MATCH , phrases, filters and a row's description."],
["server.html#text-from-text","chloedb-server","Text from text","A text field can also be a language model's text of another field's text: a summary, a category, the product a review names, keywords for search. The field's prompt tells the model what to make, and the model is a Claude model ( anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5 , its key in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ), an OpenAI chat model ( openai/<model> , OPENAI_API_KEY ), or a chat server of one's own that a directory <models>/<name> names in an endpoint.json ( {\"api\": \"chat\", \"base_url\": \"http://gpu:8000/v1\", \"model\": \"qwen3-8b\"} for vLLM, Ollama, LM Studio or LiteLLM; \"api\": \"anthropic\" for a server of Anthropic's API). The source is a stored TEXT or KEYWORD field of one value (not generated, derived or multi-valued). The text is made as captions are: before the write queues for the writer, for /v1/write , SQL INSERT and imports, kept as given when a write gives it, made again by an UPDATE of the source, stored and logged with the row so that merges and replicas never call the model. Each distinct text of a write is one request (the prompt as the system prompt, the text as the user's message, at most 1,024 tokens back, at the model's own sampling defaults), eight at once ( concurrency in endpoint.json ), each tried again on a rate limit or a server's error; one that keeps failing fails the write. An empty text makes an empty one, with no request. The answer is the field's text, without its surrounding spaces. A request costs a model call a row, so a table loaded with a million rows makes a million requests: load rows in batches and watch the provider's rate limits, or make the text elsewhere and write it with the rows. The table needs the derive_text feature, which a version of ChloeDB without it refuses to open."],
["server.html#keywords-made-of-text-languages-keyphrases-near-duplicates-entities","chloedb-server","Keywords made of text: languages, keyphrases, near duplicates, entities","Experimental, and a format change. A KEYWORD field with derive makes the database declare the format feature derive_keyword , which ChloeDB 0.2.x refuses by name, so a database that has one cannot go back to 0.2.x; a database without one opens there as before. The model names ( builtin:lang , builtin:keyphrases , builtin:minhash , dict:<table>.<field> , classifier:<name> ) are kept in the schema, so what each built-in model makes of a text is part of the format too: a later version keeps those names meaning what they do now, and a changed model gets a new name. A keyword field can hold values that a built-in model makes of a text, which need no model files and cost microseconds a row: builtin:lang (a KEYWORD ) is the text's language as an ISO 639-1 code: by its script (Han, kana, Hangul, Cyrillic, Arabic, Greek, Devanagari, Thai and others), then for Latin and Cyrillic letters by the function words it uses most. On the 10,000 test texts of papluca/language-identification (20 languages; reviews, news and single sentences) it named 96.1% right, from 85% for Italian (taken for French or Spanish) to 100% for Japanese and Vietnamese, in 32 to 42 µs a text ( chloedb-content-eval , chloedb/results/text/content_lang.json ; the Bulgarian word list was added after a first run on this set, which had 94.8%). chloedb::content::analyzer_name maps a code to the analyzer for that language. builtin:keyphrases (a KEYWORD[] ) holds the text's five main phrases by RAKE: runs of words between stopwords and punctuation, each scored by its words' co-occurrence degree over their frequency ( linear diophantine equations ), for facets and tag clouds. builtin:minhash (a KEYWORD[] ) holds 8 bands of a 32-hash MinHash of the text's word 3-grams. Two rows that share a value are likely near duplicates: texts whose 3-grams overlap by a Jaccard similarity of 0.8 share a band 98.5% of the time, by 0.5 40%, by 0.2 1.3%. SELECT dup, COUNT(*) FROM news GROUP BY dup HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 lists the groups, and a self-join on dup the pairs. dict:<table>.<field> (a KEYWORD[] ) holds the values of another table's KEYWORD field that the text names in full, read as query understanding reads values (analysed as the source field is): a table of brands, places or products tags the rows that mention them. The values are those the table holds when the row is written, read once per version of it. The dictionary is another table's: one of the derived field's own table is refused, since each write would read it again. A coordinator refuses the field for now, since each shard would tag its rows from its own share of the dictionary's rows: derive it on one server. classifier:<name> (a KEYWORD , or a KEYWORD[] ) holds a classifier 's label of the text: the text embedded by the model of the field the classifier was trained on, and the best label ( NULL below its min_score ), or for a KEYWORD[] the labels whose probability is at least a tenth. A text written before the classifier exists, or after it is dropped, has none; a row keeps the label the version of its write gave, and a new version labels the rows written after it. The source is a stored TEXT or KEYWORD field of one value, as for a language model. The values are made before the write queues for the writer, kept as given when a write gives them, made again by an UPDATE of the source, and stored and logged with the row, so merges and replicas never run the model. The table needs the derive_keyword feature, which a version of ChloeDB without it refuses to open."],
["server.html#semantic-ids","chloedb-server","Semantic IDs","A semantic ID is a short code for a row's vector, such as 12.7.201.0 , that nearby vectors share a start of: rows whose codes start with 12 are alike, those that start with 12.7 more so. Generative recommenders predict an item's code a number at a time; a filter on a prefix finds every row under it. A KEYWORD[] field declares that it holds the semantic ID of a VECTOR field, by a named codebook: sid KEYWORD[] OPTIONS(semantic_id = 'emb', codebook = 'items-rq') declares it in CREATE TABLE . The code. The codebook is residual k-means: up to 4 levels of up to 256 centroids (3 of 256 by default). The first number is the vector's nearest first-level centroid, the next the nearest second-level centroid to what is left, and so on. The last number is a tie that tells apart rows with the same levels: the first such row gets 0, the next 1. A tie is never given twice under the same levels, also after its row is deleted and merged away. The value. The field holds every prefix of the code, shortest first ( [\"12\", \"12.7\", \"12.7.201\", \"12.7.201.0\"] ), so WHERE sid = '12.7' finds every row under 12.7 , through the keyword index, and combines with any other condition. sid_at(sid, n) is the first n numbers of a code, as text ( sid_at(sid, 2) is '12.7' ; NULL past its length), for grouping and for columns: SELECT sid_at(sid, 1), COUNT(*) FROM items GROUP BY sid_at(sid, 1) . A query with sid_at computes it over the rows its scan returns, as a join does, so the scan holds at most 1,000,000 rows. Writes. The writer fills the field in from the vector, after an embedded vector is computed: a row's code is its own from then on. An upsert keeps the code while the vector's levels stay the same, and gets a new tie when they change. UPDATE cannot set the field (set the vector instead), and a write that brings a whole code keeps it, so a copy or a replica holds the same codes. The codebook is a model, kept in the models directory as <name>/codebook.safetensors . The field records its name and the SHA-256 of its file when the table is created, which fails if the codebook is not there; opening the database later checks the file against it. A database whose codebook did not open still reads, but writes that need a new code fail. A name holds one codebook, and a new codebook means a new field (or table). Train one in Python with chloedb.train.codebook , on a table's vectors or on your own ( balanced=True uses every level's numbers evenly, so fewer rows share a code), and read what it makes of your vectors with chloedb.train.codebook_report before a field names it; from Rust, Codebook::train , Codebook::report and Db::train_codebook . The next codes. A decoder that writes a semantic ID a number at a time, by beam search, asks which numbers can come next and still lead to a row that passes a filter. POST /v1/sid/next answers it for up to 64 prefixes at once, \"\" asking for the first number: Each prefix's next codes come in the order of their last numbers, with the rows under each that where matches (every row without it), and a code no matching row has is left out. The filter is worked out once per segment; the codes below a prefix are found by jumping through the field's sorted dictionary, where every prefix is a value, and each is counted against the filter. Db::sid_next answers the same from Rust. A coordinator asks every shard (gRPC SidNext , protocol 7) and adds their counts. Each shard numbers the ties of its own rows, so on a coordinator a full code can name one row on each shard: the counts of the levels are the table's, and a tie's are those of every row with that code. With a decoder. A TIGER-style decoder over these codes, each step's numbers from sid_next among the items a user does not have, comes within 0.0023 Recall@10 of published TIGER on Amazon Beauty and Sports (0.0085 below it on Toys). Alone it ranks no better than a sequence model's encode() , at 30 to 40 times its time, most of it the decoder's own; its answer, given to a query as ranked(:gen) and fused with rrf , adds to that model's and to a walk's ( results ). A database with a semantic-ID field needs the manifest feature semantic_id , and a plan that uses sid_at the plan feature of the same name, which versions of ChloeDB before them refuse by name."],
["server.html#http-api","chloedb-server","HTTP API","Every request and answer is JSON. Every answer carries the request's id in an x-request-id header: the one the client sent, if it is 1 to 64 letters, digits, - , _ , . or : , or a new one. The logs name it, and a coordinator passes it on to its shards (as gRPC metadata of the same name), so one id finds a request in every server it reached. Parameters bind :name placeholders: a number, a string, an array for a vector, an object of term weights for a SPARSE field, {\"vector_of\": \"key\", \"field\": \"emb\"} for a stored row's vector (more like this), or {\"embed\": \"text\", \"field\": \"emb\"} for the text's vector by an embedded field 's model ( {\"embed\": {\"image\": \"<base64>\"}, ...} for an image file's, by a model of images , and {\"embed\": {\"audio\": \"<base64>\"}, ...} for a sound file's, by a model of sound ); both take \"table\" for a field of another table than the first. route body answer POST /v1/query {sql, params?, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?, log?, trace?, steps?, profile?, objects?, format?, page_size?, keep_alive_ms?} , or a scroll's next page: {cursor, format?, objects?, keep_alive_ms?, timeout_ms?} {columns, rows, keys, scores, stats, took_ms, warnings?} ( warnings names a field of the body that is not one of these, such as a misspelt timeout_ms , which is left out; a field of the wrong type, \"timeout_ms\": \"100\" , is refused): each row a list of its values in the order of columns , or with \"objects\": true an object keyed by column ( {\"id\": \"p1\", \"price\": 9.5} ; a column named as an earlier one is keyed id_2 , id_3 , ...), or with \"format\": \"arrow\" the rows as an Arrow IPC stream ( application/vnd.apache.arrow.stream ), each column of one Arrow type as query_arrow types it in a client's own process, with x-chloedb-took-ms and x-chloedb-stats headers (not with log ), with \"steps\": true the query's profile in stats.steps (with profile , each step's CPU time and the request's trace too); min_seq_no and min_seq_nos name a write the read must see ( reads that see a write ); with log ( true or {context?, request?, variant?, candidates?, ts?, table?} ), a ranked query also writes what it served to a rank log and answers request and logged: {rows, seq_no?, seq_nos?} ; with page_size , a scroll 's first page and, while rows are left, its cursor (with \"format\": \"arrow\" , the x-chloedb-cursor header) DELETE /v1/query/cursors/ID {closed} : ends a scroll before its last page (false when it was not open, or is another key's) POST /v1/sql {sql, params?, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?, log?, objects?, idempotency_key?} rows for a SELECT ( log and objects as for /v1/query ), and for SHOW TABLES the tables' names (a column name , the tables the key reads, as GET /v1/tables lists them); {written, seq_no?, seq_nos?} for INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE POST /v1/rank_log/export {table?, since?, until?, events?: {table, user, item, time, action?}, history?, window?, grades?} NDJSON: one record per logged row, with its context's history and its label as of its time ( exporting the log ), or {error, status} at a failure; a coordinator refuses it (400) POST /v1/rank_log/interleaving the export's body {pairs: [{variants, requests, acted, wins, ties, delta, p}], took_ms} : which of two interleaved rankings the users preferred; a coordinator refuses it (400) POST /v1/relevance/autopilot {table?, profile?, since?, k?, max_queries?, budget?, held_out?, seed?, min_gain?, alpha?, max_slowdown?, min_queries?, max_holes?, log?, judgments?, judge?, model?, api_key?, keep?} {report, proposal, judged} : the relevance autopilot 's report of new numbers for a rank profile, measured on held-out logged queries replayed on one snapshot; admin; a coordinator refuses it (400) GET /v1/relevance/proposals , GET /v1/relevance/proposals/:id , POST /v1/relevance/proposals/:id/apply {force?} , POST /v1/relevance/proposals/:id/dismiss the proposals kept, latest first; one with its proof; applied ( CREATE OR REPLACE RANK PROFILE , refused when the profile changed since it was measured) or dismissed; admin to change GET /v1/kv/TABLE/KEY?min_seq_no=n {table, key, found, row, seq_no} : the row with that primary key, read without SQL ( rows by key ); 404 with found: false when no live row has it POST /v1/kv/get {keys, table?, columns?, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?} {table, columns, rows, seq_nos, took_ms} : per key, in their order, its row as an object ( null for none) and its sequence number; of columns alone when given PUT /v1/kv/TABLE/KEY?if_seq_no=n the row's fields as an object (the key may be left out) {written, seq_no?} : the row, written in place of any with that key DELETE /v1/kv/TABLE/KEY?if_seq_no=n&routing=v {deleted, written, seq_no?} POST /v1/write {ops: [...], table?, idempotency_key?, strict?} {written, skipped?, seq_no?, seq_nos?, receipt, warnings?} ( conditional writes ; skipped for stream events written already; seq_nos from a coordinator; receipt and strict : write receipts ) POST /v1/stream operations as NDJSON, one a line, or one event's several as {ops, source} ( streams ) NDJSON: a line per batch once it commits, {batch, ops, written, skipped, lines} , then {done, batches, lines, written, skipped} , or {error, status, lines} at the first failure GET /v1/admin/connectors , POST /v1/admin/connectors , /v1/admin/connectors/NAME ( GET , POST , DELETE ), POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/run , POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/key , POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/start and /stop , POST /v1/admin/connectors/test a connector's settings connectors : their health, state and runs; a new one (its push key's secret once); a change; a pull now; a new push key; a Kafka connector's consumer on or off; a dry run /v1/admin/kafka/test-broker ( GET , POST , DELETE ), POST /v1/admin/kafka/test-broker/produce {listen, topic, partitions, rate, username, password} ; {topic, messages: [{key, value, headers}]} the test Kafka broker in this process: its topics and counts; started (on loopback) or its rate set; stopped; events added POST /v1/connectors/NAME/push any body: JSON, or a raw email what a push connector took: {records, rows, skipped, dropped} , written with the sender's key ( connectors ) GET /v1/sources , GET /v1/sources/NAME {sources: [{name, offset, updated}]} : each stream source's last offset written ( streams ); for one, {name, offset, updated} , offset null for a source not written from POST /v1/plan {plan, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?, objects?} : a plan in JSON, as /v1/explain gives it in ir rows for a read, a compound query's plan included, {written} for a write; a coordinator runs reads, and refuses a write plan (400) until its shards take write plans POST /v1/import?format=F&table=T&strict=true&empty=default rows as JSON Lines, JSON, CSV or TSV ( loading rows ) {written, skipped_fields, ragged_rows?, took_ms, receipt} ; empty says what an empty CSV or TSV cell is: default (the column's DEFAULT, else NULL; the default), null , or text (the empty string in a text or keyword column); strict : write receipts POST /v1/import?format=sqlite&tables=A,B&plan=true&blobs_base64=true a SQLite database file ( SQLite in and out ) {tables: [{source, table, rows, created, notes}], skipped, written, took_ms} ; with plan , what it would do, writing nothing GET /v1/export?tables=A,B&fts=false&indexes=false&schemas=false the database (or those tables) as a new SQLite file, application/vnd.sqlite3 ; x-chloedb-tables names the tables (admin) GET /v1/export?format=jsonl\\|csv\\|arrow\\|parquet&tables=T one table's rows, read by a scroll over every shard on a coordinator (Parquet from one snapshot on a server, as before): JSON Lines and CSV stream a page at a time, Arrow (one batch, at most 1,000,000 rows) and Parquet (with the table's schema kept, so an import reads it back) once every page is read; a key with read on the table may ask POST /v1/schema/check a schema, in either form {schema, short, sql} ( sql the CREATE TABLE that makes it), or the error POST /v1/sql/check {sql, params?} {runs, deployment, kind, error?, status?, notes} : whether the statement would run here ( deployment single or cluster ), bound against the tables served and planned, without running it; notes what a cluster does differently ( cluster limits ) POST /v1/explain {sql, params?, hits?, profile?} runs the query and returns its plan IR as JSON ( ir ) and as lines ( tree , as EXPLAIN shows it), the executor's plan lines ( plan ), the timed steps of the run ( steps , its profile ), the bound query ( logical ) and counts; with hits: n , a ranked query's top n rows as objects with their score() and features() , the values each was ranked by (a query that does not rank rows, one without an ORDER BY or one that groups, aggregates or orders by a value it computes, ORDER BY revenue of SUM(price * sold) AS revenue , has none, and returns no hits ) POST /v1/embed {text, image or audio, field?, table?} {table, field, vector} : the vector of the text, or of the image or sound file ( image or audio , base64, for a model of images or of sound), by an embedded field's model (the first whose model is open, unless table and field name one), as an {\"embed\": text} parameter gets it; for plans, whose vectors are values. A multi-vector field 's model gives vectors , a vector a token of the text GET /v1/models {dir, fetches, models: [{name, dim, hosted, what, ready, error, technique, precision, bytes, made_of, known, fields, job}]} : the embedding models a schema can name (those ChloeDB fetches, the hosted ones it knows, and the directories of --models ), and whether each would open here: fetched, its key set, its feature built; how each makes its vectors ( technique : static , transformer , late-interaction , image-text , sound-text , hosted , hash ), a static model's table ( precision : F32 , F16 , BF16 , I8 ), its size here, the model an int8 or F16 copy was made of, the embedded fields that name it ( [{table, field}] ), and a fetch or conversion asked for here ( job : action , state running , done or failed , error ); fetches is whether fields fetch the models they name (not with --no-fetch-models ); nothing is loaded or sent POST /v1/admin/models/fetch {model} fetches a model of the ones ChloeDB fetches ( fetch-model 's names; not a path, nor another Hugging Face repository) into --models , in the background: {model, state: \"running\"} , and GET /v1/models says how it went; then the fields that name it open it and fill in their rows' vectors POST /v1/admin/models/convert {model, to?} writes static model model 's table again in I8 (the default) or F16 , as model <model>-int8 (or -f16 ) beside it, in the background, as quantize-model does: {model, from, state} . A name in --models , never a path; 409 when the copy is there already POST /v1/admin/slices , GET /v1/slices/TABLE/... {text, lat, lon, lat0, lon0, tile_m?} (geo), {vector, clusters?, codes?} (vector), or {table} to rebuild a table's slice for devices to search near their user or their user's taste, built into --slices DIR and served with its status (fresh or stale) and the devices' counts; --slices-rebuild-s N rebuilds stale slices GET /v1/media/<sha256> the bytes of the file a row's media value names, by the file's SHA-256, read as writes read files (the media store, or a place --media-fetch lists) and checked against the hash, so a UI can show it; only files that rows name, 404 otherwise; a coordinator refuses (400): ask a shard server. The type is the value's for a PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP or AVIF image, a sound or a video; a PDF keeps its type but is a download ( Content-Disposition: attachment ), and any other file (HTML, SVG, XML, JavaScript, text, ...) is application/octet-stream , a download. Every answer has X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff and Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox , so a stored file never runs as a page of the server's origin POST /v1/term_stats {sql, params?, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?} BM25 statistics and a snapshot lease for the query round that follows (it lasts for the deadline and a second), for a coordinator POST /v1/classify {name, text, k?} the classes of a text by a classifier CREATE CLASSIFIER made: label ( null below its min_score ), score , top (the k best, 3 by default, as [label, probability] ), classifier , version , table , field , labels_from ; the text embedded by the model of the field it was trained on POST /v1/understand {table, field, text, mode?, fields?, weight?} what a search's words say for the table's keyword and numeric fields, as bm25(.., understand => ..) takes them: spans (the words, the values each names, their share of the rows that say the words), dropped , ranges , ranked (the text left to rank), filters , boosts and the plan line ; a coordinator reads its shards' values POST /v1/correct {table, field, text} \"did you mean\" : {text, changed, words} , the words in no row taken for typos of the field's terms; a coordinator counts by its shards' term statistics POST /v1/highlight {table, field, query, texts, size?, pre?, post?} {texts, took_ms} : each text with what a search query of the TEXT field matches marked, as the field's analyzer and synonyms read it (stems, synonyms, typos, prefixes, phrases, and code such as C++ where its characters are), cut to the stretch of about size characters with the most matches (160; 0 : the whole text), HTML-escaped between <mark> tags, or between pre and post as given; at most 10,000 texts a request; a read key's route POST /v1/sid/next {field, prefixes, where?, params?, table?, timeout_ms?, min_seq_no?, min_seq_nos?} the codes that can follow each prefix of a semantic-ID field among the rows where matches: {next: [{prefix, next: [{code, count}]}], took_ms} POST /v1/snapshot a snapshot lease for 60 s: {snapshot, txn, ttl_s} (at most 10,000 are held at once; more get 429) POST /v1/agent {question, model?, effort?, api_key?, max_steps?} an answer and the queries it ran POST /v1/judge {query, keys, table?, fields?, model?, api_key?} {grades: [{key, grade, error?}], model, prompt, fields, usage, took_ms} : each row graded 0 to 3 by a Claude model, one request a row ( relevance judgments ); a write key POST /v1/mcp a JSON-RPC message or a batch the Model Context Protocol : tools for an agent, each a route of this table called with the caller's key; 202 for a notification POST /v1/admin/checkpoint {name, layout?, link_from?} writes a checkpoint named name in --backup-dir , with the cluster map and API keys beside it ( backups ): {checkpoint: {name, files, txn, tables, segments, segments_linked, live_docs, bytes_written, seconds, slots?, keys?}} ; on a coordinator, one of every shard, each on its primary: {name, slot_epoch, shards: [{shard, url, short, backup_dir, checkpoint}]} POST /v1/admin/check checks every byte against its checksums (writes wait meanwhile): {ok: true} , or the damage POST /v1/admin/scrub {rate_mib_s?} reads every segment against its checksum while writes go on ( scrubbing ): {scrub: {txn, segments, bytes, damaged, seconds}} ; 409 while one runs POST /v1/admin/vacuum returns free pages to the file system: {bytes_freed} POST /v1/admin/verify {timeout_ms?} on a primary: compares each replica's rows with the primary's now ( replication ), waiting up to timeout_ms (60 s): {replicas: [{url, in_sync, compared, seq_no, differing_slots, first_differing_slot?, error?}]} ; 409 elsewhere POST /v1/admin/promote {replicas?, write_acks?, force?} on a replica: makes it the primary of a new term once a majority of its shard's copies vote for it ( failover ), shipping to replicas (gRPC URLs; none: the shard's other copies, or without a cluster map the replica's --replicas ; with a key, only the copies it knows) under write_acks ( all ): {term, seq_no, replicas: [{url, role, term, seq_no} or {url, error}], votes} ; 409 while a copy holds a later transaction or a newer term, or when it is not elected (unless force ), and on a server that is not a replica GET /v1/synonyms , PUT /v1/synonyms {text} for PUT the server's synonym list: {text, rules, file, tables} , tables each table's own list ( CREATE SYNONYMS ON table AS '...' : [{table, text, rules, version}] ) POST /v1/synonyms/mine {table, field, vector? or log?, min_cos?, per_word?, max_words?, min_df?, window?, min_support?} synonyms of a text field mined from the vectors of its words (by the model of vector field vector ) or from a table of searches ( log : context , ts , text ): {proposals: [{from, to, rule, score, from_count, to_count}], rules, rows, words, took_ms} , proposed, never applied ( mined synonyms ); experimental; an admin's, within bounds whoever asks ( max_rows 200,000, max_words 5,000, min_cos 0.3 or more, per_word 10); on a server of its own, not a coordinator GET /v1/schema?table=T {table, schema, short, primary_key, shard_by, embeddings, derived} of the first table, or of T GET /v1/tables/T/paths?field=F&path=P&where=W the paths a JSON field F of table T holds (those at and below P , in the rows where keeps), from its index: {table, field, rows, paths: [{path, keys, rows, types: {string, number, bool}, distinct, min?, max?, values?: [{value, rows}]}], truncated, took_ms} ; 400 for a field that is not an indexed JSON field; a coordinator sums its shards' GET /v1/tables {tables: [...]} : every table's schema ( short and schema ), the CREATE TABLE statement that makes it ( sql ) and its rank profiles ( profiles ), the first first GET /v1/health {status, docs, segments, ...} ( docs : the default table's rows), with 503 when the status is down or failing ( health ) GET /livez , /readyz {status} , with 503 when the server must be restarted; /readyz also while it drains before it stops ( health ) GET /v1/index?table=T&sample=N&seed=S&terms=B&top=K the index inspector 's statistics (admin scope) of table T (the first without one): on a server its own, on a coordinator each replica's, each shard's and the cluster's POST /v1/index/reads/reset the inspector's read counts start again from zero, on this server only (admin scope) GET /v1/stats , /v1/config JSON GET /metrics Prometheus text GET / , /classic , /ui/* the command center , the query console and their files /v1/write takes a batch of operations, applied in one transaction: An operation writes the first table (on a database made with no tables, the first one people made, above ) unless it names another ( \"table\" ), and one batch may write several tables in its transaction. A \"table\" beside \"ops\" is the table of the operations that name none: {\"table\": \"events\", \"ops\": [...]} . The body takes ops , table , strict and idempotency_key , and any other field is refused (400) rather than ignored. Changed: a top-level \"table\" was ignored before, and its rows went to the first table. On a coordinator, an operation by key ( delete , set , add ) on a table placed by a column ( tables on shards ) gives that column's value as \"routing\" , since the key alone does not say which shard holds the row. A compound query ( WITH , a subquery, a join, SELECT DISTINCT ) runs as its plan: operators over single-table queries, all on one snapshot, on the engine CHLOEDB_ENGINE chooses ( above ). On a coordinator they run there, and each single-table query goes to the shards as its plan (typed from protocol 2, JSON before; see gRPC ). From protocol 10, on DataFusion, the shards answer a single-table query whose rows the coordinator puts one after another (one without an order, groups or scores, as a side of a join that reads a table whole) with Arrow columns rather than rows. Its plan lines list the single-table queries that ran. A coordinator has no synonym list of its own: start each shard with --synonyms FILE . A table's own list ( CREATE SYNONYMS ON table ) is a stored function, which a coordinator writes on every shard. set and add change a numeric field ( INT , FLOAT , BOOL , TIMESTAMP ) in place, without rewriting the row; set of any other single-valued field ( {\"op\": \"set\", \"key\": \"p1\", \"field\": \"status\", \"value\": \"SOLD\"} ) writes the row again with the value, keeping its vectors, so a field that a model embeds or another field is derived from is refused (an UPDATE or an upsert makes those again); upsert_if_newer writes the row only if its version field is higher than the stored row's, so a feed replayed out of order keeps the newest. An upsert writes the whole row, as INSERT OR REPLACE does: a field its doc leaves out is NULL (or its default) in the row it replaces, not the value the row had. When one does that, the answer says so in warnings (\"an upsert writes the whole row: 1 row was replaced, and each takes NULL (or the field's default) in the fields its upsert left out (name, color of p) ...\"); to change some fields and keep the others, use set (one field an operation) or SQL UPDATE . Operations see the ones before them in the batch: an add after an upsert of the same key adds to the upserted value. An add to an INT field takes an integer and fails on overflow (exact over the whole 64-bit range); one to a FLOAT field must stay finite; a missing value counts as 0. An add with a \"doc\" creates the row when no row has its key: the row is the doc, its field plus the value (a missing value counting as 0), and when the row exists the value is added to it as before. So a counter, or the weight of an edge in an edge table , starts with its first event and needs no write beforehand: {\"op\": \"add\", \"table\": \"cooc\", \"field\": \"w\", \"value\": 1, \"doc\": {\"id\": \"i1>i7\", \"src\": \"i1\", \"dst\": \"i7\"}} . The key is the doc's (a \"key\" that differs from it is refused). With --token , --cluster-token or an API key, every route except the UI's ( / , /classic , /ui/* ), /v1/health , /livez and /readyz needs Authorization: Bearer <token or key> ; the UI asks for it. Without a credential it takes (none, or a wrong one), every server answers /v1/health with {status} alone, since its full answer names its tables and their rows, and a coordinator's its copies' internal addresses; a key limited to tables sees its own tables there."],
["server.html#when-a-write-fails","chloedb-server","When a write fails","An answer of 200 means the batch is durable. On one server an error means that nothing of it was written, so a client can retry on an error. A 429 refuses it for the server's load before any work, with Retry-After saying when to send it again ( streams ). When an answer is lost (a timeout, a dropped connection, a coordinator whose other replica failed), send the retry with the same idempotency_key (or an Idempotency-Key header), 1 to 256 characters: a server that already applied that batch answers with its first written instead of applying it again, so an add counts once and an insert does not fail as a duplicate of itself. The same key with other operations is refused (422), and while the first attempt still runs a retry gets 409. A server remembers keys for an hour, or for --idempotency-ttl-s seconds (at least 60): a retry later than that is a new write, and is applied again. It records the key of each write that wrote a row in the write's own transaction, as a row of _sources named _strata/idempotency <key> that its lists of sources leave out, so the copy promoted when a primary fails before answering knows the key, and so does a server restarted since. It also keeps the latest 100,000 keys in memory, with their whole answer, and forgets them all when its role changes (promoted, elected, stepped down, configured): a primary that steps down may be sent a copy of its new primary that lacks a write it answered, and its database's row, which goes with the write, answers a retry from then. Memory is not what keeps a write from being applied twice: the writer looks each key's row up before it applies a write, so a retry that memory no longer knows while its first attempt is still queued (the oldest keys are forgotten, and every key at a change of role) is answered from the first, unapplied. A retry answered from the row gets the rows the write wrote and the server's last seq_no , which follows the write's. Rows older than the keys' lifetime are deleted every minute. A coordinator passes the key to each shard as key/shard , and gives every write and statement that has none a key of its own ( auto-... ) before it sends it anywhere: when a shard's primary does not answer and the coordinator sends the write again to the primary that replaced it, the new primary, which may hold the write already, answers it as before rather than apply it twice. A shard of protocol 19 says whether a write it refused was applied as a code (gRPC metadata x-chloedb-write : not-applied or in-doubt ), which the coordinator reads to decide whether to send it again; an older shard's message is read as before. Over gRPC, WriteRequest.idempotency_key does the same. /v1/sql takes one too (in the body, or the header), for INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE : the same statement with the same parameters sent again answers its first written . A write across shards that fails partway. Through a coordinator, a write whose rows are on several shards is one transaction on each of them, not one transaction over all. Before it sends a write of operations that spans shards, the coordinator asks each shard to check its part without writing it ( WriteRequest.validate , protocol 19): an insert of a key that exists, an if_seq_no that does not match, a type or schema error, a NOT NULL column left empty. When one refuses, nothing is written on any shard, and the error says which operation of the request failed and why ( nothing was written: shard 1 refused operation 3 of the request (...) ). A part whose key a shard recorded already passes the check, so a retry with the key still finishes a write that failed partway. What remains: nothing is held between the check and the write. Another client's write that lands in between (it inserts the same key, or changes the row an if_seq_no names) can still make one shard refuse its part after others applied theirs, and so can a shard that stops, a shard of an older protocol (which is not asked), a shard that does not answer the check, one whose writer is still busy after 100 ms or one that already holds a newer slot map (each of which the coordinator sends its part unchecked); UPDATE and DELETE statements are not checked. Such a write fails partway, as before, and a deterministic refusal then cannot be finished by sending it again: read the rows back and repair them. Any shard can fail (it stopped, or its primary is being replaced) after others committed their part, which they keep. The error says so: \"partial\": true , shards with each shard's rows or error , and an idempotency_key , the client's or one the coordinator gave the write ( auto-... , for any write of several operations, or statement, without one). Send the same request again with that key, within the hour (or --idempotency-ttl-s ) the shards keep keys for: the shards that took their part answer it as before, and the others apply theirs, so every row is written once. A shard asked again after slots moved gets other operations under its key, which it refuses (422). The coordinator's keys, one for each write and statement, are kept as its clients' are, each a row of _sources for the keys' lifetime."],
["server.html#writes-from-many-clients","chloedb-server","Writes from many clients","A server has one writer per database, a thread that applies write requests in the order they arrive. Requests that arrive while it commits wait, and the next transaction takes them all, up to 4,096 operations: one commit and one disk flush for the group, each request answered on its own. When a group's transaction fails, each of its requests runs again alone, so only one at fault fails. /v1/write , gRPC Write and stream batches group with each other. SQL INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE statements group with each other when no two can touch the same row: a statement finds its rows in the last committed version, which does not show its transaction's own changes, so two UPDATE s of one row in one transaction would lose one's change. An INSERT names its rows' keys, and an UPDATE or DELETE names them when its WHERE pins the key ( id = :k , id IN (...) , an OR of those, or an AND with one): those group when their keys differ. Any other statement has its table to itself in a group, and statements of other tables join it. The embedded database does the same for threads that write at once ( Db::write_ops and SQL writes, which the Python, JavaScript and Java packages use). With 64 clients each sending single-row SQL INSERT s or UPDATE s by key, about 6 statements shared each commit and the server answered 4,900 to 5,500 a second, against 1,600 to 1,800 when each committed alone, with the median latency down from 34–38 ms to 11–12 ms (4 cores shared with the clients; results )."],
["server.html#rows-by-key","chloedb-server","Rows by key","A table with a primary key is also a key-value store. GET /v1/kv/TABLE/KEY reads one row by its key without SQL, POST /v1/kv/get reads up to 10,000 at once (an MGET), and PUT and DELETE on /v1/kv/TABLE/KEY write and delete one; gRPC has Get for the reads. A row comes back as SELECT * gives it (the stored columns but vectors, typed as a query types them), with the sequence number of the write that last changed it on a server started with --seq-no , which a PUT or DELETE can pass back as if_seq_no ( conditional writes ). An INT key in a path is its digits. columns reads only the columns named, as a ranker reading a few features of a thousand candidates wants: when each of them is a single-valued KEYWORD , INT or FLOAT , the rows are read from those columns and the doc store is not touched. A coordinator reads the shards' whole rows and keeps the columns. A read by key probes each segment's key index, newest first, and reads the rows the memtable holds as they were written: it parses no SQL and builds nothing, so a read after each write costs what any read does. Segments of more than 4,096 keys keep a Bloom filter of their keys in memory (built from the key index on the first lookup; nothing on disk changes), so a segment that lacks the key mostly answers from one cache line. A write commits without indexing the memtable's newest chunk, which a query that needs its indexes builds first, once per change. A row whose expire_by time has passed is none. Through a coordinator, each key is asked of the shard its slot is on, all of a shard's keys in one call (every shard for a table placed by shard_by ). A query whose condition pins the key, or the shard_by column, to values ( WHERE id = 'u7' , id IN (...) , usr = 'u3' AND ts > ... ) asks only the shards those values are on, as writes are routed, and says so in its plan ( routed by key: 1 of 4 shards ); a query ranked by BM25 still asks every shard, for the collection's statistics."],
["server.html#write-receipts","chloedb-server","Write receipts","Every answer of /v1/write , /v1/kv and /v1/import carries a receipt beside written : received (the operations or rows sent), inserted (no row had the key), replaced (a document written over a row that was there), updated (a set or an add of a row that was there), deleted , collapsed (a document whose key an earlier operation of the same request wrote: a key twice in one batch leaves one row, and written counts it once) and not_found (a set , add or delete of a key no row has). Through a coordinator per_shard gives each shard's rows and counts. A shard from before protocol 21 does not count them, and its counts are null . A SQL statement's answer has no receipt. With \"strict\": true in the body of /v1/write (or ?strict=true on /v1/import ), any operation that would replace a row, write a key an earlier one wrote, or find no row is refused, and nothing is written: 409 for a row there or a key twice, 404 for a key not found. A strict import reads every row first and asks for their keys before it writes, and writes them as inserts, so a row written meanwhile fails it too. A strict write through a coordinator goes only to shards of protocol 21. An operation can ask the same alone: \"strict\": true on it, or \"if_missing\": \"error\" (or \"skip\" ) on a set , add or delete ."],
["server.html#conditional-writes","chloedb-server","Conditional writes","A server started with --seq-no numbers its database's writes ( Database::start_seq_no ): every write transaction gets the next sequence number, and each row keeps the number of the write that last changed it. It cannot be undone, and versions of ChloeDB from before it refuse to open the database rather than lose the numbers. The answer to a write that changed something carries its number, {\"written\": 1, \"seq_no\": 42} ( WriteResponse.seq_no over gRPC); requests the writer commits together share one, and a retry with an idempotency key gets the first attempt's, or, answered from the key's row, the server's last, which follows it. SELECT id, seq_no() FROM t reads each row's number. Rows written before numbering started, and rows loaded in bulk ( --demo , an import into an empty table), have 0. An operation with \"if_seq_no\": n applies only while its row's number is n, so a client that read a row can write it back without losing a write made in between: Every operation but insert , which fails if the row exists, takes it. When the row has another number, has no row, or was written by an earlier operation of the same request, the request writes nothing and gets 409 ( FailedPrecondition over gRPC), naming the key and its number. Of several requests writing a row with the same if_seq_no , exactly one applies. Without --seq-no , if_seq_no and seq_no() are refused."],
["server.html#streams","chloedb-server","Streams","A stream is a long write: POST /v1/stream takes operations as NDJSON ( application/x-ndjson ) for as long as the client sends them, and writes them in batches as they arrive: the server reads ahead while a batch commits, and the next batch takes the lines that came meanwhile, up to 4,096 operations (one line at a time while the client trickles, so a slow stream waits for no batch to fill). Each batch is one transaction; the next is applied once it has committed, and every batch is answered by a line of the answer, which streams back as NDJSON too; the line's offsets give the last offset of each source in the batch, which a consumer may commit to its log once the line arrives. The first batch that fails ends the stream with an {\"error\": ..., \"status\": ..., \"lines\": n} line, nothing after its lines lines applied. gRPC WriteStream does the same with a message per batch: the first that fails ends the stream with its error. An operation from an event stream (Kafka, Kinesis, a log) names its event's place in it: \"source\": {\"name\": \"clicks/0\", \"offset\": 81234, \"ts\": \"2026-10-30T06:00:01.250Z\"} , ts being when the event happened (ISO 8601 text, or milliseconds since the epoch, as Kafka gives them). A server keeps each source's last offset in a system table, _sources , in the same transaction as the rows: committed with them, or not at all. An operation whose offset its source has reached already is a replay's, and is skipped (and counted in skipped ). So a consumer that resumes from GET /v1/sources/<name> plus one, or from anywhere before it, applies each event once, every operation it makes included: an add to a counter counts once, and an insert does not fail as a duplicate. The same holds for /v1/write , whose operations may carry sources too. Name a source per partition ( clicks/0 , clicks/1 ), since offsets are per partition. The Python package's Kafka consumer works this way, and so does the server's own Kafka connector , which reads topics with no consumer of yours. So does chloedb-server follow-postgres , which follows a Postgres database's tables by logical replication, each transaction's operations carrying where it ends in Postgres's log ( Following Postgres ). A line is an operation, or one event's operations as {\"ops\": [...], \"source\": {...}} (the source going to each operation that names none), which a batch never splits: since an offset reached marks a replay, an event's operations must commit together. Along a stream a source's offsets increase from line to line; a line whose offset does not pass the one before it ends the stream (400), as it would otherwise be skipped. An {\"op\": \"source\", \"source\": {...}} operation moves a source's offset without writing a row (for events a consumer filters out). Through a coordinator, each batch goes to the shards its rows are on, and every shard keeps the batch's offsets, whether or not the batch gave it rows. GET /v1/sources there answers the least offset over the shards, where a stream resumes: each shard skips the events it applied already. A source some shard does not have is not listed (its first batch never reached that shard), so a stream of it starts over. Offsets move with their slots ( moving slots , from protocol 16). A shard's offset says which events its own slots hold, and slots that arrive from another shard come with that shard's events of them, which can stop at another offset: a batch that a move's flip catches in flight has committed on the shard the slots go to, for its own rows, and is held on the one they leave, for the moving ones, which the coordinator then sends to their new shard; and a shard added after a stream's last batch has no offset of it at all. So the flip carries each source's offset of the moving slots on the shard they leave, read while their writes are held, and the shard they go to checks the events of those slots against it, until a batch of the source routed by the new map passes it, whatever parts of batches routed by the old map reach it after the flip (it keeps them in _sources as _strata/arrived rows). A shard that held no slot takes them as its own offsets. Each event is then applied once across a move: the moved part of a batch caught in flight is applied when it comes again, and a replay from the start applies nothing twice. A shard answers each source's least offset over its slots, so GET /v1/sources stays where a stream resumes. Moves flipped while a server spoke a protocol before 16 carry no offsets, and the slots take their new shard's. Shards must speak protocol 6 ( gRPC ) for writes with sources; an older one is refused by name. Replicas keep their primary's offsets ( replication ), and a replica promoted to primary resumes the stream where the old primary stopped. A stream does not wait in the writer's queue: while the database is behind on flushing its memtable to disk (past twice memtable_max_docs , while the flusher flushes), or the queue is full, it reads nothing more from its client until it catches up, so TCP or HTTP/2 flow control slows the producer. A plain write then waits up to a second, and is refused with 429 and Retry-After: 1 , before any of it is applied; the Python and JavaScript clients send it again after that, for up to 10 seconds. A coordinator sends a shard its part of a write or of a statement again when the shard refused it with 429 (behind on its flushes, as a shard is while its first flushes build its indexes, its write queue full, or every core embedding), every 200 ms for up to 10 seconds, so its client is answered 429 only when a shard stays busy that long; a stream's batch then goes again a second later. chloedb_stream_lag_seconds is a histogram of the time from each event's ts to the moment its rows were visible."],
["server.html#opentelemetry-logs","chloedb-server","OpenTelemetry logs","A server takes logs as OpenTelemetry 's exporters send them, so a Collector, or an application's SDK, writes its logs into ChloeDB with no plugin. POST /v1/logs is OTLP/HTTP: an ExportLogsServiceRequest in protobuf ( application/x-protobuf ) or in OTLP's JSON ( application/json ), gzip-compressed ( Content-Encoding: gzip ) or not, answered in the request's encoding. The gRPC port serves OTLP/gRPC, opentelemetry.proto.collector.logs.v1.LogsService/Export , beside ChloeDB's own service, gzip-compressed or not. Either takes a key that may write ( Authorization: Bearer ... , as an exporter's headers set it). A Collector's exporters: The records become rows of table otel_logs (or another, named by ?table= or the header x-chloedb-table , over gRPC its metadata), which the first request creates if it is not there: ts is the record's time, or when it was observed if it has none ( observed_ts ), or when the server took it if it has neither. severity is its severity text, or the name of its number ( severity_number : ERROR for 17 to 20, WARN for 13 to 16, ...). service and host are the resource's service.name and host.name ; resource holds its other attributes. body is a string body as it is, another kind of body as its JSON text. trace_id and span_id are hex; scope is the instrumentation scope's name. attributes holds the record's attributes, its event name as event_name , and bytes as base64, in a JSON field whose paths filters read from its index. batch is a random number per request: through a coordinator it places the table's rows ( shard_by ), so that a request's rows go to one shard and requests spread over all of them. A log has no key, and the table has none. --otlp-retain PERIOD ( '30 days' , '12h' ) makes the table with that retention ( retain = '30 days' ), so its rows older than that are dropped, a window of segments at a time ( tables ); without it they are kept. A table made beforehand takes the columns of these that it has, by name, so make it first for another retention, more columns indexed or fewer kept. Its doc store is compressed with zstd : on four of Loghub's logs sent this way, otel_logs took 30 to 43 bytes a line (it has no key, and its positions are packed), and OpenSearch with its best compression 55 to 83 ( results ). The rows are searched as any are: The answer is OTLP's: an empty ExportLogsServiceResponse when every record was written, and partial_success with rejected_log_records and the first one's reason when the table refused some (attributes nested more than 16 levels, say), the others written. A body that is not OTLP is answered 400, another content type 415, and a server behind on its writes 429 or 503 with Retry-After , which exporters wait out and send again; a failure is OTLP's Status in the request's encoding (over gRPC, the call's status). A call is counted in the metrics, and audited when refused, as LogsExport ."],
["server.html#connectors","chloedb-server","Connectors","A connector keeps another system's data coming into a table: it pulls Prometheus rollups, Jira issues, ServiceNow records, Oxidized configs or any REST API that answers JSON on a schedule, or takes what a system pushes (an email, Alertmanager's webhook, any JSON), or reads Kafka topics while it is on (each event written once, its offset kept with its row), maps each record to a row and upserts it. Connectors are rows of chloedb_connectors , each writes with a key limited to its target table, their secrets stay in <db>.connectors.json ( --connector-secrets ), and the command center's Data > Connectors page shows their health. A server of its own or a coordinator runs the pulls ( --connectors off turns them off). POST /v1/admin/connectors , GET /v1/admin/connectors , POST /v1/connectors/NAME/push : Connectors ."],
["server.html#backups","chloedb-server","Backups","Copying the database's files while the server runs is not a backup: a commit reuses pages an older version freed, and the directory layout deletes files a version stops naming, so a copy ( cp , rsync, a disk snapshot, Time Machine) can pair one version's manifest with another's pages. A checkpoint is the safe copy. POST /v1/admin/checkpoint flushes the memtable, pins the version that holds every committed write and copies it into --backup-dir/<name> while queries and writes go on (they wait only for the flush). The checkpoint is itself a database, checked against its checksums before it gets its name. Beside it the server writes what a restore needs besides the rows, under the names a server serving the checkpoint with --db reads: the cluster map a shard holds, <name>.slots , and the API keys, <name>.keys.json (the keys' names, scopes and the SHA-256 of their secrets, readable by the server's user only). A checkpoint written while the cluster map changed (a slot move's flip) is not kept, and the answer says to write it again. Restore with chloedb-server restore . The answer, {\"checkpoint\": {...}} , counts what was copied: txn , tables , segments , segments_linked , live_docs , bytes_written and seconds ; name and the files written; and on a shard of a cluster slots (its shard , the map's epoch and shards ), and keys , how many, when it has keys. layout is file (default: one file) or dir (a directory of segment files, as --directory creates). Segments are immutable and keep their ids in a checkpoint, and its manifest and delete sets are named by the commit it holds, so two directory checkpoints' files share a name only when they hold the same bytes. That makes them incremental off-site backups: sync each one into the same place, CURRENT last so that the place always names a complete version, and only the files new since the last sync travel: A database opened from a copy of that place deletes the files its version does not name. A database restored from a checkpoint numbers its next commits as the original did after that checkpoint, so sync its own checkpoints into a new place. A name is letters, digits, . , _ and - ; an existing name is not overwritten. A coordinator has no database of its own: POST /v1/admin/checkpoint there has the copy taking each shard's writes (its primary) write the checkpoint into its own --backup-dir , every shard at once, and answers each shard's checkpoint with its copy and backup folder, or an error naming the shards that did not write it (those that did keep theirs: write the next under another name). Its checkpoints are of one cluster map, which the answer's slot_epoch names; each shard's is of its own moment, within the time the calls take. To have one copy write one, ask the coordinator POST /v1/admin/copies/checkpoint {\"url\": \"<the copy's gRPC URL>\", \"name\": \"...\"} , which answers the checkpoint and the node that wrote it. A checkpoint does not carry a copy's part in replication ( DB.replication ): a copy restored from one starts in the part its flags give it ( restoring a checkpoint ). The command center's Settings > Upkeep writes one of every shard ( Write a checkpoint of every shard ), and a copy's menu on Cluster writes one of that copy; operate a cluster writes them on one machine. The chloedb shell does the same offline with .checkpoint DEST [file|dir] [EARLIER] , .check and .vacuum . Not yet: point-in-time recovery between checkpoints, which needs the write-ahead log archived instead of truncated at each flush. Restoring a checkpoint chloedb-server restore --from BACKUPS/NAME --db PATH puts a checkpoint in place of a stopped server's database, with the cluster map and keys kept beside it. It copies the checkpoint beside the database, opens the copy and checks every checksum, and only then changes anything: the database's own files (the database, its -wal , -readers and -merging , its part in replication, PATH.replication , and its map, PATH.slots ) move into PATH.before-restore-<time>/ , the copy takes their place, the checkpoint's map goes beside it, and its keys are merged into the server's keys file ( PATH.keys.json , or --keys FILE ) as the servers of a cluster merge keys: a key revoked in that file since the checkpoint stays revoked. A database that does not exist yet (a new machine) is made from the checkpoint the same way. It refuses while a process has the database open, and refuses a checkpoint of another shard than the one the database's kept map names ( --force restores it all the same). Then start the server with the flags it had: a copy of a replicated shard starts in the part its flags give it ( --replicas on the shard's primary, --replica on the others), its old part having moved aside with the old files. To restore a sharded cluster, a shard, or one copy, follow the runbook Restore from checkpoints . What a checkpoint does not carry: the server's other files beside its database (the log store, DB.connectors.json , the clouds' credentials, the hub's book), which stay as they are on the machine, and the coordinator's own state (its alert rules' history, audit log and log store)."],
["server.html#cold-segments","chloedb-server","Cold segments","With --cold-store , a database in the directory layout keeps its older segments on object storage and reads them through a local cache ( how ). --cold-keep-local-gb says how much stays local: the newest segments up to that size; every 30 s, the older ones of at least --cold-min-segment-mb are uploaded, oldest first, and their local files go. Their headers and keys stay in the cache, so writes and deletes by key never wait on the store; a query fetches the parts it reads, checks each against its checksum and keeps it in the cache, up to --cold-cache-gb , past which the least recently read segments lose theirs. The store is configured as imports from S3 are, by environment ( AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID , AWS_ENDPOINT_URL for another S3-compatible store, ...). Once a segment is cold, the database opens only with its store. /metrics counts the cold segments and their bytes, the cache's bytes and limit, fetches, fetched bytes, evictions and offloads ( chloedb_cold_* ). A checkpoint fetches the cold segments whole: it is a database of local files that opens without the store."],
["server.html#security","chloedb-server","Security","A token, or loopback only. --token (or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN ) guards the HTTP API, but for the UI's files and the probes ( /livez , /readyz , /v1/health ), and every gRPC call, compared in constant time. A server with no token, no cluster token and no API key listens only on loopback addresses: an address reachable from elsewhere ( 0.0.0.0 , a LAN address) is refused at start unless --no-token says to serve it open; a cluster token or a key closes it as a token does. The token is not printed in the log; open the UI and give it in its sign-in dialog. /?token=… signs in too, and the page drops it from its address, but the first address stays in the browser's history and in the log of any proxy on the way. The security model says what each credential reaches and what is left to the operator. Browsers on other sites. A server on your laptop is reachable by every web page you visit, through your browser. It answers only to the host names localhost , 127.0.0.1 and ::1 when it listens on loopback (add names with --allow-host ), so a page that points its own name at 127.0.0.1 (DNS rebinding) gets 403. Any request but GET and HEAD (reads by POST /v1/query included) that carries an Origin other than the server's own gets 403, and the server sends no CORS headers (a preflight OPTIONS gets 405, or 401 on a server with a token: why a page cannot call chloedb-server ); /v1/import refuses the content types a page may send without asking first ( text/plain , forms, none). The UI and the clients here send their own origin or no origin, and proper content types. TLS. --tls-cert FILE --tls-key FILE (PEM) serve both HTTP and gRPC over TLS. A coordinator reaches https:// shards over TLS, trusting the public roots and --shard-ca FILE , and sends --cluster-token (by default its own --token ) with every call. Fetches by writes. A write to a media field can name a URL or a file for the server to read; only what --media-fetch lists is read, redirects included, checked against the host the HTTP client connects to, and a URL parsers could read differently is refused. Stored files are served inert. GET /v1/media/<sha256> answers a stored file with its type only for images, sounds and videos a browser shows, and anything else as a download of application/octet-stream (a PDF keeps its type), with nosniff and a sandboxing Content-Security-Policy : a writer's HTML or SVG never runs on the server's origin. Models are names. A query's model ( llm() , cross_encoder() , a GBDT ranker) is a name in --models , or a provider's model: letters, digits, _ , - and . , two such names joined by / at most, never an absolute path or .. . An endpoint.json 's key_env is a provider's key variable or a CHLOEDB_MODEL_KEY_* one, so no endpoint can be given another of the server's variables. API keys with scopes. Give clients keys, not the token. A key is sk_<id>_<secret> , sent as the token is ( Authorization: Bearer ... ), with a scope (or, for an agent, a role: ops or propose , agents and administration ): feed (every table's feed , with every row as it is written, and POST /v1/feed/keys , the keys and digests of any table's rows; health and /v1/whoami : for a cluster that follows this one), read (queries, explain, embed, schemas, health, metrics and the series), write (that, and rows: /v1/write , /v1/import , /v1/stream , OpenTelemetry logs , INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE , logged queries, the agent, which spends the server's API key), or admin (that, and changing the server: tables and rank profiles, synonyms, checkpoints, vacuum, check, verify, promotion, keys, alert rules, the log filter, the profiling settings; seeing the running and latest queries, the logs and the audit log, which show other clients' SQL; cancelling queries; /v1/export ). A request beyond its key's scope gets 403 (gRPC: PERMISSION_DENIED ). Over HTTP and gRPC alike, what a request does is checked as well as its route: a SQL statement by what it is (a table's or rank profile's CREATE or DROP needs admin , through gRPC Write too), a write plan needs write , a logged query write (and admin for the first one to a log table, which creates it), a SQLite import write (and admin when it creates tables, refused before anything is written). Over gRPC a read key may query, a write key write rows and an admin key search the log store ( Logs ) or take a profile ( Profile ), but no key, an admin key included, may make the calls between the servers of a cluster ( Replicate , Restore , Verify , Slots , Configure , the slot moves, Keys , Feed ): only the cluster's token, which a coordinator and a primary send, may. Make keys in the UI ( Access ) or with POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"dashboards\", \"scope\": \"read\", \"expires_days\": 90} , which answers the secret once (a field it does not know is refused with a 400 that names it, so a misspelt tables or preset makes no key rather than one of every table); GET /v1/admin/keys lists them (each with its owner , note and the address it was last used from, and the keys revoked, by whom and when), DELETE /v1/admin/keys/ID revokes one, and POST /v1/admin/keys/ID/rotate {\"grace_minutes\": 60} makes its successor, answering the new secret once, while the old one works for the grace period. The server keeps each key's name, scope and the SHA-256 of its secret, never the secret, in --keys FILE (default DB.keys.json beside the database, readable by its owner only; a coordinator keeps its keys in memory without --keys , and takes them from its shards when it starts). A server with no token, no cluster token and no key is open; its first key, which must be an admin key, closes it (the UI then uses that key). A server without a token keeps an admin key that never expires: it refuses to make its first admin key with expires_days , and to revoke its last admin key that never expires, since once the last admin key expired no one could manage it but by starting it again with --token . A server with keys may listen beyond loopback without a token. GET /v1/whoami says who a credential is. Keys limited to tables. A key may be limited to some tables, for an app that should touch its own and nothing else: POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"crawler\", \"tables\": {\"crawl_*\": \"admin\", \"docs\": \"read\"}} (or a list, [\"crawl_*=admin\", \"docs=read\"] ). Each grant names a table, a prefix and * , or * for every table (in any case), and a scope: read , write (rows), or admin ( CREATE , ALTER and DROP of those tables and their rank profiles). The key's scope is the most its grants give (no more than scope , when given), and it never has the server's own admin, whatever its grants: keys, shards, functions, settings, the logs and the audit log are refused (403). It may call only the routes that check the tables they touch, and every other route, gRPC and the OpenSearch listener refuse it (403, \"... is not for a key limited to tables\"): route what it checks /v1/health , /v1/cluster the tables it reads alone: each server's health counts their rows ( tables , rows , and docs , theirs too) and no other table's (a shard's or a copy's docs are left out) /v1/whoami , GET /v1/alerts nothing of a table; /v1/whoami shows the key's grants ( tables , \"limited\": true ; its scope is its tables', never the server's) GET /v1/tables , GET /v1/schema the tables it reads alone; /v1/schema with no table is the first table it may read (never a refusal of a table it did not name) POST /v1/query , /v1/sql , /v1/explain the statement's kind and every table it names, PACK n TOKENS too; a table there is none of is answered with the tables it reads alone ( no such table: a_ghost; the tables the key \"tenant-a\" sees are a_notes ), as every route's error is; a logged query ( \"log\" ) needs write on the log's table (admin to make it, on the first query logged) and read on the tables the query (and an interleaved one) reads; a shard's query is refused query parameters {\"embed\"} and {\"vector_of\"} read on the table whose model or row they use: named by table , else the table the statement queries ( embedded vector fields ) POST /v1/plan write on each table the plan writes, read on every table it reads, in subqueries and edge tables too; a sequence model's encode is refused /v1/write , /v1/kv/... , /v1/import the table of each operation (the server's first table for one that names none) POST /v1/embed , GET /v1/models read on the table whose model embeds (the first it reads, with none named); the models list the fields of the tables it reads alone /v1/keys/own its own keys (below) GET /v1/feed the feed of its tables ( below ) POST /v1/schema/check nothing: a schema checked, no table touched POST /v1/logs (OTLP) write on the logs' table ( ?table= or x-chloedb-table ; made on the first logs, as for a write key) POST /v1/rank_log/export , /v1/rank_log/interleaving read on the log's table and its events' GET /v1/apps , /v1/apps/<name>/... the listing; an app that checks its callers itself (its manifest's \"checks_callers\": true or \"limited_keys\": true : ChloeCtx, Sheets, the crawler and the app creator) gets the request with the key's grants in x-chloedb-tables and refuses what they do not allow; any other app refuses the key (403), unless the key was given the app ( apps , below) and reads every table, which then reads ( GET ) through it /v1/mcp refused, with a JSON-RPC error ( {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"error\": {\"code\": -32001, \"message\": ...}} , HTTP 403): the MCP tools take a key of every table It is refused the fleet's health, events, metrics and charts ( /v1/health/fleet , /v1/events , /metrics , /v1/timeseries ), /v1/stats , the queries' views and logs, and Tables and storage. A query's steps name a shard ( shard 0 query ) without the address of the copy that answered, for any key that is not the server's admin. The command center says what such a key cannot see on each page, and stops asking a route that refused it. Beyond its tables' routes, a key limited to tables may be given \"routes\": [\"fleet\"] ( GET of the fleet's health, events, metrics and charts) and \"apps\": [\"sheets\"] (apps it may read through, if it reads every table): POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"ops\", \"tables\": {\"logs_*\": \"write\"}, \"routes\": [\"fleet\"]} . They never widen what the key may do to a table. Presets make the keys of common jobs: POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"netops\", \"preset\": \"oncall\", \"prefix\": \"netops_*\"} , kept as the grants and allowance they expand to (a server that knows no presets reads such a key as one given them by hand, and one from before routes and apps reads it without them: it may do less there, never more). The key list and /v1/whoami show the preset's name ( \"preset\" ), and the command center's header shows it with the grants. preset grants ( prefix = team_* ) beyond its tables analyst * read, team_* admin Sheets: its own workbooks, whose queries read as the key may oncall team_* write (rows, OTLP logs), ctx_team_* write (its ChloeCtx spaces' notes), ctx_incidents read fleet : the fleet's health, events, metrics and charts ml-app team_* admin: logged queries and rank logs into its tables, plans, PACK , the models of its fields app-owner team_* admin ( app_* for the app creator's apps) the app creator: with app_* it builds, changes and publishes apps; with app_<id>_* it changes that one app An app that checks its callers runs a caller's statements as the caller. The app sends its own token with x-chloedb-as-tables set to the grants chloedb-server told it ( *=read,ana_*=admin , or *=read for a read key of every table): the server narrows the token to them, each no wider than the token itself, and checks the statement as it would the caller's key, routes included. It only narrows: a key already limited to tables that sends it (the key a server gives each app it starts ) keeps, on each table, the lower of its own scope and the caller's, and no table that either lacks, and the narrowed credential is never the cluster's root. Sheets runs its query blocks, copies of tables and sheets saved as tables this way, and the crawler its crawls. A statement is read with ChloeDB's own parser and checked by its kind and by every table it names ( FROM and JOIN , subqueries, WITH , the source of an INSERT ... SELECT , an edge table a walk follows); what its names cannot show is refused rather than allowed: a table function that is not a stored one (a stored one is checked by the tables its body reads, written out as the server runs it), a WITH table named as a table is, a name in parts, llm() (its second step is SQL a model writes), encode() , and a stored function when one of them calls llm() . Such a key with the admin scope on some tables may CREATE FUNCTION one no one has, or the same definition again (an app installs its functions at each start), when its body reads only tables the key may read and calls no model; it replaces and drops none, which stay the server's admin's, since a stored function is every client's. Such a key is never a server's first (it would not manage the server), and it rotates and is revoked as any key. The keys file keeps its digest after a mark ( t: ), so that a server from before limits, which would take it for a key of every table, never admits it, and a copy such a server wrote back without its tables admits no one until a merge brings them back. A server also makes a key of this kind for each app it starts itself ( Keys::app_key ): kept in memory only, never in the keys file nor given to other servers, made again at each start of the app, listed with \"ephemeral\": true and named app:<name> ; it does not close an open server. An operation with a \"source\" (a stream's offset, which belongs to the stream and not to any one table) is refused. A grant by a pattern ( * , rel* ) reads relevance_proposals but never writes it, since an admin applies what its rows say; only a grant that names the table writes it. - A budget of model calls per key. Each API key may make --model-calls-per-key-day model calls (10,000) a day (UTC) with the server's own credentials: /v1/agent and the on-call agent count a call a step, /v1/judge a call a row (and one for each follow-up), and a query's llm() a call a question it asks of a model, counted when it is asked (answers kept in memory are free). A call past it answers 429, before anything is sent, saying how many are left and when the count starts again. The --token , the cluster's token and an open server are the operator's own and not counted, nor is a caller that brings its own api_key . GET /v1/admin/model-budget answers {per_day, used: {key id: calls today}} . Counts are each process's, in memory: a restart, or each of two coordinators, counts on its own. Hosted embedding models (a field's model served elsewhere) are not counted yet. - Keys a key makes. A key with the admin scope on some tables may make keys within them, for what it serves: an app engine gives each of its apps a key of its own this way. POST /v1/keys/own {\"name\": \"app-x1y2z3\", \"tables\": {\"app_x1y2z3_*\": \"write\", \"sa_numbers\": \"read\"}, \"note\": \"...\"} answers the secret once; each grant must fall within a grant of the calling key with the admin scope (a prefix within its prefix, a name its prefix covers, * within * ), or it is refused (403). The key is named <caller's name>/<name> , owned by the caller, expires when the caller does, and opens nothing once the caller is revoked or has expired (revoking the caller revokes it too, and each revocation is kept); making it again by the same name revokes the one before. GET /v1/keys/own lists the caller's keys and DELETE /v1/keys/own/ID revokes one. A key so made makes none in turn, nor do the token, an open server, or an app's key in memory ( /v1/admin/keys is the token's); a caller makes at most 4,096. A key limited to tables reaches these routes. The keys file keeps the digest of such a key after a mark of its own ( d: ), so that a server from before such keys, which would not check its maker, never admits it, and a copy written back without its maker admits no one. /v1/whoami says a server makes them with auth.own_keys . - Audit log. Every change (a request that needs the admin scope and changes something), every note and every refused request (401, 403), over HTTP and gRPC ( method gRPC , route the method's name, or any for a call refused before its method is read: no credential, a wrong one, or a key limited to tables), is kept, the latest 2,000 in memory and every one appended to --audit-log FILE as JSON lines and, with a log store, to its table audit : when, kind ( change , note , refusal ), who (the key's name, token or open , and actor ), its scope, the route, the status, the time it took, the request id, the client's address over HTTP ( addr ; X-Forwarded-For only from the proxies --trust-proxy lists; a gRPC entry has none), the client ( x-chloedb-client ), the reason given ( x-chloedb-reason , URL-encoded), the object it changed with its value before and after , and what it did ( CREATE TABLE ... , created key ... , cancelled query 12 , SQLite import created tables ... , promote, shipping to ... ). A refused request whose token is a known key, revoked or expired, names that key. Reads are not audited, nor clients' writes of rows, by /v1/write , /v1/import or SQL ( INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE , however many rows they touch), as there would be too many; a write of rows from the command center ( x-chloedb-client: command-center ), and a query cancelled, are. GET /v1/admin/audit?kind=&from=&to=&who=&q=&limit= reads it (the UI's Access and audit page), from the store past what memory holds. Each coordinator keeps its own log; on a coordinator it answers every coordinator's entries, newest first, each naming the coordinator that kept it, and coordinators says which answered ( local=1 answers this one's only). GET /v1/admin/keys/clients?hours=24 (1 to 168) says which clients sent each key: by key id, each client's app, client, address, calls, failed calls, first and last call, counting every query that came with a key, not a sample; on a coordinator every coordinator's summed, each client naming the coordinators it went through. A key's rotation reads it for the clients that still have to move. - The cluster's token. The servers of a cluster call each other with --cluster-token ( $CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN ), which clients never hold: give every server of the cluster, coordinators included, the same one. A coordinator sends it to its shards and a primary to its replicas; the calls between servers take it and nothing else, so a client holding the --token or an admin key cannot ship writes, move slots or reconfigure a copy, nor make a server send the cluster's token to a server of its choosing: adding a shard or a copy, by its call or a plan's step, and a promotion that names copies outside its shard, need the cluster's token. --token stays an operator's credential for the HTTP and gRPC API, admin scope, and clients get API keys. A server without a cluster token takes its --token for both parts, as servers did before cluster tokens; it warns when the two are the same. - Keys across a cluster. The servers of a cluster hold the same keys. A coordinator merges its keys with every server of every shard, and a primary with its replicas, every 5 seconds and as soon as its keys change (a key made or revoked, or a merge that brought something new), over the internal call Keys . A merge keeps every key of either side, by id, and every revocation, which wins: a key revoked anywhere is revoked wherever it is merged, and a server that was down when it was revoked loses it at its first merge. So keys live on the shards, beside their databases ( DB.keys.json , which also lists the ids revoked): a key made on the coordinator works on every shard and every replica, a coordinator started again, or a second one, takes the cluster's keys from its shards before it serves, a key made on a shard reaches the coordinators, revoking a key on any coordinator ends it everywhere within a round, and a replica promoted to primary admits its primary's keys. GET /v1/admin/keys lists how the last merge went with each server ( peers : when, and the error of one that failed). A server with no token and no cluster token takes no keys from others, which would close it to the servers that gave them ( Keys answers FAILED_PRECONDITION ), and while it serves in a cluster (it holds a cluster map, or is a primary or a replica) it refuses to make a key, for the same reason: give the cluster a --cluster-token to share keys. A server from before Keys answers it UNIMPLEMENTED and keeps its own keys. - Sign in with Google. People sign in to the command center with their Google account, from any browser, a phone's too, and paste no key: --google-client-id ID --google-allow you@example.com=admin --google-allow @example.com=read (or $CHLOEDB_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID , and $CHLOEDB_GOOGLE_ALLOW comma-separated; on Kubernetes, in the StrataCluster's coordinatorArgs ). The sign-in dialog then shows Google's button above the key field. The server checks Google's ID token itself: signed RS256 by a key Google publishes ( https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs , kept as long as Google's answer says), for this client ( aud ), from accounts.google.com , not expired (a minute's skew either way), the address verified, and carrying the nonce the page was given in a cookie of its own. Then the allow list: an address, or @domain for every account of a Google Workspace domain (the token's hd must name it; @gmail.com is refused, since anyone can have one), each with read , write or admin ( read when none is given). Only a page of the server's own, over HTTPS or at http://localhost , may sign in (its Origin ). The person gets a session, kept as a key: GET /v1/admin/keys lists it with \"session\": \"google\" , owned by their address and named <address> (<id>) , and Access revokes it as any key. It is set in a cookie the page's scripts cannot read ( __Host-chloedb-session : Secure , HttpOnly , SameSite=Strict ), so the tab holds no key. A session lasts 12 hours unused and 7 days at most, a person keeps 8 at once (a ninth ends their oldest), it is never rotated, and each request checks the allow list as it is then: a person taken off it is refused at once, and one given a lower scope has that. A change made with the cookie needs the page's own Origin , and a cookie holds sessions alone, never an API key. Sessions reach every server of the cluster as keys do, and a coordinator that does not yet hold the session a page sends merges its keys at once (once a second at most). The keys file keeps a session's digest after a mark of its own ( s: ), so a server from before sessions never admits one. Each sign-in and each refused one is in the audit log, with the address; POST /v1/auth/signout (the menu under your name) ends the session everywhere. On the hub , sign in once: a connection's pages are the hub's, which adds that cluster's key to each call, and names you to the cluster ( x-strata-person ), whose audit log keeps your address as person beside the hub's key (Access shows \"for you@example.com, through a hub\"). The cluster takes that name as the hub's word: what it checks is the hub's key. No client secret is involved, as an ID token needs only the client's id, which is public. GET /v1/auth/google says whether sign-in is on ( {\"enabled\": false} without it). The OAuth client is made once, in the Google Cloud console of a project of yours: Google Auth Platform → Branding (an app name and a support address), Audience (Internal for your Workspace's accounts alone; External for other accounts, which you add as test users or reach by publishing the app, which the openid email profile scopes need no verification for), then Clients → Create client → Web application , whose Authorized JavaScript origins are the server's address as people open it ( https://cmd.example.com ; for a server on your machine, http://localhost and http://localhost:7700 ). It needs no redirect URI. Its Client ID ( NUMBER-....apps.googleusercontent.com ) is --google-client-id . - What is not there yet : mutual TLS, rate limits per key, and encryption at rest ( data at rest ). (Keys per table are keys limited to tables , above.)"],
["server.html#grpc","chloedb-server","gRPC","proto/strata.proto defines the service: Query , TermStats , Write , WriteStream , Sources , Schema and Health , mirroring the HTTP routes, and Hello , Release , Slots , and Replicate , Restore and Verify (a primary's calls to its replicas ), Logs , a query of the server's log store , and Profile , a profile of the server as its /v1/profile/... routes take one ( kind , the route's body as body_json , folded , in_use ; it needs an admin key or the token), which a coordinator asks of its shards' servers. The same port serves OpenTelemetry's LogsService ( OpenTelemetry logs ). Values are typed ( int , float , str , vector , list , boolean , timestamp ); statistics and fusion parts travel as JSON bytes, since they are ChloeDB's own serde types. The server compiles the proto at build time with a pure-Rust compiler, so building needs no protoc . A query travels as SQL with its parameters or, from protocol 2, as a typed plan ( QueryRequest.plan , with protocol 2 or newer): the tree of operators EXPLAIN shows, its fields named by position in the schema and its values typed, with no SQL to parse. sql then only names the query in the shard's logs. Protocol 3 adds snapshot leases for a fetch that follows a query: a query with hold keeps the snapshot it read, its own lease or a new one lasting as a TermStats lease does, and names it in its answer ( QueryResponse.snapshot ); a query with release ends its lease once answered, and Release ends leases no fetch needs. Protocol 4 places keys in slots : Slots gives a shard its slot map and its place in it, and queries, statistics calls and writes carry the map's epoch . Protocol 5 adds reads that see a write : QueryRequest.min_seq_no on a server, min_seq_nos on a coordinator, and WriteResponse.seq_nos from a coordinator. Protocol 6 keeps stream sources' offsets : write operations may carry a source , WriteResponse.skipped counts those skipped as replays, and Sources answers a server's offsets; a coordinator writes sources only to shards of 6 or newer. WriteStream (any server, from a client) streams batches of operations, each answered once committed. Protocol 7 answers the next codes of semantic-ID prefixes ( SidNext ); a coordinator refuses /v1/sid/next while a shard speaks an older one. Protocol 8 counts the rows of each level of a guarded query ( GuardCounts ), which a coordinator adds up to choose the level it sends; it refuses a guarded query while a shard speaks an older one. Protocol 9 makes the slot map the cluster map : it names each shard's servers as well as its slots, Slots with no map answers the map a server holds, Configure sets a server's part in replication while it serves, and ExportSlots , FreezeSlots and CleanupSlots move slots between shards, with the rows copied marked moved ( WriteRequest.moved ) and statements over some slots alone ( WriteRequest.slots_json ). Protocol 10 answers a compound query's single-table query as Arrow columns: a coordinator running the compound query on DataFusion asks for them ( QueryRequest.arrow ) when it puts the shards' rows one after another, without an order, groups, aggregates or scores, and a shard answers one Arrow IPC stream ( QueryResponse.arrow ) of the columns, then the rows' keys, then their scores. A column that holds only NULLs, or only empty lists, on one shard takes the type the others give it. A shard without rows answers rows (none); when one answers rows anyway (a column whose values are of several types) or the shards' types differ, the coordinator asks every shard for rows again. Shards of an older protocol are asked for rows. With 100,000 rows in each of two tables over three shards in one process, a join with GROUP BY on DataFusion took 58 ms this way against 148 ms with rows (62 ms on one database; median of 5 on the cloud container, an Intel Xeon of 4 cores). Protocol 11 reads rows by key without SQL ( Get ): a coordinator asks each key's shard, and refuses a read by key while a shard it asks speaks an older one. Protocol 12 keeps statistics by version. Protocol 13 answers a shard's feed ( Feed ), from its primary, in chunks of its lines; a coordinator refuses a feed from a shard of an older one. Protocol 14 starts a feed with a copy of the shard's rows ( FeedRequest.copy ), for a cluster that follows it; a coordinator refuses a copy from a shard of an older one, and passes on the name of the cluster a follow process writes for ( x-strata-follow ) to its shards. Protocol 15 lets a follower catch up : a copy of only the rows changed since a transaction ( FeedRequest.since , but every row of the tables in whole ), with the digests of the shard's keys ( digests ), and FeedKeys , a shard's keys of some slots or their digests; a coordinator refuses them from a shard of an older one. Protocol 16 carries the stream offsets of moving slots: Sources with moving answers each source's offset for the slots moving from the shard, and the flip's map carries them ( moving.offsets ) to the shard they go to; a coordinator asks for them only while every server speaks 16. Protocol 17 switches over to a follower: a shard holds its clients' writes on a mark ( _strata/hold ), says so in its feed ( \"hold\" ), and keeps a promotion from a cluster it followed ( _strata/promoted ); nothing else is new on the wire, and a coordinator refuses a hold or a promotion while a shard speaks an older one, which would refuse the marks as its own. Protocol 18 scrolls a shard's rows for a coordinator: QueryRequest.scroll starts a scroll of a plain scan or of rows ordered by one score or column, in shard mode, and answers its first page_size rows (none: it opens the scroll on the shard's snapshot without reading) with the scroll's QueryResponse.cursor ; a request with cursor reads its next page_size rows, keep_alive_ms keeps it, and Release with cursors ends it. A coordinator refuses a scroll while a shard speaks an older one. Protocol 19 checks a write without applying it ( WriteRequest.validate , a write across shards ), and says whether a refused write was applied ( x-chloedb-write ); a coordinator asks no check of a shard of an older one. Protocol 20 carries in each Replicate a digest of the transaction it follows ( prev_digest ), which a replica compares with its own of that term and number, refusing the call when they differ ( ReplicateResponse.differs , the transaction a call follows ); replication takes no protocol version, so a replica of an older one ignores the digest and a primary of an older one sends none. Hello answers the protocol versions a server speaks (1 to 20), the plan version it reads, the plan features it has, the epoch of the slot map it holds, and its role in replication ( STANDALONE , PRIMARY or REPLICA ) with its term and last sequence number, and on a primary that another replaced, the newer term ( replaced_by_term ). Keys , which takes no protocol version, merges the caller's API keys into the server's and answers the keys it then holds. A server refuses a protocol newer than its own, and a plan of a newer version or one that needs a feature it lacks, by name ( InvalidArgument ). Protocol 1, the servers before Hello , takes SQL, or a JSON plan ( plan_json ) for a compound query's leaf."],
["server.html#sharding-one-coordinator-many-shards","chloedb-server","Sharding: one coordinator, many shards","With tokens, give every server of the cluster the same --cluster-token ( CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN=… for all three), which they send each other, and the coordinator a --token of its own for its operator; make API keys for clients on the coordinator, and every server holds them ( security ). Without a cluster token, give the shards and the coordinator the same --token , which then makes the calls between servers too. On other machines, serve the shards with --tls-cert and --tls-key and list them as https:// . A shard may be several copies, a primary and its replicas : --shards separates shards with commas and a shard's copies with | , in any order, since the coordinator asks each copy whether it is the primary ( Hello ). Two shards of three copies each: When no server holds a cluster map yet, the coordinator gives them the first one from this list (the slots in equal runs, epoch 1). From then on the servers hold the map, which names every shard's servers, and the coordinator routes by the newest map they hold, whatever servers --shards lists: the list is where it finds them first. Operate a cluster starts such a cluster on one machine, fails a primary over and upgrades it a server at a time."],
["server.html#a-coordinator-beside-each-shard","chloedb-server","A coordinator beside each shard","A shard server can serve a coordinator too, in its own process, as Elasticsearch's data nodes answer queries: each machine then runs one server, and clients may ask any of them. The coordinator calls its own shard's gRPC service in-process: the same calls and the same checks of their token as over the network, without a connection, HTTP/2 or a second process. It reaches the other shards over the network. A read by key whose key the coordinator's own shard holds is answered without leaving the process: with two shards on one machine of 4 cores (the cloud container, an Intel Xeon), a key of its own shard took 0.39 to 0.53 ms at the client's median against 0.86 to 0.87 ms with the coordinator as a process of its own beside the shard, and a key of either shard, asking both coordinators in turn, 0.82 to 0.84 ms against 1.02 to 1.03 ms. BM25 queries from 8 clients answered 16% more a second, and from one client as fast (they wait for the other shard). The coordinator keeps no log store, API keys file or other files of its own (the shard keeps them), and takes no part in replication. A coordinator holds no data. For a query, it asks every shard for the BM25 statistics of the query's terms and a snapshot lease, which lasts until the query's deadline and a second, or from protocol 3 until the shard has answered the query (skipped when nothing in the query needs collection statistics: vectors, sparse fields, columns, and when the coordinator kept them, below), sends every shard the query with the summed statistics and its lease, in shard mode, so that each shard scores exactly as the whole collection would and returns what a merge needs (for fusions and products, each input's raw scores over its candidates), merges the answers with chloedb::cluster::merge : top k , fusion, products and facet counts; groups and aggregates come from each shard as partial states (sums, counts, minimums, maximums, and COUNT(DISTINCT) as its exact values), merged here. Kept statistics (from protocol 12): a shard's statistics of a table change only with the table's version, which every write to it that adds a row, flush, merge and restart moves on (values set in place, deletes and other tables' writes do not: the statistics count deleted rows, as each segment's own do, until a flush or merge drops them), so a coordinator keeps, for each replica and table, what its last statistics round answered of each field and term, as of the version it named. A query whose terms follow from its text alone (no fuzzy or synonyms , whose terms only a shard finds) and are all kept for a replica of every shard skips step 1: it is sent the kept sums, each replica with the version they are of, and runs only on that version. A replica that has moved on answers stale without running it, and the query is sent again with a statistics round, which keeps the new version's; that replica is then passed over for 10 ms, doubling while it keeps moving on, up to a second, so a shard written to all the time costs about one wasted query a second. Scores are exact either way, as with the lease. --stats-cache-terms sets how many terms are kept per replica (100,000 by default, about 10 MB, emptied when full; 0 keeps none), and chloedb_stats_cache_total{outcome} counts the queries sent kept statistics ( hit ), a round ( miss ) and those answered stale. On two shards of MS MARCO's first million passages and a coordinator, each on one core of a 4-core cloud container (Intel Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 2026-11-03, medians of 3 passes alternating the builds), it takes BM25 at 16 clients from 1,022 q/s to 1,270 and at one client from 2.18 ms to 1.67 (p50), with the same answers; phrases gain 20 to 25%, and a filter or a prefix 9 to 22%, as their shards' own work weighs more. On DigitalOcean (2026-11-04: shards of 2.9 CPUs on Platinum 8280 and 8358 nodes, a coordinator of 1 CPU on a Platinum 8168, both builds on the same nodes, 3 passes each; results ), kept statistics and the cheaper gRPC calls that came with them took BM25 at 16 clients from 1,375 q/s to 1,787 and at one client from 2.35 ms to 1.57 (p50), phrases at 16 clients from 1,387 to 1,886, a filter from 1,202 to 1,620 and a prefix from 1,054 to 1,298, with the same answers. Query then fetch (from protocol 3): for a page of rows with stored columns, when the shards would send in one round at least 8 times the page's rows (every shard sends its top LIMIT + OFFSET , and for a fusion or product each input's candidates), step 2 asks for the rows' keys alone, scores and sort values. Each shard holds the snapshot it ranked on (the statistics round's lease, or a new one). After the merge, the shards holding the page's rows fetch its columns from that snapshot and end their leases, and the others' leases are ended alongside. Stored fields are then read and sent for the page, not for every shard's top rows, so a deep page or a fused score answers faster, and a write between the rounds does not change the page. A shallow page from a few shards still comes in one round: with 3 shards of 20,000 demo products over localhost on a 4-core cloud container (2026-10-29), the second round cost up to 0.5 ms more than the rows it saved up to 6 times the page's rows, and saved time from 15 (a fused score: 5 to 6 ms in one round, 3 ms in two). A fetch answered by another replica than the one that ranked (the first failed) reads that replica's latest version, and leaves out a row it no longer has, as does a partial answer without a shard that failed the fetch ( chloedb_fetch_lost_rows_total ). A coordinator asks each replica the protocol versions it speaks ( Hello ) when it connects, again every minute and after the replica fails (it may have restarted on another version), and speaks the oldest any replica speaks, so shards can be upgraded one at a time: while one runs a server from before Hello , every shard gets SQL and JSON plans; once all speak protocol 2 they get typed plans, once all speak 3, pages are fetched, and once all speak 12, BM25 statistics are kept. /v1/health shows the protocol spoken ( protocol ). A sharded collection therefore ranks like one database: the tests run every query kind against three shards and one whole database and compare keys and scores. Writes are routed by primary key, to the shard holding its slot , or by a table's shard_by field ( tables on shards ); UPDATE and DELETE go to every shard. Within a shard, a write goes to its primary alone ( replication ). A shard of several servers none of which replicates (none started with --replicas or --replica ) serves reads only, and its writes are refused (409) before any server is sent them: each would take them on its own, number them its own way, and never be caught up after missing one, so its copies would diverge and reads would rotate over different databases. A coordinator started with --write-every-copy sends such a shard's writes to every server, as it did before; a write one of them refuses is then answered as failed though the others applied it. Start each shard with --seq-no for conditional writes : a coordinator passes if_seq_no and seq_no() on while every shard has one replica, or a primary that its replicas follow (replicas that each take a shard's writes number them in their own order), and its write answers carry no seq_no , since a batch can span shards, but one number per shard ( seq_nos ) for reads that see a write . UPDATE and DELETE with a LIMIT pick their rows by key first and then change those that still match; INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING runs on any rows, and DO UPDATE when its rows go to one shard. A coordinator refuses, for now, graph queries ( REACHABLE , PATH , hops() , ppr ), whose edges point at rows on other shards. What a cluster does differently lists every difference, and POST /v1/sql/check says whether a statement will run on the deployment it is sent to. Shards can also be built offline: Db::write_segment writes a segment file anywhere, and attach_segments adds built segments to a running database in one commit."],
["server.html#tables-on-shards","chloedb-server","Tables on shards","A coordinator serves the tables every shard has alike (the same names and schemas), in the first shard's order. It learns them when it connects, after CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE or DROP TABLE through it, which it runs on every shard and then asks again ( ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN excepted, which it refuses: its plans name columns by position), and when a request names a table it does not know (asking at most once a second), so a table made on each shard directly is served from then on. Alike is the same columns and options in the same order; the ids a database names its fields by on disk may differ, as they do in a shard added after a table was altered, which is given the table by one CREATE TABLE (its first table it must hold as the cluster does: alter it the same way before adding it). A table some shards lack is not served, and a shard from before tables offers only its first. It serves the rank profiles every replica holds alike in the same way, CREATE and DROP RANK PROFILE through it run on every shard's primary, and a query by a profile plans on the coordinator, written out; a shard whose profile of that name differs refuses the plan (the coordinator then asks the shards again and plans once more), and a shard from before profiles refuses it by its plan feature, rank_profiles . A query by a ranking model opens the model from the coordinator's own --models (it scores the shards' rows with it too) and sends the shards a plan naming the hash of its file; a shard whose file of that name differs refuses the plan, so every server needs the same file. A cross-encoder runs on the coordinator alone, once per query: the shards rank the rows of the phase before it, the coordinator reads the text of the best of them in the fetch round of query then fetch (every shard must speak protocol 3) and scores them, so only the coordinator needs the model. Rows are placed by their primary key, or by the field a table names as shard_by : A user's events then sit on one shard, the one whose slots hold the user (the field's value hashes as a key does), so the events of a user are written, and will be read, in one place. Queries still ask every shard. The field must be stored, single-valued, KEYWORD or INT , NOT NULL , and not the key. A row keeps its place: UPDATE of the field, and set or add on it, are refused, and a row that moves goes by a delete and an insert. A key is unique across the shards for an insert (and SQL INSERT ): the coordinator asks every shard's primary for the keys of the rows it inserts before it writes them, and refuses one another shard holds (409, naming the shard and the row's value of the field); ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING passes over it. An upsert that gives the same key another value of the field writes the row on that value's shard and leaves the old one where it was (as Elasticsearch's custom routing does), so change the field by deleting first. Operations by key give the field's value as \"routing\" ; a set , add or delete whose routing places it on a shard without that key is refused (404, naming the routing) rather than answered written: 0 , unless it says \"if_missing\": \"skip\" . SQL UPDATE and DELETE go to every shard as before. A shard refuses a write whose field value another shard's slots hold, naming the value."],
["server.html#slots","chloedb-server","Slots","A primary key hashes to one of 4,096 slots: xxh3 of the key's type and bytes, so the INT 5 and the string '5' are different keys in different slots ( chloedb_server::slots::slot ). A slot map assigns the slots to shards under an epoch, its version: by default the shards hold equal runs of slots in the order --shards lists them, under epoch 1, and --slot-map FILE gives another assignment as JSON, each shard's slots as [start, end) ranges: When a coordinator connects, it gives every replica its map and its place in it ( Slots ), and then sends the epoch with every query and write. A shard keeps its map in a file beside its database ( <db>.slots ), so it outlives a restart, and shows it in /v1/health ( slots ). It refuses (409, FailedPrecondition over gRPC): a request with an older epoch than its map's, so a coordinator still on an old map cannot write where the new map says another shard holds a key; a coordinator that gets a 409 when it connects says so and stops. A request of an older epoch under which this shard held the slots it holds now is served: a change that left its slots alone (a shard or a server added or removed elsewhere) does not interrupt coordinators that have not taken it up yet (the file keeps that epoch as since ); another map of the same epoch, such as one from a coordinator before protocol 9 listing the shards in another order or fewer of them; a new assignment needs a higher epoch; a map while rows here have keys (or shard_by values) in other shards' slots under it, saying how many and one of them. Nothing moves rows between shards yet, so a map that moves slots is refused until the shards are loaded again under it. A shard also refuses (400) a write of a key whose slot another shard holds, so that a client writing to a shard directly cannot place a row where the coordinator will not look for it. --demo-shard I/N loads the demo rows of shard I's slots under the default map. A coordinator routes by slots once every replica speaks protocol 4. While any is older, it routes by the hash before slots (FNV-1a of the key's text, modulo the shard count), gives no map, and logs a warning; it refuses --slot-map then. The two hashes place keys differently, so a collection sharded before slots is refused once its shards are upgraded, and has to be loaded again through a coordinator (or with --demo-shard ). Slots move between running shards while they serve ."],
["server.html#hot-shards-and-hot-slots","chloedb-server","Hot shards and hot slots","A coordinator counts what it routes to each slot, so that it can say which shard is hot and whether moving slots would cool it. GET /v1/cluster/slots answers, over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes ( windows : 1m , 5m , 15m ): per shard ( shards ): the slots it holds now, its keyed reads and writes a second ( reads_per_s , writes_per_s , keyed_per_s ), its share of them, its most counted slot ( top_slot ) and that slot's share of the shard's load ( top_slot_share ); the requests sent to every shard, a second ( fanout_per_s ); the 20 slots most counted ( top ), each with its shard; skew , the busiest shard's keyed load as a multiple of the mean of the shards holding slots, and a verdict with a sentence saying why ( why ): verdict when what to do quiet under one keyed operation a second nothing to tell yet even the busiest shard takes under 1.3× the mean nothing hot range a shard takes 1.3× the mean or more, spread over many of its slots move some of its slots to other shards (Cluster, Rebalance), or give it a copy more for reads hot slot as above, with half or more of its load in one slot moving slots does not split it: the slot takes its load wherever it goes. It is one key, or one shard_by value most rows share: cache it, spread its writes over several keys, or place the table by a column with more values What is counted, once a request (not again when a newer map sends it to another shard): each operation of a write, at the slot of the value that places its row (its key, or its table's shard_by value); each key of a read by key; each value a query's filter pins the placing field to ( id = ... , id IN (...) , up to 4,096 values), however many shards those are on. A request with no slot to count goes to every shard and is counted once as fan-out: a ranking by BM25 (which needs every shard's statistics), a query whose filter does not pin the placing field, UPDATE and DELETE , and a read by key of a table placed by a column. Fan-out loads every shard alike, so it is not in skew or the verdict: even does not rule out a shard slow for queries sent to all, which its took_ms and the chloedb_replica_* metrics show. A scroll is counted once, when it starts, and not for each page. The statistics round of a BM25 query, the fetch round of query then fetch, and a guard's counts are not counted, being parts of a query already counted, and neither are the coordinator's other calls to every shard (a table's JSON paths, the next numbers of a semantic ID, term statistics asked alone). A query sent again after a shard's rank profile changed under it is counted twice. Counts are kept by slot, and a slot's shard is read from the map when they are shown, so after a move a slot's past load counts for the shard holding it now. ?all=1 adds slots , every slot counted since the coordinator started, as [slot, shard, reads, writes] , and ?window=1m (or 5m , 15m ) adds instead every slot counted in that window, with slots_window and slots_secs , the window's length; the Cluster page's Key slots map shows them; totals has the reads, writes and fan-outs since then. The windows are cut at 10 s marks for the minute and 60 s marks for the others, so a window covers 50 to 60 s, 4 to 5 minutes and 14 to 15 minutes, and less while the coordinator is younger ( secs is its length, which the rates are over). Each coordinator counts what it routes: with several, add their answers. A cluster whose shards route by the hash before slots counts nothing ( by_slot: false ). The counts take a relaxed atomic add each, and the snapshots behind the windows about 1.5 MB at most. /v1/cluster carries the 5 minutes' figures ( heat on each shard: keyed_per_s , share ; slot_heat : the verdict and skew ), and /metrics the rates per shard , with no series per slot. --slot-heat-keys also samples one count in 16 into a list of the 64 keys counted most (space-saving: a key that enters in place of another takes its count, shown as error ), its counts halved every 5 minutes so that it follows what is hot now. GET /v1/admin/slots/keys shows them, an admin's only since they are key values (and not a key limited to tables, nor is /v1/cluster/slots ): each key (as JSON) with its table, its slot and shard, and its reads and writes estimated (the samples times 16). Without the option it answers 404."],
["server.html#changing-the-cluster-while-it-serves","chloedb-server","Changing the cluster while it serves","From protocol 9 the slot map is the cluster map: besides each shard's slots it names each shard's servers (their gRPC URLs), and every server keeps it beside its database. A coordinator connects to the servers --shards lists, asks each the map it holds ( Slots with no map), and routes by the newest one that names servers, so a coordinator started again on the list it was first given, or on any one server of each shard, serves the cluster as it is now. A list that also names servers new to any cluster lays the cluster out anew: the same slots, on the servers listed, under the next epoch. A list naming a server that the newest map leaves out, but that a map it served under includes (it was removed, or serves another cluster), is refused (400), so that a coordinator started on an old list does not undo the admin calls below. While every server speaks 9, the first map a coordinator gives names the servers --shards lists ( a|b for a shard's copies). The admin calls of a coordinator change the map (admin scope, recorded in the audit log ). Those that add a server to the cluster (a shard, a copy, and a plan with a step that adds one, applied or resumed) take the token the coordinator sends its servers (the cluster's token, or --token on a cluster without one): the coordinator asks the server it adds who it is with that token, so an API key, or the admin's --token beside a cluster token, may not name one (403 cluster_token_required , before any call), and a plan made with them says so in its preflight. An open cluster has no token to give away and takes them from anyone. On Kubernetes the operator adds servers with the cluster's clusterTokenSecret . call what it does POST /v1/admin/shards {\"servers\": [url, ...], \"write_acks\"?} adds a shard served by these servers, the first its primary, holding no slot until slots move to it DELETE /v1/admin/shards/:i removes shard i , which must hold no slot ( drain it first); the shards after it move down one place POST /v1/admin/shards/:i/replicas {\"url\", \"force\"?} adds a server to shard i as a replica of its primary (a shard served by one server makes that server its primary first) DELETE /v1/admin/shards/:i/replicas?url= removes a server from shard i : the coordinators stop reading from it, then its primary stops shipping to it POST /v1/admin/shards/:i/promote {\"url\", \"force\"?} makes that replica shard i 's primary, once a majority of the shard's copies vote for it ( failover ); a primary that serves steps down and follows it | POST /v1/admin/copies/verify {\"url\"} | compares that copy's shard: its primary checks each replica against itself and answers as /v1/admin/verify does | | POST /v1/admin/copies/checkpoint {\"url\", \"name\"?, \"layout\"?} | has that copy write a checkpoint into its --backup-dir , with the cluster map and keys beside it; POST /v1/admin/checkpoint {\"name\"} has every shard's primary write one ( backups ) | POST /v1/admin/plan {\"adds\", \"removes\", \"new_shards\", \"drops\", \"moves\", \"even\"} plans a change of shape on machines of your own and changes nothing (nor is it audited): the steps in order ( add_copy , handover , remove_copy , add_shard , move , drain , remove_shard ), each the admin call that makes it, the rows to copy, an estimate of the time from the rate the last move ran at, what it touches ( blast ), the checks it passes first ( preflight , each ok and a sentence), and the copies it removes, which are only those asked for: a shard it does not name keeps its copies. The command center's Cluster draft shows it before Apply runs the steps. GET /v1/admin/moves has a job's progress at its top: state , running , started_ms , started_by , rows_done , rows_total (null when not known ahead), rate_rows_s , eta_s , and the moves done of how many. A plan's rows to copy count every table, its preflight checks say each copy's disk level before and after, and its estimate uses the measured rate of the last moves job when there is one. POST /v1/admin/plan/apply {\"steps\": [...]} runs the steps on the coordinator, one plan at a time in the cluster (409 while this or another coordinator applies one); closing the page does not stop it. GET /v1/admin/plan/run follows it step by step. POST /v1/admin/plan/run/stop , /resume and /discard stop it after its current step, resume one that a restart interrupted, or forget it. The plan is kept beside the audit log ( <audit>.plan.json ), and /v1/cluster carries it as plan . GET /v1/admin/plan/run and GET /v1/admin/moves also list, under elsewhere , the plans and moves of the cluster's other coordinators. POST /v1/admin/moves/finish is refused while another coordinator moves slots. A server that joins must be new: it speaks protocol 9, holds no rows ( force drops them, for a replica) and no cluster map (a server that left a cluster keeps the map it served under, so it does not join another by mistake), and serves the cluster's first table with its schema: start it as the cluster's first shard was started, with the same --schema , or with no --schema (nor --demo ) when the cluster began with no tables, and with --seq-no when its shard is replicated. A server given a --schema cannot join a cluster that began with no tables, even of one of its tables: its first table would be that one, not the empty one such a cluster's servers hold first. On a new shard's primary the coordinator first creates the cluster's other tables ( CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ) and its rank profiles, which the primary then ships to the shard's other servers; a replica that joins a shard gets them with the copy of its primary. Then the coordinator sets each server's part ( Configure ), and gives every server of the new map the map, under the next epoch. A shard's primary is not removed while other servers serve the shard (promote another first), nor a shard's last server; a primary that stopped answering is still the primary its copies follow until another is, so it too is removed only after a promotion (by vote while a majority of the copies answer, else forced). A plan that replaces it ( POST /v1/admin/plan , or Plan the repair in the command center) promotes the copy of the latest transaction first, a step skipped when the copies have elected a primary by then. Each change takes the map a server holds as its start, so two coordinators may change the cluster: one at a time on each coordinator, and when a server holds another map of the epoch it gives (another coordinator's change came first), the coordinator takes that map up and makes its change again on it. A server that does not answer is listed in the answer's missed , and is given the map when it answers again; a change that no server took fails (503) and is not routed by, since the next change would be made from a map no server holds. A change that fails once a server took its map, or when a server's answer did not come, leaves that map in doubt, as a server may hold it: the coordinator's next change gives it again first, the same way, and is made on it once a server takes it; from then on the coordinator routes by it, even when the change made on it fails. It is dropped once every server answers without taking it, and while no server answers, the next change fails too (503), so that no other map of its epoch is made. Nor does a coordinator change the map from one older than a server said it holds: it asks the servers again, and fails (503) if it still cannot fetch that map. A coordinator that starts again forgets a map in doubt, and routes by the newest map of the servers that answer it (one copy of each shard may do), either of which can still fork the map (two maps of one epoch); like two coordinators changing the map at once, only the consensus work planned next ends that. On Kubernetes, where the operator's calls reach whichever coordinator its Service picks, a coordinator changes the map only while it holds the cluster's Lease ( <cluster>-map , coordination.k8s.io ), which one coordinator holds at a time (taken by the API server's compare-and-swap; a change waits up to 20 s for another's to end, then fails with 503 naming the holder), and it keeps there the map it is about to give, before it gives it, and when its change ends the newest map a server may hold (the map it made, or the one left in doubt): the next coordinator to change the map, another or this one started again, gives that map again first unless the servers it reaches hold it already, so that one which reaches only a server the change missed does not make a second map of the epoch. A Lease not kept for 90 s (its holder stopped) is taken by the next. A coordinator whose account may not use Leases (a Role made by an operator from before them) changes the map without one, and logs that. This narrows the fork to a coordinator paused for longer than the Lease's 90 s while it gives a map; coordinators that the operator does not run hold no Lease, so run their changes through one of them. While slots move and their rows are being copied, the primary of the shard they leave takes every change first, one server at a time before the others: it alone decides between the move's flip and any other change of the same epoch. Other coordinators take up a newer map within 2 s: each asks its servers which epoch they hold every 2 s, and at once when a Hello answers a newer epoch than its own. /v1/cluster shows each shard's servers, its slots and how many it holds ( slots_held ), the map's epoch ( slot_epoch ), and whether the map can change now ( changes : not while a server speaks a protocol before 9). A server keeps its part in replication beside its database too ( <db>.replication : role , and on a primary its replicas and write_acks ), whether flags, Configure or a promotion set it, and starts again in it without flags. Configure refuses what would lose writes: making a primary that no newer primary replaced a replica, or a standalone server holding rows one, unless force ; making a replica standalone, unless force ; and making a replica a primary (promote it instead). A replica that joins a serving primary starts out of sync, so that writes do not wait for it, and is sent a copy of the primary before anything else, since rows loaded in bulk took no sequence number; it serves reads once it has caught up."],
["server.html#moving-slots","chloedb-server","Moving slots","A coordinator moves slots, with their rows, from one shard to another while the cluster serves: onto a shard just added, or off one before removing it. These calls are admin scope and recorded in the audit log ; each starts a job of moves in the background, one move at a time, and the cluster has one move in its map at a time: call what it does POST /v1/admin/rebalance {\"chunk\"?, \"dry_run\"?} evens out the slots over the shards, at most chunk slots per move (default 256), once the disks of the copies sent rows are checked as a plan's are ( disks , preflight in plan ; a failed check refuses it, 409); dry_run answers the plan and moves nothing POST /v1/admin/rebalance {\"by\": \"load\", \"window\"?, \"dry_run\"?, \"force\"?, \"chunk\"?} evens out the keyed load instead ( by load ): moves the slots that carry it, from the busiest shard to the quietest; dry_run answers the plan and moves nothing POST /v1/admin/shards/:i/drain {\"chunk\"?} moves every slot of shard i to the others, evenly POST /v1/admin/moves {\"slots\": [[start, end], slot, ...], \"to\": j} moves these slots, all held by one shard, to shard j GET /v1/admin/moves the job running or last run here ( kind , state : running, done, failed or stopped; each move's phase , rows copied, catch-up rounds , busy chunks the shard they go to refused while behind on its flushes (each sent again a second later, for up to 2 minutes), held_ms its writes were held, ms and error ) and the move in the map ( moving ) POST /v1/admin/moves/stop stops the job once the move under way ends POST /v1/admin/moves/finish finishes a move no job here runs, one a coordinator left when it stopped: one flipped ends once the shard the slots left deletes its copies, and one copying is given up By load. An even share of the slots is not an even share of the work when some keys are read or written more than others ( hot shards and hot slots ). \"by\": \"load\" weighs each slot by the reads and writes routed to it by key over window ( 1m , 5m or 15m , default 15m ), counted by this coordinator and asked of every other ( GET /v1/cluster/slots?window= ; coordinators in the answer says whose counts are in it, and why one's are not: it did not answer, or is of a version before the windows). It plans only when the busiest shard takes 1.3× the mean of the shards or more, and moves slots from the busiest shard to the quietest, the busiest slot that fits first, until the busiest takes under 1.1× the mean. A shard just added, holding no slots, takes load first. A slot that alone takes more than the gap between the two stays where it is, since it would make its new shard the hot one (a hot slot: cache the key, or spread its writes); the plan names it ( unsplit ), and the shard's other slots may still move away from it. No shard is given more than 1.5× an even share of the slots, so that a quiet shard is not given most of the rows, nor slots that would take its fullest copy's disk past 85% (a slot's bytes being its shard's database over the slots it holds); the plan lists the shards passed over for want of room ( disk_full ). The plan's disk checks are those of a plan : disks has each copy sent rows, its volume now and after, and preflight the checks, warning from 85% and failing when the rows would not fit or take a disk to 98%; a failed check refuses the moves (409). The answer has the plan: each shard's load a second and the skew before and after ( before , after ), the moves ( from , to , count , slots as runs), slots_moved , the disk checks ( disks , preflight ), and why when there is nothing to move. It is refused (409) when the counts cover less than 48 s of 1m , or 240 s of 5m or 15m (a coordinator that started recently; force moves anyway, and dry_run answers with a warning ), or the window saw under one keyed operation a second. A dry run is not audited, as a plan asked is not. The mean is over every shard, one holding no slots too (it is where load goes first), where the verdict of /v1/cluster/slots and the Cluster page's Load lens take the shards holding slots: just after a shard is added, they may say even while a rebalance by load moves slots to it. Counts per slot follow their slots, so after the moves /v1/cluster/slots shows the load as the shards now hold it. A plan's step {\"kind\": \"rebalance\", \"by\": \"load\", \"window\"?} runs it ( POST /v1/admin/plan/apply ). A move is in the cluster map ( moving : from , to , slots and state ), so every server and coordinator knows of it. It goes: Copying. The coordinator streams every row of the slots from the primary of the shard they leave ( ExportSlots ) and writes them to the shard they go to, marked moved ; that shard holds them without serving them, and takes rows marked moved only while the move copies to it. A chunk the shard refuses because it is behind on its flushes (429, as a shard just added is while the first chunks build its indexes) waits a second and goes again, for up to 2 minutes, before the move fails. Then it copies what changed since (rows written since, by sequence number, and deletes of rows gone), again, until a round copies 2,000 rows or fewer, or after 8 rounds. Reads and writes of the slots go on at the shard they leave. Holding writes. The shard they leave holds new writes to the slots, and UPDATE and DELETE statements ( FreezeSlots : 30 s unless asked again, and the coordinator asks every 10 s), waits for those under way to commit, and the coordinator copies the last changes. Writes to other slots go on. Flip (state cleaning ). A new map gives the slots to the shard they went to, the shard they left taking it first and hiding its copies from then. The map's move carries the stream offsets of the slots on the shard they left ( offsets ), read while their writes are held, which the shard they went to keeps for them. It takes it only while it has held the writes to the slots since before their last copy, in its term: one that started again, whose primary changed, or whose hold ran out, may have taken writes no copy holds, and refuses the flip (409), so that the move is given up. On a replicated shard, its primary first writes a mark of the flip through its log ( _strata/move flip in _sources ) and waits until the copies its writes wait for hold it (else the flip is refused, 503), then takes the map; a replica takes the flip only once it applied that mark, and refuses it before (409): given the flip first, a former primary that started again as a replica would otherwise take it while the new primary took writes to the slots that no copy reached. A primary elected while its log holds the mark refuses writes to the moving slots (503) until it takes the map, and takes it on the mark. The writes it held find the map changed and are refused (409), and their coordinators send them by the new map to the new shard: they waited held_ms , a few milliseconds when the last round was small. Clean up. The shard they left deletes its copies ( CleanupSlots ), and the map drops the move. A move that fails before its flip is given up (state aborting ): the shard the slots were to go to deletes what it was sent, once the writes under way there have committed, and the move ends, its slots where they were. A shard that does not number its writes (no --seq-no ) cannot say what changed, so it holds the writes to the slots for the whole copy. Rows move by their primary keys, so every table needs one; the rows of a table placed by a column ( shard_by ) move with the slots of their column's values. Each read sees each row once: a server takes the version it reads, and the rows it hides, under its map's lock, and refuses (409) a read routed by a map that changed after it was checked; the coordinator sends it again by the new map. An UPDATE or DELETE through a coordinator changes each row once: a shard checks as the statement starts, under its writer lock, that it still serves the slots the statement was routed by, and passes over the rows it holds without serving them; a shard that refuses it is sent it again by the new map, and so is the shard that took slots the statement had yet to change, over those slots alone ( WriteRequest.slots_json ). A request routed by a map a shard has not been given yet (503) waits for it a little longer each try, 8 tries at most."],
["server.html#replication-a-primary-and-its-replicas","chloedb-server","Replication: a primary and its replicas","A shard's copies can hold the same rows under the same sequence numbers . One server is the primary, started with --replicas naming the others' gRPC addresses; each of the others is a replica, started with --replica . Both need --seq-no . The primary takes the writes and ships its numbered transactions, as its write-ahead log holds them, to each replica's Replicate call, in order and in batches of up to 4 MiB. A replica applies a batch only if it follows the replica's last transaction ( Database::apply_shipped ), under the primary's numbers and term, and refuses writes from clients (409). So every copy starts from the same rows: an empty database of the same schema, the same --demo , or a checkpoint of the primary. --demo and --copy-from load each copy on its own, and are not shipped. A replica that starts too far behind is sent a copy of the primary (below), so it needs only the primary's first table. CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE , DROP TABLE and the rank profiles' statements are numbered transactions as writes are: the primary ships each in its place among the writes, and a replica makes the same change, with the primary's table ids and, for ALTER TABLE , field ids. A replica that has a table the primary creates, lacks one it drops or alters, or holds a table it alters with another schema than the primary's before the change (other columns, or the same ones under other field ids, as in a database whose table was made by other statements) holds other rows than the primary, and refuses the batch. The primary flushes its memtables first, so that its write-ahead log, from which it refills its ship log on a restart, starts after the change. When a write is answered ( --write-acks ): all , the default: once the primary and every replica in sync hold it, and never fewer than a majority of the copies, so that an election cannot lose it. A replica that has not acknowledged it by the write's deadline ( timeout_ms , or --timeout-ms ) drops out of sync, and the write is answered if a majority holds it, else answered 504 as under majority . While fewer than a majority are in sync, a write is refused before it is applied (503). A replica that joined and has not caught up yet is not counted among the copies (nor does it vote). majority : once more than half the copies hold it, the primary among them. While fewer than a majority are in sync, a write is refused before it is applied (503). A write that a majority has not acknowledged by its deadline is answered 504, which says that it is durable on the primary under its sequence number; a retry with the same idempotency key answers it once a majority holds it, without applying it again. primary : once the primary holds it; the replicas get it afterwards. A replica is in sync while it holds what the primary committed, as far as the primary has shipped it. It drops out when a call to it fails or takes over 10 s, when its history differs from the primary's, when the primary's log no longer holds the transactions after its last, or, under all , when it misses a write's deadline. It is back in once it holds the primary's last transaction again. Every call carries the global checkpoint, the highest number every in-sync replica holds. A replica with nothing to apply is called every second, and one whose call failed is called again within 2 s. The ship log, and copies. The primary keeps its latest transactions in memory to ship, --ship-log-mb of them (64 MiB), and refills it on a restart from its write-ahead log since the last flush. A replica further behind than that is sent a copy of the primary ( Restore ), and then follows from the copy's last transaction: The primary writes a checkpoint of its database beside it (a hidden file, .<db>.copy-<id> , as large as the database's segments, removed once sent), and streams it to the replica in chunks of 1 MiB. Writes go on meanwhile: the checkpoint holds the writer only to flush and pin a version. The replica writes the copy beside its own database ( .<db>.restore-<id> , removed once installed), opens it checking every segment's checksum, and puts its tables, rows, sequence numbers and terms in place of its own in one commit ( Database::install_copy ), which empties its write-ahead log: a table only the replica had is dropped. A crash before that commit leaves the replica as it was, and one after it leaves the copy. Queries go on meanwhile, and one that started before the commit reads what it started on. The primary holds the transactions that follow the copy in its ship log until the replica has them, up to four times --ship-log-mb , so that the replica catches up from the copy however many writes came while it was sent. A replica that falls further behind while it is copied is sent another copy. The replica checks the primary's term and id as it checks a Replicate call. A copy that is cut short, damaged, or whose first table is neither the replica's, nor one ALTER TABLE could have made of it (the same name and key, each of the replica's columns kept with its type or dropped, and new ones after them), nor one of the same columns (a replica made after the table was altered) changes nothing, and the primary tries again after 30 s. So a replica made before its primary's tables were altered, or made since with their columns as they are, takes the copy, and the primary's field ids with it. A copy can reach the replica late: after the call that sent it gave up (its timeout, or a broken connection), and after a newer copy and the transactions that follow it, which the replica may have acknowledged. The replica refuses a copy that ends before its own last transaction when that transaction is of the copy's term: it is one of the copy's primary's, given after the copy was taken, and the copy would drop it (in the cluster simulator, seed 624036738 of the nightly run lost 34 acknowledged writes so, before replicas refused such a copy). A replica that holds another transaction than its primary's ( differs , below) takes the copy wherever it ends. An install goes on to the end when its call ends early, and the replica takes no other call of its primary meanwhile. A replica whose history differs from the primary's is sent a copy when its last transaction is of an older term than the primary's: it then holds transactions of a primary this one replaced (an old primary started again as a replica, below), and the copy drops them; and when it holds another transaction than the primary's under the term and number a call follows (the transaction a call follows, below). Otherwise (a last transaction of the primary's term that is not the primary's, or past the primary's last, where the digests do not tell the two apart: the replica holds the same transaction under the primary's last number, or one of the logs no longer holds it) it is not sent a copy, which would drop its transactions: the error says to start it again from a checkpoint of the primary. Terms. Each server keeps a random id beside its database ( <db>.replication ), and a replica keeps there the id and term of the primary it follows. It refuses (409) a second primary of the same term, whose history would differ, and follows the primary of a newer term. A primary of an older term than a replica's is told so, and is fenced: another primary replaced it (below). A primary that another primary ships to answers the same way: one of an older term is told this primary's term, and one of a newer term fences this primary. A replica is checked by the number and term of its last transaction, and, from protocol 20, by that transaction itself (below). One that wrote rows of its own under the same numbers before it became a replica is found out when a shipped write does not fit its rows, when a call follows a transaction of the primary's that it holds another of, or when the copies are compared (below). The transaction a call follows. Two copies can hold different transactions under one term and number: a primary's write that waited for its writer while the primary handed its shard over could commit after the primary, a replica by then, applied its new primary's first transaction, under that term and the next number, which the new primary gave a write of its own (such a write is refused unapplied since, answered in doubt, as the term it was taken in is over; a copy that wrote rows under the same numbers before it became a replica is another case). A call matched by term and number alone, so the replica counted as in sync and took the primary's next transactions on top of its own. Each Replicate now carries a 64-bit digest (xxh3) of the transaction it follows ( prev_digest ), as the primary's ship log holds it, but for the count of transactions each database keeps of its own: a replica logs what it applies with the same records, so the two digests of one transaction are equal. A replica that holds a transaction of that term and number in its own ship log compares its digest; when they differ it applies nothing, answers differs , serves no reads, logs an error and shows differs (the transaction) and txn_mismatches in its health, whose status is degraded until it is repaired. It keeps that it differs beside its database ( differs in <db>.replication ), so that, started again, when its log may no longer hold that transaction, it still refuses every call that carries a digest, and asks no copy to elect it, until it installs a copy of a primary. The primary takes it out of sync, counts it ( txn_mismatches , chloedb_replica_txn_mismatches_total ), logs an error and sends it a copy of itself, as it does a replica holding a replaced primary's transactions: the copy drops the replica's own. The primary's history is the one kept, as an election chose it; the replica's transaction was never acknowledged by the primary that numbered it. A replica that says its last transaction is one of the primary's history (a call it did not apply) counts as holding it only once a call that follows it applies, so that no write is acknowledged by a copy holding another transaction under its number; and that call always carries the primary's digest of it: when the primary's log no longer holds that transaction, the replica is sent a copy instead. The check compares one transaction, the one each call follows, not the history before it, and the replica needs it in its own ship log: one that restarted after a flush, or installed a copy since, cannot compare it, and is left to the digest rounds below; so is a replica whose last transaction is the primary's last, met by a primary whose log does not hold it (one that started again after a flush). A replica or primary of an older protocol neither sends nor reads the digest, and applies by term and number as before. Comparing copies. Every --verify-every-s (600 s), and when asked ( POST /v1/admin/verify ), the primary compares each replica in sync with itself: It takes a version of its database and ships the replica up to that version's last transaction, not past it. The call that reaches it has the replica keep the version that call leaves ( pin ), which is the same transaction's. Nothing else writes on a replica, so the two versions should hold the same rows. It asks the replica's digests of that version ( Verify ), and computes its own meanwhile. For each of the 4,096 slots , a digest is the count of rows in it and the sum of a 64-bit hash (xxh3) of each row's table, primary key and sequence number. A row of a table without a primary key is placed by that hash. Shipping goes on while both hash. A replica whose digests differ in any slot drops out of sync, and is sent a copy of the primary, as one the log no longer reaches is. Its error names the transaction compared at, how many slots differ and the first of them. A round that cannot compare (the call failed, or the replica is from before Verify ) says why in verified.error , and changes nothing else. The digests find a replica that lacks a row, holds one too many, or holds a row whose last write has another number: one loaded from other rows, or one whose shipped writes were lost, for example to a disk that acknowledged writes it never made durable. They do not cover the values: two copies that applied the same writes hold the same values, but a replica loaded with other values under the same keys before numbering began is not found. Hashing takes about 80 to 120 ms per million rows on each side (measured on a cloud Intel Xeon at 2.30 GHz, 4 cores, 2026-10-29), and shipping goes on meanwhile. Scrubbing. The digests compare rows, not bytes: a copy whose data was damaged on disk (bit rot, a bad sector) holds the same keys and numbers. Every --scrub-every-s (a day) each database server reads every segment of its database, with its delete set and updates, against its checksum, at --scrub-mib-s (64 MiB/s) at most, while writes, flushes and merges go on; POST /v1/admin/scrub runs one now (unpaced unless given rate_mib_s ), and a coordinator runs one on a copy with POST /v1/admin/copies/scrub {url} (the command center's Check its data , in a copy's menu). A query checks a section the first time it reads it, and a merge checks the segments it reads, so a scrub finds damage nobody read yet, as on a replica that serves few searches. What the last one found is in /v1/health ( scrub , and damaged when a segment fails), in chloedb_damaged_segments and the series' damaged_segments , which the built-in damaged alert (critical) watches, and on Home, which names the copy to replace."],
["server.html#failover","chloedb-server","Failover","A shard's copies elect its primary among themselves, as Raft does: each copy keeps beside its database ( <db>.replication ) the newest term it knows and the one copy it voted for in it, and grants its vote only to a copy whose last transaction (term, then number) is not behind its own. A copy wins with the votes of a majority of the copies its cluster map names for its shard, its own counted. A write answered under all or majority is held by a majority of the copies, and two majorities share a copy, so the winner holds every write that was answered. A copy first asks whether the others would vote (a pre-vote, which changes nothing), so that one that was cut off does not push the term up for nothing. Each round of an election ends once its answers decide it (a majority granted, or too few are left to), so that a copy that is down or cut off holds no round up until its call times out; and a replica that grants a vote waits a whole failover delay from then before it asks to be elected itself. A copy that the others tell of a newer term asks for the term after it next time: a term in which a minority voted for a copy that lost is never begun, and the others would refuse it forever. A replica refuses its vote while its primary called it within the failover delay (or 5 s), and a primary that serves refuses too, but for a handover asked by hand. A shard's first copies vote from the start, as Raft's first members do: its primary started with --replicas , and each replica started with --replica on a database that had no part in replication yet (no role in <db>.replication ). Until a primary calls it, such a replica votes only for a copy that, like it, has heard from no primary and holds nothing it does not: a replica whose volume was lost, its database and <db>.replication together, starts again with the same --replica on an empty database and cannot tell that from a first start, and its vote could otherwise elect a copy without the writes it had acknowledged. Once a primary calls it, it counts as a member only from the first call that finds it caught up (a primary that still counts it one sends it what it lacks first), and meanwhile grants its vote to that primary alone, so that a shard whose first primary steps down before its replicas caught up elects it again. A server made a replica to join a serving shard ( POST /v1/admin/shards/:i/replicas , or Configure ) grants none until it has caught up with a primary, so start a copy that joins later as a standalone server and add it so. Nor does a copy that no primary counts a member ask to be elected (409), as Raft's learners do not: one that joined, or lost its volume and started again empty, and that no call has found caught up since, may lack writes a majority acknowledged, and a member that missed them too would vote for it ( the specification TLC found that in ). A copy that has just joined is counted at the call after the first that finds it caught up (a primary calls each replica at least every second), so a handover to it waits for that: promote is refused (409) until its /v1/health says \"member\": true , unless force . Automatically ( --failover-after-ms , in a cluster; 0, the default, leaves failover to an operator). A replica that has heard nothing from its primary for the delay, and a random part of it more (so that two replicas seldom ask at once), asks its shard's copies to elect it; one that loses waits about the delay before it asks again. The winner begins the term it won, ships to the shard's other copies from its last transaction under --write-acks , and takes writes; coordinators send the shard's writes to it once a write finds the old primary down. A write a coordinator had already sent to a primary that then stopped answering (a process frozen, a VM paused, a network that drops its packets) is given up on once another copy took its place, and goes to that copy with its idempotency key, which the new primary answers as before if the old one had shipped the write to it. A write whose primary answers nothing, neither the write nor the coordinator's other calls, for 10 s while no copy takes its place, and one without a key whose primary was replaced, is answered with error_code write_in_doubt (503; 409 when a new primary took the shard while the write was being acknowledged): it may or may not be applied, retryable is false because the same request without a key could apply it twice, and the answer's idempotency_key (the coordinator's own when the write came without one) sends it again safely, applied once. The 10 s are meant to be longer than --failover-after-ms , its random part and an election, so that a shard that fails over has its writes sent on to the new primary rather than answered in doubt; a primary that is slow but answers is waited for as before. With the delay set, a primary calls each replica at least four times within it, and serves reads only while a majority of its copies answered a call started within three quarters of it (503 otherwise): past that, the others may have elected another primary, and it would serve what that one replaced. A primary that a newer one replaced becomes a replica of it, which ships to it, and a primary that starts again starts as a replica until its shard elects one (a primary of a shard of one copy starts as before). Start a copy again without --replicas or --replica : it takes up the part <db>.replication keeps. A former primary started again with --replicas starts as the primary of its old term, logging errors until a copy of the newer term makes it a replica, and a replica started with --replicas refuses to start ( operate a cluster ). A shard elects only while a majority of its copies is up: with one copy of three up, it has no primary and refuses writes (503) until a second copy is back. A primary that cannot write until it restarts ( failing in health : a write failed partway, as on a full disk) hands its shard over by itself: once a delay at most, it asks its replica in sync that holds the latest transaction to be promoted, as an operator's promotion would, grants that replica its vote and becomes its replica, so that writes go on at once; it serves again once the cause is fixed and it restarts. By hand. Make a replica its shard's primary through a coordinator, which asks the shard's copies again so that writes go to it at once: or on the replica itself, chloedb-server promote http://127.0.0.1:7720 ( POST /v1/admin/promote {replicas?, write_acks?, force?} ). Either way the replica asks for the votes of a majority of its shard's copies (the servers its cluster map names, or the replicas named), as a handover: a primary that serves grants it, steps down and follows the new primary, and replicas grant it although their primary calls. A copy refuses its vote to a replica missing a transaction it holds, and under writes the primary holds one or two the replica has not applied yet, so the replica catches up first: a primary asked whether it would vote in a handover takes no writes from then until it answers the vote, for 1 s at most (503, not applied, which a coordinator sends again, to the new primary once elected). It asks that primary for its vote before the other copies, and answers none of its calls until it voted: a copy that voted would tell the primary the new term in its answer, and a primary that knows a term grants no vote in it (it would be fenced and not step down). A write the old primary took before it voted but applied only after (it waited for the writer) is answered 409, in doubt: the new primary does not hold it. With --failover-after-ms , a primary that did not hear of the handover may serve reads under its lease for a moment more, so unless every copy that said it was the primary voted, the new primary takes writes only once that lease has run out ( writes_in_ms in the answer, the failover delay at most; 503 until then). The answer says what each copy said ( votes ), and is 409 when the replica loses, which names the copies that refused and why. With no cluster map and no replicas named, the copies are the replica's own --replicas . The new primary calls each copy with the cluster's token, so a promotion asked for with an API key may name only the copies the replica knows (those its cluster map names for its shard, and its --replicas ), and is refused (403) before any call otherwise; the token's holder may name any. The replica first asks the copies whether they would vote for it, which stops the primary taking writes, and waits up to 0.5 s until it holds the newest transaction one holds. Then it asks each copy its last transaction ( Hello ). While one holds a later transaction than the replica, or a newer term, it refuses (409): promote the copy with the latest transaction instead ( seq_no in each one's /v1/health ). force ( --force ) skips the checks and the votes: the replica begins a term above every term it knows and ships to the shard's other copies (or the replicas named). Use it only when a majority of the copies is lost for good: writes answered by the copies that are gone, and not by this one, are lost. A replica that knows no other copy of its shard (it has no cluster map and no --replicas , and none are named) is promoted only with force . The replicas follow the new term. One that holds transactions of the old term past the new primary's last (shipped to it, but not to the replica promoted) is sent a copy of the new primary, which drops them. An old primary that is still up and did not vote is fenced the first time it ships to a copy of the new term, or the new primary ships to it: it refuses writes (409) and reads (503), Hello and /v1/health say replaced_by_term , and a write that was waiting for its replicas is refused (409) rather than answered. With --failover-after-ms it then becomes a replica of the new primary; without, it keeps the new term beside its database and starts again as a primary only with --promote : start it with --replica instead, and what it took after its last shipment is dropped by a copy of the new primary. A stopped replica is made the primary of a new term by starting it with --promote (and --replicas ), without the checks or votes. A database that was a replica, or whose primary another replaced, does not start with --replicas alone. What a failover keeps depends on --write-acks . With all and majority , a write answered is on a majority of the copies, and the elected copy holds it. With primary , writes the old primary answered but had not shipped are lost. Until the old primary learns of the new term, it takes writes: with all and majority they are not answered (no majority acknowledges them, and they are dropped when it follows the new primary), but with primary one it takes meanwhile is answered and later dropped. A coordinator's /v1/health flags a shard two of whose copies say they are its primary ( primaries , and a warning ): the older has not heard of the newer yet. Through a coordinator. A coordinator asks each replica its role ( Hello ) and sends a shard's writes to its primary alone: the primary of the newest term that no other replaced. While a shard has replicas but none answers as its primary, its writes are refused (503). A write that the primary refuses (409) or that finds it down (503) makes the coordinator ask the shard's copies again, and the write goes once to the copy that took its place, if one did; a refusal by a primary that is still the primary, such as a conditional write's, is not sent again, and costs one Hello . Reads go to any copy that serves them (below); after a copy fails a read, the shard's primary is asked next. Reads on a replica. A replica serves reads once a call from its primary since it started reached the primary's last transaction (each call says whether the replica is behind), and stops when a call finds it behind, when it fails to apply a batch, and when it installs a copy, until a call finds it caught up again. Meanwhile reads and snapshot leases get 503 (\"read from another copy or the primary\"), and a coordinator asks the shard's next copy. So a replica serves reads while its primary's last call, a second ago at most, found it caught up. One that its primary cannot reach does not know it fell behind, and goes on serving what it holds: a read that must see a given write names it. /v1/health shows replication . On a primary: its id , term , last number ( seq_no ), write_acks , global_checkpoint , the newer term of a primary that replaced it ( replaced_by_term ), and each replica's url , seq_no , behind , in_sync , error , whether it is being sent a copy ( copying ), the copies it installed, how many times its rows were compared ( verifications ), found to differ ( divergences ) or could not be compared ( verify_failures ), the calls it refused for holding another transaction under the term and number they followed ( txn_mismatches ), and the last comparison ( verified : seq_no , ms_ago , differing_slots , error ); also verify_every_s . On a replica: its id , term , seq_no , the primary it follows and that primary's term, the global_checkpoint the primary last sent, called_ms_ago , the copies of the primary it installed ( restores ), whether it serves_reads , the reads that waited for a write ( read_waits ) or were refused ( reads_refused ), the digests it computed for its primary ( digests ), the calls it refused for holding another transaction than its primary's ( txn_mismatches ), and, until it applies a call or installs a copy, that transaction ( differs : seq_no , term ). The metrics are the chloedb_replica* families."],
["server.html#the-cluster-simulator","chloedb-server","The cluster simulator","chloedb-sim , built with the server, runs a shard's copies and two coordinators in a simulated cluster, one seed at a time. A seed draws a run: three or five copies with automatic failover, --write-acks all or majority , clients that write, read from the primary and now and then promote a replica by hand, and 20 s of faults: a network that delays, reorders and drops calls and cuts copies apart (both ways or one way), copies that crash and start again, sometimes a majority of them, and clocks that run up to 1% apart; in half the seeds, copies that pause (a process stopped or stalled: its calls and timers wait while its clock runs on, now and then for about as long as a primary's lease), and in half, links that slow down for up to 8 s, one way or both, to one other copy or every other. The copies make the protocol's decisions as chloedb-server/src/cores/shard.rs makes them, which the server runs too ( replication.rs does its I/O), on a model of their databases; the simulator does their I/O one event at a time, in virtual time, every choice drawn from the seed, so a seed replays exactly. After the faults, the network heals, every copy starts again, and the run checks that one copy began each term, that every primary elected after a write was acknowledged in an older term holds it, that a primary served no read without a write acknowledged before the read was sent, that two copies holding the same number of the same term hold the same history up to it, and that the shard settled: one primary, every copy holding what it holds. A seed runs in about 35 ms of one core (20 s of faults, 20 to 35 s of settling), so 20,000 seeds take 174 s on 4 cores (measured on a cloud Intel Xeon at 2.80 GHz, 4 cores, 2026-11-01, built with --profile ci ). cargo test --test all sim:: runs 400 seeds ( CHLOEDB_SIM_SEEDS=A..B for others), checks that a seed replays, that 400 seeds with 15% of calls lost keep the promises, that a primary cut off from every other node for a minute is replaced once (300 seeds of three copies with no other faults), and that the first, third, fourth and fifth bugs below, put back, are found again. It found five bugs, which the server and the decisions the simulator runs fix, and --bug puts back in the simulator. asks-a-known-term : a replica whose pre-vote a copy refused, as it knew the term asked for, asked for the same term again. Once a minority of the copies voted in a term for a candidate that lost, the copies holding the most transactions never learned the term from a primary and asked for it forever, refused each time, while the copies that knew it were behind: no primary until one was promoted with force (12 of the first 2,000 seeds). A replica now asks above the newest term any copy told it. takes-writes-in-a-lease : a replica promoted by hand took writes at once. A primary that did not hear of the handover still served reads under its lease, which the votes of the handover did not wait out, and served them without the new primary's writes (one seed in 20,000). The new primary now waits out the lease before it takes writes, unless the primary it replaced voted for it. refuses-older-terms : a copy that began a term and wrote nothing in it, or installed a copy of a primary that had not, refused the transactions of an older term that a newer primary held past its last, as if they came from a replaced primary, and never caught up: with two such replicas of three, the shard took no more writes (one seed in 20,000). The terms that hold no transactions are now dropped for those the primary ships. waits-for-a-first-call : a shard's first replicas granted no votes until a primary had called them, as if they had joined a serving shard. When the first primary stopped before any of them heard from it, no copy was ever elected, and the shard took no writes until one was promoted with force (20 of the first 2,000 seeds with 15% of calls lost, --drop-ppm 150000 ; none at the default rates). The shard's first copies, which its primary counts from the start, now vote from the start, and a primary keeps that it counts too, so that it votes once it starts again as a replica. replaces-the-copy-it-elected : with the primary cut off, each round of an election waited for its answer until the call's timeout, and a replica that had just granted a vote asked to be elected itself as soon as its primary's silence said, a moment later. The copy it had voted for was still a replica while its round waited, granted that replica's pre-vote, and the replica's newer term then fenced it once it was elected: the two replicas replaced each other for as long as the cut lasted (28 terms in the minute of one seed; the chaos monkey's primary-isolated saw 52 terms in 3 minutes on real servers). Found with a cut placed by hand, which cargo test --test all sim:: runs; either fix alone stops it, and the server has both. Two more are put in by hand, to check what stands behind a fix the replication tests found. commits-after-stepping-down has a primary's writer commit a client's write after the primary stepped down and applied its new primary's first batch, under that term and the next number, as the server did before a write was refused once its term is over: the copy then holds another transaction than the new primary's under one term and number. Alone it loses no acknowledged write and leaves no two histories apart under one term and number, as the copy refuses the call that follows the primary's transaction there by its digest and is sent a copy ( cargo test --test all sim:: checks it in 300 seeds); but a copy whose stale write came after its primary's last stays ahead of it under the primary's own term, which the server leaves to an operator (2 of the 300). With trusts-the-same-term too, replicas apply by term and number alone, as before protocol 20, and the copies' histories stay apart; the test finds such a seed. votes-with-a-lost-volume (audit N-E2) has a replica started with --replica on an empty database vote for any copy that holds what it holds, and count itself a member at the first call that says so, caught up or not, as it did before. --lost-volume places what that breaks: in a shard of three, copy 2 is cut off for 1 to 3 s while copies 0 and 1 acknowledge writes, copy 1 loses its volume a moment before the cut ends and starts again empty, and copy 0 crashes meanwhile, for 2 to 5 s. With the bug, copy 1's vote elects copy 2, and the writes it lacks are lost; without, copy 1 refuses it and copy 0 is elected once it is back. cargo test --test all sim:: runs 400 such seeds and finds the bug again. A volume is lost only while every other copy has heard from a primary: should one have heard from none, no copy could tell a lost volume from a first start, and the two empty copies would elect one of them, which only a flag saying it is a first start could prevent. campaigns-uncounted has a copy that no primary counts a member ask to be elected, as it could before 0.3.5, and --uncounted-candidate places what that breaks, which TLC found first: copy 1 is cut off for 1 to 3 s while copies 0 and 2 acknowledge writes, copy 2 loses its volume a moment before the cut ends and starts again empty, and copy 0 crashes within 300 ms of its return, while it catches copy 2 up one transaction a call. With the bug, copy 2, holding more than copy 1 and less than copy 0 acknowledged, is elected with copy 1's vote, and the writes it lacks are lost; without, it asks for no votes until a primary counts it. cargo test --test all sim:: runs 400 such seeds and finds the bug again. --cluster runs a second world: a cluster whose map changes and whose slots move while it serves. A seed draws two or three shards of one server each and a spare server, two coordinators whose clients write, delete and read keys, and an operator who asks the first coordinator now and then for a move, a rebalance or a drain, to add the spare as a shard, to remove an empty shard, to change a shard's servers, or to finish a move left in the map. 20 s of faults crash, restart and pause servers and coordinators (some pauses outlast a hold on writes), cut them apart, drop calls and, in half the seeds, slow the links between them for a while. Servers take maps and check what is routed to them, and coordinators change the map and move slots, as chloedb-server/src/cores/slots.rs decides, which the server runs too. The run checks that a read sees every write and delete of its key acknowledged before it was sent, and finds the key on one shard at most; and, once the faults end, that each key's row is where the map puts it, once, holding what its writes left, that no shard holds rows outside its slots, and that the cluster settled: no move under way, every server holding the map. A seed takes about 12 ms of one core, so 60,000 take 176 s on 4 cores (same machine, built with --profile ci ). cargo test --test all sim_cluster:: runs 400 seeds ( CHLOEDB_SIM_CLUSTER_SEEDS=A..B for others), checks that a seed replays, and finds each bug below again. It found six bugs in map changes and moves, which the server and the decisions the simulator runs fix, and --bug puts back: flips-an-unheld-move : the shard slots moved from took the flip although the writes to them had not been held since their last copy: it had started again, or its hold had run out while its coordinator paused, and the writes it took meanwhile reached no copy. The cleanup after the flip deleted them (7 of the first 3,000 seeds). The shard now refuses the flip then (409), and the move is given up. takes-late-copies : a copy of rows sent before a move was given up reached the shard they were to move to after it had deleted what it was sent, and stayed there once the move ended: the row was on two shards (1 of 3,000). A shard now takes copied rows only while the move copies to it, and deletes what it was sent once the writes under way have committed. changes-around-the-source : a change made while slots copied was given to every server at once. When the shard the slots leave had taken the move's flip but its answer was lost, a change made after it from the map before (the move given up, say) went to the other servers under the same epoch: two maps of one epoch, under which reads missed rows of the moving slots (1 of 3,000). That shard now takes every change first while slots copy from it, and refuses one that does not follow the map it holds. Put back now that a map in doubt is given again, the bug shows more often: a flip the shard refused reaches the other servers, which leaves it behind, and the move never ends, with rows on two shards (51 of the first 1,500 seeds). routes-by-a-map-no-server-took : a coordinator routed by a change that every server had missed, and made its next change from a map no server held. With both coordinators changing the map that forked it more often (10 of the first 2,000 seeds with --racing , against 5). A change that no server took now fails (503). gives-an-epoch-twice : a coordinator whose change failed although a server took its map, or whose answers did not come (links slowed down for a while), made its next change from the map before, under the same epoch. A server that took the first map late refused the second, and the cluster held two maps of one epoch, neither taking the other (2 of the first 20,000 seeds; its test slows links in every seed, which finds it sooner). The coordinator now keeps that map in doubt and gives it again first ( above ). forgets-a-map-given-again : a coordinator gave its map in doubt again, one server took it while another did not answer, and the change asked for then failed on it (an empty shard to remove that the map in doubt had removed already, say). The coordinator went on routing by the map before, so its next change, made while the server that took the map in doubt was down, gave that epoch again: two maps of one epoch (1 of the 30,000 nightly seeds of 2026-11-02, and 2 of 133,000 put back). The coordinator now routes by a map in doubt once a server takes it again, whatever comes of the change made from it. With both coordinators changing the map at once ( --racing ), 5 of the first 2,000 seeds still fork it: each gives a map of the same epoch to different servers before the other's reaches them. So do 4 of the first 40,000 when a coordinator that starts again forgets the map it had in doubt ( --forgetful , as the server's coordinator does; the simulated ones keep it by default). Only one place that orders the changes ends both, which is the consensus work planned next; until then, change a cluster through one coordinator at a time. CI runs chloedb-sim after the server's tests on every change: the first 3,000 seeds of each world, and 1,000 of the first world with 15% of calls lost. The nightly run, and a run started by hand, run 30,000 of each that no run checked before, a new range each day. A seed that fails is listed in the run's summary, and replays with --seed N --trace . The simulator does not cover the database's own threads and files (the fault-injection tests do), nor the shards' copies within a cluster: each shard of the second world is one server."],
["server.html#the-chaos-monkey","chloedb-server","The chaos monkey","chloedb-chaos , built with the server, breaks a real cluster rather than a model of one. It starts chloedb-server processes on this machine (by default two shards of three copies with --failover-after-ms 2000 , two spare servers to replace copies with or to add as shards, and two coordinators), puts clients on it that search, read rows by key and add 1 to counters through the coordinators, and then injects faults. Each server's gRPC port sits behind a proxy of its own, which the cluster map names, so that every call between servers crosses it; each process runs as a uid of its own, so that firewall rules can cut one off from another; and with --disk-mb each server's data sits on a tmpfs of that size, so that its disk can fill. A supervisor starts again a process that exits, after --restart-after-ms (5 s), doubling while it keeps exiting soon after it starts, as a Kubernetes StatefulSet would; a process held down (a lost node) stays down. fault what it stands for kill , stop a crash the pod comes back from; a node lost until healed pause a frozen VM or a long stall (SIGSTOP), until healed or for --ms slow-cpu CPU throttling: stopped --pct of every 100 ms delay a slow network to a server: --ms each way on its gRPC port isolate , cut a partition: a server cut off from every other process, or the calls from one to another ( --oneway ) disk-fill a ballast file leaving --free-mb on its disk corrupt bit rot: bytes flipped in its largest data file, then a restart wipe a pod rescheduled without its volume: it starts again empty bad-token a bad configuration: started again with a wrong --cluster-token flood a runaway report job: --clients heavy queries without pause, under a key of its own hot-shard a hot partition: writers adding to a few keys of one shard as fast as they are answered A target is a process's name ( node4 , coord1 ) or a role, resolved against the map the coordinators route by: primary:N , replica:N , replicas:N , shard:N , coord , any-copy , any-replica . up prints the command center's address with its token, so the cluster can be watched, and fixed, from the UI while faults come and go. The writers keep a ledger of each counter's adds that were acknowledged and of those that may have applied (answered in doubt, a timeout, a dropped connection); check holds the writes, reads every counter and reports any below its acknowledged count (a lost write) or above what may have applied (a write applied twice). run plays each scenario ( --list lists them: crashes, lost nodes, a lost majority, a shard down, a frozen primary, a partitioned primary, a coordinator cut from a replica, slow networks and CPUs, a full disk, bit rot, a lost volume, a wrong token, a flood, a hot shard, a coordinator crash, a coordinator dying while it moves slots) with what brings the cluster back (Kubernetes restarting a pod, the network healing, or the admin call a command center button makes), and records for each the clients' calls by kind and outcome, second by second, when the coordinators' fleet health (Home) and alerts first showed it and when they cleared, how long the clients took to see 10 s without a failure, and the counters' check; events.jsonl holds what happened, in order. Cutting links needs iptables and filling disks a tmpfs, so both need Linux and root; the other faults run anywhere. cargo test --test all chaos:: runs one scenario, a primary killed under writes, on a shard of three copies."],
["server.html#reads-that-see-a-write","chloedb-server","Reads that see a write","A write's answer carries its sequence number ( seq_no ). A read with \"min_seq_no\": n ( /v1/query , /v1/sql , /v1/plan , /v1/term_stats ; QueryRequest.min_seq_no over gRPC) reads a version that holds transaction n, so a client reads its own writes from any copy: A replica that has not applied it waits for it, up to a second or the read's deadline, whichever comes first, and then answers 503: the primary holds it. The wait comes before the read takes a query slot . A primary, or a server without replication, holds every write it answered, so it refuses (409) a number past its last, as a snapshot lease taken before the write does. A server without --seq-no refuses it (400), and so does a write: a write sees every write before it. Through a coordinator, a write answers seq_nos , each shard's number (0 where it wrote nothing), and a read passes them back as \"min_seq_nos\": [...] ; each shard's copy then holds that shard's write. A replica that refuses the read is passed over for the shard's primary. A replica that speaks a protocol before 5 would not wait, so such a read is sent to the primary instead, without counting against the replica. A shard whose copies each take its writes (no primary) numbers them on each copy in its own order: its number is 0, and a read naming one there is refused (400). Sequence numbers are reused across a failover: a replica that applied transactions of a primary that then stopped, before a majority held them, holds numbers the next primary gives to other writes. So a write also answers the term of its transaction, seq_term (through a coordinator, seq_terms , one per shard), and a read that passes it back as \"min_seq_term\": t ( \"min_seq_terms\": [...] ; ?min_seq_term= and ?min_seq_terms= on a read by key) is served only by a copy whose transaction of that number is of that term. A replica whose transaction there is of another term answers 503, and a coordinator asks the shard's primary; a primary answers 409, as for a number past its last. A read without the term is checked by the number alone, as before. A replica also stops serving reads when a copy asks for its vote in a newer term than its primary's, granted or not, until a primary of that term calls it."],
["server.html#on-kubernetes","chloedb-server","On Kubernetes","chloedb-server operator runs ChloeDB clusters on Kubernetes. A StrataCluster resource names the shards, the copies of each shard, the first table's schema and what each server needs (image, volume size, failover delay, write acks, the Secrets of its admin token and of its cluster token ); the operator makes a StatefulSet of copies per shard, each copy a pod with its own volume, and the coordinators behind a Service named as the cluster, and changes the cluster through a coordinator's admin calls, one step every few seconds, until it is as the resource says. More shards join the cluster and slots move to them; fewer are drained first. More copies join their shards as replicas and are sent a copy of the primary; fewer leave their shards first, a primary being handed over before its pod goes. A pod that comes back without its volume joins its shard again. New settings for the servers (image, arguments, resources, failover delay, write acks, tokens) reach each copy when the operator restarts it, one at a time: replicas first, each primary handed over to a copy in step before its pod goes, and the next restart only once every copy is back and caught up. A copy still on older settings that never started (its pod pending, with requests no node has room for, or failing on a bad argument) restarts at once with the new ones instead of being waited for, so lowering the requests or rolling a bad setting back reaches it. A larger volume size grows each copy's volume in place, where its storage class allows it; a volume never shrinks. The copies elect their primaries themselves, so a dead pod costs a few seconds of its shard's writes whether the operator runs or not. The resource's status says what it found and does ( kubectl get strata -o wide ). The coordinators run as a service account of their own, which may read and change only their StrataCluster : the command center's Control center edits its whole spec and Cluster shows its phase, through GET /v1/kube (the spec, the status, and settled once the operator has seen the latest spec; {\"managed\": false} elsewhere), POST /v1/admin/kube/plan {\"spec\": {...}} (what a merge patch of the spec would change, the operator's steps for it, what to mind, and why it is refused if it is: a volume that would shrink, a size Kubernetes does not read, a spec the operator would not take; nothing is changed) and POST /v1/admin/kube {\"spec\": {...}} (the same patch, checked as the plan is, then applied; or the sizes alone, {\"shards\"?, \"replicas\"?, \"coordinators\"?} ; admin scope, audited, and noted on the resource with who made it). Both also take {\"rollback\": version} , the spec of an earlier version in the status's history (its rollout kept), which no gate holds; POST /v1/admin/kube/resume lifts the hold a gate put on a change, or one on a version that failed; POST /v1/admin/kube/upgrade {\"image\"?, \"plan\"?, \"force\"?} rolls out a new image ( upgrades ). The spec's rollout pauses a change ( paused ), holds it while a live number is over a limit ( gates : p99Ms , errorRatio , replicaBehind , diskUsedRatio , each over for holdAfterSeconds ), and lets each restarted copy serve soakSeconds before the next restarts. The spec's fields, what each change does, and a run on kind are in the deploy README ."],
["server.html#upgrades","chloedb-server","Upgrades","A new ChloeDB version is a new image: spec.image changed by hand ( kubectl patch , the command center's Rollout view, POST /v1/admin/kube/upgrade {\"image\": \"ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server:<sha>\"} ), or by the upgrader. (Outside Kubernetes, upgrade one server at a time: each shard's replicas, then a handover and its old primary, then the coordinators, as operate a cluster does; versions and compatibility says what a cluster of mixed versions serves.) The cluster answers queries and takes writes all along: The image is tried first. A pod of it starts on each node with copies ( <cluster>-preflight , which runs chloedb-server --help ), so that an image that cannot be pulled, or does not run on a node's architecture, is refused before any server restarts; the pods are deleted once each one ran. The coordinators roll with a surge. A new coordinator is ready before an old one stops ( maxSurge: 1 , maxUnavailable: 0 ), and each one stopping drains first ( --drain-ms ), so the Service sends it nothing more before it exits. A shard of fewer than three copies takes extra ones first ( rollout.extraCopies , on by default): a third copy of a shard of two, started with the new image and sent a copy of the shard's data, joins it and catches up before either of the shard's own copies restarts, and leaves (its volume deleted) once both run the new image. Of two copies, the one left while the other restarts is not a majority: without the third, the shard would serve no reads and take no writes for the seconds each restart takes. A new image that does not run fails on that copy, before any of the shard's own restarts. One shard at a time has its extra copies, so a node needs room for one more copy: the command center's Cloud view sizes each copy's requests to leave it, and a copy no node has room for waits for one (the status says why), failing the version after progressDeadlineSeconds . The copies restart one at a time , replicas first, each primary handed over to a copy in step before it restarts (one of the shard's own before an extra one). A query a copy was answering finishes, reads go to the other copies (when none serves one for now, as while a new primary waits out the old one's lease, the coordinator asks again every 100 ms for up to 3 s), and a write that meets the handover waits for the new primary (up to 10 s) rather than failing; a write that was in flight as the primary changed may be answered 409, in doubt (kept or not), and is not sent again, as sending it twice could apply it twice. The next copy waits until every copy is back and caught up. The version is verified for rollout.verifySeconds (60): every copy up, in sync and ready, no server crash-looping, the gates under their limits, and a SELECT COUNT(*) through the coordinators answered. Only then is the version ready ( status.history[].readyMs ). A step that fails fails the version: a server that does not start with the new settings (crash-looping, an image it cannot pull, a process that exits), a step that takes longer than rollout.progressDeadlineSeconds (600), a check of the verification that fails for holdAfterSeconds , or a gate over its limit. With rollout.onFailure: rollback (the default) the operator then sets the spec back to the last version that ran everywhere, as a rollback no gate holds ( changed-by: operator ), and the status's history marks the version failed , with the step and why; with hold it restarts nothing more until resumed ( POST /v1/admin/kube/resume ) or the spec changes. A rollback that fails is held, not rolled back again. GET /v1/admin/kube/versions?limit=30 lists the images of the repository a person may pick (the StrataCluster's, else --image-repository , by default ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server ): version tags, commit tags and latest , one per digest, the newest first, each with its revision and created , and whether it is current , newer or tracked ; architecture tags ( -amd64 , -arm64 ) are left out. It works without Kubernetes. GET /v1/kube adds versions , the images the copies run (the most run first, with the copies' short names), and a rollout's progress : done , total , per_copy_s , eta_s , started_ms and phase . The plan of an upgrade ( POST /v1/admin/kube/upgrade , /v1/admin/kube/plan ) counts the copies it restarts ( restarts , those whose image differs, and restart_copies ) and estimates how long it and its rollback take ( estimate_s , rollback_estimate_s , estimate_from : 30 s a copy plus the soak until a rollout was seen to its end, then that rollout's pace). The command center's Rollouts view offers these versions. The upgrader ( spec.upgrade ) follows a tag: every checkMinutes (10) it asks the registry which image the tag names ( track , by default the tag of spec.image , such as latest ), anonymously as public images allow, and reads the commit it was built from (the label org.opencontainers.image.revision ). With mode: suggest the new image waits in status.upgrade ( latest , available ) for a person to roll it out; with mode: on the operator rolls it out itself once the cluster is ready, pinned by its digest ( …@sha256:… ), so that every server runs the same build. An image rolled back is kept in status.upgrade.rejected and not rolled out again by the upgrader (by hand, \"force\": true ). status.upgrade.running says which digest how many servers run, read from their pods. The app engine ( spec.apps , whose image is its own) follows its tag the same way: the upgrader asks which image spec.apps.image names ( status.upgrade.apps ), and with mode: on the engine's Deployment runs that image by its digest, so a new chloedb-apps:latest restarts the engine on it within checkMinutes , without a kubectl rollout restart (an operator installed before this release does not, and needs its manifest applied again). The upgrader moves the servers, never the operator, which runs from its own Deployment ( strata-system/strata-operator ): upgrade the operator first, to the image the servers are to run ( kubectl -n strata-system set image deployment/strata-operator operator=IMAGE ; it applies its CRD itself when it starts). An operator older than its servers may ask what they refuse: a 0.3.5 coordinator adds a server only with the cluster's token, and an operator from before 0.3.5 sends the admin token, so a rollout's extra copy, a copy more or a new shard stops at 403 cluster_token_required . The operator says what it runs in status.operator (since 0.3.6: its version , and in a pod its image and the digest its node pulled), and the command center's Rollouts view says when the servers run a newer version or a later build of main than it, with the command that upgrades it. With extraCopies: false , a shard of two copies serves no reads and takes no writes while one of them restarts (a primary serves a read only while a majority of its copies answer it, and a write waits for a majority of the copies in sync): on a DigitalOcean cluster of 2 shards of 2 copies (2 nodes of 2 vCPU and 4 GB, nyc3, 2026-11-02), each restart cost about 5 s of failed reads, 849 of 28,314 in an upgrade. A shard of one copy has no copy to hand its primary to: with extraCopies , it takes two."],
["server.html#autoscaling","chloedb-server","Autoscaling","Experimental. The autoscaler (the spec's autoscale , /v1/admin/kube/autoscale/* , /v1/admin/autoscale/replay ) has no compatibility promise yet: its routes, settings and what it stores may change in any release, with no upgrade path promised. Its answers carry x-chloedb-experimental , and GET /v1/catalog marks its routes ( compatibility ). The spec's autoscale holds rules that change the cluster's copies, shards or volumes from how busy and how full its servers are. Each is a sentence, and a rule left out does nothing: rule fields (default) what it does grow busyAbove (0.7), forMinutes (10), add (1), max (5) when the busiest shard's copies use more than busyAbove of their cores for forMinutes , add copies to every shard, up to max shrink busyBelow (0.35), forMinutes (60), min (2) when they use less than busyBelow for forMinutes , one copy fewer in every shard, down to min volumes fullAbove (0.8), action ( grow ), maxSize ( 1Ti ), maxShards (16) when the fullest volume is more than fullAbove full, every volume half as big again ( grow , in place, as the storage class allows), up to maxSize , or one more shard ( addShard ), which takes its share of the slots, up to maxShards . Once its change is made, it does not fire again until the reading has fallen below what it was then (or gone back under fullAbove ): a storage class that cannot grow volumes in place, or deletes not merged away yet, keep the reading high, and the rule would otherwise grow the volumes or the shards every cooldown freeze a list of {from, to, allowGrow} ( Fri 16:00 , Mon 08:00 , true) weekly windows, in UTC, in which nothing changes, or only copies are added cooldownMinutes 20 a change waits this long after the one before, whoever made it They run in that order, and a change also waits until the cluster is as its spec says (phase Ready : every new copy has caught up). How busy a shard is: the mean, over its copies that answer, of the share of its cores each used over the last minute ( detail.minute.busy of each copy in /v1/cluster ); the volume's fullness is the fullest of any server's. A rule's clock starts again at every change. mode says what a rule that fires does: on , the operator changes the spec itself, noted as the autoscaler's change ( changed-by: autoscaler <version> ) in the resource's history; suggest , the change waits in status.autoscale.suggestion until a person applies it (a POST /v1/admin/kube of its patch) or dismisses it ( POST /v1/admin/kube/autoscale/dismiss , after which the rules wait their cooldown before they suggest again); off (the default), nothing, and status.autoscale.waiting says what the rules would do. The status also keeps the latest reading, the rules' clocks, and the last 30 changes made, suggested and dismissed. The rules are not part of a version of the cluster ( history ): the changes they make are, and a rollback leaves the rules as they are. The operator refuses rules that would undo each other ( shrink.busyBelow at or over grow.busyAbove , or min over max ). Every server keeps a row a minute of its load in its log store ( load ), for a week. POST /v1/admin/autoscale/replay {\"autoscale\": {...}, \"days\": 7} (admin) runs rules on it, without changing anything: from the cluster's size (the StrataCluster 's, or the cluster's as it is, or sizes: {replicas, shards, volume} ), it takes each ten minutes' busiest shard and shares its work (how busy × how many copies) among the copies the rules would have had, a new copy only after 20 minutes of catching up; a volume fills as its share of the data. With mode: suggest a change is made once a person would have applied it: 20 minutes later in office hours (Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 18:00 UTC), two hours otherwise. The answer has each step ( points : how busy with the rules and at the fixed size, the copies, the shards, the fullest volume, what a change waits for), each change ( events ), and a summary: the changes, the minutes over 85% busy with the rules and at the fixed size, the copy-hours of each, and the minutes waited for a person. A server that is not a coordinator replays its own load. POST /v1/admin/kube/plan adds sizes (shards and replicas, before and after) and estimate (rows to copy, seconds, the rates used, and a sentence). POST /v1/admin/autoscale/replay adds copies_now (the fewest and most copies a shard has now). With no StrataCluster and no given volume, it adds volume_from : the volume is the largest disk the copies report."],
["server.html#the-simulator-a-test-run-and-a-model-of-the-cluster","chloedb-server","The Simulator: a test run and a model of the cluster","The command center's Simulator ( #/simulator , admin) sends the traffic simulator 's mix at the cluster it runs on, grades how it held, and fits a model of the cluster that predicts other shapes: what a copy more, a shard more, other machines or more rows would hold, how fast they would answer, and what they would cost. Nothing in it changes the cluster's shape. A run, POST /v1/admin/simulator (admin, one at a time), goes in six phases: It creates a table of test documents of its own, strata_sim ( id , title , body , year , category , rating , emb ), and loads a quarter of docs (default 100,000): words by Zipf's law over a vocabulary of 30,000, 64 topics that the words, categories and vectors follow, years growing to 2024, ratings skewed high. Every document is a function of the seed and its number. It sends each kind of the mix alone, one request at a time, for alone_s (2.5 s): its latency, the shard calls it made (from the request's trace) and the CPU the copies and the coordinator spent on it (each copy's system.cpu_seconds in its health, before and after, less what they spent idle). Making the requests is this process's CPU and is taken off the coordinator's. It loads the rest, and 4. measures each kind again at the full size, and writes (inserts, updates and deletes of its own rows, at the profile's shares), and the mix's main search right after a one-row insert, an insert and a search at a time. It sends the mix, an open loop (each request at its planned time whatever the answers, timed from then), at a rising rate ( shape: \"ramp\" : from about an eighth of what the model expects, × factor (1.3) every step_s (8 s), until two steps in a row miss the SLO, max_steps at most) or at a steady one ( \"steady\" , rate for seconds , in 5 s windows). A step holds when the reads' p99 is within slo_p99_ms and their errors within slo_errors . It fits the model, grades the cluster, and drops the table (unless keep ; DELETE /v1/admin/simulator/table drops a kept one). The settings are the profile's ( profile : search-app , rag or today , the files of deploy/compare/profiles ), with mix (weights by kind), write_share , slo_p99_ms , slo_errors , target_qps (what the cluster must hold), estimate_docs (the rows to predict for: by default the cluster's own, when it holds more than the test) and machine (a size to price on: a name of the reference list, or {name, vcpu, memory_gib, usd_month} ) over them. GET /v1/admin/simulator answers the run under way (its phases and steps), the last 10 reports, the presets, the kinds and their SQL, and the reference machines; GET /v1/admin/simulator/runs/:id a report; POST /v1/admin/simulator/stop stops a run after its requests under way. Reports are kept beside the audit log ( <audit>.simulator.json , or CHLOEDB_SIMULATOR_STATE ). The model. A request is a call to every shard it needs (a search asks every shard, a key lookup one, as measured). A call's CPU is a fixed part, the key lookup's, and a part that grows with the rows a shard holds as it grew between the two sizes ( b·n^e , so e is 0 for work that does not grow and 1 for work that grows with the rows). The coordinator's CPU is a part a request and a part a shard asked. Each copy is a queue of as many servers as its machine has cores (Erlang C); reads spread over a shard's copies, and writes are applied on every copy by one writer a shard. A read's latency is its calls' latency as measured, its wait in the queues, and, for a search, the slowest of its shards. A row added makes the next search on each copy of its shard rebuild the part of the memtable it changed before it answers: the search right after an insert less one alone is that rebuild's time. That search waits all of it, searches that come during it wait for the rest, and it takes the copy's CPU; every copy rebuilds for itself, so copies add none of it back, and rows written in batches (fewer commits) cut it. Values set in place and deletes do not rebuild. The cores are scaled ( kappa ) so that the model holds what the rising rate measured; not when the servers share a machine (a cluster on one host), whose limit is the machine's. The report grades the cluster out of 100 (Healthy, Tight, Oversized, At risk, Undersized, Too slow) from its checks: capacity against the target, latency with no load against the SLO, what fills first (the copies' cores, the coordinator, or the writers), how evenly the shards and their copies worked, errors, writes, searches after writes (what the rebuild after an insert costs the searches and the room), a kind far heavier than its share, and what it costs. Its advice compares shapes with the cluster's, each with what it holds, its p99 at the target and its cost a month: a copy more, a shard more, twice or half the shards, a copy less, a coordinator more (when the coordinator fills first), bigger and smaller machines, the cheapest shape that holds the target with a quarter to spare, and, when the rebuilds after writes matter, the same rows written ten a request. POST /v1/admin/simulator/predict {\"run\"?, \"shards\"?: [..], \"copies\"?: [..], \"machine\"?, \"docs\"?, \"qps\"?, \"shape\"?: {shards, copies}} answers any other shapes by a report's model at once (the page's what-if: a grid of shards by copies, and the p99 by rate of the shape picked). Prices are DigitalOcean's list prices of 1 November 2026 for a node a copy and a node a coordinator. What the model leaves out: a copy that the coordinator sends fewer reads (the balance check says so), caches warming at another size, the HTTP or gRPC of real clients at the coordinator (the test's requests start inside it), and machines of another CPU family than the test's. A test on a cluster that serves traffic shares its cores with it: the page asks first, and a steady rate the cluster has room for measures without pushing it to its limit. Choosing a copy. The Simulator also compares the coordinator's ways of choosing which copy of a shard answers a read ( --route , --hedge-adaptive and --prefer-caught-up ), two ways: By the model, at once: POST /v1/admin/simulator/routing {variants?, clients?, fault?, value?, after_write?, seconds?, seeds?, run?, setup?} (a read: it sends nothing to the servers) runs a simulation of the cluster's shards and copies in virtual time for each setting at each count of closed-loop clients (by default 1, 16 and 64), and answers each one's throughput, p50 to p99.9, hedges a second, reads right after a write, and how shard 0's reads spread over its copies, with the best at each count. variants are {route, hedge_ms, hedge_adaptive, prefer_caught_up, label?} ; by default this coordinator's settings, then each route with and without adaptive hedging, and least cost not preferring caught-up copies. The model is calibrated by a test run's measurements ( run , or the newest report with a model: each kind's CPU a call and its spread, the copies' cores, the coordinator's own time), else by ChloeDB's numbers on DigitalOcean's c-2 copies. fault puts a what-if on copy 0 of shard 0: slow (answers value ms late, 30 by default), cpu (its machine runs value other busy threads, 2), stall (stalls value ms each second, 60), slots ( value calls at a time, 1), or behind (a replica that applies writes value ms late, 50, with a fifth of the requests reading a row back right after writing it). Measured on the cluster: a test run of shape routing (admin, on a coordinator whose shards have copies) loads the test documents, times each kind alone (the model's calibration), then sends the mix through a coordinator made for the test for each setting ( variants , at most 6), on the cluster's own copies, with this coordinator's other settings: at each count of clients (at most 4), rounds times (3 by default), each setting step_s seconds a turn after two untimed, the settings taking turns and each round starting at another. after_write is the share of requests that write a row and read it back as the write answered. The report holds each turn, each setting's medians over the rounds with their range, the model's prediction of the same settings beside them and how far off it was, and the best setting at each count. This coordinator's own settings do not change. The model is a model: on DigitalOcean (c-2 copies, the coordinator on a c-32) it matched one shard of three copies within about 5% on throughput and 15% on p99, but put three shards' throughput 14 to 48% too high, and had adaptive hedging cut the p99.9 of three shards where measured it raised it. Measure before changing a production cluster's settings."],
["server.html#in-the-cloud","chloedb-server","In the cloud","Experimental. Making clusters in the cloud ( /v1/admin/cloud/* , /v1/admin/aws/* , /v1/admin/digitalocean/* , /v1/admin/gcp/* ) has no compatibility promise yet: its routes, settings and what it stores may change in any release, with no upgrade path promised. Its answers carry x-chloedb-experimental , and GET /v1/catalog marks its routes ( compatibility ). The command center's Cloud view plans a cluster on Amazon EKS, on DigitalOcean Kubernetes (DOKS) or on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), picked at its top, from what the cloud says now, and writes the files that make it. It also makes the cluster itself , installs ChloeDB on it, resizes its nodes on GKE and deletes it, once the cluster's name is typed again. Every cloud lays the copies out the same way: copy c of shard s goes on node ( s + c ) mod nodes , so a shard's copies are on different nodes while there are at least as many nodes as copies, and each copy's requests are 90% of its share of what its node has left for it: its memory and CPU less what the node keeps for itself and its cloud's agents, and less the coordinators, the operator and CoreDNS that may run beside it. With fewer than three copies a shard, a node's share is split one way more, for the extra copy an upgrade adds beside a shard's copies. Their routes need the admin scope. On AWS The view reads the region's machines, their prices and the account's quota from AWS: route what GET /v1/admin/aws the credentials as far as they are shown: where they come from, the key id masked, the account and ARN; never the secret nor a session token PUT /v1/admin/aws/credentials {\"access_key_id\", \"secret_access_key\", \"session_token\"?, \"region\"} : checked with STS ( GetCallerIdentity ), then kept; keys AWS refuses are a 400 and are not kept; audited by masked key id DELETE /v1/admin/aws/credentials forgets them and deletes their file; nothing in the AWS account changes; audited GET /v1/admin/aws/regions the regions the account has enabled GET /v1/admin/aws/machines?region= the region's current-generation machines ( DescribeInstanceTypes ) with vCPUs, memory, local disk, network, the pods EKS lets each run, the on-demand Linux price (the Price List API) and the lowest spot price, the account's on-demand and spot vCPU quotas (Service Quotas), and what an EKS cluster, a GB-month of gp3 and a public IPv4 address cost; kept six hours ( &fresh=1 fetches again) POST /v1/admin/aws/plan {\"name\", \"region\", \"machine\", \"nodes\", \"spot\", \"volume_gb\", \"public_nodes\"?, \"api_cidrs\"?, \"cluster\": {StrataCluster spec}} : which node each copy goes on, each copy's share of a node, the cost an hour and a month line by line, the quota it needs, warnings, a CloudFormation template (a VPC over three zones with the nodes in private subnets that reach out through a NAT gateway, or with public_nodes in public subnets with an address each and no gateway to pay for; the EKS cluster with the caller as its administrator and its public endpoint open to api_cidrs , CIDR blocks that must include this server's address, or with none to any address, which a warning says; a managed node group; the EBS CSI driver), the operator's and the cluster's manifests, and the commands that make it and delete it. Spot nodes for shards of fewer than three copies are warned of: two nodes taken back at once can take a shard's every copy POST /v1/admin/aws/validate the plan, and CloudFormation's own check of its template ( ValidateTemplate : free, creates nothing) The credentials are kept in <db>.aws.json beside --db ( --aws-file names another file), written readable by its owner only; a coordinator without --aws-file keeps them in memory. Without saved ones the view signs with AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID , AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY and AWS_SESSION_TOKEN . Every call is signed by the server (SigV4) and only reads: an IAM user with this policy is enough, and the view copies it: The plan's commands then make the cluster with your own aws and kubectl : aws cloudformation create-stack (and its wait), aws eks update-kubeconfig , and kubectl apply of the two manifests. To delete it, delete the StrataCluster and its volume claims first (the copies' EBS volumes are the driver's, not the stack's, and stay and cost otherwise), then the stack. A new AWS account often has a vCPU quota of 0, or 5, for on-demand and spot instances: the view shows the quota against what the plan needs and links to the request for more. On DigitalOcean The view reads the sizes DOKS takes in a region, their prices, DOKS's versions and the account's droplet limit from DigitalOcean's API: route what GET /v1/admin/digitalocean the token as far as it is shown: where it comes from, masked ( dop_…7f3c ), and its team; never the token PUT /v1/admin/digitalocean/token {\"token\"} : checked with DigitalOcean ( GET /v2/account ), then kept; a token DigitalOcean refuses is a 400 and is not kept; audited by masked token and team DELETE /v1/admin/digitalocean/token forgets it and deletes its file; nothing in the DigitalOcean account changes; audited GET /v1/admin/digitalocean/regions the regions DOKS runs in GET /v1/admin/digitalocean/machines?region= the sizes a DOKS node may be in the region (default nyc3 ), with vCPUs, memory, disk, transfer and the price an hour and a month, whether its CPUs are shared, dedicated, premium Intel or premium AMD, DOKS's versions, the account's droplet limit and the droplets it runs, and what a highly available control plane and a GiB-month of volume cost; kept six hours ( &fresh=1 fetches again) POST /v1/admin/digitalocean/plan {\"name\", \"region\", \"machine\", \"nodes\", \"ha\", \"version\"?, \"volume_gb\", \"cluster\": {StrataCluster spec}} : which node each copy goes on, each copy's share of a node, the cost an hour and a month line by line (the nodes, the control plane, the volumes), the droplets it needs, warnings, the body of DOKS's create request, the operator's and the cluster's manifests (the cluster's volumes on do-block-storage ), and the commands that make it and delete it POST /v1/admin/digitalocean/validate the plan, and DigitalOcean's answers now, fetched again: whether the account is active, DOKS runs in the region and takes the size there, offers the version, and the nodes fit the droplet limit beside the droplets running. Creates nothing The token is kept in <db>.digitalocean.json beside --db ( --digitalocean-file names another file), written readable by its owner only; a coordinator without --digitalocean-file keeps it in memory. Without a saved one the view uses DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN or DIGITALOCEAN_ACCESS_TOKEN . The server only reads ( GET /v2/account , /v2/sizes , /v2/kubernetes/options and /v2/droplets ), so a read-only token is enough for the view; the plan's commands need one that may create clusters. Prices are DigitalOcean's: a node costs its size's hourly price up to its monthly price (672 hours; a v5 size has no monthly cap, and the view says so), the standard control plane is free and a highly available one $40 a month, and volumes $0.10 a GiB-month. The plan sets ha and auto_upgrade itself, since DOKS makes control planes highly available by default from Kubernetes 1.36. Its commands make the cluster with your own doctl and kubectl : doctl kubernetes cluster create (which waits, and points kubectl at the cluster), then kubectl apply of the two manifests. To delete it, delete the StrataCluster and its volume claims, then the cluster ( doctl kubernetes cluster delete --dangerous also deletes the volumes and load balancers it made). On Google Cloud The view reads the machine types GKE takes in a region, their prices, GKE's versions and the project's quotas from Google's APIs, with no key : ChloeDB refuses service account keys. Its calls use the credentials gcloud keeps where the server runs (application default credentials), and each takes an access token of an hour from them; on a Compute Engine VM or in a GKE pod, it asks the metadata server instead. Sign in as yourself, acting as a service account that may make GKE clusters (your account needs the Service Account Token Creator role on it): The server then calls as chloedb-deployer , and the project's audit logs say so. Credentials of a key file ( service_account ) are refused, and your own sign-in without --impersonate-service-account works but is warned of: its calls are made, and logged, as you. A server on a Kubernetes cluster outside Google Cloud uses workload identity federation instead. route what GET /v1/admin/gcp where the credentials come from (gcloud's file and the service account it impersonates, a federation's file with its pool, provider and token file, or the metadata server's account and, on GKE, the cluster it runs in), the project and where it was read from (saved here, GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT , the impersonated account's address, or the metadata server), the saved project, and on a coordinator run by the operator outside GKE the commands that set up federation for its account ( federation ); never a token. Asks Google nothing PUT /v1/admin/gcp/project {\"project\", \"region\"?} : checked with Google ( compute.projects.get : a project the credentials may not read is a 400 and is not kept), then kept with who it was checked as; audited DELETE /v1/admin/gcp/project forgets it and deletes its file; nothing in Google Cloud changes; audited GET /v1/admin/gcp/regions the project's Compute Engine regions and their zones GET /v1/admin/gcp/machines?region= the machine types a GKE node may be in the region (default the saved one, else us-central1 ), from the first three zones (E2, N1, N2, N2D, C2, C2D, C3, C3D and T2D, x86-64, without GPUs), each priced on demand and Spot from the Cloud Billing Catalog (a vCPU and a GiB of the family, so a shared-core E2 costs its share), GKE's versions in the Regular release channel and the one a plan takes (its default), the region's and the project's quotas, the project's VPC networks and the region's subnetworks, and what GKE's fee, a GiB-month of balanced and SSD Persistent Disk and a node's public IPv4 address cost; kept six hours ( &fresh=1 fetches again) POST /v1/admin/gcp/plan {\"name\", \"region\", \"zone\"?, \"machine\", \"nodes\", \"max_nodes\"?, \"spot\"?, \"regional\"?, \"version\"?, \"volume_gb\", \"boot_gb\"?, \"network\"?, \"subnetwork\"?, \"workload_identity\"?, \"cluster\": {StrataCluster spec}} : which node each copy goes on, each copy's share of a node, the cost an hour and a month line by line (the nodes, GKE's fee, the volumes, the boot disks and the nodes' addresses), the quotas it needs (the family's vCPUs, CPUS_ALL_REGIONS , IN_USE_ADDRESSES , SSD_TOTAL_GB , INSTANCES ), warnings, the body of GKE's create request, the operator's and the cluster's manifests (the cluster's volumes on standard-rwo ), the commands that make it and delete it, and its Workload Identity binding POST /v1/admin/gcp/validate the plan, and Google's answers now, fetched again: the region, the machine type in the zone, the version, the quotas, the network, and which of the permissions making a cluster takes the credentials hold (Resource Manager's testIamPermissions ). Creates nothing GET /v1/admin/gcp/nodes?cluster= a GKE cluster's node pools ( cluster is its name, projects/P/locations/L/clusters/N , as the cloud list shows it; without it, the cluster this server runs in): each pool's machine type, Spot or not, zones, nodes and autoscaler POST /v1/admin/gcp/nodes {\"cluster\"?, \"pool\"?, \"nodes\"?, \"min_nodes\"?, \"max_nodes\"?} : resizes a node pool (default strata , or the cluster's only one): nodes in all, spread evenly over its zones ( setSize ), and max_nodes turns GKE's autoscaler on between min_nodes and it ( setAutoscaling , BALANCED ), max_nodes: 0 off. A 409 while GKE is busy with the cluster; audited. More nodes bill the project more The project (never a credential) is kept in <db>.gcp.json beside --db ( --gcp-file names another file); a coordinator without --gcp-file keeps it in memory. Without a saved one the view uses GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT , CLOUDSDK_CORE_PROJECT or the impersonated service account's project, and asks Google nothing until someone says to. The calls read Compute Engine ( compute.regions.list and get , compute.machineTypes.list , compute.networks.list , compute.subnetworks.list , compute.projects.get ), GKE's server config and the Cloud Billing Catalog, so the project needs the Compute Engine, Kubernetes Engine, Cloud Billing and Cloud Resource Manager APIs enabled. Prices are Google's list prices: a node costs its family's vCPU and memory prices (Spot's when it is Spot), a zonal or regional cluster $0.10 an hour of GKE's fee (the free tier takes $74.40 a month off one zonal cluster of a billing account, which the plan does not count), balanced disks $0.10 a GiB-month for the copies' volumes and the nodes' boot disks, and each node's public IPv4 address $0.005 an hour. A new project's quotas are small ( CPUS_ALL_REGIONS is often 12 vCPUs, three 4-vCPU nodes): the view shows each against what the plan needs and links to the request for more. The plan gives the nodes the network default unless it names one, and says so when the project has none ( gcloud compute networks create default --subnet-mode=auto makes one). A cluster planned here annotates its coordinators' Kubernetes service account to act as the service account that planned it, through Workload Identity (the operator's coordinatorAccountAnnotations ; \"workload_identity\": \"none\" leaves it out, or names another account). It needs a binding that only someone who may change that service account's IAM policy can make, once, and the plan gives it ( identity.command ): With it, the new cluster's own command center reaches Google Cloud as that account with no laptop, to see and resize its nodes; without it the cluster runs as well, and reaches Google Cloud only through the server that made it. From a cluster outside Google Cloud A command center on another cloud's Kubernetes (DigitalOcean's, EKS, a cluster of your own) reaches Google Cloud with no key through workload identity federation : Kubernetes gives the coordinators' account, <cluster>-coordinator , a token for a pool's provider; Google's Security Token Service trusts it, because the provider holds the cluster's signing keys, and exchanges it for a token that impersonates the service account. The credentials are then a federation's file ( \"type\": \"external_account\" ), which the server reads as it reads gcloud's: its token from a file (or a URL), exchanged at sts.googleapis.com , then generateAccessToken for the service account, an hour each. A file that runs a program for its token ( credential_source.executable ), that takes AWS's own credentials ( environment_id ), or that sends its tokens anywhere but Google's APIs is refused. On such a coordinator, GET /v1/admin/gcp gives the commands for its own account, and the Cloud view shows them. Someone who may change the project's IAM runs them once, with kubectl pointed at the cluster (for the StrataCluster e2e in strata ): The provider is given the cluster's signing keys ( --jwk-json-path ), so the cluster's issuer need not be reachable from Google; should the cluster change its keys, run gcloud iam workload-identity-pools providers update-oidc with them the same way. credentials.json holds no secret: only where the token is and which provider and service account it is for. The operator's gcpFederation mounts the coordinators' token (renewed by Kubernetes) and the file in /var/run/secrets/gcp and sets GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS , restarting the coordinators once. Only the binding's account, in that namespace of that cluster, may act as the service account, and each call is in the project's audit logs as the service account, through the pool. Making it from the view Create it makes the planned cluster with the saved key or token. The server asks the cloud for it, installs ChloeDB through the new cluster's own API, follows its copies until they answer, and deletes it when asked. The routes need the admin scope, and every change is audited with who asked (a new cluster also with its nodes and cost an hour): route what GET /v1/admin/cloud/clusters the clusters made from this server, newest first. Each has its phase ( creating , installing , starting , ready , deleting , deleted , failed ), the cloud's progress, the last 60 steps it went through, the StrataCluster's status and a line summing it up, the cost so far at the planned price, why it failed, when it deletes itself and what it will have cost by then, the hub connection that opens it ( connection ), and, once ChloeDB runs, the commands that reach it with kubectl ( kubectl -n strata port-forward svc/<name> 7700:7700 ) POST /v1/admin/cloud/clusters {\"provider\": \"aws\" \\| \"digitalocean\" \\| \"gcp\", \"plan\": {the plan's body}, \"confirm\": \"<the cluster's name>\", \"delete_after_hours\"?, \"bring\"?} : planned again from a fresh catalogue, then made; 202. delete_after_hours (above 0, at most 720) has it delete itself that long after it was asked for, and without it \"keep\": true says it is kept until someone deletes it (a 400 without either); bring names tables of this server it copies in once it is ready. A 409 when this server keeps its clusters, or the account's key, in memory only (a coordinator does unless given --cloud-file and --aws-file , --digitalocean-file or --gcp-file , or the key in its environment): restarted, it would forget the cluster, which would bill the account until someone deletes it in the cloud's console. A 409 too when the account's quota or droplet limit is too small (on GKE, when the nodes it starts with need more vCPUs than the family's quota in the region or CPUS_ALL_REGIONS leaves), or when the cloud, or this server, already has a cluster of that name. A 400 unless confirm is the cluster's name. A plan has 100 nodes at most POST /v1/admin/cloud/clusters/{id}/expiry {\"hours\": n \\| null} : it deletes itself n hours from now, or no longer deletes itself; audited GET /v1/admin/cloud/clusters/{id} one of them POST /v1/admin/cloud/clusters/{id}/delete {\"confirm\": \"<the cluster's name>\"} : deletes it; 202 DELETE /v1/admin/cloud/clusters/{id} takes it off the list, once it is deleted or failed before the cloud made anything; a 409 while it may still be billed GET /v1/admin/cloud/found?provider=digitalocean (or ?provider=gcp , or ?provider=aws&region= ) the ChloeDB clusters the account holds that this server's list does not follow, made from another server or forgotten here: DOKS clusters tagged strata or named strata-* (every region), GKE clusters of the project labelled strata=chloedb (every location), or a region's CloudFormation stacks tagged strata.chloetech.ai/cluster , each with its cloud id, state, nodes and owner, and here for the one this server runs on POST /v1/admin/cloud/found/delete {\"provider\", \"cloud_id\", \"region\"?, \"confirm\": \"<the cluster's name>\"} : puts it on the list ( found: true ) and deletes it as Delete does, its volumes first; 202. A 400 unless confirm is its name, a 404 when the account no longer has it, a 409 for the cluster this server runs on (deleting it from here would stop the server halfway) A plan whose machine is \"smallest\" gets the cheapest machine whose nodes give each copy at least 1 GiB of memory and half a CPU (on EKS, also room for the pods a node runs), and says which in its machine ; the view starts there, and Create it sends the machine it showed. A plan's StrataCluster has a token unless it says otherwise ( tokenSecret: strata-token ): installing it makes that Secret in the namespace strata with a random token when it is not there, and the plan's commands make it too. It has no demo rows unless the plan asks ( cluster.demo ), so the cluster starts with no tables. The cloud's cluster is tagged with who asked for it (on DigitalOcean strata-owner:<name> , on GKE the label strata-owner , on AWS the stack's strata.chloetech.ai/owner ). When it deletes itself is not a tag: that time can move, and a tag left behind would tell a cleanup script to delete a cluster someone kept. This server deletes it, as Delete would, when its time comes, or as soon as it runs again if it was off then. Open its command center opens a cluster made here once ChloeDB runs on it, with nothing to install: this server reaches the cluster through its Kubernetes API (the API server's service proxy, to the coordinators' Service) with the cloud credentials it holds, so the cluster needs no public address and no kubectl . The first time, it reads the cluster's token from its Secret, makes a key of its own there with it ( hub <device> , admin), keeps that key and not the token, and adds the cluster to its connections ( POST /v1/admin/hub/adopt {\"made\": \"<id>\"} ). The API server takes the Authorization header for itself, so the key travels in X-Strata-Authorization: Bearer <key> , which chloedb-server reads when a request has no Authorization . What the server does, in order: The cloud makes the cluster. - On AWS, CloudFormation's CreateStack takes the plan's template ( CAPABILITY_IAM , rolled back on failure, tagged as made by chloedb-server). The server follows the stack and how many of its resources are made, and on failure gives the first failed resource's reason. This takes 15 to 20 minutes. - On DigitalOcean, POST /v2/kubernetes/clusters takes the plan's request, and the server follows the cluster's state and its nodes. This takes 5 to 10 minutes. - On Google Cloud, GKE's projects.locations.clusters.create takes the plan's request, and the server follows the cluster until it is RUNNING (an ERROR or DEGRADED cluster fails with GKE's message). This takes 5 to 10 minutes. ChloeDB is installed. The server reads the cluster's endpoint and certificate: from EKS's DescribeCluster , with a token signed as aws eks get-token signs one, from DOKS's short-lived credentials, or from GKE's cluster, with the Google access token itself, which GKE's API servers take. It then applies, with server-side apply (applying again changes nothing), the StrataCluster resource definition, the operator, the namespace, AWS's gp3 storage class and the StrataCluster (on GKE, with its coordinators' Workload Identity annotation). The cluster's API can take a few minutes to answer after the cloud says the cluster is up, so the server tries again for about ten minutes. The copies start. The server reads the StrataCluster's status until every copy of every shard answers. The cluster is then ready , and is read again every minute (on GKE, with its nodes: a resize or GKE's autoscaler moves its count and cost an hour). Deleting deletes the StrataCluster and every volume claim in the namespace strata . It waits up to ten minutes for the volumes' disks to go, then deletes the stack ( DeleteStack ), the DOKS cluster with what it made ( destroy_with_associated_resources/dangerous ) or the GKE cluster with its nodes, and follows it until it is gone. The clusters are kept in <db>.cloud.json beside --db ( --cloud-file names another file), which holds no key or token; a coordinator without --cloud-file keeps them in memory. A server that restarts carries on with those on their way. A create interrupted before the cloud answered finds the cluster by its name rather than making a second one. The cost so far is the plan's price times the hours since the cluster was asked for; the cloud's bill is the one to trust. The view's read-only keys are not enough to make a cluster: On AWS , the keys need cloudformation:CreateStack , DescribeStacks , DescribeStackEvents , DescribeStackResources and DeleteStack , eks:DescribeCluster , and the rights to make and delete what the template holds: a VPC with its subnets, route table and internet gateway, three IAM roles (with iam:PassRole on them), the EKS cluster, its access entry, node group and two add-ons (the pod identity agent and the EBS CSI driver). The keys' user or role becomes the cluster's administrator. On DigitalOcean , the token must be able to create, read and delete Kubernetes clusters and the volumes they make. On Google Cloud , the service account needs container.clusters.create , get , list , update and delete and container.operations.get (in roles/container.admin ), and iam.serviceAccounts.actAs on the nodes' service account ( roles/iam.serviceAccountUser ; by default the project's Compute Engine default service account). The view's Check with Google Cloud says which of them the credentials hold. The nodes pull the plan's image ( ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server:latest unless changed), so it must be one they can reach. The repository's Image workflow ( .github/workflows/image.yml ) publishes it: each push to main that changes ChloeDB or the server builds it for x86-64 and arm64 (Graviton) and tags it latest and the commit's sha, and a run started by hand publishes its branch under its sha. The nodes pull it without credentials once the package is public (the package's settings on GitHub, once, after its first publish); for a private one, give the cluster an image pull secret, or name an image of your own in the plan."],
["server.html#many-clusters-from-one-command-center","chloedb-server","Many clusters from one command center","Experimental. The hub ( /v1/admin/hub/* , /c/<name>/ ) has no compatibility promise yet: its routes, settings and what it stores may change in any release, with no upgrade path promised. Its answers carry x-chloedb-experimental , and GET /v1/catalog marks its routes ( compatibility ). A chloedb-server, typically the one on your laptop, is a hub : its command center reaches other clusters as well as its own. Its Connections view keeps a book of them, each an address and an API key of that cluster, and opens each one's command center at /c/<name>/ , where every call goes through the hub, which adds that cluster's key. The browser never holds a cluster's key. The hub keeps the keys in the macOS Keychain or the Secret Service ( secret-tool ) where there is one, else in <db>.hub-keys.json , readable by its owner only; the book itself, <db>.hub.json ( --hub-file ), holds no secret. Each forwarded call names the hub in x-strata-via: hub <device> ( --hub-device , else $USER@<host> ), so the cluster's audit log shows where it came from. A coordinator the Kubernetes operator runs is a hub too, and has no database to keep the book beside: it keeps the book and the keys in a Secret of its namespace, <cluster>-hub , an entry per connection. The operator makes that Secret and lets only the cluster's coordinators read and change it (their Role names it), so a rollout keeps the connections and every coordinator of the cluster shares them; each change rewrites its own connection's entry only, and each coordinator reads the Secret again every 20 seconds for the others' changes. Its device is the cluster, <namespace>/<name> . An operator that may not write Secrets in the namespace (strata-operator-secrets not bound there) makes neither, and the coordinators keep the book in memory, as Connections then says. Adding a connection checks its address, its certificate and its key ( /v1/whoami with it), and keeps nothing it refused. A certificate signed by a public authority is checked as any HTTPS call is; a self-signed one is shown by its SHA-256 and pinned once trusted, and a later certificate that differs is refused. Plain HTTP beyond loopback is refused unless the connection allows it ( allow_plain ). A key the cluster stops taking answers 502, saying so, rather than asking the page for a login. A cluster made from the Cloud view is reached through its Kubernetes API instead, with no address of its own. route what GET /v1/admin/hub who this hub is, where it keeps the keys, and its connections (their keys' scope and expiry, never a key) POST /v1/admin/hub/connections {\"name\", \"url\", \"key\", \"color\"?, \"trust\"?, \"allow_plain\"?} : adds one; a 409 with needs_trust and the fingerprint for a self-signed certificate not yet trusted POST /v1/admin/hub/connections/{name} {\"key\"} : a new key, checked with the cluster first DELETE /v1/admin/hub/connections/{name} takes it out of the book and its key off this machine (the key still works on its cluster until it expires or is revoked there) POST /v1/admin/hub/connections/{name}/check asks the cluster who the key is POST /v1/admin/hub/adopt {\"made\": \"<id>\"} : a cluster made from the Cloud view, in the book through its Kubernetes API POST /v1/admin/hub/copy {\"from\", \"to\", \"tables\": [...]} : copies tables once, \"\" naming this server and anything else a connection /c/{name}/v1/... the call, to that cluster, with its key; only ChloeDB's API and its health A copy is one SQLite file: the source's export of the tables, with their rows and their kept ChloeDB schemas (fields, key, shard and cluster keys), sent into the target's import, which makes the tables it lacks and writes each row by its key into those it has. Run again, it writes the same rows again; it keeps no offset and does not follow later writes. The source must hold the rows: this server, or a connection that is one server (a cluster's coordinators hold none). The target takes the file whole, so it is at most its --max-body-mb . A cluster made from the Cloud view can bring tables of this server the same way once every copy answers ( bring when it is made); the result is on its steps. The All clusters view lists this server and every connection with its health, rows, tables and key, and what differs between them: each table's CREATE TABLE , the synonym rules and the alert rules, compared with the one picked. Apply makes a missing table (with no rows), replaces the synonyms, or sets the alert rule as on the reference, on the clusters that differ, production last; a table that differs is shown, not changed. Revoke this hub's keys revokes, on each cluster, the key this hub holds there and empties the book, for a device that is leaving."],
["server.html#the-feed-changes-for-another-cluster-to-follow","chloedb-server","The feed: changes for another cluster to follow","A server that numbers its writes ( --seq-no , as every shard of a replicated or operator-made cluster does) serves the changes of its transactions as a feed , in order, for another cluster to follow. GET /v1/feed?from=N on a server, or GET /v1/feed?shard=i&from=N on a coordinator, which reads shard i 's from its primary (gRPC Feed , protocol 13), answers NDJSON that goes on as writes come. after_term=T gives the term of transaction N - 1 as the reader holds it, which the shard checks. A key limited to tables reads the feed of the tables it may read: each transaction's operations on them, an empty ops when there are none (so that it counts every transaction), and no sources or unknown ; it may not ask for a copy ( copy=true , which is of every table). /v1/whoami says so ( auth.limited_feed ). The app engine follows its apps' changes this way with its own key. line what {\"feed\": {\"from\", \"last\", \"term\"}} the first line: the feed starts at from ; the shard's last transaction then {\"gone\": {\"oldest\"}} the ship log ( --ship-log-mb , 64 MiB by default, in memory) no longer holds from , or a reader fell behind it: it holds them from oldest on. The feed ends {\"diverged\": {\"seq\", \"term\"}} the shard's history is not the reader's: its transaction from - 1 has another term than after_term , or it has not reached it (a failover lost transactions the reader had). seq and term are the shard's last {\"seq\", \"term\", \"ops\": [...]} one transaction. ops are what /v1/write takes, each naming its table: upsert of the whole row ( insert in a table without a primary key), whatever wrote it ( INSERT , an upsert, an UPDATE , a field other than a number set); set of a number's new value (an add , a set , an UPDATE of a number in place); delete by key. Then the changes of tables, create_table , alter_table ( was and schema ) and drop_table ( was ), with schemas as the log keeps them (ChloeDB's JSON of a schema, field ids included), and profile (a rank profile made, replaced or dropped, def null). sources lists the stream sources' offsets it wrote, and unknown counts operations of a table dropped before the feed's start, which there is no schema left to read {\"seq\", \"term\", \"moved\": \"copy\" \\| \"cleanup\", \"epoch\"} a slot move 's copies to the shard the slots move to, or the deletes of the rows they left: the feed of the shard the rows came from had them, so they carry no operations \"hold\": true | false on a transaction the shard holds its clients' writes from this transaction on ( switching over ): every write a client made to it is in this transaction or before; or no longer (protocol 17) \"slots\": [...] on a transaction slots changed hands on this shard: {\"left\": epoch, \"to\": j, \"slots\": n, \"id\"} where they left, {\"arrived\": epoch, \"from\": i, \"slots\": n, \"id\"} where they arrived, with the same id on both shards. Each is marked before the new cluster map lets a write reach the slots, so a follower that applies a shard's transactions after an arrived only once it has applied the other shard's up to the left of the same id applies every write to those slots in order {\"last\", \"term\", \"time_ms\"} every second: the last transaction the feed reaches, which a follower's lag is counted from {\"error\", \"status\"} the feed ends On a primary the feed reaches the transactions every replica in sync holds ( chloedb_global_checkpoint ), so that one a failover loses is never read; a primary that another replaced ends its feeds with a 409 line, and a reader carries on through the coordinator from its next transaction, with after_term , from the shard's new primary. A replica serves no feed. Rows that took no number (a bulk load, attached segments) are not in it: a follower copies them first. A delete or a set in a table placed by shard_by names the row's key only, since the log does not hold where the row was. The marks of slot moves are offsets the server logs under names starting _strata/ , which _sources does not keep and clients' sources may not take. The feed shows every row as it is written, so it needs a key: a key of scope feed reads the feed of every table, POST /v1/feed/keys (the keys and digests of any table's rows), /v1/health and /v1/whoami , and nothing else. It reads all of the data, so give it to the cluster that follows this one alone; read keys and above read it too. gRPC Feed is a call between the servers of a cluster (the cluster's token). Make feed keys once every server of the cluster runs a version that has the scope: an older server refuses a keys file, and a merge of keys, that holds one. copy=true starts the feed with a copy of the shard's rows (protocol 14): every row of its tables at one version, then its transactions from the next. The copy is taken while the shard serves no change of its slot map, so its rows are those of the slots it holds at epoch , and the ship log holds the transactions after it until the reader reaches them. It waits, up to 30 s, until every replica in sync holds the version. A table without a primary key is refused, since a follower copies rows by their keys. line what {\"copy\": {\"seq\", \"term\", \"epoch\", \"tables\", \"profiles\"}} the first line: the version copied (its last transaction, its term), the slot map's epoch, the tables' schemas and their rank profiles (as profile operations) {\"copy\": true, \"ops\": [...]} up to 500 rows, as upsert operations {\"copied\": {\"seq\", \"term\", \"rows\", \"sources\", \"digests\", \"hold\"}} the copy is done: how many rows, the stream sources' offsets at the version, with digests=true the digests of its keys, and hold when the shard holds its clients' writes at the version. The transactions from seq + 1 follow, as in any feed since=n (protocol 15) copies only the rows that a transaction after n last wrote, for a follower that applied the shard's transactions up to n and fell behind the ship log; after_term is then the term of transaction n as the follower holds it, and a shard that holds it under another term, or has not reached it, refuses with 409 (a failover lost transactions the follower had). whole=a,b copies every row of tables a and b all the same (those that changed meanwhile). digests=true adds to the copied line the digests of the shard's keys at the version, by slot (that of the shard_by value, or of the key): [[slot, count, \"sum\"], ...] , the slots that hold keys, with how many and the sum of a 64-bit hash of each (of its table's name in lower case and its key), in hex. Two clusters' digests add up over their shards whatever their numbers of shards, so a follower compares its own with the source's to find the slots whose keys differ. POST /v1/feed/keys {\"tables\": [...]} ( feed scope) answers a server's or a cluster's digests now ( {\"digests\"} ), and with \"slots\": [...] the keys those slots place ( {\"keys\": [{\"table\", \"key\", \"place\"}]} , place the shard_by value of a table placed by one), each of the rows it serves, read while every shard serves the same slot map (gRPC FeedKeys per shard, the cluster's token)."],
["server.html#following-another-cluster","chloedb-server","Following another cluster","chloedb-server follow keeps one cluster (the follower ) a moment behind another (the source ): it copies the source's rows, tables and rank profiles, then applies each source shard's feed through the follower's coordinator, until it is stopped. Run it as its own process (on Kubernetes, a Deployment of one), beside the follower: --source is the source's coordinator (or a server of its own), read with --source-key , a key of the feed scope there; --target is the follower's coordinator (or server), written with its cluster token ( --target-token ). --source-ca and --target-ca name CA certificates (PEM) to check their TLS certificates by. --name is what the follower calls the source. --batch (1000) caps the operations of one write. The copy. The first time, it opens a copy feed on every source shard and keeps them only once every shard copies at the same slot-map epoch with the same tables and rank profiles (else it tries again, every second, 30 times: a slot move or a change of tables was under way). It makes the tables the follower lacks (one the follower has with another schema stops it, with both CREATE TABLE statements), the rank profiles, then writes the rows and goes on with each shard's transactions after its copy. GET /v1/admin/follow says copied once every shard's copy is written. The order. Each source shard's transactions are applied in their order for each table; two shards' rows are apart, so their writes interleave. A change of a table ( ALTER TABLE , DROP TABLE , a rank profile) is applied once, when every shard has reached it, so that each shard's rows before it go to the table as it was and those after to the table as it became; writes of other tables pass it. Slots that change hands on the source are ordered by their marks: a shard's transactions after slots arrived wait until the follower has applied the other shard's up to their left . A shard the source adds is read from its first transaction once slots leave for it; what it holds before they arrive (its tables, the move's copies) the follower has already. The follower's own slots may move meanwhile: its coordinator routes the writes. Where it is. The follower keeps what it applied in its own stream sources, written with each write, under follow/<name>/ : per source shard the transaction up to which every one is applied ( <copy>/<i>/at ), per shard and table the last transaction applied ( <copy>/<i>/t/<table> ), written with the rows they cover, the slot marks passed, the changes of each table applied and the tables made from the source ( <copy>/made/<table> ). A follow process killed and started again goes on from there, and a write that failed partway is written again whole: as the follower takes no other writes, rows written twice in order are the rows written once. One killed in its copy copies again, from where that copy began. A copy made again names its marks afresh ( copy_ms ). Catching up. A source shard's ship log ( --ship-log-mb ) holds its latest transactions only. A follower that fell further behind (its follow process stopped a while, or slower than the source's writes) finds a shard's feed gone , and catches up on every shard at once (protocol 15): a copy of the rows each shard changed after what the follower applied ( since ), but every row of a table that is not as the source has it now (one altered, dropped and made again, or new: the follower drops its own and makes it again), and drops the tables it made from the source that the source dropped meanwhile. Then the sweep: it compares the digests of its keys by slot with the source's at the copy's version, and in the slots whose differ (256 at a time) deletes the keys the source no longer holds, which no transaction after the copy deletes; a key of a table placed by shard_by counts with its value, so a row the follower holds under an old value is deleted there. Then it follows on from the copy. What a catch-up began from is kept first ( <copy>/<i>/since , <copy>/whole/<table> ), so one cut short begins again from the same. --copy copies every row again whatever the follower kept, and sweeps too: after a failover of the source lost transactions the follower had applied ( diverged ), which stops it with that advice. Only its writes. Its first write marks the follower as following name ( _strata/follows <name> in each shard's _sources ). From then on the follower's shards refuse every other write (409: \"this cluster follows east\"), from clients, SQL, imports and the command center alike; the follow process's carry x-strata-follow: <name> with the cluster's token, which a key may not send (403). POST /v1/admin/follow/stop {\"name\": \"east\"} (an admin key) stops following: the follower takes its clients' writes again and refuses the follow process's, which stops. Started again, a follow process marks the follower as following once more and goes on from where it was. Status. Every 2 s the follow process tells the coordinator it writes through how it stands ( POST /v1/admin/follow/status ): copying , catching up , following , held with the error (a write the follower refused, which it tries again every second; a source shard's feed that stopped, read again from its next transaction), or stopped . Per source shard: applied , the last transaction its feed reaches ( last ), how far behind ( behind ), how many it holds read ( queued ); in all, behind , its lag ( lag_ms : since the source first reached a transaction it has not applied, as the feeds' lines of every second say; while it copies, since the copy began or, catching up, since it fell behind), rows_copied and the keys its last copy deleted ( keys_deleted ). GET /v1/admin/follow shows it beside the marks. The coordinator it writes through has its lag in chloedb_follow_lag_seconds and chloedb_follow_behind_transactions (by name ), and its series in follow_lag_s and follow_behind : a follow process silent for over 10 s counts as behind for as long as it was silent too. The default alert follow-behind fires when the lag stays over 60 s for a minute. Its limits, as it is now: every table needs a primary key (a copy refuses one without); a delete or set in a table placed by shard_by names only the key: the follower deletes or sets the key under each shard_by value it holds it, so a key held under two values is deleted from both; a change of a table while slots move on the source may leave the shards' feeds without one order: the follower then holds, and says so in its status; the source's shards are not removed while it follows (a shard's feed that ends for good stops it); a source server keeps the schemas of the tables it dropped in memory: one restarted after a DROP TABLE the follower had not yet applied gives the table's rows and its drop as unknown , so the follower keeps the table, and a table made again by that name stops it, with both CREATE TABLE statements (stop following, drop the table on the follower, and start again with --copy ); a shard the follower adds takes its clients' writes until the follow process next writes its marks, within 2 s."],
["server.html#switching-over","chloedb-server","Switching over","A follower takes over from its source by hand: a planned switch-over (a move to another cloud, a region drill) loses no write; one that is not planned (the source is gone) loses the writes the follower had not applied. chloedb-server switch-over runs the planned one: It holds the source's writes : POST /v1/admin/hold-writes on the source (an admin key, --source-key ; --source names its URL, by default the one the follow process says). Each source shard writes a mark ( _strata/hold ) once the writes under way have committed, and refuses every client's write after it, a change of tables included, with 503 and Retry-After (\"this cluster holds its writes\"): each client's write commits before the mark or is refused. A slot move's own writes go on. The hold is kept with the rows, so a new primary of a shard holds too, until POST /v1/admin/hold-writes {\"hold\": false} ends it; GET /v1/admin/hold-writes says whether every shard holds. It waits ( --wait-s , 120) until the follower applied every source shard's transactions up to its hold: the feed marks the hold's transaction ( \"hold\": true ), the follower keeps it with what it applied ( <copy>/<i>/held ), and GET /v1/admin/follow says source_holds and, per source shard, held . It promotes the follower : POST /v1/admin/follow/promote {\"name\": \"east\"} there (an admin key, or the cluster's token, --target-token ). The follower stops following, which ends the follow process, and takes its own clients' writes from then on. Its promotion is kept on every shard ( _strata/promoted east ): a follow process of east started again is refused, as is its write (\"this cluster was promoted from east\"), and only one that copies afresh ( --copy ) follows east again. In two writes: the first stops following and holds the follower's own writes once the follow process's under way have committed, so each shard's transaction of it is the last of the source's ( seqs ); the second keeps those numbers and ends the hold. A promotion cut short between them is finished by the next call. The promotion answers what the follower applied of each source shard ( applied ), and lost : 0 when planned. Clients write to the follower (those refused with 503 by the source, try the new primary). The old source follows it back , if wanted, without a copy: chloedb-server follow --reverse --source <new primary> --target <old source> --name west . The process reads the promotion ( GET /v1/feed/promoted , feed scope) and checks that each of the old source's shards holds no transaction with rows after what the new primary applied of it (its own feed from there); then it follows the new primary from the promotion's numbers, and its first write ends the old source's hold (as it follows, the follower guard refuses its clients). Refused when the old source took writes since, as after a promotion that was not planned. Started again, with --reverse or without, it resumes; so a switch-over and back (a drill) works the same way the second time, the first cluster following the one promoted from it. Not planned. When the source cannot be held, switch-over stops and says why; --unplanned promotes all the same, losing the writes the follower had not applied, as many as its follow process last said ( behind , in lost ). POST /v1/admin/follow/promote without \"unplanned\": true refuses (409) while the source does not hold its writes as far as the follower applied. If the old source comes back, it may hold writes the new primary never saw: it rejoins as a new follower, chloedb-server follow --copy --source <new primary> --target <old source> --name west , which writes the new primary's rows over its own and deletes, in the sweep, the keys the new primary lacks; its status says how many ( keys_deleted ). Nothing stops the old source from taking writes if clients still reach it after a promotion that was not planned: a hold is the only fence, so promotion stays a person's call."],
["server.html#following-on-kubernetes-and-from-the-hub","chloedb-server","Following on Kubernetes, and from the hub","On a cluster the operator runs, spec.follow sets it to follow another; the operator runs the follow process as a Deployment of one pod ( <cluster>-follow , strategy Recreate ), and changing spec.follow restarts no server: The pod runs chloedb-server follow --source <source> --target http://<cluster>:7700 --name <name> --once <askedMs> (with --copy or --reverse for those starts, and --source-ca from caSecret ), with the source's key from keySecret and the cluster's token from its Secret. Its place is kept in the follower's own _sources , so a pod started again resumes. --once MS is for a process something starts again by itself: a copy afresh is made once, not again on a restart, and once the cluster stopped following the source or was promoted from it at or after MS , the process waits (status stopped , \"waiting to be stopped\") instead of following again. Stopping and promoting ( POST /v1/admin/follow/stop , /promote ) remove spec.follow as well. POST /v1/admin/follow/pause {\"name\": \"east\", \"paused\": true} (an admin key) sets paused , which keeps the Deployment at no pod; the follower still refuses its clients' writes, and {\"paused\": false} starts it again from where it was. A follower whose follow process runs elsewhere answers 409 there. A hub ( many clusters from one command center ) sets one connection of its book to follow another: POST /v1/admin/hub/follow {\"follower\": \"west\", \"source\": \"east\"} (an admin key of the hub). The hub makes a key of the feed scope on the source (\"follow west\"), keeps it in a Secret of the follower's namespace through the follower's Kubernetes API, and sets the follower's spec.follow ; the hub is not on the data path after that. Optional: name (by default the source's connection name), start ( follow , copy , reverse ), source_url (by default the public URL the source says it has, else its address in the book), key_secret (a Secret made already, for a follower the hub reaches at its address rather than through its Kubernetes API) and ca_secret . With \"plan\": true nothing is made: the answer lists the steps and why it is refused, if it is: a follower the operator does not run, one that follows another cluster already, a source with no address the follower's pods reach (a cluster made from Cloud setup has none until its coordinators are exposed), a self-signed source without ca_secret , or a follower the hub cannot write a Secret on without key_secret . In the command center, All clusters draws an arrow from each source to its follower with how far behind it is (amber past 60 s, red when its follow process is silent or held), and Follow… on it, or on a cluster's tile, opens that plan, then starts it. Follow (under Change) is the follower's own page: the source, the lag, what each source shard applied, and Pause , Resume , Stop following and Promote . Promote runs the switch-over: it holds the source's writes through the hub when the source is in the book, waits for source_holds , and promotes, after which it offers to make the old source follow back. When it cannot hold the source, promoting loses what the follower had not applied, and the count, as the follow process last said it, is typed to confirm. The page also holds and releases the cluster's own writes. The SQL workbench and Import say when a cluster follows another, and a write it refuses for that links to the source."],
["server.html#deadlines-admission-and-metrics","chloedb-server","Deadlines, admission and metrics","Every query runs under a deadline ( timeout_ms up to --max-timeout-ms , or the server's default) and gets its share of the cores: a query arriving when r are running searches its segments on about cores / r threads, its own and threads it borrows from one pool the whole server shares, as many as the cores ( chloedb_query_pool_threads ). A query starts no threads of its own, and when the pool is busy it goes on with the threads it has instead of waiting for more. At most --max-concurrent run at once; the rest wait for a slot, and one still waiting at its deadline is refused with 429 ( ResourceExhausted over gRPC) and counted in chloedb_rejected_total . With --admission-wait-ms , a client's query waits for a slot that long at most, not until its deadline, and is then refused the same way, with Retry-After . Past its capacity a server otherwise queues every query until its deadline. On a cluster of two 2-core nodes (a coordinator, 2 shards of 2 copies) at 1,000 clients that sent their next query as soon as they had an answer, the queries waited a median of 1.6 s and a p99 of 2 s; with --admission-wait-ms 100 , and clients that waited out Retry-After , the queries it took ran in a median of 0.26 s and a p99 of 0.53 s, while it answered 22% fewer of them: a refused query is cheap, not free, and the coordinator refused about 1,500 a second. Clients that send a refused query again at once, without waiting, keep the coordinator busy refusing and few queries run, so enable it only where clients wait out Retry-After (the Python client does). A coordinator's calls to its shards always wait until their deadline, since the coordinator's own slots bound them; set the flag on the coordinators (the operator's coordinatorArgs ) or on a single server. A query still running at its deadline stops and returns an error. So does a query whose caller stopped waiting (a client that hung up, a coordinator's hedge that lost), and it holds its slot and counts as running until it has stopped. A query checks between segments and, inside a segment, every 1,024 rows it groups, orders by a column or lists, every 1,024 rows a graph walk expands (16 when each hop is a vector search), and every 1,024 rows a join hashes or compares, so it stops within milliseconds even in one large segment. A coordinator does not retry a query that a replica refused with 429 on that replica's sibling, which would only take on both loads: the query fails, or a partial answer leaves the shard out. A read every replica of a shard failed is asked again every 100 ms, for up to 3 s within its deadline, only while one of them is up and refused it for now (a primary outside its lease, as when one of two copies restarts, or one another primary replaced); a shard none of whose replicas answers fails it at once, or a partial answer leaves it out. Hedges (asking another replica when one has not answered after --hedge-ms ) are budgeted at 5% of the calls to shards, with up to 10 saved for a burst, so that a slow cluster is not sent twice its load. A replica outrun after it had --hedge-ms to answer is put in its cool-off, asked last for 5 s; one outrun sooner, as a replica a hedge asked is when the replica asked before it answers moments later, was not given its time and is not. Which replica of a shard a read asks first is --route 's choice. least-cost (the default) asks each in turn, unless another replica, drawn at random, costs clearly less (by more than a quarter, and by a millisecond at least): a replica's cost is the round trip of its recent queries as this coordinator saw them (at least 1 ms), times one more than its calls in flight, raised for its recent errors and refusals for load (429) and for the load it reported in the coordinator's last survey (its query slots or query memory, whichever is fuller). A replica whose round trip this coordinator has not seen in the last 10 s, or has not seen past its first query, counts as the median of the others, so a new replica or one passed over is asked again in its turn. Coordinators share nothing of this, and the draw keeps them from all sending their reads to the replica that looks cheapest at once. round-robin asks each in turn whatever its cost, those in their cool-off (failed, or outrun after --hedge-ms , in the last 5 s) last. Under least-cost those go last too, unless every replica of the shard is in its cool-off: then they are ordered by cost as if none were. Under heavy overload each replica is outrun in its turn, and least-cost asked them all in turn before these two rules (0.3.4) and did worse than round-robin : on DigitalOcean (the machines below) with copy 0's node also running 24 CPU-bound threads, at 64 clients it served 2,557 queries a second (p99 230 ms) where round-robin served 2,775 (p99 61 ms), and with the two rules 4,252 (p99 32 ms); with 48 such threads, 3,164 and 3,228 against 4,220 (p99 33 ms). Measured with chloedb-route-bench --external on DigitalOcean (one shard of 1M rows, its 3 copies each on a c-2 node of its own with 2 dedicated vCPUs, the coordinator and clients on a c-32 node, the servers' answer cache off), least-cost cut p99 against round-robin from 10.0 to 7.3 ms at 16 clients and from 36 to 24 ms at 64 on copies alike, where its median at 64 clients was 16% higher (9.7 against 8.4 ms); with one copy given a third of its node's CPU it served 5,575 queries a second at 64 clients where round-robin served 3,523. A read that must ask one replica (the one holding its lease, or the kept statistics it scores by) asks it first either way, and writes go to the primary; under least-cost a read takes kept statistics only of the replica the route asks first (without them it asks that replica for its statistics, which are then kept), so that a replica asked less while it was slow is asked in turn again once it is not. Each coordinator shows what it observed of each replica in /v1/cluster ( observed ) and in the chloedb_replica_* metrics, whichever route it takes. --hedge-adaptive hedges a query after the replica asked has taken longer than it usually does rather than after --hedge-ms : its tail, the average of its recent plain query round trips (the last 30 s or 200) plus six times their average deviation, at least 2 ms and at most --hedge-ms , which it takes until the replica answered 20 queries. Other calls (statistics, fetches, reads by key) keep --hedge-ms , and hedges stay within the same budget (5% of calls). A replica outrun by such a hedge before --hedge-ms was slower than usual, not failing, so it is not put in its cool-off either. Measured with chloedb-route-bench on one machine (Apple M1 Pro, 10 cores, 32 GB, macOS 27.0.1, shards, coordinator and clients in one process over loopback; chloedb-server/results/route-mac-phase2/ ), it brings the 99.9th percentile of a replica that stalls now and then down by more than half under least-cost (55 to 19 ms at 16 clients); hedges there compete for the same cores, so p99 rises by about 5% when the machine is saturated. With each replica on cores of its own ( chloedb-server/results/route-containers/ , three containers on 2 vCPUs each in a Docker VM on the same Mac), it costs nothing measurable at p99 or p99.9 on replicas alike, and cuts that 99.9th percentile from 59 to 10 ms. On DigitalOcean (the machines above), it cut the 99.9th percentile of one copy that stalls for 60 ms a second from 61 to 15 ms at 16 clients under least-cost , but with 3 shards of copies alike, where a query waits for all three, it raised least-cost 's 99.9th percentile from 15 to 21 ms at 16 clients and from 39 to 60 ms at 64; it is off by default for that. --prefer-caught-up (on by default, with --route least-cost ; --prefer-caught-up=false turns it off) routes a read that must see a write ( min_seq_nos ) by each replica's round trips of such reads, which include its wait for the write, unless the replica is known to hold the write (it answered a read after it, or a later one); other reads go by its plain queries' round trips. A replica that applies writes late is then asked few reads after a write and its share of plain reads as before, and is not put in its cool-off when such a read outruns it. One found behind is asked such a read again a minute later to see whether it caught up. A read still waits on the replica it is sent to for the write it must see, so this changes where reads go, not what they see. On DigitalOcean (the machines above, one shard whose primary acknowledges writes alone and ships them to one of its two replicas 50 ms late), it raised the queries a second of a mix of writes and reads after them from 1,640 to 2,156 at 16 clients and from 1,961 to 2,851 at 64, against least-cost without it. /v1/cluster shows the averages apart ( plain_rtt_ms , after_write_rtt_ms ), the newest write each replica is known to hold ( held ) and its tail ( tail_ms ), and the coordinator's route , hedge_adaptive and prefer_caught_up ."],
["server.html#limits","chloedb-server","Limits","A query may reach at most --max-result-window rows (10,000), as Elasticsearch's max_result_window : one whose LIMIT plus OFFSET is larger fails with 400, and so does one without a LIMIT that matches more rows. A ranked query without a LIMIT returns its best 100. Counts and groups are not rows, but a GROUP BY may make at most --max-groups groups (65,536, as Elasticsearch's search.max_buckets ) and fails with 400 at the next one, before its LIMIT is applied. A cross-encoder's rerank phase may score at most --max-rerank rows (20: ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 scores twenty abstracts of SciFact, NFCorpus or ArguAna in about 1 s at the median and at most 1.3 s at p99 on four cores in f32 , a quarter of the default 5 s deadline, where a hundred take 4.4 to 4.8 s at the median, so that many would miss it; its int8 export takes 0.4 s for twenty and 1.8 to 2.0 s for a hundred, which a larger --max-rerank can allow): one whose count is larger fails with 400 before any work. Queries that need a model not yet open wait for one of them to open it, and share that copy. A compound query's single-table queries, WITH results and join sides may hold up to 1,000,000 rows each, as before; its answer is held to the window. A coordinator checks LIMIT and OFFSET before it asks its shards, and a shard lets a coordinator's single-table query reach 1,000,001 rows. To read more rows than the window, scroll . Reading past the window: scrolls A scroll reads a query's answer a page at a time, every page from the version of the database the first read, as Elasticsearch's scroll does; the window then caps each page, not the whole answer. POST /v1/query with page_size answers the first page, at most page_size rows and at most --max-result-window , and, while rows are left, a cursor . The cursor alone asks for the next page, {\"cursor\": \"...\"} (with format , objects , keep_alive_ms or timeout_ms , and nothing of the query's), until the last page, which answers no cursor. With \"format\": \"arrow\" each page is an Arrow stream and the cursor comes in the x-chloedb-cursor header. The answer is the query's own, in its order: the rows a query with no window answers. Rows written, changed or deleted after the first page are not in it. What a scroll keeps between pages depends on the query: A plain scan (no ORDER BY , groups, counts or rerank phases) resumes after its last row, segment by segment, and holds nothing but its place: it can read a table of any size. An ordered or ranked query runs once, at the first page, and holds its rows' places in order (24 bytes a row), at most what --max-scroll-rows leaves; a ranked query without a LIMIT answers its best 100, as it does at once. Groups, counts and compound queries (joins, WITH , UNION , DISTINCT ) run once and hold their rows. A coordinator scrolls too. Each shard it asks starts a scroll of its own (protocol 18, gRPC ), on the copy that answers, which keeps it on a lease of its snapshot; the coordinator merges the shards' pages as they come. Rows in no order come one shard's after another's (the order a coordinator answers them in at once); rows ordered by a column, a path or one score (BM25, a similarity, a ranking expression) come in their order, the coordinator asking each shard for more rows whenever its rows run out before the page is full, so it holds at most two pages of the shards' rows between pages. A shard's scroll is kept open while the coordinator's is: one that a page did not ask is asked for a page of no rows every 30 seconds, and it is kept a minute past the coordinator's keep-alive. Groups, counts, fused scores, rerank phases, guarded queries and compound queries run once on the coordinator, which holds their rows, at most --max-scroll-rows , as one server does. Each shard reads one version, the one it had when the scroll started; the shards' versions are their own, as a coordinator's queries' are. A scroll whose shard fails (its copy went down, say) ends, saying so: a scroll of the shard's rows from another copy would read another version, so start the scroll again. A scroll ended early, by DELETE , its keep-alive or its LIMIT, ends its shards' scrolls too. The open scrolls hold at most --max-scroll-rows rows, or rows' places, between them (1,000,000). A scroll whose query would hold more than all of it fails with 400; one that would hold more than the others leave is refused with 429 until some end. chloedb_scrolls_open and chloedb_scroll_rows_held show them. Each scroll keeps a lease on the version it reads, so the files of that version stay until it ends, as a snapshot lease does. A cursor is random and is read only by the key that started it (another key's is answered 404). It ends with its last page, with DELETE /v1/query/cursors/ID , or when no page is asked for over its keep-alive: keep_alive_ms , given with page_size or with any page (60 s by default, the lease TTL; at most an hour). A page that fails, at its deadline say, leaves the cursor where it was, so asking again reads the same page; a page that answered and was lost on the way is not served again. Asking for a cursor's next page while its last is being read is refused with 409. The running queries share a memory budget, --query-memory-mb (a third of the cgroup memory limit, or of RAM; merges have their own, a half). As Elasticsearch's request circuit breaker, a query counts what it builds up against it as it goes: its groups, COUNT(DISTINCT) values and percentiles' values, the rows it orders or lists before its LIMIT, the rows it returns with their stored text, the nodes and neighbour lists of a graph walk, and a compound query's single-table answers, join tables and joined rows. A query that would go past the budget stops and is refused with 429 ( ResourceExhausted over gRPC, which a coordinator passes on), and it gives back what it held, as every query does when it ends. chloedb_query_memory_bytes shows what the running queries hold and chloedb_query_memory_trips_total how many were refused. The counts are estimates of the structures a query grows, not of every allocation; index data is mapped from the file and not counted. An ORDER BY with a LIMIT keeps about twice LIMIT plus OFFSET rows as it goes, not every match. Filters are kept between queries, as Elasticsearch's node query cache and Solr's filter cache keep theirs: the second time a segment sees a filter, in a second query, the documents it keeps there are kept, and later queries test a bit for each candidate instead of reading the filter's columns again (a fixed filter beside one that changes with every query is kept on its own). They take up to --filter-cache-mb (64 MiB), least recently used out first; a filter larger than a quarter of that is not kept. Only filters that read the segment's own rows are kept: not a field with values written in place, a typo or synonym expansion, a graph, a subquery or a JSON path, nor a segment still in memory. Nor a list of the table's keys ( id NOT IN (...) , the items a user has seen): it is a request's own, its set would hold nearly every row of the segment, and a few hundred requests going round would push each other out before any came back; what is fixed beside it is kept. Deletes are checked apart, and a segment's filters go with it when it is merged away, so answers do not change. A query's plan says from the cache for a filter it found there. The log store's database keeps none. Answers are kept too, up to --answer-cache-mb (64 MiB, least recently used out first): the same query (its SQL, parameters and options, and over gRPC its plan) on the same version of the table is answered from memory, without a slot or the executor. A write moves the version, so a kept answer is never stale; it is simply not found again. A query that reads the clock, random numbers, uuid() or a model ( llm( ) is not kept, nor a scroll, a held snapshot, a profile, an Arrow answer, or an answer larger than a 128th of the budget (512 KiB of the default). A coordinator's shards keep their parts of its answers, so a coordinator's repeated query is answered by each shard from memory. Its steps say answer cache , and chloedb_answer_cache_total{outcome=\"hit\"|\"miss\"} , chloedb_answer_cache_answers and chloedb_answer_cache_bytes show how it does. On a coordinator over 2 shards, all on one 4-core machine, with queries drawn from a Zipf law (the 100 commonest were half of them), it answered 3,568 to 3,671 queries a second instead of 2,192 to 2,256 and each query cost the shards 0.4 ms of CPU instead of 1.0; with every query different it was as fast as without it. An idle HTTP connection costs the server about 31 KB, most of it the 8 KiB buffers HTTP/1 reads and writes through, and a file descriptor: 10,000 connections held open on a coordinator took 314 MB and opened in 1.2 s on one 4-core machine. 100,000 clients then need about 3 GB over the coordinators (about 800 MB each on four) and an open-file limit above 100,000 on each: the server raises its soft limit to the hard one when it starts, so raise the hard limit where it is lower ( LimitNOFILE in a systemd unit, ulimit -Hn in a shell; containers usually allow 1,048,576). Over gRPC or HTTP/2 one connection carries many clients' queries at once. Write requests are held in one queue, from embedding (a server embeds on as many writes at once as it has cores) through waiting for the writer to being applied. With --max-queued-writes (1,024) in it, a write or a SQL INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE is refused with 429 before any work, and counted in chloedb_write_errors_total{status=\"429\"} ; chloedb_write_queue_requests shows how many it holds. A body larger than its route allows is refused with 413: --max-body-mb (256 MiB) for /v1/write and /v1/import , 16 MiB for every other route. Statement budgets The text of a statement is bounded before it is parsed ( --max-statement-bytes , --max-statement-elements , and 1,000 levels of nesting), and its calls of stored functions written out hold at most 250,000 expressions. A budget is less, and is checked on what the statement becomes, not on what it says: SELECT f(1) may be a few bytes that write out to thousands of expressions, and tags @> :tags fifteen bytes with 100,000 tags. As ClickHouse's max_expanded_ast_elements , max_rows_to_read and max_execution_time and PostgreSQL's statement_timeout per role, it has five parts, each left out when not given: budget bounds checked nodes the expressions of the statement written out (its stored functions and rank profiles in place) before it binds depth how deep they nest; a subquery is a level before it binds list the values of one list: IN (...) , ARRAY[...] , a list parameter before it binds plan_ms the time one plan takes to make as it plans, where the deadline is checked rows_read the rows its scans, filters and groups visit, and the rows its parts answer (a join's sides, a WITH result) as it runs, every 1,024 rows, and as each part answers A statement's budget is the tightest of three: the server's --statement-budget , its key's, and its request's \"budget\" on /v1/query , /v1/sql , /v1/explain and /v1/sql/check ( {\"budget\": {\"rows_read\": 100000}} , or \"budget\": \"rows_read=100000\" ). A request may so lower its key's budget, never raise it. A key gets one when it is made, POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"dashboards\", \"scope\": \"read\", \"budget\": {\"nodes\": 20000, \"rows_read\": 5000000}} , and POST /v1/admin/keys/ID/budget {\"rows_read\": 1000000} lowers it (a budget is lowered, never raised, as an expiry is brought nearer: the servers of a cluster merge a key's two budgets to the tighter, so a raise would not reach them; a key that needs more is a new key). A key another key made has its maker's budget. The key list shows each key's budget . A statement past its shape's budget is a 400 query_invalid before it runs, one past rows_read a 400 as soon as it reads the row past it, and one past plan_ms a timeout, each naming the budget. A coordinator holds a statement to nodes , depth , list and plan_ms as it plans it; rows_read is counted by a single server, not yet across a coordinator's shards. Embedded, ExecOptions::budget is the statement's budget, and Db::query_with , Db::sql_with , Db::query_leaf_with (Python's query_arrow ), Db::pack_with , Db::explain_with and Db::prepare_write_with plan under it, with the deadline, on a thread with the stack a deep statement needs. A short statement plans on the thread that serves the request, a longer one on a blocking thread, where it holds none of the threads that serve the others and stops at its deadline: a statement is short by its text and its parameters both, so a short text that binds a long list plans on the blocking thread too. Planning, EXPLAIN ( /v1/explain ) and /v1/sql/check included, stops at the request's deadline, and checks it as it writes out each call of a stored function, as it binds each query, and every thousand values of a list."],
["server.html#health","chloedb-server","Health","GET /v1/health reports status , and docs and segments , the rows and segments of the default table (the first table, or on a database made with no tables the first one people made; a coordinator sums its shards'), with tables the rows of each. Changed: on a database made with no tables, docs counted the placeholder, so 0, and so did the clients' count() . ok . degraded : on a database, its flushes fail (a full disk), and error says why. Writes still commit, to the write-ahead log, until the memtable holds twice its limit; then they are refused until a flush succeeds. On a coordinator, a replica does not answer, or is not ok , but every shard has one that answers. failing : the database cannot write until the server restarts. A write failed partway (a full disk, an I/O error) and the database refuses writes until it is reopened, or the writer thread stopped. Reads still work; merges stop. On a shard's primary, hand the primary over to a replica in sync first (with --failover-after-ms it does so itself), then fix the cause and restart it: restarted alone, it fails again while its disk is full. down : a coordinator has a shard with no replica answering. It answers 503 for down and failing . /livez and /readyz answer 503 only for failing , so that an orchestrator restarts the server (the Kubernetes manifests use them as probes), and /readyz also while the server drains: with --drain-ms N ( CHLOEDB_DRAIN_MS ; 0 by default, 5,000 under the operator), a server sent SIGTERM answers /readyz 503 ( \"status\": \"draining\" ) and closes each HTTP connection after its answer for N ms while it goes on serving, so that a load balancer stops sending it requests before it stops; then it stops as it does without the flag, finishing the requests it holds (over TLS too), for up to --stop-grace-ms (20 s), and ending the feeds followers read. Neither asks a coordinator's shards, so one slow shard cannot take every coordinator out of service at once; /v1/health shows the shards. The writer thread survives a panic while applying a write: the requests in that batch get 500, the panic is counted in chloedb_writer_panics_total , and the next batch goes on. GET /metrics serves, in Prometheus text format, with # HELP and # TYPE lines for every family and durations in seconds. Its families are named chloedb_* ; servers from before the rename name the same families strata_* (a dashboard over both versions matches {__name__=~\"(chloedb|strata)_.*\"} ): metric labels what chloedb_build_info version , simd always 1: the server's version and the SIMD kernels it runs process_start_time_seconds , process_cpu_seconds_total when the process started, and the CPU time it used process_resident_memory_bytes , process_open_fds memory in RAM (mapped database pages included) and open file descriptors (Linux) chloedb_http_requests_total , chloedb_http_request_duration_seconds route , status HTTP requests by route pattern ( other for none) and status, and their duration by route chloedb_grpc_requests_total , chloedb_grpc_request_duration_seconds method , code gRPC calls by method and status code, and their duration by method chloedb_queries_total kind , outcome queries by kind ( bm25 , vector , sparse , hybrid , boosted , expression , reranked , decay , graph , column , facet , aggregate , count , filter , compound , invalid ) and outcome ( ok , partial , error , timeout , rejected for no slot or no memory, cancelled by an operator) chloedb_query_duration_seconds kind time to answer a query, waiting for a slot included chloedb_query_admission_wait_seconds time a query waited for a slot ( --max-concurrent ) chloedb_queries_running queries running now chloedb_rejected_total queries refused because no slot came free before their deadline chloedb_query_memory_bytes , chloedb_query_memory_limit_bytes memory the running queries hold, as counted, and their budget ( --query-memory-mb ; absent with no limit) chloedb_query_memory_trips_total queries refused because the budget was spent chloedb_query_pool_threads , chloedb_query_pool_queued threads in the pool queries borrow from to search segments in parallel, and the threads queries asked for that none was free to give yet chloedb_writes_total , chloedb_written_docs_total write requests that succeeded, and rows they wrote chloedb_write_errors_total status write requests that failed, by HTTP status chloedb_write_duration_seconds time from a write request to its commit, embedding and waiting for the writer included chloedb_write_queue_requests , chloedb_write_batches_total write requests held ( limits ): embedding, waiting for the writer or being applied; and the groups the writer applied in one transaction each chloedb_idempotent_replays_total writes answered from their idempotency key instead of being applied again chloedb_slow_requests_total what queries and writes the slow log wrote down chloedb_embedded_texts_total , chloedb_embed_duration_seconds for texts the server's models embedded for writes ( write ), for query parameters ( query ) and for rows stored without vectors ( backfill ), and how long each took chloedb_embed_requests_total , chloedb_embed_retries_total , chloedb_embed_failures_total , chloedb_embed_tokens_total table , field , model requests a hosted model sent, sent again after a rate limit or failure, and failed for good, and the tokens its provider counted chloedb_agent_runs_total questions the agent was asked chloedb_docs , chloedb_segments , chloedb_deleted_docs , chloedb_memtable_docs a database's rows, segments, deleted rows merges have not dropped yet, and rows not yet flushed chloedb_file_bytes , chloedb_free_bytes , chloedb_wal_bytes its size on disk, the free space in its file, and its write-ahead log chloedb_snapshot_leases snapshot leases held chloedb_failing , chloedb_flush_failing 1 when the database is failing , or its flushes fail ( health ) chloedb_writer_panics_total write batches whose application panicked chloedb_merges_running , chloedb_merge_memory_bytes merges running now and their estimated memory ( merges ) chloedb_merges_total , chloedb_merges_limited_total merges finished, and merges cut (or put off) to fit the memory budget or an interrupted merge's chloedb_merges_interrupted , chloedb_merge_failing groups of segments whose merge a crash interrupted; 1 while merges fail chloedb_damaged_segments segments the last scrub found failing their checksums chloedb_shard_calls_total shard , outcome a coordinator's calls to each shard (replicas, retries and hedges together) by outcome: ok , error , timeout chloedb_shard_retries_total , chloedb_shard_hedges_total calls a coordinator asked of another replica after one failed, or after the first had not answered within --hedge-ms chloedb_replica_inflight shard , replica a coordinator's calls to a replica not answered yet chloedb_replica_query_rtt_ms shard , replica the round trip of a coordinator's queries to a replica over the last 2 s or 20 queries, which --route least-cost compares replicas by chloedb_replica_overhead_ms shard , replica that round trip less the time the replica counted (its admission wait and run): the network and decoding chloedb_replica_err_rate shard , replica the share of a coordinator's recent calls to a replica that failed by the replica's fault chloedb_shard_protocol the protocol a coordinator speaks to its shards: the oldest a replica speaks chloedb_fetch_queries_total , chloedb_fetch_lost_rows_total a coordinator's queries answered by query then fetch, and rows of their pages no fetch found chloedb_stats_cache_total outcome a coordinator's BM25 queries by where their statistics came from: hit (kept, no statistics round), miss (a round), stale (a replica had moved on: sent again with a round) ( kept statistics ) chloedb_stats_cache_terms terms whose statistics a coordinator keeps, over every replica chloedb_stale_stats_total queries a shard answered stale, sent kept statistics of an older version chloedb_slot_epoch the epoch of the slot map a coordinator routes writes by; 0 while it routes by the hash before slots chloedb_shard_keyed_ops_per_second shard , kind , window reads ( kind=\"read\" ) and writes a coordinator routed to a shard by key, a second, over the last 1m , 5m or 15m , by the slots it holds now ( hot slots ); a gauge, since a shard's count falls when its slots move chloedb_fanout_queries_per_second window requests a coordinator sent to every shard, a second chloedb_slot_ops_total kind reads, writes and fan-outs ( kind=\"fanout\" ) a coordinator routed, over every slot chloedb_slot_heat_skew the busiest shard's keyed load over the last 5 minutes, as a multiple of the shards' mean (0: none counted) chloedb_replicas_avoided replicas a coordinator asks last for now, after they failed or were outrun chloedb_replication_role 1 on a primary , 2 on a replica chloedb_global_checkpoint the highest sequence number every in-sync replica holds (on a replica, as its primary last said) chloedb_replica_in_sync , chloedb_replica_behind replica on a primary: 1 while a replica is in sync, and the transactions it does not hold yet chloedb_replica_shipped_total , chloedb_replica_failures_total , chloedb_replica_copies_total replica on a primary: transactions a replica applied from it, calls to it that failed or that it refused, and copies of the primary it installed chloedb_replica_verifications_total , chloedb_replica_divergences_total , chloedb_replica_verify_failures_total replica on a primary: times a replica's rows were compared with the primary's, times they differed, and rounds that could not compare chloedb_replica_applied_total , chloedb_replica_restores_total on a replica: transactions it applied from its primary, and copies of the primary it installed chloedb_replica_serves_reads on a replica: 1 while it serves reads chloedb_replica_read_waits_total , chloedb_replica_reads_refused_total on a replica: reads that waited for a write they must see , and reads refused, while it caught up or because it had not applied such a write in time chloedb_replica_digests_total on a replica: digests of its rows it computed for its primary to compare chloedb_follow_lag_seconds , chloedb_follow_behind_transactions name on the coordinator a follow process writes through: how far behind its source it is, in seconds and in transactions A coordinator's /metrics does not ask its shards: each shard serves its own, and the coordinator shows how its calls to them went. strata_query_ms (milliseconds) became chloedb_query_duration_seconds . A server's health gives rows (every table) and tables (rows per table). A copy's detail gives its limits ( slow_ms , max_concurrent , max_rows , timeout_ms ) and system.memory_limit_bytes (the cgroup's limit, else the machine's memory). A failed change keeps its error in the audit entry's after.error , and its event says it in the title."],
["server.html#fleet-health","chloedb-server","Fleet health","GET /v1/health/fleet answers whether the cluster serves, judged over its copies rather than the coordinator's own process, in one answer the command center's Home shows: status ( ok , warning , critical ), a one-sentence headline , since_ms (since when that status holds), checks (shards serving, the share of queries failing and their p99 against the SLO over the last 5 minutes, copies answering, replication lag, disk, alerts firing), each ok , warning or critical with its value, and problems , worst first, each with where (a shard or a copy, by its short name such as s1-0 ), since_ms , impact (what clients feel) and next (what to do, with the command center's view that does it). The headline holds while nothing changes: it says what crossed a line (\"s0-0's disk is over 85% full\", \"Searches are failing: more than the SLO's 1% fail\"), and the problem's title and the checks carry the numbers now. Each coordinator judges with what the others see: they tell each other, every 5 seconds, which copies their last survey did not reach ( GET /v1/coordinators?window=1 's unreached ). A copy that does not answer this coordinator, but answered another's survey begun since, and then still does not answer a survey of this coordinator begun after that (a copy coming back answers one coordinator a few seconds before the other), is cut off from this one, not down: Home says \"This coordinator cannot reach s0-1, which coord-b reaches\" (a warning, shard-0-cut-off , with the network to look at), or, when this coordinator's clients fail for it, that searches or writes through this coordinator fail while the others' clients are served; and every coordinator's Home names a copy another coordinator cannot reach ( coordinator-<name>-cut-off ), from that coordinator's surveys begun since this one last found the copy down. The copies check lists the copies down here that another coordinator reaches ( unreached_here ) and those another cannot reach ( unreached_elsewhere ), which make it a warning. --slo-error-ratio (0.01) and --slo-p99-ms (250) set the SLO. On a shard server the same checks describe that server alone. The headline judges searches over the last minute, and the SLO checks over 5 minutes. A failure that ended is a problem of its own: \"Searches failed for 62 s (10% at worst) and answer again\" ( error_ratio_recovered , p99_recovered , with from_ms , to_ms , worst ); a failure longer than the 5 minutes starts at its first failing point in the hour the coordinator keeps. Each problem has since_ms (when it began) and first_signal_ms (its earliest signal). The answer's since_ms is the headline problem's, and not_ok_since_ms is when the status left ok . Equal problems are one problem (\"4 copies' disks are 97% full\"), in the same order on every look. The alerts problem counts this coordinator's firing, unsilenced rules once each, \"on N copies\", and its impact names each with its severity and where. GET /v1/cluster says what each shard can do: state ( ok , degraded , no_reads , no_writes , down ), serves_reads , takes_writes , majority , failover , primary and why , a sentence. With failover on, a shard of two copies that loses either serves no reads: its primary serves a read only while a majority of its copies answer it. A copy that does not answer has role: \"unknown\" , with last_role , down_since_ms and last_seen_ms , so a dead primary is no longer the shard's primary. Copies carry a short name ( short ), taken from their address so that a copy keeps it when another copy of its shard leaves or joins, and every coordinator says the same: s1-0 for a server the operator runs (shard 1, the pod numbered 0), else the host's first label ( a0 for http://a0.example:7701 ) with the port unless it is 7701, and host:port for an IP address or localhost ( 127.0.0.1:8004 ); two copies that would share a name are named by their host and port. Copies also carry name , node , zone , version and backup_dir , and a copy that answers its position ( seq_no , the term and number of its last write) and the cluster map's epoch it holds ( slot_epoch , 0 for none: the coordinator gives the map to a primary or replica that holds an older one, such as a replica rebuilt on an empty disk, and leaves a standalone server at a copy's address without one, since a server added as a replica must hold none); on Kubernetes the operator gives each server its image, pod, node and the cluster's name. One snapshot, taken at most every 2 seconds, answers every caller. --cluster-name , --environment ( production , staging : the command center treats any environment not named as a lower one, such as dev , test , staging , qa , local , sandbox or demo , or none, as production) and --public-url (for links in alerts' webhooks) name the cluster in /v1/cluster and /v1/whoami . ?brief=1 leaves out each copy's detail : 3.5 KB against 20 KB for 4 copies. The command center polls it on views that show no copy's numbers."],
["server.html#several-coordinators","chloedb-server","Several coordinators","A cluster can run several coordinators over the same shards, for example as a Kubernetes Deployment behind one Service. They find each other through the shards. Each coordinator names itself on its health calls to its shards (gRPC metadata x-strata-coordinator , -name and -version , sent with the cluster token). Each shard keeps who named itself for ten minutes. Every 5 seconds, each coordinator reads the union of what its shards heard. GET /v1/coordinators lists them: {coordinators: [{url, name, this, last_seen_ms, version}], gone, live_ms} . A coordinator not heard from for a minute is under gone . Any server answers it, a shard included. A coordinator names itself by --peer-url ; on Kubernetes it uses its pod's address ( CHLOEDB_POD_IP ) and port, since --public-url is the address they share. When coordinators know each other, what one does it does on all. They call each other with the cluster token and the header x-strata-peer , and a call that carries it is not passed on again. GET /v1/queries lists every coordinator's running queries. Another coordinator's ids read id@name , with local_id and nodes (who answered). DELETE /v1/queries/id@name cancels the query on that coordinator. POST /v1/admin/contain and its DELETE reach every coordinator, and each rule says where it holds ( reach ). A rule is kept in --contain 's file with the removals. A coordinator that starts takes up the others' rules and drops those removed while it was down. /v1/health/fleet counts every coordinator's searches. Each coordinator reads the others' last minute and five minutes from GET /v1/coordinators?window=1 , kept 20 s. coordinators lists each one with its own qps , p99_ms , recent_error_ratio and cpu , or why it did not answer. /v1/index?table= counts the reads every coordinator made ( reads_from says who answered), from each one's GET /v1/index/reads?table= . POST /v1/index/reads/reset starts them all again. Alerts: one coordinator sends the messages, and acknowledgements, silences and notes reach every coordinator ( Alerts ). Plans: one plan runs at a time in the cluster, and every coordinator's Cluster page shows it ( Changing the cluster )."],
["server.html#events","chloedb-server","Events","GET /v1/events?from=&to=&kind=&limit= lists what happened, the newest first: copies down and back ( copy_down , copy_up ), new primaries ( primary_changed , with whether failover chose it), shards losing and regaining a majority ( majority_lost , majority_back ), shards and copies added or removed, jobs of moves ( moves_started , moves_finished ), log level changes ( log_level ), alerts firing and resolving, acknowledged, silenced or noted, Contain rules added, removed and ended, every other audited change ( change ) and notes. One action makes one event. Each has a severity , a one-sentence title , its detail , where ( shard , copy , table , rule ) and who . A coordinator compares its cluster's snapshots every 5 seconds, so a copy that goes down is an event when it happens, not when someone looks. POST /v1/events/notes {\"text\", \"ts_ms\"?, \"where\"?} (admin) adds a note, audited. The latest 20,000 events are in memory, and with a log store each is also written to its table events , kept 30 days, which answers ranges older than memory."],
["server.html#logs","chloedb-server","Logs","The server logs to stderr, as text or, with --log-format json , one JSON object per line ( timestamp , level , target , message , the event's fields, and span with the request's id , method and route). Every line written while serving a request names its id. At the default level, info , it logs what it opened and where it listens, a query or write that took --slow-ms or more ( slow query , with its kind, outcome, rows and SQL, cut to 2,000 bytes; slow write , with the rows written and the SQL of an INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE ; parameters are not logged), a request that failed on the server's side (status 500 and up, at most ten lines a second, with how many were held back), a merge or background flush that failed, and a database that stopped accepting writes. debug adds a line for every request answered ( answered , with its status and duration), and the engine's flushes and merges. The libraries the server is built on log only warnings unless the filter names them ( --log info,h2=debug )."],
["server.html#the-log-store-logs-kept-in-chloedb","chloedb-server","The log store: logs kept in ChloeDB","A server also keeps what it logs, the queries it answers, and its load, in a ChloeDB database of their own: its log store, DB.logs beside the database it serves ( --log-store PATH ; off for none). A coordinator keeps one only when --log-store names it. It holds three tables: logs , a line a row: id , its time ( ts , a TIMESTAMP , and minute and hour , whole minutes and hours since 1970, to GROUP BY ), the server it was logged on ( node , --node-name ), level ( error , warn , info , debug , trace ), target (the module), message , the event's other fields as JSON text ( fields ), and the request it was logged for ( request_id , method , route ). A line's status , error and time ( ms ) are also columns of their own. queries , a query a row: id , ts , minute , hour , node , request_id , kind , outcome , whether it was a shard's part of a coordinator's query ( shard ), its sql , its statement's shape ( fingerprint , as /v1/queries/stats groups them), ms , n_rows , error , and the rest of what the query log keeps of it, its trace, plan and profile ( steps ) among it, as a JSON object ( detail ). Every slow or failed query is kept, and of the others at most --log-queries a second. load , a minute a row: id , ts , minute , hour , step (whole ten minutes since 1970), node , role ( coordinator , primary , replica or standalone ), shard , and over the minute the mean qps and cpu (cores busy), cores , busy ( cpu over cores ), the slowest p99_ms and the volume's disk_used_ratio . Autoscaling replays its rules on it. They are searched with SQL like any table, POST /v1/logs/query {\"sql\": \"...\", \"params\": {...}} (or the gRPC call Logs , with an admin key), with MATCH and bm25 over the text, GROUP BY over the levels, nodes and minutes, and joins between the two: A coordinator answers for the whole cluster: it asks every replica of every shard (the Logs call, as a shard's part), the other coordinators' stores ( POST /v1/logs/query with \"scope\": \"local\", \"partial\": true , so the queries their clients sent and their own lines are in it too) and its own store, and merges their answers as it merges its shards', so COUNT(*) and GROUP BY count every server and ORDER BY ts interleaves their lines. The answer's nodes gives each server's rows and time, or its error: a server that does not answer is named there, and the answer goes on without it (an older server, without a store, answers 501). Such a search reads one table with conditions each store's index answers, so that each store's answer can be merged: a join, a subquery, or a LIKE or ILIKE on a text column such as message (which a store checks row by row after reading every line) is refused, with an error that names it. Find words with MATCH instead ( MATCH(message, '\"alert firing\"') for a phrase, MATCH(message, 'alert firing') for both words); LIKE 'abc%' on a KEYWORD column ( level , node , target , route ) is merged. Any query runs on one server's own lines, sent to that server with \"scope\": \"local\" (to a coordinator, its own lines and its clients' queries, when it keeps a store). GET /v1/logs/store gives the store (its path, rows, size, retention, what it wrote, dropped and deleted), its tables, and on a coordinator every server's store; /v1/cluster shows each replica's under detail.log_store . A writer thread writes what waits every second, or as soon as 4,096 lines wait, in one transaction it does not sync: a crash of the process loses nothing written, a power cut the latest lines. Up to 100,000 lines wait in memory; past that the newest are dropped and counted. Every 30 seconds the store deletes the rows older than --log-retain and, while it takes more than --log-store-mb , the oldest quarter of what is left, then merges, which frees their pages. Reading the logs writes none: what the store's own threads log (its writes, flushes and merges) stays out of it, its searches are not queries of the server's, and the log viewer's requests are not logged as answered. The store is a database like any other, so it can be opened on its own, by a server ( --db app.chloedb.logs --log-store off ), from Python or with chloedb-server export while no server holds it. The metrics name it chloedb_log_store_* : rows{table} , written_total{table} , bytes , waiting_lines , dropped_total{reason} ( lines past the waiting limit, queries past it, failed writes, sampled queries over --log-queries ) and deleted_total{reason} ( expired , size )."],
["server.html#what-the-server-is-doing-now","chloedb-server","What the server is doing now","Beside /metrics , a server keeps what an operator (and the UI ) needs to see what it is doing, in memory, without a Prometheus to scrape it: GET /v1/queries lists the queries running now, the longest running first: the SQL (cut to 4,000 bytes), kind, how long it has run, the request's id, whether a coordinator asked it of this shard, and its trace so far. DELETE /v1/queries/ID cancels one: it stops at its next check (within milliseconds, as at a deadline) and answers 499, counted as outcome=\"cancelled\" in chloedb_queries_total . Cancelling a coordinator's query drops its calls to the shards, which stop theirs. GET /v1/queries/recent?since=MS&limit=N gives the latest 500 queries that ended, the newest first, each with its number ( id ), SQL, statement shape, kind, outcome, rows, time, trace, and its profile's number of steps ( steps_n ) and CPU time ( cpu_ms , when measured); GET /v1/queries/slow the latest 200 that took --slow-ms or more (or the threshold set since, below); GET /v1/queries/finished/ID one of them with its profile's steps and the executor's plan lines ( ?full=true gives them in the lists too); GET /v1/queries/request/ID those a request ran (its x-request-id , which a coordinator passes to its shards, so each shard shows its part of a coordinator's query under the same id). A trace is the timed spans of one request, from its start: parsing and binding its SQL ( plan ), waiting for a write it must see, waiting for a query slot ( admission ), executing, and on a coordinator every call to a shard's replica ( TermStats , Query , Fetch and so on) with the shard, the replica, and how it ended ( ok , error , or outrun for a call another replica answered first); a hedge or a retry is marked again . POST /v1/query with \"trace\": true answers with its own. A query's profile is the timed steps of its execution, an EXPLAIN ANALYZE of every query, which the list of queries keeps ( /v1/queries/finished/:id ) and its answer has in stats.steps when the request asks, with \"steps\": true or \"profile\": true (in /v1/query , /v1/sql and /v1/plan ; /v1/explain always answers them). Without, an answer leaves them out: they were most of its bytes, and a coordinator spent a sixth of its CPU reading its shards' steps and writing them out again. A coordinator asks its shards to leave theirs out too ( QueryRequest.skip_steps ), so its own list keeps its calls to the shards and its merge, and each shard's list its own steps. The profile is a tree in pre-order (each step's depth ), each step with what ran ( op : rank , bm25 , vector , segment , filter , aggregate , project , a compound query's leaf , join or distinct , a coordinator's shard and merge , and the server's own wait for a thread , finish and hand back around the run), what it ran on ( detail ), when it began and how long it took ( start_us and us , from the request's start as the trace's spans are), the rows it ended with, and for a segment's step the segment ( seg ) and the thread that searched it ( thread : 0 the query's own, 1 and up the helpers that joined it). With \"profile\": true each step has its CPU time too ( cpu_us , of every thread that worked for it, at a system call a step, about 0.3 µs) and the bytes it allocated and its allocations ( alloc_bytes , allocs , of every thread that worked for it, as the server's allocator counts them, below), and the answer has the request's trace. A coordinator's profile holds each call to a shard ( shard ) with the shard's own steps inside it ( on the shard ), on the coordinator's clock with the network's time split evenly each way; with \"profile\": true they have their CPU time and allocations too: the coordinator asks each shard for them ( QueryRequest.profile ), and a shard measures them for every query with cpu_all (below). A query keeps at most 4,000 steps ( stats.steps_dropped counts the rest). GET /v1/profile/settings and PUT /v1/profile/settings {\"slow_ms\": 50, \"cpu_all\": true} (admin) set, while the server runs, the time from which a query is kept among the slow ones ( null : none) and whether every query's CPU time is measured. On a coordinator, GET /v1/profile/settings?node= and PUT /v1/profile/settings?node= {...} read and set a copy's (its URL or short name), over the same gRPC call a profile takes; the change is audited on the coordinator (\"profiling on s0-0: slow_ms 25\") and on the copy. ?coordinator= (its URL or name) reads and sets another coordinator's, through this one. Both last until the server restarts: --slow-ms sets the threshold it starts with, and measuring every query's CPU time has no flag. POST /v1/profile/cpu {\"seconds\": 5, \"hz\": 99} (admin) samples every thread of the server for seconds (at most 60), hz times a second of each thread's CPU time (at most 1000, from SIGPROF on Linux and macOS; 501 elsewhere; macOS does not interrupt the thread that spent the CPU time, so a profile there shows little of where it goes: a query run in a loop on one thread got none of 534 samples on a macOS CI runner, where Linux gives it more than half), and answers what they were running: the threads by pool ( threads , each with its count and samples ), each distinct stack with its frames from the root by place in names and its samples ( stacks , at most 50,000; dropped counts the samples of the rest), samples , seconds the process's CPU time ( cpu_seconds ) and the cores it kept busy ( cores ). With sql (and params ) it profiles that query, run again and again for that long by clients callers at once (1 to 64): it runs once first, so a query that fails answers at once, its runs are not kept among the latest queries, and the answer has their times ( runs: {count, clients, min_ms, median_ms, p99_ms, max_ms, error} ). ?format=folded answers folded lines ( thread;root;...;leaf samples ) for flamegraph.pl, inferno or speedscope. A sample stands for its share of the process's CPU time, measured while the profile is taken ( cpu_seconds ): the system's timer gives the whole process at most one sample a tick (250 a second on many Linux kernels), so a busy server gets fewer than hz a CPU second ( rate ). With glibc 2.35 or later the samples include the C library (malloc, memcpy, system calls; 40% of the samples of a query run in a loop on the demo data); before it, and on other systems, samples there are not taken ( blocked ), since unwinding from them could deadlock. One profile is taken at a time (409 while another is). Sampling slows the threads it interrupts: in the cloud container (4 cores), a BM25 query run in a loop by one caller ran 6% fewer times a second at 99 Hz and 11% fewer at 997 Hz, and by four callers within the noise (about 4%). The frames are named from the binary's symbol table after the timer stops. The sampler's table of stacks takes about 70 MB of memory the first time a profile is taken, which the server keeps from then on (each later profile replaces it and gives the old one back to the system). The debug information read to name the frames, of a CPU or an allocation profile, is given back once they are named. One profile of each kind at a time: a second answers 409 with who takes the first and for how many seconds more (\"a CPU profile of this server is being taken, by ops-key, for 4 s more: one at a time\"). Each is audited with its object (\"CPU profile of NODE\"), the server ( node ), seconds , hz or every_bytes , and the sql and clients of a query run in a loop. GET /v1/profile/memory (admin) answers the heap as the server's allocator counts it ( heap : the bytes in use, in_use_bytes , and their peak, peak_bytes , kept every 100 ms; the bytes allocated, allocations and frees since the server started), the resident memory and its peak, the bytes the running queries hold against --query-memory-mb and the number of threads; the series has heap_bytes , heap_peak_bytes , alloc_bytes_s and allocs_s , and each of the latest queries profiled its bytes allocated ( alloc_bytes ). The server's allocator is the system's, counting: each allocation and free adds to a counter of the bytes in use (one of 64, picked by thread), and each thread counts what it allocated, which a query's steps read. POST /v1/profile/heap {\"seconds\": 5, \"every\": 2097152} (admin) takes an allocation profile: about one allocation in every every bytes a thread allocates (4 KiB to 64 MiB, default 2 MiB; the gaps are random, so each byte is as likely as any other to be sampled) records its stack and the bytes it stands for (its size over the chance it had to be sampled), and is watched until it is freed. The answer is shaped as a CPU profile's, each stack with the bytes allocated ( bytes ), the allocations ( allocs ) and the bytes still in use when the profile ended ( in_use ), with the bytes allocated and allocations counted while it was taken ( allocated_bytes , allocs ), the heap in use at its start and end, samples , and untracked (samples past the 65,536 whose frees are watched, which count as freed). With sql it profiles a query run in a loop as a CPU profile does; ?format=folded answers folded lines of the bytes allocated ( &value=in_use : of those still in use). One is taken at a time (409 while another is), and a program that uses the crate without the server's allocator answers 501. GET /v1/profile/threads (admin) lists every thread of the process with its name, state, CPU time and context switches (Linux; empty elsewhere). On a coordinator, each of the four ( cpu , heap , memory , threads ) with ?node=URL , a replica's URL as GET /v1/cluster lists it, is that server's: the coordinator asks it over gRPC ( Profile ) and answers what it answered, with node in its JSON. The server takes the profile itself, a query of it run on its own rows. A URL not in the cluster answers 404, a server that is not a coordinator 400, and a node whose server is older than the call 501. What it costs, measured on a BM25 query run in a loop on the demo data in the cloud container (4 cores), against the same binary with the system's allocator: counting every allocation, the median of 14 paired rounds of 5 s was 5% fewer runs a second with one caller (the rounds ranged from 30% fewer to 27% more: that container is noisy) and within 1% with four callers. Taking an allocation profile walks a thread's stack for each sample: the loop ran 3 to 6% fewer times a second sampled every 2 MiB, 6 to 29% at 512 KiB and 20 to 46% at 64 KiB (medians over runs of the benchmark, one and four callers). GET /v1/queries/stats gives statistics per statement shape, as PostgreSQL's pg_stat_statements : the SQL with every literal replaced by ? (a list of values by ?... ), so that queries differing only in their values count as one, with calls, errors, total, mean, minimum, maximum and percentile times, rows per call and its share of the time spent, the most time first. It keeps 2,000 shapes, dropping the least called for a new one; POST /v1/queries/stats/reset starts over. GET /v1/timeseries?since=MS gives a point every 2 seconds for the last hour ( ?from=MS&to=MS reaches further back: from memory while it holds from , else a point a minute from the log store's load table, an hour a point past 10,000 minutes, with source and interval_ms saying which): queries per second (by kind too), query latency percentiles and the histogram of the interval, errors, refusals, partial answers, HTTP requests and their 4xx and 5xx, writes and rows written per second, slow requests, CPU (cores busy), resident memory, queries running and slots free, query memory, and on a database its rows, segments, deleted rows, memtable rows, file, log and reusable bytes, merges, the write queue, leases, the disk's free and total bytes, the failing flags, and replication (a primary's replicas in sync and the most transactions one is behind; a replica's sequence number and whether it serves reads). GET /v1/logs?after=SEQ&level=warn&request=ID&q=TEXT&limit=N gives the latest 5,000 log lines, as the log writes them (with each line's request id, method and route), from line after on; PUT /v1/logs/filter {\"filter\": \"debug\", \"expires_s\": 1800} changes what is logged while the server runs, as --log names it, for 30 minutes by default ( 0 : until set again), then goes back on its own; both answers say the filter, when it expires ( expires_ms ) and who set it ( set_by ), and each change is an event. On a coordinator, ?node= (a copy's URL or short name) sets that copy's filter. A client's 4xx answers are logged at INFO, and a query that ran out of its client's own deadline at WARN, counted as client_deadline rather than the copy's failure. A coordinator tells a shard, in the call's metadata ( x-strata-deadline: client ), that its part runs to the client's own deadline; a shard whose part passes it counts client_deadline and logs a warning rather than an error. GET /v1/load?from=&to= on a coordinator reads each copy's own minutes from its log store (16 at a time, 10 s each): copies ( shard , url , short , name , points of t , qps , cpu , cores , busy , p99_ms , disk_used_ratio ) and missing (the copies that did not answer, and why). The log store's load rows hold, besides the rate and CPU, error_ratio , errors_s , client_errors_s , timeouts_s , rejected_s , p50_ms , writes_s and mem_bytes . GET /v1/system gives the process (version, uptime, cores, SIMD, resident memory, CPU time, the memory limit), the database's path and the space left on its disk, and the server's limits; GET /v1/routes requests by route and queries by kind, with their mean and 99th percentile times; GET /v1/cluster where the server stands: its role and health, or on a coordinator every replica of every shard with its role, health, latest point of the series, version and how long it took to answer, and the slots each shard holds. GET /v1/routes?from=MS&to=MS answers over that window from minute snapshots (an hour a snapshot past a day), with from_ms , to_ms and held_from_ms when the server holds less than the window; without from , since the server started."],
["server.html#alerts","chloedb-server","Alerts","The server checks alert rules against each new point of the series ( above ) and, on a coordinator, against the cluster's state. A rule names one of the point's values ( p99_ms , error_ratio , disk_used_ratio , replica_behind_max and so on); some are made of others: http_5xx_ratio (5xx answers over all HTTP requests, weighted by each point's requests, leaving out the 504s of queries whose client's own deadline passed, which are the client's; like error_ratio and availability with eval: mean , it is judged over a window of 100 requests or more, or 10 failures or more, and counts as none failed below both), replica_behind_s (how long the replica furthest behind has lacked a transaction the primary committed, read from the primary's seq_no over the series; a replica being sent a whole copy, or not heard from since the primary started, is left out; a point alone, without the series, gives transactions behind over the write rate, and no value when nothing is written), disk_hours_to_full (the window's least-squares slope of disk_used_ratio , 87,600 when it does not fill) and key_error_ratio (the worst key's failed calls over its calls in the window, keys with 30 calls or more; the alert's where names the keys, their calls and owners, and its message goes to the route named after the worst key's owner when there is one) and key_busy_ratio (the queries the busiest key ran at once on average, its calls' time over the window, per core of the server, counted only for a key with over half of the window's query time while another key made 30 calls or more, and 0 otherwise; the default rule key-flood , \"A client crowds out the others\", fires over 1 for a minute, resolves under 0.5, names the key with its share, the queries it ran at once and its owner, and links to its queries in Queries, Running now), how the points of a window ( window_s ) make one value ( eval : their mean , max , min , the rate of a count, or all , a value every point must pass), a comparison ( > , >= , < , <= ) and a threshold, how long the condition must hold before the alert fires ( for_s ), the threshold that resolves it ( resolve , by default the threshold itself), or, with vs: \"before\" , the window's value as a multiple of the same window just before it ( p99_ms mean over 5 min > 2 with vs: \"before\" fires when the p99 doubled; a latency, a metric ending in _ms , under 20 ms in the window counts as unchanged, so a p99 going from 1 ms to 2 ms does not fire, and p99_ms under half the p99 objective too, --slo-p99-ms , so with the default 250 ms a p99 going from 26 ms to 54 ms does not either), a severity ( critical , warning , info ), and who it is for ( owner , runbook , route ). An alert goes pending when its condition holds, firing once it has held for_s seconds, and back to ok (resolved) when the window's value passes resolve . A window without any value of the metric is no data: it keeps the alert as it was (a firing alert stays firing, with no_data_since_ms ) and never resolves it. A rule's scope is self (this server's points) or each_copy : every copy of every shard evaluates it on its own points and reports its states in its health detail ( detail.alerts ), and a coordinator gathers them into one alert per rule whose where lists the copies that are not ok ( copy , short , state , value , since_ms ; unheard_since_ms for a copy that stopped reporting, rule_differs for one that runs another version of the rule). A coordinator gives its each_copy rules to its copies: PUT /v1/alerts/rules on a coordinator answers pushed: {copies, took, failed: [{copy, short, error}]} , and a copy that did not take them (down, or an older chloedb-server) gets them again within 30 seconds of answering. A copy keeps its own self rules, and a copy whose rules were edited after the coordinator's is left alone. Until a copy runs the rule as edited, the coordinator judges that copy's value by the rule as it is now, so no FIRING names a value that passes its rule; the alert says copies.rule_differs (and copies.push_failed ), and its message says how many copies run the old rule. A copy not heard from for a minute has no data for its each_copy rules: what it said before it went down no longer keeps an alert firing, and \"A copy is down\" says why. A copy whose points never carry the rule's metric (a primary for a replica's lag) is left out and counted in copies.not_reported ; the rule has no data only when no copy has the metric. A window the series does not cover yet, after a restart, is judged on the points there are once they span 30 seconds (or half the window), at once for a rule that fires on any one point ( max over > ), and the rule's state says window_partial and window_covered_s . A coordinator also has built-in rules over the cluster: a shard without a majority ( shard-no-majority ), a copy down, a shard with fewer copies than asked ( under-replicated ), a primary that changed, partial answers, a copy that stopped reporting ( copy-silent ), and the availability of searches. A server starts with rules for a database that refuses writes, failing flushes or merges, data a scrub found damaged on disk ( damaged ), a disk over 90% full (warning) and over 97% (critical), a disk that fills within 12 hours at the last 30 minutes' rate, query p99 over the SLO ( --slo-p99-ms , 250 ms), a p99 that doubled against the 5 minutes before, more queries failing than the SLO allows ( --slo-error-ratio , 1%; the built-in rule over searches that fail takes it too, and both follow a changed SLO unless someone changed their threshold), one key's calls failing over 20%, refused queries (429), server errors over 1% of requests, a replica over 30 seconds behind, out of sync or not serving reads, query memory over 90% spent, and a write queue over 512; each has a resolve threshold, so a value at the threshold does not page on and off. The built-in partial answers rule is a mean over 2 minutes (over 0.1/s, resolved under 0.02/s). PUT /v1/alerts/rules {\"rules\": [...]} (admin) replaces them, audited with the rules before and after, and the UI's Alerts page edits them; rules and routes are kept in --alerts FILE if given, else in memory. Next to that file, alerts.state.json keeps each alert's state, acknowledgement, silence, notes and when and by whom its FIRING message was sent: a restarted server keeps them, and does not page again what it already paged. An edit of a rule that stays on keeps its alert, judged by the new condition at the next check (it resolves then if the value passes, with \"the rule was edited by X\"); turning a rule off or deleting it resolves it at once. GET /v1/alerts lists the rules with their states ( firing is how many fire); GET /v1/alerts/:rule one rule with its changes over 7 days. POST /v1/alerts/:rule/ack , POST /v1/alerts/:rule/silence {\"minutes\", \"reason\"} ( DELETE ends it) and POST /v1/alerts/:rule/notes {\"text\"} (write scope) are audited and become events ( alert_acked , alert_silenced , alert_note ), as are firing and resolving ( alert_fired , alert_resolved ). Each change of an alert is kept in memory and in the log store's alerts table for 7 days. POST /v1/alerts/backtest {\"rule\": {...}, \"from\"?, \"to\"?} replays a rule (as it would be saved, or a saved rule by its id, \"rule\": \"p99-jump\" ) over memory's points or up to 7 days of the load table, the last day by default, and says when it would have fired and resolved; it changes nothing. from and to are milliseconds or seconds since 1970, as numbers or digits, or ISO 8601 text ( 2026-11-12T08:00:00Z ); anything else is a 400. A server without a log store backtests over the points memory holds: the window is cut to them, and the answer's note says what it covers rather than what was asked. On a coordinator a rule over the cluster's state is replayed from its events (copies down and up, majorities lost and back), and an each_copy rule is backtested by every copy over its own points, answering each copy's spans and the points of the six that fired most or came closest ( source: \"copies\" ). Every answer says covered_from , the first time it had points for; key_error_ratio and key_busy_ratio cannot be replayed. A coordinator's events start when it does: what was already down then counts from its start, as its alert did. GET /v1/alerts?brief=1 answers each rule's state and, of those not ok, their since, value, acknowledgement, silence, cause ( held_by ) and copies, without events, routes or where : what the bell needs, about a fifth of the whole (5 KB of 27 KB on a 2x2 cluster in a storm). With several coordinators of one cluster, one sends the messages: the one with the lowest URL among those heard from in the last 45 seconds ( GET /v1/alerts says pager: {this, sends, coordinators} ). It tells the others what it paged, and they show \"paged by X\"; one that is not told within a minute of an alert becoming sendable (firing, and neither held nor silenced) sends it itself, saying why. A rule over a coordinator's own traffic (its searches' errors, p99, a key's failing calls) can fire on one coordinator alone: every coordinator shows it as firing, with here (its own state) and elsewhere (the coordinators where it fires or waits, from what they last told it, at most 45 s old), counts it in firing_shown and firing_elsewhere , and takes an acknowledgement of it, which goes to the one where it fires. That one sends the message at once once the one that sends the messages said, 15 s or more after it fired, that it does not see it, and sends its resolution too. Coordinators telling each other their alerts are not audited: the person's change is, on the coordinator where it was made. A coordinator restarted with its state file sends no FIRING message for its first 20 seconds: by then it has taken what the others paged, and pages nothing twice. Rules, routes, acknowledgements, silences and notes made on one coordinator reach the others ( POST /v1/alerts/peer , GET /v1/alerts/peer , admin, with the header x-strata-peer ), the newest edit of the rules winning. In a storm a cause holds back what it explains: a shard without its majority or a copy down holds its shard's replica rules (out of sync, behind, not serving reads, under-replicated), a shard without its majority holds its copies down, and either holds the coordinator's traffic rules (searches fail, errors, p99) that are no more severe. A key's failing calls are held the same way. A held alert shows held_by and held_why and sends no message of its own. Once its cause resolved it stays held for its own window and wait (at most 10 minutes, held_until_ms ), which still include the outage, and pages only if it still fires then. firing_shown counts what fires neither silenced nor held. Firing and resolved alerts go to the routes that take their severity, unless the alert is silenced: GET and PUT /v1/alerts/routes (admin) list and set them ( name , kind : webhook , pagerduty or opsgenie , its url , a key for PagerDuty's Events API v2 routing key or Opsgenie's API key, kept and never shown, the severities it takes, all when empty, and repeat_min , after which a critical alert nobody acknowledged is sent again), each with how its last delivery went, and POST /v1/alerts/routes/:name/test sends a test message. A message says the cluster, the rule, where it fires, the value against its threshold (with the digits it takes to differ from it; a 0/1 metric \"is on\" or \"is off\"), the rule's owner, the rule's runbook when it has one (the built-in rules have none in the message: GET /v1/alerts links each to its runbook in runbook_docs ) and a link to the alert's page (from --public-url ), as {\"text\": \"[chloedb FIRING warning] Disk almost full on prod/search/s1-0: disk_used_ratio = 0.934 (> 0.9, mean over 5 min). ... Runbook: ... https://cc.example/#/alert/disk-full\", \"alert\": {...}} , which a Slack incoming webhook takes as it is, with dedup_key ( strata/<cluster>/<rule>/<fired at> , in the old name still, the same in its RESOLVED; a test message's is strata/<cluster>/test/<ms> ; PagerDuty and Opsgenie routes open and close one incident with it), each try of a message carrying the same Idempotency-Key , and [chloedb RESOLVED warning] ... when it resolves. --alert-webhook URL is a route of every severity. Firings are logged and counted in chloedb_alerts_firing{severity} ."],
["server.html#who-sends-the-queries-and-containing-them","chloedb-server","Who sends the queries, and containing them","Every query is kept with who sent it: the key's name and id ( key , key_id ), the client's address ( addr , from X-Forwarded-For only behind --trust-proxy ), its application ( x-chloedb-app ), its client ( x-chloedb-client , command-center for the UI), its parameters ( params ; --redact-params keeps only their types) and its deadline ( deadline_ms ), in the running and finished queries, the statements and the log store's queries table. A query ends ok , partial , error , timeout , client_error (the client's mistake, such as a misspelt column), client_deadline (the client's own deadline, shorter than the server's), refused (429) or cancelled , and the series counts the client's failures apart from the server's ( client_errors_s , client_deadlines_s ); error_ratio is the server's failures over the queries that ended, and absent when none ended. GET /v1/queries/stats?from=&to= gives each statement shape over a window, counted a minute at a time for the last day: calls, errors, times, its share of the time, the keys and clients that sent it, whether it is new ( first_seen_ms ) and the window before ( prev ) to compare. GET /v1/queries/stats/:fingerprint is one statement: its trend, its time by key and by shard, its slowest and latest calls with their parameters, and its errors. On a coordinator both are every coordinator's: the counts of each shape are added up over the coordinators that answer ( coordinators says which), each statement saying how many calls went through each, and a statement's slowest and latest calls name their coordinator ( local=1 for this coordinator's alone). GET /v1/queries?group=shape groups the running queries by shape and key, and DELETE /v1/queries?fingerprint=&key= (admin) cancels a group at once, audited with the reason. POST /v1/admin/contain {\"match\": {\"fingerprint\"?, \"key\"?}, \"action\": \"limit\" | \"refuse\", \"concurrency\"?, \"expires_s\"?, \"reason\"} (admin) limits a statement shape or a key to concurrency queries at once, or refuses it, at admission, for expires_s (15 minutes by default): a refused query is answered 429 naming the rule. GET /v1/admin/contain lists the rules and DELETE /v1/admin/contain/:id ends one; each is audited and an event, and so is a rule that expires (who system , reason expired ). Contain rules are each server's own, in memory."],
["server.html#merges","chloedb-server","Merges","A local database merges its segments on merger threads as writes add them ( how merges work ). --merge-threads sets how many merges may run at once (default: half the cores, 1 to 4), --merge-memory-mb the memory they may use together (default: half of the cgroup memory limit, or of RAM), and --merge-write-mb-per-sec how fast they may write (default: no limit). GET /v1/stats shows them under merges , and the server logs a merge that fails. A server killed for running out of memory during a merge restarts into smaller merges of the same segments."],
["server.html#the-ui","chloedb-server","The UI","GET / serves the command center, a console for one server or a whole cluster. It reads the same API as any client (the cluster's shards, fleet health, the series, alerts, events, running and recent queries, the logs, keys and the audit log), so what it shows is what the server reports, and every change it makes is an API call a key's scope allows. Its requests carry x-chloedb-client: command-center , and a change it makes carries the reason it asked for in x-chloedb-reason , which the audit log keeps with it. A button that changes something says, when the key lacks the scope, which scope it takes. The navigation has five groups: Build , for the data and the queries (Start here, Tables, saved searches, the query builder, the SQL workbench, import and export, connectors, models, the schema, slices), Watch and Run the cluster , for running a server or a fleet (a coordinator lists those two first), Apps , the programs the server shows as views of their own ( below ), and Labs , the tools for search engineers, collapsed (pin one to keep it in the sidebar). Watch Home answers one question first, whether the fleet is serving, from GET /v1/health/fleet : the numbers behind the answer (shards serving reads and taking writes, p99 and the share of queries failing against the SLO, copies answering, the replica furthest behind, the fullest disk), where the trouble is on a map of every shard, what would restore service, since when, and what changed just before. Alerts lists what is firing first, grouped by rule, with where each rule fires (which copies, which shard) and whether someone acknowledged or silenced it. In a storm what a cause explains is listed under it, held back, with Acknowledge all. Rules show fires and flaps over the range, and a noisy filter; a rule some copies have not taken yet says so. Rules follow, with their scope, owner, runbook and how often they fired over the range. A rule with no data says so plainly. An alert's page ( #/alert/<rule> , the link in every notification) is where an incident is worked: the metric around the alert with failovers, rollouts and changes marked, the blast radius, what changed just before it fired, the failing statements, the affected copies with links to their logs, the profiler and Cluster, the log lines grouped by pattern, and a shared timeline of acknowledgements, silences and notes. The rule editor evaluates over a window (mean, max, min or rate), resolves at its own threshold, and backtests a change over the last 7 days before you save it (saying the span it had points for, each copy's line for an each_copy rule), saves with how many copies took it, and compares with the window before for a relative rule. Routes send messages by severity, as webhooks, PagerDuty or Opsgenie, can send an unacknowledged critical again, and show whether their last delivery worked. Timeline shows what changed in the fleet on one time axis, from GET /v1/events : alerts, copies going down and coming back, primaries changing, rollouts, moves, people's changes and notes, with a summary of the night. Add a note from it. Logs searches the log store , every server's lines at once, over the range, from one search bar ( level>=warn server:s1-0 \"no answer\" ), kept in the link. A line opens a pane with its fields and where to go next: its request on every server, its queries, the copy in Cluster, the lines around it. Patterns groups similar messages, Live adds new lines at the top, and Log levels changes what a server logs until an expiry, with a reason ( PUT /v1/logs/filter ). Servers that did not answer are named on one line. Log explorer searches the log lines in the database's own tables (any table with a TIMESTAMP : syslog, OpenTelemetry logs , an app's JSON lines, and any other table's rows by their time), with a key limited to tables too (the oncall preset's): a search box (words, \"phrases\" , host:core-rtr-02 , level>=warn , -term , or WHERE ... ), the last 15 minutes to a week ( ts > now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' ) or a window, lines per minute ( time_bucket ), the newest lines followed live, the lines around one on the same host, the lines grouped by pattern, and the SQL it ran. A line is its text (the column most of the newest lines fill, or one picked under the search box, as its time column is), with the row's other fields beside it when the table is not a log (it has no level or host), when the text is empty, or when asked; a table with no text column shows its fields, and a word in its search is a field's exact value. Without a log table it shows how to send logs: the table, the key, an OpenTelemetry Collector's settings and a JSON lines import ( Search your logs ). Queries shows what runs now, grouped by statement shape and API key. A group can be cancelled at once, with a reason for the audit log, or contained: its shape or key refused for a while. Statements cover the range, compared with the window before, with cards for errors by statement and key, new shapes and those that grew most. History reads 7 days of queries from the log store. Each statement has its own page ( #/statement/<fingerprint> ) with its trend and the events around it, why it is slow or failing, its time by shard, copy and key, and its slowest calls with their parameters. A query's trace sums up the fan-out (the rounds, the critical path, the copy it waited for) and draws every call on request. Performance covers the fleet over the range: findings that each say which rule fired on which numbers, and never report that all is well without data; failures a second by cause, with the clients' errors and deadlines apart from the server's; and, on a coordinator, every copy's p99, CPU, disk and lag. Drag across a chart to zoom every chart to that span. Feedback ( #/feedback ) holds what people asked of the command center itself ( below ): each item with the screenshot its sender marked up, the view it is about, and its status and priority, to read, put in order and hand to someone, or to an agent. Run the cluster Cluster (a coordinator) is one canvas for the whole cluster. In Live , every shard is a tile and every copy a pip in it, coloured by one lens at a time (health, CPU, disk, p99, version, replication lag, and load: the reads and writes the coordinator routed to the shard by key over 5 minutes, against the mean of the shards) on the SLO's scale, grouped by shard, node or zone, with the problems named first; 192 shards of 3 copies fit on one screen. Click a shard for its copies (role, term, lag, version, why the coordinator avoids one), its diagram, its logs, profile and statements, and each copy's operations: make it the primary, compare its replicas, write a checkpoint, remove it. Each operation says why when it is not offered (a primary is never removed from here, a copy that is down, being copied or behind is not made the primary, and nothing changes while a rollout runs). Fail a node or a zone in a what-if to see which shards would keep serving. Under the map, Key slots draws the 4,096 slots as a 64 × 64 grid, each coloured by the reads and writes routed to it over 1, 5 or 15 minutes, with a line between shards, the coordinator's verdict (a hot range or a hot slot, hot shards and hot slots ) and the slots most counted; a slot opens its shard. What needs attention names a shard the verdict calls hot. Even out by load… (on a hot range in Key slots and What needs attention, and in Capacity) shows the rebalance by load it would run, each shard's keyed load now and after and the disks of the copies sent rows, before it runs it as a plan's step; on counts that cover too little of the window, or a disk without room, it shows the plan and does not offer to run it. Even out the slots… shows its plan the same way, with the disks of the copies sent rows. In Draft , drag a copy to another shard or node, drag a shard onto another to move slots, or drag the end handle to add shards; on a phone each drag is also a button. Nothing applies until the plan does: its steps in order (repairs first), the rows each copies and how long that takes at the rate the last move ran, what it touches, what is checked first ( POST /v1/admin/plan ), and what it removes, which is only what was asked. One Apply , typed when anything is removed, runs the admin calls one at a time on machines of your own, or patches the StrataCluster once on Kubernetes; then its progress shows, with Stop . Capacity says the autoscaler's suggestion in words and the headroom left, and Changes lists the cluster's events and audited changes. #/topology , #/fleet and #/control , the views this canvas replaced, open it. Profiler is the diagnostics of one server: the coordinator, or a copy found by its short name ( s1-0 ), node or shard. It asks first above the safe sampling rates and says what they cost. Profile a query (once, 5 or 20 times, with each run's time and the median run's profile), or open one that ran among the slow or the latest, and set the slow threshold and CPU time for every query while the server runs. A profile has its time, CPU time and how parallel it ran; a flame chart of every step beside the request's trace, segments that ran at once on their threads and a coordinator's shard calls side by side, clicked to zoom; the operator tree with each step's total and self time, CPU time and rows (an EXPLAIN ANALYZE ); the segments by the thread that searched them; the time by operator; and on a coordinator each shard's call split into the network and the shard's own time. CPU samples every thread of the server for 1 to 30 seconds, whatever its clients send or a query run in a loop by 1, 4 or 16 callers at once, and draws a flame graph of what they ran: every frame as wide as its CPU time, coloured by whose code it is (ChloeDB, the server, the async runtime, the standard library), split by thread pool or merged, with a thread filter, a search that shows a function's share and click to zoom; the functions by self and total CPU time; and downloads as folded stacks or JSON. Memory charts the heap in use, its peak and the resident memory with the bytes allocated a second, and takes an allocation profile of the whole server or of a query in a loop: a flame graph of the bytes allocated, of those still in use when it ended or of the allocations, and the functions by them. Below it, every thread of the process, by pool or each, with its state, its CPU now and its waits and preemptions a second. A Profile link (from a statement, History or Running now) fills the Profiler's query and its parameters and never runs it on open; Profile asks first when the statement is slow or contained. Query path (a coordinator) runs a query, with its parameters, 10 to 100 times, one after the other, says first how many shard calls that makes, and takes each run's steps apart: a radar with the coordinator in the middle and a spoke per shard as long as its call (the shard's own time thickened inside it), each shard's call times over the runs against the coordinator's hedge ( --hedge-ms ), and which shard was the last to answer how often, which tells a slow shard from chance. From the same runs it estimates the p99 if the coordinator asked another copy sooner, and how many calls that would send twice. Tables and storage ( #/tables ) answers four questions for every table at once: which is growing, which shard is skewed, which shard is hot, which copy is stale. Above them, every table added up: the bytes of all tables on all copies (on a cluster, as each copy says them), on one copy, their rows and the largest table; the list has a column of each table's bytes on all copies and a row of the totals under it. Sizes come from GET /v1/index/tables ; a row opens its shards against the mean and its copies' lag, and the table's name opens Table details ( #/inspector?table= ): how many documents and fields it has, how its bytes split between each field's index, the doc store and the rest (a treemap), the size of its documents and of each field's values (histograms and percentiles), each field's fill, tokens, distinct terms and range, how fresh each copy is, how the shards compare, and how often its documents are retrieved and its keys asked for. Click the cluster, a shard or a replica in the map at its top and every number follows ( below ). Rollouts (a coordinator the operator runs) shows where a change is: how many copies run the new version, at what pace, and when it ends. When a health gate holds the change (the search p99 and failed searches the coordinator's clients see, the replica furthest behind, the fullest volume, each a live chart with its limit a dashed line to drag), it says which gate, which copy is slow, and that staying held is safe, with Resume and Raise the limit . Every action gives its cost first; a rollback counts only the copies that differ, and a resume asks for a reason. Versions lists the published images by tag, commit and build date, newest first ( GET /v1/admin/kube/versions ), with the ones the copies run marked. Pick one and the operator's plan shows (the copies it restarts, how long that takes, how to go back) before anything restarts. On machines of your own it shows the chloedb-server version each server runs. Autoscaling writes the rules as sentences, each setting an underlined word to click for its choices, each rule with its switch, and replays the servers' last week of load on them at once: how busy the busiest shard's copies would have been, beside a fixed size to pick, with the time over 85% and the frozen hours hatched, each change marked, the copies of each shard and the fullest volume below, the copy-hours of both, and what happened on a day to click. Act , Ask a person and Off set what a rule that fires does; Undo and Save rules appear once the rules differ from what was loaded, and saving shows the operator's plan first. On a cluster the operator runs it also shows a change the rules suggest, in words (the servers it adds or removes, the room they need, the rows copied and the time), with Apply (removing servers asks for the cluster's name) and Dismiss . On machines of your own it says that nothing runs the rules, replays them only, and links to Cluster's Capacity. Simulator sets up a test run : a workload (a profile, or weights by kind), the test documents, a rising or steady rate, the SLO and a target, and asks before it runs, more firmly on a production cluster. While it runs it shows each phase and the rate against the p99 step by step. Its report leads with a grade and a sentence, then what the cluster holds, its p99 at the target, what fills first and what it costs, the checks behind the grade, the steps measured beside the model's curve, where the work went (the coordinator and each copy), a What if of shards, copies a shard, machines, rows and rate (with a grid of what each shape holds, the cheapest that holds the target ringed), the advice with its gains and costs, each kind's numbers, and what the model assumes. Its Choosing a copy test compares the coordinator's ways of choosing a shard's copy: a tick a setting, the clients, the reads right after a write; Preview answers at once by the model, with a what-if of a slow, busy, stalling or late copy, a table of the settings by clients (p99, p99.9, p50, throughput or reads after a write, the best of each count marked, each against this coordinator's), and how shard 0's reads spread; Measure runs the settings on the cluster in turns, and its report shows the medians measured with the model's under each, and every turn. Access and audit opens on Changes : who changed what, from where, the value before and after, and why. Refusals and every audited request have their own tabs. Keys show their owner, last use and expiry; revoked keys stay listed, and a key can be rotated with a grace period, its new secret shown once. It also says who you are and checks the server's posture (a token, keys, loopback, TLS). Settings edits, on Kubernetes, every fleet-wide setting of the StrataCluster's spec, each saying what it does at its value, when it makes the cluster less safe, where its value comes from and what applying it takes (a restart of every server, one at a time, of the coordinators, or nothing). The coordinator plans the change, and one Apply , with a reason, hands it to the operator, whose progress then shows. On machines of your own it shows the flags the cluster reports and how to change one, a server at a time. Upkeep says how the replicas compare (copies that differ, copies never compared), runs Compare every shard now and Write a checkpoint of every shard ( backups ), and shows the fullest disks against 80% and 90%. This coordinator shows its process, limits, configuration and the /metrics text. A server that is not in a cluster keeps its operations (check, vacuum, checkpoint, compare the replicas, a snapshot lease, reset statistics), each answering in words. Cloud setup plans a cluster on Amazon EKS , DigitalOcean Kubernetes or Google Kubernetes Engine , picked at its top (and in the address, #/cloud?provider=digitalocean or ?provider=gcp ), starting from the running cluster's size. Save an AWS access key or a DigitalOcean token (checked with the cloud, then shown only masked), or, for Google Cloud, which takes no key, the project (the page shows the gcloud sign-in it calls with, or says how to sign in); when the keys come from the server's environment it asks the cloud nothing on open and says what using them would call. The view reads the region's machines with their prices and the account's limit: AWS's vCPU quotas, DigitalOcean's droplet limit, or the project's vCPU and address quotas. The machines are a chart of memory against price an hour (coloured by what each is for; on AWS, on demand or spot, Graviton filled and x86-64 hollow; on DigitalOcean, shared CPUs hollow; the suggested ones ringed) and a table to sort and filter; pick one, then the nodes (1 to 1,000), shards, copies of each, coordinators and each copy's volume (on DigitalOcean also the control plane and the Kubernetes version; on Google Cloud the autoscaler's most nodes, Spot, one zone or three, and GKE's version), and see where every copy goes, each copy's share of a node's memory and CPU, the cost an hour, a day and a month line by line, and whether the account's limit allows it. Check with AWS has CloudFormation check the template, Check with DigitalOcean asks DigitalOcean again for the account, the region, the size, the version and the droplet limit, and Check with Google Cloud asks Google again for the region, the machine type, the version, the quotas, the network and which permissions the credentials hold. Create it makes the cluster after its name is typed again (and not while the account's limit is too small). Clusters made here follows each one: where it is (the stack or cluster, ChloeDB, the copies, ready), the cost so far, its last step and what happened before it, the StrataCluster's status, why it failed, the commands that open its command center, on GKE Nodes… (how many, and GKE's autoscaler), and Delete , also after typing its name. The files that make the cluster yourself (the template, DOKS's request or GKE's) and the operator's and the cluster's manifests also download, with the commands that make the cluster and delete it. Follow shows the cluster this one follows: the source, how far behind it is and what each source shard applied, with Pause and Resume (where the operator runs the follow process), Stop following and Promote , a switch-over that holds the source first ( following on Kubernetes, and from the hub ). It also holds and releases the cluster's own writes. Build SQL workbench is an editor in tabs that completes tables, fields (after an alias too), a JSON field's keys at each level ( attrs. , then attrs.dims. , each key with its share of rows and its type), functions and parameters as you type. On production (an environment starting with prod , or none set) it is read-only until someone allows writes for 15 minutes with a reason. Every write first shows how many rows it changes, on which shards, and asks for a reason, kept in the audit log; an UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE , and a DROP , need the table's name typed. A statement that fails shows what kind of error it is, the server's whole message, its line with the place marked (in the editor too) and buttons that write the name it may have meant (a column, table, function or keyword) into the editor; a fix for a write never runs by itself. SHOW TABLES , DESCRIBE t ( SHOW COLUMNS FROM t ) and SHOW CREATE TABLE t are answered from the catalog, with which fields are indexed and stored and what searches each, and EXPLAIN SELECT ... opens the plan. Parameters get a form, where a vector parameter can be a text the server embeds: text given for similarity(f, :q) of an embedded field is embedded by its model. The answer is a grid with each row's score as a bar, a chart of any numeric columns, the plan as a tree with what ran on each segment, the query's profile (with the trace and each step's CPU time when profile is ticked), and the JSON; it exports as TSV, CSV or JSON Lines. An answer larger than a query returns ( --max-result-window , 10,000 rows) shows its first 10,000 rows, read as a scroll, under a banner that says how many the query matches, with Export all : every row as a CSV, written a page at a time (to the file chosen, where the browser can stream one), or a table read whole ( SELECT * FROM t ) as Parquet from GET /v1/export when the key may export. Health profiles the answer's columns by SQL over every row it matches: values, NULLs, distinct values, the least and the most, and a number's spread; it flags a column that is always NULL, one with a single value, and a sum that a few rows make (the top 1% of rows holding half of it). Numbers read the same in the grid, a chart (whose axis has round ticks: 0, 500M, 1B) and Sheets: grouped digits, two decimals from 1,000 up and six below. The sidebar lists the tables and fields to drag into the editor, with snippets written for the table's own fields; history and saved queries stay in the browser, and a link opens a query ( #/sql?q=... ). A link with store=logs (Logs' As SQL ) opens a tab that runs SELECT on every server's log store instead. A write that changes every row of a table, or over 1,000 rows, runs only once the table's name is typed, whatever its WHERE or LIMIT. Schema draws every table as a card with its fields, linked by references (crow's feet), edge tables and EDGES fields, and inside each card the vectors embedded from text, generated and derived text and semantic IDs. Drag the cards, hover one to see its links, click it for its fields, links, models, rank profiles and CREATE TABLE , and for each JSON field the tree of its paths (the share of rows holding each, its types, its range or values; click one to facet it or query its rows), and query, template or drop it. Design a table builds a new one by dragging types into it, with each type's options, table options ( shard_by , cluster_by , retain , edges ) and templates; the server checks it as you go and shows the statement that creates it. Import and export loads a CSV, TSV, JSON Lines or JSON file into a table or a new table made for it: the first rows show with the type each column reads as and the field it goes to, before anything is written, and the upload shows its progress. A SQLite file shows its import plan first, table by table. The database (or some tables) downloads as a SQLite file, and the stream sources show each one's last offset, with a box that sends operations to /v1/stream . Labs Query builder builds a query as a flow of stages: drag blocks and fields onto Find (words, typos allowed, and meaning, fused by rank or by weights), Keep rows where (conditions by type, with a keyword's most common values to pick from), Boost (recency, closeness to a value, a number), Group and measure , Order and Show . The SQL is written as you build and runs as you build, and the links between stages count the rows each kept: the table's, those that pass the filters, those scored and those shown. \"Edit as SQL\" opens it in the workbench. A JSON field's paths are listed under it as fields of their own ( attrs.dims.w ), each with the share of rows that hold it, to drop on any stage. Relevance lab runs two rankings of a query side by side (BM25, typos allowed, vectors, rank fusion, a weighted hybrid, a boost, a rank profile, or any expression), with lines showing where each row moved. Grade rows 0 to 3 (kept in the browser), and each ranking gets its nDCG, its share of good rows and its first good row; over every graded query, a bar per query shows which ranking won, and a weighted hybrid's weight is tuned by trying eleven. A ranking can be kept with its table as a rank profile. Grade with Claude grades the rows of both rankings not graded yet through /v1/judge ; the measures then read your grades, Claude's or yours where you gave one, and the evaluation says how well your grades and Claude's agree (Cohen's linear-weighted kappa). Recsys eval measures logged rankings against what users did next. It joins the rank log to an events table (pick its user, item, time and action columns), grades each served row by the actions within a window, and gives each variant its nDCG, hit rate, MRR and gain at k, with the lift of one over another and whether it is significant (Welch's test over requests). Interleaved requests count as a variant of their own, and a card says which of their two rankings the users preferred (team-draft credit and a sign test). Charts show the share of good rows by position, how well each logged feature alone tells good rows from the rest (its AUC), the measures over time and by a user segment; open a request for the user's history, the rows it served and their grades, and set a grade by hand. The settings download as training data ( POST /v1/rank_log/export ) or copy as curl . Semantic IDs draws a table's codebook as a sunburst: the inner ring is one level of codes, the outer the next, each sized by its rows and coloured by a keyword field's commonest value (paler when mixed). Click a code to zoom into it, and a filter limits it to the rows that pass. The decoding columns are what /v1/sid/next answers a generative recommender at each step, and each code lists its rows with their full codes and how many codes each level uses. Autocomplete completes as you type from a table of queries and counts ( PREFIX , the most used first, then typos allowed with fuzzy ), with the completion ghosted in the box ( Tab takes it). A timeline shows each keystroke's time on the server and round trip, and a benchmark measures the latency by prefix length, exact and with typos; a card says whether the table is clustered by its key ( cluster_by ), which lets an ORDER BY ... LIMIT read its best blocks first. Understanding is what models make of each row and of each search, as two workflows, and where the understanding components are configured. Enrich rows adds fields that a model makes of a row's text when it is written. You pick the field the models read, then add steps: a language ( builtin:lang ), main phrases, near duplicates, the names it mentions ( dict:<table>.<field> ), a classifier's class, a language model's text by a prompt, a vector for meaning, or a caption or transcript of a file. Each step is tried on sample rows before anything is added ( /v1/derive/preview , llm() , embed() ). The fields are added in one ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN . Rows written before them are then filled in, 200 rows a write, by an UPDATE that writes each row's source again so the writer makes the values (the server fills in vectors itself). A table of the fields made so far shows how many rows have each, their commonest values, and fills in the rest. Enrich queries reads a search in steps and adds what it reads to the request. Spelling ( /v1/correct ) changes the words, and a classifier's class, the language and the names it mentions become filters; the values the words name, synonyms, typo tolerance, expansion and a vector's similarity become options of bm25 and rrf that the query runs itself. Context the caller knows ( country = CL ) adds filters too. The page shows the request as SQL with its parameters, runs it beside the words alone with the rows only it finds marked, and saves it as a search whose filters are parameters ( NULL leaves one out). Inspect a row shows a row's text, its derived fields and classes, what each built-in model makes of it, and its chunks ( chunks_of ). Configure trains and drops classifiers ( CREATE CLASSIFIER ), edits the table's synonym list ( CREATE SYNONYMS ), and mines proposals for it ( /v1/synonyms/mine ). Media search finds images (or sounds) by a description, by a file dropped on the page, or by a row (more like this), over a vector embedded from a MEDIA field, with the caption's words mixed in by a slider, a keyword field's values and the colours a model named as filters. Results are a grid of the files ( GET /v1/media/<sha256> ) with their rank and score; the map lays every row out by its vector's two main directions, the hits and the query among them. Graph explorer draws rows as nodes and their links as edges (an edge table, an EDGES field, or a vector field's nearest rows), laid out by forces: drag a node to pin it, scroll to zoom. Search to add nodes, open one to add its neighbours ( REACHABLE ), rank the graph from it with personalized PageRank ( ppr ), and shift-click another, or type its key, for the shortest path ( PATH ); colour and size follow fields, and each walk shows its SQL. Query console is the earlier UI, below ( #/classic ). One time range applies to every view and is kept in the link: 15 minutes to 7 days, or a fixed window (past the last hour the series come from the log store's minutes). Views without a time axis (the SQL workbench, Schema, Labs) hide it. Share copies a link that opens the same view and window, paused; it never holds a key. When the API does not answer, the top bar says so and since when, and every panel says how old its data is. Ctrl K ( ⌘K ) finds a shard, a copy ( s9-2 ), a node, a table, a statement, an alert, a key or a view, and what to do about it; g then a letter goes to a view, and ? lists the shortcuts. On a phone the navigation is behind the menu button and panels open full screen. The page follows the system's light or dark theme unless you choose one. The page and its modules ( chloedb-server/ui/ ) are compiled into the binary. The page names them under /ui/v-<hash of them all>/ , which the browser keeps for good, so a reload asks the server for the page alone; a new build's page names new paths. To work on them without rebuilding, start the server with CHLOEDB_UI_DIR=chloedb-server/ui : it then reads them from there on each request, and the page names them under /ui/ alone."],
["server.html#feedback","chloedb-server","Feedback","Any view sends feedback about itself: press f (or the speech bubble in the top bar, or \"Send feedback\" in the palette), and the view as it is becomes a picture to draw on, with boxes, arrows, a pen and text, each numbered, and black boxes over what must not be kept (a key, a customer's data). A note says what is wrong or what should change, and the item is a bug, a change, an idea or a question. It is sent with POST /v1/feedback (a write key) and kept as a row of the table cc_feedback , which the server makes the first time. The picture is made in the browser, and nothing is sent anywhere but the server: From the page copies the page, puts its stylesheet beside it and draws the copy as an SVG image onto a canvas (no prompt; an app's frame shows as a box), and From the screen takes the tab with the browser's screen capture, which asks first and takes everything pixel for pixel. What is sent is one picture with the marks drawn on it, so a blacked-out part is not in what the server keeps. The marks go too: each with its number, tool, place on the picture in CSS pixels, what it says, and what was under it on the page (a CSS selector, its text, the card and the view's heading), which is what lets an agent find the code. So do the view and its address, the window's size and theme, the server's version, the sender's key name, and, if the sender agrees, the last errors of the page. Feedback ( #/feedback , g then f ) lists the items, open ones first by priority (P0 now to P3 some day) or newest first, filtered by status, kind and words; each has its thumbnail, and opens with its screenshot, note, marks, the page's context and its triage: status ( new , triaged , planned , doing , done , wontfix ), priority, assignee and resolution. Copy for an agent copies a prompt that says what to change and how to mark it done. route body answer POST /v1/feedback {note, kind?, title?, page?, route?, url?, shot?, thumb?, shot_w?, shot_h?, marks?, context?} ; shot and thumb are data: URLs of a PNG, JPEG or WebP image (6 MB and 256 KB at most); route is a route of the command center ( #/ and a path), url an http or https address {id, ts} : write scope GET /v1/feedback?status=open&kind=&q=&assignee=&limit=100&thumbs=1 {feedback, counts, statuses, kinds, exists} , newest first, without the pictures ( thumbs=1 : with each thumbnail); status is open (new, triaged, planned, doing), all , or a list GET /v1/feedback/:id?shot=1 the item, its marks and context as JSON, shot_url ; shot=1 adds the screenshot as a data: URL GET /v1/feedback/:id/shot , /thumb the picture, served inert as media files are PATCH /v1/feedback/:id {status?, priority?, assignee?, resolution?, kind?, title?, if_status?} the item: write scope; if_status refuses with 409 when the status is another, so that two agents do not take one item (a server or coordinator triages one item at a time, so of two takers at once the second gets 409; two coordinators do not wait for each other) DELETE /v1/feedback/:id {deleted} : admin scope Agents read the same rows. With SQL, SELECT id, kind, priority, title, note, page, marks_text FROM cc_feedback WHERE status IN ('new', 'triaged', 'planned') ORDER BY priority, ts , where marks_text is the marks one a line ( 1. box \"too small\" on \"Shards\" (button.btn: Add shard) ) and MATCH finds words of the note, the title and the marks. Over MCP , list_feedback , get_feedback (which answers the screenshot as an image the model sees) and update_feedback . An agent takes an item with {\"status\": \"doing\", \"assignee\": \"<who>\", \"if_status\": \"new\"} and closes it with {\"status\": \"done\", \"resolution\": \"<the pull request>\"} . A key limited to tables reaches these routes with a grant on cc_feedback : read to read, write to send and triage. A mark names what it points at by its label (a button's words, a field's label, a cell's column), never by a field's value or a cell's content, and names nothing that meets a blacked-out area: what is there stays in the picture, where the sender can black it out."],
["server.html#the-index-inspector","chloedb-server","The index inspector","GET /v1/index?table=T (admin scope) answers what the command center's Index inspector shows, for one table. On a server it is that server's ( cluster , with shards empty); on a coordinator it asks every replica of every shard for its own, over the Profile call of gRPC with kind index (no change to the shard protocol: a server older than the coordinator answers that it has no such profile, and its replica shows that), and answers each replica's, each shard's and the whole cluster's: A shard's statistics are its primary's (the first copy that answered, without one), and its reads its copies' added up; the cluster's are its shards' added up, with copies_bytes the bytes of every copy. behind is how many writes a copy is behind its shard's primary. stats : documents live, deleted and in memory; bytes on disk and in memory, split into each field's index (by section), the doc store (compressed, and store_raw before compression), the primary key and other sections, and segment overhead; each segment's documents and bytes; and per field its kind, how many documents have a value ( present ), the size of its values ( size : a histogram of buckets [lo, hi, documents] , four to an octave, with mean , p50 , p90 , p99 ), the items of a list, tokens per document of a text field, its share of the doc store, its distinct terms ( terms : low and high bounds over the segments and an estimate ), and the range of a number or a timestamp. doc_bytes is the same histogram for whole stored documents. freshness : when the table was last written ( last_write_ms ) and how many writes it took since the server started, and when the database's file last changed. reads : of documents retrieved (rows returned in query answers) and of keys asked for ( GET /v1/kv , POST /v1/kv/get , Get over gRPC): the total, the distinct keys read, the share of the table read at least once ( coverage ), how often each was read ( per_key ), the concentration curve ( curve : the share of reads taken by the most read fraction of the keys read, with top1 and top10 ), the most read keys ( top , each with its count, the most it may be too high, err , and its share), and, for keys, those no row has ( missing ). GET /v1/index/tables lists every table at once: its documents, bytes and segments by shard ( bytes are one copy's, each shard's primary), on a coordinator its bytes on every copy ( copies_bytes , the sum of what each copy of each shard says it holds, with copies_measured and copies_missing , the copies that did not answer and are left out; also per shard), skew (the shard furthest over the mean, and by how much), writes and reads a minute, last_write_ms , the copies behind ( stale ), and its growth over a day and a week from the log store's hourly tables rows; totals adds the tables up ( tables , failed , docs , bytes , segments , and on a coordinator copies_bytes and incomplete , the tables a copy did not answer for). It samples no document and is kept 30 s; the command center's Tables and storage reads it. Writes and reads a minute are measured against a look the server keeps every minute, so they are there on a first look once the server is 30 s old: over the last 5 minutes at most ( rate_window_s ), and for each shard ( shards[i].writes_per_min , reads_per_min ). The drill-down ( GET /v1/index ) measures over the same window ( rates ). What is exact and what is estimated: documents, bytes, segments and index sizes are read from every segment; distinct terms too, but those of a large field are estimated with a HyperLogLog sketch, which also merges across shards. Value sizes, fill and document sizes come from sample documents (2,000 by default, at most 100,000) drawn at random from each server with seed , weighted to its whole table; the fill of a field that is not stored, and tokens per document, come from every document's length norm instead (exact below 24 tokens, rounded down by at most 12.5% above). terms=false skips the term sketches. Reads are counted in memory, since the server started or since POST /v1/index/reads/reset , and are approximate: each table keeps its 256 most read keys (Space-Saving: a count is at most its err too high), and a hash sample of the keys read that halves itself to stay under 4,096 entries gives the distinct keys, the coverage and the frequencies ( exact while it holds every key). The first 10,000 rows of an answer are counted by key and the rest in the totals ( skipped ). A coordinator counts what each replica served through it (by the shard and replica each row came from), and every server counts what it answers its own clients; a shard does not count the coordinator's questions, so nothing is counted twice. Profiling runs and the parts of a compound query are not counted. --no-read-stats turns counting off."],
["server.html#the-query-console","chloedb-server","The query console","GET /classic , and the command center's Query console , serve the query console, a single page with six tabs. Build writes SQL from a form: search text (ranked, or only matching rows, with typo tolerance and synonyms), \"similar to\" a stored row or a description, rank fusion or a weighted sum with a slider, conditions, a boost by closeness ( decay ), a facet, the columns and the row count. Conditions nest in groups of all of ( AND ), any of ( OR ) and none of ( NOT ), and a condition can be \"is among the field of the rows where …\", an IN (SELECT ...) subquery. Join related rows pairs each row with the rows it links to, the rows linking to it (for each EDGES field) or the rows sharing a value, with conditions and columns for the related rows; Summarize groups by any fields of either side and computes counts, distinct counts, sums, averages, minimums and maximums, with HAVING and an order. The SQL updates as you type; \"Edit as SQL\" takes it to the editor. Clicking a keyword field in the sidebar shows its most common values, and clicking a value adds a condition. Each result row has a \"similar\" link that searches for rows like it, and a \"links\" link that opens its neighbourhood in the Graph tab. A search that finds nothing offers to search again allowing typos, or with synonyms. Graph (for a table with an EDGES or VECTOR field) starts from keys, the top rows of a search or the rows matching a condition, follows links (out, in or both ways, optionally one label) or similarity (each row's k nearest), and finds the rows within some hops, the shortest path to other rows, or the rows ranked by personalized PageRank, with conditions on the rows found. The answer is a table and a picture: rings by hop distance, a path left to right, or PageRank as node size. Clicking a node shows its row, expands its links or starts a new walk from it. SQL is an editor with parameters, results and the plan the executor recorded, segment by segment, with example queries one click away. It only reads (it sends POST /v1/query , which takes only a SELECT ): a write or a schema change opens in the SQL workbench, which checks it first. Ask an agent takes a question in plain language. Schema and import uploads a JSON Lines, JSON, CSV or TSV file (or pasted rows) into the table, showing the upload's progress and the columns it skipped. It shows the table's schema in the short form, and designs new ones: fields by type with their options, vector fields embedding chosen text fields by a named model, a first draft guessed from a file's first rows (numbers, lists, vectors, short values as keywords and longer ones as text, a unique id -like column as the key), JSON that edits both ways, a check against the server, a download, and the commands that create and fill a database with it. Query language is a guide to the SQL with examples written against the table's own fields and words from its rows: boolean, phrase, prefix, typo and synonym searches, conditions, aggregates and HAVING , subqueries and WITH , joins, graph queries, hybrid ranking and boosts, and what is not supported. \"Try it\" runs an example in the SQL tab. It also edits the server's synonym list."],
["server.html#in-a-terminal","chloedb-server","In a terminal","chloedb-server cc [URL] is the command center in a terminal, for an SSH session or a machine without a browser. since 0.3.5 It reads the routes the command center reads ( /v1/cluster , /v1/health/fleet , /v1/alerts , /v1/queries/stats , /v1/queries , /v1/logs , /v1/index/tables , /v1/events ), every 2 seconds ( --every ), and sends its changes to the same routes with the same key, so a key may do there what it may do in the browser, and the audit log keeps each change with its reason and x-chloedb-app: chloedb-cc . Nothing new runs on the server. The key comes from CHLOEDB_KEY (or CHLOEDB_TOKEN ), else from a prompt that does not show it; there is no flag for it, so it is never in the shell's history or the list of processes. --ca FILE trusts a CA for an https URL. The screens need 80 columns by 24 rows, and NO_COLOR draws them without colour. Without a terminal it exits, naming chloedb-server admin for scripts. key screen what it shows 1 , g h Home the fleet's health: its headline, the copies that answer, p99 against the SLO, errors, replication lag, the fullest disk, the alerts firing, and each problem with what to do next 2 , g c Cluster a map of the shards, or of the nodes ( G ), each tile coloured by the lens L picks (health, p99, CPU, disk, errors, lag, version); beside it the shard picked, each copy's role, node, lag, p99 and CPU, and why the shard is in its state 3 , g a Alerts every rule, firing first; a acknowledges one, s silences it for the minutes typed with a reason, u lifts the silence 4 , g q Queries the queries running now ( x cancels one) and the statements of the last 15 minutes, by their share of the time, p99, calls or errors ( o ) 5 , g l Logs the newest lines, followed as they come; f sets the level and / the text they hold 6 , g o Tables each table's rows, size, segments, reads and writes a minute, skew and last write, and the one picked shard by shard 7 , g w SQL a statement run as the key, its rows as a grid, ↑ ↓ its history 8 , g t Timeline the events, newest first: changes, alerts, primaries and failures The g letters are the browser's. Space pauses the reading, r reads at once, : takes a command (a screen's name, lens p99 , ctx , writes , sql SELECT … , q ), and ? lists the keys. Changes ask first, in a dialog that says what the change does, and on production (an environment that names no test or sandbox, or none on a server that is not this machine's or is reached through a hub, as the browser decides) for a reason the audit log keeps; Enter does nothing until it is given. On Cluster , p makes the copy picked its shard's primary, by its copies' votes ( POST /v1/admin/shards/{i}/promote ), refused for what the browser refuses it: it is the primary, does not answer, is being copied or is behind, or the shard has one copy, or the key is not admin. While a plan runs on the coordinator, a band names its step and S stops it after that step; it goes on when the terminal closes. On SQL , as in the SQL workbench, production is read-only until W allows writes for 15 minutes with a reason, which goes with each write; each write asks first, and an UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE , a DROP or a TRUNCATE needs its table's name typed. Draft and its plans (copies and shards added, moved and removed), the schema, the query builder, keys and the audit log, the profiler, rollouts, the cloud views and the apps stay in the browser. Through a hub , the URL is the connection's, https://hub:7700/c/<name> , and the key is the hub's (an admin key of the hub): each call goes through the hub, which adds that cluster's key, so the terminal never holds it ( many clusters from one command center ). :ctx lists the hub's book and opens another of its clusters, and :ctx - the hub itself. A hub on another machine that listens on its loopback is reached through a tunnel: ssh -L 7700:127.0.0.1:7700 host , then chloedb-server cc http://127.0.0.1:7700/c/<name> ."],
["server.html#apps","chloedb-server","Apps","An app is a program of its own that the command center shows as a view: the crawler , the spreadsheets , network operations , ChloeCtx and the app engine are four. --app name=url (repeatable, or CHLOEDB_APPS=a=url,b=url ) names each, and the server reaches it there; browsers never do, so the app needs no address of theirs and no keys (the app engine's studio is the exception: it runs code a model wrote, so it keeps an address of its own and signs browsers in with a link its view hands out). On Kubernetes, spec.crawler , spec.sheets , spec.chloectx and spec.apps of a StrataCluster have the operator run them beside the cluster and set this on its coordinators, and give each app a key limited to its tables and a token of its own (below) ( the crawler on Kubernetes , the spreadsheets on Kubernetes , ChloeCtx on Kubernetes , the app engine on a cluster ): GET /v1/apps lists the apps (read scope), each with its manifest, the app's GET /app.json : title , description , icon_svg (the shapes of a 24-unit outline icon; only shapes and their geometry are drawn), version , and view , the path of its view module, as /v1/apps/<name>/<view> . An app that does not answer is listed with up: false and why. Manifests are read again after 15 seconds. /v1/apps/<name>/<path> is sent on to the app's <path> , with its query, body, content-type , accept , if-none-match and if-modified-since , and the app's token as Authorization: Bearer in place of the caller's key: its own, from the file of its name in --app-token-dir ( CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN_DIR , read as it is sent) or from CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN_<NAME> (its name in capitals, - as _ ), else the server's CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN . Give each app a token of its own, so that one app cannot call another as the server. The caller's key is checked first, read scope for GET and HEAD and admin scope for the rest; a key limited to tables only reaches an app whose app.json says \"limited_keys\": true (it holds such keys to their grants itself), with any grant that reads. The app is told who it was in headers the server sets (a caller's own of those names are not passed on): x-chloedb-principal (the key's name, token or open ), x-chloedb-key (its id), x-chloedb-scope , and x-chloedb-tables for a key limited to tables ( crawl_*=admin,*=read ). An app may trust them because they come with its token. The answer comes back with its content-type , content-disposition , etag , last-modified and x-app-* headers, and cache-control: no-cache : a browser asks again each time, and an app that tags its answers (its view, say) answers 304 with no body to one that holds it since 0.3.4 . A request's body may be as large as an import's ( --max-body-mb , 256 MiB), an answer is at most 32 MB, in at most 120 s, and an app that does not answer is a 502. On Kubernetes no app holds the cluster's admin token once its coordinators make keys limited to tables ( /v1/whoami 's auth.app_keys ): the operator keeps a Secret per app, <cluster>-<app>-credentials , with key , a key of the cluster limited to the app's tables (named <app>-app , made through the coordinators and made again when the tables change), and token , random, which the app's own API requires and the coordinators send it (their STRATA_APP_TOKEN_DIR , the Secrets mounted as a folder of files, so no restart is needed when one is made). spec.<app>.tables names the tables ( table=scope , as above); by default network operations get netops_*=admin , the spreadsheets get sheets_*=admin, *=read (the cluster's tables open in them as sheets, read only), ChloeCtx ctx_*=admin, chloectx_memory=read, chloectx_mem_*=read (each project chloectx sync copies has a table of its own, chloectx_mem_<project> ; its Seen before asks the on-call agent, which is the server's admin, and goes without), the app engine sa_*=admin, app_*=admin (from which it makes each of its apps a key of its own, apps-app/app-<id> , limited to app_<id>_* ), and the crawler *=admin (a crawl fills the table it names). The crawler that grows the cluster ( crawler.grow , the default) keeps the admin token as its key, since adding shards is the server's admin; its token is its own. Until an app's Secret is made, and with coordinators from before such keys, the app runs with the admin token, as it did before. An app taken out of the spec loses its key and its Secret. When an app's key is made again (its tables changed, or its Secret lost the key), the new key is made before the old one is revoked, and the key's id is on the app's pod template ( strata.chloetech.ai/key-id ), so its pod restarts with the new key rather than calling with a revoked one. When making the new key fails (a timeout, an error of the coordinators, coordinators rolling), the Secret keeps the key it has, with its id and tables, so the pod neither restarts nor gets the admin token, and the next reconcile tries again; an app with no key yet then gets no key at all (the Secret is marked strata.chloetech.ai/key-pending ) until one is made. Only coordinators from before keys limited to tables give an app the admin token. The operator writes these Secrets only where the ClusterRole strata-operator-secrets is bound: operator.yaml (and the cloud view's install) binds it in the namespace strata ; for clusters in another namespace, bind it there ( kubectl -n NS create rolebinding strata-operator-secrets --clusterrole=strata-operator-secrets --serviceaccount=strata-system:strata-operator ), or that cluster's apps keep the admin token. Its ClusterRole, bound everywhere, only reads Secrets (each cluster's tokenSecret ). Apps the server starts A server of its own, or a coordinator, starts the crawler ( chloedb-crawl serve ), Sheets ( chloedb-sheets serve ) and ChloeCtx ( chloectx app ) itself when their programs are installed in its own directory, as an install puts them (a build's target/ has none of them, so tests and cargo run start nothing). Each is a child process that listens on a loopback port of its own and is listed under its name, as if it had been given with --app : the server binds that port itself and holds it while it runs, and hands the listening socket to the app as file descriptor 3 ( CHLOEDB_LISTEN_FD=3 ) at each start, so no other process can take the port while the app starts or restarts; it takes requests only with a token the server makes for it at each start, which no one else holds; it calls the server back with a key of its own, scoped to the tables it keeps (the crawler crawl_* , Sheets sheets_* and reads of the rest, ChloeCtx ctx_* and reads of chloectx_memory ), made at each start, held in memory only and never in the keys file; it is never given the server's token, cluster token or keys: its environment is the server's with every credential taken out (the PATH , the home, the locale, proxies, CA files, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and the crawler's CHLOEDB_CRAWL_CONTACT and CHLOEDB_CRAWL_ALLOW_PRIVATE stay); it runs in a process group of its own and, on Linux, ends when the server ends; when it exits it is started again after a second, then up to a minute between tries; when the server stops it gets SIGTERM, and SIGKILL 10 s later; its output goes to the server's log, each line under app=<name> , and GET /v1/apps lists its process : program , pid , running , restarts , last_exit and its last 40 lines, which the command center shows when the app does not answer. The crawler a server starts does not grow the cluster ( --grow off ), and names the tables it fills crawl_<host> . A replica, a shard (a server with a cluster token and no --shards or --replicas ), a shard with its coordinator beside it, a pod the Kubernetes operator runs (which runs the apps as Deployments of their own) and a server over TLS start none; --start-apps off turns it off anywhere, and an --app of the same name takes the app's place. The servers chloedb-crawl --local and chloedb-sheets serve --local start are given CHLOEDB_START_APPS=off , since those programs are their apps. The navigation lists the apps under Apps , each at #/app/<name> . Its view module is JavaScript the command center loads through the server (with the signed-in key) and runs with its own helpers: the module's default export's render(el, ctx) draws into el and may return a function that stops it, and ctx holds core , charts and data (the command center's modules), app (its listing), api(path, opts) (the app's API through the server), path() , href(...) and onPath(f) for the view's own paths under #/app/<name>/ . An app's view is trusted as the server's own pages are: an admin names the app on the server's command line."],
["server.html#the-agent","chloedb-server","The agent","POST /v1/agent , and the query console's Ask an agent tab, give a Claude model one tool, run_sql , which runs a read-only SELECT on the table. The model sees the schema and a description of ChloeDB's SQL (it runs facet queries to learn a field's values), then searches, filters, ranks, counts and facets until it can answer, for at most max_steps model calls (8 by default, 20 at most). The answer lists every query it ran, with its row count and time, so you can check its work or open a query in the editor. Writes are refused. The model is a setting: --model sets the server's default ( claude-haiku-5-5 ), and a request or the UI can name any other model id and an effort level. The API key comes from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY on the server, or from the request ( api_key , never stored by the server; the UI keeps it in the browser only if you tick \"remember\"). On Kubernetes, a StrataCluster 's secretEnv gives it to every server and coordinator from a Secret ( model keys )."],
["server.html#relevance-judgments","chloedb-server","Relevance judgments","POST /v1/judge grades rows of a table against a query, 0 to 3, the scale of TREC's judgments and of the relevance lab's grades, with UMBRELA's prompt (Upadhyay et al., 2024: the open reproduction of the prompt with which Thomas et al., SIGIR 2024, found an LLM agreeing with Bing's searchers as well as its human labellers did, and the one TREC 2024's RAG track judged with). Each row is its own request, so a grade does not depend on the other rows or their order; the passage is the row's stored TEXT fields (or the fields named), each on a line after its name and cut at 4,000 characters. Up to 200 rows a request, eight at a time; an answer without a grade is asked once more for one, and a row still without one, or not in the table, gets grade: null and an error . The model and key are the agent's. On 25 TREC-COVID queries, pooling the top 10 of BM25 and of dense retrieval (464 rows), claude-haiku-5-5 's grades agreed with TREC's on \"relevant\" for 84% of the rows TREC judged (Cohen's kappa 0.57), were the more lenient (27% of the rows TREC judged not relevant got 2 or 3), and named the same winner of the two rankings on 21 of the 24 queries TREC's judgments decide; they reproduce the size of a gap less well (Kendall's tau 0.32 between the per-query differences). They are a good stand-in for telling which ranking is better on queries nobody has judged, and a weaker one for absolute nDCG. The study ( chloedb-server/results/judge/ ) sent the prompts through Claude Code, where the model thinks before most answers; the endpoint asks without thinking."],
["server.html#the-on-call-agent","chloedb-server","The on-call agent","Experimental. The on-call agent ( /v1/admin/oncall/* ) has no compatibility promise yet: its routes, settings and what it stores may change in any release, with no upgrade path promised. Its answers carry x-chloedb-experimental , and GET /v1/catalog marks its routes ( compatibility ). POST /v1/admin/oncall (admin) has a Claude model look into a firing alert ( {\"alert\": \"<rule>\"} ) or a question about the cluster ( {\"question\": \"why did p99 double at 14:05?\"} ) the way an operator would, and report what it found and what it would do. It has the read-only views of this page as tools: health ( fleet for every coordinator's), the cluster's shards and copies, each copy's load, the host, the alerts and one rule's events, the events timeline, the running, slow and recent queries and the statistics by statement, this server's log lines, the metrics series and the contain rules. Each tool call is a request to the server's own route with the caller's key, through the same checks and audit as any other, named oncall in the query log ( x-chloedb-app ), and the model sees at most 12,000 characters of each answer. It changes nothing. What it would do is a proposal: a contain rule (limit or refuse a statement or a key for a while), removing one, cancelling a running query, or silencing an alert. Each is kept for an admin to approve or reject, and can be approved for an hour; after that it is expired , so an action seen fit then is not applied to a cluster that has moved on. Approving one makes its request with the approver's key and the proposal's reason ( x-chloedb-reason: on-call proposal p3: <why> ), so the audit log and the contain rule name the admin who approved it. The report comes as a summary, the evidence (what each tool showed), the likely cause and how sure the model is, the proposals, and what to watch. note: true also writes it as a note on the events timeline. A run takes at most max_steps model calls (12 by default, 30 at most); the model, effort and key are the agent's. Approval is a person's, with an admin key: an approval by an agent is refused ( agents and administration ). A run goes on in the server whether or not its caller waits: \"wait\": false answers {\"run\": \"oc...\", \"state\": \"running\"} at once, and GET /v1/admin/oncall/runs/<id> shows it as it goes, with each tool call (what it asked, how long it took, and the first 4,000 characters of what the route answered), what the model wrote between them, and then its report and proposals. POST /v1/admin/oncall/runs/<id>/stop stops it before its next model call. {\"question\": \"...\", \"follows\": \"<id>\"} asks a follow-up in the same conversation, so the model remembers what it looked at. GET /v1/admin/oncall says whether the server has a Claude key and lists the runs kept, newest first. The server keeps the latest 50 runs and 200 proposals beside its audit log ( <audit>.oncall.json , or the file CHLOEDB_ONCALL_STATE names), so a restart loses neither; a run under way when it stopped is marked failed."],
["server.html#in-the-command-center","chloedb-server","In the command center","On-call agent (under Operate, g n , an admin's) is the agent's page. Pick a firing alert and click Investigate , or ask a question (a few common ones are one click away); the options set the model, the effort, the model calls at most and whether the report goes on the timeline, and take a Claude key for the tab when the server has none (sent with each run, never kept). Each step shows as the agent takes it, with what it read in words, and a click opens what the route answered and a link to the view that shows the same. The report comes in its sections, each proposal as a card with what it does, why, the exact request it makes, and how long it can still be approved: Approve shows all that again before it applies it with your key, Reject takes a reason. Ask a follow-up under the report. The side lists the proposals waiting for an admin (the navigation counts them) and the investigations kept, with their follow-ups; Copy gives a conversation as Markdown, and the download as JSON. An alert's page has an Investigate button that starts a run on it."],
["server.html#agents-over-mcp","chloedb-server","Agents over MCP","An agent such as Claude Code, Claude Desktop or any client of the Model Context Protocol drives ChloeDB through tools: it lists the tables, reads their CREATE TABLE statements, checks a statement before it runs it, queries, writes, and looks up the syntax it needs in this documentation, which the server carries. POST /v1/mcp speaks the protocol's Streamable HTTP transport (versions 2025-06-18, 2025-03-26 and 2024-11-05), answering each message with JSON; chloedb-server mcp speaks it over stdin and stdout, for a client that starts its servers as commands, and sends each message to a server's /v1/mcp . tool does route list_tables every table's CREATE TABLE , primary and shard keys, rank profiles, models GET /v1/tables describe_table one table's schema in full GET /v1/schema query one SELECT : its columns and at most max_rows rows (100 by default, 2,000 at most), saying when it cut them POST /v1/query explain how a SELECT runs, and with hits the top rows' scores and features POST /v1/explain check_sql binds a statement against the tables without running it: what it would do, or why it is refused none: bound in the server execute INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE , CREATE , ALTER , DROP , rank profiles POST /v1/sql write rows as JSON operations, in one transaction POST /v1/write import many rows at once into a table: CSV, TSV or JSON lines as text ( data ), or objects ( rows ); create: true makes the table from the rows first (admin key); answers how many it wrote and the columns it skipped POST /v1/import get_rows rows by primary key POST /v1/kv/get json_paths the keys a JSON field holds, with counts, types and common values GET /v1/tables/<t>/paths list_models the embedding models a VECTOR field may name GET /v1/models judge grades rows 0 to 3 for a query, by a Claude model ( relevance judgments ) POST /v1/judge search_docs sections of the query language and Build an app that match some words none: the documents are compiled in list_feedback what people asked of the command center, with each item's marks as text ( feedback ) GET /v1/feedback get_feedback one feedback item in full, and its screenshot as an image GET /v1/feedback/<id>?shot=1 update_feedback an item's status, priority, assignee or resolution; if_status takes it only if no one did first PATCH /v1/feedback/<id> whoami the key's name and scope GET /v1/whoami A tool's call is a request to the route in its last column, made inside the server with the caller's Authorization header, so it passes every check that route makes: the key's scope decides what an agent may change (a read key's execute of an INSERT answers the 403 that /v1/sql does, as the tool's error, which the model reads), contain rules apply, the audit log names the key, and the latest queries name the client's app as mcp (or mcp-stdio ). Give an agent a read key to explore, a write key to load rows, and an admin key only to make tables. A failed call answers its route's status and error ( 403: ... ), so a model can fix its statement and try again; an argument a tool does not take is the protocol's error (-32602). An answer longer than 200,000 characters is cut, saying so. The server also offers the query language reference and the Build an app guide as resources ( strata://docs/query-language , strata://docs/build-an-app ) and, at initialize , a few lines of instructions on ChloeDB's SQL. It keeps no session (any coordinator answers any message), sends no requests of its own, and has no stream to open ( GET /v1/mcp answers 405). A request from a browser page of another origin is refused, as on every route."],
["server.html#agents-and-administration","chloedb-server","Agents and administration","An agent administers ChloeDB the way a person does, through the same routes, with three things a person does not need: a key that says what it may do, a way to ask before it changes what it may not change alone, and answers it can act on without reading their sentences. What a route risks: its tier. Every route has one, which GET /v1/catalog lists: tier what who may, alone 0 reads: views, plans, dry runs a key of the route's scope; an ops or propose key the server's views too 1 bounded, and undone by itself: a contain rule (it expires), a query cancelled, an alert acknowledged or silenced (for a while), a note, a log filter, a profile, a scrub or verify an ops or admin key, with a reason 2 changes the cluster or its data, and can be undone: a plan applied, a spec, an upgrade, rank profiles, synonyms, alert rules, keys, rows an admin key (an agent's says why, in x-chloedb-reason ); an ops or propose key files a change request 3 loses data or costs money: a shard removed, a promotion, a follow stopped, writes held, a key revoked, a tenant deleted, a cloud cluster made or deleted, vacuum, a DROP TABLE , a DELETE or UPDATE of every row as tier 2, and an agent never does it alone, even with an admin key: the server refuses it change_request_required Keys for agents. POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"oncall-bot\", \"scope\": \"ops\", \"expires_minutes\": 60, \"on_behalf_of\": \"dana\"} makes a key for an agent. Two scopes are for agents: propose reads what a read key reads and the server's views (health, the cluster, load, alerts, events, queries, logs, the audit log, plans and dry runs), and files change requests; ops does that, and the tier 1 actions alone. Neither writes a row nor calls a tier 2 or 3 route, nor the routes that copy every row out or carry other clusters' credentials ( /v1/export , the hub, a tenant's SQL, a cloud's credentials). \"agent\": true marks a key of another scope as an agent's (a role key is one already), expires_minutes (5 at least) makes it last as long as one task, and on_behalf_of names the key the person it acts for, which the audit log keeps. A key an agent makes (with its admin key, or a person's key sent with an agent's headers) is an agent's too, for the person its maker acts for, expiring no later than its maker and within its maker's statement budget; an agent asking for \"agent\": false or for another person is refused ( change_request_required ), and it rotates agents' keys alone, since the new secret of a person's key would be a key outside its fence. A role key is kept as a read key with its role beside it, so a server from before roles admits it as a read key. GET /v1/whoami answers may : the tier a key may act at alone ( act_alone_up_to_tier ), whether it may file and approve change requests, and whether it must give a reason. Who is an agent. A request is an agent's when its key is an agent's ( agent , or a role), when it comes through MCP ( /v1/mcp , chloedb-server mcp ) or the on-call agent, or when it says so in x-chloedb-agent-model , x-chloedb-agent-session or x-chloedb-agent-run (each at most 120 characters, kept in the audit log's agent ). An agent with a person's admin key and none of these looks like a person: give agents agent keys. Reasons and caps. An agent's tier 1 request and up says why in x-chloedb-reason (or is refused reason_required ), and an agent's contain rule lasts an hour at most, its silence two hours ( limit_exceeded ). The audit log names an agent's entries actor: \"agent\" , with its model, session, run and app ( agent ), and the person it acts for ( on_behalf_of ). Error codes. Every error answer carries, beside its sentence ( error ), a stable error_code , whether the same request may succeed later ( retryable ), a hint and the section of this page that explains it ( docs ): The codes are unauthenticated , scope_refused , table_refused , reason_required , limit_exceeded , change_request_required , approval_refused , change_not_pending , plan_stale , table_not_found , column_not_found , query_invalid , schema_invalid , idempotency_conflict , contained , overloaded , deadline_exceeded , cancelled , unavailable , write_in_doubt (a write through a coordinator that may or may not be applied: send it again with the answer's idempotency_key ; Failover ), and a general one for each status ( not_found , conflict , too_large , unsupported_media_type , unprocessable , rate_limited , not_implemented , bad_request , internal , upstream ). The field is error_code , not code , which the Python and JavaScript clients read as their own class of error. A code is never renamed or reused, only added; GET /v1/catalog lists them with their hints. Errors answered before a route's handler runs have the same shape: a body that is not JSON (400), a missing or wrong content type (415), a body over its limit (413, naming the limits), a path no route matches (404), a method the route does not take (405), a connection past --max-connections (503 overloaded ), and a write that failed partway through a coordinator, which also says partial and each shard's part ( shards ). Over gRPC the code travels in the metadata chloedb-error-code . Dry runs. POST /v1/admin/changes/preview {\"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/v1/admin/plan/apply\", \"body\": {...}} answers, for a change of any route, without changing anything: its tier , what it would do, whether it is reversible and its undo , its blast (what clients feel meanwhile), what it affects (for SQL, the rows a DELETE or UPDATE matches and a DROP loses, counted by the statement's own WHERE ), the route's own plan where it has one (a plan of the cluster's shape, a Kubernetes spec, an upgrade, a statement bound against the tables), a plan_id and a fingerprint . Change requests. POST /v1/admin/changes {\"method\", \"path\", \"body\"?, \"reason\", \"what\"?} files a change for a person to approve: its dry run is kept with it, and it waits pending for an hour ( GET /v1/admin/changes?state= , GET /v1/admin/changes/<id> ). A person with an admin key approves it ( POST /v1/admin/changes/<id>/approve ) or rejects it ( .../reject {\"reason\"} ), and whoever filed it may withdraw it ( .../withdraw ). An approval by an agent is refused ( approval_refused ), whatever its key: an agent never approves its own change or another agent's. Approving dry runs the change again, and when its plan differs from the one it was filed with (other steps, another table, twice the rows or half of them; a few rows more or less, up to 10, do not count), it is not applied but fails plan_stale , and the agent files it again. Applied, it is the approver's request, with the request's reason, audited as theirs and naming the change request ( change_request ). Every role key reads the change list, and the desk it is kept in ( <audit>.oncall.json , readable by the server's user alone), so a change request carries no credential: the routes that make or show a key's secret ( /v1/admin/keys , a key's rotation, /v1/keys/own , a connector's config, test and key), take another cluster's (the hub's connections) or a cloud's (its credentials and their checks), and those no role key calls, are refused as targets ( scope_refused ), and so is a body with a field named as a credential holds (a secret, token, password, Authorization , an API or private key). What it was answered is kept with any such field (hidden) . Change requests live beside the on-call agent's proposals, in the same list ( GET /v1/admin/oncall/proposals ), and the command center's On-call agent page lists both under Waiting for an admin , with each change's tier, what it touches, its undo and who filed it. An agent's SQL. An agent's DROP TABLE , DROP of a rank profile or function, ALTER TABLE ... DROP , and DELETE or UPDATE without a WHERE are refused change_request_required , even with an admin key: it dry runs the statement and files it. The catalog. GET /v1/catalog?q= lists every route of the server (read from the router itself, so it lists exactly the routes there are) with its methods, scope, tier, whether a role key may call it, its dry run and its MCP tool, and every error code. Jobs, and waiting. GET /v1/admin/jobs?kind= lists what runs in the background in one shape ( {kind, id, state, running, started_ms, done, of, eta_s, url} ): a plan being applied, slot moves, on-call runs, change requests waiting. GET /v1/events?after=<id>&wait_s=30 answers as soon as an event after that one comes, 30 seconds at most, so an agent waits on a job instead of polling. Runbooks. The runbooks say what to do for each alert and fleet problem; the server compiles them in, GET /v1/runbooks?for=<alert or problem id> (or q=<words> ) finds one, and each fleet problem's next names its runbook and the read it makes ( api ). Over MCP. A role key or an admin key sees the operations tools beside the data tools: the reads ( health , cluster , load , system , alerts , events , queries , logs , timeseries , contain_rules , audit , catalog , jobs , runbook ), the plans ( plan_reshape , plan_kube , plan_upgrade ), change requests ( dry_run , propose_change , list_changes , get_change ) and the tier 1 actions, each with a reason ( contain , remove_contain , cancel_query , silence_alert , ack_alert , note ). A tool's error says its code and hint after the route's sentence ( 403: ... [code scope_refused: ...] ). chloedb-server mcp names its session in x-chloedb-agent-session , and the model from CHLOEDB_AGENT_MODEL . From a shell. chloedb-server admin makes one request and prints its JSON ( --json on one line), for a script or an agent without MCP, with --url , --key (or CHLOEDB_URL , CHLOEDB_KEY ) and --reason ( CHLOEDB_REASON ): It exits 0 when answered 2xx, 1 when refused or failed otherwise, 2 when refused for who the key is ( unauthenticated , scope_refused , reason_required , change_request_required , approval_refused , ...), 3 when stale ( plan_stale , change_not_pending : look again), 4 to retry later (a retryable code), and 5 when the server was not reached."],
["operate-cluster.html","Operate a cluster","","This page runs ChloeDB as a production-shaped cluster on machines of your own, without Kubernetes: two shards, each held by three copies that elect their primary among themselves, a coordinator in front of them, a token that the servers send each other and API keys for clients. It then checks the cluster's health, loads and searches rows, kills a primary and watches the failover, writes checkpoints, and upgrades the cluster one server at a time while it serves. Everything starts on one machine, on loopback, so that each command runs as written. On virtual machines each copy is a machine of its own (or a systemd unit), and the addresses are theirs. On Kubernetes the operator does all of this for you ( on Kubernetes ). You need chloedb-server on your PATH ( install ), and curl and python3 ."],
["operate-cluster.html#the-layout","Operate a cluster","The layout","server what it holds HTTP gRPC s0a shard 0: its first primary 7711 7721 s0b , s0c shard 0: replicas 7712, 7713 7722, 7723 s1a shard 1: its first primary 7714 7724 s1b , s1c shard 1: replicas 7715, 7716 7725, 7726 coord no data: it routes each write to the shard that holds its row, and asks every shard for a search 7700 off The copies of a shard talk to each other, and to the coordinator, over gRPC. Clients talk to the coordinator over HTTP. A shard elects a primary only while a majority of its copies answer, so three copies keep a shard working through the loss of any one of them; with two, every restart of a copy stops the shard's writes for a few seconds ( failover )."],
["operate-cluster.html#tokens","Operate a cluster","Tokens","The cluster token is what the servers send each other: a primary to its replicas, the coordinator to the copies. Every server of the cluster gets the same one, and only it may make the calls between servers (shipping writes, changing the cluster map, sharing keys); clients never hold it. Every server reads it from CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN . The admin token is the coordinator's --token , for you, the operator. Clients get API keys, made below. On virtual machines, keep both in each unit's environment file ( EnvironmentFile= , mode 0600) rather than on the command line, where ps shows them."],
["operate-cluster.html#start-the-copies","Operate a cluster","Start the copies","Each copy is the same command with its own name, ports and, on its first start only, its part in replication: --seq-no numbers the database's writes, which is how a primary ships them to its replicas. It cannot be undone. --failover-after-ms 2000 : a replica that hears nothing from its primary for two seconds asks its shard's copies to elect it, and a primary that cannot reach a majority of its copies stops serving reads. Without it (the default, 0) a shard whose primary dies takes no writes until you promote a replica by hand. --replicas makes a copy a primary that ships to the copies it names. --replica --replicas makes a replica, which takes only what its primary ships and refuses clients' writes, and names its shard's other copies, which it ships to once it is elected. The role flags are for a copy's first start. A copy keeps its part (primary or replica, its term, the primary it follows) beside its database, in db.chloedb.replication , and a copy started again without --replicas or --replica takes it up again: that is how every restart on this page starts one, and a systemd unit's command leaves them out once the copy has started once. A replica started again with --replicas refuses to start, and says to use --replica . --node-name names the copy in the coordinator's views and the log stores' rows; --backup-dir is where its checkpoints go. --start-apps off keeps each server from starting the apps installed beside chloedb-server (the crawler, Sheets, ChloeCtx), which a cluster runs beside its coordinator, if at all ( apps on a cluster ). On machines of their own, each copy listens on its machine's address ( --listen 10.0.0.11:7700 --grpc 10.0.0.11:7701 ) and names the others by theirs. A server with a cluster token, a --token or an API key may listen beyond loopback; one with none of them refuses to. Between machines, serve every server over TLS ( --tls-cert FILE --tls-key FILE ), list the copies as https:// , and give the servers that call them --shard-ca FILE when your certificates are not signed by a public authority ( security )."],
["operate-cluster.html#start-the-coordinator","Operate a cluster","Start the coordinator","--shards lists the copies' gRPC addresses: a comma between shards, and | between the copies of one shard. Which copy is the primary is not in the list: the coordinator asks each copy its part, and sends a shard's writes to the copy that answers as its primary. The first time, the coordinator gives every server the cluster map (each shard's copies, and its slots: 2,048 of the 4,096 each here), and every server keeps it beside its database. From then on the coordinator routes by the newest map its servers hold, so a coordinator started again on this same list, or on one copy of each shard, serves the cluster as it is now, after any changes ( changing the cluster while it serves ). The coordinator holds no data and needs no --db : run two or more of them behind a load balancer, each with the same flags and --peer-url naming the address the others reach it at ( http://10.0.0.21:7700 ), so that they find each other through the shards ( several coordinators )."],
["operate-cluster.html#check-its-health","Operate a cluster","Check its health","GET /v1/cluster is the coordinator's view of every shard and copy. This script prints the part an operator reads first, and is used for the rest of the page: The cluster is ready once every copy answers and every replica has caught up with its primary: A shard's state is ok , degraded (a copy down, the shard still serving), no_writes , no_reads or down , and why says it in a sentence. GET /v1/health/fleet sums up the whole cluster in one headline, with each problem's impact, what to do next and the runbook for it; it is what the command center's Home page shows: For a load balancer or a process supervisor, every server answers GET /livez (the process runs) and GET /readyz (it serves) without a credential. The command center is the coordinator's address in a browser, http://127.0.0.1:7700 , which asks for the admin token or a key; chloedb-server cc http://127.0.0.1:7700 is the same in a terminal, with the key in CHLOEDB_KEY or typed at its prompt."],
["operate-cluster.html#keys-for-clients","Operate a cluster","Keys for clients","Give each client a key of its own, with the least scope it needs: here a write key for an application and a read key for Prometheus. Keys are made on the coordinator, and reach every copy of every shard within a few seconds: Each copy keeps the keys, as SHA-256 digests and never the secrets, in db.chloedb.keys.json beside its database, so a coordinator started again takes them from its shards. The read key scrapes every server's /metrics , the copies' included. On a shard's primary, its replicas' state is there, by replica: Scrape the coordinator and each copy as targets of their own, with the key in Prometheus's authorization.credentials_file . Two things are not in /metrics yet: the coordinator's view of the cluster (a shard without a primary or without a majority, a copy down), which is in /v1/cluster and the coordinator's built-in alerts, and a label naming the shard on a copy's series, which you add in the scrape config. The coordinator's alerts go to a webhook, PagerDuty or Opsgenie ( alerts ); --alert-webhook URL sends them all to one URL."],
["operate-cluster.html#load-and-search","Operate a cluster","Load and search","Making a table takes the admin token, and runs on every shard: The application writes with its key. Each row goes to the shard whose slots hold its key, and the answer counts what each shard took. Give every write an idempotency key: when an answer is lost, or a shard's primary changes under it, the same request sent again with the same key is applied once ( when a write fails ). A search asks every shard, with the whole collection's BM25 statistics, so it ranks as one database would: What a cluster does differently lists the few statements a coordinator refuses or runs another way."],
["operate-cluster.html#kill-a-primary","Operate a cluster","Kill a primary","s0a is shard 0's primary. Kill it without warning, as a crashed machine would, and send a write for shard 0 straight away: Until shard 0 has a new primary, about two seconds here, its writes are refused with 503 and an answer that says what happened and that the write is safe to send again: The loop above sends it again, with the same idempotency key, until a new primary takes it. Shard 1's writes, and the searches of both shards, went on all along: a replica serves reads while its primary's last call found it caught up. Every write answered before the kill is on a majority of shard 0's copies ( --write-acks all , the default, waits for every replica in sync and never fewer than a majority), and the elected copy holds all of them. The coordinator now sees s0a down and another copy as shard 0's primary: Bring s0a back with the command that started it, without its role flags. A copy that was its shard's primary starts again as a replica (\"was its shard's primary: starts as a replica, until its shard's copies elect one\", its log says), finds the primary its shard elected meanwhile, and follows it: it is sent what it missed, and serves reads once it has caught up. Started again with --replicas , as at its first start, it would start as the primary of its old term and log errors until the newer primary fenced it; so give the role flags on a copy's first start alone. A shard of three copies with two of them down has no majority: it takes no writes and elects no primary until a second copy answers. Its reads go on only from a replica that still serves them. When a copy will not come back, replace it: POST /v1/admin/plan plans the repair, and the runbooks say what to read and do for each alert."],
["operate-cluster.html#checkpoints","Operate a cluster","Checkpoints","Copying a database's files while its server runs is not a backup: a copy can pair one version's manifest with another version's pages. A checkpoint is: the copy writes a consistent copy of its database into its --backup-dir while it goes on serving. Through the coordinator, ask one copy of each shard (any copy will do; a replica keeps the work off the primary): Beside each checkpoint the copy writes its shard's cluster map ( NAME.slots ) and the cluster's API keys ( NAME.keys.json : names, scopes and the hashes of their secrets), under the names a server serving the checkpoint reads. POST /v1/admin/checkpoint {\"name\": \"...\"} on the coordinator writes one on every shard's primary at once, all of one cluster map. A name is letters, digits, . , _ and - , and an existing one is never overwritten. The command center writes one from a copy's menu on its Cluster page, and one of every shard from Settings > Upkeep . Copy the three files of each checkpoint off the machines: with \"layout\": \"dir\" a checkpoint is a directory of segment files, which a sync to object storage sends only the new ones of ( backups ). To restore, stop the server and put the checkpoint in place of its database with chloedb-server restore --from s0b/backups/s0b-before-upgrade --db s0b/db.chloedb : it checks the checkpoint before it changes anything, moves the database's own files aside, puts the map beside it and merges the keys in. A copy's part in replication ( db.chloedb.replication ) is not in a checkpoint: start a restored copy with the role flags it had. The runbook Restore from checkpoints restores a whole cluster, a shard or one copy this way, and restoring a checkpoint says what the command does. There is no point-in-time recovery between checkpoints."],
["operate-cluster.html#upgrade-one-server-at-a-time","Operate a cluster","Upgrade one server at a time","A rolling upgrade keeps the cluster answering searches and taking writes, one server at a time: In each shard, the replicas first, one at a time: stop it, start it again on the new binary, and wait until it has caught up and serves reads. A replica must not be older than its primary, which may write what an older version cannot read. Then hand the shard's primary over to an upgraded replica ( POST /v1/admin/shards/<i>/promote ), and upgrade the old primary, now a replica, as the others. The coordinators last, one at a time. While versions are mixed, the cluster serves what the oldest server involved speaks, and versions and compatibility says, release by release, what has an order of its own. Read the changelog of every release you skip. On each machine, the new binary is the release's archive, checked against its .sha256 : Here the new binary is the one on your PATH already, so the upgrade is a rolling restart in the same order, which is how you rehearse one. restart stops a copy with SIGTERM (it ends the requests under way first), starts it again without role flags, waits until the copy's own /v1/health says it serves reads (a replica does once it holds its primary's last transaction), and then until the coordinator sees the whole cluster well. Wait for both before the next copy: two copies of a shard down at once leave it without a majority. handover makes a copy its shard's primary: --versions lists which servers run which version: halfway through a real upgrade it shows two. The coordinator goes last. With one coordinator, its clients are refused for the second it takes to start again; with several behind a load balancer, restart them one at a time, each with --drain-ms 5000 , so that /readyz answers 503 for five seconds after SIGTERM and the load balancer sends it nothing more before it stops: Every row is there. Under writes, a replica's restart costs the clients nothing: its shard's writes wait for the copies in sync, and reads go to the other copies. A handover holds the shard's writes for a moment, a second at most, while the new primary catches up: the coordinator sends a write that meets it on to the new primary, and a write that was in flight as the primary changed may be answered 409, in doubt, which the same request with the same idempotency key settles."],
["operate-cluster.html#next","Operate a cluster","Next","Runbooks : what to read and do for each alert and each problem the fleet view names. chloedb-server : every flag; replication , failover and slots in depth; adding shards and copies while the cluster serves. Security model : what the tokens and keys protect, and what to set before a cluster holds data that matters. Versions and compatibility : what mixes in a cluster during an upgrade."],
["cluster-limits.html","What a cluster does differently","","A coordinator over shards ( sharding ) answers most requests as one server does: the tests run every query kind against three shards and one database and compare the answers. This page lists what it refuses, and what it does in another way, so that nobody finds out in production. A table placed by a column ( OPTIONS(shard_by = '...') , tables on shards ) has a few differences of its own, listed after. POST /v1/sql/check with {\"sql\": ..., \"params\": ...} says whether a statement will run on the deployment it is sent to, without running it: {\"runs\": true, \"deployment\": \"cluster\", \"kind\": \"write\", \"notes\": [...]} , or runs: false with the error it would get. A pipeline's CI can send its statements to the cluster it deploys to, with a key of every table, a read key included (a key limited to tables is refused)."],
["cluster-limits.html#refused-on-a-cluster","What a cluster does differently","Refused on a cluster","what on one server on a cluster instead Graph queries: REACHABLE , PATH , hops() , ppr() run refused: a row's edges point at rows other shards hold run them on a server that holds the whole table, or walk from the application a round of reads by key per hop ( graphs ) ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN runs refused: the coordinator's plans name columns by place add a column and stop reading the old one INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE whose rows go to several shards runs refused (400) one statement per row or per shard, or INSERT OR REPLACE The relevance autopilot ( POST /v1/relevance/autopilot ) runs refused: it replays queries on one snapshot run it on a server with a copy of the tables /v1/export of the database as SQLite ( format=sqlite ) the admin's refused: a coordinator holds no files format=parquet , jsonl , csv or arrow export one table's rows from a cluster EXPLAIN through /v1/sql refused on both POST /v1/explain Recommenders: CREATE RECOMMENDER , recommend(...) ( query language ) run refused: a recommender counts each user's events in order, and they are spread over the shards run it on a server that holds the events table; or keep counts in an edge table or next-items table and rank with ranked(...) of what you read"],
["cluster-limits.html#different-on-a-cluster","What a cluster does differently","Different on a cluster","FULL JOIN runs on the coordinator over every row of both sides, which it reads whole from the shards (a JOIN or LEFT JOIN reads the second side's rows by the first side's join values only); it answers as one database does. A write across shards is one transaction a shard, not one over all. The coordinator checks each shard's part first and writes nothing when one refuses; what can still fail partway, and how to finish it with the idempotency key, is in when a write fails . UPDATE ... LIMIT n and DELETE ... [ORDER BY ...] LIMIT n are two steps: the coordinator reads the keys of the rows the statement's WHERE , ORDER BY and LIMIT pick (over every shard), then runs the statement without its LIMIT over those keys alone ( WHERE (...) AND key IN (...) ). A row changed in between so that it no longer matches is left alone, so fewer than n rows may change. The table needs a primary key, and the LIMIT is at most 10,000. An UPDATE or DELETE whose WHERE has a subquery ( WHERE id IN (SELECT ...) , NOT EXISTS (...) ), which reads rows of other shards, is two steps the same way: the coordinator reads the keys of the rows its WHERE picks over every shard, then runs it over those keys alone ( WHERE key IN (...) , the subquery read once, in the first step). Without a LIMIT it writes at most 10,000 rows, and one that picks more is refused, with nothing written: narrow its WHERE , or run it in parts. One whose WHERE reads only its own row (a TEXT column compared with = , LIKE ) runs on each shard, as any write does. INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING goes as an insert_if_absent operation (protocol 21: a shard of an older version refuses it as an unknown operation). ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE runs as written on the one shard its rows go to. DDL ( CREATE TABLE , ALTER TABLE , DROP TABLE , rank profiles, functions) runs on every shard, one change a shard: a shard that fails leaves the others changed, and the statement is sent again with IF NOT EXISTS , IF EXISTS or OR REPLACE . ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN c ... NOT NULL without a default is refused by each shard that has rows, and the error counts every such shard's rows. A SQLite or Parquet import that creates its table creates it on every shard by CREATE TABLE through the coordinator, as its plan ( plan=true ) says. Exports : GET /v1/export?format=jsonl|csv|arrow|parquet&tables=T reads the table by a scroll over every shard, with read on the table. Each page is read from the shards as they are then, not one snapshot of all of them: a write during the export may or may not be in it. Arrow is one batch built in memory (at most 1,000,000 rows); Parquet is written a page at a time and keeps the table's schema. Rank-log export and interleaving credit ( /v1/rank_log/export , /v1/rank_log/interleaving ) run their queries over every shard, each read whole by a scroll, rather than from one snapshot: a request logged during the export may be left out or come with part of its events. Conditional writes ( if_seq_no , seq_no() ) need every shard to number its writes alike (one replica, or a primary its replicas follow), and answers carry each shard's number ( seq_nos ), not one. Write receipts count per shard ( receipt.per_shard ); a shard from before protocol 21 does not count, and its counts are null . A strict write goes only to shards of protocol 21."],
["cluster-limits.html#a-table-placed-by-a-column-shard_by","What a cluster does differently","A table placed by a column ( shard_by )","A row goes to the shard of its shard_by value, not of its key. So: A primary key is unique across the shards for INSERT . Before it writes, the coordinator asks every shard's primary whether it holds the keys being inserted (one read by key a shard, for the whole request), and refuses a key another shard holds (409, naming the shard and the row's value of the column). Two inserts of one key through one coordinator wait for each other. Two coordinators inserting one key at the same moment with different shard_by values are not ordered by this check, and can both succeed: send the inserts of one key through one coordinator, or give the table a shard_by equal to the key's own placement. INSERT OR REPLACE (upsert) replaces a row only on the shard of its shard_by value. An upsert that gives an existing key another value of the column writes a second row there and leaves the old one (as Elasticsearch's custom routing does): change the column by deleting the row first. ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING passes over a key that another shard holds; DO UPDATE of a key that another shard holds under another value of the column is refused (409), since it would insert a second row. Writes by key give the column's value as \"routing\" . A set , add or delete whose routing places it on a shard that has no row with the key is refused (404, naming the routing and the column) rather than answered written: 0 ; \"if_missing\": \"skip\" passes over it as before. DELETE /v1/kv/T/K without routing on such a table is refused (400). The column cannot change: UPDATE of it, and set or add on it, are refused. A row that moves is a delete and an insert. Queries ask every shard, as for any table."],
["runbooks.html","Runbooks","","What to do when an alert fires or the fleet view names a problem, written for an operator and for an agent alike. Each runbook names the alerts ( GET /v1/alerts ) and fleet problems ( GET /v1/health/fleet , each problem's id ) it is for, then the steps: what to read first, what an ops key may do alone (tier 1), and what needs a person's approval (tier 2 and 3, filed as a change request with POST /v1/admin/changes ). The tiers are explained in chloedb-server . The server compiles this page in: GET /v1/catalog lists the routes the steps name, the MCP tool runbook finds a runbook by an alert's or a problem's id ( runbook {\"for\": \"copy-down\"} ), and each fleet problem's next.runbook names the one to read. The last section, on upgrading servers outside Kubernetes, is for no alert."],
["runbooks.html#queries-are-slow","Runbooks","Queries are slow","For: p99 , p99-jump , p99_recovered Read where the time goes: GET /v1/queries/slow (tool queries , which: slow ) and GET /v1/queries/stats group the slowest statements by their fingerprint; GET /v1/load shows each server's CPU and queue. Read what changed around the jump: GET /v1/events?from=<ms> (tool events ): a plan applied, a primary changed, a copy down, a rollout. One statement or one key holding the cores (tier 1, an ops key with a reason): limit it with a contain rule, POST /v1/admin/contain {\"match\": {\"fingerprint\": \"...\"}, \"action\": \"limit\", \"concurrency\": 2} (tool contain ); an agent's rule lasts an hour at most. One query running for minutes (tier 1): cancel it, DELETE /v1/queries/<id> (tool cancel_query ). The cluster is short of CPU for its load (tier 2): plan more copies or shards, POST /v1/admin/plan (tool plan_reshape ), and file the plan as a change request."],
["runbooks.html#searches-fail","Runbooks","Searches fail","For: errors , availability , 5xx , error_ratio , error_ratio_recovered , partial-answers , key-failing Read which statements fail and where: GET /v1/queries/recent?outcome=error and GET /v1/logs?level=error (tools queries , logs ). Each error answer carries an error_code ( GET /v1/catalog lists them): query_invalid and table_not_found are a client's, unavailable and internal the cluster's. Errors of one client ( key-failing ): tell the key's owner; nothing to change on the server. Shards that do not answer ( partial-answers , unavailable ): follow \"A copy is down\" below. Errors after a change: read GET /v1/events and GET /v1/admin/audit for what changed, and file the change that undoes it (each dry run's undo says which) as a change request."],
["runbooks.html#queries-are-refused","Runbooks","Queries are refused","For: rejected , query-memory , key-flood , contained Read what is refused and why: GET /v1/queries (running now, grouped by key and statement) and GET /v1/admin/contain (the contain rules in force; tool contain_rules ). An answer refused by a contain rule has code contained . One key crowds out the others (tier 1): limit the key, POST /v1/admin/contain {\"match\": {\"key\": \"...\"}, \"action\": \"limit\", \"concurrency\": 4} , and tell its owner. A contain rule that refuses too much (tier 1): remove it, DELETE /v1/admin/contain/<id> (tool remove_contain ). The load is real and lasting (tier 2): raise --max-concurrent or --query-memory-mb in the spec ( POST /v1/admin/kube/plan , then a change request), or add copies."],
["runbooks.html#a-copy-is-down","Runbooks","A copy is down","For: copy-down , copy-silent , under-replicated , shard-no-majority , copies , shards , coordinators_gone Read which copy, since when, and what else happened: GET /v1/health/fleet (tool health ), then GET /v1/events?kind=copy_down,primary_changed and the copy's own lines, GET /v1/logs?q=<copy name> . A copy that restarts by itself (a pod killed for memory, a rollout): wait for it, GET /v1/events?after=<id>&wait_s=30 answers as soon as its next event comes. Silence the alert meanwhile (tier 1, two hours at most for an agent): POST /v1/alerts/copy-down/silence {\"minutes\": 30} (tool silence_alert ). A copy that does not come back (tier 2): replace it. POST /v1/admin/plan with the copy left out plans the repair (Cluster: Plan the repair, in the command center); file the plan as a change request. A shard with no majority takes no writes, and its copies cannot elect a primary. Its searches go on while a replica that is up serves reads (the shard's state is no_writes ); when the copy left is its primary, it serves no reads either ( no_reads ), since with failover a primary serves reads only while a majority of its copies answer. Bring a copy back, or remove the dead one (tier 2 for a replica, DELETE /v1/admin/shards/<i>/replicas ; promoting by force is tier 3 and a person's alone)."],
["runbooks.html#a-replica-is-behind","Runbooks","A replica is behind","For: replica-behind , out-of-sync , replica-reads , lag , follow-behind Read how far behind and why: the primary's GET /v1/health ( replication.replicas : each replica's seq_no , how many transactions it is behind , and whether it is in_sync ; on /metrics , chloedb_replica_behind ), and GET /v1/load (the primary's write rate, the replica's disk and CPU). GET /v1/cluster says each copy's role and whether it serves reads; its txn counts the transactions of each process since it started, so it does not compare two copies. GET /v1/admin/follow for a follower cluster. A replica catching up after a restart: wait, with GET /v1/events?after=<id>&wait_s=30 , and silence the alert for the while (tier 1). A replica out of sync must not become the primary: do not promote it. Replace it (tier 2: a plan and a change request) if it does not catch up."],
["runbooks.html#the-disk-is-filling","Runbooks","The disk is filling","For: disk-full , disk-critical , disk-filling , disk Read which tables grow: GET /v1/tables and GET /v1/load (each copy's disk). Old rows a table keeps for a set time: a retention ( retain ) on the table drops them (tier 2, a change request). More room (tier 2): add disk in the spec ( POST /v1/admin/kube/plan ), or add a shard and move slots to it ( POST /v1/admin/plan ), each filed as a change request. Deleted rows still on disk: POST /v1/admin/vacuum frees them for good (tier 3: it loses old versions, and is a person's alone)."],
["runbooks.html#writes-fail-or-queue","Runbooks","Writes fail or queue","For: failing , flush-failing , merge-failing , write-queue , write-p99 Read the server's lines: GET /v1/logs?level=error (a full disk, an I/O error). A database that refuses writes ( failing ) on a primary: with --failover-after-ms it hands its shard over to a replica in sync by itself; without it, hand the primary over first (tier 3, POST /v1/admin/shards/<i>/promote , a person's). Then fix the cause and restart the server. Slow writes with --write-acks all : a replica or its link is slow; follow \"A replica is behind\"."],
["runbooks.html#data-damaged-on-disk","Runbooks","Data damaged on disk","For: damaged Read which copy and which segments: GET /v1/health/fleet and GET /v1/events?kind=damaged . Verify again (tier 1): POST /v1/admin/verify . Replace the copy (tier 2): plan the repair and file it as a change request; never make the damaged copy the primary."],
["runbooks.html#a-shards-primary-changed","Runbooks","A shard's primary changed","For: primary-changed Read why: GET /v1/events?kind=primary_changed,copy_down and the old primary's lines ( GET /v1/logs?q=<copy name> ). Nothing to do when the old primary is back as a replica in sync; it resolves ten minutes after the last change. A handover asked by hand fires it too. Otherwise follow \"A copy is down\"."],
["runbooks.html#a-rollout-is-held","Runbooks","A rollout is held","For: rollout , held Read what the gate saw: GET /v1/kube (the operator's status and the gate's reason) and GET /v1/events . When the reason is understood and the change is wanted (tier 2): POST /v1/admin/kube/resume , filed as a change request; otherwise roll back ( POST /v1/admin/kube {\"rollback\": <the version before>} ), also a change request."],
["runbooks.html#restore-from-checkpoints","Runbooks","Restore from checkpoints","For: restore , lost-data , bad-write Rows were deleted or overwritten by mistake, or a shard's copies all lost their disks, and the cluster has to go back to its checkpoints. Restoring is a person's alone (tier 3): it stops the cluster and takes back every write since the checkpoint. These steps are for servers you start yourself (by hand, from systemd, in a container); on Kubernetes the operator keeps its pods running and has no restore step yet. Have checkpoints of every shard from one cluster map: POST /v1/admin/checkpoint {\"name\": \"nightly\"} on the coordinator writes one on each shard's primary, into its --backup-dir , with the shard's cluster map ( nightly.slots ) and the cluster's API keys ( nightly.keys.json ) beside it, and answers the slot_epoch they are of. Copy the three files of each shard off the machine. A checkpoint of one copy is POST /v1/admin/copies/checkpoint {\"url\": \"...\", \"name\": \"...\"} . Read what you are going back to: each shard's checkpoint names its shard and epoch ( nightly.slots ), and GET /v1/cluster gives the epoch now ( slot_epoch ). Slots moved since the checkpoint when the two differ: then restore every shard, never one alone, or rows would be in two shards or in none. Stop the coordinator and every copy of the shards you restore. On each copy, put its shard's checkpoint in place of its database: chloedb-server restore --from /backups/nightly --db /data/shop.chloedb (with --keys FILE when the server has --keys FILE ). It checks the checkpoint before it changes anything, moves the database's own files aside into /data/shop.chloedb.before-restore-<time>/ , puts the shard's map beside it and merges the keys in (keys revoked since stay revoked). It refuses while a server has the database open and when the checkpoint is of another shard than the copy's. A copy on an empty disk is restored the same way, or started empty: its primary sends it every row, and the coordinator gives it the cluster map. Start each copy with the flags it had ( --replicas on the shard's primary, --replica on the others, the same --cluster-token and --seq-no ), then the coordinator with its --shards . Check before you let writes in: GET /v1/health on the coordinator, and GET /v1/cluster , where each copy of a shard says the same slot_epoch and the same seq_no , its replicas in sync, and the shards' rows add up to the checkpoint's ( SELECT count(*) through the coordinator). The files moved aside can go once it does."],
["runbooks.html#upgrades-outside-kubernetes","Runbooks","Upgrades outside Kubernetes","On Kubernetes the operator rolls a new version through the cluster ( upgrades ). On machines of your own, upgrade one server at a time, waiting after each until the cluster is well again: In each shard, its replicas, one at a time: stop it with SIGTERM, start it on the new binary without --replicas or --replica (it takes up the part its <db>.replication keeps), and wait until its own GET /v1/health says replication.serves_reads and GET /v1/cluster says every shard ok . A replica must not be older than its primary. Then hand the shard over to an upgraded replica (tier 3, a person's: POST /v1/admin/shards/<i>/promote {\"url\": \"<its gRPC URL>\"} ), and upgrade the old primary, now a replica, as the others. The coordinators last, one at a time, each with --drain-ms behind a load balancer. Operate a cluster runs this on one machine, and versions and compatibility says what a cluster of mixed versions serves."],
["security.html","Security model","","This page says who can reach a ChloeDB server, what each credential lets its holder do, what is open without one, and what is left to you: the network around the servers, the disks, and the files the servers write. The flags and routes it names are described in full in the server's security section . To report a vulnerability, see SECURITY.md ."],
["security.html#before-a-server-leaves-your-machine","Security model","Before a server leaves your machine","A server on one machine for one person needs nothing: it listens on loopback only. Before it listens anywhere else: Give it --token (or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN ) and keep that token for the people who run it. Give clients API keys of the scope they need, limited to their tables where they are an app's. In a cluster, give every server, coordinators included, the same --cluster-token ( $CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN ), and no client. Serve TLS ( --tls-cert , --tls-key ), or keep the servers on a private network: without TLS a token or a key crosses the network as plain text. Let only the cluster's own servers, its clients and its monitoring reach the HTTP and gRPC ports. Start the servers with umask 077 , or keep their folders readable by their user alone ( data at rest ), and keep the files that hold the tokens (a systemd unit's EnvironmentFile ) mode 0600. Pass --start-apps off on a server that should run no app ( apps ). Operate a cluster does all of this for a cluster on one machine."],
["security.html#listeners","Security model","Listeners","listener default flag HTTP (the API, the command center, /metrics ) 127.0.0.1:7700 --listen gRPC (clients, and the calls between a cluster's servers) 127.0.0.1:7701 --grpc , off for none OpenSearch's REST API off --opensearch-listen A server with no token, no cluster token and no API key is open : every request is its admin's. An open server refuses to start on an address reachable from elsewhere ( 0.0.0.0 , a LAN address): \"0.0.0.0:7700 is reachable beyond this machine: set --token (or $CHLOEDB_TOKEN), make an API key, or pass --no-token to serve it open\". --no-token serves it open anyway, for a network you trust as a whole. A token, a cluster token or an API key each close the server. The key a server makes for each app it starts does not. Once the server is closed, every route needs Authorization: Bearer <token or key> , except these, which answer anyone: the command center's files ( / , /classic , /ui/* ), which hold no data: the page asks for a key before it reads anything; the probes /livez , /readyz and /v1/health , for load balancers and orchestrators. Every server, alone, a shard or a coordinator, answers /v1/health with {status} alone to a request without a credential it takes (none, or a wrong one). Every gRPC call needs a credential, and the OpenSearch listener takes the same token and keys, as a Bearer token or as the password of Basic authentication."],
["security.html#credentials","Security model","Credentials","credential who holds it what it may do --token the people who run the server everything over HTTP and gRPC, with the admin scope. On a server without a cluster token it is also the cluster's token --cluster-token every server of a cluster, never a client the calls between servers ( Replicate , Restore , Verify , Slots , Configure , the slot moves, Keys , Feed , FeedKeys ), which no API key may make, and everything the token may an API key ( sk_<id>_<secret> ) a client, an agent, an app what its scope or role allows, on every table or on the tables it is limited to a Google sign-in session a person in the command center the scope the allow list gives their address, as long as it gives it an app's token chloedb-server, when it sends a request on to an app the app's own API Tokens are compared in constant time. A server keeps no key's secret: the keys file ( --keys , by default DB.keys.json beside the database, mode 0600) holds each key's name, scope, grants and the SHA-256 of its secret, and the ids of the keys revoked. The servers of a cluster share their keys every 5 seconds over Keys , with the cluster token, so a key made on any coordinator works on every shard, and a key revoked anywhere is revoked everywhere within a round ( keys across a cluster ). The calls between servers take the cluster token and nothing else. A client holding the --token or an admin key cannot ship writes to a replica, move slots or reconfigure a copy, nor make a server send the cluster token to an address of its choosing: adding a shard or a copy, and a promotion that names copies outside its shard, need the cluster token."],
["security.html#api-keys-and-scopes","Security model","API keys and scopes","Make keys in the command center ( Access and audit ) or with POST /v1/admin/keys , which answers the secret once. A key may expire ( expires_days , expires_minutes ), is rotated with a grace period during which both secrets work, and is revoked at once. The server's security section lists every route each scope reaches. scope may feed read the feed of every table, with every row as it is written, and POST /v1/feed/keys , the keys and digests of any table's rows; /v1/health and /v1/whoami . It is for a cluster that follows this one: it reaches few routes, but what it reads is all of the data read queries, explain, embed, schemas, health, metrics and the series, and the feed write that, and rows: /v1/write , /v1/import , /v1/stream , INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE , OpenTelemetry logs admin that, and changing the server: tables, rank profiles, keys, checkpoints, promotion, alert rules; the running queries, the logs and the audit log, which show other clients' SQL; /v1/export What a request does is checked as well as its route: a SQL statement by its kind (a CREATE TABLE or a DROP needs admin , whichever route sends it), a write plan needs write . Keys limited to tables. POST /v1/admin/keys {\"name\": \"crawler\", \"tables\": {\"crawl_*\": \"admin\", \"docs\": \"read\"}} makes a key whose grants each name a table, a prefix and * , or * , with a scope. Such a key never has the server's own admin (keys, shards, functions, settings, the logs and the audit log), it may call only the routes that check the tables they touch, and gRPC and MCP refuse it. A statement is read with ChloeDB's own parser and checked against every table it names; what its names cannot show ( llm() , a table function that is not a stored one, a name in parts) is refused. Such a key sees its own tables alone in /v1/health and /v1/cluster (their rows, and no shard's or copy's docs ), and an error for a table that does not exist names only the tables it sees ( no such table: a_ghost; the tables the key \"tenant-a\" sees are a_notes ). Presets ( analyst , oncall , ml-app , app-owner ) make the keys of common jobs. Keys a key makes. A key with the admin scope on some tables may make keys within its own grants ( POST /v1/keys/own ), which expire when it does and stop working when it is revoked. An app engine gives each of its apps a key this way. Agents. Give an agent a key of role propose (it reads the server's views and files change requests) or ops (that, and the bounded actions: a contain rule, a query cancelled, an alert silenced, each with a reason), rather than an admin key. Whatever its key, an agent's request that loses data or costs money (tier 3: a DROP TABLE , a DELETE of every row, a promotion, a key revoked) is refused with change_request_required : it files a change request, which a person approves with their own key, and an agent's approval is refused. A key an agent makes is an agent's too: it acts for the person its maker acts for, expires no later than its maker and stays within its maker's statement budget; asking for \"agent\": false or for another person is refused with change_request_required , and an agent rotates only agents' keys. The audit log names an agent's requests actor: \"agent\" ( agents and administration ). Model calls. Each key may make --model-calls-per-key-day calls (10,000) a day to a hosted model with the server's own credentials; the token, the cluster token and an open server are not counted."],
["security.html#browsers","Security model","Browsers","A server on your laptop is reachable by every page your browser opens. Three rules keep those pages out: Host names. On loopback the server answers only to localhost , 127.0.0.1 and ::1 , and the names --allow-host adds, so a page that points its own name at 127.0.0.1 (DNS rebinding) gets 403. On another address it answers to every name unless --allow-host lists them, and its credential guards it. Origins. Any request but GET and HEAD (reads by POST /v1/query and /v1/sql included) that carries an Origin other than the server's own gets 403: \"a page from http://evil.example cannot change this server\". The server sends no CORS headers, so a browser's preflight ( OPTIONS ) never gets a permission: it is answered 405, or 401 on a server with a token. Clients outside a browser send no Origin , and pass. A web app's page therefore calls a route of the app's own, which queries ChloeDB ( why a page cannot call chloedb-server ). Content types. A route that takes JSON refuses a body without application/json (415), and /v1/import refuses the types a page may send to another site without asking first ( text/plain , forms, none). Every answer carries X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff , X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN , Referrer-Policy: same-origin and Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none' , unless its route sets its own. A stored file ( GET /v1/media/<sha256> ) is served as an image, a sound, a video or a PDF, or else as a download, with a sandboxing policy, so a writer's HTML or SVG never runs on the server's origin. The command center keeps a key you paste in the tab's session storage, which ends with the tab. Paste it in the sign-in dialog rather than opening /?token=... : the page drops the token from its address at once, but the first address is in the browser's history and in the log of any proxy between you and the server. A person signed in with Google holds no key at all: their session is a cookie the page's scripts cannot read ( __Host-chloedb-session , Secure , HttpOnly , SameSite=Strict ), lasting 12 hours unused and 7 days at most, and the allow list is checked again on every request ( sign in with Google )."],
["security.html#tls","Security model","TLS","--tls-cert FILE --tls-key FILE (PEM) serve HTTP and gRPC over TLS, with rustls. A coordinator reaches https:// shards over TLS, trusting the public roots and --shard-ca FILE . There is no mutual TLS: the servers of a cluster know each other by the cluster token. Behind a proxy that ends TLS, the hop from the proxy to the server is plain HTTP unless the server serves TLS too."],
["security.html#apps","Security model","Apps","An app is a program of its own (the crawler, Sheets, ChloeCtx, network operations, the app engine) that the command center shows as a view ( apps ). Calls to an app go through the server: /v1/apps/<name>/... checks the caller's key first (read scope for GET and HEAD , admin for the rest), then sends the request on with the app's own token in place of the key, and tells the app who called ( x-chloedb-principal , x-chloedb-scope , and x-chloedb-tables for a key limited to tables). Give each app a token of its own ( --app-token-dir , CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN_<NAME> ), so that one app cannot call another as the server. An app's view runs in the command center's page , with the signed-in person's key, as the server's own pages do: name only apps you trust on a server's command line. An app that checks its callers runs a caller's statements as the caller: it sends its own token with x-chloedb-as-tables set to the caller's grants, and the server narrows the token to them, never wider than the token itself. Apps the server starts. With --start-apps auto , the default, a server of its own or a coordinator starts the crawler, Sheets and ChloeCtx when their programs are installed beside its own, as an install puts them. Each listens on a loopback port the server holds, takes requests only with a token the server makes at each start, and calls the server back with a key limited to its own tables (named app:<name> ), kept in memory and never in the keys file. Its environment is the server's with every credential taken out. A replica, a shard, a pod the Kubernetes operator runs and a server over TLS start none ( apps the server starts ). An app started this way makes its tables in the database (ChloeCtx's ctx_* ), and ChloeCtx downloads its embedding model; --start-apps off starts none. ChloeCtx as an app, by hand. chloectx app refuses to start without --token on any address, loopback too, unless --insecure says otherwise; on loopback without a token it answers only requests whose Host is this machine ( 127.0.0.1 , localhost , [::1] ). Give it a token wherever you run it ( ChloeCtx as an app )."],
["security.html#requests-the-server-makes","Security model","Requests the server makes","Connectors reach the public internet only, unless a connector's config says \"allow_internal\": true ; cloud metadata addresses are refused even then. Their credentials go to the origin of their url alone, and a redirect to another origin fails the run ( what a connector reaches ). Their secrets are kept in DB.connectors.json , mode 0600, and a push connector's key writes its table and reads nothing ( keys ). Media fields read a URL or a file only when --media-fetch lists it; nothing is fetched by default ( media fields ). Models are names: a query names a model in --models or a provider's, never a path, and an endpoint.json names only a provider's key variable or a CHLOEDB_MODEL_KEY_* one. Cloud credentials and the hub. The command center's cloud view keeps the AWS and DigitalOcean credentials it saves in DB.aws.json and DB.digitalocean.json , mode 0600; the hub keeps other clusters' keys in the macOS Keychain or the Secret Service where there is one, else in DB.hub-keys.json , mode 0600."],
["security.html#data-at-rest","Security model","Data at rest","ChloeDB does not encrypt its files: use the encryption of the disk or the volume (LUKS, FileVault, an encrypted cloud volume) where the data needs it. The database, its -wal , the log store ( DB.logs ), the audit log file ( --audit-log ) and checkpoints are made with the server's umask, so under the common 022 anyone on the machine may read them. The keys file, connector secrets, the cloud view's credentials and the hub's keys are mode 0600 whatever the umask. Start the server with umask 077 (systemd: UMask=0077 ) and every file it makes is its user's alone, or keep its folder mode 0700. A checkpoint ( --backup-dir ) holds every row of the database: keep it as you keep the database. Beside it are the keys file as it was ( NAME.keys.json , mode 0600: the keys' names, scopes and the SHA-256 of their secrets, never a secret) and the shard's cluster map ( NAME.slots ), which chloedb-server restore puts back."],
["security.html#logs","Security model","Logs","The log store ( --log-store , by default DB.logs beside the database; off keeps none; a coordinator keeps none unless given one) keeps the server's log lines and the queries it answered for 7 days ( --log-retain ), up to 1 GiB ( --log-store-mb ). It keeps every slow and failed query, and up to 200 others a second ( --log-queries ). Queries' values. The running and finished queries, and the log store, keep each query's parameters with their values. --redact-params ( CHLOEDB_REDACT_PARAMS ) keeps only their types. A value written into the SQL text is part of the statement and kept with it: pass anything secret as a parameter. Credentials. A token or a key is not written to the log, the log store or the audit log, a wrong one included; the log and the audit log name a key by its name and id, and the token as root token (the log) or token (the audit log). The audit log keeps every change (a request that needs the admin scope and changes something), every note and every refused request, the latest 2,000 in memory and every one in --audit-log FILE and the log store's audit table: who (the key's name and id), the route, the status, the client's address, the reason given, and what changed, before and after. Reads are not audited, nor writes of rows, by /v1/write or by SQL, except those the command center sends ( audit log ). Behind a proxy, --trust-proxy names the proxies whose X-Forwarded-For the audit log believes."],
["security.html#chloectx","Security model","ChloeCtx","ChloeCtx's stores hold transcripts of your Claude Code sessions, which may contain anything the sessions saw. The stores. Each project's store is a folder under ~/.local/share/chloectx/projects/ (or ~/.local/share/engram/projects/ on a machine from before the rename), made mode 0700. The daemon's socket. A project's daemon listens on a Unix socket in its store's folder ( engram.sock ), which only you can open. When that path is too long for a socket, it uses a folder of yours alone: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR , then /tmp/engram-<uid> , then ~/.cache/chloectx , each only if it is a real folder owned by you that no one else may write to; a folder someone else made is refused. The inspector ( chloectx ui ) listens on 127.0.0.1 by default, and every request needs the random 128-bit token in the address it prints, which the page moves into a cookie ( HttpOnly , SameSite=Strict ) on its first load. It offers only the daemon's read operations; its one change, starting an upgrade, needs the page's own header and origin. Its page runs only its own script (a Content-Security-Policy by hash, frame-ancestors 'none' ), so text from a store cannot run as a script. Started on another address it warns that anyone who can reach it and has the token can read every store, and it serves plain HTTP: forward the port over SSH instead ( the live inspector ). Redaction. ChloeCtx replaces keys, tokens, private keys, passwords in URLs and headers, and values assigned to names such as DB_PASSWORD with markers before it stores or syncs anything; a password written in prose is not caught. Treat a store like your shell history ( where it keeps what it stores )."],
["security.html#what-it-does-not-do","Security model","What it does not do","Encrypt data at rest. Mutual TLS between the servers or with clients. Limit the rate of requests per key or per client address. Keep a separate log of reads and of clients' writes of rows. Sign release archives: each comes with a .sha256 , PyPI's packages carry attestations ( SECURITY.md )."],
["sqlite.html","SQLite in and out","","ChloeDB imports the tables of a SQLite database, making a ChloeDB table of each with a schema read from SQLite's declarations and the values the columns hold, and exports a ChloeDB database to a new SQLite file that any SQLite program can read: rows, keys, references, indexes and an FTS5 index over the text. An export keeps each table's ChloeDB schema in the file, so importing it back gives the same schemas and rows. Parquet files go in and out the same way, and a SQLite file may be an s3:// URL too: Parquet and S3 ."],
["sqlite.html#import","SQLite in and out","Import","From the command line, into a database no server has open: A SQLite file is recognised by its first bytes (or --format sqlite ), and can be mixed with JSON and CSV files in one command ( --table names the table of those files' rows). A database that does not exist is created from the first SQLite file's tables. Each SQLite table goes into the ChloeDB table of its name, spelled as a ChloeDB identifier ( Order Details becomes Order_Details , and column names likewise; letters lose their accents, so Größe is Grosse ; a name of no letter or digit ChloeDB can spell, such as 名前 , is named by its place, column_2 or table_3 ; a name that is already an identifier keeps it, and a respelled one that meets another takes a suffix, so x-y beside x_y is x_y_2 ): A table ChloeDB has keeps its schema: each column fills the field of its name, the others are left out and listed. The rows are upserted by key, 10,000 a transaction, or bulk-loaded when the table is empty. A table without a key that has rows is refused before anything is written, since nothing tells its rows from the file's and importing the same file again would repeat every row: --append ( ?append=true over HTTP, append=True in Python, append: true in JavaScript) adds them beside its rows, and dropping the table first ( DROP TABLE ) replaces them, as the import makes it again. A table ChloeDB lacks is created, with the schema --schema gives for it, or the one an export kept for it, or else one made from the SQLite table, and its rows are bulk-loaded: a value that does not fit is reported with its table and row. The tables an import creates are made in one commit, and the empty ones are loaded in one more, all of them or none (a failed load leaves the new tables empty), so that a file of thousands of small tables costs as much as their number rather than its square: each commit writes the list of every table. Tables are created referenced tables first. In Python, chloedb.from_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\", \"shop.chloedb\") creates a database, db.import_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\", tables=[\"orders\"]) imports into an open one, and chloedb.plan_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\") returns the plan ( Python ). From Rust it is chloedb::sqlite (feature sqlite ): import_new , import , plan and Source for each step."],
["sqlite.html#how-sqlite-columns-become-fields","SQLite in and out","How SQLite columns become fields","SQLite has five storage classes and lets any column hold any of them, so the importer reads every row once before it loads them, to see what each column holds, and decides by its declared type and its values: SQLite column ChloeDB field a type naming INT (SQLite's integer affinity) INT REAL , FLOAT , DOUBLE , DECIMAL , NUMERIC FLOAT (SQLite keeps 12.00 in a DECIMAL column as the integer 12; it stays a float) BOOL , BOOLEAN holding 0, 1, true or false BOOL DATE , DATETIME , TIMESTAMP holding ISO 8601 text, Unix time or Julian days TIMESTAMP text in a column an FTS3, FTS4 or FTS5 table indexes TEXT , analysed english for a porter tokenizer and simple otherwise other text: a primary key, a foreign key, a column of an index, or labels rather than prose (none over 256 bytes, and 2.5 words or fewer on average, or at least 100 values repeating each value ten times on average); a word holds a letter, so phone numbers have none KEYWORD other text TEXT F32_BLOB(n) , FLOAT32(n) or VECTOR(n) holding little-endian float32 blobs (as libSQL and sqlite-vec write them) or JSON arrays VECTOR(n) JSON holding arrays of strings or integers a multi-valued KEYWORD or INT no declared type from the values: INT , FLOAT , or text as above other BLOB left out (ChloeDB keeps no bytes), or base64 text in a stored, unindexed KEYWORD with --blobs-base64 A column whose values break its declared type, such as 'n/a' in an INTEGER column, becomes text, its numbers with it. Integers in a date column are Unix time in seconds, milliseconds or microseconds, chosen by the largest one (up to 10^11 seconds, the year 5138; up to 10^14 milliseconds); a column that mixes them, such as one written in seconds and later in milliseconds, reads each integer in the unit of its size, and its note says so; reals under 10^7 are Julian days. In a FLOAT column, an integer past 2^53 rounds to the nearest double (9223372036854775807 reads as 9223372036854775808), and an infinite real is NULL (a NOT NULL column holding one may hold NULL ). A vector holding NaN or an infinity (as a float32: a JSON number past 3.4e38 is one) is NULL too, since a VECTOR holds finite numbers, and text that is not UTF-8 reads each byte that is not as U+FFFD. The plan's notes say when any of these happens, with how many values. Constraints carry over where ChloeDB has them: Keys. A single-column INTEGER or text primary key is the table's key. A composite key, a key on another type, one holding NULLs (SQLite allows them outside INTEGER PRIMARY KEY ), or one holding two values that are one KEYWORD (the integer 1 and the text '1' , which a column without a type keeps apart) leaves the table without a key, and the notes say why. References. A single-column foreign key to an imported table's key is a reference ( tables ); integer codes referencing a text key become text to match it. NOT NULL and DEFAULT . Both carry over. A default is kept when it is a constant of the field's type, or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (and CURRENT_DATE , datetime('now') ) on a timestamp, which becomes \"now\" . Indexes only steer the choice of KEYWORD over TEXT : ChloeDB indexes every field. Left out, and listed in the plan's notes: UNIQUE constraints other than the key (not enforced), CHECK constraints, triggers, collations, views, composite foreign keys, virtual tables other than FTS tables that hold their own text (an FTS table over another table's text marks those columns TEXT ; a contentless one holds no text), and tables with no column to import (only BLOBs, without --blobs-base64 ). A virtual table keeps its data in tables of its own, which SQLite marks as shadow for the modules it has (FTS3, FTS4, FTS5 and R-tree: notes_data , geo_node ), and these are never imported; a table that only begins with a virtual table's name, such as notes_archive beside notes , is a table like the rest. Vector tables of sqlite-vec ( vec0 ) need its extension, which the importer does not load, and SQLite cannot tell their own tables from others: beside CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE vecs USING vec0(...) , vecs_chunks and every vecs_... table are left out and listed, and imported when named ( --tables vecs_chunks )."],
["sqlite.html#damaged-files-and-files-being-written","SQLite in and out","Damaged files and files being written","The importer checks the file before it reads a row ( PRAGMA quick_check ), and refuses one that is damaged, saying where: a copy taken while a program was writing it is the usual cause, and sqlite3 live.db \".backup copy.db\" or VACUUM INTO 'copy.db' makes one that is whole. A file whose writer stopped mid-transaction, with its -journal beside it, is refused until SQLite rolls the transaction back, which it does when a program that may write opens the file ( sqlite3 file.db 'PRAGMA quick_check' ). From the command line, the importer reads a database in WAL mode with its -wal file, as SQLite does; an upload is the main file alone, so a server's answer has a note when the file is in WAL mode: checkpoint it first ( PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE) ) or send a copy made with VACUUM INTO . The importer reads each table twice, once to plan and once to load, and each read sees one moment of it: rows another program writes in between are imported though the plan did not see them, and one that does not fit the plan stops the import with its row, so import a copy of a database that is being written ( .backup gives every table the same moment). A file another program holds locked is waited for up to 5 seconds."],
["sqlite.html#the-plan","SQLite in and out","The plan","--plan ( chloedb.plan_sqlite in Python, chloedb::sqlite::plan in Rust) prints what an import would do without writing anything: Each table is a short-form schema with its source table, the columns read (SQLite column: field) and notes on what did not carry over. Edit it (a field's type, analyzer, multi , a vector's index, the table's name) and give it back with --schema : a schema applies to the SQLite table its source names, or else to the one of its name, and the columns fill the fields of their names. --schema also takes one schema or an array of them."],
["sqlite.html#over-http-and-in-the-ui","SQLite in and out","Over HTTP and in the UI","A running server imports a SQLite file sent to POST /v1/import as ?format=sqlite (or with the content type application/vnd.sqlite3 , or no format at all: the server knows the file by its first bytes), up to --max-body-mb (256 MiB): ?plan=true answers the plan and writes nothing, ?tables= picks tables, ?blobs_base64=true keeps BLOB columns and ?append=true adds rows to tables without a key that have rows, which answer 409 otherwise; a request with no body answers 400. The server makes the plan and reads every row once before it writes, so that a value that does not fit writes nothing; then it creates the tables it lacks, as CREATE TABLE does (in one commit), and upserts the rows through its writer 1,000 at a time, as an import of rows does, the rows of small tables sharing a request (the rows before a failed write stay written, and the error says how many). Once it writes, an import goes on to its end though the client hangs up or times out, and the server logs each import with its rows and seconds. Two imports at once of a table the server lacks both succeed, one creating it; of a table without a key, one writes it and the other answers 409 (with ?append=true both add their rows; two coordinators of a cluster do not see each other's imports). A table the server has keeps its schema, so to import with schemas of your own, create the tables first with CREATE TABLE and import into them. A coordinator creates a table it lacks on every shard, as CREATE TABLE through it does, and imports into it; a primary of replicas ships the tables it creates to them with the rows. GET /v1/export sends the database as a new SQLite file, written from one snapshot to a temporary file and streamed from it: ?tables=a,b for some tables, ?fts=false , ?indexes=false and ?schemas=false as --no-fts , --no-indexes and --no-schemas below. A coordinator holds no files, so it exports no SQLite file; it exports one table's rows as JSON lines, CSV, Arrow or Parquet ( ?format=jsonl , csv , arrow , parquet with ?tables=T , read by a scroll over its shards, with read on the table: cluster limits ). In the query console ( /classic ), the Schema and import tab has both: a SQLite file to preview (the plan, table by table) and import, and a button that downloads the database as SQLite. The Python Client has import_sqlite , plan_sqlite and export_sqlite ( Python )."],
["sqlite.html#export","SQLite in and out","Export","In Python, db.export_sqlite(\"shop.sqlite\") ; in Rust, chloedb::sqlite::export . The rows of every table come from one snapshot, and the file is written under another name and renamed into place when complete; an existing file is not overwritten. Each table becomes a SQLite table of the same name: ChloeDB field SQLite column KEYWORD , TEXT TEXT ; the key is TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL INT INTEGER ; the key is INTEGER PRIMARY KEY , the rowid FLOAT REAL BOOL BOOLEAN , 0 or 1 TIMESTAMP TIMESTAMP , UTC text the way SQLite's date functions write it: 2026-10-01 12:30:05.250 VECTOR(n) F32_BLOB(n) : n little-endian float32 values, which libSQL's vector functions and sqlite-vec read as they are multi-valued KEYWORD or INT JSON , an array ( json_each lists its values) MEDIA JSON , the file's description ( uri , sha256 , mime , ...); the files stay where their URIs point EDGES JSON , an array of keys SPARSE JSON , an object of term weights With them: References become REFERENCES clauses, and NOT NULL and DEFAULT carry over ( \"now\" is CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ). ChloeDB does not enforce references, so a row may name a key that is gone; SQLite checks foreign keys only when a connection turns them on ( PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON ), and then only on later writes. B-tree indexes on each indexed single-valued KEYWORD , INT , FLOAT , BOOL and TIMESTAMP field other than the key, named <table>_<field>_idx , and on a MEDIA field's json_extract(field, '$.sha256') , as ChloeDB indexes it ( --no-indexes leaves them out). An FTS5 index over each table's TEXT fields, <table>_fts , with its text in the table itself ( content= ) and triggers that keep it in step with inserts, updates and deletes ( --no-fts leaves it out). Search it as FTS5 is searched: SELECT t.* FROM t_fts JOIN t ON t.rowid = t_fts.rowid WHERE t_fts MATCH 'wizard' ORDER BY rank . The ChloeDB schemas in a table _strata_tables ( name , position , schema in the short JSON form), which an import of the file reads to restore them ( --no-schemas leaves it out; an import then makes schemas from the SQLite tables). A table of that name without the name and schema columns is the file's own, and imported as the others are."],
["sqlite.html#what-sqlite-does-not-hold","SQLite in and out","What SQLite does not hold","The data round-trips; ChloeDB's indexes and search do not, since SQLite has no counterpart: Vector indexes. HNSW and IVF indexes and the compressed codes stay behind; the vectors are exported. SQLite searches them with an extension (sqlite-vec, libSQL's vector index) or by a scan. An embedded field's model is recorded in the kept schema only. Text analysis and ranking. FTS5 tokenizes on its own: porter unicode61 stands in for the english analyzer (ChloeDB stems with Snowball English and drops stopwords; Porter stems a little differently and keeps them), unicode61 for simple , and a table has one tokenizer for all its text fields. FTS5's bm25() is not ChloeDB's BM25, and fused, decayed and boosted scores, typo tolerance and synonyms have no SQLite form. Graphs. EDGES are JSON arrays of keys; walks and PageRank over them are queries SQLite cannot run without recursive SQL of your own. Generated fields export their text as a plain column, not as a SQLite generated column; an import with the kept schema recomputes it. Fields that are not stored hold what the indexes give back, often NULL. ChloeDB's file features : segments, snapshots, the write-ahead log, replication and sequence numbers."],
["parquet.html","Parquet and S3","","ChloeDB reads Parquet files, and directories of them as Spark, DuckDB, polars and pandas write them, into a table, making the table's schema from the columns' types and values when it does not exist, and writes a table to a Parquet file that any of those tools reads. An export keeps the table's ChloeDB schema in the file, so importing it back gives the same schema and rows. Wherever a file is read or written, an s3:// URL works too: an object, or every object under a prefix, on AWS S3 or any store that speaks its API."],
["parquet.html#import","Parquet and S3","Import","From the command line, into a database no server has open: A Parquet file is recognised by its name ( .parquet ), its first bytes or --format parquet , and a directory by the Parquet files in it, at any depth; names starting with _ or . , such as Spark's _SUCCESS and its .crc checksums, are passed over. The files of a directory make one table, read in the order of their paths, and may differ in columns: each file's columns fill the fields of their names. Parquet and SQLite files, and JSON and CSV files, can be mixed in one command. The rows go into the table --table names, else the one an export kept in the file, else a table named for the file or directory ( events.snappy.parquet and events/ make events ): A table ChloeDB has keeps its schema: each column fills the field of its name (in any case), the others are left out and listed. The rows are upserted by key (inserted, in a table without one), 10,000 a transaction, or bulk-loaded when the table is empty. A table ChloeDB lacks is created, with the schema --schema gives, or the one an export kept in the file, or else one made from the columns, and its rows are bulk-loaded in one commit: a value that does not fit is reported with its file and row, and nothing is written. A database that does not exist is created with the first Parquet file's table. In Python, chloedb.from_parquet(\"events.parquet\", \"lake.chloedb\") creates a database, db.import_parquet(\"out/clicks/\", table=\"clicks\") imports into an open one, and chloedb.plan_parquet(\"events.parquet\") returns the plan ( Python ). From Rust it is chloedb::parquet (feature parquet ): import_new , import , plan and Source for each step."],
["parquet.html#how-parquet-columns-become-fields","Parquet and S3","How Parquet columns become fields","Parquet columns have types, so a table made from a file takes them, and reads the first 100,000 rows only to choose between a few: Parquet / Arrow column ChloeDB field integers of any width, decimals with no scale INT floats, other decimals FLOAT (a decimal as a double: the plan notes it) booleans BOOL dates, timestamps of any unit or time zone TIMESTAMP (a date at midnight UTC) text that is the key, dictionary-encoded, or labels rather than prose KEYWORD other text TEXT fixed-size lists of numbers, or lists of numbers all of one length VECTOR(n) lists of text or of integers multi-valued KEYWORD or INT lists of vectors multi-vector VECTOR(n)[] structs, maps, other lists JSON binary left out (ChloeDB has no binary type), or base64 text in a stored KEYWORD with --blobs-base64 times of day, durations, intervals, columns of NULLs only left out Text reads as labels when no value is over 256 bytes and the values have 2.5 words or fewer on average, or each value repeats ten times on average (as the SQLite import judges). Column names that are not ChloeDB identifiers are respelled ( City Name becomes City_Name ; the plan lists each). A column named id (in any case) of integers or text without NULLs in the sample is the key; --key names another, and --key \"\" makes a table without one. A NaN or infinite float is read as NULL, and the import says how many were. A column fills only the field of its own name, and nothing renames one on the way in. A key column named otherwise, such as _id in BEIR's corpora and in MongoDB's documents, therefore does not fill a table keyed id : an import into one fails with bad value for field id: NULL does not fit a KEYWORD field , and a table made from the file has no key unless --key names one, since only a column named id becomes the key by itself. The plan's columns says which field each column fills, but it is read, not followed: a plan given back as --schema (or schema= ) gives the table's schema, and a columns edited to {\"_id\": \"id\"} fails the same way. Two ways through: Keep the column's name as the key. --key _id ( key=\"_id\" in Python, ?key=_id over HTTP) makes _id the key of the table the import creates, and SQL reads it as any column ( WHERE _id = 'd1' ). To choose the fields' types, analyzers or an embedded vector field, create the table first with a key named _id and import into it with --table . Rename the column before the import , in Arrow from Python, and write the rows with import_arrow into the table you made: A table made from a file without --table is named for the file, so Hugging Face's corpus-00000-of-00001.parquet makes corpus_00000_of_00001 : give --table corpus ( table=\"corpus\" ). Into a table that exists, a column fills its field when its values do: any integer, float or decimal for a number, dates, timestamps and ISO 8601 text for a TIMESTAMP , a list of n numbers (or 4n little-endian float32 bytes) for a VECTOR(n) , a list for a multi-valued field, structs, maps and lists for a JSON field. Integers that are Unix times (seconds, milliseconds or microseconds, by their size) fill a TIMESTAMP too."],
["parquet.html#directories-and-hive-partitions","Parquet and S3","Directories and Hive partitions","A directory Spark writes with partitionBy holds its files under key=value directories ( year=2026/month=11/part-00000-….snappy.parquet ). Each key becomes a field, INT when every value is an integer and KEYWORD otherwise, and each row takes its file's values; __HIVE_DEFAULT_PARTITION__ is NULL. Into a table that exists, a key fills the field of its name. A directory whose files keep different ChloeDB tables (an export of several, below) is refused: import its files one at a time."],
["parquet.html#export","Parquet and S3","Export","An export reads the rows of one snapshot. It writes a .parquet file when the name says so (one table), and otherwise a new directory with a <table>.parquet file a table ( --format parquet , or a name ending in / ; without either, the export is a SQLite file ). The files are compressed with Zstandard, in row groups of about 128 MB, and written under another name and renamed into place when complete; an existing file or directory is an error, but for an empty directory. In Python it is db.export_parquet(\"events.parquet\", tables=\"events\") , from Rust chloedb::parquet::export . ChloeDB field Parquet / Arrow column INT INT64 FLOAT DOUBLE BOOL BOOLEAN TIMESTAMP TIMESTAMP(MICROS, UTC) TEXT , KEYWORD STRING VECTOR(n) a list of n floats (Arrow FixedSizeList(Float32, n) , which NumPy, PyTorch and Spark's array<float> take) VECTOR(n)[] a list of those multi-valued KEYWORD , INT a list of STRING , of INT64 JSON , SPARSE , EDGES , MEDIA STRING , the value's JSON text Only the key's column is not nullable. Each file keeps its table's ChloeDB schema as JSON in its key-value metadata ( strata.schema ), which other tools ignore and an import restores: analyzers, vector indexes and their kinds, embedding models, references, defaults and generated fields' definitions, which Parquet has no place for. A field neither stored nor recoverable from an index is NULL, and a generated field holds its text; the export's notes list what does not carry over."],
["parquet.html#s3-and-s3-compatible-stores","Parquet and S3","S3 and S3-compatible stores","s3://bucket/key (or s3a:// , as Spark writes) works wherever a file does: chloedb-server import and export , and in Python from_parquet , import_parquet , export_parquet , from_sqlite , import_sqlite , export_sqlite and import_file . A URL names one object, or every object under a prefix: one that ends in / , or that names no object ( s3://lake/raw/events reads raw/events/year=2026/part-0.parquet and the rest). The objects are read into a temporary directory, keeping their paths under the prefix, eight at once, and imported from there; an export is written to a temporary file or directory and then sent, in parts of 16 MB for large files. An object already at the URL of an export, or under its prefix, is an error, checked before anything is sent. The store is configured as the AWS tools are, by environment variables: variable what AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID , AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY , AWS_SESSION_TOKEN the credentials; without them, a web identity ( AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE and AWS_ROLE_ARN , as EKS gives pods), ECS task credentials, or EC2 instance metadata AWS_REGION (or AWS_DEFAULT_REGION ) the bucket's region AWS_ENDPOINT_URL a store other than AWS S3: https://nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com for DigitalOcean Spaces, https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com for Cloudflare R2, MinIO's or Ceph's address AWS_ALLOW_HTTP=true for an endpoint over plain http:// , such as a local MinIO AWS_VIRTUAL_HOSTED_STYLE_REQUEST=true bucket.host addresses instead of host/bucket Profiles in ~/.aws are not read. The S3 client is object_store 's, in ChloeDB's feature s3 (on by default in chloedb-server and the Python package)."],
["parquet.html#over-http-and-in-the-ui","Parquet and S3","Over HTTP and in the UI","POST /v1/import takes a Parquet file in the body ( application/vnd.apache.parquet , or recognised by its first bytes; up to the server's --max-body-mb ), into the table ?table= names or the one an export kept in the file, with ?key= , ?blobs_base64=true and ?plan=true as on the command line. The rows are checked before any is written and then written through the writer, so a value that does not fit writes nothing; an import that creates the table needs an admin key, and a coordinator imports into tables its shards have. GET /v1/export?format=parquet&tables=events answers one table as a Parquet file (a database of one table may leave out tables ), and ?schemas=false leaves out the kept schema. From Python's client: client.import_parquet(path, table=None, key=None) , client.plan_parquet(path) and client.export_parquet(path, table=None) . The command center's Import view reads a dropped Parquet file's plan from the server: the table it goes to (named in a box, which re-plans), each column's field and type, the key and what is left out, then imports it with its progress. Its Export card writes SQLite or Parquet, a Parquet file a table chosen."],
["parquet.html#arrow-from-python","Parquet and S3","Arrow from Python","db.import_arrow(data, table=None) writes the rows of anything that gives an Arrow stream ( __arrow_c_stream__ ): a pyarrow Table, RecordBatch or RecordBatchReader, a polars DataFrame, a DuckDB relation, an answer of query_arrow , and a pandas DataFrame through pyarrow. Each column fills the field of its name, read as an import reads a Parquet column; the others are left out and listed. Into an empty table the rows are bulk-loaded in one commit, otherwise upserted by key. With query_arrow going the other way, a table moves between ChloeDB and dataframe libraries without JSON in between."],
["parquet.html#with-spark","Parquet and S3","With Spark","Spark reads an export as it reads any Parquet ( spark.read.parquet(\"s3a://lake/exports/events.parquet\") ): vectors as array<float> , timestamps as timestamp , lists as arrays, JSON as strings to from_json . What Spark writes ( df.write.partitionBy(\"year\").parquet(\"s3a://lake/raw/events\") ) imports as a directory, partitions and all. A data source that lets Spark read from and write to a running server is a later step."],
["parquet.html#what-it-does-not-do","Parquet and S3","What it does not do","Read Parquet in place. An import copies the rows into ChloeDB's own files, which hold the indexes Parquet has none of; a query never reads a Parquet file. Several tables from one directory. A directory is one table, as Spark, DuckDB and polars read one; an export of several tables is a directory of files, imported a file at a time. Partitioned exports. An export writes one file a table, in row groups; it does not split a table by a column into key=value directories. Stream from S3. An import reads all the objects to local disk before it reads them, and an export writes the file locally before it sends it, so both need the space."],
["postgres.html","Following Postgres","","chloedb-server follow-postgres keeps ChloeDB in step with a Postgres database by logical replication: it copies the tables of a publication, then applies every insert, update, delete, TRUNCATE and new column a moment after Postgres commits it, so ChloeDB serves search over the rows Postgres holds without a nightly job or a Debezium and Kafka pipeline in between. It speaks Postgres's own protocol ( pgoutput , the plugin Postgres ships, from version 10), and keeps what it applied in ChloeDB's _sources , in the same transactions as the rows, so a follower stopped and started again goes on from there and applies each change once. This is ChloeDB reading from Postgres, not ChloeDB speaking to Postgres clients: ChloeDB serves no Postgres protocol of its own, so psql , DBeaver, Metabase and dbt's Postgres adapter cannot connect to it. Running SQL lists the ways in."],
["postgres.html#set-up-postgres","Following Postgres","Set up Postgres","Postgres writes what logical replication needs once wal_level is logical (a restart takes it): The role signs in by SCRAM-SHA-256 (Postgres's default), MD5 or a password, and needs REPLICATION and SELECT on the tables. On managed Postgres, logical replication is a setting ( rds.logical_replication on RDS and Aurora, cloudsql.logical_decoding on Cloud SQL; Supabase and Neon have it on). Since Postgres 15 a publication can leave columns out ( FOR TABLE users (id, name, bio) ) and rows ( WHERE (published) ); the follower copies and follows what it publishes. Each table needs a key: its primary key, or a unique index named as its replica identity ( ALTER TABLE t REPLICA IDENTITY USING INDEX t_code_key ). A key of several columns becomes one KEYWORD column _key in ChloeDB, their values joined by : ( 42:en ), beside the columns themselves."],
["postgres.html#run-it","Following Postgres","Run it","option --source the database's URL (or CHLOEDB_POSTGRES_URL ); PGPASSWORD gives a password it leaves out; sslmode is disable , prefer , require , verify-ca or verify-full ; without one, require for a database on another machine (a connection that could fall back to plain text would send the password's proof and every row in the clear) and prefer for one on localhost or a loopback address, and sslrootcert (or --source-ca ) a CA certificate to check the server's by; with verify-* and no CA given, the public CAs are trusted --target ChloeDB's HTTP URL: a server, or a cluster's coordinator ( --target-token or CHLOEDB_CLUSTER_TOKEN , --target-ca ) --name the name ChloeDB knows the database by, which its marks in _sources name --publication the publication ( strata ) --slot the replication slot it makes and reads ( strata_<name> ) --batch the most rows of one write while copying, and of the transactions written together (1,000) --copy-workers how many tables are copied at once (4) --copy copy everything again, with a new slot --drop-slot drop the slot, release the tables it followed (they take other writes again) and exit The first run makes the replication slot, which holds a snapshot of the database, and copies each table as of that snapshot while Postgres goes on taking writes; it then streams the slot's changes from the snapshot's position, so no change between the two is missed or applied twice. A ChloeDB table it lacks is made from the Postgres table; one that exists keeps its schema (see below ). It logs how far it is every 30 seconds ( applied , the server's WAL position, behind_bytes ), and GET /v1/sources on ChloeDB shows the last LSN it applied ( postgres/<name>/<copy> ), with when. Stop it whenever: started again it resumes from what ChloeDB applied. While it is stopped, Postgres keeps the WAL the slot has not consumed , which can fill its disk over days; when you stop following for good, drop the slot ( --drop-slot , or SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('strata_shop') ). Postgres 13 and later can cap what a slot keeps ( max_slot_wal_keep_size ); a slot that lost WAL it needed is copied afresh on the next start."],
["postgres.html#what-it-applies","Following Postgres","What it applies","Inserts and updates are upserts of the whole row by key; an update that changes the key deletes the old row. A large value an update leaves unchanged, which Postgres does not send (TOAST), is kept: taken from the old row with REPLICA IDENTITY FULL , or else read from ChloeDB's row. Deletes are by key. TRUNCATE deletes every row of the table. Columns added in Postgres are added to the ChloeDB table ( ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ), and a column renamed while the follower runs is renamed. The rows from before the column read what they read in Postgres: a constant default ( ADD COLUMN vip boolean DEFAULT false ), which Postgres keeps beside the table rather than writing into its rows, so the stream never sends it, becomes the ChloeDB column's DEFAULT ; a default Postgres writes into each row (a volatile one, as random() or gen_random_uuid() , or a type ChloeDB's DEFAULT cannot hold, as jsonb ) has the table copied again, as of then, with the changes since applied over it. A column dropped in Postgres stays in ChloeDB, NULL (or its default) in rows written since. A changed type keeps the ChloeDB column's, and the values are read as it reads them. Tables joining the publication ( ALTER PUBLICATION ... ADD TABLE , or a table created under FOR ALL TABLES ) are copied when the follower sees them, within 30 seconds, then followed. A table leaving it is no longer followed; its ChloeDB rows stay. A table renamed in Postgres goes on into the ChloeDB table of its old name. ChloeDB's tables the follower writes take no other writes: an INSERT , UPDATE or DELETE of one, or a POST /v1/write , is refused (409, followed_table ), since a row written in ChloeDB would answer what Postgres does not until Postgres changed it and the follower wrote over it, and a transaction applied again after a restart must write what it wrote before. Write the change in Postgres. The follower names itself on its requests ( x-chloedb-postgres-follower ), and a server reads which tables are followed from the follower's marks within seconds of a copy. Columns may still be added in ChloeDB (an embedded vector, say). A table that leaves the publication, or whose follower's slot is dropped ( --drop-slot ), takes writes again. Transactions are applied whole, each in one write (several at once while Postgres sends faster than they are written), but one of more than 1,000 changes, more than 16 MB of values, or with a TRUNCATE , is written in steps: a reader can see part of it, as it can see part of any write across a cluster's shards. Changed: a follower of 0.3.7 or earlier adds a new column with its type only, so the rows from before read NULL in it until Postgres next changes each of them (add the column in ChloeDB first, with the same default, or copy again with --copy ), and its server takes other writes to the followed tables, whose rows then differ from Postgres's, with no warning, until Postgres changes them."],
["postgres.html#how-postgres-columns-become-fields","Following Postgres","How Postgres columns become fields","Postgres ChloeDB boolean BOOL smallint , integer , bigint , oid INT real , double precision , numeric FLOAT text , varchar , name , citext , xml TEXT ( KEYWORD as the key) char(n) , uuid , enums, and other types ( inet , interval , ...) KEYWORD , their text date , timestamp , timestamptz TIMESTAMP (UTC; infinity is NULL ) json , jsonb JSON (a value that is not an object becomes {\"value\": ...} ) arrays of text types, uuid or enums KEYWORD[] arrays of integers INT[] pgvector's vector(n) , halfvec(n) VECTOR(n) pgvector's sparsevec SPARSE bytea , tsvector , other arrays, vector without a dimension left out"],
["postgres.html#choosing-chloedb-types","Following Postgres","Choosing ChloeDB types","A text or varchar column becomes TEXT , which is searched by its words. It groups ( GROUP BY country , SELECT DISTINCT country ) and compares with = by its stored text, read row by row, where a KEYWORD 's index answers both ( differences from PostgreSQL ). A column of names, codes or statuses that you group and filter by its whole value ( brand below) belongs in a KEYWORD . To choose the types, analyzers, an embedded vector or fields of ChloeDB's own, make the table in ChloeDB before the first run, with the Postgres key as its key and the columns of the names Postgres has: The follower writes each Postgres column into the field of its name as that field reads it (an integer into a KEYWORD , a real[] into a VECTOR(n) ), adds the published columns the table lacks, and leaves the other fields to ChloeDB: here emb is embedded from each row as it arrives. A value a field cannot read (text in an INT ) stops the follower with the table, column and value; fix the table and start it again."],
["postgres.html#how-a-restart-resumes","Following Postgres","How a restart resumes","What the follower applied is in ChloeDB's _sources , written in the same transaction as the rows it covers: postgres/<name>/copy is when the copy it follows from began; a copy made again names every other mark afresh, under postgres/<name>/<copy>/ . postgres/<name>/<copy> is the LSN where the last applied Postgres transaction ends. Every operation of a transaction carries it as its stream offset, so a shard that applied the transaction skips it when it comes again, and a follower started again streams from the least over the shards ( streams ). A transaction written in steps numbers them ( .../step , carried by each step's operations), and its last step writes how many steps such transactions took so far ( .../base ): replayed, it numbers its steps as before, each shard skips the steps it applied, and a TRUNCATE already done is not done again. .../copied says the copy finished, and .../t/<oid>/<table> which Postgres table each ChloeDB table follows. The follower tells Postgres how far it applied, so that the slot lets go of the WAL before it. A copy cut short is made again, from a new slot."],
["connectors.html","Connectors","","A connector brings another system's data into a table of chloedb-server , and keeps it coming: it reads the system on a schedule (a pull), takes what the system sends (a push), or, for Kafka, keeps reading its topics while it is on (a stream); it turns each record into a row by a map, and upserts the rows by the table's key, so a record read twice is the same row (a stream writes each event once, by its offset). Connectors are configured as rows of a table, each writes with a key limited to its own target, and the command center's Data > Connectors page shows each one's health. kind pull or push what it brings in default table prometheus pull PromQL queries over each new step ( query_range ), joined by labels into one row per subject and minute: the rollups an incident needs, the full series staying in Prometheus (or Thanos, Mimir, VictoriaMetrics) prometheus_rollups jira pull issues matching a JQL query updated since the last run (Jira Cloud's /rest/api/3/search/jql , or Data Center's /rest/api/2/search ) jira_issues servicenow pull records of a table ( change_request , incident , problem , ...) updated since the last run, through the Table API servicenow_records oxidized pull, and push from its hook device configurations from oxidized-web: each node whose backup changed is fetched and kept as a version device_configs mail push emails handed over raw: headers, text, and the maintenance fields carriers attach (the BCOP X-MAINTNOTE iCalendar properties: provider, maintenance id, circuits, window, impact, status) mail_messages webhook push any system that posts JSON; preset alertmanager for Alertmanager's webhook webhook_events , alertmanager_alerts http_json pull any REST API that answers JSON: records at a path, the next page at another, a cursor kept from the records http_records kafka stream Kafka (or Redpanda, MSK, Confluent Cloud) topics, read by the server itself while the connector is on, each event written once: its partition's offset is kept with its row ( Kafka topics ) kafka_events Other ways rows come in stay as they are, and the Connectors page lists their sources beside the connectors: OpenTelemetry logs ( POST /v1/logs , server.md ), streams with offsets applied once ( POST /v1/stream , which a consumer of your own can write), Postgres by logical replication ( follow-postgres ), and files ( SQLite , Parquet and S3 )."],
["connectors.html#make-one","Connectors","Make one","POST /v1/admin/connectors with an admin key: name : 1 to 48 lowercase letters, digits, - and _ . target : the table it writes. Left out, the kind's own table, which is made then (its columns are in the table above's defaults ). A table that does not exist and is not the kind's own is refused, unless config.create_target is true , which makes it with the columns of the connector's own map when it has one (each typed by its as : int an INT , float a FLOAT , bool a BOOL , time or {\"now\": true} a TIMESTAMP , list a KEYWORD[] , json a JSON , hash a KEYWORD , any other TEXT , or the type a spec says: {\"path\": \"$.action\", \"type\": \"KEYWORD\"} for a value kept whole, to group or filter by, rather than searched as words; id its primary key, or none without an id ), else with the kind's columns. every_s : seconds between pulls (at least 10; 0 runs only when asked). The defaults: 60 for Prometheus, 300 for Jira, ServiceNow and HTTP pulls, 900 for Oxidized. A failed pull waits twice as long after each failure in a row, up to 32 times its interval and never more than an hour; one that stopped at its limit (20,000 records a run) runs again at once. config : the kind's settings (below) and an optional map . secrets : passwords and tokens, by name. They are never written to a table nor answered: the server keeps them in <db>.connectors.json beside the database, readable by its user only ( --connector-secrets FILE ; a coordinator without one keeps them in memory, so give it one, or name each secret as \"env:VARIABLE\" , which the server reads from its environment at each run, as a Kubernetes Secret gives it). An env: secret names a variable whose name starts with CHLOEDB_CONNECTOR_SECRET_ ( --connector-env-prefix chooses another prefix), so that no connector reads the server's own tokens, model keys or cloud credentials; another name is refused with a 400. enabled (default true ) and note . The answer has the connector, made_target , and for a push connector its key's secret, push_secret , shown this once. GET /v1/admin/connectors every connector with its health , state (cursor, last run, last ok, failures in a row, runs, rows written) and last 12 runs; the kinds and their settings; whether pulls run on this server ( scheduler ); the stream sources GET /v1/admin/connectors/NAME one, with its last 50 runs and the map it writes with POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME changes the settings the body gives ( secrets are merged, a null taking one out) DELETE /v1/admin/connectors/NAME its config, state, secrets and push key; the rows it wrote stay, and its runs for their 30 days POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/run a pull now; answers what it did POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/key a new push key in place of the old one, which stops working now POST /v1/admin/connectors/test a dry run: what a connector would read and the first ten rows it would write, nothing written. The body is a connector's ( kind , target , config , secrets , given as values: an env: one is refused; or name alone for a saved one), with sample , the body of a push, for a pushed kind POST /v1/connectors/NAME/push a system's push: any body (JSON, or a raw email), written with the sender's key. A body with no records at the connector's records path (Alertmanager's: $.alerts ), or with no message for a mail connector, is refused with 422, which says what the body has instead (its top-level keys), so that a sender set up wrong finds out; an empty list there is a push of no records, answered 200"],
["connectors.html#health","Connectors","Health","health ok the last run went well (and, for a pull, within three times its interval, or 15 minutes when that is longer) warning the last run went well but found something wrong: state.last_warning (and the run's warning ) says what. An http_json pull whose records path matched nothing in an answer that had a body (no key of that name, as against an empty list) reads 0 records and says so: \"the records path $.items matched nothing; the top-level keys are data, next\". A dry run answers it as warning too invalid its row fails the API's checks ( invalid says why): it does not run failing the last run failed: state.last_error says why, state.failures how many in a row (a request that failed says why, from its causes: the connection refused, a name that does not resolve, TLS, a timeout; an error names a URL without its query, and a secret's value nowhere: it reads *** , since a read key reads these tables) stale a pull whose last good run is older than that pending , waiting a pull not run yet (one run by hand, every_s 0, too); a push connector nothing was pushed to yet running , disabled"],
["connectors.html#configured-as-rows","Connectors","Configured as rows","Each connector is a row of chloedb_connectors ( id its name, kind , target , enabled , every_s , config as JSON text, push_key its key's id, note , created , updated ), each run a row of chloedb_connector_runs (trigger, started, took, ok, records read, rows written, records that made no row, columns left out, error, cursor after, warning ; kept 30 days), and each connector's cursor and counts a row of chloedb_connector_state (its rows_total counts every row each run wrote, an upsert of a row already there too, so it can be more than the target's rows; the Connectors page's column says \"rows written\"). The tables are made with the first connector, with updated_by , who changed the config last, and the runs' warning and the state's last_warning (a server adds the columns it lacks to older tables). SQL reads them, and only the server writes them: a statement or a write that changes one of them ( INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE , CREATE , ALTER , DROP , /v1/write , /v1/kv ) answers 403 server_owned for every key, an admin's too, since a row would choose the URL a connector sends its credentials to and the table its run's key writes. Change a connector through the API ( POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME {\"enabled\": false} turns one off), which checks it and writes the change in the audit log. The schedule reads chloedb_connectors again every 10 seconds and checks each row as the API does: a row that fails (one written with the server's own token, say, or by an older server) is shown with health invalid and why, and is neither pulled nor pushed to until an admin changes or deletes it."],
["connectors.html#what-a-connector-reaches","Connectors","What a connector reaches","A connector asks its system from inside the database's network, so by default it reaches the public internet only: an address on the server's own machine, a private network (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 100.64/10, fc00::/7) or another reserved range is refused, checked on each URL and again on each address a name resolves to. A system inside the network is reached when the connector's config says so, \"allow_internal\": true (a Prometheus at http://prometheus:9090 , oxidized-web on the management network). Link-local addresses, where clouds answer instance metadata (169.254.169.254, fe80::/10), are refused even then. The hosts of HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY are reachable, as the proxy is the operator's. A connector's credentials (its basic or bearer auth, and its headers , where {secret:NAME} is expanded) go to the origin of its url ( instance for ServiceNow) only. It follows a redirect within that origin, five at most; a redirect to another origin fails the run with where it led. An http_json next page on another origin is asked without them, and a {secret:NAME} in a URL read from an answer is expanded only when the configured url names that secret and the page is on its origin. An answer is read up to 32 MiB ( \"max_body_mb\" , 1 to 1024), and a run that gets more fails saying so."],
["connectors.html#keys","Connectors","Keys","A connector writes with a key of its own, never with the server's: A pull runs inside the server and writes its rows through the server's own API, with a key the server makes for the run (named connector-run:<name> , kept in memory only) that may write the target and nothing else; the connectors' own tables are written with another ( connectors ), which may touch only those. So a pull's rows are checked as any client's are, and a map cannot write another table. A push connector gets a key of the keys file when it is made, named connector:<name> and limited to writing its target (a key limited to tables ). The sending system holds it, and a push is written with the key it came with: one leaked lets its holder write that table, nothing else, and read nothing: the server refuses such a key (by its name, connector: ) every route but the push, POST /v1/write and GET /v1/health and /v1/whoami , so it cannot query or list even its own table. It is listed in Access and audit, rotated with POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/key , and revoked when the connector is deleted. On an open server (no token, no key) a push needs no key, and none is made."],
["connectors.html#who-runs-the-pulls","Connectors","Who runs the pulls","A server of its own, or a coordinator; with several coordinators, the one that sends the alerts' messages (the lowest URL among those that answer), so each pull runs once. A replica or a shard runs none, and --connectors off ( CHLOEDB_CONNECTORS=off ) turns them off on any server. A push may go to any coordinator. A pull that runs twice anyway (a coordinator taking over) writes the same rows again, as upserts."],
["connectors.html#maps","Connectors","Maps","A record is the JSON a connector read: a Jira issue, a point of a series, an alert, an email. config.map names each column of the row and how to fill it: spec value \"$.fields.summary\" a path into the record: $ the record, .name or [\"name\"] a key, [0] an element, [*] every element \"{key}-{fields.status.name}\" a template, each {path} replaced by its value as text any other value itself {\"path\" \\| \"template\" \\| \"const\" \\| \"now\": true, \"split\": \",\", \"values\": {...}, \"as\": \"...\", \"default\": ...} the value, then in this order: text split into a list; a value replaced by another, by its text ( \"*\" for any other); a conversion ( text , int , float , bool , time , list , first , json , lower , upper , hash , text_of ); a default when it came out empty A column the map does not name is left out; with no map at all (and no preset), the record is the row. The row is then fitted to the target's columns: a column the table does not have is left out and named in the run ( dropped ), a time in any of the forms systems write (ISO 8601 with any offset, ServiceNow's 2026-11-13 03:00:00 , an email's date, iCalendar's 20261113T030000Z , seconds or milliseconds since 1970) becomes ISO 8601 text for a TIMESTAMP , a number in text a number, one value a list for a KEYWORD[] or INT[] , and an object or list text for a TEXT or KEYWORD . A row without the table's primary key makes no row, and the run counts it ( skipped ) with the first reason."],
["connectors.html#default-tables-and-presets","Connectors","Default tables and presets","Each kind writes its own table with a map of its own. Some targets of the network operations app have presets, used when the connector writes that table and gives no map: kind target servicenow netops_changes , netops_tickets a change's number, window, state as the app's status, CI as its devices; an incident's fields as a ticket jira netops_changes , netops_tickets an issue as a change (custom fields named in config.fields : window_start , window_end , devices , risk , approver , rollback_plan ) or a ticket oxidized netops_configs a version of a device's configuration mail netops_maintenance a carrier's notice: provider, maintenance id as the reference, its first circuit, window, impact and status, the text as raw A Prometheus connector writes any table whose columns are its query names and key labels: {\"queries\": {\"cpu\": \"...\", \"mem\": \"...\"}, \"key\": [\"device\"]} writes rows {id, ts, device, cpu, mem} ( id the key labels' values and the time), so netops_device_metrics takes them as they are ( rename renames a label: {\"instance\": \"device\"} )."],
["connectors.html#the-kinds-settings","Connectors","The kinds' settings","prometheus : url (the base URL); queries ( {\"column\": \"PromQL\"} ); key (the labels that name a row's subject; default every label but __name__ ); rename ; step_s (default 60); lag_s (how far behind now to stay, default 60, so a step is complete); backfill_s (how far back the first run reads, default 3600); auth ( basic with username and secret password , or bearer with secret token ). A run asks for the steps after the last one it wrote, up to 10,000 steps, and keeps 20,000 records at most: when its series make more, it ends at an earlier step and the next run goes on from there; when one step's series alone are more, the run fails and says so (aggregate them with sum by or topk ). jira : url ; jql ; username (the account's email: Cloud signs in with it and the secret token , an API token; without username , token is sent as a bearer token, as a Data Center personal access token is); api ( cloud or server ; default cloud for *.atlassian.net ); fields ( {\"name\": \"customfield_10050\"} , read into the record's custom ); backfill_days (default 30). The record: key , url , project , issuetype , summary , description (text, from Atlassian's document format too), status , priority , assignee , reporter , labels , components , created , updated , resolution , resolved , due , custom . Since a date in JQL is read in the account's time zone, a run asks for updated >= \"-Nm\" (minutes on Jira's own clock) with a minute or two of overlap. servicenow : instance ; table ; query (an encoded query that narrows them); fields ; username and the secret password (or the secret token as a bearer token); backfill_days (default 30). A record is read with sysparm_display_value=all and kept flat: each field's display value (a choice's label, a reference's name) and, where it differs, its value as <field>_value ; a date-time field's value, which is UTC (its display is in the user's zone). A run asks for sys_updated_on>=javascript:gs.minutesAgoStart(N) , on the instance's own clock. oxidized : url (oxidized-web's); username and secret password if it is behind basic auth; max_nodes (per run, default 200). A pull reads /nodes.json and fetches /node/fetch/<group>/<node> for each node whose last backup is newer than the one it fetched. The record: id ( <node>@<first 12 hex digits of the SHA-256> ), device , ip , model , group , ts , version (the backup's time in seconds, rising per device), lines , sha256 , body . Oxidized's exec hook pushes a node as soon as it is stored: mail : no settings. The body is the raw message (any content type but JSON), or JSON {\"raw\": \"...\"} (or a list of those). Postfix hands a mailbox's mail over with a pipe ( notices: \"|curl -sS -X POST https://.../v1/connectors/carriers/push -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk_...' --data-binary @-\" in /etc/aliases ), fetchmail with --mda , and providers' inbound routes post it. The record: id (the Message-ID), from , sender , sender_domain , to , subject , date , received , body (the first text part, else the HTML part's text), and from a BCOP calendar part provider , account , maint_id , circuits , start , end , impact , status , calendar ; without one, provider is the sender's domain's name. A notice in another format keeps its text in body for a parser that reads it. webhook : records (the path to the list of records in a request; default the body, or its list); preset: \"alertmanager\" (records $.alerts , each alert a row keyed by its fingerprint and start, its labels and annotations as searchable text). In Alertmanager: http_json : url ( {cursor} is replaced by the cursor, URL-encoded); records ; next (the path to the next page's URL, or a path on the same host); cursor (the path in a record whose highest value is the next cursor) and start (the first one); headers ( {secret:NAME} is replaced by a secret); auth , username ."],
["connectors.html#kafka-topics","Connectors","Kafka topics","A kafka connector reads topics into a table while it is on, and writes each event once. Its section on Data > Connectors lists the Kafka connectors with their consumers live: state, events a second, lag (each partition's end less the next offset to write, since there is no consumer group for Kafka's own lag tools to see), events and rows since the server started, replays skipped, events that made no row, and a table of partitions; Start and Stop turn one on and off (kept in its row, so it stays so across restarts), and Add a Kafka connector has a form with a dry run that connects and shows the rows a few events would make. Each event once. Each partition is a stream source named <prefix><topic>/<partition> (prefix kafka/<name>/ unless prefix says another), whose last offset the server keeps in _sources in the same transaction as the rows its events made. A consumer starts each partition after the offset ChloeDB has, not after one committed to Kafka, so a stop, a restart of the server or of a broker, or a coordinator taking over writes no event twice and misses none (a second write of an offset is a replay, and skipped). A partition ChloeDB has no offset of starts at its first event ( \"start\": \"earliest\" , the default) or its end ( \"latest\" ). Another prefix reads the topics again from the start; two connectors with the same prefix share their offsets. Who reads. The server that runs the pulls ( above ) keeps one consumer per connector: a task per partition fetching, and one writer writing the rows in batches through the stream path, as POST /v1/stream does. It commits nothing to Kafka and joins no consumer group. New partitions of its topics are found within 30 seconds and read from their first event. Rows. An event's value is JSON ( \"format\": \"json\" , the default) or text ( \"text\" ). The map reads the event as {topic, partition, offset, ts, key, value, headers} : $.value.x a field of the value, $.key , $.offset , $.ts (milliseconds since 1970; \"as\": \"time\" makes it a time). Without a map, a JSON object's fields are the row; the default table, kafka_events , keeps each event as a row ( id <topic>/<partition>/<offset> , topic , partition_ , offset_ , ts , msg_key , value as JSON, text the value's text, searched, headers ). A keyed table whose row has no key gets <topic>/<partition>/<offset> ; a keyed table's rows are upserted, another's inserted. An event no row comes of (a value that is not JSON, a map that fails) still moves its partition's offset, and is counted with the first reason. A tombstone (an event without a value) writes nothing, or, with \"tombstones\": \"delete\" , deletes the row of its key. When it fails. A broker that cannot be reached, a sign-in refused or a write that fails ends the consumer's session, and the next one starts after a wait that doubles up to a minute, from ChloeDB's offsets again; the connector's health is failing until it streams. Events that the topic's retention deleted before they were read are counted ( lost ) and said in a warning, and the partition goes on from its first event kept. A partition whose offsets went back (a topic emptied or made again) stops the consumer with an error that says so, since its new offsets would be taken for events written already: give the connector another prefix to read it. The settings, in config : brokers (bootstrap brokers, host:port , joined by commas or a list); topics (joined by commas or a list); start ; format ; map ; prefix ; tombstones ( skip or delete ); tls ( true to connect with TLS, checked against the public roots, or against ca_pem , a PEM certificate); sasl ( {\"mechanism\": \"PLAIN\" | \"SCRAM-SHA-256\" | \"SCRAM-SHA-512\", \"username\": \"...\"} , the password the secret password ); allow_internal (the bootstrap brokers are checked as a pull's URL is: a private address, or this machine, only with true ); batch (events written at a time, 1 to 4,096, default 1,000); max_wait_ms (how long a fetch waits for new events, 10 to 10,000, default 500); fetch_kb (1 to 65,536, default 1,024). The API answers a Kafka connector with \"streams\": true and its consumer as stream ; POST /v1/admin/connectors/NAME/run is refused for one (it streams while it is on), and its runs table gets a row per session ( trigger stream , every 10 minutes while it lasts)."],
["connectors.html#the-test-broker","Connectors","The test broker","To try a connector without Kafka, the server has a test broker: a broker in its own process that speaks as much of Kafka's protocol as a consumer needs (and producing, so any Kafka client can write to it), keeps topics in memory (the last 200,000 events of each partition), and writes made-up click events ( {event_id, user, item, action, page, at, price} , keyed by user) into a topic at a rate. On Data > Connectors the Test Kafka broker card starts and stops it, sets the rate, sends an event typed in, and Connect a connector to it fills the form with its address, its topic and a map into demo_clicks . By the API: The API starts it on a loopback address only, and it stops with the server; username and password make it ask clients to sign in with SASL PLAIN. For one other machines reach, run it on its own: chloedb-server kafka-test-broker --listen 0.0.0.0:19092 --advertise test-kafka.internal --topic demo.clicks --rate 20 ( --advertise is the host its clients are told to connect to after the first; --username with CHLOEDB_KAFKA_TEST_PASSWORD for a sign-in). Kafka's own tools that need no consumer group read and write it too: rpk topic consume demo.clicks -X brokers=127.0.0.1:19092 . It is for trying and testing, not a Kafka: one broker, no replication, nothing kept on disk."],
["connectors.html#limits","Connectors","Limits","One run reads 20,000 records at most (a push too: one of more answers 413, and a push's body is 32 MiB at most, or --max-body-mb when that is smaller), and writes them 500 rows (or about 4 MB) at a time; a run that stopped at the limit runs again at once. An http_json pull whose cursor does not move its URL on (no cursor path, or no {cursor} in the URL) keeps the next page's URL in its cursor instead ( {\"next\": URL, \"cursor\": ...} , a secret in it kept as its {secret:NAME} ), so the next run goes on from that page, and the run that reads the last page clears it. A run that fails partway keeps the rows it wrote and its cursor, so the next one reads them again, as upserts. A pull runs for 10 minutes at most; each request to the system waits 60 seconds. Pulls are not yet spread over coordinators or shards, and a connector's runs are one at a time. A Kafka connector's writer writes up to batch events (default 1,000) at a time, and each partition's fetch asks for up to fetch_kb (default 1 MiB); one server reads every partition of its Kafka connectors (no consumer group spreads them over servers). Not yet connectors: IMAP mailboxes (hand the mail over instead) and S3 prefixes watched for new Parquet files."],
["opensearch.html","The OpenSearch API","","chloedb-server --opensearch-listen 127.0.0.1:9200 answers OpenSearch's REST API on a port of its own: an application written for OpenSearch or Elasticsearch (Gitea, Mastodon, a client library) indexes into ChloeDB and searches it without a change. Each index is a ChloeDB table; queries in OpenSearch's Query DSL become SQL."],
["opensearch.html#run-it","The OpenSearch API","Run it","flag environment what --opensearch-listen ADDR CHLOEDB_OPENSEARCH_LISTEN where the API listens (off when not given) --opensearch-version V CHLOEDB_OPENSEARCH_VERSION the version GET / reports, 2.11.0 by default; a 7.x version ( 7.10.2 ) answers as Elasticsearch 7 does, which clients of Elasticsearch 7 check for (elasticsearch-ruby 7.17, which Mastodon uses, refuses OpenSearch 2.x itself) It shares the server's TLS ( --tls-cert ), hosts ( --allow-host ) and credentials. With a token or keys, a request carries one as Authorization: Bearer <token> , or as the password of Basic authentication (any user name), which is how most OpenSearch clients send one: http://user:<token>@host:9200 . Reads need a key of scope read , writes of documents write , and changes to indexes, mappings, aliases and settings admin ; an index made by its first document needs admin too. On a coordinator, or beside one ( --coordinator-listen ), the indexes are tables of the cluster, split over its shards."],
["opensearch.html#tested-with","The OpenSearch API","Tested with","client how result opensearch-py 34 checks: indexes, helpers.bulk , searches, scroll, aggregations, documents, aliases, errors 34 of 34 pass, as they do on OpenSearch 2.11 Gitea 1.24.6 its issue indexer and code indexer set to elasticsearch at this port, next to a second Gitea on OpenSearch 2.11, the same repository and issues in both 42 of 42 searches find the same issues and files Mastodon 4.3 its four indexes (accounts, tags, statuses, public statuses: analyzers, mappings) and the searches its services make (account autocomplete and full search with follower boosts, hashtag search, status search over two indexes), replayed on 1,500 accounts, 1,200 tags and 4,000 statuses next to OpenSearch 2.11 112 of 114 searches return the same hits in the same order; the other two differ in the order of two hits tied at score 0 elasticsearch-ruby 7.17 (Mastodon's client, through Chewy) its product check, index creation, _bulk , a search with script_score works with --opensearch-version 7.10.2 OpenSearch 2.11 itself 100 requests sent to both, answers compared field by field 96 identical; the others are listed under Differences OpenSearch 2.11 on TREC-COVID 3,000 searches of ten kinds over 171,332 abstracts every one finds as many hits; see Numbers for the first pages Gitea's app.ini : Mastodon's .env.production : ES_ENABLED=true , ES_HOST=localhost , ES_PORT=9200 , ES_PRESET=single_node_cluster , and the server started with --opensearch-version 7.10.2 ; then tootctl search deploy ."],
["opensearch.html#what-it-answers","The OpenSearch API","What it answers","APIs : GET / , _cluster/health , _cluster/settings , _cluster/state , _nodes , _cat/indices|aliases|health|count (text with v , h , s , or format=json ), index PUT / GET / HEAD / DELETE (names, patterns with * , _all , comma lists, aliases), _mapping and _settings (read and add), _aliases / _alias , _stats , _refresh , _flush , _forcemerge (accepted; ChloeDB needs none of them), _doc , _create , _update ( doc , upsert , doc_as_upsert , detect_noop ), _source , _mget , _bulk ( index , create , update , delete ), _search , _msearch , _count , _search/scroll , _delete_by_query , _update_by_query (without a script), if_seq_no , version_type=external , refresh , filter_path , pretty , _source filtering, and gzip request bodies. Queries : bool ( must , filter , should with minimum_should_match , must_not ), match ( operator , minimum_should_match , fuzziness , zero_terms_query ), match_phrase , match_phrase_prefix , match_bool_prefix , multi_match ( best_fields , most_fields , cross_fields , phrase , phrase_prefix , bool_prefix , field^boost , field.* ), query_string and simple_query_string (Lucene's syntax: fields, AND / OR / NOT , + / - , phrases, * and ~ ), term , terms , ids , range (with date math: now-7d/d ), exists , prefix , wildcard (a trailing * ), fuzzy , constant_score , dis_max , function_score ( field_value_factor , gauss / exp / linear on numbers and dates, script_score , weight , every score_mode and boost_mode ), script_score , nested (as paths of objects), knn (with filter ), match_all , match_none , and term / terms on _index . Scripts : script_score runs the part of Painless that scoring scripts are written in: numbers, params.x , doc['field'].value of a number or a date, _score , + - * / , Math.log10 , log , log1p , exp , sqrt , pow , abs , max , min , and Painless's saturation , sigmoid and decayNumericGauss|Exp|Linear , with an optional return and ; . Anything else is refused with a script_exception that names what it met. Searches : from / size (to 10,000, or the index's max_result_window ), sort (fields, _score , _doc , missing ), search_after , track_total_hits , post_filter , highlight ( pre_tags , post_tags , fragment_size , number_of_fragments , encoder ), fields / docvalue_fields , version , seq_no_primary_term , collapse (the first hit of each value), scrolls (past 10,000 hits when in the documents' order), and searches of several indexes at once ( a,b/_search , a pattern, an alias of several), which merge their hits in the search's order. Aggregations : terms ( size , order , min_doc_count , missing , include / exclude lists), composite ( terms and date_histogram sources, after ), histogram , date_histogram ( calendar_interval , fixed_interval , min_doc_count , extended_bounds ; in UTC), range , date_range , filter , filters , global , missing , avg , sum , min , max , value_count , cardinality (exact), stats , extended_stats , percentiles , top_hits , nested in each other."],
["opensearch.html#how-an-index-is-kept","The OpenSearch API","How an index is kept","An index books is the table os_books : its key _id , _version , _hash (a hash of the id, the order scrolls page through), and _source , a JSON field holding the document, whose index answers term, range, prefix and exists queries and the aggregations on every keyword, number and boolean, mapped or not. Fields that need more have columns of their own: text a TEXT column, date a TIMESTAMP, knn_vector a VECTOR, and long / double an INT or FLOAT holding the field's smallest value, which scores read. A text field without a keyword sub-field is kept in its column alone, and put back into _source when read. The mappings, settings and aliases are rows of the table opensearch_indices . Dynamic mapping adds fields as OpenSearch does ( text with a keyword sub-field, long , float , boolean , date , objects), adding their columns with ALTER TABLE ; dynamic: false and strict are kept. Text is read by one of two of ChloeDB's analyzers, chosen from the field's: english for OpenSearch's english analyzer or a custom one with a stemmer, simple (lowercased, folded, nothing dropped) for the rest. Before ChloeDB reads it, the text is cut into words as the field's analyzer would: the standard tokenizer keeps merge_segments , mastodon.social , children's , 3.14 , a word with a soft hyphen in it and a word with combining marks, viramas or tone marks ( हिन्दी , ผู้ใหญ่ ) whole, makes each emoji ( ★ , 👩‍💻 , a flag), each ideograph (Chinese, Japanese) and each hiragana a word, and a run of katakana one, and cuts at ² , ¼ , ① , ☆ and ✓ . Each character from U+00A0 to U+1FBFF was compared with OpenSearch 2.11's _analyze : they differ only for a combining mark that follows a space; accents are kept ( sévère is not severe ), unless the analyzer has an asciifolding filter; the english analyzer, and a stemmer filter of possessive_english , drop a word's 's ; whitespace keeps every word between spaces whole, and keyword the whole text; word_delimiter_graph cuts where case or digits change ( CatsOfMastodon2026 is cats of mastodon 2026 ); an edge_ngram or ngram tokenizer or token filter makes the field's column hold the n-grams themselves, each a word, so that a query's words match them and BM25 counts them as Lucene does (on Mastodon's account search, scores agree with OpenSearch's to five digits); a field's search_analyzer cuts the query's text."],
["opensearch.html#differences","The OpenSearch API","Differences","What a request gets that OpenSearch would answer otherwise: Analysis : only what the list above names. Stop words other than English's, synonyms, other languages' stemmers, pattern tokenizers (taken as ChloeDB's words), path_hierarchy , shingle , phonetic filters and normalizers are not applied. Chinese and Japanese are cut a word per ideograph, as the standard tokenizer cuts them, not into the cjk analyzer's pairs or a dictionary's words. Ranking : BM25 with OpenSearch's defaults (k1 1.2, b 0.75) over the same words and the same one-byte lengths, so scores agree to about five digits when the words do, and two documents of nearly equal scores can swap places. Hits of equal scores come in the order of their _id (OpenSearch's in the order they were written), which ChloeDB keeps the same on one server and over shards. A phrase ( match_phrase , or one in query_string ) scores the sum of its words' BM25, where OpenSearch scores how often the phrase itself occurs: phrase searches find the same documents and rank them otherwise. A ranked query lists the rows none of its words scores after the others, with score 0. Queries : wildcard takes a trailing * alone; match_phrase 's slop is ignored; nested queries read the paths of objects, without matching inside one object; regexp , terms lookups, geo_* , more_like_this , percolate , span_* , intervals , rank_feature and random_score are refused, as are per-function filter s and min_score ; a range's time_zone is not applied (dates are UTC). Searches : aggregations read one index at a time; collapse takes no inner_hits ; point-in-time searches are refused; track_scores is ignored (a field sort scores nothing); a scroll ordered by score or a field is limited to 10,000 hits. Aggregations : composite returns at most 9,999 groups a page; cardinality is exact; a histogram of a number field that some documents hold an array of is refused; scripts are refused. Documents : a keyword holds at most 256 bytes as a term; _source holds at most 1,000 values, objects 16 deep and paths of 256 bytes (a document past them is refused, naming the limit); a date field holds one date; versions are read before the write, so two writers of one document at once can both pass an if_seq_no check. Cluster : one node and one shard are reported; settings other than max_result_window and the analysis are kept and ignored. A write is searchable once it returns, so refresh is accepted and needs no wait."],
["opensearch.html#numbers","The OpenSearch API","Numbers","OpenSearch 2.11 (its default 1 GB heap) and ChloeDB's OpenSearch API on the same machine, one at a time, the other stopped: a cloud container of 4 vCPUs (Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz) and 15.7 GB, 2026-11-08. BEIR's TREC-COVID (171,332 abstracts) in one index, written with _bulk , then ten kinds of search 300 times each from one client, the same requests to both; OpenSearch's request cache off. The script, its output and how it was run: chloedb-server/results/opensearch . search OpenSearch p50 / p99 ms ChloeDB p50 / p99 ms match , one word 2.96 / 8.06 1.70 / 3.06 match , a topic's words 6.41 / 18.99 2.22 / 4.79 match + term + range filters 9.47 / 31.87 3.69 / 7.57 multi_match 6.89 / 30.16 4.10 / 9.75 match_phrase 2.97 / 5.84 1.86 / 4.86 term filter 2.00 / 7.00 1.49 / 2.23 range , sorted by date 2.96 / 6.46 1.52 / 2.44 terms aggregation 6.82 / 18.93 4.30 / 5.56 date_histogram of every document 9.73 / 15.50 6.71 / 8.80 match + highlight 7.69 / 24.84 3.27 / 5.66 OpenSearch ChloeDB searches a second, 8 clients 638 1,183 _bulk of the 171,332 documents 21.4 s 39.0 s resident memory, empty 1,348 MiB 50 MiB resident memory, after the searches 1,652 MiB 726 MiB on disk 415 MB (195 MB after a _flush ) 260 MB All 3,000 searches find as many hits on both. Of the 2,400 that return hits, 808 return the same first page in the same order, and 1,292 the same scores or dates in the same places, with ties in another order (ChloeDB's by _id ); the 300 phrase searches find the same documents and rank them otherwise ( Differences )."],
["compatibility.html","Compatibility","","What this code promises about the files it reads and writes, the protocol its servers speak to each other, and the names it was known by before; and what it does not promise yet. It describes the code as it is on main (November 2026, every crate and package at version 0.3.7 but the early Workbench, chloedb-workbench , at 0.1.0). The changelog says what changed. ChloeDB is pre-1.0: the statements below are the current behaviour, which the code enforces, not a support commitment with a time limit."],
["compatibility.html#the-database-file","Compatibility","The database file","A database is one file (or, with directory , a directory with a CURRENT file naming its manifest). Its format has two layers. Format version. The file's meta slots carry a format version ( FORMAT_VERSION in chloedb/src/storage/pager.rs , now 3): format what it is 1 a database holding only the table it was created with 2 a database of several tables 3 format 2 with the list of features the database uses; the last format number: later changes add features instead This code opens formats 1 to 3 and refuses a higher number (\"unsupported format version\"). Features. A format-3 manifest lists the features it uses, {required: [...], optional: [...]} ( chloedb/src/storage/manifest.rs ). A reader refuses a database that requires a feature it does not know, and names the feature; it ignores optional features and keys it does not know. This code knows: added_fields , bool , cluster_by , cold_segments , compact_keys , default , derive , derive_keyword , derive_text , edge_table , expire_by , field_ids , functions , json , json_text , media , multi_vector , not_null , packed_positions , rank_profiles , rabitq , retain , semantic_id , seq_no , shard_by , sources , sparse_embed , substring:1 , timestamp , vector_partition and zstd (the list FEATURES in manifest.rs is the authority), and analyzer:<name>:<version> for each analyzer but english ( Analyzer::feature in chloedb/src/text/analyzer.rs ). 0.3.0 added analyzer:<name>:1 for its new analyzers ( simple 's, from before, is unchanged), substring:1 (a substring index), sparse_embed (a SPARSE field a model fills) and derive_keyword (a KEYWORD field derived from a text). Each is written only by a database that uses it: a 0.2.x build refuses such a database by name and opens every other database 0.3.0 wrote. What it writes. Every database this code creates is format 3 with feature field_ids (each field named by an id rather than its position). Other features are added as a database starts using them (a BOOL or TIMESTAMP field, a NOT NULL field or a DEFAULT, sequence numbers with --seq-no , a rank profile, ...). So a database stays readable by an older build exactly as long as it uses only features that build knows, and an older build that meets a newer feature refuses the file by name rather than misreading it. Starting to use a feature cannot be undone. Rollups. A rollup made by 0.3.3 or later keys its rows by number, where one made by 0.3.1 or 0.3.2 keys them by text. 0.3.3 reads and refreshes both. 0.3.2 reads the new kind but cannot refresh it, so its definition carries the option needs_chloedb_0_3_3 , which 0.3.2 refuses by name (\"no option needs_chloedb_0_3_3\"): a refresh by 0.3.2 fails with that message rather than leaving the rollup's newest buckets stale. Open a database whose rollups 0.3.3 made with 0.3.3 or later. A rollup made by 0.3.4 or later that counts a field's values ( COUNT(f) , or AVG(f) , which keeps count_f so that its mean skips NULLs) has a column 0.3.3 does not write; its definition carries needs_chloedb_0_3_4 , which 0.3.3 refuses by name in the same way. 0.3.4 refreshes a rollup made before with AVG(f) as it was made, without count_f . Databases from before field ids. A database written before field ids (format 1, 2, or 3 without field_ids ) no longer opens for use. upgrade() ( chloedb.upgrade(path) in Python, upgrade(path) in JavaScript; chloedb/src/upgrade.rs ) copies it into a new database of this format in its place and keeps the old files beside it as <path>.before-field-ids . It refuses a database that numbers its writes (replication) or keeps stream offsets ( _sources ), since the copy could not continue their numbers: such a database is rebuilt from its source or from a replica instead. Not promised. There is no downgrade: a file a newer build changed may not open in an older one. There is no stated period for which an old format stays readable by new builds; formats 1 to 3 and the upgrade() path are what this code has, and a change to that will be in the changelog."],
["compatibility.html#the-packages-and-the-engine","Compatibility","The packages and the engine","The Python wheel, the JavaScript addon and the Java library each compile the engine ( chloedb/ ) into themselves from the same commit. A package version therefore opens what the engine of its commit opens (above), no more: a wheel built from an older commit refuses a file that uses a feature added since, by name. The Java jar checks that its native library is from the same checkout ( Native.ABI ) and refuses a mismatched pair. The HTTP clients ( chloedb.connect in Python, chloedb/client in JavaScript) contain no engine: they speak the server's /v1 HTTP JSON API and work with any server that has the routes they call. A client newer than its server may send an option the server does not know, and an older server may ignore it rather than refuse it (one from before Arrow answers ignores \"format\": \"arrow\" ): the clients detect and fall back where their documentation says so (Arrow answers, scrolls), and otherwise assume a server of their own commit. Not promised. A package does not yet expose the engine commit or the format version it was built with, and nothing checks that a client and a server are of the same release. Every crate and package carries the version of the last release (0.3.7; chloedb-workbench its own, 0.1.0), and keeps it between releases: name a build from a commit after a release by its commit."],
["compatibility.html#a-cluster-of-mixed-versions","Compatibility","A cluster of mixed versions","Servers of a cluster (coordinators, shards, replicas, followers) talk over gRPC with a numbered shard protocol ( chloedb-server/src/grpc.rs : MIN_PROTOCOL 1, PROTOCOL 21; the list of what each adds ). When a coordinator or a peer connects it calls Hello with the range it speaks (1 to 21) and the server answers its own range, the plan version it reads (plan IR version 1) and the plan features it has. The two use the highest protocol both speak; if the ranges do not meet the connection is refused, naming both ranges. A coordinator asks again every minute and after a copy fails, since it may have restarted on another version. A coordinator keeps each shard copy's protocol, speaks the oldest any of them speaks, and uses a newer call only where every server it needs speaks it. Where one does not, it refuses that request by name (\"a coordinator refuses a scroll while a shard speaks an older one\") or falls back (rows instead of Arrow columns; a read sent to the primary when a replica is older than protocol 5) rather than sending a call the shard would misread. A server refuses a protocol newer than its own, and a plan of a newer version or one that needs a feature it lacks, by name. 0.3.0 added the plan feature terms_syntax , which only a search with query expansion ( bm25(.., expand => n) ) uses: a 0.2.x shard refuses such a search by name, so upgrade the shards before using it. 0.3.1 added the plan features asof_join ( ASOF JOIN ), date_bin (a GROUP BY of date_bin or date_trunc run on the columns) and approx_percentiles ( approx_percentile ): a 0.3.0 shard refuses a query that uses one by name, so upgrade the shards first here too. 0.3.2 moved the shard protocol to 21 (write receipts' counts, strict writes, insert_if_absent ) and added the plan feature full_join ( FULL JOIN ): a coordinator counts and writes strictly only through shards of 21, and a 0.3.1 shard refuses a full join by name, so upgrade the shards before the coordinator. 0.3.4 keeps the shard protocol at 21 and adds no plan feature: the terms a read after a write passes back ( min_seq_term , min_seq_terms ) are new fields that a 0.3.3 server leaves out, reading by the number alone, so 0.3.3 and 0.3.4 mix in either order. A key's statement budget is kept by 0.3.4 only: a 0.3.3 server admits the key without it, so give keys budgets once every server runs 0.3.4. 0.3.5 keeps the shard protocol at 21 and adds no plan feature and no field, so 0.3.4 and 0.3.5 mix in either order. One step is ordered: a 0.3.5 coordinator of a cluster with a cluster token adds a server (a shard, a copy, or a plan's step that adds one) only with that token, and answers an API key or the admin token 403 cluster_token_required . On Kubernetes, upgrade the operator before the coordinators: the 0.3.5 operator adds servers with its clusterTokenSecret , and an older one sends the admin token. 0.3.6 keeps the shard protocol at 21 and adds no plan feature and no field, and a slot move's flip from a replicated shard waits for a mark that the shard's primary writes through its log: a replica of it takes the flip only once it applied that mark. A primary of 0.3.5 or earlier writes none, so a 0.3.6 replica under one refuses that flip and the maps after it, until a later flip from the shard is marked or the replica is replaced. Finish the upgrade before moving slots. 0.3.7 keeps the shard protocol at 21 and adds no plan feature and no field, so 0.3.6 and 0.3.7 mix in either order. So a rolling upgrade works shard by shard: while versions are mixed, the cluster serves what the oldest server involved speaks, and the newer features turn on as the last server is upgraded (some, such as slot maps, only once the coordinator restarts after that). A replica is sent copies of its primary's rows and files, so the file-format rules above apply between them too: a replica older than its primary refuses a feature the primary started using. Upgrading the replicas of a shard before its primary avoids that. Not promised. There is no stated lowest version a cluster can be upgraded from in one step, and mixed-version clusters are tested for the protocol fallbacks above, not as a matrix of releases."],
["compatibility.html#old-names","Compatibility","Old names","ChloeDB was called Strata, and ChloeCtx was called Engram, until November 2026. The old names below are still accepted, and are promised \"for a release\": they will be removed in a release that the changelog names in advance. what new name old name, still accepted programs chloedb-server , chloedb-crawl , chloedb-sheets strata-server , strata-crawl , strata-sheets , in the image and the release archives container image ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-server ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/strata-server settings CHLOEDB_* STRATA_* the server an app writes to chloedb-crawl --server URL , chloedb-sheets --server URL (0.3.2) --strata URL models folder ~/.local/share/chloedb/models (0.3.2) ~/.local/share/strata/models , used where it exists and the new folder does not HTTP headers and gRPC metadata x-chloedb-* x-strata-* Python package import chloedb , ChloeDBError import strata , StrataError (with a DeprecationWarning ) JavaScript errors ChloeDBError StrataError JDBC URLs jdbc:chloedb: jdbc:strata: ChloeCtx program chloectx engram , a link to it ChloeCtx settings CHLOECTX_* ENGRAM_* repository chloetech-ai/chloedb chloetech-ai/strata and rkonow/engram , which GitHub redirects website https://chloetech-ai.github.io/chloedb/ the old address does not redirect For the server in particular: when both a CHLOEDB_* setting and its STRATA_* name are set, the new one wins; an answer carries its headers under both names ( x-chloedb-took-ms and x-strata-took-ms ); the browser UI moves what it saved under strata.* to chloedb.* the first time it loads; the MCP server introduces itself as chloedb , and the OpenSearch API's cluster is named chloedb . The Kubernetes operator's pods start the programs by their old names, with STRATA_* settings, which every image understands. The metrics were renamed outright, from strata_* to chloedb_* : a dashboard over servers from before and after the rename can match {__name__=~\"(chloedb|strata)_.*\"} . So were the alert messages, which begin [chloedb FIRING ...] rather than [strata FIRING ...] . Some names have not been renamed yet and keep the old spelling: the file format ( STRATADB magic, ChloeCtx's memory.strata and engram.sock ), the gRPC services, the Kubernetes operator's resources (the StrataCluster kind, the strata.chloetech.ai API group, the strata-system namespace), and the app engine in chloedb-apps/ . The default models directory is ~/.local/share/chloedb/models from 0.3.2 ( ~/.local/share/strata/models before); a machine that has the old folder and no ~/.local/share/chloedb keeps using it, as a machine that already has ChloeCtx's old folder, ~/.local/share/engram , does."],
["compatibility.html#experimental-features","Compatibility","Experimental features","These parts of chloedb-server work and are documented, but carry no compatibility promise yet: their routes, settings and what they store may change in any release, including a patch release, with no upgrade path promised. Each answer of their routes carries the header x-chloedb-experimental: <feature> , GET /v1/catalog marks their routes ( \"experimental\": \"<feature>\" , and the features under experimental ), and the command center marks their pages. feature routes what it is cloud /v1/admin/cloud/* , /v1/admin/aws/* , /v1/admin/digitalocean/* , /v1/admin/gcp/* clusters made on AWS, DigitalOcean or Google Cloud from the command center hub /v1/admin/hub/* , /c/<name>/ the hub : a book of other clusters, and calls forwarded to them autoscale /v1/admin/kube/autoscale/* , /v1/admin/autoscale/* , the StrataCluster 's spec.autoscale the operator's autoscaler oncall /v1/admin/oncall/* the on-call agent The list is EXPERIMENTAL in chloedb-server/src/catalog.rs . A feature leaves it when its shape is settled; the changelog says so."],
["compatibility.html#demos","Compatibility","Demos","These apps show what ChloeDB can do and are not part of ChloeDB's promises ( chloedb-netops is attached prebuilt to the server's release, as the crawler and Sheets are, but carries no promise of its own): chloedb-netops , network operations on a simulated carrier network; chloedb-hn , Hacker News searched, asked in sentences and completed as typed; chloedb-arxiv , every arXiv paper with search, the recommender and feeds; chloedb-gh , GitHub's public history since 2011: repos by what they do, why they trend, what people use instead; chloedb-sound , free sounds searched by how they sound, with the moments inside long recordings; chloedb-backbone , the internet's routing from public data: what is wrong now, where, and how it connects; chloedb-swarm , simulated users, an internal test instrument; examples/feature-store , a shop ranked on features ChloeDB keeps; examples/parley , a social network of LLM agents. They are on a release train of their own. CI's required check, CI result , does not test them, and a change only under them runs nothing there; the Demos workflow ( .github/workflows/demos.yml ) tests them against a chloedb-server of the same commit, on a pull request that touches one and every night. A fault in a demo does not hold a ChloeDB release, and fixing one does not wait for the engine's tests. An app whose manifest says \"maturity\": \"demo\" is marked as a demo in the command center."],
["compatibility.html#not-promised-yet","Compatibility","Not promised yet","Semantic versioning, or any version bump rule. A support period for releases; fixes go to main and the next release ( SECURITY.md ). That a release tag is never rebuilt: the release workflows replace the assets of an existing tag ( --clobber ). Check a download against the release's SHA256SUMS (Python, JavaScript) or .sha256 (server), or PyPI's attestations ( checking what you download ). Signed release archives, or an SBOM. Stability of anything not described above: the Rust crate's API, the command center's routes, the operator's resource beyond its documented fields."],
["chloectx.html","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","","ChloeCtx gives Claude Code a memory that outlives its sessions. It records every prompt, answer, tool call and compaction summary of a project into a ChloeDB file on your machine, has a small model write down the decisions and rules each turn agreed, and brings the relevant ones back when a session starts and with each prompt, without the model having to ask. The model also gets tools to search that history itself. This page starts with what you install and use day to day. How it works follows, for those who want the design: what it stores, when it brings history back, and how it ranks."],
["chloectx.html#install-it","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Install it","ChloeCtx runs on macOS and Linux (on Windows, inside WSL 2), and needs the Claude Code CLI, logged in ( claude auth status shows \"loggedIn\": true ). The quickest way is the Claude Code plugin, which downloads a prebuilt chloectx on its first session; in Claude Code: Or install it from a checkout, for every session or for one project: Then start a new Claude Code session: a session that was already open has neither the hooks nor the memory tools. chloectx doctor , run in a project's folder, checks the setup and says how to fix each line that failed. A project install covers that one folder: a git worktree of the same repository is a folder of its own and needs its own chloectx install (they then share one store), or install for every session instead. Install ChloeCtx has every route (the plugin's details, a prebuilt binary, remote machines and dev containers), what each one changes in Claude Code's settings, upgrades and removal."],
["chloectx.html#what-happens-on-its-own","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","What happens on its own","Once it is installed there is nothing to run. In each session: At the start , Claude gets a briefing of at most 6,000 characters: the rules first, then decisions, planned work with its requirements, and facts. It is the same text from one session to the next while the notes are, so Claude Code's prompt cache holds it; what changes (how many records, the open tasks, where the last session stopped) comes with the first prompt, and in the briefing itself when a session is resumed ( claude --continue , --resume ) or compacted. With each prompt , up to four notes that match the prompt come with it, and earlier records about it when the evidence is strong enough. Once the store holds 20 records, a small model also searches memory for the prompt in the background; what it finds comes with the turn's next tool result or with the next prompt, so a quick question answered without a tool call is answered before it arrives. After each turn , a small model ( haiku by default) writes down the decisions, rules, facts and planned work the turn agreed. When Claude decides to , it calls the memory tools: memory_ask , memory_search , memory_timeline , memory_sql , memory_remember and memory_forget . ChloeCtx's own model calls run through claude -p and are billed to the CLI's login: about $0.003 a turn in all with Haiku once the store holds 20 records, none of it holding up a turn ( what each call costs ). chloectx doctor shows what your own store measured over the last 30 days. A rule is only as good as the note it became. The briefing shows the newest 40 rules, 12 decisions, 10 items of planned work and 10 facts while its 6,000 characters last, each note whole up to 600 characters (a fact up to 300), so a project with many long notes can push the oldest out. The rules come under a line that tells Claude to follow them and, when a request would break one, to ask you first rather than take the request as lifting the rule. A rule that must never be missed is safest as its own short note ( chloectx remember preference \"Never suggest recurring todos in v1\" ), or in CLAUDE.md , which Claude Code always loads."],
["chloectx.html#use-it-from-a-shell","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Use it from a shell","Run these in a project's folder: except ui and stores , which cover every store, each one works on that project's store. chloectx ask prints the context a model would be given, not a written answer. Three commands are for maintenance and cost model calls: chloectx ingest-transcript ~/.claude/projects/<project>/<session>.jsonl back-fills a session ChloeCtx did not record (one it recorded live is stored a second time); chloectx distill writes down the notes of the turns not distilled yet, one model call per turn ( --again distills every turn again but a forget's, --dry-run counts them without a model call); chloectx consolidate merges overlapping notes now. distill and consolidate print one line of JSON when they are done (for distill : the turns, how many it distilled, and the notes it wrote). A capsule hands a slice of this memory to another agent as one read-only file it queries: capsules . Blame and receipts explain chloectx blame ."],
["chloectx.html#where-the-time-went","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Where the time went","chloectx report reads the store and writes nothing. For each session of the last --days days (7 by default; --session picks one by its id or the start of it) and in all, it splits the time between the records into: share from to model a prompt, or a tool's result the next tool call, or the answer tools a tool call its result (while any call runs) waiting an answer, or a notification (a permission prompt, an idle notice) the next prompt; one wait counts up to 30 minutes, the rest is away , counted apart compaction a compaction's start its summary Anything that ends in a prompt is waiting (a turn the user interrupted, which has no answer). A permission wait lasts until the tool's result, so it includes the tool's own run after the user answered. Records keep Unix seconds, so every span is whole seconds, and a tool call has a length only when its record says how long it took: calls under a second, and calls recorded before ChloeCtx timed them, count as instants. Then: the commands that took most time, normalised as error to fix matches them ( cd x && cargo test 2>&1 | tail is cargo test ), with their runs, time per run and failures; time by tool; errors seen three or more times, by signature, with the session they came back most often in; files read four or more times in one session; compactions per hour of use. Last, up to five suggestions, each with the numbers behind it: a slow command run often, a loop on one error, a file reread often, permissions waited on often, frequent compactions. --json prints the same as JSON."],
["chloectx.html#where-it-keeps-what-it-stores","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Where it keeps what it stores","Each project's store is a folder outside the repository, because transcripts can contain secrets: ~/.local/share/chloectx/projects/<folder>-<hash>/ (under $CHLOECTX_HOME/projects/ when that is set, and in ~/.local/share/engram/projects/ on a machine that has that folder from before the rename). It holds the ChloeDB file memory.strata and its write-ahead log, state.json (each session's live window and last prompts), events.log (the recall, search and distillation decisions), receipts.strata ( receipts ) and snapshots/ . chloectx doctor names the store of the folder it runs in. A repository has one store, whichever of its git worktrees a session runs in: the store of a linked worktree (a folder whose .git file points at <repo>/.git/worktrees/<name> ) is that of the same folder in the main checkout, so agents in different worktrees read and add to one memory, through one daemon. A main checkout and a folder outside git keep the store they always had. Each session remembers the folder it runs in (in the store's session state, and in events.log with its branch): its paths are stored relative to it, and notes tied to the code are checked against its files, not the main checkout's. CHLOECTX_PER_WORKTREE=1 gives each worktree its own store again. A redaction pass replaces keys, tokens, private keys, passwords in URLs and headers, and values assigned to names such as DB_PASSWORD with markers before anything is stored or synced; a password written in prose is not caught. chloectx redact applies it to a store written before ( what is redacted ). Treat a store like your shell history."],
["chloectx.html#forget-something","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Forget something","chloectx forget (and the model's memory_forget ) deletes what ChloeCtx stored, for good: read Forget something in the install guide for what it matches and what it leaves before you use it."],
["chloectx.html#settings","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Settings","Set these in the environment Claude Code starts in, or in the env of its settings. variable effect CHLOECTX_DISABLE=1 turns the hooks off: nothing is recorded or recalled. The memory tools stay; start Claude with --disallowedTools mcp__chloectx to leave them out of a session too CHLOECTX_PER_WORKTREE=1 a store for each git worktree, instead of one for the repository CHLOECTX_HOME , CHLOECTX_MODELS relocate the store and the models CHLOECTX_DISTILL=0 turns distillation off (or \"distill\": {\"enabled\": false} in the store's config.json ) CHLOECTX_DISTILL_MODEL the model the distiller and consolidation use ( install --distill-model writes it into the settings) CHLOECTX_DEEP query engine mode: off , tool , async (default), sync CHLOECTX_DEEP_MODEL , CHLOECTX_DEEP_PLANNER_MODEL , CHLOECTX_DEEP_RANKER_MODEL , CHLOECTX_DEEP_HARD_MODEL the query engine's models (default haiku ; the hard model is unset) CHLOECTX_DEEP_PLAN , CHLOECTX_DEEP_RANK none skips the planner; local ranks without a model call (defaults: model , model ) CHLOECTX_CONSOLIDATE=0 turns consolidation off CHLOECTX_SESSION_CONTEXT=1 recall also searches with the session's last records ( session context ; or \"rank\": {\"tail\": {\"enabled\": true}} in the store's config.json ) CHLOECTX_NEXT_FILES=1 before an edit, also the files usually edited next, with a note on each ( when to load ; or \"next_files\": true in the store's config.json ) CHLOECTX_ERROR_FIXES=0 turns error to fix off ( when to load ; or \"error_fixes\": false in the store's config.json ) CHLOECTX_EMBEDDER static (default), bert , or hash:<dim> for a model-free hash embedder Each is still read under its name from before the rename, ENGRAM_* , when the new one is not set."],
["chloectx.html#how-it-works-the-problem","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","How it works: the problem","A long session on a complex project outgrows any context window. Claude Code then compacts it into a summary of about 15,000 characters. Summaries keep the plan and lose the particulars: exact numbers, error messages and file paths; the reason an approach was rejected; a preference the user stated once, three hours earlier. A new session starts with only CLAUDE.md and the auto-memory index. The session that built ChloeDB ran for 32 hours and wrote 43.7 MB of transcript across two compactions; its summaries hold under 0.1% of that text. Three requirements follow: Store without being asked. Capture must not depend on the model remembering to save. Load without being asked, but only when it helps. Injected context costs tokens and attention, and irrelevant context hurts accuracy. Injection must be gated, budgeted, and must skip what the model can already see. Let the model ask precisely. When the model knows it needs history, it gets search, timeline and SQL tools over the same store."],
["chloectx.html#architecture","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Architecture","Only one process at a time may write a ChloeDB file, and hooks are short-lived processes that run concurrently with the MCP server. So one daemon per project owns the database, and hooks and the MCP server are its clients over a Unix socket. Lazy start. The first client to find no socket spawns the daemon under a spawn lock; the daemon exits after 30 idle minutes. Its first hook takes 50–72 ms, because the 129 MB embedding table is memory-mapped rather than read. Warm state. The embedder, segment readers and the memtable stay in memory between hooks. Reads in parallel, one writer. Reads run on each client's own thread against a snapshot, any number at once, and the searches behind one prompt share that snapshot and run side by side while cores are free. Every write goes to one writer thread, which commits whatever is queued in one transaction and builds the new version before the write returns, so a reader never waits for a write or for the index build after it. In-memory chunks hold 256 records and scan their vectors instead of building an HNSW graph, so that build stays cheap. A full memtable becomes a segment, and segments merge, on ChloeDB's background threads, not on the writer's: with 150,000 appends back to back in the cloud container (4-core Xeon), the longest took 0.2–0.4 s, against 12–15 s when the writer flushed and merged itself ( results ). Never in the way. A client that cannot reach the daemon within its deadline prints nothing and exits 0. A memory failure never blocks a prompt or a tool call. The store lives outside the repository, one folder per project, and a repository's worktrees share one ( where ). A redaction pass replaces secrets with markers before anything is stored or synced ( what is redacted )."],
["chloectx.html#the-data-model","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","The data model","One ChloeDB table, memory . Each record is one key–value pair with attributes. field type meaning id KEYWORD, primary key <session-prefix>:<seq> session KEYWORD Claude Code session id kind KEYWORD prompt , answer , tool_call , tool_result , error , episode , summary , decision , preference , fact , todo , note , entity , event tag KEYWORD tool name, summary section, distilled , planned , superseded , … turn , seq , ts INT user turn, project-wide sequence number, Unix seconds importance FLOAT salience prior in [0, 1]; for an entity, how many records link to it files TEXT paths the record touches (path components are terms) text TEXT, stored the value: one chunk of at most 400 bytes, injected as is ctx TEXT, stored the turn's request (≤ 200 characters); for notes also the situations they matter in ( When: ) and the code they were written against ( Anchor: ); part of the lexical key, never shown body TEXT, generated from text + ctx the lexical key that bm25 and MATCH search emb VECTOR(d), HNSW the dense key: an embedding of text alone (for notes, text and its When: situations) links EDGES edges to the turn a record came from, the files it touches ( f:path ) and the entities it is about ( e:name ), which are records of kind entity Keys are small and precise. A 400-byte window matches a question better than a 1,500-byte chunk in which the answer is one fact among many, and a context budget holds about ten such windows instead of five snippets. The turn's request joins the lexical key, so that a terse tool result (\"test result: ok\") is found by the words of the task it served; it stays out of the vector, where it would pull every response towards its request. The study behind these choices is in the results . Records come in three levels: The kind sets a salience prior ι: preference 1.0, decision 0.9, summary 0.8, prompt 0.7, error and episode 0.6, answer 0.5, tool call and result 0.3."],
["chloectx.html#when-to-store-always","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","When to store: always","Storage is cheap (a durable commit costs about 4 ms on a Mac, where it flushes the drive's write cache, and runs off the hook's path) and a missed record cannot be recovered later. hook stored SessionStart session registered; live window opened UserPromptSubmit prompt , plus preference records for cue sentences (\"always\", \"never\", \"from now on\", …) PostToolUse tool_call and tool_result (or error ), asynchronously; a call that took a second or more says how long ( Took: 12s in its context, timed from its message in the transcript) Stop the answer, the commentary written between tool calls, the turn's episode , then distilled knowledge PreCompact commentary not yet captured; an event ( compact_start ) PostCompact the summary, by section; the live window restarts here Notification an event tagged notification:<type> ( permission_prompt , idle_prompt , ...) SubagentStop an event tagged subagent_stop SessionEnd flush Events are records of when something happened rather than of what was said: a permission prompt, an idle notice, a built-in sub-agent that finished, a compaction that started. They are never injected or returned by search, recall or a timeline unless asked for by kind; chloectx report reads them. chloectx ingest-transcript back-fills old sessions from Claude Code's JSONL transcripts."],
["chloectx.html#distilled-knowledge","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Distilled knowledge","Raw records hold what was said ; a later session needs what was agreed : a decision and its reason, a client's rule, the requirements of work announced for later. Much of it never reaches the code, and a request that depends on it rarely sounds like earlier work, so search alone misses it. After each Stop , off the prompt's path, the daemon gives the turn (request, commands, reply) to a small model through claude -p , with up to 40 live notes for context: the ones most related to the turn wherever they are in the history, then the newest. Seeing the related ones is what lets the model update a note written weeks ago instead of adding a near-copy next to it. The prompt names the day the turn took place, so a relative date in it (\"tomorrow\", \"next Tuesday\") is counted from that day even when the turn is distilled later ( chloectx distill , a backlog). The model returns JSON lines: item stored as tag decision, with the reason decision distilled rule, constraint, convention preference distilled requirement of work planned but not done todo planned fact about the project or its environment, failed approaches fact distilled an instruction for the task at hand (\"don't touch cart.py\") note , kept to its session task replaces: id the old item is retagged, and names its successor ( superseded_by ) superseded done: id the planned item is retagged done retired: id , a noted item about work the turn scrapped, reverted or dropped (\"scrap it\", git restore ) replaced by the turn's note that says so (the one sharing most of its words), else retagged superseded , else retired A new item whose analysed terms overlap an existing one by a Jaccard index J ≥ 0.6 is dropped. A note is superseded only by a stored note that names it and shares at least 30% of its words; when the replacement was dropped or is about something else, the old note stays live. A decision the prompt stated, kept at once in the user's words (below), is superseded by the turn's distilled decision or rule that holds at least half of its words, so one decision is one note. Retired and superseded notes leave the briefing and recall. What the user only asks about or assumes is not the project's: \"write a function for a 2.5% fee, code in the reply only\" does not make 2.5% its fee, and an answer the assistant gave from what is noted already adds nothing. Who said it. Every item says whether the request stated it or the assistant found it, and the record keeps its source in a By: line: user , workbench (a brief Workbench wrote), agent:<name> (another agent's brief or message) or assistant[:<name>] . Only the user makes rules. A rule the user stated is a preference every session is told unless it is an instruction for the task at hand: the model kept it to the task, or the part of the request it came from tells the agent what not to touch or only to do now (\"Don't touch money.py or cart.py\", \"Only change totals.py\", \"for now\", \"in this PR\"). A statement of how the project works (\"Fee calculations use ROUND_HALF_UP, because the regulator audits them\"), a client's or ops' convention (\"Some context for this project, from the kickoff with the client: …\", \"Two things from ops: …\") and anything the user said is standing (\"always\", \"never\", \"from now on\", \"by default\", \"everywhere\", a convention, a list after words that announce rules such as \"A few ground rules before we start: …\" or \"Conventions:\", but not \"rules for this task\", or an answer to the assistant's question asking for one) is a rule; when it is unclear, a rule, which you can retire, rather than a note no other session sees. Only a rule has a scope: a decision, requirement or fact the model calls \"for this task\" is kept as the project's. An instruction for the task, and anything an agent or Workbench told an agent to do, becomes a note for that session's task, which other sessions, the briefing and recall never see. A decision or fact another agent stated, or a Workbench agent concluded, reads \"The agent lead said: …\"; it is that agent's claim, not a fact. A person's prompt that states a decision (\"My decision: …\", \"We decided …\") is kept at once as the user's decision, whether or not the distiller runs: its line, up to a sentence that asks for something else (\"Then run …\"). A note the agent keeps with memory_remember is the user's when the agent says so ( said_by: user ) or, when it does not, when the turn's prompt says most of its words; otherwise it is the assistant's. Workbench tells ChloeCtx who wrote each prompt with CHLOECTX_PROMPT_SOURCE (the session's task), CHLOECTX_PROMPT_LOG (later prompts it typed) and CHLOECTX_AGENT_NAME ; a prompt that is a brief relayed to the session ( <coordinator-relay> , <relay from=\"NAME\"> , or one that opens \"The coordinator sent a message while you were working:\" or \"Another Claude session sent a message:\") is that agent's ( coordinator , NAME, another-session ); a prompt nothing names is the user's. Said again. An instruction the user gives and has to give again, later in the session or in another one, is a rule after all: the second time, it becomes a preference tagged repeated that every session is given (\"Build apps from the CREATE statements (the user has said this in 2 sessions)\", or \"... 2 times\" within one session), linked to what it repeats and superseding the notes among them. Which earlier sayings a request says again is a model's to judge, in a call of its own after each of the user's turns: it is shown the user's earlier requests and instructions most like this one, from this session and others (prompts, 6 by the vectors and 6 by BM25, and notes for a task and rules, 8 and 8), and the agent's reply before it. A request says one again when it asks for the same thing in any words, or complains that it still is not done (\"why does it still say Strata?\"); one that only shares a subject, asks for something else or changes its mind does not, nor a question that asks what holds (\"What error code do we return when a client retries a payment?\", \"When can I deploy to staging?\"): that asks memory, and a mark on it is dropped even when the model makes one, unless the question complains (\"didn't I say …?\", \"why does it still …?\") or asks for it (\"could you always …?\"). Nothing of the request's own turn is an earlier saying (the preference picked from its words, the notes an earlier distillation of it wrote), nor anything stored after it; nor is the same request sent again word for word (a scripted run), or a format the user pastes onto each task in the same words (six words or more, word for word in each earlier prompt). The model quotes the request's words that say it again and writes the rule; a mark whose quote is not in the request is dropped, because a model given a request with little in it copies what it was shown. A correction is reworded more than words or vectors follow (in a week of real sessions an instruction and its saying again scored a cosine of 0.05 to 0.57, different instructions up to 0.73). An instruction that says what a rule already says (one kept this way, or one the user gave as a rule) means the rule did not hold, and the rule is written anew: in the newest words, counted once more, and with the situations ( when ), files and entities of both, so it is the newest rule and is recalled in this situation from now on. It counts the sessions and times it was said, the times it was said again once kept, and of those the times the session's context already held it when the prompt came ( Sessions: , Said: , Said again: , Said again after it was shown: ): shown and said again, the agent broke a rule it had; not shown, the rule was missing when it mattered. Only the user's own words count. chloectx repeats [--days 30] [--json] lists, without a model and without writing, the rules said again and what the store holds that was said again in nearly the same words; --judge FILE asks the model about cases of real sayings and counts the repeats it finds. Distillation costs about $0.0013 a turn and 8 s with a small model, and the check of what the user said again about $0.0005 more for each of the user's turns; neither delays a prompt. Each note also carries the situations it will matter in ( when ), written at the same time: \"choosing a library or standard-library module\", \"parsing a config file\" for a note that the client machines run Python 3.8. A later request rarely uses the words of the note it needs; it uses the words of the task. The situations join the note's lexical key and its embedding, so \"add a settings.toml loader\" finds the Python 3.8 note although they share no word. This is document expansion (doc2query) done once, when the note is written."],
["chloectx.html#consolidation","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Consolidation","Notes accumulate. Every eight distilled turns (or on chloectx consolidate ), the daemon groups live notes that say related things: pairs whose embeddings have a cosine of at least 0.80, or 0.55 when they share an entity, joined strongest first into groups of at most ten. A small model rewrites each group as the smallest set of notes that keeps every detail, number, name and reason that still holds: a later change replaces the earlier version (\"30 days, changed from 14\"), duplicates merge, notes on different subjects stay apart, and rules, requirements and reasons are never dropped. Each merged note links to the notes it replaces ( supersedes ), which are retagged superseded and leave the briefing and recall, and is dated by the newest of them, not by when it was merged; a note the model would only reword is kept as it is."],
["chloectx.html#notes-tied-to-the-code","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Notes tied to the code","A note about code goes stale when the code changes. Each note records what it was written against: the commit and branch, a content hash of the files it names or the turn changed (up to six), and the identifiers it mentions ( ROUND_HALF_UP , load_config ). When a note is shown (briefing, recall, file notes, the query engine), ChloeCtx checks those files in the working tree, reading each once: what changed decision or fact rule or requirement nothing shown as is shown as is a file it names, its identifiers still there \"app/billing.py changed since this note; check the current code\" the same the file is gone, or an identifier it names no longer occurs may be out of date : moved to its own briefing section the code may now break it : stays with the rules, with \"the code changed since this was stated; check that it still follows it\" A rule does not go stale because the code moved away from it: a teammate who switched invoices to banker's rounding did not cancel the client's requirement, so the rule stays where the model will see it, with a warning that the code may now break it. Branches. Worktrees of one repository share one store, but not their code. Each session's worktree, branch and commit are kept as an event when its hooks first name them and whenever the branch changes, so every record has a branch: its anchor's, else its session's at the time. A file an agent read or changed is anchored too (commit, branch, content hash). When a record is shown in another worktree, ChloeCtx asks git for the merge base of the record's branch with this HEAD: a record written after that commit came from work this worktree does not have, and is labelled \"from branch wb/paybutton, not merged here: not a description of this worktree's code\", unless its anchored files are the same here. A rule the user gave holds on every branch and is never labelled, and a note that names no file (a decision or fact said in conversation) is labelled only when its branch has commits this HEAD lacks. The briefing moves such notes to the section of notes that may be out of date, and the query engine's ranker is told never to present them as the current code."],
["chloectx.html#when-to-load","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","When to load","Records still inside the model's context are never injected. For each session s the daemon keeps the window start w s , the sequence number at session start or at the last compaction, and every query excludes $$ {\\, r : r.\\text{session} = s \\;\\wedge\\; r.\\text{seq} \\ge w_s \\,} \\;\\cup\\; \\text{injected}_s $$ where injected s is the set of records already injected since w s . There are three push points and one pull path: SessionStart : a briefing , always, at most 6,000 characters: the rules first, then decisions, planned work with its requirements and facts, each note whole (a note longer than 600 characters, which the distiller never writes, is cut at a word, says that memory_timeline shows the rest, and is not counted as shown) and checked against the current code. It opens with an instruction to search memory before a design decision. It names no counts and no ages (a note's date, not \"4 min ago\"), so it is the same text from one session to the next while the notes are, and Claude Code's prompt cache holds it; what changes every session (how many records, the last activity, the open tasks at the last checkpoint and where the previous session stopped) comes with the session's first prompt. After a compaction or on a resume that part is in the briefing itself, and after a compaction it also recalls the records most relevant to the last two prompts. UserPromptSubmit : knowledge, then gated recall. Up to four knowledge records not yet shown that match the prompt on their own text, bypassing the gate: $$ \\cos\\bigl(\\phi(q), \\phi(r)\\bigr) \\ge 0.40 \\;\\lor\\; \\mathrm{cov}(q, r) \\ge 0.30, \\qquad \\mathrm{cov}(q, r) = \\frac{\\sum_{t \\in q \\cap r} \\mathrm{idf}(t)}{\\sum_{t \\in q} \\mathrm{idf}(t)} $$ Then the main search and a three-way gate: strong evidence injects the top records up to 4,000 characters; weak evidence injects three records up to 1,200, marked as a weak match; otherwise nothing. 3. PreToolUse on Edit / Write : file notes. Up to two older errors, decisions or rules anchored to that path, once per file per window. With CHLOECTX_NEXT_FILES=1 (an experiment, off by default) the hook also names the files earlier sessions usually edited next: B follows A when an edit of B came within three edits after one of A (consecutive edits of one file count once), weighted by one over the gap and summed over sessions, and files above 1 are shown, at most two, each with its newest note. The daemon counts these from the edits the store holds as tool calls, reading only what was stored since it last looked, so nothing new is written. On the memory bench it changed neither how many tasks were solved nor which files were edited ( results ), so it stays off. 4. PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure on Bash : error to fix. A command that fails and then passes, with edits or other commands between, is written down as a fix: an error record tagged fix holding the error's line, the steps (the last eight edits and commands that change something; ls , grep , git status and the like do not count) and what showed it fixed (the same command passed, or failed with another error). The same command means the same after cd , pipes, redirections, timeout and variable assignments are taken off ( cd x && cargo test 2>&1 | tail is cargo test ); a failure is followed for 60 tool calls, and one that passes with nothing between is not a fix. When a command later fails with the same error, in any session of the project, its fixes come with the failure, at most two, once a session. Errors are matched by their signature: the telling line (a compiler's error: , a panic, the last line of a Python traceback, fatal: , ...) with numbers, hashes, temporary paths and the project's path masked, or one sharing four in five of its words. The fix links to the prompt of the turn that fixed it. 5. Pull: MCP tools. memory_ask (by search alone, below ; with deep , the query engine), memory_search , memory_timeline (the neighbourhood of a hit), memory_sql (one read-only SELECT over memory , with no model calls, a 2-second deadline and a 1,000-row cap), memory_remember and memory_forget (by its words, every record that holds a fact; chloectx forget TEXT from a shell, and chloectx forget --everywhere TEXT for a fact in every store, setup ). Sessions in worktrees of one repository share one daemon, so each tool call says which session made it (Claude Code's session id, and its process): a note kept with memory_remember is filed under the session that kept it, as is chloectx remember run from a session's Bash, and chloectx remember from your own terminal is manual:<seq> . The tools leave out what the current session's window already holds, except the notes it kept or that were distilled from it, so a note just remembered is found at once. The briefing also ends with a one-line index of the notes it had no room for (\"31 older notes are not shown here, about: …\"), so the model knows what to ask for. Text from outside the project is data. What WebFetch , WebSearch and every MCP tool returned (a page, an issue, a mail, a chat message) is stored, but it is never put into a prompt by itself: the prompt's recall, query understanding and the file notes leave it out, since whoever wrote that page or issue could otherwise plant an instruction that every later session got as \"earlier work\". memory_search and memory_timeline still find it, and show it fenced as quoted data ( <third_party_text source=\"WebFetch\"> , its own closing tag escaped inside), and every block of recalled records says that records are data and only the user's messages are instructions. Stores written before this keep their records; only what recall picks changes."],
["chloectx.html#ranking","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Ranking","BM25 and vector search each return their top n = 50 under the exclusion filter. Scores are min-max normalised over the union C , a missing score counting as 0: $$ \\mathrm{rel}(r) = w\\,\\widehat{\\mathrm{bm25}}(r) + (1 - w)\\,\\widehat{\\cos}(r) $$ Priors then break near-ties without overriding relevance, with ι the salience prior and τ a recency scale: $$ S(r) = \\mathrm{rel}(r)\\,\\bigl(1 + \\lambda_\\iota\\,\\iota(r)\\bigr) + \\lambda_t\\, e^{-(\\mathrm{seq}_{\\max} - \\mathrm{seq}(r))/\\tau} $$ Answers that admitted not knowing and repeats of the same question are excluded, because they would only replay a failure. Packing is greedy by S : at most twelve records, at most eight per user turn. Every injected line carries its provenance (kind, session, turn, relative time and id), and up to five further candidates follow as one line each for the model to open with memory_timeline . Session context (off by default). With CHLOECTX_SESSION_CONTEXT=1 the prompt's search also uses what the session was just doing. Its last 20 records give a BM25 query (the files they name and the first line of their errors, at most 24 words) and the vectors of the newest four prompts, answers, tool calls and errors, which rank records by maxsim . A record's best normalised score in those two lists is added to rel with weight 0.5, and records about one of the session's files score 15% more. The gate still reads the prompt's own evidence, so the session reorders what is injected but not whether anything is. On the memory bench it made no measurable difference (198 of 208 runs against 199) and it adds 5.8 ms to a prompt's searches at a million records ( results ), so it stays off."],
["chloectx.html#the-gate","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","The gate","Min-max scores cannot gate, since the best candidate always scores 1. The gate uses absolute evidence about the best candidates: the highest cosine and term coverage among the top five, how far the best BM25 and cosine scores stand above the tenth, whether BM25 and vectors agree on the top record, and whether the prompt refers back (\"earlier\", \"we decided\", \"last time\", …): $$ G = a\\,\\cos_{\\max} + b\\,\\mathrm{cov} {\\max} + c\\,\\mathbb{1} {\\text{cue}} + d\\,\\mathbb{1} {\\text{agree}} + e\\,(\\cos_1 - \\cos {10}) + f\\,\\Bigl(1 - \\frac{\\mathrm{bm25}_{10}}{\\mathrm{bm25}_1}\\Bigr) $$ The weights come from a logistic fit on LongMemEval-S ( chloectx/scripts/fit_gate.py ): positives are questions whose evidence the packed injection contains; negatives are questions asked against another conversation's store, abstentions and misses. θ hi holds precision 0.9 on the fit and θ lo precision 0.7. The gate is the weakest part of the design; the results say why."],
["chloectx.html#answering-a-question","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Answering a question","memory_ask (and chloectx ask ) answers by search alone: no model call runs between the question and the answer, because the work was done when memory was written (the messages, the notes the distiller wrote, the user's rules). Four searches run side by side on one snapshot of the store ( context.rs ), each with the live-window exclusion: search over what the context shows rules preferences, by vector, cosine at least 0.3; with them the rules the notes search finds, and every rule of a store that has at most four at most four, first, in at most a fifth of the budget notes decisions, rules, facts, plans (hybrid) current versions only, each with the note it replaced (\"changed DATE; was: …\"), dated and ordered by the message each came from (a merged note by its newest source) replies the assistant's answers (hybrid) the three exchanges these hits rank first: the start of the user's message (200 characters) and the part of the reply closest to the question (1,000) excerpts prompts and answers (hybrid) the 400 characters of each closest to the question Notes take 40% of the 12,000-character budget the rules leave, excerpts 25%, and both then fill the rest in rank order; the replies come on top, so a context is about 15,500 characters (about 3,900 tokens). A question about order or a summary (\"in what order\", \"summarize\", \"a timeline of\") has its answer spread over many exchanges, which a search for its words misses: it gets one and a half times the budget, the replies of eight exchanges (500 characters each), and every live note in place of the searched ones, oldest first (at most 50,000 characters, the newest kept), about 43,000 characters in all on BEAM. It takes about 6 ms a question on BEAM's stores, 11 ms for a summary. On BEAM 100K (20 conversations, 400 questions; results ) this context scores 62.9, against 55.0 for the query engine's picks and 63.4 for an observational memory that hands the reader its whole log of observations, about 153,000 characters, with the same reader and judge. Both sides move when their notes are written again: over two runs each, search alone averages 61.6 and observational memory 62.1. With deep: true ( chloectx ask --deep , or a model ) memory_ask runs the query engine below instead."],
["chloectx.html#query-understanding","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Query understanding","One search, however good its ranking, cannot answer \"where does an unknown product code get caught, and why do we raise instead of skipping the line?\". The answer is spread over a file, a decision and the error that led to it, and each is found by different words. The query engine turns a request into several searches, runs them all, and has a model pick what answers it. Plan. The planner sees the request, the session's two previous prompts, the store's size and time span, and the names of the entities and files notes are about. It writes up to eight searches, several of one type if that helps, covering each part of a question and the constraints a task touches without naming them (the machines it runs on, file formats, earlier failed attempts). It also says whether memory can matter at all, and whether the request is hard. Search. Every search runs in parallel, plus two on the request itself, each with the live-window exclusion: type runs text the hybrid search above (BM25 and vectors) keywords BM25 alone: identifiers, error text semantic vectors alone, with a sentence phrased like the record hoped for notes decisions, rules, requirements and facts only entity records linked to named entities (matched by id, stem or embedding), two hops through the graph file records about a path ( MATCH(files, …) ) sql a WHERE and ORDER BY over memory , checked to be a single condition Fuse. Lists are merged by reciprocal rank, Σ 1/(61 + rank), so a record found by several searches comes first; the best 30 go on. Rank. The ranker reads the 30 (600 characters each, with kind, date and any staleness label) and picks at most eight, each with why it matters, plus a one-to-three sentence reading that cites ids, and a confidence. Only picks the session has not seen are shown. It runs in one of four modes ( \"deep\": {\"mode\": …} in the store's config.json , or CHLOECTX_DEEP ): mode when it runs how its result arrives off never tool only when the model calls memory_ask with deep (or chloectx ask --deep ) the tool's result async (default) after every prompt of four words or more, once the store holds 20 records, in the background with the next tool result; if the turn ends first, with the next prompt, when the picks are still new and the ranker's confidence is high or medium. The end of a turn does not wait for it, but a search already finished then whose picks hold a rule or decision of the user's that the session was not shown is given at once, and Claude Code runs the model again to check its answer against it (the turn may be the session's only one). With \"stop_recheck\": true the end of the turn waits up to stop_wait_secs (at most 3 s) and, when the picks are new, asks the model once to check its answer against them (Claude Code runs the model again for that, and it says only what changed) sync the same prompts, before the model starts in the prompt's context, after a wait (12 s at the median and 16 s at p90 in the cloud container, six requests at a time) Both model calls go through claude -p with no tools and no prompt caching (each prompt is new, so a cache write would cost more than it saves), like distillation, and together read about 5,800 tokens and write 1,150: $0.0012 a request with a small model ($0.58 for 500 LongMemEval questions). Two settings make it cheaper. \"planner\": \"none\" ( CHLOECTX_DEEP_PLAN=none ) skips the planning call: the ranker reads the best 30 of the request's own two searches, taken deeper. \"ranker\": \"local\" ( CHLOECTX_DEEP_RANK=local ) replaces the ranking call with a logistic model over twelve features of each candidate (fused rank, similarity, term coverage, kind, length, recency) and keeps the top 5, with no model call; with planner also none the engine is free. On LongMemEval the default answers best: ranker only loses temporal questions, and the local ranker keeps about a quarter of the gain ( results ). The models are parameters: planner_model and ranker_model (default haiku , or CHLOECTX_DEEP_MODEL for both), and hard_model , a stronger ranker used only when the planner calls a request hard ( CHLOECTX_DEEP_HARD_MODEL ). memory_ask also takes a model for one question, so a user who allows a more expensive model can have it for the questions that need it."],
["chloectx.html#cost-and-latency-targets","ChloeCtx: long-term memory for Claude Code","Cost and latency targets","path target measured PostToolUse , Stop client < 10 ms queued; embedded and committed in the background UserPromptSubmit < 100 ms p99 at 1M records 24.9 ms p50, 38.6 ms p99 as a hook process SessionStart < 150 ms (daemon up), < 1 s with spawn 50–72 ms including daemon start ingest > 1,000 records/s 48,000–52,000 records/s with the static embedder memory_ask (search alone) < 100 ms 6.9 ms a question on BEAM 100K's stores, one thread (11 ms for a summary) query engine (planner and ranker) off the prompt's path unless sync 12 s p50, 16 s p90, $0.0012 per request with Haiku The default embedder is static (Model2Vec potion-retrieval-32M: token lookup and mean, 512 dimensions, microseconds per record). BGE-small (384 dimensions) is optional ( --features bert ) and costs about 50 ms per record on a CPU. The full design, including the evaluation plan, is in chloectx/DESIGN.md ."],
["chloectx-setup.html","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","","ChloeCtx runs next to your own Claude Code: hooks record each session into a local ChloeDB file per project, and a small MCP server lets the model search that history. This page takes a fresh macOS or Linux machine to a working install in a few minutes, shows how to check it, and covers remote boxes, dev containers and upgrades."],
["chloectx-setup.html#what-you-need","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","What you need","why check macOS or Linux (Windows through WSL 2) the daemon talks to the hooks over a Unix socket uname -s Rust, stable to build ChloeCtx from source (not for the prebuilt binary ) cargo --version (install from rustup.rs ) a C compiler to build from source: the tokenizer's regex library is C cc --version ( xcode-select --install on macOS, build-essential on Debian/Ubuntu) curl downloads the 129 MB embedding model once curl --version Claude Code CLI, logged in the hooks and the MCP server plug into it, and the distiller calls claude -p after each turn claude auth status shows \"loggedIn\": true Python 3 is only needed for the scripted demo and the evaluation harnesses, not for ChloeCtx itself. The CLI login matters more than it looks. The distiller runs claude -p in the background, so an expired CLI login breaks it even while interactive sessions (or the desktop app) keep working. Recording and recall still work in that state, but nothing new is written down. chloectx doctor checks for it. ChloeCtx makes model calls of its own, through claude -p with a small model ( haiku by default), billed to the CLI's login: one after each turn to write down decisions and rules (about $0.001 to $0.002); after each of your own turns that is like something you said before, one more to see whether you had to say it again, which makes it a rule (about $0.0005); and, once a project's store holds 20 records, two more after each prompt of four words or more, in the background, to search memory for it ( query understanding , about $0.001 to $0.002 a prompt). chloectx doctor shows what these calls cost in your own store over the last 30 days. None of them holds up a turn: what the search finds comes with the turn's next tool result, or at the next prompt; a find that holds a rule or decision of yours the session was not shown, ready when the turn ends, is given then, and Claude checks its answer against it. CHLOECTX_DISTILL=0 turns the first two off, CHLOECTX_DEEP=off (or tool , only when the model asks) the last. The session's own model reads ChloeCtx's context too: the briefing, up to 6,000 characters (about 1,500 tokens), at the start of each session and again on each claude --continue or --resume , and the notes that come with a prompt. The daemon that makes these calls finds the claude CLI where Claude Code's hooks found it, on its own PATH , or in the usual install places ( ~/.local/bin , ~/.claude/local , /opt/homebrew/bin , /usr/local/bin ); CHLOECTX_CLAUDE names it outright. chloectx doctor shows which one the running daemon uses."],
["chloectx-setup.html#as-a-claude-code-plugin","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","As a Claude Code plugin","The repository is a Claude Code plugin marketplace, named chloedb , with ChloeCtx as its plugin chloectx . In Claude Code: On Claude Code 2.1.275 or later, /plugin install chloectx --marketplace chloetech-ai/chloedb does both. From a shell, claude plugin marketplace add chloetech-ai/chloedb && claude plugin install chloectx@chloedb installs it for every session, and --scope local (in the project's folder) for one project only. The plugin's name comes from the repository's .claude-plugin/marketplace.json , not from the address it is added by: chloetech-ai/strata , the repository's old address, still redirects and gives the same chloectx@chloedb . It needs no Rust and no clone of your own. Then start a new session. Its hooks and its MCP server run a small launcher in the plugin ( scripts/chloectx-launch ), which runs the first chloectx it finds: $CHLOECTX_BIN , chloectx on your PATH , or ~/.local/bin/chloectx . When there is none, it sets ChloeCtx up once, in the background: it downloads the prebuilt chloectx of the release that matches the plugin's version (the archive chloectx-<platform>.tar.gz of the release chloedb-server-v<version> , from the releases ; the newest release when that one has none), checks its SHA-256 against the release's .sha256 and refuses one that differs, and installs it as ~/.local/bin/chloectx , so chloectx doctor , chloectx ui and the other commands work from a shell too; where there is no prebuilt one (Windows outside WSL 2, Alpine and other musl systems, glibc older than 2.35), or the download failed, it builds it from a checkout with cargo (Rust and a C compiler; install.sh --no-wire in $CHLOECTX_SOURCE , or in the clone of the marketplace), which takes several minutes; it fetches the embedding model ( chloectx fetch-model , 129 MB, SHA-256 checked). Until that is done, which is usually well under a minute, the hooks record nothing, and the first session's start says so; the memory tools come with the next session if they were not ready within 25 seconds. The launcher writes what it did to ~/.claude/plugins/data/chloectx-chloedb/setup.log , and when it cannot set up, the next session's start says why. To watch it, or to try again by hand: ~/.claude/plugins/cache/chloedb/chloectx/<version>/scripts/chloectx-launch setup . When the plugin is updated ( /plugin update , or Claude Code's auto-update) to a new version, the launcher replaces a chloectx it downloaded with the new version's; one you installed yourself is yours to upgrade. Claude Code clones the whole repository for the marketplace, into ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/chloedb : 665 MB on 17 November 2026 (Claude Code 2.1.326), of which 213 MB is .git and most of the rest benchmark results the plugin does not use. claude plugin marketplace add chloetech-ai/chloedb --sparse .claude-plugin chloectx/plugin checks out only the plugin, about 2 MB, which leaves no checkout to build from on a machine without a prebuilt chloectx . Either way, the prebuilt chloectx takes another 137 MB in ~/.local/bin and the embedding model 129 MB. How it differs from chloectx install --user : the plugin chloectx install --user ( ./install.sh ) hooks the same eleven events, from the plugin, through its launcher in ~/.claude/settings.json MCP server plugin:chloectx:chloectx , tools mcp__plugin_chloectx_chloectx__* chloectx at user scope, tools mcp__chloectx__* the memory tools' permission asked the first time each is used (add mcp__plugin_chloectx_chloectx to permissions.allow to stop asking) allowed by the install ( mcp__chloectx ) distillation model haiku , unless CHLOECTX_DISTILL_MODEL is set (in the settings' env , say) --distill-model , written into the settings' env upgrade /plugin update chloectx@chloedb chloectx upgrade remove /plugin uninstall chloectx@chloedb (keeps ~/.local/bin/chloectx , the model and the stores) chloectx uninstall --user (keeps the binary and the stores) Both use the same stores, one per project. A project with its own chloectx install keeps using that one, and the plugin's hooks step aside there, as the user-level ones do. With both the plugin and chloectx install --user , the hooks of the user settings record and the plugin's step aside, but a session has the memory tools twice; chloectx doctor warns, and chloectx uninstall --user leaves only the plugin. Doctor shows the plugin: launcher variable effect CHLOECTX_BIN the chloectx to run, wherever it is CHLOECTX_PREFIX where a downloaded or built chloectx goes: DIR/bin (default ~/.local ) CHLOECTX_VERSION the release to download, instead of the plugin's version CHLOECTX_RELEASES_URL where the releases are (default https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb/releases/download ) CHLOECTX_SOURCE a checkout to build from when there is no prebuilt chloectx CHLOECTX_BUILD=0 never build from source CHLOECTX_PLUGIN_WAIT how long the MCP server waits for the setup, in seconds (default 25)"],
["chloectx-setup.html#prebuilt","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Prebuilt","since 0.3.2 From release 0.3.2, the ChloeDB installer fetches chloectx prebuilt (macOS 11 or later, Linux with glibc 2.35 or later), with no Rust and no clone; then fetch the model, wire it into Claude Code and check: Without --distill-model , the distiller runs the Claude Code CLI's default model, and chloectx doctor warns that a small one is enough; install again with --distill-model haiku to change it. Release 0.3.1 and earlier have no prebuilt chloectx : build it from source, below. This installer (the website's install.sh ) is not the repository's ./install.sh , which installs ChloeCtx from a checkout and wires it in one step (next section), and takes the same prebuilt binary when it can."],
["chloectx-setup.html#install-in-one-command-from-source","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Install in one command, from source","install.sh does five things and can be run again at any time: builds chloectx in release mode (a few minutes the first time), or takes the release's prebuilt one (below); copies it to ~/.local/bin/chloectx , the path the hooks call; stops any running ChloeCtx daemons, so they restart on the new binary; downloads the embedding model (potion-retrieval-32M) unless it is already there; runs chloectx install --user --distill-model haiku and then chloectx doctor . option effect --project DIR install into one project's .claude/settings.local.json and .mcp.json instead of every session --distill-model NAME the model that writes down decisions and rules after each turn (default haiku ; --distill-model \"\" uses the CLI's default model) --prefix DIR install the binary to DIR/bin (default ~/.local ) --no-wire install the binary only, and leave Claude Code's settings alone --no-model do not download the embedding model (run chloectx fetch-model later) --prebuilt take the release's prebuilt chloectx , never build --source build from the checkout, never download the binary From the release after 0.3.2, install.sh can take the binary that release carries instead of building it. Without --prebuilt or --source , it takes the prebuilt binary of release chloedb-server-v<version> (the version in chloectx/Cargo.toml ), checked against the release's SHA-256, in two cases: when the checkout is that release's (the tag, with chloectx/ and chloedb/ unchanged since), and when there is no Rust on the machine (then it says the checkout may be newer than the release). Otherwise it builds, so a checkout of main, or one you changed, runs what is in it. To install such a release with nothing to compile, clone its tag: git clone --branch chloedb-server-v<version> https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb , then ./install.sh in it. (A checkout of 0.3.2 or earlier has the install.sh that always builds.) A prebuilt binary does not know the checkout it came with, so chloectx upgrade and chloectx doctor cannot compare it with one or rebuild it, even when ./install.sh took it in a checkout of yours. To upgrade a checkout of a release tag, move it to the new release's tag and run the installer again, which takes that release's prebuilt binary: git fetch --tags && git checkout chloedb-server-v<version> && ./install.sh . CHLOECTX_SOURCE=/path/to/chloedb chloectx upgrade is for a checkout on a branch, such as main, which it pulls and builds; on a tag (a detached HEAD) it stops and says so. Sessions that were already open pick ChloeCtx up when they restart."],
["chloectx-setup.html#or-let-your-agent-do-it","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Or let your agent do it","Tell Claude Code (or another coding agent) in any session: Clone https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb and set ChloeCtx up for my Claude Code by following its AGENTS.md. AGENTS.md tells the agent to check the prerequisites without installing anything unasked, to ask you whether to install for every session or one project, to run install.sh and chloectx doctor , and to report what failed. Two things stay with you: claude auth login if the CLI is logged out (it is interactive), and starting a new session afterwards, because the session that ran the install started before the hooks existed."],
["chloectx-setup.html#or-step-by-step","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Or step by step","chloectx install --user merges eleven hooks and a permission for the memory tools into ~/.claude/settings.json and registers the MCP server at user scope with claude mcp add-json . Each run saves the previous settings.json next to it as settings.json.chloectx-<time>.bak , and so does chloectx uninstall --user ; nothing deletes these backups, so remove the old ones once you are happy. A project install backs up the project's .claude/settings.local.json and .mcp.json the same way, when it changes them. It adds those two files to the repository's .git/info/exclude , since they hold this machine's paths, but not the .bak files next to them: git status shows them (as ?? .claude/ ). Delete them, and do not commit them. Every settings file is written to a file beside it and renamed into place, so an interrupted install leaves the old file whole, and its keys stay in the order they were. A project with its own chloectx install keeps using that one; the user-level hooks step aside there, so nothing is recorded twice."],
["chloectx-setup.html#check-it","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Check it","doctor checks the current directory's project. The version line compares the binary with the checkout it was built from; a prebuilt binary has none, and the line says how to upgrade it instead ( below ). Once a session has run there, the last line becomes the store itself (records, sessions, daemon round trip), lines for the store's size on disk and the daemon's own claude CLI follow, and a last distillation line reports whether the most recent distillation worked. Every failed line says how to fix it, and the command exits non-zero when anything failed. It also says what ChloeCtx's own model calls do and cost, with the settings the hooks see (the environment, then the env of the project's local settings, the project's settings and the user settings, then the store's config.json ): query understanding says when it runs: after the prompt, in the background ( async , the default), at prompt time with the prompt waiting for it ( sync , a warning), only when the model asks ( tool ), or never ( off ). It names the setting it came from, the model calls each prompt makes, and whether the end of a turn waits for it, which it does only with stop_recheck on (a warning: Claude may then run again). query understanding cost and distillation cost are measured from the store's events.log over the last 30 days: how many calls, the median time, the dollars in all and per call, and how many failed. Claude Code login says whether the distiller and query understanding can run: both call claude -p , which needs the CLI to be found and logged in."],
["chloectx-setup.html#try-it-for-real","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Try it for real","A two-minute check that memory carries across sessions. In any project: Start claude and state something only a conversation would know, for example: \"Rule from the client: all amounts are integer cents, never floats, because their ledger rejects fractional cents. The server runs Python 3.8, so no match statements.\" Let it answer, then exit. Start a new session in the same directory and ask: \"Write billing.py with total(items), following every rule the client gave us.\" The new session starts with ChloeCtx's briefing, which lists the rules the distiller wrote down after the first session, and the code should use integer cents and avoid match . Then look at what happened:"],
["chloectx-setup.html#explore-your-stores-chloectx-ui","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Explore your stores: chloectx ui","chloectx ui is the live inspector pointed at your own data. The left column picks a store (one per project directory, newest first) and a session, and replays that session from its records: prompts, answers, tool calls (open one to see its stored result), the briefing at session start, every recall decision with its gate score and the records it injected, and the notes the distiller kept, superseded or closed. A session that is still running grows as it goes. The right side has every panel of the inspector, scoped to the chosen store: the live feed of records and daemon events, knowledge, the table, the graph, the vectors, an SQL console and the storage layout. It is built into the binary (no Python, no checkout needed) and only reads: each store is opened through its project's daemon, as the hooks do, so it is safe to keep open while sessions write. It listens on 127.0.0.1, and every request must carry the random token in the URL it prints, because stores hold transcripts. Links keep the store, session and tab in the address ( ?store=…&session=…&tab=knowledge ), so a view can be reopened later. It is also the quickest way to debug an install. A DISTILL event with an error in the live feed means the distiller cannot run claude -p ; a RECALL · SKIP on every prompt with few candidates means the store is still small; no records at all for a session means its hooks did not run."],
["chloectx-setup.html#where-everything-lives","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Where everything lives","what where binary ~/.local/bin/chloectx (from install.sh or cargo install --root ~/.local ) hooks ~/.claude/settings.json (user install), the project's .claude/settings.local.json , or the plugin's hooks/hooks.json under ~/.claude/plugins/cache/chloedb/chloectx/<version>/ MCP server ~/.claude.json at user scope, the project's .mcp.json , or the plugin's .mcp.json the plugin's launcher state ~/.claude/plugins/data/chloectx-chloedb/ : setup.log , and ready once it has set up stores ~/.local/share/chloectx/projects/<folder>-<hash>/ , one per project directory (a repository's worktrees share its main checkout's): memory.strata and its WAL, state.json , events.log (recall and distill decisions), daemon.log , project.txt . A machine that has ~/.local/share/engram from before the rename, and no ~/.local/share/chloectx , keeps using the old folder embedding model ~/.local/share/chloectx/models/potion-retrieval-32M/ (or in the old folder, as the stores) daemon socket engram.sock in the store, or, when that path is too long, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR , /tmp/engram-<uid>/ or ~/.cache/chloectx/ : the first that is a directory of yours alone (a /tmp/engram-<uid> another user made, or a link, is skipped) model calls' working directory distill/ in the stores' folder, private to you (the distiller's claude -p reads project settings from where it runs) Store directories are created readable by their owner only. ChloeCtx redacts secrets before it stores anything ( below ), but a store is still a transcript of your sessions: treat it like your shell history."],
["chloectx-setup.html#secrets-and-chloectx-redact","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Secrets and chloectx redact","Every record is redacted as it is stored, whatever wrote it (the hooks, ingest-transcript , remember , the distiller, compaction summaries), and again as chloectx sync sends or pulls it. So are the prompts kept in state.json and the lines of events.log . A secret is replaced by a marker that names its kind, and the text around it stays, so DB_PASSWORD=hunter2 is stored as DB_PASSWORD=[REDACTED:password] . What is caught: keys and tokens by their shape: Anthropic ( sk-ant- ), OpenAI ( sk- , sk-proj- ), GitHub ( ghp_ , gho_ , ghu_ , ghs_ , ghr_ , github_pat_ ), GitLab ( glpat- ), npm ( npm_ ), AWS access key ids ( AKIA , ASIA ), Google API keys ( AIza ) and OAuth tokens ( ya29. ), Slack tokens ( xoxb- and the like) and webhooks, Discord webhooks, Stripe ( sk_live_ , rk_live_ , whsec_ ), DigitalOcean ( dop_v1_ ), Hugging Face ( hf_ ), SendGrid, ChloeDB API keys ( sk_<id>_<secret> ) and JWTs; PEM private key blocks, also one cut off before its END line; Authorization: headers ( Bearer , Basic and others), Bearer tokens, curl -u user:password , and the password in a URL ( postgres://app:…@db ); values assigned to a name that ends in a word for a secret (token, secret, password, passwd, passphrase, credential, or a key such as api_key , AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY , apiKey ): NAME=value in an environment dump or a .env file, name: value in YAML, \"name\": \"value\" in JSON, name = \"value\" in code. It is pattern-based, so it is best effort: a password written in prose, or a random string under an unremarkable name, is stored as it is. It leaves alone what only looks random (git hashes, digests, UUIDs) and code that names a secret without holding one ( token = get_token() , password: string , primary_key ). A store written before this redaction can hold secrets it would now catch. chloectx redact , in the project directory, rewrites them: The project's daemon rewrites each record whose text redaction changes, redacts state.json and events.log the same way, and, when a sync is set up, sends the rewritten records to the server again so they replace its copies. Then the daemon is stopped and the store's file replaced by a fresh copy of its live rows, because the pages the old versions were on would otherwise stay in the file until later writes reuse them; a hook that comes in those few seconds records nothing. It prints how many records, session states and log lines changed and which kinds of secret it found, never the secrets. What it cannot reach is up to you: backups made with chloectx backup , a memory.strata.before-field-ids copy left by a format upgrade (it names it), and the server's own backups."],
["chloectx-setup.html#forget-something-chloectx-forget","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Forget something: chloectx forget","A fact usually lives in more than one record: the prompt that said it, the tool output that showed it, the answer and the notes written from it. So forget it by its words, in a project's folder: Given words that are not record ids (3 characters or more, whatever their case), it lists, before it asks: the records whose own text holds them, which are deleted, each with its kind and first line; the records that hold them only in their context, which stay. Every record of a turn carries that turn's request as its context, so a rule you gave in the same prompt as a private aside is one of these: the rule is kept, and the lines of its context that held the words are cut; how many session states, log lines and receipts hold them, which are scrubbed. --yes skips the question; without a terminal and without --yes it only lists. Then the project's daemon deletes the records, cuts the words from the other records' context, and scrubs them from every file of the store: state.json (the session's last prompts), events.log and daemon.log , and every receipt that holds them ( receipts.strata ). The automatic snapshots are dropped, the segments of memory.strata and receipts.strata that held the old copies are rewritten, and the free pages of both files, where those copies were, are written with zeros. For five minutes after, nothing that holds the words is stored, so a note being distilled at that moment does not bring them back. Only the segments that held them are rewritten, not every segment with an older delete, so the cost is their size, not the store's. By id, chloectx forget ID... deletes those records only, with the same scrubbing of receipts and snapshots; the other records of their turn stay. memory_forget is the same for the model: asked to forget something, Claude passes its words as text (or one record's id ). The turn that calls it stores no reply, card or narration and is not distilled, so no note says again what was forgotten, and nothing that holds the words is stored until the turn ends. It answers once the records are deleted and the files scrubbed, and the segments are rewritten after the answer (no snapshot is taken meanwhile), so a large store does not run past MCP's 20 s deadline; chloectx forget waits for all of it and says so. With a sync set up, the deleted records' rows on the server are deleted too, the records whose context was cut are sent again to replace the server's copies, and the store keeps the deleted ids (in sync-forgotten.json ) so that no pull stores them again, whichever machine pushes them back; a delete the server did not answer is sent again with each push. A machine that pulled a record before keeps its copy until it forgets it there. To find a fact in every project, --everywhere looks in every store chloectx stores lists, by text or by id, and lists what it finds by store: A daemon started to look into a store is stopped afterwards. What forget cannot reach, and says when it can see it: named snapshots ( chloectx snapshot drop NAME ), a memory.strata.before-field-ids copy left by a format upgrade, backups made with chloectx backup , packs written with chloectx pack , Claude Code's own transcripts of your sessions (under ~/.claude/projects/ ), the copies other machines pulled from a sync server before the forget, and the server's own backups. To back a store up, run chloectx backup DEST in the project directory. It copies the store while sessions go on: a consistent checkpoint of memory.strata (copying the file itself while the daemon writes can catch it half way through a commit), state.json , the config and events.log , into a new directory DEST . To restore, run chloectx stop and copy those files back into the store's directory. A backup is as private as the store. variable effect CHLOECTX_DISABLE=1 the hooks do nothing in this session CHLOECTX_HOME where stores (and, by default, models) live; default $XDG_DATA_HOME/chloectx or ~/.local/share/chloectx ( engram in place of chloectx when only that folder exists) CHLOECTX_MODELS where the embedding models live CHLOECTX_DISTILL_MODEL the distiller's model ( install --distill-model sets it in the settings' env ) CHLOECTX_DISTILL=0 no distillation (read from Claude Code's environment too, whichever process started the daemon) CHLOECTX_DEEP query understanding: async (default), sync , tool or off CHLOECTX_CLAUDE the claude CLI the model calls run, when it is in none of the places the daemon looks CHLOECTX_EMBEDDER static (default), bert , or hash:<dim> for a model-free hash embedder Each of them is still read under its name from before the rename, ENGRAM_* ( ENGRAM_DISABLE=1 ), when the new one is not set."],
["chloectx-setup.html#other-environments","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Other environments",""],
["chloectx-setup.html#a-remote-machine-over-ssh","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","A remote machine over SSH","Install on the remote machine as above; the hooks run wherever claude runs. To browse its stores from your laptop, forward the port and open the URL chloectx ui prints: chloectx ui does not try to open a browser inside an SSH session."],
["chloectx-setup.html#dev-containers-and-codespaces","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Dev containers and Codespaces","Run the installer when the container is created, for example in .devcontainer/devcontainer.json : The image needs Rust and a C compiler (the ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/rust feature provides both), and Claude Code must be logged in inside the container. A container's home directory is usually thrown away on rebuild, so point CHLOECTX_HOME at a mounted or persistent path (as above) if memory should survive rebuilds. Inside a container, start the inspector with chloectx ui --no-open and open the printed URL through the forwarded port."],
["chloectx-setup.html#windows","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Windows","Use WSL 2 and run everything, including Claude Code, inside the Linux distribution. Native Windows is not supported: the daemon uses Unix sockets and flock ."],
["chloectx-setup.html#claude-code-on-the-web","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Claude Code on the web","Cloud sessions run in a fresh container each time, so a ChloeCtx store there starts empty and is lost when the container is reclaimed. To keep memory across them, sync it with a ChloeDB server ( below ): the environment's setup script installs ChloeCtx and names the server, the environment holds the server's key in a variable, and each session pulls the project's memory when its daemon starts."],
["chloectx-setup.html#keep-the-memory-on-a-chloedb-server","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Keep the memory on a ChloeDB server","chloectx sync copies each project's memory to a table of its own, chloectx_mem_<project> , on a ChloeDB server or a cluster's coordinator, and back. The store stays a local file, so hooks and recall never wait on the network: the daemon pushes what it recorded every 30 seconds and as it exits, and when it starts it pulls the project's records the store lacks (written on another machine, or by a container that is gone) and embeds them here; a project is its git repository ( owner/name of origin ), so checkouts in different folders share one memory, and --rename owner/old=owner/new lets a checkout of a renamed repository join the new name's; chloectx sync status says what the project syncs as ( synced_as ), and chloectx doctor names its table ( chloectx_mem_ , the name in lowercase letters, digits and _ , then 8 hex digits of its hash: chloectx_mem_acme_web_1a2b3c4d ), which the server can search too ( SELECT content FROM chloectx_mem_acme_web_1a2b3c4d ORDER BY bm25(content, '...') DESC LIMIT 10 ); a folder with no origin syncs as its absolute path, and its table is named after that ( /home/you/code/app syncs into chloectx_mem_home_you_code_app_ and its hash), so another machine shares it only from the same path. A project that is renamed starts its table again. The table is named by what the project syncs as: its repository ( owner/name , from origin ) or, with no remote, its folder. When that changes (an origin added, a repository renamed), setup says so and the next push sends every record to the new table. chloectx sync status warns when the server holds fewer of the project's records than the store sent or took from it, and chloectx sync push --again sends them all again. (0.3.7 and earlier keep the mark of what they sent, so the new table starts without the older records: run chloectx stop in the project's folder, delete the store's sync-state.json , and chloectx sync push .) One key per project and machine. A table per project is the boundary between projects: a key limited to one project's table ( \"tables\": {\"chloectx_mem_acme_web_1a2b3c4d\": \"write\"} ) reads and writes that project and nothing else, so a laptop's key reads no other project's sessions, and a key that leaks writes only into its project. On a cluster's command center, \"Make a sync key\" on the ChloeCtx page (an admin's) asks for the project and the machine, makes the project's table and such a key, and shows the setup line with it (with --no-create , since that key cannot make tables). Without it: setup creates the project's table, which needs a key with the admin scope ( --no-create skips it; the first push creates it when the key can), and an admin makes the key with POST /v1/admin/keys . A machine keeps a key for each project: setup --token-stdin , run in the project's folder, keeps the key as that project's ( sync-keys/<table>.token in ChloeCtx's home), and the first key set up on a machine is the machine's too ( sync.token ), which a project without a key of its own sends. --every-project makes the key the machine's as well (an admin's key, or one that may write several projects' tables). chloectx sync status says which key the project sends ( key_from ), and setup and chloectx doctor name the table a refused key needs. A key for operations (role propose or ops , an on-call agent's) reads no synced memory and no local store through ChloeCtx's app, whatever else it reads. What another machine sent waits for you. Each row names the machine that pushed it ( origin : CHLOECTX_SYNC_ORIGIN , else a random id ChloeCtx keeps in sync-origin in its home). A record pulled from another machine is stored with a link to that machine, and its rules and decisions stay out of the session's briefing, the prompt's context and searches until you accept the machine: chloectx sync accept lists the machines and a few of their rules, --origin ID (repeatable) or --all accepts them, now and on later pulls (the machines are added to trusted in sync.json ). Facts, notes and the conversation itself are not held back. A key that may write a project's table could otherwise plant a \"user preference\" that every machine of the project showed as its own. A cloud container is a new machine each time: give it a stable CHLOECTX_SYNC_ORIGIN in the environment, and accept that one once. From 0.3.3 and before. Every project shared one table, chloectx_memory , and any key that could write it read every project's transcripts. A project's rows there are still pulled, where the key may read that table, held back as from a machine no one accepted ( unknown ); nothing is pushed there any more. Once every machine of a project syncs into its own table, an admin removes the old rows ( DELETE FROM chloectx_memory WHERE project = 'acme/web' ) and revokes the old shared keys. CHLOECTX_SYNC_URL and CHLOECTX_SYNC_TOKEN override the settings. What is sent is what the store holds, transcripts of your sessions included, redacted again on the way ( secrets ): use a server you control, over HTTPS, with a key (plain http:// is refused except to this machine, 127.0.0.1 or localhost ; CHLOECTX_SYNC_ALLOW_HTTP=1 allows it). Sync adds records and deletes the forgotten ones: one retagged after it was pushed is not sent again, and one forgotten here has its row on the server deleted and is never pulled back ( forget ). A key limited to the project's table cannot delete its rows in the table of 0.3.3 and before, chloectx_memory ; those stay there, held back, until an admin removes them. Two machines writing the same project at once can, rarely, give two records one id ( manual:<seq> from chloectx remember in a terminal; run from a session's Bash, its note has that session's id); the server keeps the last one sent."],
["chloectx-setup.html#upgrade-and-remove","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Upgrade and remove","The binary knows the commit and the checkout it was built from ( chloectx --version names the commit). chloectx doctor fetches that checkout's upstream branch (waiting at most 3 s; upgrade --check waits 20 s) and says how many commits on it change ChloeCtx or ChloeDB since the build, or, when the fetch failed, that it could not check and what the last fetch said ( upgrade --check then exits 1), and a session that starts while the binary is behind says so once a day, from what the checkout last fetched. chloectx upgrade pulls the checkout (fast forward only: it stops when local commits or changes are in the way, and --no-pull builds the checkout as it is), stops the daemons of the old build, runs install.sh --no-wire into the binary's own prefix, writes the user-level install again (a version may add hook events; the distillation model you chose is kept), and ends with doctor . A prebuilt binary, or one built outside a checkout (no .git ), cannot upgrade itself: chloectx upgrade and doctor say to run the installer again ( curl -fsSL https://chloetech-ai.github.io/chloedb/install.sh | sh -s -- --with ctx , which replaces the binary, keeps the stores and installs chloedb-server too), or point CHLOECTX_SOURCE at a checkout on a branch. A checkout of a release tag upgrades by moving to the new tag ( above ), and one on main with git pull && ./install.sh ."],
["chloectx-setup.html#keep-the-stores-tidy-chloectx-stores-and-chloectx-prune","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Keep the stores tidy: chloectx stores and chloectx prune","Every directory a session runs in gets its own store, including the throwaway folders tools make under the system's temporary directory, so stores pile up over months. A health check of the MCP server makes one too: claude mcp get chloectx starts the server, and with it a daemon and a store, for the folder it runs in, even one where no session ever ran. chloectx stores lists them all, most recently used first, with the project each records, its size on disk and when it was last used, and marks the ones prune would remove: the project folder no longer exists, and the store was not used for a week (a folder missing for less, or on a drive that is not mounted, such as /Volumes/… or /media/… , may have moved and is only marked; chloectx move OLD NEW attaches the store to the folder's new place); the project is in a temporary folder ( /tmp , /var/folders on macOS) and the store was not used for a day; the store names no project (one from before the daemon wrote project.txt , whose daemon.log does not name it either) and was not used for a month. chloectx prune lists those and asks before it removes them ( --yes skips the question, --dry-run only lists, --older-than DAYS adds every store not used for that long). A store whose daemon is running is never removed. Removing a store deletes that project's memory for good. chloectx doctor counts the stores and says when ten or more could go. The repository was called rkonow/engram until October 2026. A checkout made before then keeps working, because GitHub redirects the old address: upgrade it with git pull && ./install.sh in its folder (still named engram ), and git remote set-url origin https://github.com/chloetech-ai/chloedb points it at the new name."],
["chloectx-setup.html#chloectx-was-called-engram","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","ChloeCtx was called Engram","ChloeCtx was called Engram until November 2026. git pull && ./install.sh moves an install to the new names, and leaves what it stored where it is: The program is ~/.local/bin/chloectx , and ~/.local/bin/engram is a link to it for a release, so whatever still calls the old name keeps working. chloectx install (which install.sh runs) replaces the entries the old install wrote into Claude Code's settings: the hooks that called engram , the MCP server engram and its permission mcp__engram (the server is chloectx now, and its tools mcp__chloectx__* ), and ENGRAM_DISTILL_MODEL , which becomes CHLOECTX_DISTILL_MODEL with the same model. An engram server or permission is taken for ChloeCtx's only when the same settings hold the old hooks of the program it runs, since another tool may be called engram. chloectx uninstall removes both the old and the new entries. Until you run it again, chloectx doctor warns about the old ones. The settings are CHLOECTX_* , and each ENGRAM_* one is still read when the new one is not set. The stores and the embedding model stay in ~/.local/share/engram on a machine that has that folder and no ~/.local/share/chloectx : install.sh moves nothing, and chloectx doctor warns which folder is in use. chloectx upgrade moves it: with the daemons stopped, it renames ~/.local/share/engram to ~/.local/share/chloectx and leaves engram as a link to it (not when CHLOECTX_HOME is set). A new install uses ~/.local/share/chloectx . A store's files keep their names ( memory.strata , engram.sock ), and a daemon started by the old program keeps serving until it stops, which install.sh asks it to do. Sessions that were already running keep the old tool names until they restart."],
["chloectx-setup.html#troubleshooting","Install ChloeCtx on your machine","Troubleshooting","symptom cause and fix doctor : Claude Code login fails, a session starts with ChloeCtx is not writing down decisions or rules , or DISTILL events show OAuth session expired the CLI's own login expired; run claude auth login . Recording and recall keep working meanwhile, but turns from that period are not distilled again later. Until a distillation works again, each session's start says so, to you and to the model recalled records or rules that are a harness's markup ( <system-reminder> , <wake> , <coordinator-relay> ) stored by an earlier version, which kept the envelopes some harnesses wrap prompts in. Prompts are now stored without them, recall and the briefing pass the old ones over, and a daemon retires the rules it had picked out of them once, when it first starts on the store no records for a session the session started before ChloeCtx was installed (restart it), CHLOECTX_DISABLE=1 was set, or the project has its own install that points at a binary that moved ( chloectx doctor in that directory says which) the model has no memory_* tools the MCP server is not registered or the session predates it: claude mcp get chloectx , then restart the session. With the plugin, /mcp lists plugin:chloectx:chloectx ; when it failed to start, the plugin was still setting up (see its setup.log ), and the next session has the tools a session starts with ChloeCtx could not set itself up (the plugin) the launcher found no chloectx and could not download or build one; the message and ~/.claude/plugins/data/chloectx-chloedb/setup.log say why. Install chloectx another way (above), or point CHLOECTX_BIN at one, and start a new session daemon did not answer in time read the store's daemon.log ; a missing embedding model is the usual cause ( chloectx fetch-model ) distillation costs about $0.001 to $0.002 a turn with a small model, after the turn has ended; chloectx doctor shows the cost and the median time your store measured over the last 30 days, and CHLOECTX_DISTILL=0 turns it off doctor : distillation warns that it uses the Claude Code CLI's default model the install was made without --distill-model . Install again with it: chloectx install --project /path/to/project --distill-model haiku for a project install (the warning names --user , which is for every session) doctor : last distillation failed with something other than the login the line shows what the CLI wrote on stderr and its exit status (an old CLI that does not know a flag, a crash, a proxy), and doctor makes one small model call to say whether it still fails doctor : daemon's Claude Code CLI fails the running daemon was started by a process that has no claude on its PATH , and it is not in the usual places either: set CHLOECTX_CLAUDE to its path, or chloectx stop so that the next Claude Code hook starts the daemon a project folder was moved or renamed, and its sessions start with an empty memory stores are kept by the folder's path: chloectx move OLD NEW attaches the old store to the new folder. stores marks a store whose folder went missing in the last week, or is on a drive that is not mounted, and prune leaves it alone. A linked git worktree uses its main checkout's store, whatever folder it is in doctor in a git worktree: hooks fails with none in ~/.claude/settings.json or …/.claude, and the chloectx plugin is not enabled a project install writes its hooks into that folder's .claude/settings.local.json , which git does not carry into other worktrees. Run chloectx install --project in the worktree too (it then shares the main checkout's store), or install for every session a project should not be recorded add \"env\": {\"CHLOECTX_DISABLE\": \"1\"} to that project's .claude/settings.local.json , or set CHLOECTX_DISABLE=1 in the shell you start claude from the memory tools are still there with CHLOECTX_DISABLE=1 it turns the hooks off, not the MCP server: start Claude Code with claude --disallowedTools mcp__chloectx to leave the tools out of a session too"],
["chloectx-inspector.html","The live inspector","","A local web app for watching ChloeCtx work: what the hooks store, what the distiller writes down and what recall injects, for your own sessions or for a chat in a scratch project. Every panel is a query against the project's ChloeDB file, made through the same daemon the hooks talk to. Nothing is simulated: chat turns are real claude -p runs. Try it in your browser A static copy replays a recorded session. Its SQL runs in a JavaScript model of ChloeDB over the recorded records and vectors. The inspector runs in two ways: chloectx ui points it at the stores of your own sessions, and demo/run.sh runs it with a chat column against a scratch project."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#on-your-own-stores-chloectx-ui","The live inspector","On your own stores: chloectx ui","Once ChloeCtx is installed , chloectx ui serves this page from the binary over every store under ~/.local/share/chloectx/projects (or the folder from before the rename, ~/.local/share/engram , on a machine that has it; or --home DIR ). Pick a store (one per project directory) and a session. The left column then replays the session from its records, in place of the demo's chat: the prompts, the answers and the narration between tool calls; each tool call, with its stored result loaded when you open it, and errors in red; the briefing injected at session start and, for every prompt, the recall decision, its gate score and the records it injected (each one a link); the notes the distiller kept after each turn, with what they superseded or closed, and the turn's episode card. A session that is still running grows as it goes, and the panels on the right work as described below, against the chosen store. Nothing is written to the stores: reads go through each project's daemon with the same read-only operations the demo allows, so the inspector can stay open while sessions write. It has no control to delete a record: copy the record's id from it and run chloectx forget ID in the project's folder ( forget something ). The server listens on 127.0.0.1 and wants the random token in the URL it prints (kept in a cookie after the first load); on a remote machine, forward the port with ssh -L 8765:127.0.0.1:8765 . The address keeps the view ( ?store=…&session=…&tab=knowledge ). The header shows the version the server runs and the commit it was built from, and whether the checkout it came from has a newer one: when the page opens it fetches the checkout's upstream branch (at most every ten minutes; check fetches again) and counts the commits since the build that change ChloeCtx or ChloeDB, as chloectx upgrade --check does. When there are some, Update runs chloectx upgrade (pull, build, install, doctor ) and shows its output as it goes, kept in upgrade.log beside the stores. The build takes several minutes. The page that started it keeps running the old binary: restart chloectx ui to use the new one. ChloeCtx's daemons restart new on the next hook, and new Claude Code sessions use it on their own. A pull that cannot fast-forward (local commits, a detached checkout) fails as it does on the command line, and nothing is installed."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#the-demo-chat-in-a-scratch-project","The live inspector","The demo: chat in a scratch project","option effect --model the model for the chat (default: the claude CLI's) --distill-model the model ChloeCtx distills each turn with (default: --model ) --embedder hash:256 a model-free hash embedder, so nothing needs downloading --data DIR where the scratch project and its store live (default ~/chloectx-demo ; ~/engram-demo on a machine that has that one from before the rename) --budget USD maximum spend per chat turn (default 2.0) The server is standard-library Python ( chloectx/demo/server.py ) and the page is one HTML file ( chloectx/demo/index.html ). Chat turns run with --setting-sources project,local , so your own plugins and hooks stay out of the demo. The browser can only call the daemon's read-only operations."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#a-walkthrough","The live inspector","A walkthrough","The screenshots on this page come from one short session recorded for this documentation, with a small model for the chat and for distillation and the hash embedder. Two turns in a first session: We're starting a small Python CLI for a community library to track book loans. Decisions: store loans in a JSON file (loans.json), not SQLite, because the librarian's laptop is locked down and nothing can be installed. Always use ISO 8601 dates. Later we will need an overdue report that prints at most 40 characters per line, for the receipt printer. For now, create loans.py with add and return commands. Keep it short. Add a small unittest for add and return, and run it. Then a new Claude Code session , whose context starts empty: Add the overdue report now. Before you start, tell me what storage we chose and why, and any constraint on the report's output. The new session answered correctly: JSON in loans.json because nothing can be installed on the librarian's laptop, only the standard library, and at most 40 characters per line for the receipt printer. None of it was in the new session's context when it started. It came from the briefing ChloeCtx injected at SessionStart (1,741 characters), built from the knowledge the distiller wrote after the first turn. Recall for the prompt itself was skipped by the gate (G = 3.61 on 30 candidates), because the briefing had already delivered what mattered. By the end, the three turns had become 42 records in three segments: prompts, tool calls and results, answers, episode cards, entities, and the distilled decision, rule and planned work."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#the-panels","The live inspector","The panels",""],
["chloectx-inspector.html#finding-an-entity-or-a-file","The live inspector","Finding an entity or a file","The box at the top right completes the names of entities and the paths of files as you type ( count finds count sheet , src/ the files under src ), most linked first: an entity row counts the records that link to it. It is a prefix query on the id column, PREFIX(id, 'e:count') for names and PREFIX(id, 'f:src/') for paths, ordered by that count. When the exact prefix leaves room in the list of ten, names one typo away (two from six letters) follow, marked as typos, so cuont still finds count sheet . Picking one opens it with the records that link to it. The graph's seed box completes the same way."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#live","The live inspector","Live","Every record the hooks and the distiller write, and every daemon decision as it happens: SESSIONSTART with the briefing size, RECALL with the gate score and its evidence features ( max_cos , max_cov , bm_gap , cos_gap , agree , cue ), and DISTILL with the items kept and the cost."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#knowledge","The live inspector","Knowledge","The durable knowledge: decisions, preferences (rules), facts and planned work. Each item says where it came from ( distilled by the model, or cue when a sentence like \"Always use ISO 8601 dates\" was picked up directly from the prompt), and links to the turn and entities it is about. Superseded and completed items can be shown too."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#table","The live inspector","Table","The memory table itself, paged, filterable by kind, session and a MATCH expression."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#graph","The live inspector","Graph","Records are linked to the turn they came from, the files they touch ( f:loans.json ) and the entities they are about ( e:receipt printer ). These are ChloeDB EDGES fields, so the panel can run REACHABLE traversals from any seed, as in the graph queries ."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#vectors","The live inspector","Vectors","Every record's vector, projected on its first two principal components. A text query is embedded with the store's embedder and its top ten neighbours are drawn and listed with their similarity. Here why JSON instead of SQLite finds the distilled decision first (0.415)."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#query","The live inspector","Query","An SQL console over the store, read-only, with the executor's plan and timings. A text parameter bound as a vector is embedded by the store's embedder, so hybrid queries work as written. It can also run ChloeCtx's own hybrid search, the one recall uses."],
["chloectx-inspector.html#storage","The live inspector","Storage","The table's schema and fields, its segments, the memtable, and the file's size by component."],
["chloectx-capsules.html","Capsules: handing work to the next agent","","A capsule is a slice of a project's memory packed into one read-only ChloeDB file, which one agent hands to another. The receiving agent does not read it: Claude Code mounts it as MCP tools, and the agent asks it questions, under its own budget, when it needs to. What we measured. A handoff note alone is enough when it is long and complete. A capsule pays off when the note is short and loses things, as a sub-agent's report usually does. With a short note (about 2,000 characters), the next agent solved 72% of follow-up tasks. With the same note plus a capsule, it solved 89%, about 20,000 more input tokens and $0.0013 more per run. A capsule answers in 8 to 9 ms and mounts in about 65 ms. Making one takes 0.2 s and no model calls."],
["chloectx-capsules.html#use-it","Capsules: handing work to the next agent","Use it","pack writes a directory with two files: memory.strata : the chosen records with their vectors, links and indexes, in one file. By default it holds decisions, rules ( preference ), facts, open tasks, notes, the entities they link to, and the conversation (prompts, replies, episodes, summaries, errors). Raw tool calls and their results stay behind, because they are bulky and where secrets pass. Every record is redacted again on the way out. manifest.json : what it holds (counts by kind, the entities it is about), the embedder its vectors need, the source store's last sequence number, and the file's SHA-256. mount serves one or more capsules over stdio as capsule_ask (the question answered from the capsule, packed to a token budget, with no model call), capsule_search , capsule_timeline and capsule_sql , the same code as ChloeCtx's memory_* tools. It copies the file into a private temporary directory, checks the copy against the manifest's hash and opens it, so mounting never changes the capsule. A capsule can come from someone else, so mount treats it as data: a file that carries stored functions or rank profiles (which pack never writes, and which could call a model with your API key from inside capsule_sql ) is refused, the embedder must be the one the manifest names (or one given with --embedder ), and the manifest's title and topics are cut to one line of bounded length and shown as the packer's words, not instructions. With ChloeCtx installed in the receiving project, the capsule tools' calls and answers are not recorded into that project's memory, whatever the MCP server is named. A record that redaction changed while packing gets a vector of its redacted text, --max-records keeps the newest records, and the manifest names the project's folder, not its path. Its MCP instructions say what the capsule holds, so the agent knows when to ask. --log FILE (or CHLOECTX_CAPSULE_LOG ) writes one JSON line per call with its time. Not yet done: signatures (the hash names the file but does not say who made it), the receipts' named snapshots, and peer queries against another agent's live memory."],
["chloectx-capsules.html#results","Capsules: handing work to the next agent","Results","Method. The four held-out projects of the memory benchmark (stockroom, slotwise, bakehouse, kestrel; 52 follow-up tasks) were used. ChloeCtx recorded each project's history once. Every handoff lane then ran on that same history and repository, with ChloeCtx turned off for the run (no hooks, no memory tools), and handed the next agent one of the following: lane what the next agent gets nothing nothing long note a handoff note in its system prompt. Haiku wrote it from the transcripts of every history session, with no length limit (5,800 to 12,100 characters) short note a note Haiku was asked to keep under 1,500 characters (it wrote 1,900 to 2,300), about the size of a sub-agent's final report capsule the capsule, mounted as MCP tools long note + capsule, short note + capsule both ChloeCtx ChloeCtx itself: briefing, recall and memory tools whole history a fork of one session that holds the whole history, as an upper bound Each task ran four times per lane, for 1,664 runs. Haiku did all the work. The harness is chloectx/scripts/memory_bench.py (its BENCH_HANDOFF variants), the pooling is capsule_report.py , and every run, note and history is in chloectx/results/capsules/ . The runs were measured on 12 November 2026 in a 4-core cloud container, with four projects running side by side, and that load is in the wall times. lane solved needs an earlier session: solved $ per run input tokens per run, p50 wall s, p50 / p95 runs that asked the capsule or memory nothing 94/208 (45%) 42/156 0.0047 131,037 15 / 31 0 short note 149/208 (72%) 97/156 0.0048 132,284 17 / 35 0 capsule 189/208 (91%) 137/156 0.0063 160,170 18 / 37 154 short note + capsule 186/208 (89%) 134/156 0.0061 147,227 18 / 39 147 long note 198/208 (95%) 146/156 0.0049 139,422 17 / 39 0 long note + capsule 198/208 (95%) 146/156 0.0061 150,554 18 / 39 125 ChloeCtx 197/208 (95%) 145/156 0.0071 + 0.0019 (its query engine) 165,094 20 / 37 50 whole history 198/208 (95%) 146/156 0.0117 194,426 11 / 32 0 Paired comparisons (labels shuffled within each task, 20,000 times): comparison solved p input tokens per run wall s per run short note + capsule against short note +17.8 points < 0.0001 +20,340 +1.7 long note + capsule against long note 0 points 1.0 +21,646 +1.2 capsule alone against long note −4.3 points 0.011 +30,998 +0.7 capsule alone against nothing +45.7 points < 0.0001 +24,753 +3.3 What this shows: A complete note is as good as anything here. A long note did as well as ChloeCtx and as the whole history in context, at 42% of the whole history's cost per run. Adding a capsule to it bought nothing and added about 22,000 input tokens per run. A capsule recovers what a short note drops. The short note lost facts on two of the four projects (slotwise 28/40, bakehouse 34/72). Adding the capsule brought them to 38/40 and 60/72. On the other two projects the short note was already enough. Agents do use it. With a capsule mounted, the agent asked it in 60 to 74% of runs, almost always through capsule_ask . The cost is the answer's size, not the engine. Each capsule_ask returned about 16,000 characters (about 4,000 tokens, the default budget), which accounts for most of the extra input. A smaller default budget is the next thing to try. The capsule's own numbers: measure value records and size 273 to 770 records; 0.95 to 2.5 MB (3.3 to 3.5 KB a record, vectors and indexes included) chloectx pack , backup and cut included 161 to 223 ms model calls to pack or to query none (its notes were distilled while ChloeCtx recorded the history, which cost $0.03 to $0.12 per project) mount ready, hash check and copy included 61 to 67 ms p50, 82 to 87 ms p95 one call 8 to 9 ms p50, 11 to 14 ms p95, 18 ms at most, over 433 calls A handoff note cost $0.007 to $0.019 to write. Both are made once per handoff. Limits. Four small projects, one history each, and one small model. The tasks were written alongside the histories and aim at facts the histories state. The long note was written from complete transcripts by a model asked for completeness, which is the best case for a note. A sub-agent writing from its own context, under its own budget, is closer to the short note."],
["chloectx-receipts.html","Receipts and chloectx blame","","ChloeCtx puts records from earlier work into an agent's context: on every prompt, after a compaction, and when the agent calls memory_search . Each of those recalls now writes a receipt , and the store is kept as a snapshot after a recall that showed something, so any recall can be replayed later on exactly the data it read. chloectx blame starts from a line of code and walks back to the edit that wrote it, the prompt behind the edit, and the receipts of what memory had put in the agent's context before it. (The values above are made up to show the layout.)"],
["chloectx-receipts.html#what-a-receipt-holds","Receipts and chloectx blame","What a receipt holds","field what id , ts , session , turn , source which recall: prompt , compact or search snapshot the store's commit the recall read ( txn , and seq_no in a store that numbers its writes), the newest record's number (recency is measured from it) and the record count inputs everything else the result depends on: the query, the session's live window, the records already shown to the session, and the ranking, gate, packing and knowledge parameters as they were decision , gate , evidence the gate's score and what it decided ( Inject , Brief , Skip ) rows each candidate (up to 40): its rank, final score, raw BM25 and cosine, importance, distance from the newest record, the tokens it took or would have taken, a hash of its text, and the decision text , text_sha , tokens the context the agent was given, as given A row's decision is one of knowledge (a decision, rule or fact shown on its own text, ahead of the gate), take , excerpt (taken as a snippet), listed (named by id under \"Further candidates\", or a memory_search result), or skip: with the reason: shown before , over budget , per-turn cap , record cap , uncertain , same question , shown as knowledge , the gate held back , below the limit . The words are those of ChloeDB's PACK receipts, so a ChloeCtx receipt and a PACK ... WITH RECEIPT answer read alike, and tokens are counted as PACK counts them ( chloedb::query::pack::count_tokens ). Receipts live in their own ChloeDB file beside the store, receipts.strata (table receipts ). The store's file and its format are unchanged. A receipt is written once the hook has answered, so it adds no write to the prompt's critical path. The file is flushed every 256 receipts and when the daemon stops, so its write-ahead log (replayed when a daemon starts) stays small, and receipts older than 90 days, or past the newest 20,000, are dropped when the daemon starts."],
["chloectx-receipts.html#kept-snapshots","Receipts and chloectx blame","Kept snapshots","After a recall that showed records, once the hook has answered and before anything else is written, the daemon keeps a snapshot of the store, at most once an hour ( min_interval_secs , 3600 by default): snapshots/auto-<commit>/ in the store's directory, a checkpoint in ChloeDB's directory layout. It is not taken when the store has not changed since the last one. What a snapshot costs: Time. The checkpoint flushes the store's memtable, then reads every segment whole and checks its checksums, and checks the copy: a few passes over the store, whether or not a segment is linked. Tenths of a second for a store of tens of MB; seconds for one of a GB. This runs after the hook has answered; when it runs, the prompt's own record is stored after it, and chloectx stop waits for both. Space. Each snapshot hard-links the segment files of the one before it that no merge has replaced since, and writes the rest. The live store is a single file, which a directory snapshot cannot share bytes with, so the first snapshot is a full copy, and every segment a merge writes is copied again by the next snapshot while older ones keep its inputs. The store's smallest merge tier spans up to 8,000 live rows, so each snapshot's flush makes a small segment that is merged again and again within it, and each snapshot keeps one of those outputs. With no budget, a simulation of the merge policy at 50 turns a day, 60 records a turn and hourly snapshots over an 8-hour day (the fourth audit, not a measurement) gave snapshots of about 3 times the store after a week (143 MB beside 46.5 MB) and 4 times after 30 days (1.16 GB beside 279 MB); taken after every turn ( min_interval_secs: 0 ), about 10 times after a week. On a file system where hard links fail (some network and FUSE mounts, exFAT), every snapshot is a full copy, and chloectx doctor says so. So the automatic snapshots are kept to a budget: at most the store's size ( budget_store_ratio , 1.0; at least 32 MB; max_bytes sets one in bytes, and 0 for both sets none), counting each file once however many snapshots link it, and counting named snapshots too, which are never dropped for it. Past it, the oldest automatic ones go, never the newest. Automatic snapshots older than 3 days are thinned to the last of each day ( daily_after_days ; 0 keeps them as taken). Those older than keep_days (30), or past the newest max_auto (2,000), are dropped too. All of this runs when the daemon starts and before each new snapshot, whatever the settings say now. Anything under snapshots/ that no snapshot owns (a checkpoint a stopped daemon left half written) is removed when the daemon starts. chloectx stats shows the bytes of the store, the receipts and the snapshots, and the budget ( receipts , snapshots , total_bytes ), and chloectx doctor says them in one line, disk . A receipt replays exactly on the snapshot kept at its commit: the replay runs the same retrieval code ( receipt::retrieve , which the recall itself runs) with the receipt's inputs, and must give the same rows, scores and decisions. When no snapshot was kept at that commit (a recall that showed nothing, or one inside min_interval_secs ), it replays on the nearest later one and says so. With hourly snapshots that is most receipts: about one in six replays exactly at 6 recalls an hour, and the rest on the next snapshot, whose store holds the records written since; after 3 days only the last snapshot of each day is left, and after the budget fills, fewer. It is also replayed on today's store, which shows what has changed since: a record superseded, deleted or outranked. chloectx snapshot keep NAME keeps one by name, until chloectx snapshot drop NAME ; chloectx snapshot list lists them. Settings, in the store's config.json : chloectx receipts off turns receipts and automatic snapshots off for the project's store (it writes receipts.enabled in its config.json ), and chloectx receipts off --purge also removes the receipts and automatic snapshots kept so far; chloectx receipts on turns them back on. Without receipts.enabled in config.json , CHLOECTX_RECEIPTS=0 in Claude Code's environment turns them off: each hook sends it to the daemon, whichever process started the daemon. With receipts off, memory_forget and chloectx redact still scrub the receipts kept before."],
["chloectx-receipts.html#commands","Receipts and chloectx blame","Commands","blame path:line reads the line from a regular file inside the project, after links and .. are resolved, of at most 8 MiB; any other path is refused, so neither the command center's Blame tab nor the daemon's socket can be used to read a file elsewhere. Every memory_search answer ends with its receipt's id, so an agent (or the person reading its transcript) can ask why a record ranked where it did."],
["chloectx-receipts.html#in-the-command-center","Receipts and chloectx blame","In the command center","ChloeCtx's page in ChloeDB's command center ( chloedb-server --app chloectx=URL , then Apps → ChloeCtx) lists every space it can read; a Claude Code store of the machine the app runs on opens with three more tabs (Receipts, Blame, Snapshots and capsules) beside Overview, Ask, Memories, Sessions, Graph and Connect ( #/app/chloectx/store/<store>/receipts , …/blame/<path:line> ): Receipts : the store's newest receipts (when, which recall, what it was for, records and tokens shown, the gate's decision, the session and turn). Open one for its candidates (rank, score with a bar, BM25, cosine, tokens, decision), the query and the gate, the text the agent was shown, and the replay on the kept snapshot and on today's store. The kept snapshots are under Snapshots and capsules : keep one by name, drop a named one, or cut a capsule (pick the kinds of record) with the claude mcp add line that mounts it. Blame : type path:line . It shows the line, its commit, the edit, the prompt and the tool calls before it, the notes from the turn, each receipt of what memory had recalled before the edit (with what has become of each record since), the replay, and a \"worth a look\" list. Each receipt links to its page under Receipts. They read the Claude Code stores of the machine chloectx app runs on, so they are off unless it is started with --local-stores (or CHLOECTX_APP_LOCAL=1 ): a store is a transcript of its sessions, and every user of the command center could read it. Without it the tabs say how to turn it on. A store whose daemon is stopped is started when read. The API behind them, under the app's prefix ( /v1/apps/chloectx ): request answer GET /v1/local this machine's stores GET /v1/local/:store/receipts?session=&limit= the newest receipts GET /v1/local/:store/receipts/:id?replay=0 one receipt, replayed unless replay=0 GET /v1/local/:store/blame?target=path:line blame, as chloectx blame --json GET , POST {name} /v1/local/:store/snapshots , DELETE /v1/local/:store/snapshots/:name the kept snapshots; keep one; drop one GET , POST {title?, kinds?, since_days?} /v1/local/:store/capsules the capsules cut from the store, newest first; one cut, its manifest and folder GET /v1/local/:store/overview , /sessions , /records , /notes[/:id] , /graph , POST /v1/local/:store/ask what the other tabs read, as a team space's calls of the same names answer ( the app ) GET /v1/local/:store/stats chloectx stats --json"],
["chloectx-receipts.html#how-blame-finds-the-edit","Receipts and chloectx blame","How blame finds the edit","Blame reads the line from the file and looks among the stored tool calls ( Edit , MultiEdit , Write , NotebookEdit ) of that file for the newest that added it: the line is a whole line of the edit's new text and not of the text it replaced (an edit that only kept the line around its change did not write it). A relative path is taken from where chloectx blame runs; in a request to the daemon, from the project. When none does (the line was written outside a recorded session, or changed by hand since), it takes the newest edit of the file before the line's commit (from git blame ), and says which it did. Stored edits keep up to 1,100 characters of their new text, so a line deep inside a long Write may only be found this second way. The edit's turn gives the prompt, the tool calls before the edit and the notes distilled from the turn. The receipts of that turn, and of up to three earlier turns of the session that showed records, give what memory had put in context. Each record shown is checked against the store as it is now: deleted, superseded (and by what), or from an untrusted source (a tool's output; the web for WebFetch and WebSearch )."],
["chloectx-receipts.html#forgetting-and-redaction","Receipts and chloectx blame","Forgetting and redaction","A receipt and a snapshot hold copies of what was recalled. So memory_forget (and the forget request) scrubs the text of the receipts that showed the forgotten records (their rows keep ids and scores, and lose the record's text hash) and removes a forgotten prompt from the query of the receipts it was asked as, in one pass over the receipts however many records went. Forgetting by text ( memory_forget with text , chloectx forget TEXT ) also replaces every string of every receipt that holds it (a query, the text shown), and then receipts.strata is compacted and its free pages written with zeros. The forget drops every automatic snapshot, and rewrites the store's segments that held the records without them, with no snapshot taken until that is done, so no snapshot taken afterwards holds their bytes. Only the segments that held them are rewritten (those whose live rows fell with the delete, and those a flush or a merge wrote since that still hold them, marked deleted), not every segment with an older delete, and then the free pages of the store's file, where the old segments were, are written with zeros ( Database::zero_free_pages ); pages an open snapshot still reads are left, and said. memory_forget answers before the rewrite, which runs after the answer, and chloectx forget waits for it. Named snapshots are kept and listed, for you to drop. chloectx redact redacts the receipts as it does the records, and drops the automatic snapshots too. Replays of the receipts before then can no longer be exact."],
["chloectx-app.html","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","","chloectx app runs ChloeCtx beside a ChloeDB cluster, as the crawler and Sheets do, and the command center shows it under Apps. Its memory is not a file on one laptop: it lives in the cluster's tables, so any number of agents and people can write to it and ask it for context. Its first page lists every memory it can read, as spaces to pick from, and a space opens on what it knows now: Team spaces. Memory that any agent writes notes and conversations to and asks for context before a task, within a token budget: a table of the cluster each. An answer is found by search alone (BM25 and vectors, no model call), and it says what went in, why, and what was left out. Claude Code projects. What Claude Code sessions remembered: a project chloectx sync copied into the cluster (a table per project, chloectx_mem_<project> ; chloectx_memory for one synced before 0.3.4), and, with --local-stores , the stores of the machine the app runs on, where Receipts, Blame and capsules are too ( Receipts and blame ). A store and its synced copy show as one project. Seen before , a page of its own: the cluster's firing alerts, each beside the past incidents it looks like and the fix that worked. One button hands the alert to the cluster's on-call agent with those incidents as its context. The demo's team space, asked \"Who approves a refund of 1,200 EUR?\". The answer leads with the current decision (over 1000 EUR or 900 GBP, a second approver from finance) and what it replaced, then what else bears on it; the scores, what was left out and the context as the agent gets it are below. 14.4 ms, no model call."],
["chloectx-app.html#start-it","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Start it","A chloedb-server with chloectx installed beside it (in the same directory, as an install puts them) starts it by itself, with a token and a key of its own ( apps the server starts ); its key may make and write ctx_* tables and read chloectx_mem_* and chloectx_memory , so the on-call hand-off of Seen before, which needs an admin key, is refused there. Started by hand: option default what --server ( CHLOEDB_URL ) http://127.0.0.1:7700 the chloedb-server (a coordinator, or a server on its own) the spaces live in --key ( CHLOEDB_KEY ) none its key for the app's reads and writes: an admin key, since a new space is a new table and the on-call agent takes admin calls --token ( CHLOECTX_APP_TOKEN ) none the token every caller must send ( Authorization: Bearer ); chloedb-server sends its CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN . Without one, chloectx app refuses to start unless --insecure --insecure off serve any caller with no token; without it, chloectx app refuses to start with no token, on loopback too, since whoever reached the port (on loopback, any program of the machine) could read and write every space and start model calls billed to its key. On loopback it then answers only requests whose Host is this machine ( 127.0.0.1 , localhost , [::1] ), so that a page reaching it through DNS rebinding is refused --host , --port 127.0.0.1 , 7760 where it listens --embedder ( CHLOECTX_EMBEDDER ) static hash:<dim> for tests without the model Model calls (writing a conversation's notes down, drafting an incident from a postmortem) go to Anthropic's API when ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set (model CHLOECTX_APP_MODEL , by default Haiku), else to the claude CLI when it is on the PATH ( CHLOECTX_DISTILL_MODEL ). CHLOECTX_APP_MODEL=off turns them off. Everything else, asking included, works without a model. At most four conversations are distilled at once and 256 wait; past that an exchange is stored without notes (its answer says skipped , and /v1/info counts it under distill.skipped ). The app serves 64 requests at once and answers one more with 503. The app refuses requests that carry an Origin header. chloedb-server checks the signed-in key and reaches the app with the app's token and who called ( x-chloedb-scope , and x-chloedb-tables for a key limited to tables); start the app with --token wherever it runs (see Security model ). The app's manifest says \"checks_callers\": true , and it refuses what the caller's key may not do itself: reading a space or asking it needs read on its table ( ctx_<space> ), adding or retiring notes and exchanges write there, making a space admin there (it makes the table), the incidents are the space incidents 's ( ctx_incidents ), and what is not a space's (building apps, handing an alert to the on-call agent, this machine's stores) needs an admin key of every table to change and a key of every table to read. So a write key records notes, and a key limited to ctx_ops_*=write writes the spaces ops_* and nothing else ( GET /v1/spaces lists the spaces it reads). A request with none of those headers (a program calling the app itself with its token) may do anything, as before."],
["chloectx-app.html#on-kubernetes","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","On Kubernetes","A cluster the operator runs gets ChloeCtx from its StrataCluster , with nothing to start by hand: The operator runs chloectx app from the cluster's own image (it carries it, as it carries the crawler and the spreadsheets) as a Deployment of one pod, <cluster>-chloectx , behind a Service of that name on port 7760, and restarts the coordinators so that they list it: Apps → ChloeCtx appears in the command center. It reads and writes with the cluster's admin token ( tokenSecret ), which its API requires too, so the coordinators send it that token and nothing else reaches it (a cluster without a tokenSecret is open to whoever reaches it, and the operator starts its ChloeCtx with --insecure to match), and it calls the model with the cluster's secretEnv ( ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ; without one, everything but writing a conversation's notes down and drafting incidents works). The embedding model is not in the image, which every server pulls: an init container downloads it from Hugging Face (125 MB) into the pod's scratch volume each time the pod starts. The spaces are tables of the cluster, so a pod started again (a new image, a node replaced) has them all; the upgrader moves it with the servers, since it runs their image (from the image of 2026-11-10 on: an older one has no chloectx ). spec.chloectx default paused false no pod for now (the coordinators still list it, as an app that does not answer; the spaces stay) embedder static hash:<dim> for a cluster that cannot reach Hugging Face: no download, vectors from hashed words. Notes keep the vectors they were written with, so after a change earlier ones match by their words only args , resources more arguments of chloectx app , and its container's resources (50m CPU and 128 MiB asked, 1 GiB at most; it uses about 40 MiB) Removing spec.chloectx deletes its Deployment and Service and restarts the coordinators without it; the spaces stay in ctx_spaces and ctx_<space> ."],
["chloectx-app.html#where-the-memory-is","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Where the memory is","Each space is a table of the cluster, ctx_<space> , with ChloeCtx's own records ( the data model ): notes ( decision , preference for rules, fact , todo , note ), messages ( prompt , answer ), entities, and links between them. The spaces themselves are rows of ctx_spaces . A table per space keeps BM25's statistics per space, so one team's words do not weigh on another's ranking, and SQL reads a space like any table: The app keeps nothing of its own: a restart, or another copy of it, finds every space in the cluster. It numbers the records it writes, so one app writes to a cluster's spaces at a time. Notes are never overwritten. A note that replaces another marks the older one superseded and links them both ways ( supersedes , superseded_by ); a context shows the current version with what it changed, and the history keeps every version."],
["chloectx-app.html#the-api","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","The API","JSON over HTTP; through chloedb-server, the same paths under /v1/apps/chloectx . call body answer GET /v1/spaces , POST /v1/spaces {name, title?, description?, kind?} the spaces with their counts ( by_kind , every record; live_by_kind , the notes not replaced, retired or done); the new space (one that exists is left as it is); kind is notes (the default) or incidents , a team's own incidents for Seen before DELETE /v1/spaces/:s the space, deleted by an admin of its table: its table ctx_<space> dropped and its row of ctx_spaces removed, {deleted, table, kind} ; it cannot be undone POST /v1/spaces/:s/notes {kind, text, replaces?, entities?, session?, when?} the note ( kind : decision , rule , fact , todo or note ); when (or ts ) is when it happened, for a note written down after the fact: seconds since 1970, or ISO 8601 text ( 2026-09-14 , 2026-09-14T03:10:00Z ); by default, now GET /v1/spaces/:s/notes?kind=&limit=&all=1 the notes, newest first; the current ones unless all=1 GET /v1/spaces/:s/notes/:id the note, the versions it replaced and those that replaced it DELETE /v1/spaces/:s/notes/:id the note, retired: kept, tagged retired (not superseded : nothing replaced it), and in no context again POST /v1/spaces/:s/exchanges {user, assistant, distill?} the two messages; with a model, their decisions, rules and facts follow as notes in a few seconds ( GET /v1/info counts them) POST /v1/spaces/:s/ask (or GET ?q=&budget= ) {question, budget?} {context, tokens, ms, items, left_out} ; budget is 200 to 50,000 tokens (4,000 left out), and one outside that is refused GET /v1/incidents?space= , POST /v1/incidents {title, symptom, cause, fix, alert?, replaces?, when?, space?} the incidents of every incidents space the key reads (or of space ), each with its space and the fixes it replaced; the new one, in incidents or the team's space , dated when (the incident's own date) GET /v1/incidents/firing?space= the cluster's firing and pending alerts, each with its closest incidents and the context the on-call agent would get GET /v1/incidents/match?q=&alert=&space= the incidents closest to a description POST /v1/incidents/match {lines \\| text, space?, alert?, n?} the incidents closest to log lines pasted from an outage (redacted as notes are, at most 4,000 characters) POST /v1/incidents/draft {text} or {run} incidents a model drafted from a postmortem or an on-call run's report; nothing is kept POST /v1/incidents/handoff {alert} the on-call agent's run, started with the closest incidents POST /v1/spaces/:s/apps/suggest {n?} (2 or 3) apps a model suggests from the space's notes: {apps:[{title, pitch, why, kinds, description}]} ; nothing is built GET /v1/spaces/:s/apps/estimate what a build costs: the median of the app engine's past first builds, its model and what is left of its cap POST /v1/spaces/:s/apps/items {kinds?, exclude?} the notes a build would copy POST /v1/spaces/:s/apps {title, description, kinds?, exclude?, confirm: true} the build, started (409 without confirm , 402 when the engine's cap is spent) GET /v1/spaces/:s/apps the space's builds: building , loading , ready or failed , with their cost and rows loaded POST /v1/spaces/:s/apps/:id/open a one-time link into the app engine's studio, at the built app An answer of ask has, for each record in the context, its section ( rule , note , timeline for a question about the whole history, reply and excerpt for past conversations), its tokens (about four characters each), its scores ( bm25 for the question's words, cos for its meaning) and, for a note that replaced another, replaced : what it changed. left_out names what the searches found and the context leaves out, and why: superseded (a later note replaced it), retired , uncertain , or budget . The context is built as ChloeCtx's own memory_ask builds it for Claude Code ( how ), with its sizes scaled to the budget."],
["chloectx-app.html#a-space-as-an-mcp-server","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","A space as an MCP server","POST /v1/spaces/:s/mcp speaks the Model Context Protocol (streamable HTTP, JSON answers; a GET gets 405) with three tools on that space: ask ( question , budget : the context, as ask above, with the ids of its notes), remember ( kind , text , replaces? , entities? : a note, session mcp ) and notes ( kind? , limit? : the current ones with their ids). A space that does not exist is 404, not a new space. To connect Claude Code, through chloedb-server: A space's page in the command center has a Connect Claude Code card that makes the key and shows this line with it filled in: a key limited to the space's table, ctx_billing=write , which may read and write that space and nothing else (below)."],
["chloectx-app.html#keys-limited-to-tables","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Keys limited to tables","chloedb-server lets a key limited to tables call the app, because the app's manifest says \"limited_keys\": true : it tells the app the key's grants ( x-chloedb-tables ), and the app, which writes with a key of its own, holds the caller to them: a space is its table ctx_<space> (read to read and ask it, MCP included, write to add, replace or retire notes and hand over exchanges), the incidents are ctx_incidents (read, write to record or draft), a Claude Code project is read from its table ( chloectx_mem_<project> , or chloectx_memory for one synced before 0.3.4), and GET /v1/memory lists the projects whose tables the key reads. A key for operations (role propose or ops , which the server says in x-chloedb-role ) reads no Claude Code project and no local store: those are people's sessions. Creating a space and building apps from one take an unlimited admin's key, and no limited key reads the machine's local stores (Receipts, Blame). GET /v1/spaces lists the spaces the key may read. A caller with no such header (an admin's key, the server's token) is not limited."],
["chloectx-app.html#spaces-and-what-each-shows","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Spaces and what each shows","#/app/chloectx lists the spaces, grouped (team spaces; Claude Code projects) with what each holds, and a filter. A space's page ( #/app/chloectx/space/<name> , …/project/<owner/name> , …/store/<store> ) has a switcher to another space and these tabs: Overview : how many rules, decisions, facts and open to-dos it holds now, its sessions and records, the rules (which go into every context), the latest decisions and facts, the open to-dos, what changed (notes that replaced an earlier one, with the old text struck through) and the latest sessions. Ask : a question, answered as an agent would be: the note that answers it first, the rules that apply, what else bears on it and what past conversations said, then the scores behind each (BM25, cosine, section, tokens), what was left out and why, and the context as the agent gets it. A link carries the question ( …/ask/<question> ). A synced project, which has no store to build a context from, answers with its records ranked by BM25. Memories : every note, by kind, current or with its history; a note opens on the versions it replaced and those that replaced it. A team space's notes can be replaced or retired here. Sessions : each session titled by its first prompt, read as the conversation it recorded: turn by turn, the prompt, the tool calls (folded), the answer and the notes written down from the turn. Every record searches all of them (BM25) and filters by kind. A record opens in a drawer with its whole text (up to 4,000 characters), its context, files, links and id. Graph : how its memory is connected: each note (a circle, by kind; a replaced one hollow), the subjects it is about (entities, larger the more notes name them), the files it touched, the session it came from (off by default) and the note it replaced (a dashed link). A click lights up a node's neighbours and lists them, with its history, its session, Ask about it or Blame it ; a search finds a subject, file or note; the link carries the selected node ( …/graph/e:refund ). Connect : how to use the space. A team space: the claude mcp add line with a key limited to its table, the HTTP calls and the SQL that read it, and apps built from it. A Claude Code project: its MCP tools, its CLI, and how to sync it here. Receipts , Blame , Snapshots and capsules : a store of this machine only ( Receipts and blame ). The older links ( #/app/chloectx/inspector/<project> , …/receipts/<store> , …/blame/<store> ) open the same space's tab. Until a project is synced, the projects group offers a setup instead: given the project ( owner/name ) and the machine, a button (for an admin) makes the project's table and a key that may read and write only it, named for the machine, and shows the chloectx sync setup command with the key filled in. One key per project and machine: a key of one project reads no other's sessions, and a key revoked stops one machine. Rules and decisions one machine sends reach another's sessions once someone there accepts it ( chloectx sync accept ). This needs ChloeCtx 0.3.4 or later; an older one syncs every project into chloectx_memory . GET /v1/memory/table?project= answers the table's name and CREATE TABLE. A project is its git repository, so every checkout shares one memory ( keep the memory on a ChloeDB server ). What the tabs read, beside the space calls above: call answer GET /v1/spaces/:s/overview {records, sessions, first, last, by_kind, live, rules, decisions, facts, todos, changes, recent_sessions} : live counts the current notes by kind, each list holds the newest 12, changes the notes that replaced another with was (the older texts) GET /v1/spaces/:s/sessions {sessions: [{session, records, first, last, turns, title}], by_kind} : the latest first; title is the first prompt's first line, turns its prompts GET /v1/spaces/:s/records?session=&kind=&q=&limit=&before= {records, more} : a session's in order, else the latest first ( before , a seq , pages back), or the best matches for q (BM25); at most 500 GET /v1/spaces/:s/graph {nodes, edges} : nodes {id, type, label} of type note (with kind , text , tag , current , ts , session ), entity , file ( f:<path> ) and session ( s:<id> , its title); edges {s, t, label} : about , touches , from (the session) and supersedes ; the newest 1,500 notes GET /v1/memory {table, exists, projects: [{project, records, sessions, last}], create} ( create : the table's CREATE TABLE ) GET /v1/memory/overview?project= , /v1/memory/notes?project=&kind=&all=1 , /v1/memory/graph?project= a synced project's overview, notes and graph, as a space's GET /v1/memory/sessions?project= , /v1/memory/records?project=&… its sessions and records, as a space's A store of this machine answers the same calls under /v1/local/:store/ ( the list )."],
["chloectx-app.html#a-demo-to-show-it","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","A demo to show it","chloectx/demo/seed.py plays a scripted Claude Code history into ChloeCtx, so every page has something to show: twenty sessions over four weeks on harbor , a made-up order and payments service (refunds, webhooks, rate limits, migrations, a double-refund incident, a release process), with corrections along the way (a refund limit raised twice, a currency added, a rate limit changed for partners, a debug print that logged a key). It runs the real hooks and daemon, each turn at its date in the story ( CHLOECTX_CLOCK_OFFSET ), with a scripted distiller ( demo/scripted_distill.py ) in place of the model, so it makes no model call and takes about 15 seconds: --space writes the story into a new team space through the API ( --space-reuse adds to one that exists), --sync copies the store into the cluster as the project acme/harbor , in a table of its own, chloectx_mem_acme_harbor_58e121ba ( keep the memory on a ChloeDB server ), and --key-env names the variable holding the server's key. All of it is made up: no transcript of anyone's sessions is in it."],
["chloectx-app.html#seen-before","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Seen before","Seen before on a two-shard cluster in the cloud container (the alerts are real: its disk was over 90% full, and a test load raised the p99). \"Disk full within 12 hours\" finds the incident recorded for the same alert first, and the p99 alert the merge incident, whose first fix (a restart) was replaced by a better one. An incident is a note in the space incidents , or in a team's own space of kind incidents ( POST /v1/spaces {name, kind: \"incidents\"} ): a title, the symptom, the cause, the fix, and the alert rule it fired, if one did. Seen before reads every incidents space the signed-in key may read, so a team that keeps its own sees its incidents beside the shared ones, and log lines pasted from an outage find the incidents that look like them as an alert does. For each firing alert, it searches the incidents with what the alert says (its name, metric, description and the copies it fires on). The incidents recorded for the same rule come first; another must be close in meaning (cosine 0.3, or 0.2 when it shares a word) and at least half as close as the closest. A match shows the fixes it replaced struck through, so an agent does not repeat one that was given up. Hand to the on-call agent starts the cluster's on-call agent ( POST /v1/admin/oncall ) on the alert with the closest incidents and the incident space's rules in its question; the server needs a Claude key for it. When the run ends, keep what it found drafts an incident from its report for a person to check and record. A postmortem pasted into Record an incident is drafted the same way."],
["chloectx-app.html#create-an-app-from-a-space","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Create an app from a space","A space's page has Create an app from this context . Suggest apps asks the model for apps the space's notes could become (one call, on at most 150 notes); each says why, and which kinds of notes it reads. Build this... opens the build: its name, the request the app engine gets (editable), the kinds of notes, and every note that goes in, each with a tick to leave it out. Before anything is spent it shows what a build costs, the median of the engine's past first builds ( sa_requests ), and what is left of the engine's cap; the build starts only on Build it , which sends confirm: true . The cluster's app engine (the app apps , chloedb-apps ) builds the app from the request and leaves out its example rows. When it is done, ChloeCtx maps the picked notes, 30 at a time, onto the new app's tables with its model and inserts them through the engine; a row that names a note it was not given, or a table the app does not have, is dropped. The app gets copies of the picked notes and nothing else: no exchanges, no notes of other spaces, and later notes do not follow. Builds are rows of ctx_builds , and a restart follows those still building. Open in the studio asks the engine for a one-time link to the app."],
["chloectx-app.html#tests","ChloeCtx as an app of a ChloeDB cluster","Tests","chloectx/tests/app_cluster.rs runs the app against two shard servers and a coordinator of this build, with a stub of Anthropic's API: spaces, notes replaced and retired, contexts within a budget, an exchange distilled, incidents matched to an alert rule that fires, a handoff refused without a key, an app suggested and built from a space against a stub of the app engine (refused without confirm ), the inspector's overview, sessions and records of a space and of a local store, a capsule cut, and a restart. CI runs it in its server smoke test: CHLOECTX_UI_DIR=chloectx/ui serves the page from disk, without a rebuild."],
["apps.html","Apps on a cluster","","Some programs built on ChloeDB run beside chloedb-server and show in its command center under Apps : the crawler , which turns a site into a table, Sheets , spreadsheets whose cells are rows of ChloeDB, and ChloeCtx , memory spaces that agents write to and ask for context. Each is a program of its own that talks to the server over HTTP and serves a small API and a view; the server lists it and forwards to it. This page starts a server with Sheets and ChloeCtx beside it and uses both through the server, the way the command center does. You need chloedb-server , chloedb-sheets and chloectx on your PATH . since 0.3.2 From release 0.3.2, the installer puts the three there prebuilt, with nothing to compile: Release 0.3.0 and earlier have no prebuilt Sheets or ChloeCtx. With those, or to build from source, build each in its directory of a checkout of the repository ( cargo build --release ; Rust and a C compiler, several minutes) and add their target/release directories to PATH ."],
["apps.html#how-an-app-attaches","Apps on a cluster","How an app attaches","The server learns of an app from --app name=URL , and sends each request under /v1/apps/<name>/ on to it with a token of its own, CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN , in place of the caller's key. Callers never reach an app directly: the server checks their key first (read scope to read, admin to change anything), and an app refuses requests that do not carry the token. The apps keep everything in the server's tables, so a restart, or another copy of the app, finds it all there."],
["apps.html#start-a-server-and-two-apps","Apps on a cluster","Start a server and two apps","One token for the apps, a server that lists both, and the two apps pointed at the server: --start-apps off keeps the server from starting the apps installed beside it on its own (it does by default, which is all a first run needs; this page starts them by hand to show how they attach). --embedder hash:64 makes ChloeCtx's vectors from hashed words, which needs no download; leave it out to use its embedding model ( chloectx fetch-model fetches it once). Without --token the server listens on loopback only and anything on this machine that reaches 127.0.0.1:7700 can change it; give it a token, and its clients keys, before it serves anyone else ( security ). The server lists the apps once they answer, which is what the command center's Apps menu shows: Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 in a browser to use them; the rest of this page drives them from a script."],
["apps.html#sheets-a-workbook-with-a-formula-and-a-query","Apps on a cluster","Sheets: a workbook with a formula and a query","A small client for the apps' APIs through the server: A workbook of sales by region with a total, saved as a table of the server, then a SQL query of that table written into the sheet: The total is a formula, computed by IronCalc; the rows from row 8 are the query's answer, and Refresh in the view (or POST /v1/books/<id>/refresh ) runs it again when the table changes. The workbook's cells are themselves rows of the server, so the server can search every workbook at once."],
["apps.html#chloectx-a-memory-space-and-its-context","Apps on a cluster","ChloeCtx: a memory space and its context","A space is a table of the server, ctx_<space> , that agents write notes to and ask for context within a budget of tokens. A decision, then a newer one that replaces it: The context holds the current decision and says what it replaced; the old one is left out. No model is called to answer: it is a BM25 and vector search under the space's rules ( how ChloeCtx searches )."],
["apps.html#the-crawler","Apps on a cluster","The crawler","The crawler attaches the same way ( --app crawler=URL ), and chloedb-crawl serve --local DIR starts a cluster with it already listed. Crawl a site into search runs it end to end."],
["apps.html#on-kubernetes","Apps on a cluster","On Kubernetes","A cluster the operator runs gets each app from a field of its StrataCluster : spec.crawler , spec.sheets , spec.chloectx , spec.netops ( network operations ) and spec.apps (the app engine, a prototype). The operator runs them from the cluster's own image and lists them on its coordinators, with nothing to start by hand ( server on Kubernetes )."],
["crawler.html","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","","chloedb-crawl takes a URL, reads the site's pages, finds their fields, makes a table from them and fills it, and adds shards to the cluster as the table grows. It is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, so it runs on the machine next to the cluster or on another one; the command center shows it as an app."],
["crawler.html#run-it","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","Run it","On one machine, install both programs, then start the crawler with a cluster of its own (on Kubernetes, one field of the cluster's resource starts it: below ). since 0.3.2 The installer puts both in ~/.local/bin prebuilt, and the crawler finds the server beside it: Or build both from a checkout (any platform with Rust; the only way before release 0.3.2): Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → Crawler . Type the site's address under New crawl and press Crawl , or Preview first to see the table it would make; Options holds the rest of the settings below. Each crawl gets its own page, where you watch it fill the table and grow the cluster, and pause or stop it. --local DIR starts one shard server and a coordinator on 127.0.0.1:7700 with their databases and logs in DIR , and adds a shard each time one holds more than --split-rows rows of a crawl's table (100,000 by default, at most --max-shards , 8). The same DIR again serves the same cluster, with the shards it grew."],
["crawler.html#from-the-command-line","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","From the command line","The same crawl runs without the command center too. crawl takes the address, prints its progress every two seconds, then what it did (the table it made and why, the columns it added, the shards it added) and the CREATE TABLE it ran; add --stay to keep the cluster serving after the crawl. preview prints what the form's Preview shows, without ChloeDB: At the default pace, a page a second for one site ( below ), 600 pages take about ten minutes. books.toscrape.com is a sandbox made for crawlers, so --per-host 4 --delay-ms 100 is fine there; keep the defaults for sites you don't run."],
["crawler.html#against-a-cluster-that-runs-elsewhere","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","Against a cluster that runs elsewhere","The crawler writes to any chloedb-server, one server or a coordinator, with a key that can write (and an admin key to grow a cluster): --server was --strata before 0.3.2; the old name still works. and the coordinator shows it as an app when told where it is, with the crawler's token: --grow says how the cluster gets a shard: kube asks the Kubernetes operator for one more ( POST /v1/admin/kube , the coordinator must run under the operator), servers=URL,URL adds empty servers started for it, by their gRPC URLs, one at a time, and off never grows. Each new shard gets its share of the slots by a rebalance ( POST /v1/admin/rebalance ) while the crawl keeps writing."],
["crawler.html#on-kubernetes","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","On Kubernetes","A cluster the operator runs gets a crawler of its own from its StrataCluster , with nothing to start by hand: The operator runs chloedb-crawl serve from the cluster's own image (it carries both programs) as a Deployment of one pod, <cluster>-crawler , behind a Service of that name, and restarts the coordinators so that they list it: Apps → Crawler appears in the command center, and a crawl starts from its form. The crawler writes with the cluster's admin token ( tokenSecret ), which its API requires too, so the coordinators send it that token and nothing else reaches it. It grows the cluster through the operator, a shard at a time: spec.crawler default grow true add a shard (one more in spec.shards ) when a crawl's table holds more than splitRows rows on one splitRows 100,000 maxShards 8 the most shards it grows the cluster to paused false no crawler pod for now (the coordinators still list it, as an app that does not answer) args , resources more arguments of chloedb-crawl serve (as [\"--contact\", \"ops@example.com\"] , whom site owners reach), and its container's resources (50m CPU and 64 MiB asked, 1 GiB at most) The upgrader moves the crawler with the servers, since it runs their image. Removing spec.crawler deletes its Deployment and Service and restarts the coordinators without it; the tables it filled stay. Its crawls live in its pod, so a pod started again (a new image, a node replaced) starts with none."],
["crawler.html#what-it-does-with-a-page","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","What it does with a page","Fetching. One queue per host, read breadth first. robots.txt is read before a site's first page and again once a day (RFC 9309: the longest rule wins, * and $ in paths, its own groups, chloedb-crawl and strata-crawl (its old name), or else * ); a 4xx answer for it allows everything, and a 5xx, a 429 or no answer keeps the crawler off the site until it reads it again, a minute later (or when its Retry-After says, up to 10 minutes). Sitemaps named there, or at /sitemap.xml , add their pages as seeds. Only HTML is kept, at most max_bytes a page; a URL is normalised once (no fragment, no tracking parameters, the query's parameters sorted) and fetched once, redirects included; a redirect (5 at most) is followed only to a page the crawl would follow as a link (its scope, include and exclude ) and its site's robots.txt allows; robots.txt is read to 500 KB and a sitemap to 50 MB; a page whose text another page already had is counted as a duplicate and not written. Links are queued in turn from each shape of link on the page ( /catalogue/*/index.html , /catalogue/category/... ), so the first pages read are a site's items as well as its lists. rel=\"nofollow\" links and pages with noindex or nofollow robots meta tags are honoured. Reach. The crawler fetches the public internet only: an address on its machine ( localhost , 127.0.0.1 ), a private network ( 10.x , 192.168.x , fd00::/8 ), a link-local one ( 169.254.169.254 , cloud metadata) or another reserved range is refused, whether a page links to it, redirects to it or its name resolves to it (checked again as each connection is made). An intranet crawl says so when the crawler starts: --allow-host HOST[:PORT] (repeatable) for one site, --allow-private (or CHLOEDB_CRAWL_ALLOW_PRIVATE=1 ) for everything. A proxy named by HTTPS_PROXY is reachable whatever its address, and resolves the names it is asked for itself. Politeness. A site gets at most per_host requests at once (1), and between the starts of two the longest of delay_ms (1 s), its robots.txt's Crawl-delay (up to 10 minutes; every site the crawl reaches, not only the seeds') and twice the time it has been taking to answer (up to 30 s), so a site that slows down is asked less often. A site that pushes back waits before its next request: a 429, 502, 503 or 504 as long as its Retry-After says, else 5 s, doubled at each refusal in a row up to 5 minutes, and the page is asked again later (4 times at most); a 403 or no answer waits the same, and the page is not asked again. After 10 refusals in a row, or when a site asks to wait more than 10 minutes, the crawl gives up on that site and drops its queued pages; the crawl's events say when a site was made to wait and when it was given up. The user agent names the crawler and links to the page for site owners , with the crawl's contact ; a crawl's own user_agent keeps the crawler's name after it. Answers are asked compressed (gzip, Brotli). Fields. Each page gives: from as the fetch url (the key), host , depth , status , fetched , bytes , fetch_ms the page title , body (the main text: <main> , <article> or [role=main] , else the body without navigation, headers and footers), words , links , and when the page has them description , headings , lang , canonical , published , modified , author , image , site_name , kind , keywords JSON-LD ld.* : the page's main entity ( Product , Article , Recipe ...), @type as type Open Graph and other meta tags og.* ( og:price:amount is og.price_amount ) microdata md.* tables and lists of terms specs.* : rows of a key and a value ( <th>UPC</th><td>a897...</td> , <dt> / <dd> ) the site's template page.* : learned from the first pages (below) your selectors rule.* : name=selector , name=selector@attr , name[]=selector for every match The template. On the first pages ( sample , 30), the crawler looks for an element of one tag and classes ( p.price_color ) that most pages have at most three times, whose text changes from page to page and is not the title. Those are the site's own fields, named from their classes ( price_color , stock_level ), and read from every page after. The table. It is made from the first pages: the fetch's and the page's columns above, a column for each other field that at least promote_at (0.3) of the pages have, typed by its values (a type fits when 90% of the values read as it: BOOL , INT , FLOAT with currencies and thousands separators read, TIMESTAMP , KEYWORD for short values, else TEXT , and lists), and data JSON holding every field of the page, so a field that is not a column is still a path to query ( WHERE data.specs.isbn = '...' ). An existing table is used as it is, with the columns it lacks added. With embed , a vector field emb is embedded from the title and body by that model. As it grows. Every 100 pages the crawler looks again at the fields the pages since the start have had: one that has reached promote_at (over 50 pages at least) gets a column ( ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS ), and the rows written before it get its value from their data (a set by key for each). Rows are written in batches of batch (200) through POST /v1/write , each under an idempotency key, so a batch sent again after a lost answer writes once; a 429 waits as the server asks, a partial write is sent again with the key it returns, and rows a server refuses are found by halves and listed as problems."],
["crawler.html#the-command-centers-view","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","The command center's view","Apps → Crawler lists the crawls and the cluster's shards, each a bar of its rows against the split threshold, with the shards it added and the rebalances. New crawl takes a URL and the options above. Preview reads the URL and up to nine pages it links to and shows the table they would make: its columns with their types and where each comes from, the CREATE TABLE , the pages read, the template learned, every field found with the share of pages that had it, and three rows as they would be written. Crawl starts it. A crawl's page shows pages fetched and rows written a second, with the moments the table was made and shards were added; the shards filling and splitting; the table's columns, with when each was added, and the fields found so far with the column each went to; a search of the table through ChloeDB ( MATCH and bm25 over the body, or the latest rows); what it did, with the SQL it ran; the last pages read; and problems (robots.txt, timeouts, answers that were not HTML, refused rows). Pause, Resume and Stop act at once; a stopped crawl writes the pages it has read. The view is a module the crawler serves ( chloedb-crawl/ui/view.js , compiled in; CHLOEDB_CRAWL_UI_DIR=chloedb-crawl/ui reads it from disk), loaded and run by the command center as the apps section describes."],
["crawler.html#options","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","Options","The same names are the JSON fields of POST /v1/crawls and POST /v1/preview , and the command line's options ( --max-pages , --no-robots , --rule , ...). option default seeds the URLs to start from table from the first seed's host ( books_toscrape_com ) the table to fill scope host which links to follow: host (the seeds' hosts), domain (their sites, subdomains included), prefix (under the seeds' paths), any include , exclude regular expressions on URLs: follow only links that match one of include , never those that match exclude keep regular expressions: write rows only for pages that match (the others are read for their links) max_pages 1,000 URLs to fetch at most max_depth 5 links from a seed; a list's next page (a link with rel=\"next\" , or named \"Next\", \"Next page\", \"Older posts\" or an arrow) is as deep as the list, so a long list is read to its end. A crawl that ends says why: its page limit, its depth (with the links it left out, also in the status's beyond_depth ), or no links left concurrency , per_host 16, 1 requests at once, and to one host delay_ms 1,000 between the starts of two requests to a host (longer when its robots.txt asks, or when it is slow to answer) robots , sitemaps on obey robots.txt; read sitemaps sample 30 pages the table is made from promote_at 0.3 the share of pages a field needs to be a column rules fields from CSS selectors, as above learn on learn the site's template embed a model to embed the title and body with, into emb user_agent chloedb-crawl/0.3.7 (+https://chloetech-ai.github.io/chloedb/chloedb/crawler-bot.html) a crawl's own is sent with that after it, unless it names chloedb-crawl (or strata-crawl , its old name) contact --contact of chloedb-crawl serve (or CHLOEDB_CRAWL_CONTACT ) whom a site's owner reaches about the crawl, an email address or a URL, sent in the user agent: chloedb-crawl/<version> (+https://...; ops@example.com) timeout_ms , max_bytes , batch 20,000, 5 MB, 200"],
["crawler.html#api","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","API","chloedb-crawl serve answers on --listen ( 127.0.0.1:7800 ). It takes no request that carries an Origin header, so no web page can drive it from a browser, and with --token (or CHLOEDB_CRAWL_TOKEN ) none without Authorization: Bearer <token> ; with --local and no token it makes one and gives it to the coordinator it starts. request GET /v1/info the crawler's version, the ChloeDB it writes to and what it is, the growth policy and the default options POST /v1/crawls starts a crawl from the options: {id, table} ; two crawls may not fill one table at once GET /v1/crawls the crawls, newest first GET /v1/crawls/:id one crawl: its phase, counts, rates and a second-by-second series, the table's plan and SQL, the fields found, the last pages and problems, what it did, and the cluster's growth POST /v1/crawls/:id/pause , /resume , /stop DELETE /v1/crawls/:id forgets a crawl that has ended (its table stays) POST /v1/preview the options and pages (6, at most 20): the preview, nothing written GET /v1/grow the shards' rows of the crawls' tables, slots moving, and the shards added GET /app.json , GET /ui/view.js the manifest and view the command center reads Through chloedb-server the crawler checks its callers ( \"checks_callers\": true in its manifest): a key that may write starts crawls, which are its own, and pauses, resumes, stops and forgets only those (an admin of every table steers any); a key limited to tables lists only its own crawls. A crawl writes its table as the key that started it: the crawler sends its token narrowed to that key's grants ( x-chloedb-as-tables , server ), so a key limited to crawl_* crawls into crawl_* tables only, and a table it may not write is the server's 403. Called directly with its token, it answers as before."],
["crawler.html#limits","chloedb-crawl: a site into a table","Limits","Pages are read as their HTML arrives: a page built by JavaScript in the browser shows only what its HTML holds (JSON-LD and meta tags often carry its fields anyway). A crawl lives in the process: its queue and seen set are in memory, and a restarted crawler starts again (rows are written by URL, so a page written twice is one row). A site's scope ( domain ) takes the last two labels of a host, or three when the second last is short ( example.co.uk ), without the public suffix list. The growth policy counts the rows of the crawls' tables on each shard, not their bytes, and adds a shard at a time; it does not remove shards. A list column added during the crawl is filled from the rows written after it only (a set by key takes one value)."],
["sheets.html","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","","chloedb-sheets is a spreadsheet app that keeps its workbooks in ChloeDB. A workbook has the things you expect of one: sheets, formulas (IronCalc's engine, over 300 of Excel's functions, array formulas and spills), number formats, fonts, colors, borders, merged cells, frozen panes, conditional formats, named ranges, sorting, filters, find and replace, charts and pivot tables. It also has what a spreadsheet kept in a database can have: Every cell is a row of a ChloeDB table , so every workbook can be searched at once (BM25 over the cells' text), and a workbook opened in two windows shows what the other changed. The cluster's tables open as sheets , live: rows paged in as you scroll (past the server's result window, by the sort's key: the key's column or a number, time or boolean column, so a table of any size is read to its end), sorted by any column, filtered with a SQL WHERE and searched with MATCH , and edited in place (each change is a write to the table). Cells can be filled by SQL : a query of the cluster's tables writes its answer into a sheet from a cell on, is kept with the workbook, and runs again when you refresh it, so the cells (and the formulas and charts over them) follow the table. A sheet becomes a table : its first row names the columns, each column gets the type its cells hold, and its rows are written to ChloeDB by a key you choose. Files come in and go out : Excel workbooks (.xlsx, formulas and formats kept), CSV and TSV, and SQLite databases, whose tables become sheets of a workbook or tables of the cluster; a workbook goes out as .xlsx, a sheet as CSV. Like the crawler , it is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, and the command center shows it as an app."],
["sheets.html#run-it","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","Run it","Install both programs, then start chloedb-sheets with a cluster of its own. since 0.3.2 The installer puts both in ~/.local/bin prebuilt, and chloedb-sheets finds the server beside it: Or build both from a checkout (any platform with Rust; the only way before release 0.3.2): Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → Sheets . --local DIR starts a chloedb-server on 127.0.0.1:7700 ( --local-listen ) with its database in DIR , lists chloedb-sheets on it as the app sheets , and stops it when chloedb-sheets stops; the same DIR again serves the same workbooks. Against a cluster that runs elsewhere, give chloedb-sheets the server (a coordinator or one server) and a key, and give the server the app. Its first start creates the two tables it keeps workbooks in, which takes an admin key; after that a key that can write is enough: --server was --strata before 0.3.2; the old name still works. --token (or CHLOEDB_SHEETS_TOKEN ) is the bearer token its API requires; the server sends its CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN ( apps ). Requests that carry an Origin header are refused, so a page on another site cannot reach the app even from inside the network. It checks its callers itself ( \"checks_callers\": true in its manifest), so chloedb-server sends it keys limited to tables, and keys that may only write, with who they are ( x-chloedb-principal , x-chloedb-scope , x-chloedb-tables ). A key that may write makes workbooks, which are its own: it changes them, shares them ( Share , POST /v1/books/:id/share {shared} ) and deletes them, and another key opens one only once it is shared, or when it is an admin of every table. A workbook made before owners existed is everyone's. What a workbook reads from the cluster (its query blocks, the tables opened as sheets, a sheet copied to a table) runs as the caller's key: chloedb-sheets sends its own token narrowed to the caller's grants ( x-chloedb-as-tables , server ), so a query reads only the tables the signed-in key may read, and a refusal is the server's 403. The analyst preset's keys make their own workbooks this way. Called directly with its token (not through chloedb-server), it answers as before, as the token's."],
["sheets.html#on-kubernetes","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","On Kubernetes","A cluster the operator runs gets spreadsheets of its own from its StrataCluster , with nothing to start by hand: The operator runs chloedb-sheets serve from the cluster's own image (it carries it, as it carries the crawler) as a Deployment of one pod, <cluster>-sheets , behind a Service of that name, and restarts the coordinators so that they list it: Apps → Sheets appears in the command center. It reads and writes with the cluster's admin token ( tokenSecret ), which its API requires too, so the coordinators send it that token and nothing else reaches it. Its workbooks are rows of the cluster's tables, so a pod started again (a new image, a node replaced) has them all; the upgrader moves it with the servers, since it runs their image. spec.sheets default paused false no pod for now (the coordinators still list it, as an app that does not answer; the workbooks stay) args , resources more arguments of chloedb-sheets serve , and its container's resources (50m CPU and 96 MiB asked, 1 GiB at most) Removing spec.sheets deletes its Deployment and Service and restarts the coordinators without it; the workbooks stay in sheets_books and sheets_cells ."],
["sheets.html#from-the-command-line","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","From the command line","import and export move files without the UI:"],
["sheets.html#the-app","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","The app","Sheets lists the workbooks, newest first ( Mine , Shared or All , with each one's owner), the cluster's tables, a search over every workbook, and an import area that takes a file dropped on it. A SQLite file asks which of its tables to bring and where: as sheets of a new workbook, or as tables of the cluster (through chloedb-server's own SQLite import, with the signed-in key, keeping the columns' types, keys and indexes). A file is not trusted: a SQLite file's views come in only when named (a view runs its query as it is read, and that query may not end), and reading one stops after 60 s for the list of tables and 180 s for the import; an xlsx file may expand to at most 512 MB and hold at most 2 million cells. A workbook is a grid drawn on a canvas, so a sheet of a million rows scrolls as one of ten; its cells are read a tile at a time as they come into view. Above it: the toolbar : undo and redo, the format painter, number formats (and decimals, currency, percent), font size, bold, italic, underline and strikethrough, text and fill colors, borders, merging, alignment and wrapping, frozen rows and columns, sorting and filters, Σ (sum, average, count, max, min, median of the cells above or of each column), charts, pivot tables, conditional formats, named ranges, SQL queries, and clearing formats; the formula bar , with the name box (a cell, a range or a name to go to) and, as a formula is typed, the functions that match and the arguments of the one the caret is in; clicking or dragging over cells while typing a formula puts their reference in, and the ranges a formula refers to are outlined in color. The grid does what a spreadsheet's does: arrows, Tab, Enter (back to the column a run of Tabs started from) and Ctrl+arrows to move, Shift to extend, typing to replace a cell and F2 or a double click to edit it, Ctrl+Enter into every selected cell, the fill handle (drag it, or double click it to fill down the data), column and row resizing (double click a column's edge to fit its contents), cut, copy and paste (within the app formulas move and formats come along; from other programs, tab-separated text), right-click menus to insert, delete, hide and show rows and columns, and the usual shortcuts ( More → Keyboard shortcuts lists them). The sheet tabs below rename, recolor, duplicate, hide, move (drag them) and delete sheets, and the status bar shows the sum, average, count, minimum and maximum of the selection. A filter puts buttons on the header row of a range: each lists the column's values with their counts, to choose the ones shown, and sorts. It is kept with the workbook. A chart (column, bar, line, area, pie or scatter, stacked or not) is drawn over the sheet from a range whose first row names the series and first column holds the categories; drag it by its title, resize it from its corner, double click it to edit it. Summarize makes a pivot table of a range on a new sheet: its rows grouped by the values of the columns chosen, with sums, counts, averages, minimums, maximums or distinct counts of others, and a total row. Conditional formatting colors cells by their value (greater than, between, text that contains, duplicates, the top 10, above average, a formula), and draws color scales, data bars and icons. Queries lists the workbook's SQL blocks, to go to, edit, run again or drop. A table of the cluster opens at #/app/sheets/t/<table> as a sheet whose columns are the table's, named and typed in the header. Rows are read a page of 100 at a time as they come into view, ordered by the key (or by the column whose header you click, then by the key), and read again every five seconds. Typing into a cell sets that field of that row ( {\"op\": \"set\"} by key); pasting sets many; Delete clears them. Row adds one, Column runs ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN , Delete rows deletes the rows selected (after asking), and Open as a workbook copies the rows shown (the filter and order included) into a new workbook, for formulas, charts and pivots over them. A row's key does not change in place. Numbers are shown as Excel's General format shows them, to 15 significant digits and no more ( 226159459.83 , 4.1271186440678 , 1.5E+20 ), in the grid, a CSV and an xlsx file alike; a number format of the cell's own still applies. Charts do not go into an .xlsx file : the file keeps the cells, formulas and formats, and the download says how many charts it left out (the x-app-left-out header of GET /v1/books/:id/xlsx )."],
["sheets.html#how-workbooks-are-kept","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","How workbooks are kept","Two tables hold every workbook. chloedb-sheets creates them at its first start: table one row what it holds sheets_books a workbook its name, where it came from, its sheets' names, its counts, and its frame: the workbook without its cells (styles, column widths, row heights, merges, names, conditional formats, frozen panes), IronCalc's model encoded with bitcode, as base64; and its charts, queries and filters as JSON sheets_cells a cell book:sheet:row:col as its key, the sheet's name, its reference ( B7 ), what it shows ( text , searched with BM25), its number, its input (a formula, or what was typed), its style's index and kind sheets_cells is sharded by the workbook ( shard_by ), so a workbook's cells sit on one shard and are read in one pass, and clustered by it, so they are read in order. After each change chloedb-sheets compares every cell it touched with what it wrote last, and writes only the cells that changed, in one transaction with the workbook's row; the 350,000 cells of a 50,000-row SQLite import took 8.7 s to write, a change to one cell of it 0.1 to 0.15 s, on the container this was built in. A workbook is held in memory while it is used and let go after 30 minutes unused, or sooner, the least recently used first, when the open workbooks hold more than 1.5 million cells together ( CHLOEDB_SHEETS_OPEN_CELLS ); a write ChloeDB refuses is retried every 30 seconds, and the workbook says it is not saved yet until one succeeds. Changing an open workbook from two places is safe: chloedb-sheets applies changes to a workbook one at a time, and each viewer reads what changed since the version it holds ( GET /v1/books/<id>/changes?since= ) every three seconds, or redraws the sheet when rows or columns moved."],
["sheets.html#its-api","chloedb-sheets: spreadsheets in ChloeDB","Its API","Each route is under the app's address (through chloedb-server: /v1/apps/sheets/... ), and takes the token. route what GET /v1/books?whose=mine\\|shared\\|all , POST /v1/books {name} the workbooks the caller may open (with owner and shared , and the caller as me ); a new one, the caller's POST /v1/import?name=&format=&tables=&header= a file (the body) as a new workbook: xlsx, csv, tsv or sqlite (by default what the bytes are); a SQLite file's tables , a CSV file's header POST /v1/sqlite/tables a SQLite file's tables, their columns and row counts, and its views ( \"kind\": \"view\" , not counted) POST /v1/from-table {table \\| sql, name, limit} a table of the cluster, or a SELECT's answer, as a new workbook GET /v1/search?q=&limit= the cells and workbooks that hold the words GET , DELETE /v1/books/<id> a workbook's summary (sheets, names, charts, queries, filters, whether it can undo); deleting it POST /v1/books/<id>/ops {ops} changes, in order, each one step to undo: set , set_many , array_formula , clear , style , border , insert_rows , delete_rows , insert_cols , delete_cols , move_rows , move_cols , col_width , row_height , hide_rows , hide_cols , freeze , grid_lines , merge , unmerge , add_sheet , delete_sheet , rename_sheet , move_sheet , duplicate_sheet , sheet_color , hide_sheet , autofill , paste , paste_text , sort , filter , replace , define_name , update_name , delete_name , add_format , delete_format , undo , redo , rename_book , chart , delete_chart , summarize ; the answer has the cells that changed GET /v1/books/<id>/cells?sheet=&r0=&r1=&c0=&c1= , /layout?sheet= , /values?sheet=&range= an area's cells (what each shows, its type, style and input); a sheet's layout; a range's values as numbers GET /v1/books/<id>/changes?since= what changed since a version POST /v1/books/<id>/copy {sheet, area} an area as a clipboard for paste , and as tab-separated text GET /v1/books/<id>/find?q=&sheet=&case=&whole=&formulas= the cells that hold a text GET /v1/books/<id>/export?format=xlsx\\|csv&sheet= the workbook as a file POST /v1/books/<id>/query {sheet, row, col, sql, limit, id} , POST /v1/books/<id>/refresh , DELETE /v1/books/<id>/queries/<qid> a SQL block (new, or id 's again); every block again; one dropped (its cells stay) POST /v1/books/<id>/to-table {sheet, table, key} a sheet as a table of the cluster An import holds at most 2,000,000 cells, and a query block or a table copied into a workbook at most 100,000 rows."],
["netops.html","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","","A demo. chloedb-netops shows what ChloeDB can do; it is not part of ChloeDB's releases or their promises. It is tested on a train of its own (the Demos workflow, .github/workflows/demos.yml ), outside CI's required check, so a fault in it does not hold a ChloeDB release, and the command center marks it as a demo ( compatibility ). chloedb-netops is a network operations center kept in ChloeDB: the inventory, the topology, a point a minute of telemetry, device logs, alerts, incidents, changes, configurations, runbooks, and agents that work an incident and say what they looked at. It comes with a simulated carrier network, Meridian (10 sites, 64 devices, 120 links, 12 services), and a simulator that writes each minute of it and breaks it in six ways, so the whole app can be used, and its agents checked, without a network of your own. It is there to show what one database does for an operations team when search, SQL, graphs and time all live in it: An incident is a query, not a pile of alerts. Alert rules are SQL over the telemetry tables. The alerts they raise are grouped into one incident by walking the topology ( REACHABLE over the links table), so a fibre cut is one incident with eight alerts, not eight pages. The root-cause agent shows its work. It ranks the alerting devices over the graph (personalised PageRank), reads the logs, changes, optics and flows around them, tests six causes, and names one with a confidence and the evidence for it. Every step keeps the SQL it ran, the rows it read and the time it took. What it packed into its context, and what it left out, is kept as a receipt ( PACK … TOKENS WITH RECEIPT ). Nothing runs without an approval. The agent proposes actions (reroute a span, roll a change back, restart a process, open a ticket with the circuit's provider) with their risk and the exact commands. A person approves or rejects each one; autopilot, off by default, lets the low-risk ones through. The topology is found from the logs. LLDP neighbour and routing-adjacency lines in syslog imply links; the app builds them and compares them with the inventory: links the logs confirm, links nobody documented, and documented links nothing has spoken on. Logs are searched three ways in one place : by words (BM25), by any substring ( LIKE answered from an FM-index, for an interface name or half an address), and runbooks and past incidents by meaning (a static embedding model on the server, no API call). Most of a shift is not an incident. The home page is the shift: a handover written from the tables, and one list of what waits (approvals, unowned alerts, changes due, carrier notices that collide). A box at the top takes whatever a ticket says (an address, a circuit id, a customer, an interface) and answers what it is and how it is doing. Every incident has a room that its people shape. The room opens with a board of panels chosen for the suspected cause. Anyone on the call adds a panel by asking for it in a sentence, removes what is noise, and everyone sees the same board. A panel is one read-only query kept as a row. Engineers and leadership read the same tables. A switch at the top turns the shift's pages into objectives, incident cost, risk, carriers and a written weekly report, and each number opens the rows behind it. The past is a table. Every version of each device's configuration is a row, so the change behind an incident is a diff, and \"who put this line here\" is a query. Which runbook closed which incident is kept as a grade the ranking can learn from. Like the crawler and the spreadsheets , it is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, and the command center shows it as an app."],
["netops.html#run-it","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","Run it","Build both programs, then start chloedb-netops with a server of its own (releases after 0.3.2 carry it prebuilt too: the installer's --with netops puts it beside the server, which it then finds without --server-bin ): Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → Network operations . The first start makes the tables and writes six hours of history ( --history-hours ), which takes some seconds; the page shows its progress. From then on the simulator writes a minute of telemetry every minute, evaluates the alert rules, and injects a fault every 45 minutes ( --fault-every ; 0 : only when you press Inject a fault ). --local DIR starts a chloedb-server on 127.0.0.1:7700 ( --local-listen ) with its database in DIR , lists chloedb-netops on it as the app netops , and stops it when chloedb-netops stops; the same DIR again is the same network, with its incidents. Against a server or a cluster that runs elsewhere, give chloedb-netops the server (a coordinator or one server) and a key, and give the server the app: The key needs admin scope on the tables netops_* (a key limited to tables , netops_*=admin , is enough: the app touches no other table). --token (or CHLOEDB_NETOPS_TOKEN ) is the bearer token its API requires; the server sends its CHLOEDB_APP_TOKEN ( apps ). Requests that carry an Origin header are refused, so a page on another site cannot reach the app even from inside the network. On a coordinator over shards the graph steps ( REACHABLE , ppr , PATH ), which a coordinator does not answer yet, are walked by the app over the same links table; every page says which of the two it used. The app knows a coordinator from its /v1/health , or from the first graph statement it refuses, and then sends it none. --no-simulate turns the simulator off: the app reads its network from the inventory tables and writes no telemetry of its own ( your own network ). --topology (or CHLOEDB_NETOPS_TOPOLOGY ) says where the network comes from. The default, both , serves two from one app: the network as its inventory lists it, and beside it the same simulated network read back from what it reports (its BGP-LS, LLDP, SNMP, BMP and ONT tables: a network that reports itself ). The switch at the top of the page, Inventory | Discovered , moves between them, and the page remembers the choice. The second is built the first time it is opened, which takes a minute or two (the page says how far), in tables of its own ( netops_disc_* , under /d/ in the app's API); from then on the app simulates both, each with its own faults. inventory serves only the first and discovered only the second, each in the netops_* tables. With --no-simulate , both is inventory ."],
["netops.html#on-kubernetes","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","On Kubernetes","A cluster the operator runs gets the app from its StrataCluster , with nothing to start by hand: The operator runs chloedb-netops serve from the cluster's own image as a Deployment of one pod, <cluster>-netops , behind a Service of that name (port 7840), and restarts the coordinators so that they list it: Apps → Network operations appears in the command center. It holds a key limited to netops_* and a token of its own (the Secret <cluster>-netops-credentials ), or the cluster's admin token until the coordinators make such keys. Its network is rows of the cluster's tables, so a pod started again goes on from them. spec.netops default paused false no pod for now (the coordinators still list it, as an app that does not answer; the tables stay and nothing is written to them) tables netops_*=admin the tables its key may touch args , resources more arguments of chloedb-netops serve ( --fault-every 0 , --history-hours 2 , --no-simulate ), and its container's resources (50m CPU and 96 MiB asked, 1 GiB at most) Removing spec.netops deletes its Deployment and Service and restarts the coordinators without it; the tables netops_* stay."],
["netops.html#the-pages","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The pages","page what it is for My shift the home page: a handover of the last twelve hours written from the tables, what waits for you most urgent first (approvals, alerts nobody owns, changes due, carrier notices that collide, open tickets), the changes and carrier windows of the next day and a half, and notes left by the shift before Diagnose where it is broken : the stack by place, the lowest broken layer named as where to start, one broken thing investigated five ways, and a trace between two points hop by hop Network the open incident, the network's map with each link's load, traffic and loss, services against their objectives, time to detect and to resolve by cause Incidents every incident; an incident's room has the likely cause with its confidence, what happened, why it is probably the cause (each claim with the rows behind it) and what it is not, the proposed actions, its board , a map of where it is that replays it minute by minute, the agent's steps with their SQL, the receipt of what it was shown, the timeline, the blast radius and similar incidents Alerts what fires and who owns it (acknowledge, hand back, silence), the silences in force, and the rules: each is a SQL query you can read, test against the last hours and save Changes the change calendar by day, the collisions (two changes whose devices overlap in a window, && over INT[] ), the change-risk agent's score, and carrying a change out: start it (its devices are silenced for the window), verify it against the network as it was before, complete it or roll it back Customers every customer with where they attach and whether anything is wrong on their path; a customer's page answers \"is it the network?\" with what was checked along the path from their port to the backbone, and a reply ready to send Carriers carriers' maintenance notices, each matched to the circuit it names and checked for what it collides with: the other plane's maintenance in the same window, a change on the router that carries the other plane, and whether the surviving plane has the capacity; a notice can be pasted as it arrived Discovery on the discovered network only (the switch's Discovered , or --topology discovered ): the seven feeds the network was read from with their rows, the rules that turn them into an inventory, and every site, device, link, BGP session and estate hub with the feeds that found it and how ( below ) Topology the network as the inventory has it, as the logs say it is, and the difference; the path between two devices; the lines of syslog a link was read from Services each service's success rate and latency against its objective, the devices it rides on and the customers on it Explore logs by words or by substring, flows (who talks to whom), and one search box over runbooks, incidents, configurations and tickets Devices the inventory with each device's metrics, what happened on it in the last day, logs, configuration history and blame Agents the six agents, their runs and what each cost in queries, rows and tokens; capacity, advisories and configuration drift; autopilot Map the whole network on one plane, from regions down to one customer's router: zoom in and detail appears, what is broken colours the place that holds it at every zoom, a click on an object says what is wrong and what it has to do with everything around it, and a search box takes a sentence ( below ) Dashboards the backbone's busiest links (utilisation, loss, received optical power), the busiest devices and the slowest services over three hours, what is not ok by metro and by kind, faults a day by cause over two weeks, and incidents by cause; the estate's panels follow the place in view on the map Data the 48 tables (each network has its own) with their definitions and row counts, where each one's rows come from and whether they are arriving, and the simulator's settings Leadership the switch at the top: services against what was sold, incidents with their cost in customer-minutes and revenue exposed, time to detect and resolve, what caused them and which followed a change, the risk register (spans that cannot fail over at their measured peak, software with open advisories, contracts ending), carriers by cost and downtime, and the week written as a report The box above every page searches, and completes as it is typed ( below ). An id, a site or a circuit opens on the map; a sentence becomes the map's query; anything else is looked up: an address (matched to the customer whose prefix holds it), a circuit id, a customer, a device, an interface, an AS number, with what the thing is and whether it is healthy. Each page has a SQL button that lists the queries behind what it shows, with their times."],
["netops.html#the-map","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The map","The backbone of 64 devices is what a carrier's core is. What it carries is far more: metro hubs with their routers and power plants, street cabinets, cell sites and business parks, and the ONTs, radios and customer routers behind them. The simulator makes that estate too ( --estate N , about 200,000 objects by default, 0 for none): 18 kinds of object in netops_estate_nodes , what feeds what in netops_estate_edges (an edge table, upstream to downstream), and two months of faults and changes on every object in netops_estate_events . A few dozen faults are kept going, some of them caused by a change a few minutes before. Two alert rules watch it ( estate-outage and estate-degraded , SQL over the two tables like the others): an access node, a hub router or a hub's power plant that is down or degraded with objects behind it raises an alert, grouped by its hub; a down one with 20 or more behind it is major and opens an incident, which the estate's agent works (what it reported, how far it reaches and which customers, a change before it, who is on call) and proposes sending field services. The degraded ones wait on My shift , and Diagnose lists them under the backbone's layers. Pasting an object's id into the search opens it. The Map page draws all of it without ever asking for all of it. Places are boxes, one inside the other: a region holds its metros, a metro its core devices and its hubs, a hub its routers, its power plant and its sites, and a site its access node and what is behind it. A box shows a name and a count until there is room for what is inside it, and then it is divided by layer: the backbone in a band along a metro's top, aggregation and power along a hub's, the premises under a site's access node. Shapes say what a thing is: a cell site is a circle with its sectors around the tower, a business park a box with its corners cut, a router a diamond, a power plant or a tower a triangle. Zoom is a query. Every object has a place on a plane and a level of detail from which it is worth drawing. Regions, metros and hubs are read once; from there on the page asks for the objects and links in its window at its level of detail, two range queries, and draws what comes back. Red is a GROUP BY . The objects that are down or degraded, and a count by place of what is behind them, are two queries every ten seconds; the page adds them up through site, hub, metro and region, with the backbone's open incidents. A place is coloured by how much the broken things inside it carry: a hub's router weighs more than a cabinet, a cabinet more than one home. A click is a walk. For one object: what it hangs from ( REACHABLE against the edges) and the nearest of those that is broken, which is where its trouble comes from; what depends on it, counted by kind and state ( REACHABLE along the edges, grouped); what else is in trouble within three hops; the changes and faults of thirty days on it and on everything around it, with the change made just before the trouble began; and how faults that read like this one ended before (BM25 over the events). A click on an object also lights what it hangs from and what depends on it, and fades the rest; the panel beside the map has tabs for the object: Overview (the walk above), Metrics (a minute's series for the backbone's devices and their links; for the estate, its latest reading and its faults and changes a day over sixty days), Events , Logs (syslog for the backbone; the estate's events as lines), Related (the chain it hangs from, what depends on it by kind, the customers behind it, the trouble around it), Incidents and Config (its properties, and for the backbone the latest configuration). They are read by /v1/map/detail ."],
["netops.html#the-box-above-every-page-and-the-assistant","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The box above every page, and the assistant","The box has two modes, Search and Ask (the buttons before the box switch them; Tab completes the suggestion chosen with the arrows, or moves on to the page), and a chip that says where it looks: the object picked, the place the map shows, or everywhere (a click on the chip searches everywhere). As it is typed, /v1/suggest completes it from the tables, from what was typed before the word, and from where the person is: typed completed with how the start of an id (\"swi\", \"hr1-h3\") objects, the broken ones first, in the place in view PREFIX(id, :w) over the column's dictionary; with fuzzy => 'auto' when nothing matches exactly a word of a name (\"park 7\", \"insur\", \"Lon\") sites, hubs and metros, customers ILIKE from the substring index, at the start of a word the start of a circuit (\"LUM-A\") circuits, with their carrier and plane PREFIX(circuit, :w) kind: , state: , vendor: , in: the values in the place in view, with counts a GROUP BY the start of a sentence (\"OLTs in London \") what the map's reader has not been told yet: a state (with how many it would find), what happened, by whom, a reading, a place in the graph the reader's plan of the words so far, and one GROUP BY state a word that asks for the next (\"that is not\", \"changed in\", \"about\", \"by\") the words that can follow it words of an earlier question questions asked before BM25 over netops_searches In Ask mode it offers questions about the object picked, and after a question the ones that usually follow it (after \"what is wrong\", what changed before it started, who is affected, what to do). The assistant, on the right of every page, answers them from the app's own API: what is wrong and where the trouble starts, the change made before it, what depends on it and the customers behind it, what to do (the change to check, how such faults ended before, runbooks by BM25), how it went before, what depends on it lit on the map, and a watch that reads the object every 30 seconds and says when it changes. Each answer lists the queries it ran. Anything else becomes the map's sentence search. The answers are written from the query results by fixed rules, not by a model; the command center's own Ask (Ctrl+J) is the server's agent over any table. Each completion is one to four queries of 1 to 3 ms on one server with 170,000 objects (a name matched at a word's start takes up to 70 ms the first time). ChloeDB has no next-word model: the sentence's next words come from the reader's vocabulary, and earlier questions from BM25."],
["netops.html#asking-in-a-sentence","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","Asking in a sentence","The search box takes a question such as \"everything changed in the last week about a firmware upgrade by m.rossi with latency above 15 ms\", or \"customer routers in Frankfurt behind hr1-h03-fra that are not ok\". It names up to five kinds of condition, and ChloeDB answers them in one statement: in the sentence becomes a kind, a vendor, a version, a place, a state (\"degraded Nokia OLTs in Frankfurt\") comparisons on keyword columns a customer's name ILIKE from the substring index a reading (\"latency above 40 ms\", \"high latency\", \"loss over 1%\") a comparison on the object's latest reading, or of two of its columns ( latency_ms > base_ms * 2 ) what happened, when, about what, by whom (\"changed last week about QoS by a.khan\", \"went down in the last 3 days because of power\", \"not changed in the last month\") a subquery over the events table: time, author, cause, and MATCH on the words; NOT IN for what did not happen where it sits (\"behind ag1-fra\", \"upstream of ...\", \"within 2 hops of ...\") REACHABLE over the edge table The same conditions run a second time grouped by place, so the map shows the matches at every zoom: a count on each region, metro, hub and site that holds some, and a ring on each object once it is drawn. A sentence becomes a plan, and only a plan becomes SQL: every value in it is one the estate has or a parameter. Two readers make plans. With --rewrite-model (default anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5 , empty for none) the app asks the server's own llm() to rewrite the question, over the row it was saved as in netops_searches ; the model is told the plan's shape and what it may name, and its answer is checked field by field. When the server has no model (no key, no network), a reader of fixed vocabulary answers instead, and the page says which of them read the sentence. The fixed reader lists the words it took nothing from beside the count, which is then the answer to the rest of the question; an id, a place or a name the estate does not have refuses the question rather than being dropped from it (\"ag9-zzz is not an object of the estate\", \"Did you mean London?\"), and a place one letter off is read and said. A customer is matched by its whole name when the question gives it (\"Northwind Energy\"), and \"this week\", \"this month\" and \"this year\" start on their first day. The model's path is covered by a unit test of the checking, not by a run against a model: this build was only run where the server had none. Measured on one server in a 4-vCPU cloud container, with 170,337 objects, 173,133 links and about 470,000 events: the estate is written in about 25 seconds; a window is read in 10 to 75 ms depending on how much is in it; the eight queries behind one object take about 17 ms together; the statement above takes 5 to 10 ms, a walk of the 20,000 objects behind one aggregation router with a condition 22 to 30 ms, and \"not changed in the last month\" about 290 ms. On a cluster of shards the range queries, the grouping and the subquery run through the coordinator as they are. A coordinator does not walk graphs yet, so the walks are done by the app over the estate it made, and the page says so. The rows a walk reached are then read by key, at most 400 keys a statement, when there are up to 1,600 of them; a larger walk is read as plain queries over the hubs, metros or regions that hold it, 10,000 rows a page, and the app keeps the rows its walk reached. A search counts its matches by place in the app and reads its first 300 in full by key, so no statement names thousands of keys."],
["netops.html#where-is-it-broken","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","Where is it broken","A list of alerts says that things are wrong, not where the trouble begins. The Diagnose page answers that, and then asks about the broken thing in the ways that only one database holding the topology, the logs, the history and the readings can. The stack, by place. A grid with a row per layer (power and reachability, optics, links, capacity, routing, device health, services, customers) and a column per site, plus one for the backbone between sites. Each cell is read from the last three minutes of the tables. A fault shows on its own layer and on every layer above it, so the lowest layer with something broken is named as where to start, and what shows above it is marked as likely following from it. Investigate takes one link, device or service and asks five things, each card saying which of ChloeDB's abilities answered it, with how many queries and in what time: Graph queries in SQL : ppr() over the links table ranks the devices closest to everything that alerts now, and REACHABLE() with array overlap lists the services and customers it takes down. Substring search : every log line that names its interface, on any device, by LIKE '%…%' answered from the FM-index, so the neighbours' view of it is there too. History as rows : the changes whose devices overlap it ( && over INT[] ) and the configuration versions written on it; when there are none, a change is ruled out. Hybrid search : past incidents and runbooks like it, by BM25 and an embedding fused with rrf() in the ORDER BY . Time series : its last hour, a point a minute. Trace a path walks from a customer, a device or a site to another ( PATH() over the links table) and shows each hop and the link it was reached over with what they read now, marking the hop where it goes wrong. On the simulated network an investigation is 13 queries; in a 4-vCPU cloud container (one chloedb-server on local disk, the 64-device network with six hours of telemetry) they took about 12 ms together. The page's SQL button lists each."],
["netops.html#an-incidents-board","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","An incident's board","Most incidents fall into a few kinds, and the first minutes of each kind look alike; what differs is the question someone needs answered at minute twenty. So an incident's room is not built from nothing, and it is not fixed either. It starts from its cause's template. When the room opens it has the panels every incident needs (loss on the links involved, error log lines by kind, the customers at its sites). When the root-cause agent names a cause, that cause's panels join: receive power and input errors for a failing optic, link state, the surviving plane's load and latency for a fibre cut, BGP sessions and configuration versions for a bad change, traffic in and top talkers for a DDoS, CPU and BGP updates for a software defect, reachability and temperature for a power fault. People add to it by asking. loss on LON-NYC last 2 hours , cpu on cr1-fra , logs \"LINK-3-UPDOWN\" , who sends the most , customers on ag1-ams . A small reader that knows the readings, the tables and the network's names (devices, links, circuits, sites) turns the sentence into a query and says what it understood; it refuses what it cannot read rather than guess. A SELECT can be pasted instead. No model writes code here. It is shared and kept. A panel is a row of netops_panels holding one read-only query, so everyone in the room sees the same board, a removed panel stays removed, who added what is on the timeline, and the board is still there for the postmortem. It learns in the plainest way. What people added by hand to other incidents of the same kind is offered, one click each, on the next one."],
["netops.html#the-agents","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The agents","agent what it does Triage sizes a new incident: the alerts, the devices around them, the services and customers that ride on those, and who is on call Root cause ranks the alerting devices over the topology, reads the logs, changes, optics and flows around them, tests six causes, finds similar incidents and the runbook, and proposes actions Change risk scores a planned change: its blast radius, other changes on the same or neighbouring devices in its window, open incidents nearby Capacity finds the links whose 95th percentile runs hot, and the pairs that cannot carry each other's traffic if one fails Advisories and drift joins vendor advisories with the inventory's software versions, and searches every configuration for what should not be there Topology from logs reads LLDP and routing-adjacency lines out of syslog, builds the links they imply, and compares them with the inventory The agents are code that queries ChloeDB, not calls to a language model: each conclusion comes from rows a query returned, and a hypothesis is kept only when the incident's own alerts and fresh evidence near them support it. That is what makes a run cheap enough to start on every incident, and what lets the page show every step. When the root-cause agent names a cause it says why, in three parts that the incident's room shows under Likely cause : What happened : an account in order, written from the evidence: when each end lost the light, where the traffic went, what did not change first, and what that means. Why this is probably the cause : each claim (an alert, log lines, a reading, a change, a flow, an advisory, past incidents with the same cause) with what it points at, as links to the device, link, change or incident, and the query that shows the rows behind it. The page runs the query again on a click, so the rows come from the tables, not from the agent's say-so. A claim that something did not happen (no change on either router, no loss of signal) is made only after its own query came back empty. What it is not : each of the other explanations the agent tests, and the query that rules it out. They are rows of netops_evidence , one a claim, kept with the run. The simulator keeps an answer key for each fault it injects ( netops_faults ), which the agents never read. An incident's room can check the agent's answer against it, and the Agents page counts how many were right."],
["netops.html#the-six-faults","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The six faults","fault what happens Fibre cut on a backbone circuit a backbone circuit loses light; its traffic lands on the other plane, which runs hot A change breaks BGP on a border router a routing-policy change is committed on a border router and its sessions drop An optic slowly fails receive power on a link decays, errors climb, and the link starts to flap Volumetric DDoS on a customer prefix an amplification attack floods a site's borders and its firewalls Routing daemon pegs a core router's CPU a software defect named in a vendor advisory makes the routing process spin A site loses one power feed feed A at a small site fails; the devices on it go dark together A fault mitigates when its action is approved (or after some minutes on its own) and its incident resolves when the alerts clear."],
["netops.html#your-own-network","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","Your own network","The app only reads tables, with the SQL its pages show. The simulator is one writer of them; anything that writes to ChloeDB can be another. chloedb-netops serve --no-simulate is the app on a real network: The network is the inventory tables. At start the app reads netops_sites , netops_devices , netops_links , netops_services , netops_customers and netops_bgp and works from those: the topology graph, an incident's scope, a customer's path, a notice's circuit. With no devices it creates the tables, says which to fill, and stops. The inventory is read at start, so restart the app after it changes. It writes no telemetry. Each minute, once that minute has had a minute to arrive, the alert rules run over whatever is in netops_link_metrics , netops_device_metrics , netops_service_metrics , netops_flows and netops_syslog ; alerts are grouped into incidents over the topology and the agents work them, exactly as on the simulated network. What the app writes is what it derives: alerts, incidents, timelines, agent runs, proposed actions, panels, silences. An approved action is recorded, not pushed. The app does not log in to devices. Approval, who gave it and the commands proposed are kept; carrying them out stays with the people and tools that do that today. The Data page says what is arriving. Each source shows live when rows landed in the last five minutes (or, for records, when the table holds any), silent or empty otherwise, with its rate and lag. With the simulator on, sources read simulated . What usually feeds each table: table from netops_devices , netops_links , netops_sites , netops_circuits the inventory (NetBox, or its database followed with follow-postgres ) netops_link_metrics , netops_device_metrics , netops_service_metrics a rollup a minute from the metrics store (Prometheus), one row per link, device or service netops_syslog chloedb-server's OTLP logs endpoint , from an OpenTelemetry Collector with a syslog receiver netops_flows flow summaries a minute (top talkers), not raw flows netops_changes , netops_tickets the ITSM tool netops_configs a configuration backup tool, a row per version netops_maintenance carriers' mail: POST /v1/maintenance/parse {\"text\": \"...\"} reads a notice as it arrived into its circuit and window; a row written with only raw and status new is read the same way within a minute netops_alerts the rules here, and POST /v1/alerts/webhook in Alertmanager's format ( {\"alerts\": [{\"status\": \"firing\", \"labels\": {...}, \"annotations\": {...}}]} ), which join incidents the same way A metrics row carries the link's or device's id as the inventory has it and a ts ; the Data page shows each table's columns. The simulator's data is marked as synthetic on every page, and the mark is gone with the simulator off."],
["netops.html#a-network-that-reports-itself","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","A network that reports itself","Nobody has to list a network for the app to know it. A carrier's routers and switches already say what they are and what they are cabled to; the discovered network (the switch's Discovered , or --topology discovered ) is built from that alone. With the simulator on, its network first reports itself into seven tables, as collectors on a real network would fill them, and the app then reads its sites, devices, links, BGP sessions and the estate behind the backbone back from those rows and writes them into netops_sites , netops_devices , netops_links and netops_bgp ( netops_disc_sites and so on when the app serves both), where every page reads them as before: table what it holds on a real network netops_snmp_system each agent's system group (sysName, sysDescr, sysObjectID, sysLocation) and ENTITY-MIB's model, serial and software an SNMP poller (Telegraf's snmp input) netops_snmp_ifaces interfaces (IF-MIB's names, speeds and ifAlias) and their addresses (IP-MIB) the same poller netops_lldp LLDP neighbours, a row a port (lldpRemTable) the same poller, or gNMI's openconfig-lldp netops_bgpls_nodes , netops_bgpls_links the IS-IS database: routers, and adjacencies with their bandwidth, delay ( RFC 8571 ) and administrative group a BGP-LS session ( RFC 9552 ) from a route reflector to a collector (GoBGP, pmacct) netops_bmp_peers each router's BGP sessions and the routes it takes from each BMP ( RFC 7854 ) to a collector (OpenBMP, pmacct) netops_gpon_onts each OLT's ONTs on its PON ports the OLT's element manager, or its SNMP ONT table The rules, in the order they run: Devices. An SNMP agent whose sysLocation names a point of presence ( CODE | City, CC | Region | lat,lon ) is a device there. sysObjectID's enterprise number gives the vendor, sysDescr the operating system, ENTITY-MIB the model, serial and software. Devices are numbered in the order of their management addresses. Links. An LLDP neighbour, or an IS-IS adjacency in BGP-LS, is a link; the two are one link when the adjacency's addresses are on the ports LLDP names. Capacity and delay come from BGP-LS's TE attributes (else the port's speed), the plane from the administrative group, the carrier and circuit from the ports' descriptions. Roles. In the IS-IS database with eBGP sessions in BMP: a border router. Adjacent to another site: a core router. Any other IS-IS router: an aggregation router. Outside the IGP, a switch next to a firewall (its sysDescr says so) is a spine, and the rest are leaves. A fabric makes a site a data centre, two border routers a hub, and anything else a point of presence. The estate. Hub routers are what LLDP shows hanging from the backbone, access nodes what hangs from hub routers. An OLT's ONTs come from its ONT table; a business park's customer routers and a cell site's radios from LLDP (the customer by the port's description). sysLocation names the hub and the site each belongs to. What the network does not report is written beside the feeds: the services sold and the customers who bought them. The simulator's own numbers for how loaded each link is and how fast each service answers are matched to what was found by name; they add nothing to it. Read back, the network is the simulator's, device for device and port for port ( discover.rs tests it, the estate object for object at 200,000), with one difference that is the point: the two cables the made-up inventory forgot are there, and the two links it kept after they were removed are not. On the simulated network the feeds are about 230,000 rows, read in about 3 seconds as scrolls. Each site, device, link, session and hub says which feeds found it and by which rule in netops_discovered , and the Discovery page shows them, with the feeds' rows and the queries that read them; the Network map places each site where its devices' sysLocation puts it. A restart reads the feeds again and keeps the inventory (and what is down in it) when it finds the same network. A database made one way is refused by the other: start the other way with another --local directory, or with --reset . With --no-simulate --topology discovered , the app reads feeds written by something else, and stops if they name no device."],
["netops.html#the-tables","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","The tables","table what it holds netops_sites sites: points of presence and data centres netops_devices devices, as the inventory records them netops_links the inventory's links: an edge table, so graph queries walk the topology netops_seen_links links read from the logs (LLDP neighbours, routing adjacencies), compared with the inventory netops_circuits leased circuits and who provides them netops_bgp BGP sessions with transit providers and peers netops_services services sold, their objectives and the devices they ride on netops_customers customers, where they attach and what they are known by (prefix, port, circuit) netops_snmp_system , netops_snmp_ifaces , netops_lldp , netops_bgpls_nodes , netops_bgpls_links , netops_bmp_peers , netops_gpon_onts what the network reports, from which --topology discovered builds it ( above ) netops_discovered how each site, device, link, session and estate hub was found: the feeds and the rule netops_link_metrics a point a minute per link: the rollup an incident needs, not a metrics store netops_device_metrics a point a minute per device netops_service_metrics a point a minute per service: requests, errors, latency netops_flows flow summaries a minute: who talks to whom netops_syslog device logs, searched by words (BM25) and by any substring (an FM-index) netops_alert_rules alert rules: each one is a SQL query over these tables netops_alerts alerts the rules raised netops_incidents incidents: alerts correlated over the topology, with the cause the agent named netops_timeline what happened in each incident, in order netops_changes changes, planned and done, with the devices each touches ( INT[] , so scopes overlap with && ) netops_configs every version of each device's configuration, for blame and search netops_tickets tickets, open and closed netops_runbooks runbooks, found by words and by meaning netops_judgments which runbook closed which incident: grades the ranking can learn from netops_oncall who is on call, by team and shift netops_advisories vendor advisories, joined with the inventory's software versions netops_agent_runs each run of an agent netops_agent_steps every step of every run, with the SQL it ran netops_receipts what each agent was shown and what was left out netops_evidence why the root-cause agent named its cause: what happened, each claim with the query that shows its rows, and what it ruled out netops_actions actions agents proposed, and who decided them netops_silences what is kept from paging, by whom, why and until when netops_maintenance carriers' maintenance notices, each matched to a circuit and a window netops_handovers notes left from one shift to the next netops_panels each incident's board: one read-only query a panel netops_estate_nodes everything the network is made of, from regions down to a customer's router: its place on the map, its level of detail, its state and its latest readings netops_estate_edges what feeds what, upstream to downstream (an edge table) netops_estate_events faults, their clearing and changes on every object, with their words, cause and author netops_searches the questions asked in a sentence: who read each, the plan and the SQL it became netops_faults the simulator's answer key (agents never read it) netops_sources where each table's rows come from netops_state the app's own bookkeeping The Data page shows each table's CREATE TABLE , and they can be queried in the SQL workbench like any other:"],
["netops.html#what-it-is-not","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","What it is not","Out of the box it is a demonstration on synthetic data, and it has been run on real feeds only in its own test (readings written to the server from outside, with the simulator off). The telemetry is a point a minute per link, device and service, which is what an incident needs; ChloeDB is not a metrics store, and a real deployment keeps its high-resolution metrics where they are and writes rollups here. Approving an action changes the simulated network, or is only recorded with the simulator off: nothing is ever sent to a device. The reader behind \"add a panel\" knows a fixed set of readings and tables; it is not a language model. The inventory is read once at start, and at most 10,000 rows a table. With --topology both , once the discovered network has been opened, the app simulates two networks and writes twice the telemetry. The feeds the discovered network is read from are the simulator's own, written the way collectors would write them; its rules expect sysLocation to carry a site code, city, region and coordinates, as an operator's convention would, and have not met a real network's gaps (a port with LLDP off, a hostname that differs from sysName)."],
["netops.html#test-it","chloedb-netops: network operations on ChloeDB","Test it","tests/cluster.rs starts the app on a real chloedb-server (built in ../chloedb-server/target , or CHLOEDB_SERVER_BIN ) and checks the tables, the topology found from logs, each of the six faults against its answer key (and that every claim of its evidence has rows behind it, or none when it says nothing happened), an approval, the webhook, where it is broken during a fibre cut (the stack by place, an investigation, a trace), the shift's pages (handover, lookup, a customer's check, acknowledging and silencing, a change carried out and verified, carriers' notices and their collisions), an incident's board, the leadership numbers, a restart on the same folder, and then the simulator off: readings written to the server from outside open an incident that the agent diagnoses. Another starts it with --topology discovered and checks the network read back from its feeds, the Discovery page, a fault diagnosed on it, a restart, and that the made-up inventory is refused on its tables. A third starts it as it starts by default, with both, and checks the two networks side by side: each in tables of its own, the SQL shown naming the tables it ran on, a fault on one and not on the other, and a restart that reads the second again rather than building it again."],
["hn.html","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","","A demo. chloedb-hn shows what ChloeDB can do; it is not part of ChloeDB's releases or their promises. It is tested on a train of its own (the Demos workflow, .github/workflows/demos.yml ), outside CI's required check, and the command center marks it as a demo ( compatibility ). chloedb-hn keeps every public Hacker News item in ChloeDB, about 49 million stories, comments, jobs and polls from October 2006 on, and searches them the way people look for things on HN: a story they half remember, what HN said about a tool, who knows a subject, when something was talked about, or a question asked in a sentence. Questions in a sentence, answered by SQL, no model. \"which post has received the most comments\", \"who posted the most stories about rust\", \"how many Show HN posts about postgres in 2023\", \"when was HN most into bitcoin\", \"what was the first story about docker\". A small grammar ( src/nl.rs ) reads the question into what it asks (the top item by a measure, a count, people, sites or years), the topic and the filters, and the answer above the results is one query, shown with it. Anything the grammar does not read is searched as words. Autocomplete of questions, phrases and people. As a search is typed the box offers whole questions the grammar reads ( which post → \"which post has received the most comments\"), the phrases HN's titles use most ( kubernetes o → \"kubernetes operator\"), people and sites. The phrases are a table, hn_complete , keyed by kind and text, so completing is a prefix of its sorted key: one run of the dictionary, read from a few blocks ( PREFIX(id, 'p:kubernetes o') ). Instant, a keystroke at a time. Each keystroke shows the stories whose titles hold the words typed so far, the last one as a word or the start of one ( rust asy is MATCH(title, 'rust (asy OR asy*)') ), by points while that word is being typed and by BM25 times the votes once a space follows it: one statement, no count, no meaning. Enter runs the full search, which reads the last word whole. It is off until the Instant switch beside the box turns it on, and it and Fast are one at a time: switching one on switches the other off. A question being typed still gets its completions. Words and meaning together. Titles, texts and the words of each link are ranked by BM25 with typos, stems and synonyms, fused with a static embedding model's similarity on the server, and weighed by points ( pow(points + 1, 0.35) ) and a gentle decay for age. Every word must be there for searches of up to three words, and most of them for longer ones, so the counts and facets match the results. Exact text, punctuation and all. `C++` , `std::move` , `k8s.io/api` in backticks, or Match by → exact text , are matched where their characters are, by ILIKE answered from each segment's FM-index ( OPTIONS(substring = true) ), which tokenized search cannot do. Highlights the way the index reads. A server of 0.3.7 or later marks each result with POST /v1/highlight : stems, synonyms, typos and code as the field's analyzer reads them, cut to the stretch with the most matches. Filters in the words. by:pg , site:github.com , points>500 , comments>100 , 2019..2021 , before 2015 , -crypto , show hn , ask hn , \"exact phrase\" ; each is shown as a removable chip of what was understood. Threads, people and the graph. A story opens as its whole thread; a comment opens in place with the path to it. People pages show what someone writes about, when, and who they talk to (the reply graph, hn_replies ). Who knows ranks the people who comment most on a subject, weighed by the discussion they start. Similar stories by meaning, and the same link discussed again. What HN thinks. A question about opinions gathers the comments that speak to it, packed into a model's budget by the database ( PACK n TOKENS ... DEDUPE ... WITH RECEIPT ), and a model behind the server's llm() sums them up with every claim cited to a numbered comment. Answers are kept, so a question asked again costs nothing. It, Weekly and Changed minds are the parts that call a model. A link across HN. Links takes a page or a site and shows everywhere HN met it: each time it was submitted, the comments that cite it in other threads, the people who submitted, discussed or cited it, and the links discussed beside it, drawn as one graph ( GET /v1/link?u= ). A site alone ( sqlite.org ) lists its most discussed pages, the pages submitted again and again, and who posts it most. The week on HN. Weekly is a digest of a week's ten most voted stories (one per link, jobs left out): what each is, why it mattered, what the thread said, with the top comments quoted, and an opening over the whole week. The app reads each story's linked page once (public http and https pages only, with a timeout) and llm() writes an entry from the page, the post and its most replied comments; entries are kept in hn_answers , so each story is written once ( --digest-model , at most --digest-per-day new entries a day). The canon. Canon ranks the books and papers HN recommends, by the people who linked or named each, one person once: for all years, for a span of them ( 2010..2014 ) or by a word of the title. A work's page shows the people who named it a year, its best received comments with the name marked, and how it was counted. canon/build.py builds hn_canon from the cluster: the comments that link Amazon, Goodreads or arXiv, the works' titles from Open Library and the arXiv API, books merged across editions by title and first author, then every work counted. A comment counts when it links the work, uses its short name (SICP), writes the title as a title (each word capitalised: \"Getting Things Done\", not \"getting things done\") beside a word about books or papers, or writes it beside one of its authors' names, which is the only way to name a title of one or two words (\"Steve Jobs\", \"Deep Learning\"); a paper counts only from the year of its arXiv id. So a title that is also a common phrase, a person or a subject (\"World Models\", \"On Writing\") is not counted every time it is written. Changed minds. For a topic ( rust , bitcoin , remote work ), the people who wrote about it in four years or more, and for one of them an arc: their comments that BM25 finds most about it, three a year at most, read by a model through llm() for where each stands (from -2, against, to 2, for, or no view when a comment only explains a detail) with a quote, and what they thought first and last; a year's stance is the mean of its comments that take a side, so neutral and technical comments are shown but do not move it. A quote the comment does not hold word for word is dropped, and every point links to its comment. Arcs are kept in hn_answers ; a new one counts against --ask-per-day . Fast mode. The switch beside the search box ranks by words alone (BM25, no meaning and no reranking) for every search until it is switched off. Live. With --feed the app follows the HN API: new items as they are posted, and the points and comments of the stories that change. Like the crawler and network operations , it is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, and the command center shows it as an app ( Apps → Hacker News )."],
["hn.html#run-it","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","Run it","Build both programs, then prepare the archive and start the app with a server of its own: prepare --download fetches the archive's monthly Parquet files (about 12 GB, from open-index/hacker-news ) and writes the app's rows: stories, comments (each with its story's title, depth, place among its siblings and the discussion under it), people, the reply graph and the links between stories. --from 2024-01 and --to take part of it; --comments-since keeps every story and only the newer comments. complete writes autocomplete's rows from them. serve --local DIR starts a chloedb-server with its database in DIR , makes the tables, loads the rows when they are empty, and lists the app as hn ; open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → Hacker News . Against a server or a cluster that runs elsewhere, load into it and give the server the app: --comment-model none keeps the comments without vectors: they take a third of the bytes, and are found by their words and their story's title. The people and sites questions group by author or site; a server whose --max-groups (65,536 by default) is below HN's 1.1 million authors answers them for a topic but not over the whole archive, so give it --max-groups 2000000 ."],
["hn.html#the-questions-it-reads","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","The questions it reads","asked answered by which post has received the most comments / the most upvoted story about X / the top 5 Show HN posts in 2023 the top stories by comments, points or date, with the question's filters ( ORDER BY comments DESC ) the first / oldest / newest story about X ORDER BY created how many stories mention X / number of comments about X in 2020 COUNT(*) with the same match as the results, and its years who posted the most about X / who commented the most / who has the most karma GROUP BY author ; HN's archive carries no karma, so karma is the points a person's stories earned, and the answer says so who knows the most about X the people who comment most on it, weighed by the discussion they start which sites are posted the most about X GROUP BY site when was HN most into X / which year did X peak the years' counts, each as a share of that year's stories (HN grew; raw counts favour late years) what does HN think about X the cited summary above, the one answer a model writes Each answer shows its SQL ( see the SQL ). The grammar's templates are also autocomplete's questions, so what the box offers it can answer."],
["hn.html#read-the-linked-pages","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","Read the linked pages","crawl reads each page the stories link to, once, into hn_pages , with the crawler 's fetcher and frontier: robots.txt read before a site's first page and obeyed, one request at a time to a site and a second between two (longer when its robots.txt or its answers ask), redirects checked hop by hop, public addresses only, HTML only, at most 5 MB a page. The links are the stories' canonical links ( curl ) of at least --min-points points, most voted first. Every link asked becomes a row saying what came of it ( ok , noindex , robots , http_4xx with its code, network , timeout , not_html , ...), so the table also says which of HN's links still answer, by the year HN first linked them. A page whose <meta name=\"robots\"> says noindex keeps no text. A run skips the links already in the table, so a crawl that stops goes on where it was; --again network,timeout asks those that failed that way again. --contact goes in the user agent, for a site's owner to reach whoever runs it."],
["hn.html#tables","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","Tables","table what hn_stories stories, Ask and Show HN, jobs and polls: title, text, link (its site and words), author, time, points, comments, kind, topics, a vector of the title and text hn_comments comments: text, author, time, parent and its author, story and its title, depth, place among siblings, replies, the discussion under it (and a vector, unless --comment-model none ) hn_users people: stories, comments, points earned, first and last item, replies received, people talked to hn_replies the reply graph: who replied to whom, how often hn_links which discussion links to which: a story or one of its comments naming another's item?id= hn_pages the linked pages as crawl read them, keyed by canonical link: what came of asking, the HTTP code, the year HN first linked it, the most voted story and how many link it, the page's title, description, text (at most --max-text characters), words, language, date, author and image, and a vector of its title, description and text hn_canon the canon: a row a book or paper, with its title, authors, cover, link, the people who named it in all and a year, its best received comments, the statement that found its candidates, the rule that kept them and how many each part kept hn_complete autocomplete: phrases of titles ( p: ), sites ( s: ), topics ( t: ) and people ( u: ), each with how many stories hold it and their points hn_answers , hn_searches , hn_state kept answers, the searches people ran (their words and counts only) and the feed's place"],
["hn.html#the-api","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","The API","route what GET /v1/search?q=&only=all\\|story\\|comment&sort=best\\|date\\|points\\|comments&mode=hybrid\\|words\\|meaning\\|exact\\|fast\\|instant&page= results, what was understood, the answer when it is a question, facets, \"did you mean\", and every query run with its time GET /v1/suggest?q= completions: questions, phrases, people, sites, stories GET /v1/item/:id , /v1/item/:id/around a thread; similar stories and the same link again GET /v1/user/:id , /v1/experts?q= a person; who knows a subject GET /v1/trends?q=a,b&by=year\\|month how often each was talked about GET /v1/ask?q= what HN thinks, cited GET /v1/front the front page as SQL: points over age, as HN ranks it GET /v1/facets?q= a search's facets on their own (the page asks for them once the results are shown) GET /v1/ask/comments?q= the comments an answer is made from, before the model is asked GET /v1/trending , /v1/spikes?year= what titles are suddenly about (the last hours against the year before); the months' strongest spikes GET /v1/link?u= a page or a site across HN: its submissions, citing comments, people, related links and the graph of them; with no u , the most discussed stories GET /v1/digest?week=YYYY-MM-DD a week's digest: the stories picked, the entries and opening already written, the week's totals; the last full week by default GET /v1/canon?kind=book\\|paper&from=&to=&q= , /v1/canon/work?id= the canon, ranked by everyone or by the people of the years asked; one work with its comments and how it was counted GET /v1/minds?topic= , /v1/minds/arc?topic=&who= the people who wrote about a topic for years, with the arcs already read; one person's arc (read and kept when it is new) GET /v1/digest/write?week=&id= writes one entry ( id a story of the week's picks, or intro ) and keeps it"],
["hn.html#measured-against-hnalgoliacom","chloedb-hn: Hacker News on ChloeDB","Measured against hn.algolia.com","eval/compare.py asks both the same queries, made from stories both hold: a story found again from three words of its title, the same with a typo, the story described in other words, and topics whose top ten a model grades blind to which engine found what. The results are in chloedb-hn/eval/results , with the machine and the date they were measured on. eval/instant.py types six searches a character at a time and asks both at each keystroke, as their pages do. On 2026-11-16, while crawl was writing pages into the same cluster, the app's instant search took 10 ms at the median on the server (22 ms at the 90th percentile) against Algolia's 13.5 ms (34 ms), and its round trip from the same cloud machine 48 ms against Algolia's 199 ms ( the numbers ). eval/latency.py times the same searches on both, each asked twice, as the server reports it and from end to end. On 2026-11-16 (a cloud machine against the deployment on DigitalOcean, four shards of 16 GB machines with the data on their own disks), the app's whole search took 100 ms at the median the first time a query was asked and 67 ms the second; hn.algolia.com's engine reported 17 ms, and its full round trip from the same machine was 243 ms against the app's 140 ms. eval/plain.py times the simplest statements on the same queries: BM25 of the titles alone took 15 ms at the median, as Algolia's 16.5, and the substring index with no ranking took 8 ms ( the numbers )."],
["arxiv.html","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","","A demo. chloedb-arxiv shows what ChloeDB can do; it is not part of ChloeDB's releases or their promises. It is tested on a train of its own (the Demos workflow, .github/workflows/demos.yml ), outside CI's required check, and the command center marks it as a demo ( compatibility ). chloedb-arxiv is an explorer of every paper on arXiv: 3.25 million papers up to this week, the people who wrote them, the citations between them, about 1,100 topics found from what they say, techniques and domains, and a feed per reader that reads like a research newsletter. It is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, like the crawler and the spreadsheets , and the command center shows it as an app. Everything it does is a query to ChloeDB, and each page shows the queries it ran and their times ( SQL at the bottom right): Search by words and meaning at once. Titles and abstracts are ranked by BM25 and by the meaning of their text (a static embedding model on the server, no API call), fused by rank ( rrf ), with typos corrected from the titles' own words and the query's synonyms ( RAG , retrieval augmented generation ). A query is read for what it says: a year ( since 2023 ), a person ( by Yann LeCun ), a category ( cs.IR ), a kind ( surveys of ), an id. Facets (year, category, technique, domain, kind, people) come from the same query. A question is answered in a paragraph whose claims cite the papers they come from ( llm() on the server). What to read next is ChloeDB's recommender. Every paper a work cites is a reading event, 5.2 million of them, counted by CREATE RECOMMENDER reading ON ax_events(...) : what is read together, what is read after. A paper's Read next fuses those counts with PageRank from it over the citation graph ( ppr(ax_cites, :id) ) and the nearest papers by meaning. For you does the same from the papers a reader opens and keeps here, which are events of the recommender as soon as they are written. People, topics, techniques, domains, categories and venues each have a page : papers a year, who leads, what rose, the people nearest a person in the coauthor graph ( ppr(ax_coauthors, :who) ), a topic summed up from its papers with each claim cited. What's rising , over a span the reader picks (a month, three, six, a year or two): the techniques, domains and topics whose share of papers grew most against the span before, and only where the growth is beyond chance. Gaps. Rising techniques that have barely reached a domain where the techniques most like them already work. Over the last three years a technique's papers are counted by domain and by area; its look-alikes are the techniques whose domain counts point the same way (cosine, with the domain in question left out), and its expected papers in a domain are its papers times the look-alikes' share of that domain. A gap is a domain with a quarter of the expected papers or fewer, where the technique and the domain both grow faster than the median, their areas overlap, and two of the three closest look-alikes use the domain more than arXiv does. A gap's page shows the few papers that already combine the two, what the look-alikes did in the domain, and the people who write on both. The list is about 320 GROUP BY queries, kept for twelve hours ( src/gaps.rs ). New topics. Topics that barely existed and now hold hundreds of papers, born in the last year, two or three. A topic's papers are counted by month over five years (one GROUP BY topic, month for 150 topics at a time) and smoothed over three months; it was born in the first month its count reached a fifth of its peak, when the year before held an eighth of the peak or less, so an old topic that merely grew is not new. Each shows its curve with the month it was born, the papers that started it (its most cited from six months before to two after) and where it is now: rising, fading, at its peak or steady, from its share of arXiv's papers over the last six full months against the six before ( src/born.rs ). How techniques spread. When each technique left the field it grew up in and reached the next, year by year, with the most cited paper of its first two years in each. A technique's papers are counted by primary category and year (one GROUP BY pcat, year each); it reached a category in the first year with five or more papers there and a twentieth of its best year there, two years running. It grew up in the earliest of the categories that hold a twentieth of its papers, and earlier uses of the same words in small categories, often another meaning (a transformer in power systems), are left out. Pick a field to see what reached it and when ( src/spread.rs ). Who coined it. Ask who coined chain of thought (or who first used , where does X come from ): the answer names the papers that made the term known, the most cited early papers that use it in the sense of its most cited papers (the sense is their titles' meaning, so a 1996 solar physics paper that says chain of thought is set apart as another sense), the literal first use, the year it took off, and when it reached each area. A term in use before arXiv began says so. What to cite. Paste a draft's abstract and references (arXiv ids, DOIs, links or written-out titles): you get the papers others cite together with those references (the recommender's pair counts, normalised by both papers' counts) and those nearest to them by meaning, fused by rank, minus what you already cite, each with why it is there. On 15 papers of October 2026 (posted after the citation data, so their own lists are not in the counts), with a fifth of the references each one resolves hidden, it put 14 of the 72 hidden references in its first 30, against 7 for raw pair counts and the abstract, 3 for the abstract alone and none for PageRank over the citations. What is new here. On a paper's page: the three earlier papers it is closest to (those it cites first, then the most cited of the 40 nearest by meaning posted before it), and what it adds to each and shares with them, written once by llm() and kept. Trends and the atlas. Any words, compared as a share of each year's papers. The atlas places the topics on one map, near topics near, colored by arXiv area, sized by papers, a ring on those that grew most last year. Feeds you shape yourself. A feed says what you want in your words: topics, papers like some you name, categories, techniques, domains, people whose new papers always go in, words that keep a paper out, kinds (surveys, benchmarks, datasets, tools), how many papers, how many of one topic, for whom, and how much code, citations and known authors count. The papers an issue would hold are SQL and answer as the knobs move (no model is called). Write issue then reads each paper in full from its HTML page on arxiv.org (its sections, figures and tables) and explains it, so the issue can be read without opening the papers: an opening on what the week's papers share, a contents list, and for each paper in brief , the problem, the approach, the results with their numbers, the takeaway, the limits, the terms a newcomer needs, the one or two figures that explain it best (copied from the paper, with what to notice in each) and its main results table. A paper without an HTML version is explained from its abstract. While it writes, a screen follows it: the papers it chose, which one it is reading, explaining or copying a figure of, how long each took and about how long is left, with a line to smile at while arXiv's 15 seconds between pages pass; the page can be left and the screen comes back when the feed is opened again ( GET /v1/jobs/<id> holds the writer's phase, paper and steps). Each issue has a page, a Markdown copy for a newsletter and an RSS feed. A feed set to write itself writes its issue every Monday from 06:00 UTC, so its RSS brings each week's issue to a feed reader with no one pressing a button (an hourly round writes the feeds due, longest waiting first, six at a time; a reader has three such feeds). A feed's owner can share it with a team by a link: whoever opens it can change the feed and write its issues with no sign-in, the feed stays the owner's, and Stop sharing makes the link stop working ( POST /v1/feeds/<id>/team ); the shorter styles of a weekly research newsletter (a paragraph a paper, brief, bullets) remain. Standing questions. Ask once ( tell me when someone uses diffusion models for weather forecasting ) and every new paper that answers it comes to you, on a page and in an RSS feed. Every hour the papers of the last week are searched for the question (all its words and most of them by BM25, and its meaning, fused by rank), and the fifteen closest not read for it yet go to the model in one call ( llm() on the server), which says for each whether it does what the question asks and why. Every verdict is kept, so a paper is read once per question and an hour with nothing new calls no model; a new question reads the closest papers of the last month at once. A reader keeps ten questions ( src/watch.rs ). People to work with. On a person's page: the people whose work of the last four years is nearest theirs in meaning but who have never written with them or with anyone they write with, with the techniques each would bring, their papers nearest the person's work, and the shortest way to them in the coauthor graph (through people with the fewest papers, since a name with thousands is many people sharing it). Their eight newest and eight most cited papers of the last six years are one text; the 500 nearest papers are read and each author scores by their papers' ranks there ( src/collab.rs ). Who could review it. On a paper's page, or for a title and abstract pasted in with its authors: the people who know its subject (their papers among the 500 nearest to it in the last six years), with eight papers or more and one in the last three years, its authors and everyone who wrote with one of them in the last four years left out and named, each with their nearest papers and seniority. Rising people , on the Rising page: people whose first paper is at most five years old, by the citations to their papers of the last one or two years (two cited papers or more, of 20 authors or fewer), by area. Where someone's work went. A person's page shows their most used techniques year by year as a river, each band a technique and its width their papers using it that year. Code and data. The share of papers that link their code, year by year, in all of arXiv or an area; the repositories behind the most papers; the techniques whose papers share code most and least; the newest and most cited papers with code and those with models or data on Hugging Face ( src/code.rs ). has:code in a search keeps only papers with code. Labs. The organisations behind 2.5 million papers (78%), from the authors' affiliations in OpenAlex 's public snapshot: the labs with the most papers of the last twelve months, those whose share of the papers grew most, who is moving into a technique (a button on its page), and a page per lab with its papers a year, what it is known for and moving into, who writes its papers, the labs and countries it writes with. A paper's page names its organisations. OpenAlex names organisations for fewer of the newest papers, so growth is a lab's share, not its count ( src/labs.rs ). Likely classics. Recent papers that look, in their first months, like the papers that went on to be the most cited 1% of their year and area, by area and by how recently they were posted, each with its chance against the usual 1% and what weighed most. A logistic regression per window (no citations yet, or the arXiv papers citing it in its first 3, 6 or 12 months) is trained on 2016 to 2021 on what was known then: early citations, the authors' earlier papers, how fast its category and techniques grew, team size, code and kind. On 2022, which it did not see, 33% of its top 1% with six months of citations were in the real top 1%, against 18% for ranking by early citations and the authors' record ( tools/classics.py , which writes ax_classics and the models' card; GET /v1/classics?area=&days=&sort= ). For and against. State a claim ( LLMs can self-correct their reasoning without external feedback ) and get the papers whose results support it, those that contradict it and those that find it holds only in some settings, each with the sentence of its abstract that shows it. The 40 papers nearest the claim are found by the words of its subject (all of them, and all but one) and by its meaning, fused by rank, and the model reads their abstracts against it ten to a call, all at once ( llm() on the server). A side counts only with a sentence that is in the abstract word for word (spacing, case and punctuation aside), and the answer is kept, so a claim asked again calls no model ( src/claims.rs ). Explain at my level. A paper's page explains it for a student, a practitioner or an expert, each hard term of its abstract with its meaning at that level, the paper the field credits with the term (the earliest of its most cited papers on arXiv, as Who coined it reads it, or none when the words were in use before arXiv began) and a link to the full answer. The level is kept in the browser: topic summaries are written for it and a new feed's audience follows it. Each explanation is kept per level ( GET /v1/explain/<id>?level= ). From a reading list to related work. Name up to 30 papers (ids or links, a reading list, or your library) and get their BibTeX at once, made from their rows with no model (authors surname first, the venue when one is known, the arXiv identifiers; keys like vaswani2017attention ), and a related-work section in LaTeX written by the model from their abstracts, grouped by theme under \\paragraph{} and citing every paper by its key. A citation of a key not in the list is taken out, a paper left uncited is named, and the section is kept. Given a sentence on your own paper, each paragraph ends with how it differs ( src/related.rs ). It stays current. --live reads the new and revised papers from arXiv's OAI-PMH interface every six hours, three seconds between pages, and a new paper goes to the topic whose centroid its text is nearest."],
["arxiv.html#where-the-data-comes-from","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","Where the data comes from","Papers : arXiv's metadata ( librarian-bots/arxiv-metadata-snapshot , CC0), and the weeks after the snapshot from OAI-PMH . Citations : Crossref's references of arXiv papers' published versions ( cometadata/crossref-arxiv-citations ). A citing work that is an arXiv paper, by its DOI, makes an edge of the citation graph; every citing work is a reader of the recommender. Topics : tools/topics.py clusters every paper's embedding into 1,500 topics with k-means fitted on 300,000 older papers and 250,000 of the last two years (so recent research is not folded into a few catch-all topics), drops the topics fewer than 150 papers would join ( --min-papers ; 1,093 remain), places each paper in the nearest of its ten closest topics with a small pull toward topics that hold its primary category, puts each topic in one of 17 fields (arXiv's areas, by its papers' categories), names each from its terms and its most cited titles, and places them on the map. A topic that is mostly an old one keeps its id ( --keep ), and its name when its centroid has not moved ( --labels ). New papers are placed by the same rule in src/live.rs . Organisations of each paper come from OpenAlex 's public snapshot (CC0) on S3, read without a key: tools/oa_orgs.py reads only the columns it needs (whether a work is on arXiv, its arXiv link, its authors' institutions) from the 744 GB of Parquet files, about 30 GB, and tools/labs.py joins them with the papers' rows into ax_lab_papers and ax_labs . Affiliations for a feed's papers are read from each paper's HTML page on arxiv.org, once a paper and fifteen seconds apart, as arXiv's robots.txt asks. The app never reads arxiv.org's search or API pages."],
["arxiv.html#run-it","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","Run it","Build both programs, prepare the rows (a few GB of downloads), then start chloedb-arxiv with servers of its own: Open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → arXiv . --only cs.,stat.ML on prepare keeps a slice, which fits on a laptop."],
["arxiv.html#two-servers-split-by-job","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","Two servers, split by job","The papers, people, topics and their words live on one chloedb-server (the search server , --strata ); the reading events, the recommender, the citation and coauthor graphs, what readers keep, and the model calls live on another (the graph server , --graph ). A recommender and a graph walk each need their rows in one database, so these tables are not sharded over a coordinator; splitting by job gives each server a machine of its own instead. Without --graph , one server holds everything. The graph server holds a row per paper and per person ( ax_nodes ) for the walks to rank. deploy/k8s.yaml and deploy/app.yaml run it on Kubernetes: two nodes of 16 GB, a server on each, and the app beside the graph server. Each database is on its node's own SSD rather than a network volume, the graph database (3.6 GB) is read into memory after a start, and the search server allows a GROUP BY of two million groups, since \"who writes most\" on a broad subject groups every author of tens of thousands of papers. deploy/backup.yaml takes a checkpoint of each server a night ( POST /v1/admin/checkpoint into --backup-dir , hard-linking what the previous one has) and keeps the two newest. With --public the app serves the page at / to anyone, reads and a reader's own library and feeds, within --per-minute requests a minute from one address; https://arxiv.chloetech.ai is this, through a web proxy. --answer-model names the model of summaries and TL;DRs, and --issue-model the one that explains an issue's papers from their full text (both are called through the graph server's llm() , which holds the provider's key); --llm-per-day caps new calls a day, and everything written is kept, so it is written once. arXiv asks programs to read a page every 15 seconds, so the first issue of ten papers takes about ten minutes; a page and its figures, once read, are kept ( ax_notes , ax_figures )."],
["arxiv.html#the-tables","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","The tables","table server what ax_papers search a paper: title, abstract, people, categories, dates, techniques, domains, kinds, counts, topic, and emb (its text embedded by the server) ax_authors search a person: papers, citations, h-index, first and last paper, categories and techniques ax_topics , ax_concepts , ax_cats , ax_venues search topics, techniques and domains, categories, venues, with counts by year ax_complete search what the search box completes: phrases of titles, people, techniques, categories ax_nodes graph the graphs' nodes: every paper's id and every person's key ax_cites , ax_coauthors graph edges: who cites whom, who writes with whom and how often ax_events graph reading events: a citing work's references, and what readers open and save here reading_* graph the recommender's counts ( CREATE RECOMMENDER reading ) ax_feeds , ax_issues , ax_notes , ax_answers , ax_library graph feeds, their issues, the papers' pages as read and what the model wrote, readers' libraries ax_figures graph the figures issues show, each image copied once from the paper's page ax_labs , ax_lab_papers search each organisation with its papers in all, in the last twelve months and the twelve before; each paper's organisations with the columns a lab page counts by ax_watches , ax_matches graph readers' standing questions, and each paper read for one with the model's verdict and why"],
["arxiv.html#tests","chloedb-arxiv: arXiv on ChloeDB","Tests","tests/cluster.rs starts two chloedb-servers through serve --local --local-graph , loads a small made-up arXiv into them (embedded by the hash:512 model, so nothing is downloaded), and checks every page: search, a paper's citations and what to read next, a person, a topic, the atlas, events becoming For you, a feed's preview, what to cite for a draft, people to work with, reviewers for a paper and for a pasted abstract with its authors left out, a standing question kept, listed, read as RSS and dropped, and that gaps, new topics, spread, rising people, code, labs and trends answer."],
["github.html","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","","chloedb-gh is an app of a ChloeDB cluster built on GH Archive : every public event on GitHub since 12 February 2011, about ten billion of them, in a cluster of four shards on machines with 8 GB of memory each. It finds repos by what they do, says why a repo's stars moved, what people use instead, who keeps a repo going, what is rising and what to star next. It is a client of chloedb-server over HTTP, as the crawler and the spreadsheet app are, and the command center shows it as the app gh ( chloedb-server --app gh=URL ). Every request needs the token the command center's app proxy sends, unless the app runs with --public : then it serves its page at / and the page's reads to anyone, at most --per-minute (600) requests a minute from one address and --at-once (8) answered at once, the rest waiting for a turn. Readers never send SQL: each page asks its own statements. It runs that way at https://gh.chloetech.ai."],
["github.html#the-pages","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","The pages","page what it shows from Search repos by what they do: the words of their name, description and topics, and the meaning of the query against a vector of each repo, ranked by both and by stars gh_repos ( MATCH , bm25 , similarity ) A repo its stars month by month, the days and months they jumped (five and four times the usual) with the releases of those months, and its posts on Hacker News, its vital signs (work this year against last, merged pull requests, how many people do half the work, days since the last), who keeps it going, the repos that do the same job (by meaning and by the stargazers they share), what its stargazers also starred, and the names it had gh_events , gh_stars , gh_repo_months , gh_repos , gh_repo_names , gh_repo_people , gh_people A person what they did, year by year, where they work and what they starred lately (public activity only) gh_people , gh_events , gh_stars , gh_actors Rising the repos whose stars over the last days most outran the four weeks before, and why gh_stars_daily Trends each language's and topic's share of the stars, year by year; growing and shrinking gh_lang_months , gh_topic_months Ask a question in a sentence, turned into one statement by patterns, no model (\"fastest growing rust repos this year\", \"who works on rust-lang/rust\") all of them For you repos to star next from a login's stars and work: what the people who starred the same repos starred gh_stars , gh_people Weekly the week's rising repos, new ones and releases of the big ones gh_stars_daily , gh_events How it works the tables, their rows and each shard's share, and what each page asks every table A repo's page comes in two answers: /v1/repo/<owner>/<name> has everything but the alternatives and what its stargazers also starred, and /v1/repo-more/<id> brings those two once the rest is shown, since its stargazers' stars take seconds for a big repo. Each answer carries the statements it ran and their times, which the page shows under SQL."],
["github.html#the-data","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","The data","GH Archive keeps each hour of GitHub's public event stream as a gzipped JSON file. Its formats changed over the years, and the load reads them all: From February 2011 to February 2012 events carry the repo's and the person's ids. From about March to September 2012 (the timeline format) they carry neither: a repo is owner/name and a person a login. From October 2012 to 2014 repos have ids and people only logins. From 2015 on, both have ids. The years without ids get them from the years with: the logins and names of 2011-2012 and 2015-2016 ( --logins , --names ), the id that held a name first winning; a login or a name no later event explains gets a negative id of its own. From October 2025 GitHub's events no longer carry a repo's description, language or topics, so repos made since have none in the archive; api-meta fills them from GitHub's search API (below). Only what the events say publicly is kept: ids, logins, repos, kinds of event and times. The old format's actor_attributes (names and email addresses) is never read."],
["github.html#the-tables","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","The tables","table rows placed by gh_events every event: t (seconds), kind , repo , actor , n (commits of a push) shard_by repo , sorted by repo in each segment gh_stars every star of a kept repo again, with the person as a KEYWORD ( actor_k ) too: an IN of a thousand people reads their own lists of rows, where the events in repo order would be read whole shard_by repo gh_stars_daily stars and forks of each repo each day shard_by repo gh_repos each repo with two stars or more, keyed by its id: latest name, what the events (and GitHub's search, for repos with 50 stars or more) last said of it, its stars counted from the events and GitHub's own count, and for repos with ten stars or more (the ones searched by meaning) a vector of what it does, embedded from etext , their name, description and topics: a shard's vectors and graph then fit an 8 GB machine key repo gh_vec the 3.3 million repos of gh_repos with a vector, with only their stars and language beside it, in a cluster of their own ( deploy/vec.yaml ) that the app's searches by meaning go to ( --vec ): there each part's quantized vectors and graph, about 500 MB, are locked in memory, where beside the events a new search read them from the SSD (1 to 1.5 s; 40 to 110 ms in a cluster of their own) key repo gh_repo_names every name a repo had, with when it was first seen shard_by repo gh_people each person's events summed, a row a person a shard ( part , which the page adds up): how many, their stars, their work, by kind, by year and their 24 busiest repos with the first and last work in each, so a person's page reads four rows where its statements over gh_events read a shard's whole actor column (about 30 s); beside gh_vec , in the same database shard_by part , sorted by actor gh_repo_people the 30 people who did the most work in each repo of gh_repos , with their first and last work there shard_by repo gh_actors each person's latest public login key actor gh_repo_months what happened in each repo each month: stars, forks, pushes, commits, pull requests opened and merged, issues, comments, releases, people shard_by repo gh_lang_months , gh_topic_months the same summed by language and by topic, a part a shard ( part ), which queries add up shard_by part Rows are placed on shards as chloedb-server places them (a key's slot is xxh3-64 of its typed encoding modulo 4096, slot s on shard s * n / 4096 of a new cluster), so each shard's database is built offline, on its own machine, and the cluster's coordinator finds every row where its slot map says it is."],
["github.html#the-load","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","The load","The load runs on the cluster's own machines ( chloedb-gh/deploy/work.yaml : a pod a node, its folder on the node's SSD, /work ): chloedb-gh prepare --from 2015-01-01 --to DAY --part N/4 --out /work/out on each node, every fourth day: downloads each hour, keeps the events, dedupes a day at a time, and writes Parquet rows a table, a shard and a day ( out/TABLE/sS/DAY.parquet : events, stars, names, actors, meta). A day done is written down ( out/done/DAY.json ) and skipped when it runs again. A worker holds one day in memory (about 1 GB for the busiest days of 2022), so 8 GB machines run three at a time ( --jobs 3 ). The years before 2015 , in the order that gives each its ids: 2011-02-12 to 2012-02-29, then its actors and names to every node ( deploy/pull.py --shard all --tables actors,names ), 2012-10-01 to 2014-12-31 with --logins and --names , its names to every node, and 2012-03-01 to 2012-09-30. chloedb-gh api-meta --out /work/out : what GitHub's search API says today of every repo with 50 stars or more (about 876,000), 100 repos a call: the repos made since October 2025 first. Search answers at most 1,000 repos a query, so the walk splits bands of stars, then days of creation, until each part has fewer. Without a token it makes at most 10 calls a minute from one address (a call every 7 s, about a day for the walk); with GITHUB_TOKEN , 30. Its rows are meta and names rows ( api-*.parquet ), which reduce reads after the days'. Each node takes its shard's rows from the others ( deploy/pull.py --shard N --into /work/in ; each node serves /work/out with a list a shard). chloedb-gh reduce --input /work/in --input /work/out --shard N sums a shard's rows into one row a key ( gh_repos , gh_repo_names , gh_actors ), months by repo, language and topic, and the star rows of gh_stars ; only repos with --vec-min-stars (10) or more get the etext their vector is made of. Once every node has its shard's rows, each deletes its rows of the other shards, so that a shard's rows, its sums and its database fit the node's disk. chloedb-server import loads each table into the shard's database, with the server of the cluster's image ( --schema chloedb-gh/deploy/schemas/TABLE.json ), after reduce and not beside it: the two together go past an 8 GB machine. A table's files go in as one folder (the node's own and the ones it pulled, hard-linked together): an import loads an empty table in bulk from its first folder only, and a second folder goes through the write path, many times slower. Each shard's slot map goes beside its database before its server first starts, as gh.chloedb.slots : {\"shard\": N, \"since\": 1, \"map\": {\"epoch\": 1, \"shards\": [[[0, 1024]], [[1024, 2048]], [[2048, 3072]], [[3072, 4096]]]}} , the map its rows were placed by. A shard that a coordinator gives its first map reads the key of every row of every table to check that it belongs there, and on 2.8 billion rows that runs an 8 GB machine out of memory; a shard that already holds the map takes the coordinator's next one without the check. Then deploy/k8s.yaml starts a chloedb-server a node on its database ( /mnt/chloedb/gh/db ), the coordinator over them and the app (the shards without the embedding model, --no-fetch-models : with it, each start reads the 3.3 million repos that have no vector, to embed them, which takes minutes and more memory than the machine has; the coordinator embeds the words of a search itself, and a write to gh_repos brings its vector), and deploy/backup.yaml writes a checkpoint of each shard every night and copies the newest off the node to a DigitalOcean volume. deploy/vec-build.yaml builds each node's part of gh_vec beside its running shard (the repos of its reduce output that have an etext , imported with the shard's server and model, and the slots file of its part: about seven minutes a node), and deploy/vec.yaml serves the four parts, gh-v0 to gh-v3 , each beside its shard in a container of its own with no scheduled scrub (reading a part's 4.5 GB against its checksums every day pushed the vectors out) and tools/pin beside the server, which locks the part's quantized vectors and graph in memory ( mlock , about 500 MB: a container of their own was not enough, since after half an hour without searches the node's reclaim took them anyway, and a new search went back to 0.4 to 1 s), and their coordinator gh-vec , which embeds the words of a search; the app runs with --vec http://gh-vec.strata.svc:7700 . Without --vec , it searches gh_repos by meaning. deploy/people-build.yaml adds gh_people and gh_repo_people to a copy of each part, gh-side.chloedb , on a 20 GB volume of its own a node ( gh-side-N , mounted at /side ), which deploy/vec.yaml then serves: on each node chloedb-gh people reads the shard's events in 24 passes, a 24th of the people each (a pass holds a shard's sums for about 2.3 million people in memory, 1.4 to 1.8 GB; 12 passes ran a 3 GB container out, and one of 16 did), chloedb-gh repo-people keeps each repo's 30 busiest people, and the server imports both into the copy, since an import needs the database closed (the passes take about 32 minutes a node). With the copy of the part (4.5 GB) and the pass files (3.2 GB), a shard's summaries (2.3 GB once imported) need about 10 GB, where each node's SSD has 4 to 6 GB above the kubelet's eviction line (15% free), below which it evicts the node's pods, the shard among them. Without the two tables the app reads gh_events for the same answers."],
["github.html#run-it-on-one-machine","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","Run it on one machine","A few days of the archive into one database: serve without --token answers anyone on the machine; CHLOEDB_GH_UI_DIR=chloedb-gh/ui reads the view from disk without a rebuild."],
["github.html#against-githubs-search","GitHub Explorer: GitHub's public history on ChloeDB","Against GitHub's search","chloedb-gh/bench/ holds 99 searches written as a person looking for a kind of project would type them (\"terminal file manager\", \"self-hosted photo and video backup like google photos\"), each with the repos that answer it, written before either engine's answers were seen. search_vs_github.py asks GitHub's repository search (its default best-match order, within its rate limit) and the explorer's /v1/search the same words, compares repos by their GitHub ids (a repo renamed since is the same repo), and scores the first ten: whether an answer is first, whether one is in the ten, and MRR@10. Six searches are close to ones the ranking was compared on while it was written; the report gives the scores with and without them."],
["sound.html","chloedb-sound: Sound Explorer on ChloeDB","","A demo. chloedb-sound shows what ChloeDB can do; it is not part of ChloeDB's releases or their promises. It is tested on a train of its own (the Demos workflow, .github/workflows/demos.yml ), outside CI's required check, and the command center marks it as a demo ( compatibility ). chloedb-sound keeps free sounds in ChloeDB and finds them by how they sound: field recordings, effects and music that anyone may reuse with credit, each with its author, licence and a link back to where it came from. It listens with CLAP ( clap-htsat-unfused ), which puts sounds and sentences in one space of 512 dimensions, run by chloedb-server itself (built with --features audio ). Search by how it sounds. \"rain on a tin roof\", \"a crowd cheering far away\", \"a cello played softly\": the words are heard through CLAP's text tower and ranked against every sound's vector, fused with BM25 over its title, tags, labels and description ( 0.75 * tmm(similarity(emb, :v)) + 0.25 * tmm(bm25(words, :q)) ). Rank by picks one of the two. Facets count the source, the kind, the licence and the length of the best 200. Steer it. More like this and Less like this on any result move the search towards and away from that sound's vector; not takes words to move away from (\"no thunder\"); the chips (heavier, lighter, drier, wetter, closer, farther, busier, calmer) move it between two sentences. Each is shown as a chip of what was understood. Moments inside long recordings. Every sound is cut into ten-second windows every five seconds, each with its own vector ( au_moments ), so a search can land 7:40 into a half-hour dawn chorus. Moments returns the best moment of each sound ( DISTINCT ON (sound) ) and the other good moments of each. What is in it. A sound's page shows, along its waveform, which of FSD50K's 200 labels each moment sounds like (each moment's vector against the labels', au_labels , embedded by the server from a sentence each). Listen. Record ten seconds with the microphone or drop a file, and find what it sounds like: the clip is embedded by POST /v1/embed and searched like any sound; a moment matched above 0.95, or above 0.9 and at least 0.03 ahead of the next recording's, is called the same recording. Scenes. Describe a place (\"a rainy café by a harbour\") and get its layers: the sounds nearest the words and each part of them, one for each thing they sound like most (their best label by sound), played together with a level for each. Drift. From one sound to another in steps, each step the sound nearest to a point on the way between their vectors (a slerp). Map. Every sound placed by how it sounds (t-SNE of the vectors, made by tools/prepare.py ), or by where it was recorded when its source says. Credit and licences. Each sound carries its title, author, source page and licence, shown on the page and copied as one line (\"“Title” by author, source (page), licence (link)\"). Only CC0, public domain, CC BY, CC BY-SA and the Free Art License (and the GFDL on Commons) are kept: no non-commercial, no-derivatives or Sampling+ sound is loaded. Like the crawler and Hacker News , it is a program of its own, in Rust, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP only, and the command center shows it as an app ( Apps → Sound Explorer ). Previews are MP3 files on the app's disk, served by it with HTTP ranges, since a cluster's coordinator keeps no media files."],
["sound.html#run-it","chloedb-sound: Sound Explorer on ChloeDB","Run it","Build the server with CLAP and the app, download the sounds, make the rows, and start the app with a server of its own: serve --local DIR starts a chloedb-server with its database in DIR , makes the tables and the labels (the server fetches CLAP, 600 MB, the first time a field names it), and lists the app as sound ; open http://127.0.0.1:7700 and go to Apps → Sound Explorer . tools/prepare.py decodes each sound with ffmpeg, writes its preview and waveform, sends each ten-second window to the server's CLAP ( POST /v1/embed through au_labels ' model, about three windows a second on four cores with AVX-512), and writes the rows; it needs ffmpeg and numpy, and a run that stops goes on where it was. --concurrency sounds are made at once, and --embed URL,URL,... shares the windows among several servers with CLAP (each window to the one with the fewest at work). Of more than 4,000 sounds the map is a t-SNE of 4,000 of them, and each other sound is placed among the ten it sounds most like. Give serve the rows with --rows and it loads them when the tables are empty, and plays the previews from their audio/ folder ( --audio for another). The three sources, and what is kept of each: source what licences kept FSD50K Freesound clips labelled by hand with 200 classes of the AudioSet ontology; the evaluation set's, the same number of each class, or with --split both --clips 0 every clip of both sets (43,379 of 51,197 qualify) CC0, CC BY Free Music Archive 30-second clips of fma_small , or with --set large --shards 0 of all of fma_large (9,918 of its 105,024 tracks qualify), with their genres CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC0, Free Art License (about one track in ten) Wikimedia Commons sounds under its sound categories, with descriptions and, for some, a place CC0, public domain, CC BY, CC BY-SA, GFDL Freesound's own archive is not used: its terms do not allow a copy of it on another site. Against a server or a cluster that runs elsewhere, load into it and give the server the app: On Kubernetes, deploy/k8s.yaml runs it on one small node of its own (2 vCPUs, 4 GB): the server with CLAP, the app and a pod to work in, with the binaries, the model, the rows, the previews and the database on the node's own disk. A bigger set is made on machines of their own and copied in: deploy/batch.yaml runs a server with CLAP on each node of a temporary pool, and the first downloads the sounds, makes the rows with --embed over all of them and builds the database. The whole of FSD50K and fma_large 's clips that qualify, with a sample of Commons, came to 53,591 sounds and 132,486 moments, embedded in about six hours on three DigitalOcean 8-vCPU machines (s-8vcpu-16gb, AMD and Intel, about 6.5 windows a second together, a third of the speed per core of a Xeon with AVX-512); the database is 735 MB and the previews 8.5 GB. --public serves the page at / and its reads to anyone, without the token, within --per-minute requests a minute from one address (120 by default; the previews are not counted) and --listens-per-minute clips heard (6), and refuses calls from another site's pages. It serves no SQL: the page's SQL drawer shows the statements each answer ran. A server without CLAP (built without the audio feature, or without the model) still serves the app: searches by words are ranked by BM25 alone and say so, and the pages that hear words or clips answer with an error."],
["sound.html#tables","chloedb-sound: Sound Explorer on ChloeDB","Tables","table a row is its vector au_sounds a sound: title, words (title, tags, labels, description), author and its page, source page, licence and its link, kind, tags, labels, genres, length, year, place, preview file, waveform (160 peaks), place on the map, number of moments emb VECTOR(512) : the mean of its windows that start every ten seconds, the vector the server makes of the whole file au_moments ten seconds of a sound, every five seconds ( shard_by = 'sound' , so a sound's moments are on one shard) emb VECTOR(512) au_labels one of FSD50K's 200 labels, with a sentence (\"the sound of rain\") emb VECTOR(512) OPTIONS(embed = 'prompt', model = 'clap-htsat-unfused') : the server embeds it, and words and clips are embedded through this field"],
["sound.html#the-api","chloedb-sound: Sound Explorer on ChloeDB","The API","Every answer carries queries , the SQL it ran, for the page's SQL drawer. route what GET /v1/search?q=&like=&unlike=&not=&steer=&source=&kind=&lic=&min_s=&max_s=&by=&page= sounds by words and by sound, steered, filtered and faceted; by is both , sound or words GET /v1/sound/:id a sound with its credit, what is in it moment by moment, sounds like it and more by its author GET /v1/moments?q=&min= the best moment of each recording, and its other good moments, in recordings at least min seconds long (15 by default; songs are left to Music ) POST /v1/listen {audio: base64} (WAV, FLAC, MP3 or Ogg, at most 2 MB): what it sounds like, and whether it is one of the sounds GET /v1/scene?q= a scene's layers: a sound for each thing the place sounds like, with a level GET /v1/drift?from=&to=&steps= the sounds on the way from one to the other GET /v1/map every sound's place on the map and by where it was recorded GET /v1/home , /v1/labels , /v1/status the front page, the labels, the counts GET /v1/audio/:id a preview (MP3), with ranges; ?b64=1 as JSON for the command center"],
["sound.html#tests","chloedb-sound: Sound Explorer on ChloeDB","Tests","cargo test runs the app against a real chloedb-server ( CHLOEDB_SERVER_BIN , or one built in ../chloedb-server ) with nine made-up sounds and their moments: search by sound, steering, filters, words, a sound's credit, drift, the map, previews with ranges, and the public limits. CHLOEDB_SOUND_CLAP=1 against a server built with --features audio tests the pages that hear words and clips too; without it the test checks that words are still searched by BM25."],
["backbone.html","Backbone Explorer: the internet's routing on ChloeDB","","chloedb-backbone is an app of a ChloeDB server built on public data about how the internet's networks reach each other: every BGP update a RIPE RIS collector hears, checked as it arrives against RPKI and against the origins RouteViews saw the day before, with PeeringDB 's exchanges and buildings, CAIDA 's relationships between networks, RIPE Atlas 's measurements to the K root server and IODA 's outages. It says what is wrong now, where, and how the problems connect. It is a client of chloedb-server over HTTP, as the crawler and the spreadsheet app are, and the command center can show it as the app backbone ( chloedb-server --app backbone=URL ). Every request needs the token the command center's app proxy sends, unless the app runs with --public : then it serves its page at / and the page's reads to anyone, at most --per-minute (600) requests a minute from one address. Readers never send SQL: each page asks its own statements, and shows them under SQL. It runs that way at https://backbone.chloetech.ai."],
["backbone.html#the-pages","Backbone Explorer: the internet's routing on ChloeDB","The pages","page what it shows from Home what it is and where its data comes from, the open incidents, the world coloured by them, the slowest countries and the busiest prefixes, and how to read the console bb_incidents , bb_alerts , bb_updates , bb_ping Console one screen that stays while you move: every update a minute for the last six hours, with the RPKI-invalid ones and the incidents along it ( /v1/timeline ); the networks you watch, kept in your browser, with their alerts and their routes' updates in the last hour ( /v1/watch ); the open incidents on the left; a map whose camera flies from the world to a country to a network; and an inspector on the right with what is wrong, what changed, the numbers and the records of what is on the map the pages below A country its buildings, exchanges and RIPE Atlas probes where they are, its networks placed where their buildings are (else their probes), each probe coloured by its time to K, and where slow traceroutes leave it bb_fac , bb_netfac , bb_ix , bb_member , bb_probe , bb_ping , bb_trace A network its buildings, exchanges and probes on the map, a line to where it meets each provider, what it hangs from and what depends on it ( REACHABLE over the relationships), trouble within three hops, its prefixes and their updates a minute, its alerts bb_p2c , bb_p2p , bb_updates , bb_roa A prefix every update replayed into who holds a route to it now, its origins and their RPKI state, its paths now beside an hour ago (the ones that are new and the ones that are gone), drawn country to country on the map, the routes covering it and inside it, its ROAs bb_updates , bb_roa An incident its alerts, who announced, carried and owns the space, its countries on the map, its window on the timeline, and the updates a minute around it bb_alerts , bb_updates Diagnose for the slowest countries and those with the most probes, the stack from users to the K root server (time to K, paths that jump, peering, routing alerts, outages) and the lowest layer that is red bb_ping , bb_trace , bb_alerts , bb_outage Rules each alert rule's statement, how long it took and what it found bb_rules How it's built the tables, their rows, and where each source stands every table"],
["backbone.html#the-rules","Backbone Explorer: the internet's routing on ChloeDB","The rules","Each rule is one statement over the tables, run every minute; its rows are the alerts, kept open while it finds them, and alerts that share an owner, an announcer, a carrier or a country are grouped into incidents. rule finds rpki-invalid a route a ROA gives to another network, or longer than its ROA allows, heard by 3 or more peers (IPv4 /24 and IPv6 /48 or shorter: longer ones are mostly blackholing) new-origin a prefix announced by a network that is neither yesterday's origin nor its customer or provider, with no ROA to say which is right more-specific a prefix no one announced yesterday, inside space another network announces, from a network that is not its customer and no ROA covers flapping a prefix announced and withdrawn 10,000 times an hour or more withdrawn twenty or more of a network's prefixes withdrawn by five or more peers at once blackhole a prefix tagged for blackholing ( 65535:666 ) ioda a country or a region whose routes, pings or traffic IODA sees fall slow-country a country with five or more probes whose median time to the nearest K root server is over 100 ms"],
["backbone.html#running-it","Backbone Explorer: the internet's routing on ChloeDB","Running it","feed --resume starts the archive after the newest update kept, so a restart fills its gap, and --hours 24 stops it after a day so a supervisor starts it again after a new fetch and load . RIS Live sends every collector's messages whatever filter it is asked for, so the feed keeps those of --collectors itself. On Kubernetes, chloedb-backbone/deploy/k8s.yaml runs the server, the feed and the app on a node of their own, and deploy/backup.yaml checkpoints the database each night and copies it to a volume."],
["backbone.html#sizes","Backbone Explorer: the internet's routing on ChloeDB","Sizes","Measured with one collector, rrc00 (about 90 peers), on 16 and 17 November 2026: about 5.5 million updates an hour, about 100 bytes an update stored, so about 13 GB a day; the updates are kept three days. The pages answered in 0.1 to 0.6 seconds on a 4-core machine. The feed holds RPKI's 1.04 million ROAs and yesterday's 1.44 million origins as prefix maps, about 4 GB with the allocator's share."],
["app-engine.html","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","","chloedb-apps is a prototype app engine on ChloeDB. You describe an app in a sentence (\"a stock room: items, suppliers, what is low, purchase orders\"), and the engine has a model plan and write it, checks what it wrote, stores every piece in ChloeDB and runs it. You then change it by asking (\"add a Check price online button on each item\"), and every change is a new version you can go back from. Everything an app is lives in ChloeDB, as rows: Its pieces are SQL statements. Screens are CREATE SCREEN , rules are CREATE RULE , tables are CREATE TABLE , and each is a row of sa_capabilities . A version is a row of sa_compositions that lists the pieces it is made of, so going back is one write. Tables only grow, so an app's data survives every change and every step back. Pieces are shared. Components ( ui.calendar(props) ) and helpers ( lib.dates.addDays(day, 1) ) are written once and used by every app. They are found for a new request by words (BM25) and by meaning (a vector column), and linked in a graph that ChloeDB walks ( REACHABLE , ppr ) to offer an app a newer version. 150 of them ship in chloedb-apps/library/ . What people do is recorded in ChloeDB ( sa_events ), and the engine counts patterns in it (a search typed again and again, the same field changed row after row) to suggest changes, or to build one beside the live version for the owner to try, keep or drop. Apps get services: sign-in, files, email (held in an outbox unless a mail server is set), address search and maps, and, when the owner switches them on, a model and the web (below). It is a program of its own, in Node.js, that talks to chloedb-server over HTTP. Views run in sandboxed frames in the browser, and server functions run in QuickJS compiled to WebAssembly, with no network and a time limit."],
["app-engine.html#run-it","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","Run it","You need Node.js 20 or later, chloedb-server, and Claude Code signed in ( claude on the PATH). The engine calls claude -p , so it uses that sign-in and needs no API key of its own. run.sh starts a chloedb-server of its own on 127.0.0.1:7720 , with its database in chloedb-apps/data/ . --strata URL uses a server that is already running instead (started with --seq-no , for live updates). Every option is in the engine's README . A first version of a small app takes about a minute and a bigger one 2 to 4 minutes; a change takes 20 seconds to 2 minutes. The time and the cost of each build are on its timeline (Versions → Timeline)."],
["app-engine.html#on-a-cluster","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","On a cluster","A cluster the operator runs gets the engine from its StrataCluster , beside the crawler and the spreadsheets: The operator runs the engine's own image ( ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-apps : Node, Playwright's Chromium for the drawn checks, and Claude Code) as a Deployment of one pod, <cluster>-apps , behind a Service of that name, and restarts the coordinators so that they list it: Apps → App creator appears in the command center. The app creator is a page of the command center. Describe an app and build it there: the conversation of changes and builds (each build's progress, its screens drawn while the model writes the rest, its timeline), the app itself on a phone, a tablet or a computer (drawn 1280 px wide and scaled to fit the stage), and what it is made of: Made of measures how much of the code that runs the engine generated from the CREATE TABLE, CREATE SCREEN and CREATE RULE statements the agent wrote, how much the agent wrote from scratch (JavaScript, or a screen written out as JSON), and how much is the library's, piece by piece ( GET /api/apps/<id>/made , engine/provenance.mjs ); Code reads each piece's SQL and edits a CREATE SCREEN with no model; Time is where its builds' time and cost went; Data (the first tab after a build, with each table as a CSV), Versions , Design and Reach are the app's tables, versions, looks and switches. The bar shows what the app's builds cost in all, and its menu renames the app ( POST /api/apps/<id>/rename {name} ; a name given there, or when the app is made, stays: a later plan does not rename it, and a label that starts the description, like acme-shop , stays in front of the name the plan picks), duplicates it with its rows ( POST /api/apps/<id>/duplicate ), exports its rows as JSON ( GET /api/apps/<id>/export ) and deletes it ( POST /api/apps/<id>/delete ): its own tables app_<id>_* and no other app's, its pieces, versions, use, settings, members and files; the shared library stays. Usage , beside the app's screens, is the studio's Activity view over the whole stage: each visit step by step, the paths people take between screens, how easy the app is to use and what needs a look. The app's screens run as they do in the studio: in frames with the studio's sandbox and policy (no same origin, so a view reads nothing of the command center's, its key included, and no network), each read and write sent to the page as a message, which calls the engine for that one app. People's sign-in does not pass through chloedb-server, so the creator shows an app as a visitor sees it; with publicUrl , Its own page opens the app as people use it, and Studio opens the standalone studio. Live updates come by long polls of GET /api/events , since chloedb-server forwards whole answers. Its apps are rows of the cluster, each read and written with a key of its own. The engine reaches the coordinators with a key limited to its tables, sa_* and app_* (the operator makes it, <cluster>-apps-credentials , when the coordinators can, keys for apps ; spec.apps.tables changes it; an operator whose ClusterRole cannot make Secrets gives it the cluster's admin token, tokenSecret , instead), and gives every app a key made from that one ( POST /v1/keys/own ), limited to the app's tables app_<id>_* (write) and the table its library's table functions read ( sa_numbers , read), named apps-app/app-<id> . An app's app.sql , inserts, updates and removes go with its key, so the server refuses another app's rows, the engine's people and sessions, and every other table of the cluster, whatever the engine's rewrite of the SQL lets through; the keys end when the engine's key is revoked. An engine given the admin token (an operator from before such keys, deploy/kube.yaml ) makes itself the limited key, follows the feed of changes with it too (the server sends a limited key the operations of its tables alone), and keeps the token only for an older server's feed, and says so in its log; GET /api/status shows which ( keys ). A coordinator over shards walks no graphs, so the engine walks its own ( engine/walk.mjs ), and it tells the model that an app reads its links a step at a time. The studio and each app's own page have an address of their own , publicUrl , through a node's port, a load balancer, or an Ingress of your own in front of the Service. Files and map tiles the creator's frames show are loaded from there too (without it, through chloedb-server, which needs the key the frames cannot send). Studio asks the engine, through chloedb-server and so only with an admin key, for a link that signs the browser in once, within two minutes, for 30 days. Anything else that reaches the studio's address gets a page that says where to sign in. Keys limited to tables build apps by their tables. Beside a cluster the engine checks its callers ( \"checks_callers\": true , engine/callers.mjs ): reading an app takes read on its tables ( app_<id>_* ), using it as its screens do (rows, functions, files) takes write there, and changing, publishing, renaming or deleting it takes admin there; building a new one, or a copy, takes admin on every app's tables ( app_* ), which the app-owner preset gives. A key limited to tables lists only the apps whose tables it reads, follows one app's events ( /api/events?app=<id> ), and never gets the studio's login or the graph of every app. A key of every table keeps read to look and admin to change. Apps their owners publish are open to everyone, as far as the owner says. Publish in the app creator asks who can see this? ( POST /api/apps/<id>/publish {on, access, name, hosts} , engine/publish.mjs ): only me (not published, the default), visitors can look ( access: \"look\" : read what its screens show, no writes), customers can look and add ( \"add\" : also add rows, upload files and run its functions, and edit or delete only the rows they added themselves, as sa_events records who added each), or anyone can do anything ( \"change\" , which asks for the app's name to be typed). An app that never chose (a publish that names no access, or an app published before the choice existed) is at look until its owner picks more. A visitor who may only look is not offered the app's forms, nor the buttons and row actions that add, change or remove; a call their access does not allow is a 403 marked refused that says what was refused (\"read-only for visitors\"), shown as a quiet note, never as an error. See it as a stranger opens the app's own page as someone with no account would see it under the choice being weighed (each call carries x-sa-as: visitor , which only ever lowers what the studio's session may do), and the tab lists the columns that look like emails, phone numbers or addresses, which a public app shows to anyone. A published app is reached at /a/<id> on the engine's address, at <name>.<domain> when the engine has a domain ( spec.apps.publish , below), and at hosts of the owner's own (a CNAME to <name>.<domain> ). The public reaches the app as it runs and nothing of how it is made: its page and the runtime's files, its state without its requests, versions, people or outbox, and the calls its screens make under its access, and app.ask , app.fetch and app.email under the app's Reach switches and its model calls a day. What a visitor writes is checked on the server: a column whose forms offer a fixed list of choices takes only those (and the values the app's own code writes to it), a field is at most 10 KB, a row 64 KB and a list 100 items ( STRATA_APPS_PUBLIC_FIELD_MAX , STRATA_APPS_PUBLIC_ROW_MAX ), and a refusal is a 400 that says why; a failure tells a visitor nothing of shards or physical tables. An error a visitor's browser or call hits is recorded under Usage and never starts a repair, so nothing a stranger sends reaches the model. The app's people ( GET /api/apps/<id>/people ) are listed only to its signed-in members, with their emails only for its owner; one account serves every app on the engine, but a visitor is a member of an app only once they sign up or in on it, and signing up with an email that has an account signs in with the right password and otherwise says only that the email and password cannot be used. Building and changing it, other apps, versions built to try, the library and the studio stay behind the studio's login. Each visitor is held to 240 calls a minute and 20 of the dear ones (model, web, mail, accounts, uploads); STRATA_APPS_PUBLIC_RATE and STRATA_APPS_PUBLIC_DEAR_RATE change that. The model's key is the cluster's ( secretEnv 's ANTHROPIC_API_KEY , as the agents use), and spendCapUsd stops every model call once the engine has spent that much (25 US dollars unless set; 0 for no limit). What it keeps outside the cluster (files people gave its apps, map tiles, what the model has cost, Claude Code's home) is on a volume of its own, <cluster>-apps , kept when spec.apps goes and deleted with the cluster. spec.apps default publicUrl where browsers reach the studio and each app's own page; without it the creator works in the command center, but cannot open the studio or an app's own page, and its frames show no files publish open published apps to everyone, at <name>.<domain> : domain is your own ( apps.example.com , with a DNS record *.apps.example.com pointing at the load balancer) or sslip (the load balancer's address as a name of sslip.io, kids-reading.203-0-113-5.sslip.io , with no record); email for Let's Encrypt. The operator adds a load balancer, <cluster>-apps-public (ports 80 and 443; most clouds bill for one), and Caddy beside the engine, which gets a published app's certificate the first time someone opens it, after asking the engine whether the host is one of its apps ( GET /api/tls/ask ), and keeps the certificates on the engine's volume serviceType , nodePort ClusterIP the Service's type: ClusterIP (put an Ingress in front), NodePort (30000 to 32767) or LoadBalancer (most clouds bill for one) spendCapUsd 25 what the model may cost, in US dollars, counted across restarts storage 1Gi its volume's size (it only grows) and storageClassName image ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-apps:latest the engine's image env the engine's own settings ( STRATA_APPS_MODEL , STRATA_APPS_SMALL_MODEL , ...); secrets go in secretEnv paused false no pod for now (the apps stay in their tables) args , resources more arguments of the engine, and its container's resources (250m CPU and 600 MiB asked, 1.5 GiB at most) The image runs alone as well, with a chloedb-server of its own on its volume: docker run -p 7730:7730 -v apps:/data -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ghcr.io/chloetech-ai/chloedb-apps (without STRATA_APP_TOKEN there is no login, so keep that on your own machine)."],
["app-engine.html#a-model-and-the-web-per-app","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","A model and the web, per app","An app's code can ask a model, reach the web and run on a schedule, each only once the app's owner has switched it on in the studio, under Model and web : what the app calls limits A model app.ask(prompt, {json, system}) : a small model (Haiku; STRATA_APPS_ASK_MODEL ) summarizes, sorts, extracts or drafts the same question gets the kept answer ( sa_asks ) at no cost; 200 questions a day per app by default (the owner can change it; STRATA_APPS_ASKS_PER_DAY ), 20 a minute Web search app.ask(prompt, {web: true}) : the model searches the web before it answers kept answers expire after 6 hours; a call stops at $0.25 Web pages and APIs app.fetch(url, {method, json, headers, secret}) : JSON parsed, an HTML page's readable text and title; CREATE SOURCE and PULL read feeds and JSON lists into tables the hosts the owner allowed, on public addresses only (below); 2 MB and 10 seconds a request; GET answers kept 10 minutes; 1000 a day per app Schedules EVERY ... on a rule or function, and CREATE SOURCE at most every 15 minutes; stops after 5 failures in a row Until the owner switches it on, the call answers that it is off, and the app shows that instead of failing (it is not sent to the repair). A build says which of these the code uses, which are still off, and which hosts and secrets it needs that the owner has not given it. In a build's tests the calls answer empty and reach nothing. Under the web, the owner also allows each host an app reads from (GET, HEAD) and, separately, each host it may send to (POST); a host covers its subdomains. Hosts the code names, or tried and was refused, are listed for the owner to allow, and every hop of a redirect must be allowed too, so a change (asked for, built from use, or a repair) never widens what an app reaches by itself. Apps switched on before host lists keep \"any public host\" until the owner replaces it. An API key is a secret : the owner pastes it once, binds it to the hosts it may go to, and says where it goes ( Authorization: Bearer , a header of its own, or a query parameter). Code names it, app.fetch(url, {secret: 'weather'}) , and never holds it. Secrets are sealed with AES-256-GCM in ChloeDB, apart from the app's code, with STRATA_APPS_SECRET_KEY (or a key the engine keeps in DATA/secret.key ), sent only to their hosts, never shown again, and replaced by [secret] in an answer that echoes them. A key written into code fails the build's checks. A fourth switch, Schedules , lets rules and functions run on their own: CREATE RULE brief EVERY DAY AT '07:00' TZ 'Europe/Madrid' AS ... , or a source that fills a table from a feed or a JSON API, CREATE SOURCE news FROM FEED 'https://news.example.com/rss' EVERY 1 HOUR INTO stories (title, link, published, summary) KEY link (each item added once, by its key). A schedule runs at most every 15 minutes, under the app's daily limits, and stops after 5 failures in a row; the studio lists each with its next run, its last result, Run now and Pause. app.fetch refuses addresses on the machine or its networks: loopback, private and link-local ranges, carrier-grade NAT, unique-local and link-local IPv6, and IPv4 inside IPv6. Every address a name resolves to is checked when the connection is made, and again after every redirect, so a name that points inside the network is refused too. Only ports 80, 443, 8080 and 8443 are open, and a URL may not carry a name or password."],
["app-engine.html#before-you-share-it","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","Before you share it","It is a prototype for one person or a small team: Run alone, the studio has no sign-in. Anyone who can reach it can change any app and spend on the model. Keep it on 127.0.0.1 , put it behind the shared-key gate in chloedb-apps/deploy/ (a link with a key, kept in a cookie), or run it beside a cluster ( above ), where it signs in the cluster's admins. An app is for the people who run the studio until its owner publishes it. Beside a cluster, an app's own pages need the studio's sign-in, so only the cluster's admins open them, until its owner publishes it ( above ): then anyone with its address uses it as far as the owner chose, and signs up and in with the engine's own accounts. Signing in with Google and others is planned. Set a spending limit. STRATA_APPS_SPEND_CAP_USD stops the model once the engine has spent that much, counted from what Claude Code reports and kept across restarts. The apps' own questions count apart: together they stop at a day's share ( STRATA_APPS_ASK_CAP_USD , a quarter of the limit by default, and $20 a day with no limit set), and builds and checks get the rest, so a published app's visitors cannot stop the studio's builds, nor builds the apps, nor one app the others for more than a day; one app's questions stop at $2 a day ( STRATA_APPS_ASK_USD_PER_DAY ). A published app suggests changes from its use rather than building them, unless its owner sets its mode to build. App data is checked in code, not on the server. People sign in to apps, but row rules ( lib.access ) run in the app's views and functions; the server does not enforce them yet. A piece that passes the checks can still be wrong. The engine opens every view after a build, draws each on a phone and a computer (with Playwright installed), fixes what its rules measure, and runs the app's tests; going back a version is the safety net for the rest."],
["app-engine.html#tests","chloedb-apps: apps built from a description","Tests","The tests that need a chloedb-server find chloedb-server/target/release/chloedb-server (or STRATA_SERVER_BIN ) and are skipped without one; CI runs them all against the server it builds ( STRATA_APPS_NEED_SERVER=1 ), and again against a cluster of two shards ( STRATA_APPS_TEST_SHARDS=2 , which starts the shards and a coordinator over them in place of a server). No test calls a model."]
]};
